-- Idempotent upserts. Focus-group rows intentionally absent (predicted rates only in this batch).
START TRANSACTION;


> Before or during installation, this agreement may be modified by mutual agreement of the parties as to “extras” or other departures from the attached plans. Such modifications, however, may be authorized only in a writing signed by both parties.

During the work, Timothy added a Bethlehem-star pulley, painted sheep stalls, hand-lettered Scripture banners, and other extras that Mary orally requested for orally agreed prices in addition to the contract price. Timothy installed the extras. Mary later refused to pay anything for the extras, totaling $4,700 at the agreed prices, solely because no written, signed authorization was ever made.', 'Mary hired Timothy, a scenic designer, under a written agreement to design and install a traveling Nativity play set for Mary’s private Advent festival at a fixed price of $18,400. The agreement attached detailed plans and provided:

> Before or during installation, this agreement may be modified by mutual agreement of the parties as to “extras” or other departures from the attached plans. Such modifications, however, may be authorized only in a writing signed by both parties.

During the work, Timothy added a Bethlehem-star pulley, painted sheep stalls, hand-lettered Scripture banners, and other extras that Mary orally requested for orally agreed prices in addition to the contract price. Timothy installed the extras. Mary later refused to pay anything for the extras, totaling $4,700 at the agreed prices, solely because no written, signed authorization was ever made.

If Timothy sues Mary for the extras, which, if any, of the following will effectively support Mary’s defense?', 'If Timothy sues Mary for the extras, which, if any, of the following will effectively support Mary’s defense?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ14375.md","source_question_id":"14375","variant_slug":"nativity_star_rig","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"56050200","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A written services contract required signed written authorization for extras, but the buyer orally requested extras, agreed to prices, and the contractor performed them. Can the buyer avoid paying solely because the written authorization was missing?","review_truth":"A party who orally requests and receives extra work cannot usually use the no-oral-modification clause as a defense after the other party materially relies by performing the requested extras.","dominant_trap":"A","anchor_card":{"card_id":"RC-CONTRACTS-NOM-EXTRAS-01","title":"Signed-writing clause vs requested extras","signal":"The buyer orally asks for extras, gets them, then refuses to pay only because no signed writing exists.","student_move":"Cut timing and consideration doctrines; then use the Gold Key to kill the condition defense.","tiny_rule":"Requested-and-performed extras can defeat a signed-writing-only defense.","trap":"The condition label feels exact because the writing clause really exists.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONTRACTS-NATIVITY-STAR-RIG-01","statement":"A no-oral-modification clause is not an automatic defense when the party invoking it orally requested the change and the other party materially relied by performing the extra work.","type":"exception","trigger":"signed-writing-only defense after orally requested and performed extras","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts Sections 89(c) and 150","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONTRACTS-NATIVITY-STAR-RIG-01","statement":"When the call asks what supports a named party’s defense, test each doctrine label against that defense before deciding whether the doctrine is true in the abstract.","type":"call_focus","trigger":"three short doctrine names plus a no-valid-defense residual","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"timing check","prompt":"A written agreement is later orally changed during performance. Does the parol evidence rule supply the defense?","answer":"No. The parol evidence rule targets prior or contemporaneous agreements, not later modifications."},{"drill_type":"call_focus","target_skill":"defense matching","prompt":"The buyer’s only defense is no signed authorization. Which is more responsive: preexisting duty rule or no valid defense after requested performance?","answer":"No valid defense after requested performance."},{"drill_type":"anchor","target_skill":"no-oral-modification reliance","prompt":"The buyer orally requests extras, the contractor performs, and the buyer refuses because the contract required written authorization. What tiny rule controls?","answer":"Requested-and-performed extras can defeat the signed-writing-only defense."}]}'),
  ('9c41d43e-7c08-503a-84a4-58d6c451433f', '14379_psalm_lightshow', 'CONTRACTS', 'Contract Content and Meaning', 'Parol Evidence Rule', 'interpret_ambiguity_vs_add_or_contradict_terms', 'A private company that stages ticketed Christian arts events signed a written production-services contract for a Psalm 23 drone-light show. The opening recital stated:

> “This Agreement, between Esther Events, LLC (hereafter called ‘Sponsor’), and Manna Media, Inc., and Lydia, its President (hereafter called ‘Producer’), witnesseth:”

The signatures appeared in this format:

> ESTHER EVENTS, LLC
> By /s/ Esther Vale, Managing Member
>
> MANNA MEDIA, INC.
> By /s/ Timothy Reed, Vice President
>
> /s/ Lydia Stone, President

Manna Media, Inc., later became insolvent and failed to produce the show. Esther Events sued Lydia individually for breach. At trial, Lydia offered evidence from negotiations before the contract was signed that only Manna Media, Inc., was to be legally responsible for producing the show.', 'A private company that stages ticketed Christian arts events signed a written production-services contract for a Psalm 23 drone-light show. The opening recital stated:

> “This Agreement, between Esther Events, LLC (hereafter called ‘Sponsor’), and Manna Media, Inc., and Lydia, its President (hereafter called ‘Producer’), witnesseth:”

The signatures appeared in this format:

> ESTHER EVENTS, LLC
> By /s/ Esther Vale, Managing Member
>
> MANNA MEDIA, INC.
> By /s/ Timothy Reed, Vice President
>
> /s/ Lydia Stone, President

Manna Media, Inc., later became insolvent and failed to produce the show. Esther Events sued Lydia individually for breach. At trial, Lydia offered evidence from negotiations before the contract was signed that only Manna Media, Inc., was to be legally responsible for producing the show.

If the court finds the written contract to be completely integrated, is Lydia’s proffered evidence admissible?', 'If the court finds the written contract to be completely integrated, is Lydia’s proffered evidence admissible?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ14379.md","source_question_id":"14379","variant_slug":"psalm_lightshow","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"52040300","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"not_supplied","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A completely integrated contract ambiguously lists an officer with a company, and the officer offers pre-signing negotiations to show only the company was bound; is the evidence admissible?","review_truth":"A complete integration blocks prior negotiations used to add or contradict terms, but ambiguity in the writing can still be explained with extrinsic evidence.","dominant_trap":"A","anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-CONTRACTS-PER-AMBIGUITY-01","title":"Purpose Gate for Parol Evidence","signal":"Complete integration plus outside negotiations.","student_move":"Ask what the evidence is being used to do.","tiny_rule":"Interpret ambiguity can come in; add or contradict terms cannot.","trap":"four-corners absolutism","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONTRACTS-AMBIGUITY-PER-01","statement":"Complete integration blocks prior negotiations used to add or contradict terms, but it does not block prior negotiations used to explain an ambiguity in the writing.","type":"exception","trigger":"complete integration plus unclear signer capacity in the recital or signature block","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts §§ 214(c), 215","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONTRACTS-PSALM-LIGHTSHOW-01","statement":"Before choosing yes or no, label the purpose of the proffer: explain ambiguity, add a term, contradict a term, or attack formation.","type":"trap_spotting","trigger":"two yes answers and two no answers in a parol-evidence admissibility call","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"four-corners overclaim","prompt":"A complete integration is ambiguous about who signed as a party; outside negotiations are offered to explain that ambiguity. Does a four-corners-only answer survive?","answer":"No. It overclaims because ambiguity evidence may explain the writing."},{"drill_type":"trap_spotting","target_skill":"purpose gate","prompt":"Classify the proffer: add a term, contradict a term, or explain ambiguity.","answer":"Explain ambiguity."},{"drill_type":"residual","target_skill":"correct result with correct reason","prompt":"Two choices say admissible. One says no contradiction; one says ambiguity. Which reason wins?","answer":"Ambiguity."}]}'),
  ('28cfbb1e-46ef-5a19-9e84-a0a92e6893b0', '14382_bethlehem_lanterns', 'CONTRACTS', 'Contract Content and Meaning', 'Parol Evidence Rule / Usage of Trade', 'objective trade usage vs prior oral exclusivity statement', 'Peter’s Pilgrim Supply, a private regional distributor that sells Christian craft goods to bookstores and camp stores, signed a written agreement with Lydia Lantern Works. Under the agreement, Peter’s would distribute Lydia’s “Bethlehem Star” devotional lantern kits for four years throughout an eleven-town river district. The agreement required Peter’s to “promote in good faith the sale of Bethlehem Star lantern kits” in that district, but it said nothing about whether Peter’s could distribute other devotional lantern kits. Nine months later, Peter’s agreed with Barnabas Bright Crafts to distribute Barnabas’s battery-free devotional lantern kits in the same eleven-town district.', 'Peter’s Pilgrim Supply, a private regional distributor that sells Christian craft goods to bookstores and camp stores, signed a written agreement with Lydia Lantern Works. Under the agreement, Peter’s would distribute Lydia’s “Bethlehem Star” devotional lantern kits for four years throughout an eleven-town river district. The agreement required Peter’s to “promote in good faith the sale of Bethlehem Star lantern kits” in that district, but it said nothing about whether Peter’s could distribute other devotional lantern kits. Nine months later, Peter’s agreed with Barnabas Bright Crafts to distribute Barnabas’s battery-free devotional lantern kits in the same eleven-town district.

If Lydia Lantern Works sues Peter’s Pilgrim Supply for breach of their distribution contract, which of the following facts, if established, would most strengthen Lydia’s case?', 'If Lydia Lantern Works sues Peter’s Pilgrim Supply for breach of their distribution contract, which of the following facts, if established, would most strengthen Lydia’s case?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ14382.md","source_question_id":"14382","variant_slug":"bethlehem_lanterns","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"52040300","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium","deciding_phase":"CLASH","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A written goods-distribution agreement requires good-faith promotion but is silent on competing lines; which fact best strengthens the maker''s breach claim when the distributor later carries a competing line?","review_truth":"A regular trade usage can supply or qualify a missing contract term when it can reasonably fit with the writing, but a prior oral exclusivity promise is vulnerable when it contradicts a final written agreement.","dominant_trap":"D","anchor_card":{"card_id":"RC-CONTRACTS-TRADE-USAGE-SOURCE","title":"Source of the missing term","signal":"Written goods contract is silent; choices offer trade practice, oral words, business harm, and competitor conduct.","student_move":"Cut actor/effect facts; clash the two source facts.","tiny_rule":"Trade usage can be a UCC source of meaning; prior oral contradiction is not the same source.","trap":"Direct word-match to ''exclusive.''","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONTRACTS-TRADE-USAGE-01","statement":"Under the UCC, trade usage can explain, supplement, or qualify a written goods agreement if it can reasonably be read consistently with the express terms. A prior or contemporaneous oral agreement cannot contradict a final written expression.","type":"distinction","trigger":"A written goods contract is silent on a restriction, and the choices compare industry practice with a prior oral promise.","authority":"UCC 1-303(c)-(e); UCC 2-202(a).","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONTRACTS-TRADE-USAGE-01","statement":"When two finalists try to fill a silent written term, sort by source: objective trade practice is the responsive source; a prior spoken label is the trap source.","type":"clash","trigger":"Choices split between industry practice and a pre-signing oral exclusivity statement.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"wrong_element","prompt":"A contract breach claim needs a missing exclusivity term; a choice shows only a 4% sales drop. Cut or keep?","answer":"Cut. It shows possible harm, not the source of the exclusivity term."},{"drill_type":"CLASH","target_skill":"source-axis","prompt":"Two finalists: uniform trade usage vs prior oral exclusivity statement. Name the axis.","answer":"Source of contract meaning: objective trade usage vs prior oral promise."},{"drill_type":"ANCHOR","target_skill":"UCC trade usage","prompt":"Can a regular trade usage supplement a written goods agreement when it reasonably fits the express terms?","answer":"Yes. That is the UCC trade-usage unlock."},{"drill_type":"TRAP","target_skill":"parol evidence bait","prompt":"Why is a pre-signing ''exclusive'' quote dangerous in a written contract item?","answer":"It is prior oral evidence and may contradict the final writing; it is not the same as trade usage."}]}'),
  ('9e542786-314e-5f9c-83a9-183ed155264a', '14391_noble_fir_nativity', 'CONTRACTS', 'Defenses to Enforceability', 'Supervening impracticability / illegality (mistake as alternative theory)', 'mutual mistake versus supervening impracticability as the framed discharge theory on supervening-illegality facts', 'Mary chairs the Living Nativity committee at a church in California and on May 20 wrote to several wholesale evergreen nurseries asking about 100 noble firs for the church''s annual outdoor Nativity scene. On June 1, she received the following signed letter from a wholesale tree farm in Oregon run by Paul:

> "We have just obtained 100 of the noble firs you inquired about and can supply them for $180 each. We can guarantee shipment no later than August 1."

On June 10, Paul sold and delivered the same firs to another church for $215 each. Unaware of that transaction, Mary on the morning of June 11 mailed Paul a letter rejecting the offer, but, changing her mind an hour later, retrieved from her local post office the letter of rejection and immediately dispatched to Paul a letter of acceptance, which Paul received on June 14. On June 9, a valid federal Plant Protection Act quarantine order making the interstate shipment of noble firs from Oregon punishable as a federal crime had become effective, but neither Mary nor Paul was aware until June 15 that the quarantine was already in effect.', 'Mary chairs the Living Nativity committee at a church in California and on May 20 wrote to several wholesale evergreen nurseries asking about 100 noble firs for the church''s annual outdoor Nativity scene. On June 1, she received the following signed letter from a wholesale tree farm in Oregon run by Paul:

> "We have just obtained 100 of the noble firs you inquired about and can supply them for $180 each. We can guarantee shipment no later than August 1."

On June 10, Paul sold and delivered the same firs to another church for $215 each. Unaware of that transaction, Mary on the morning of June 11 mailed Paul a letter rejecting the offer, but, changing her mind an hour later, retrieved from her local post office the letter of rejection and immediately dispatched to Paul a letter of acceptance, which Paul received on June 14. On June 9, a valid federal Plant Protection Act quarantine order making the interstate shipment of noble firs from Oregon punishable as a federal crime had become effective, but neither Mary nor Paul was aware until June 15 that the quarantine was already in effect.

As between Mary and Paul, which of the following is an accurate statement?', 'As between Mary and Paul, which of the following is an accurate statement?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ14391.md","source_question_id":"14391","variant_slug":"noble_fir_nativity","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"56050602","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium","deciding_phase":"CALL","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"On June 1 an out-of-state wholesaler offers 100 items to a buyer; on June 9 a federal criminal statute takes effect making the interstate sale a crime (unknown to both); on June 10 the wholesaler sells the same items to a third party; on June 11 the buyer mails a rejection, retrieves it the same hour, and sends an acceptance the wholesaler receives on June 14. Was a contract formed, and if so, is it enforceable?","review_truth":"A supervening criminal statute that makes performance illegal voids the contract on multiple theories; the original NCBE key frames the discharge as mutual mistake, but supervening impracticability is also defensible.","dominant_trap":"D","anchor_card":{"card_id":"RC-CONTRACTS-SUPERVENING-ILLEGALITY-01","title":"Supervening illegality — mistake vs impracticability","signal":"A federal criminal statute takes effect between offer and acceptance, unknown to both parties, and the contract performance has become illegal.","student_move":"Resolve formation first (revocation needs receipt; rejection needs receipt; acceptance is dispatch). Then apply the Gold Key: the NCBE framed key is mutual mistake; impracticability is co-credited.","tiny_rule":"When a supervening criminal statute triggers, both mutual mistake and supervening impracticability may discharge; the NCBE''s framed key is mutual mistake.","trap":"The dominant trap is the most-picked answer (impracticability). It is doctrinally true; the discrimination is which framing the NCBE key picks.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONTRACTS-NOBLE-FIR-NATIVITY-01","statement":"A supervening criminal statute that makes performance illegal supports BOTH mutual mistake (Restatement § 152) and supervening impracticability (Restatement § 261); the original NCBE key uses mutual mistake as the framed answer, with impracticability also credited.","type":"distinction","trigger":"federal criminal statute takes effect mid-transaction, both parties unaware","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts §§ 152, 261; Plant Protection Act, 7 U.S.C. § 7714","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONTRACTS-NOBLE-FIR-NATIVITY-01","statement":"The mailbox rule covers acceptance (effective on dispatch) but NOT rejection (effective on receipt); a mailed-then-retrieved rejection never reaches the offeror and never terminates the offeree''s power of acceptance.","type":"distinction","trigger":"stem describes a mailed rejection followed by retrieval and a replacement acceptance","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"asymmetry check (acceptance vs rejection)","prompt":"A buyer mails a rejection on Monday morning, retrieves it from the post office the same hour, and mails an acceptance the same afternoon. The seller receives the acceptance on Wednesday. Is the acceptance effective?","answer":"Yes. The mailbox rule makes acceptance effective on dispatch; the rejection was retrieved the same hour and never received, so it never terminated the offer."},{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"notice element (revocation)","prompt":"On June 1, A offers goods to B. On June 5, A sells the same goods to C. On June 7, unaware of the resale, B mails an acceptance to A. Is the offer revoked?","answer":"No. Revocation requires receipt of notice by the offeree; the resale to C is not, by itself, notice to B. Restatement § 50(1)."},{"drill_type":"anchor","target_skill":"supervening-illegality discharge","prompt":"A federal criminal statute takes effect between offer and acceptance, making performance illegal, unknown to both parties. A contract is later formed. On which theories is performance excused, and which theory does the NCBE framed key typically use?","answer":"Both mutual mistake (Restatement § 152) and supervening impracticability (Restatement § 261) may discharge; the NCBE framed key is mutual mistake, with impracticability co-credited per official scoring notes."}]}'),
  ('4e935f89-8f71-5c63-bb68-5258dd574659', '14394_lydia_puppet_stage', 'CONTRACTS', 'Defenses to Enforceability', 'Defenses to Enforceability', 'defense to Paul’s oral guaranty versus defects in Lydia’s loan', 'At a private Christian craft fair, Lydia asked Barnabas, a longtime family friend, ''Would you lend me $1,600 to buy a portable puppet stage for my paid parable-story classes? I’ll repay you with interest one year from today.'' Paul, Lydia’s uncle, said, ''If Lydia does not repay you as promised, I will.'' Barnabas immediately handed Lydia $1,600 in cash. Lydia bought the puppet stage. When the debt came due, Lydia and Paul both refused to pay anything.', 'At a private Christian craft fair, Lydia asked Barnabas, a longtime family friend, ''Would you lend me $1,600 to buy a portable puppet stage for my paid parable-story classes? I’ll repay you with interest one year from today.'' Paul, Lydia’s uncle, said, ''If Lydia does not repay you as promised, I will.'' Barnabas immediately handed Lydia $1,600 in cash. Lydia bought the puppet stage. When the debt came due, Lydia and Paul both refused to pay anything.

In Barnabas’s action against Paul to recover $1,600 plus interest, which statement best summarizes Paul’s best defense?', 'In Barnabas’s action against Paul to recover $1,600 plus interest, which statement best summarizes Paul’s best defense?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ14394.md","source_question_id":null,"variant_slug":"lydia_puppet_stage","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"54030400","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium","deciding_phase":"CALL","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"Lydia gets a cash loan after Paul orally promises to pay if Lydia does not; Paul received no apparent economic benefit. What is Paul’s best defense to liability?","review_truth":"An oral promise to answer for another’s debt is unenforceable unless a writing exists or the guarantor’s leading purpose was the guarantor’s own economic advantage.","dominant_trap":"C","anchor_card":{"card_id":"CONTRACTS-SOF-SURETY-MAIN-PURPOSE","title":"Suretyship main-purpose gate","signal":"If the borrower does not pay, I will.","student_move":"Ask whether the guarantor was mainly seeking personal economic advantage.","tiny_rule":"No self-benefit means oral guaranty stays in the writing-required bucket.","trap":"no-consideration dominant trap and one-year bait","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONTRACTS-LYDIA-PUPPET-01","statement":"An oral promise to answer for another person’s debt is usually inside the Statute of Frauds; the main-purpose exception removes it only when the guarantor’s leading purpose is the guarantor’s own economic advantage.","type":"exception","trigger":"A speaker says, ''If the borrower does not pay, I will,'' and no signed writing from that speaker appears.","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts §§ 110, 112, 116","last_minute_review":true},{"id":"GK-CONTRACTS-LYDIA-PUPPET-02","statement":"Consideration does not have to move into the guarantor’s pocket; performance given to the borrower can support the guarantor’s promise if it was bargained for.","type":"distinction","trigger":"The guarantor points to receiving no money personally even though the lender gave the borrower the money after the guaranty.","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 71","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONTRACTS-LYDIA-PUPPET-01","statement":"In a best-defense array, sort defenses by whose promise they attack: the guarantor’s promise beats borrower-loan defects when the lawsuit is against the guarantor.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"The call asks for the guarantor’s best defense, but several choices attack the borrower’s loan.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"Gold Key recall","target_skill":"suretyship main-purpose exception","prompt":"A guarantor orally promises to pay another’s debt and receives no apparent economic advantage. What defense is triggered?","answer":"Statute of Frauds suretyship defense; no main-purpose exception."},{"drill_type":"answer_array","target_skill":"defense sorting","prompt":"The borrower’s loan has an omitted term, but the guarantor is sued. Which promise should the answer attack?","answer":"The guarantor’s promise."},{"drill_type":"trap recovery","target_skill":"consideration to third person","prompt":"The lender gives money to the borrower after the guarantor promises to pay if the borrower defaults. Is the guarantor’s no-cash-received point the best consideration defense?","answer":"No; performance can be given to the borrower and still count."}]}'),
  ('3ca5a2cd-b362-58c5-ab42-7d4b08c5d80a', '14396_easter_handbell_plan', 'CONTRACTS', 'Defenses to Enforceability', 'Public Policy', 'statutory-violation forfeiture vs protected-class enforcement', 'Lydia owned an antique set of handbells that she used for neighborhood Easter concerts. She paid Barnabas Protection Plans for a written equipment-protection agreement covering accidental damage to the handbells. The agreement omitted coverage disclosures that a state service-plan statute requires for agreements of that kind. During a Good Friday rehearsal, a riser collapsed and damaged the handbells, a loss covered by the agreement’s stated terms. Barnabas refused payment, arguing that its omission made the agreement unenforceable because the agreement violated the statute.', 'Lydia owned an antique set of handbells that she used for neighborhood Easter concerts. She paid Barnabas Protection Plans for a written equipment-protection agreement covering accidental damage to the handbells. The agreement omitted coverage disclosures that a state service-plan statute requires for agreements of that kind. During a Good Friday rehearsal, a riser collapsed and damaged the handbells, a loss covered by the agreement’s stated terms. Barnabas refused payment, arguing that its omission made the agreement unenforceable because the agreement violated the statute.

Is Lydia likely to recover for the loss?', 'Is Lydia likely to recover for the loss?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ14396.md","source_question_id":"14396","variant_slug":"easter_handbell_plan","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"54031000","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium","deciding_phase":"CLASH","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":"Lydia is the purchaser protected by the service-plan statute, while Barnabas is invoking its own omission.","distilled_core_question":"A company writes a covered-loss agreement that omits terms required by a protective statute, then refuses to pay after a covered loss; can the protected buyer enforce?","review_truth":"When a statute is meant to protect one party to a contract, the other party cannot usually use its own statutory violation to avoid the protected party’s enforcement claim.","dominant_trap":"A","anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-CONTRACTS-PROTECTED-STATUTE-01","title":"Protected-class statute beats violation-alone forfeiture","signal":"The drafter invokes its own statutory omission against the protected buyer.","student_move":"Cut wrong-frame answers first, then use the Gold Key on the violation-alone answer.","tiny_rule":"The protected party may enforce when nonenforcement would frustrate the protective statute.","trap":"Every statutory violation voids the deal.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONTRACTS-PROTECTED-STATUTE-01","statement":"A statutory violation does not automatically let the violating drafter escape the deal. If the statute was meant to protect the party seeking enforcement, enforcement may serve the statute instead of frustrate it.","type":"exception","trigger":"one party invokes a contract’s statutory defect against the party the statute protects","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts §§ 178, 198 cmt. b; protected-class public-policy principle","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONTRACTS-PROTECTED-STATUTE-01","statement":"Do not stop at the yes/no outcome. On a right-outcome/wrong-reason pair, ask whether the reason answers enforceability or drifts into interpretation.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"two yes answers, one based on protected class and one based on drafter construction","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"wrong_frame","prompt":"A buyer sues to enforce a covered-loss protection agreement after the drafter omitted terms required by statute. Which answer is off-call: protected-class enforcement or strict construction against the drafter?","answer":"Strict construction is off-call unless the dispute is about ambiguous language."},{"drill_type":"CLASH","target_skill":"protected-class exception","prompt":"Two answers remain: no because the contract violated a regulatory statute; yes because the claimant is in the class protected by that statute. Name the axis.","answer":"Violation-alone forfeiture versus protected-class enforcement."},{"drill_type":"CALL","target_skill":"anchor escalation","prompt":"A statute-required term is missing from a contract, and the drafter invokes the violation against the protected buyer. Which Gold Key controls?","answer":"Protected-class statute beats violation-alone forfeiture."}]}'),
  ('fc5e7468-e075-51f4-a2a1-98c50cbba54c', '14397_fish_keychains', 'CONTRACTS', 'Defenses to Enforceability', 'Statute of Frauds', NULL, 'Daniel runs a private Jonah-themed escape-room booth for Christian conventions. He called Lydia, a wholesaler of ordinary catalog-stock olivewood fish keychains, and said that he would buy 800 keychains for his booth if Lydia could get them to him within seven days. Daniel did not mention a price. At the time of the call, the market price for 800 of those keychains was about $9,600. Lydia did not say yes or no on the call. Six days later, her driver arrived at Daniel''s storage unit with the 800 keychains and an invoice for $9,600. Before the driver arrived, the convention organizer had canceled Daniel''s booth space. Daniel refused to receive the keychains or pay the invoice.', 'Daniel runs a private Jonah-themed escape-room booth for Christian conventions. He called Lydia, a wholesaler of ordinary catalog-stock olivewood fish keychains, and said that he would buy 800 keychains for his booth if Lydia could get them to him within seven days. Daniel did not mention a price. At the time of the call, the market price for 800 of those keychains was about $9,600. Lydia did not say yes or no on the call. Six days later, her driver arrived at Daniel''s storage unit with the 800 keychains and an invoice for $9,600. Before the driver arrived, the convention organizer had canceled Daniel''s booth space. Daniel refused to receive the keychains or pay the invoice.

Is there an enforceable contract between Daniel and Lydia?', 'Is there an enforceable contract between Daniel and Lydia?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ14397.md","source_question_id":null,"variant_slug":"fish_keychains","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"54030400","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"PURE_ANCHOR","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"Buyer orally orders ordinary goods worth more than $500, leaves the price open, seller delivers, buyer refuses to accept, and no writing exists. Is the deal enforceable?","review_truth":"An oral sale-of-goods deal priced at $500 or more is unenforceable unless it satisfies the UCC writing requirement or an exception.","dominant_trap":"C","anchor_card":{"card_id":"CONTRACTS-SOF-OPEN-PRICE-01","title":"Formation is not enforceability","signal":"Oral goods deal over $500 with no writing and a missing price term.","student_move":"Check the writing gate before rewarding the open-price rule.","tiny_rule":"Open price can be filled, but the UCC writing gate still blocks enforcement unless an exception applies.","trap":"Yes because open price does not defeat contract formation.","confidence":"PURE_ANCHOR"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONTRACTS-FISH-KEYCHAINS-01","statement":"For goods priced at $500 or more, the UCC writing gate must be satisfied before the oral deal can be enforced. Mere shipment does not unlock the partial-performance exception when the buyer refuses to receive and accept the goods.","type":"rule","trigger":"Oral sale of goods worth thousands, no writing, delivery refused.","authority":"UCC § 2-201(1), (3)(c)","last_minute_review":true},{"id":"GK-CONTRACTS-FISH-KEYCHAINS-02","statement":"Open price and writing are separate gates. UCC § 2-305 can fill a missing price term, but it does not satisfy the UCC § 2-201 writing requirement.","type":"distinction","trigger":"A Yes answer says the missing price term does not defeat the deal.","authority":"UCC §§ 2-305(1), 2-201(1)","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONTRACTS-FISH-KEYCHAINS-01","statement":"Lock the call word enforceable before grading the Yes choices. A choice can prove formation with an open price and still miss the separate writing gate.","type":"call_focus","trigger":"The call asks enforceable contract, while a choice talks only about price-term formation.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"anchor_recall","target_skill":"UCC statute-of-frauds threshold","prompt":"An oral order for ordinary goods worth $4,800 has no writing and the buyer refuses delivery. Is the deal enforceable?","answer":"No, absent a UCC § 2-201 exception."},{"drill_type":"bait_doctrine","target_skill":"formation versus enforceability","prompt":"A choice says yes because missing price does not defeat a sale-of-goods contract. What call word can make that true rule a trap?","answer":"Enforceable."},{"drill_type":"exception_check","target_skill":"partial performance under UCC § 2-201","prompt":"Seller ships goods, but buyer refuses to receive and accept them. Does shipment alone satisfy the partial-performance exception?","answer":"No."}]}'),
  ('571fcbd2-0d02-54a5-af3a-f1a078b4012a', '14400_mechanical_camel', 'CONTRACTS', 'Defenses to Enforceability', 'Mistake / Lack of Mutual Assent', NULL, 'Pass-1 Step 1 — black-letter rule being tested.
A contract may be rescinded for mistake when the mistaken fact existed at contracting, went to a basic assumption of the bargain, materially harmed the party seeking rescission, and was not a risk that party bore.

Pass-1 Step 2 — invariant layer preserved.
The issue remains buyer-side rescission for mistake. The credited outcome remains the hidden, undiscoverable, main-use defect. The dominant trap remains the pedigree/authenticity paperwork mistake. The call remains a “which scenario best supports buyer rather than seller rescission” call. The answer-array geometry remains a 3-vs-1 scenario set: three post-sale surprises fail because they do not support buyer-side mistake rescission; one hidden main-use defect survives.

Pass-1 Step 5 — locked final Christian variation.

Mary, who knew nothing about stage machinery, inherited a motorized camel prop from her late uncle’s attic. The camel had an annoying habit of jerking and startling children during rehearsals. Mary sold the camel for $2,400 to Daniel, an experienced designer of touring Christmas pageants, who knew that Mary was ignorant about stage machinery. Before the sale, Mary told Daniel, “I hate to say it, but this camel jerks around and is nothing special.”', 'Pass-1 Step 1 — black-letter rule being tested.
A contract may be rescinded for mistake when the mistaken fact existed at contracting, went to a basic assumption of the bargain, materially harmed the party seeking rescission, and was not a risk that party bore.

Pass-1 Step 2 — invariant layer preserved.
The issue remains buyer-side rescission for mistake. The credited outcome remains the hidden, undiscoverable, main-use defect. The dominant trap remains the pedigree/authenticity paperwork mistake. The call remains a “which scenario best supports buyer rather than seller rescission” call. The answer-array geometry remains a 3-vs-1 scenario set: three post-sale surprises fail because they do not support buyer-side mistake rescission; one hidden main-use defect survives.

Pass-1 Step 5 — locked final Christian variation.

Mary, who knew nothing about stage machinery, inherited a motorized camel prop from her late uncle’s attic. The camel had an annoying habit of jerking and startling children during rehearsals. Mary sold the camel for $2,400 to Daniel, an experienced designer of touring Christmas pageants, who knew that Mary was ignorant about stage machinery. Before the sale, Mary told Daniel, “I hate to say it, but this camel jerks around and is nothing special.”

Which one of the following scenarios would best support an action by Daniel, rather than Mary, to rescind the sale?', 'Which one of the following scenarios would best support an action by Daniel, rather than Mary, to rescind the sale?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ14400.md","source_question_id":"14400","variant_slug":"mechanical_camel","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"54030600","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"An experienced buyer purchases a quirky pageant prop from an ignorant seller; which later fact gives the buyer the best rescission case?","review_truth":"Rescission for mistake is strongest when a hidden, existing fact defeats the contract’s basic purpose and the party seeking rescission did not knowingly take that risk.","dominant_trap":"A","anchor_card":{"card_id":"GK-CONTRACTS-MECHANICAL-CAMEL-01","title":"Mistake rescission: hidden main-use fact, no assumed risk","signal":"A buyer wants rescission after a new fact appears.","student_move":"Check direction, purpose, and risk.","tiny_rule":"Hidden main-use defect can support rescission; known risks, side uses, and hidden upside do not.","trap":"Treating any surprising post-sale fact as enough.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONTRACTS-MECHANICAL-CAMEL-01","statement":"A mistake supports rescission only when the mistaken fact existed at contracting, went to a basic assumption with material adverse effect, and the party seeking rescission did not bear that risk. A known defect, a collateral side-use problem, or a hidden upside does not unlock buyer rescission.","type":"rule","trigger":"A buyer seeks to rescind after discovering a post-sale fact about the thing sold.","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts §§ 152, 154.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONTRACTS-MECHANICAL-CAMEL-01","statement":"On a buyer-rather-than-seller rescission call, sort each scenario by direction and purpose: buyer harmed or buyer helped, main use or side use, hidden risk or known risk. Keep the fact that damages the buyer''s main expected use without repeating the warning already in the stem.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"The call asks which later scenario best supports one named party''s rescission action.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"direction_check","target_skill":"buyer-versus-seller rescission sorting","prompt":"A buyer discovers the item was made by a more famous maker than the paperwork showed. Which party does that fact help?","answer":"It helps the buyer or supports seller regret; it does not support buyer rescission."},{"drill_type":"risk_allocation","target_skill":"known-risk elimination","prompt":"The seller warned that the prop jerks; later it jerks and injures the buyer. Does that support mistake rescission?","answer":"No. The later event matches the known risk."},{"drill_type":"purpose_axis","target_skill":"main use versus collateral use","prompt":"A touring prop earns money for three years but cannot be used to manufacture replicas after retirement. Main-use defect or side-use defect?","answer":"Side-use defect."}]}'),
  ('831c7dd6-3034-5e23-ab47-860874038d79', '14406_advent_lanterns', 'CONTRACTS', 'Formation of Contracts', NULL, 'formation first vs option-label shortcut', 'Preparing for a fall youth-ministry expo, Lydia Supply, a private retreat-supply reseller, sent Barnabas Crafts a purchase order for 1,200 battery-powered Advent lantern kits. Barnabas received the order by courier on May 6. The form called for delivery no earlier than August 20 and no later than September 10, but a bold box above the signature line read: “Lydia Supply reserves the right to cancel this order at any time before August 12.” On May 8, Barnabas mailed Lydia Supply a signed reply stating, “We accept your order,” and Lydia Supply received it on May 9.', 'Preparing for a fall youth-ministry expo, Lydia Supply, a private retreat-supply reseller, sent Barnabas Crafts a purchase order for 1,200 battery-powered Advent lantern kits. Barnabas received the order by courier on May 6. The form called for delivery no earlier than August 20 and no later than September 10, but a bold box above the signature line read: “Lydia Supply reserves the right to cancel this order at any time before August 12.” On May 8, Barnabas mailed Lydia Supply a signed reply stating, “We accept your order,” and Lydia Supply received it on May 9.

As of May 10, which statement is accurate as to whether a contract was formed?', 'As of May 10, which statement is accurate as to whether a contract was formed?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ14406.md","source_question_id":"14406","variant_slug":"advent_lanterns","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"55020202","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium","deciding_phase":"CALL","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"Buyer orders goods but reserves an unrestricted right to cancel before the delivery window; seller accepts; before the cutoff, was a contract formed?","review_truth":"An unrestricted right for the buyer to cancel before performance makes the buyer''s promise illusory, so the seller''s acceptance does not create a binding sales contract.","dominant_trap":"B","anchor_card":{"card_id":"GK-CONTRACTS-ILLUSORY-CANCEL-01","title":"One-sided cancellation before performance","signal":"A purchase order says one side may cancel at any time before the performance window.","student_move":"Do not label it as an option first; ask whether that party made a real commitment.","tiny_rule":"Unrestricted cancellation before performance makes that side’s promise illusory.","trap":"Treating the one-sided escape hatch as a formed option contract.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONTRACTS-ILLUSORY-CANCEL-01","statement":"A buyer''s apparent promise is illusory when the buyer reserves an unrestricted right to cancel before it must perform; the seller''s acceptance does not convert that escape hatch into an option contract.","type":"rule","trigger":"A purchase order or acceptance set says one side may cancel at any time before the date performance is due.","authority":"UCC §1-103(b); Restatement (Second) of Contracts §77; UCC §§2-204, 2-206, and 2-205 for the formation and firm-offer contrast.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONTRACTS-ILLUSORY-CANCEL-01","statement":"When the answer set splits between ''no contract'' and ''contract formed with a label,'' decide formation before choosing the label. A familiar label cannot save a formed-contract answer if the cancellation power means one side has not made a real commitment.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"Two choices say no contract; two choices say contract formed but fight over who may cancel.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"Gold Key recall","target_skill":"illusory promise recognition","prompt":"A buyer orders goods and reserves the right to cancel at any time before delivery. The seller accepts before the cutoff. What is the formation problem?","answer":"The buyer''s promise is illusory because the buyer can cancel before it must perform."},{"drill_type":"answer-array read","target_skill":"formation before label","prompt":"Two choices say no contract and two choices say contract formed with a cancellation label. What question comes first?","answer":"Whether a contract formed at all."},{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"actor-language check","prompt":"The stem gives the cancellation right only to the buyer. A choice says either party may cancel. What is the break?","answer":"NOT_TRUE; the choice adds a cancellation right for the seller that the stem does not give."}]}'),
  ('9d2ab307-c2be-5ef8-bd2d-3dc7357049b0', '14409_sunken_ark_hall', 'CONTRACTS', 'Formation of Contracts', 'Assumption of risk for unknown subsurface conditions', NULL, 'Lydia owned a private, for-profit Christian family camp called Mustard Seed Adventures. For an agreed price of $8 million, Barnabas Construction, a company specializing in large themed projects and excavation, contracted to design and build on Lydia’s lakeside parcel a Noah’s-Ark-shaped indoor climbing hall. Because sinkholes were common in the area, a local safety ordinance required the hall’s foundation and underground utilities to be excavated at least 18 feet.

Barnabas began digging and, at 11 feet, encountered a continuous shelf of limestone. When the contract was made, neither Lydia nor Barnabas knew of the limestone, and neither had hired a geotechnical expert to test for subsurface rock. Barnabas accurately claimed that removing enough limestone to build as planned would cost an additional $1.2 million and would probably leave it with a net loss of $800,000 on the job. Barnabas refused to continue unless Lydia promised to pay an additional $1 million for the completed hall.', 'Lydia owned a private, for-profit Christian family camp called Mustard Seed Adventures. For an agreed price of $8 million, Barnabas Construction, a company specializing in large themed projects and excavation, contracted to design and build on Lydia’s lakeside parcel a Noah’s-Ark-shaped indoor climbing hall. Because sinkholes were common in the area, a local safety ordinance required the hall’s foundation and underground utilities to be excavated at least 18 feet.

Barnabas began digging and, at 11 feet, encountered a continuous shelf of limestone. When the contract was made, neither Lydia nor Barnabas knew of the limestone, and neither had hired a geotechnical expert to test for subsurface rock. Barnabas accurately claimed that removing enough limestone to build as planned would cost an additional $1.2 million and would probably leave it with a net loss of $800,000 on the job. Barnabas refused to continue unless Lydia promised to pay an additional $1 million for the completed hall.

If Lydia refuses and sues Barnabas for breach of contract, which of the following will the court probably decide?', 'If Lydia refuses and sues Barnabas for breach of contract, which of the following will the court probably decide?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ14409.md","source_question_id":"14409","variant_slug":"sunken_ark_hall","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"54030600","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"source_not_supplied","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"An experienced excavation contractor takes a fixed-price build job, must dig deep, skips subsurface testing, hits unknown rock, and demands more money. Does the owner prevail for breach?","review_truth":"A contractor who prices specialized excavation without testing known subsurface risk cannot escape a fixed-price contract merely because the job becomes more expensive.","dominant_trap":"A","anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-CONTRACTS-RISK-BUCKET-01","title":"Risk bucket beats surprise","signal":"Specialist contractor, fixed price, required excavation, no testing, and moderate cost increase.","student_move":"Ask who carried the risk before asking whether the surprise was mutual or expensive.","tiny_rule":"Unknown subsurface conditions usually stay with the specialist contractor when the contractor had the chance to test and priced the job anyway.","trap":"Mutual-mistake and impracticability answers that omit assumption of risk.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONTRACTS-SUNKEN-ARK-01","statement":"Risk beats surprise: a specialist who takes a fixed-price excavation job, knows digging is required, and skips available testing usually carries the unknown-site-condition risk.","type":"distinction","trigger":"Specialized contractor + fixed price + required excavation + no subsurface test + moderate added cost.","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts §§ 152, 154, 261.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONTRACTS-SUNKEN-ARK-01","statement":"Read the array as outcome plus reason. Do not stop at ''excused'' or ''owner prevails''; keep the answer whose because-clause puts the subsurface-risk bucket on the right party.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"Two answers excuse the contractor and two answers favor the owner.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"gold_key_recall","target_skill":"risk allocation","prompt":"A specialist contractor takes a fixed-price excavation job, skips testing, and hits unknown rock. Name the risk bucket.","answer":"The contractor usually bears the unknown subsurface-risk bucket."},{"drill_type":"answer_array","target_skill":"because-clause checking","prompt":"Two choices say the owner wins. One says actual ignorance; one says risk assumed. Which reason is responsive?","answer":"Risk assumed."},{"drill_type":"mold_id","target_skill":"half_truth","prompt":"Contract voidable for mutual mistake because both parties lacked the subsurface fact. What is missing?","answer":"The no-risk-assumed condition."}]}'),
  ('39285044-4be1-59f5-9fda-67e8cc799c2e', '14412_gospel_puzzle_invoice', 'CONTRACTS', 'Formation of Contracts', 'Preexisting duty', 'support direction for consideration', 'Stephen, who designs Bible-themed puzzle nights, completed a private Road to Emmaus escape-room script for Lydia. Lydia owed Stephen a fixed and undisputed $1,840 fee that was due on May 10. Lydia did not pay by that date. On May 25, Stephen told Lydia that, if Lydia promised to pay the $1,840 on or before November 15, Stephen would not sue to collect the fee. Lydia orally agreed. On June 10, Stephen sued Lydia to collect the fee that had become due on May 10. Lydia moved to dismiss Stephen’s complaint.', 'Stephen, who designs Bible-themed puzzle nights, completed a private Road to Emmaus escape-room script for Lydia. Lydia owed Stephen a fixed and undisputed $1,840 fee that was due on May 10. Lydia did not pay by that date. On May 25, Stephen told Lydia that, if Lydia promised to pay the $1,840 on or before November 15, Stephen would not sue to collect the fee. Lydia orally agreed. On June 10, Stephen sued Lydia to collect the fee that had become due on May 10. Lydia moved to dismiss Stephen’s complaint.

Should the court grant Lydia’s motion?', 'Should the court grant Lydia’s motion?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ14412.md","source_question_id":"14412","variant_slug":"gospel_puzzle_invoice","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"55020204","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"Lydia owed a fixed, undisputed private service fee that was already due, then promised only to pay that same fee later in exchange for Stephen’s promise not to sue; should Lydia’s motion to dismiss Stephen’s early collection suit be granted?","review_truth":"A promise to pay a fixed, undisputed, already-due debt later is not new consideration for a creditor’s promise not to sue.","dominant_trap":"C","anchor_card":{"card_id":"CONTRACTS-ANCHOR-PREEXISTING-DUTY-01","title":"Same debt later is not new consideration","signal":"fixed and undisputed debt already due","student_move":"Ask what new thing the debtor gave for the creditor’s no-suit promise.","trap":"bargain language hides the missing new exchange","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONTRACTS-GOSPEL-PUZZLE-INVOICE-01","statement":"A promise to pay a fixed, undisputed, already-due debt later is not new consideration for a creditor’s promise not to sue; the debtor must give something new.","type":"rule","trigger":"fixed and undisputed debt already due, followed by a promise to pay the same amount later for no-suit forbearance","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 73; Foakes v. Beer, (1884) 9 App Cas 605","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONTRACTS-GOSPEL-PUZZLE-INVOICE-01","statement":"When the choices split over consideration for opposite promises, identify the promise the motion must enforce and ask what new thing supports that promise.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"two answers say no while pointing to different promises, and two answers say yes by treating delay or bargain language as enough","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"anchor_recall","target_skill":"preexisting duty","prompt":"A fixed, undisputed debt was due last month. The debtor promises to pay the same amount six months later if the creditor promises not to sue. Is the creditor’s promise enforceable?","answer":"No. Same debt later is not new consideration."},{"drill_type":"answer_array","target_skill":"support-direction mapping","prompt":"Two choices say no, but one says no consideration supports the debtor’s promise and the other says no consideration supports the creditor’s promise. Which promise matters when the debtor seeks dismissal of the creditor’s suit?","answer":"The creditor’s no-suit promise matters."},{"drill_type":"trap_spotting","target_skill":"bargain-language trap","prompt":"A choice says yes because the debtor bargained for forbearance. What is missing?","answer":"New consideration from the debtor."}]}'),
  ('aaee18ef-bead-5bd6-b7e4-b90677aede60', '14418_jericho_lamp_credit', 'CONTRACTS', 'Formation of Contracts', 'Formation of Contracts', NULL, 'Paul, who runs a small shop making hand-carved Scripture stands in Oregon, wrote a signed note to Barnabas, a supplier of portable reading lamps and Bible-journaling kits in Savannah. The note said: Lydia is opening a private Advent pop-up bookstall at a Christian artists'' fair near Savannah. If you let her have the goods on credit, I will make good any loss up to $18,000 if she defaults. /Signed/ Paul. Lydia showed the note to Barnabas. Barnabas then sold and delivered $14,400 worth of lamps and journaling kits to Lydia on credit and promptly sent Paul notice of the sale.', 'Paul, who runs a small shop making hand-carved Scripture stands in Oregon, wrote a signed note to Barnabas, a supplier of portable reading lamps and Bible-journaling kits in Savannah. The note said: Lydia is opening a private Advent pop-up bookstall at a Christian artists'' fair near Savannah. If you let her have the goods on credit, I will make good any loss up to $18,000 if she defaults. /Signed/ Paul. Lydia showed the note to Barnabas. Barnabas then sold and delivered $14,400 worth of lamps and journaling kits to Lydia on credit and promptly sent Paul notice of the sale.

Which of the following is NOT an accurate statement concerning the arrangement between Paul and Barnabas?', 'Which of the following is NOT an accurate statement concerning the arrangement between Paul and Barnabas?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ14418.md","source_question_id":"14418","variant_slug":"jericho_lamp_credit","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"55020102","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"35_percent_correct","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A signed guarantor promises to cover a buyer''s credit default if a seller extends credit; the seller extends credit and promptly notifies the guarantor. Which statement about the arrangement is not accurate?","review_truth":"A unilateral offer accepted by performance may still require notice when the offeror otherwise lacks an adequate way to learn of the performance.","dominant_trap":"A","anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-CONTRACTS-NEGATIVE-NOTICE-01","title":"NOT accurate + performance notice","signal":"A negative call asks for the inaccurate statement in a unilateral credit-guaranty setup.","student_move":"Invert the call first, then test the notice phrase against the performance-acceptance anchor.","tiny_rule":"Performance can accept a unilateral offer, but notice can still matter when the offeror otherwise lacks a reliable way to learn that performance occurred.","trap":"Picking an accurate contract rule because it sounds familiar.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONTRACTS-NOTICE-PERFORMANCE-01","statement":"When a unilateral offer is accepted by performance, notice is usually not needed unless the offeree has reason to know the offeror otherwise lacks an adequate way to learn of the performance promptly and reliably.","type":"exception","trigger":"A signed promise to cover another person''s credit purchase, followed by the creditor extending credit and notifying the promisor.","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 54.","last_minute_review":true},{"id":"GK-CONTRACTS-UNILATERAL-CREDIT-01","statement":"A promise to pay if the creditor extends credit invites acceptance by the creditor''s performance, not by a return promise.","type":"distinction","trigger":"The promisor says, in substance, ''If you let this person have goods on credit, I will cover the loss if they default.''","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 54.","last_minute_review":true},{"id":"GK-CONTRACTS-SURETYSHIP-WRITING-01","statement":"A promise to answer for another person''s debt falls within the suretyship branch of the Statute of Frauds, so a signed writing matters.","type":"rule","trigger":"A promisor agrees to make good the loss if a buyer defaults on credit extended by a seller.","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts §§ 110, 112.","last_minute_review":true},{"id":"GK-CONTRACTS-CONSIDERATION-PERFORMANCE-01","statement":"Consideration can be the creditor''s requested performance even when the promisor receives no consideration from the third-party buyer.","type":"rule","trigger":"The creditor extends credit to the buyer because the guarantor asked the creditor to do so.","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 71.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONTRACTS-NEGATIVE-STEM-01","statement":"On a NOT-accurate call, invert the normal residual: three accurate statements are wrong answers, and the credited answer is the inaccurate statement.","type":"call_focus","trigger":"The call asks which statement is NOT accurate.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true},{"id":"SK-CONTRACTS-ANSWER-ARRAY-01","statement":"Read this array as 3 accurate anchors versus 1 notice overclaim; do not pick the best-sounding accurate rule.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"Three choices state familiar accurate contract doctrines and one says notice never matters.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"negative_stem_flip","target_skill":"call_focus","prompt":"A question asks which statement is NOT accurate. Three choices state accurate rules and one overstates notice. What is the first move?","answer":"Invert the call and hunt the inaccurate statement."},{"drill_type":"anchor_recall","target_skill":"performance_acceptance_notice","prompt":"A unilateral offer is accepted by performance, but the offeror otherwise has no adequate way to learn the performance happened. What must the offeree do?","answer":"Use reasonable diligence to notify, ensure the offeror learns within a reasonable time, or rely on an offer term excusing notice."},{"drill_type":"trap_sort","target_skill":"consideration_source","prompt":"A guarantor receives no consideration from the buyer, but the seller extends credit at the guarantor''s request. Is the guaranty unsupported by consideration?","answer":"No. The seller''s requested credit extension supplies consideration."}]}'),
  ('ba9e5b9f-b998-580d-bea3-2b2272b1fba1', '14424_christmas_pageant_lantern_bid', 'CONTRACTS', 'Formation of Contracts', 'Promissory Estoppel', 'Does the added fact supply lock-in reliance before acceptance, or only collateral cleanup after revocation?', 'Esther, a general event contractor, was preparing a sealed bid for Lydia''s private Christmas Pageant Pavilion. Paul, a lighting installer, sent Esther this signed email: ''Will install all overhead lantern trusses and speaker wiring for the pavilion, per Lydia''s specifications, for $32,000 if you accept within a reasonable time after the prime contract is awarded. /s/ Paul.'' Ruth, Stephen, and Barnabas sent similar sub-bids of $40,000, $42,000, and $44,000. Esther used Paul''s $32,000 figure in preparing and submitting her own sealed bid to Lydia. Before the bids were opened, Paul truthfully told Esther that his sub-bid had been based on an $8,000 computational error and was therefore revoked. Shortly afterward, Lydia awarded Esther the pavilion contract. Esther then contracted with Ruth to do the lighting work for $40,000. Esther sues Paul to recover $8,000.', 'Esther, a general event contractor, was preparing a sealed bid for Lydia''s private Christmas Pageant Pavilion. Paul, a lighting installer, sent Esther this signed email: ''Will install all overhead lantern trusses and speaker wiring for the pavilion, per Lydia''s specifications, for $32,000 if you accept within a reasonable time after the prime contract is awarded. /s/ Paul.'' Ruth, Stephen, and Barnabas sent similar sub-bids of $40,000, $42,000, and $44,000. Esther used Paul''s $32,000 figure in preparing and submitting her own sealed bid to Lydia. Before the bids were opened, Paul truthfully told Esther that his sub-bid had been based on an $8,000 computational error and was therefore revoked. Shortly afterward, Lydia awarded Esther the pavilion contract. Esther then contracted with Ruth to do the lighting work for $40,000. Esther sues Paul to recover $8,000.

Which of the following, if proved, would most strengthen Esther''s prospect of recovery?', 'Which of the following, if proved, would most strengthen Esther''s prospect of recovery?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ14424.md","source_question_id":"14424","variant_slug":"christmas_pageant_lantern_bid","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"55020300","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A subcontractor withdraws a mistaken low sub-bid after the general contractor used it in a sealed prime bid; which added fact best supports recovery for the higher substitute price?","review_truth":"A subcontractor bid is strongest for the general contractor when the bid foreseeably locks the contractor into its own prime bid before acceptance.","dominant_trap":"B","anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-CONTRACTS-SUB_BID_LOCKIN-01","title":"Sub-bid recovery needs lock-in reliance","signal":"Low sub-bid used in prime bid, then revoked before award.","student_move":"Find the fact that shows the general contractor was locked into the prime bid.","tiny_rule":"Lock-in beats mitigation."},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONTRACTS-SUB_BID_LOCKIN-01","statement":"A subcontractor bid can become temporarily irrevocable when the subcontractor should expect the general contractor to rely on it in a prime bid and the contractor is locked into that reliance before acceptance.","type":"rule","trigger":"A low subcontractor bid is used in a sealed prime bid, then the subcontractor revokes before the prime contract is awarded.","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts §87(2); Drennan v. Star Paving Co., 51 Cal. 2d 409 (1958).","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONTRACTS-SUB_BID_LOCKIN-01","statement":"On a ''most strengthens recovery'' array, first identify the missing legal link. A fact that locks the claimant into reliance beats facts about later mitigation, fault, or general fairness.","type":"call_focus","trigger":"The choices are all helpful-sounding facts, but only one explains why the revoked offer stayed open.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"Gold Key recall","target_skill":"subcontractor-bid reliance","prompt":"A subcontractor revokes a mistaken low bid after the general contractor used it in a sealed prime bid. Which fact matters most: mitigation, negligence, no bid shopping, or bid bond lock-in?","answer":"Bid bond lock-in."},{"drill_type":"wrong-element cut","target_skill":"separate liability link from damages cleanup","prompt":"A choice proves the contractor found the cheapest substitute after revocation. What element does that help, and what element is still missing?","answer":"It helps damages mitigation; it does not show temporary irrevocability."},{"drill_type":"answer-array navigation","target_skill":"most-strengthens call focus","prompt":"In a strengthening array, three choices sound helpful but only one supplies the missing legal link. What is the first question to ask?","answer":"Which fact makes the claim legally work?"}]}'),
  ('2ea548c8-ea40-521e-8f30-5935cedaf18b', '14430_psalm_room_option', 'CONTRACTS', 'Formation of Contracts', 'Formation of Contracts', 'Was Timothy''s power of acceptance terminated before Daniel received acceptance?', 'On September 3, Daniel and Timothy signed a writing in Daniel''s apartment above a Christian bookstore. In the writing, Daniel, in consideration of $75 to be paid by Timothy, offered Timothy the right to lease Daniel''s privately owned rooftop music studio, the "Psalm Room," for 12 months at a rent of $3,600 if Timothy acted within 21 days. The writing further provided, "This offer will operate as an option only if and when the $75 option payment is actually received." By September 14, Daniel had received no payment or other communication from Timothy. Daniel then leased the Psalm Room to Naomi for the same 12-month period at a rent of $4,100. On September 15, Daniel received a letter from Timothy enclosing a cashier''s check for $75 payable to Daniel and stating, "I am hereby exercising my option to lease the Psalm Room and am prepared to sign the lease whenever you are ready."', 'On September 3, Daniel and Timothy signed a writing in Daniel''s apartment above a Christian bookstore. In the writing, Daniel, in consideration of $75 to be paid by Timothy, offered Timothy the right to lease Daniel''s privately owned rooftop music studio, the "Psalm Room," for 12 months at a rent of $3,600 if Timothy acted within 21 days. The writing further provided, "This offer will operate as an option only if and when the $75 option payment is actually received." By September 14, Daniel had received no payment or other communication from Timothy. Daniel then leased the Psalm Room to Naomi for the same 12-month period at a rent of $4,100. On September 15, Daniel received a letter from Timothy enclosing a cashier''s check for $75 payable to Daniel and stating, "I am hereby exercising my option to lease the Psalm Room and am prepared to sign the lease whenever you are ready."

Which of the following, if proved, best supports Timothy''s suit against Daniel for breach of contract?', 'Which of the following, if proved, best supports Timothy''s suit against Daniel for breach of contract?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ14430.md","source_question_id":null,"variant_slug":"psalm_room_option","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"55020101","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"not_supplied","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"An unpaid option condition made the offer revocable, but the offeree sent payment and acceptance within the stated time. What fact best supports breach after the offeror dealt with someone else?","review_truth":"A revocable offer is not revoked until the offeree receives direct notice or reliable information of revocation before accepting.","dominant_trap":"A","anchor_card":{"card_id":"CON-FORM-OPTION-REVOCATION-01","title":"Revocable is not revoked","signal":"Unpaid option consideration plus later acceptance attempt","student_move":"Separate option status from offer status.","tiny_rule":"No option means the offer can be revoked; it does not mean the offer already ended.","trap":"Treating failed irrevocability as automatic revocation.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONTRACTS-PSALM-ROOM-01","statement":"A failed option is not the same as a revoked offer; an offeree may still accept a revocable offer before effective revocation.","type":"distinction","trigger":"The option payment was not made, but the offeree later sends payment and acceptance within the stated time.","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts §§42-43; Berryman v. Kmoch, 221 Kan. 304, 559 P.2d 790 (1977).","last_minute_review":true},{"id":"GK-CONTRACTS-PSALM-ROOM-02","statement":"When option status is expressly conditioned on actual payment, an unpaid recital does not make the option already binding.","type":"threshold","trigger":"The answer says a recital makes the deal binding despite a payment-before-option condition.","authority":"Berryman v. Kmoch, 221 Kan. 304, 559 P.2d 790 (1977).","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONTRACTS-PSALM-ROOM-01","statement":"Do not stop at ''no option.'' Ask the next call question: was the revocable offer actually revoked before acceptance?","type":"call_focus","trigger":"The stem says the option payment was unpaid but the offeree later sends payment and acceptance.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"anchor_flash","target_skill":"revocable_vs_revoked","prompt":"An option payment was not made. The offeror sold the asset to another person. The offeree accepted before learning of the sale. Is the offer automatically revoked?","answer":"No. The offer is revocable, but indirect revocation requires the offeree to receive reliable information before acceptance."},{"drill_type":"cut_drill","target_skill":"condition_override_trap","prompt":"A choice says an unpaid recital makes an option binding even though the writing says option status starts only on actual payment. Name the break.","answer":"NOT_TRUE, flat_misstatement."},{"drill_type":"issue_sense_drill","target_skill":"wrong_element","prompt":"A choice says the offeree obtained financing before accepting. The call asks whether the offer was revoked before acceptance. Name the break.","answer":"NOT_RESPONSIVE, wrong_element."}]}'),
  ('7020de60-0604-5894-85fb-2f5327c14d02', '14434_crossed_handbells', 'CONTRACTS', 'Formation of Contracts', 'Mutual Assent', NULL, 'After a few rounds of bargaining, Daniel, who operated a shop selling premium handbell sets, knew that Hannah wanted a new set for her nephew''s hymn-themed wedding procession. On March 10, Daniel sent Hannah a signed, dated note saying, ''If by Friday we land on the same handbell set and price, are we agreed?'' Hannah wrote ''Yes,'' signed below it, and returned the note on March 12. On March 14, Hannah mailed Daniel a signed letter offering to buy ''one new five-octave handbell set, with all carrying cases and accessories, for $24,600 cash on delivery not later than April 15.'' By coincidence, Daniel mailed Hannah a signed letter on March 14 offering to sell her ''one new five-octave handbell set, with all carrying cases and accessories, for $24,600 cash on delivery not later than April 15.'' The letters crossed in the mail and were received and read by both Hannah and Daniel on March 15. In an action brought by Hannah, if Daniel denies that the parties had a binding contract on March 16, which argument most persuasively supports Hannah''s position?', 'After a few rounds of bargaining, Daniel, who operated a shop selling premium handbell sets, knew that Hannah wanted a new set for her nephew''s hymn-themed wedding procession. On March 10, Daniel sent Hannah a signed, dated note saying, ''If by Friday we land on the same handbell set and price, are we agreed?'' Hannah wrote ''Yes,'' signed below it, and returned the note on March 12. On March 14, Hannah mailed Daniel a signed letter offering to buy ''one new five-octave handbell set, with all carrying cases and accessories, for $24,600 cash on delivery not later than April 15.'' By coincidence, Daniel mailed Hannah a signed letter on March 14 offering to sell her ''one new five-octave handbell set, with all carrying cases and accessories, for $24,600 cash on delivery not later than April 15.'' The letters crossed in the mail and were received and read by both Hannah and Daniel on March 15. In an action brought by Hannah, if Daniel denies that the parties had a binding contract on March 16, which argument most persuasively supports Hannah''s position?

which argument most persuasively supports Hannah''s position', 'which argument most persuasively supports Hannah''s position', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ14434.md","source_question_id":null,"variant_slug":"crossed_handbells","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"55020100","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium","deciding_phase":"CALL","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"Buyer and seller of goods exchange signed, matching writings after agreeing that matching terms within a short window would make a deal; seller later denies a contract. Which argument best supports the buyer?","review_truth":"For a sale of goods, matching manifestations can show agreement even when no one can identify a clean offer-and-acceptance moment.","dominant_trap":"A","anchor_card":{"card_id":"CON-REM-CROSSED-FORMATION-01","title":"Crossed writings: preserve the uncertainty","signal":"Two matching writings cross and the call asks whether a contract already exists.","student_move":"Do not force a clean acceptance story; ask whether the answer preserves the uncertain formation moment.","tiny_rule":"Article 2 can recognize agreement even when no exact formation moment is identified.","trap":"Treating identical cross-offers as an offer followed by acceptance.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONTRACTS-CROSSED-HANDBELLS-01","statement":"Article 2 can find a sale-of-goods contract from writings that show agreement even when no single offer-and-acceptance moment can be pinned down; do not relabel identical cross-offers as a responsive acceptance.","type":"distinction","trigger":"Two signed writings with identical goods, price, and delivery terms cross after a prior note asking whether matching terms will make a deal.","authority":"UCC § 2-204; Restatement (Second) of Contracts §§ 22-23.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONTRACTS-CROSSED-HANDBELLS-01","statement":"When two choices both like the matching letters, pick the one that preserves the stem''s uncertainty instead of pretending there was a clean offer-then-acceptance sequence.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"Matching signed letters plus a call asking for the best support for a contract despite no clean formation moment.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"gold_key_recall","target_skill":"Article 2 flexible formation","prompt":"Two signed sale-of-goods writings match, but neither clearly accepts the other. Which answer shape is safest?","answer":"The answer that preserves agreement despite an uncertain formation moment."},{"drill_type":"trap_sort","target_skill":"cross-offer trap","prompt":"A choice says identical cross-offers equal offer plus acceptance. Label the mold.","answer":"flat_misstatement, anchor_assisted."},{"drill_type":"issue_sense","target_skill":"mirror-image bait","prompt":"A sale-of-goods answer invokes no mirror-image requirement, but both writings have identical terms. What filter breaks?","answer":"NOT_RESPONSIVE; the issue is not mismatched acceptance terms."}]}'),
  ('b5ef1f13-54ca-5d89-9263-c8de20f125ef', '14437_lydias_verse_card_boxes', 'CONTRACTS', 'Formation of Contracts', 'Firm offer / acceptance after three-month cap', 'revocable-but-open versus revoked-or-lapsed', 'On November 29, Lydia, who ran a private Bible-memory tutoring program, received from Barnabas, a merchant who sold devotional craft supplies, an illustrated price sheet and a signed letter. The signed letter stated:

> “For every item listed on the enclosed sheet that you request during the next calendar year, we will sell you as many as you order. We promise that this offer and these listed prices will stay firm for that year.”

Barnabas and Lydia exchanged no further messages until March 29, four months later, when Barnabas received Lydia’s emailed purchase order for “300 olive-wood verse-card boxes, item #40 on your sheet.”', 'On November 29, Lydia, who ran a private Bible-memory tutoring program, received from Barnabas, a merchant who sold devotional craft supplies, an illustrated price sheet and a signed letter. The signed letter stated:

> “For every item listed on the enclosed sheet that you request during the next calendar year, we will sell you as many as you order. We promise that this offer and these listed prices will stay firm for that year.”

Barnabas and Lydia exchanged no further messages until March 29, four months later, when Barnabas received Lydia’s emailed purchase order for “300 olive-wood verse-card boxes, item #40 on your sheet.”

Did Lydia’s March 29 email constitute an effective acceptance of Barnabas’s offer at the prices listed on the sheet?', 'Did Lydia’s March 29 email constitute an effective acceptance of Barnabas’s offer at the prices listed on the sheet?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ14437.md","source_question_id":"14437","variant_slug":"lydias_verse_card_boxes","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"55020101","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium","deciding_phase":"CLASH","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":"Barnabas had not revoked before receiving Lydia’s order, and the order came within the stated next-calendar-year window.","distilled_core_question":"A merchant promised in a signed writing to keep goods prices firm for one year, no consideration was paid, and the buyer ordered after four months before any revocation. Was the order an effective acceptance at the listed prices?","review_truth":"A merchant’s three-month firm-offer cap ends only irrevocability; the offer can still be accepted before revocation or lapse.","dominant_trap":"D","anchor_card":{"card_id":"CON-FORM-FIRM-CAP-REVOCABLE-NOT-REVOKED","title":"Revocable is not revoked","signal":"Merchant signed assurance longer than three months; acceptance after month three; no revocation fact.","student_move":"Ask whether the seller actually revoked or whether the answer only says the offer became revocable.","tiny_rule":"The firm-offer cap ends irrevocability only.","trap":"Treating the cap as lapse.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONTRACTS-FIRM-CAP-01","statement":"UCC firm-offer cap controls irrevocability, not the life of the offer; after three months without consideration, the offer is merely revocable unless revoked or lapsed.","type":"rule","trigger":"A merchant’s signed price assurance lasts longer than three months and the buyer accepts after month three before any revocation.","authority":"UCC § 2-205; UCC § 2-206.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONTRACTS-FIRM-CAP-01","statement":"Separate two statuses before choosing: irrevocable and open. A choice proving the offer is no longer firm does not prove it was revoked or lapsed.","type":"clash","trigger":"One answer says the offer was accepted before revocation; another says no because irrevocability lasted only three months.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"clash","target_skill":"Separate offer status from irrevocability status.","prompt":"A merchant promises firm prices for one year; a buyer accepts after four months; no revocation occurs. Pick between ''no because three months'' and ''yes because no revocation.''","answer":"Yes because no revocation; the cap ends irrevocability only."},{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"Cut the option-contract overrange.","prompt":"No consideration was paid, but the answer says the signed merchant letter created a one-year option contract.","answer":"Cut it as overrange; the unpaid firm offer is not irrevocable beyond three months."},{"drill_type":"call_focus","target_skill":"Lock the call before choosing.","prompt":"The call asks whether the order was an effective acceptance, not whether the offer was still irrevocable.","answer":"Choose the answer that addresses revocation/lapse before acceptance."}]}'),
  ('dc59c7d1-b50f-5716-b9db-28efafbec852', '14438_nativity_ornament_kits', 'CONTRACTS', 'Formation of Contracts', 'Illusory promises', 'temporary cancellation right versus binding promise after the cutoff', 'For a fall Bible-story festival, Lydia, who operated a private Christian gift shop, sent Martha, an artisan who made olive-wood nativity ornament kits, a signed order for 720 kits. Martha received the order on March 3; Lydia had sent it by courier on March 1. The order required shipment no earlier than May 12 and no later than June 12. Printed in bold type across the front of the order form was this statement: “Lydia reserves the right to cancel this order at any time before May 1.” Martha’s mailed acknowledgment, stating “I accept your order,” reached Lydia on March 9. Lydia did not cancel the order. Martha shipped the kits to Lydia on May 12. The kits conformed to the order in all respects. When they arrived on June 6, Lydia refused to accept them.', 'For a fall Bible-story festival, Lydia, who operated a private Christian gift shop, sent Martha, an artisan who made olive-wood nativity ornament kits, a signed order for 720 kits. Martha received the order on March 3; Lydia had sent it by courier on March 1. The order required shipment no earlier than May 12 and no later than June 12. Printed in bold type across the front of the order form was this statement: “Lydia reserves the right to cancel this order at any time before May 1.” Martha’s mailed acknowledgment, stating “I accept your order,” reached Lydia on March 9. Lydia did not cancel the order. Martha shipped the kits to Lydia on May 12. The kits conformed to the order in all respects. When they arrived on June 6, Lydia refused to accept them.

Which of the following is an accurate statement as of June 6 after Lydia rejected the kits?', 'Which of the following is an accurate statement as of June 6 after Lydia rejected the kits?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ14438.md","source_question_id":"14438","variant_slug":"nativity_ornament_kits","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"55020202","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":"Lydia did not cancel before May 1 and Martha shipped conforming kits after that date.","distilled_core_question":"A buyer orders goods while reserving a right to cancel before a stated date, does not cancel by that date, the seller ships conforming goods after the date, and the buyer rejects them. What is the buyer''s status after rejection?","review_truth":"A promise that is freely cancelable for a stated period can become binding when the cancellation period passes without cancellation.","dominant_trap":"B","anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-CONTRACTS-ILLUSORY-CUTOFF-01","title":"Cancellation-window cutoff","signal":"The buyer can cancel only before a named date and then does not cancel.","student_move":"Mark the cancellation deadline, then ask what legal status exists after that date.","tiny_rule":"A freely cancelable promise may stop being illusory when the cancellation window closes unused.","trap":"Calling the missed deadline an option or a condition subsequent.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONTRACTS-ILLUSORY-CUTOFF-01","statement":"A promise that can be canceled freely until a stated deadline is weak during the cancellation window, but the missed deadline can leave a binding promise once the cancellation right expires and the other side has accepted.","type":"rule","trigger":"buyer reserves a right to cancel only before a named date and then lets that date pass","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 77; UCC §§ 2-204 and 2-206","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONTRACTS-ILLUSORY-CUTOFF-01","statement":"On an as-of-date call, sort the choices by time. A choice that uses an expired cancellation right to describe a later shipment is a timing trap.","type":"trap_spotting","trigger":"a cancellation deadline appears before the shipment and rejection dates","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"date sorting","prompt":"Buyer may cancel before June 1, does not cancel, seller ships June 10, buyer rejects June 20. Is a choice relying on the cancellation right to call June 10 shipment only an offer safe?","answer":"No. It uses an expired cancellation right."},{"drill_type":"anchor","target_skill":"illusory-promise cutoff","prompt":"What happens to a freely cancelable buyer promise after the named cancellation deadline passes unused?","answer":"It can become a binding promise if the seller has accepted."},{"drill_type":"wrong-frame","target_skill":"condition label resistance","prompt":"A choice says a missed cancellation deadline is a condition subsequent whose failure created the contract. What is the trap?","answer":"The choice imports the wrong label and reverses the consequence."}]}'),
  ('c729f709-a3e6-56af-8451-883a52725528', '14447_jericho_trumpet_deposit', 'CONTRACTS', 'Formation of Contracts', 'Promissory Estoppel', NULL, 'Timothy, a cheerful but chronically overextended collector of Christian festival props, received an audio message from Lydia, his careful aunt, who kept a color-coded prayer-and-budget journal:

> “On Reformation Day next month, I will send you $7,200 to help you get your finances steady. Spend it wisely; this is the last rescue I can make.”

Before that day, Timothy signed a contract with a festival vendor to rent a $58,000 portable “Jericho trumpet” sound-and-light rig and to make a $7,200 deposit the morning after Reformation Day. After Lydia learned of the rental contract, she repudiated her promise.

If Timothy sues Lydia for $7,200, which of the following is Lydia’s best defense?', 'Timothy, a cheerful but chronically overextended collector of Christian festival props, received an audio message from Lydia, his careful aunt, who kept a color-coded prayer-and-budget journal:

> “On Reformation Day next month, I will send you $7,200 to help you get your finances steady. Spend it wisely; this is the last rescue I can make.”

Before that day, Timothy signed a contract with a festival vendor to rent a $58,000 portable “Jericho trumpet” sound-and-light rig and to make a $7,200 deposit the morning after Reformation Day. After Lydia learned of the rental contract, she repudiated her promise.

If Timothy sues Lydia for $7,200, which of the following is Lydia’s best defense?

Which of the following is Lydia''s best defense?', 'Which of the following is Lydia''s best defense?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ14447.md","source_question_id":"14447","variant_slug":"jericho_trumpet_deposit","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"55020300","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"not_supplied","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A donor promised a future cash gift for financial stability, the promisee relied by taking on an expensive private rental, and the donor repudiated after learning of that reliance; what is the donor''s best defense?","review_truth":"A relied-on gift promise is enforceable only when enforcement is necessary to avoid injustice.","dominant_trap":"C","anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-CONTRACTS-PE-GIFT-GATES-01","title":"Promissory Estoppel Gate List","student_move":"When reliance appears after a future gift promise, cut broad no-gift and invented-extra-gate answers.","tiny_rule":"Gift promise plus reliance can be enforceable only if enforcement is needed to avoid injustice.","dominant_trap":"C"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONTRACTS-PROMISSORY-ESTOPPEL-GIFT-01","statement":"A future gift promise can be enforceable by promissory estoppel only when the promisor should expect reliance, reliance occurs, and enforcement is needed to avoid injustice; the rule does not require equivalent loss or a benefit to the promisor.","type":"rule","trigger":"a gratuitous future promise followed by reliance and a defense asking why enforcement should fail","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 90(1)","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONTRACTS-JERICHO-TRUMPET-01","statement":"When every choice says future gift plus reliance unless something else, read the answers as a gate list. Keep the choice that names the real gate, then use the stem facts to see whether that gate helps the defending party.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"one choice deletes the reliance exception, two choices invent extra conditions, and one choice names the injustice gate","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"gate_list_cut","target_skill":"anchor_assisted CUT","prompt":"A promisee relies on a future gift by taking on an expensive obligation. Which gate matters: no gift enforcement, equivalent loss, benefit to promisor, or injustice?","answer":"Injustice."},{"drill_type":"dominant_trap_recovery","target_skill":"overclaim detection","prompt":"Why is ''a future gift promise is not enforceable'' too broad when the stem includes reliance?","answer":"Promissory estoppel can enforce some relied-on gift promises."},{"drill_type":"fabricated_rule_scan","target_skill":"extra-condition rejection","prompt":"Name two conditions not required for promissory estoppel in this item.","answer":"Substantial equivalence between promise and reliance loss; economic benefit to the promisor."},{"drill_type":"fact_application","target_skill":"injustice gate application","prompt":"Why does the expensive Jericho-trumpet rental help Lydia rather than Timothy?","answer":"It is reliance, but it undercuts the claim that enforcement is needed to avoid injustice because it conflicts with the financial-stability purpose."}]}'),
  ('9bb315ed-d2cd-5ef8-954e-0d2674b67b9b', '14451_psalm_trail_quitclaim', 'CONTRACTS', 'Formation of Contracts', 'Sufficiency of consideration', 'consideration from a doubtful quitclaim versus later worthlessness', 'While designing a private Scripture-calligraphy retreat on her ranch, Lydia already owned the main meadow but wanted a recorded trail easement across a neighboring canyon so guests could reach the Psalm Path overlook. The trail claim was being litigated between Barnabas and Naomi under an old survey. Barnabas''s lawyer had told him his trail claim was doubtful. Lydia knew only that the dispute was unresolved. Lydia signed a writing promising to pay Barnabas $9,000 in six semiannual installments for a quitclaim deed of whatever interest Barnabas had in the trail easement. Barnabas immediately executed and delivered the quitclaim deed, and Lydia paid the first $1,500 installment. A month later, a court entered a final judgment for Naomi, ruling that Barnabas had no interest in the trail easement. When the second installment came due, Lydia refused to pay. In Barnabas''s action against Lydia for breach of contract, Barnabas will probably', 'While designing a private Scripture-calligraphy retreat on her ranch, Lydia already owned the main meadow but wanted a recorded trail easement across a neighboring canyon so guests could reach the Psalm Path overlook. The trail claim was being litigated between Barnabas and Naomi under an old survey. Barnabas''s lawyer had told him his trail claim was doubtful. Lydia knew only that the dispute was unresolved. Lydia signed a writing promising to pay Barnabas $9,000 in six semiannual installments for a quitclaim deed of whatever interest Barnabas had in the trail easement. Barnabas immediately executed and delivered the quitclaim deed, and Lydia paid the first $1,500 installment. A month later, a court entered a final judgment for Naomi, ruling that Barnabas had no interest in the trail easement. When the second installment came due, Lydia refused to pay. In Barnabas''s action against Lydia for breach of contract, Barnabas will probably

In Barnabas''s action against Lydia for breach of contract, Barnabas will probably', 'In Barnabas''s action against Lydia for breach of contract, Barnabas will probably', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ14451.md","source_question_id":null,"variant_slug":"psalm_trail_quitclaim","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"55020205","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium","deciding_phase":"CALL","confidence":"PURE_ANCHOR","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A buyer promised installments for a quitclaim of a disputed trail claim, received the deed, then stopped paying after a court said the claimant owned nothing.","review_truth":"A bargained-for quitclaim of a doubtful claim can be consideration even when the claim later proves worthless.","dominant_trap":"B","anchor_card":{"card_id":"CON-REMEDY-PSALM-QUITCLAIM","focus":"doubtful-claim consideration","drill":"When a later ruling makes a claim worthless, ask what was bargained for at execution."},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONTRACTS-PSALM-01","statement":"A bargained-for quitclaim, release, or written surrender of a doubtful claim can supply consideration even if the claim later proves worthless.","type":"rule","trigger":"the promisor asked for a no-warranty deed or release of whatever interest the claimant had","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts §§ 71, 74, 79","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONTRACTS-PSALM-01","statement":"In a two-win/two-lose array, keep the because clause attached to the result; a correct outcome with an unnecessary substitute theory is not the residual.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"one win answer uses the bargain itself and another win answer reaches for estoppel","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"anchor recall","target_skill":"consideration sufficiency","prompt":"A buyer pays for a quitclaim of a disputed claim; the claim later fails. Is the quitclaim capable of being consideration?","answer":"Yes, if it was bargained for."},{"drill_type":"answer-array scan","target_skill":"correct-result/wrong-reason cut","prompt":"Two choices say win; one says consideration, one says estoppel. The facts show a bargained-for deed. Which survives?","answer":"The consideration answer."},{"drill_type":"dominant-trap recovery","target_skill":"after-the-fact valuation","prompt":"A later judgment makes a claim worthless. Does that automatically erase consideration for a prior release?","answer":"No."}]}'),
  ('b91540b2-46dd-5f87-ac76-47feb4f30880', '14457_advent_kits_requirements', 'CONTRACTS', 'Performance, Breach, and Discharge', 'Requirements contracts', 'formed requirements contract versus formation-stage avoidance', 'Bethany Craft Kits, a private company owned by Lydia, assembles Scripture-memory Advent craft kits. In a signed writing, Bethany agreed to buy from Martha’s Paper House all of the cardstock, ribbon, and sticker sheets that Bethany would require for the next twelve months. Martha’s Paper House agreed to fill those requirements and to sell the items at 12% off the prices posted in its online catalog, but it reserved the right to change the catalog prices during the year.

After the parties had ordered and delivered under the agreement for four months, Martha’s Paper House notified Bethany that future orders would be charged at full catalog prices with no 12% discount.', 'Bethany Craft Kits, a private company owned by Lydia, assembles Scripture-memory Advent craft kits. In a signed writing, Bethany agreed to buy from Martha’s Paper House all of the cardstock, ribbon, and sticker sheets that Bethany would require for the next twelve months. Martha’s Paper House agreed to fill those requirements and to sell the items at 12% off the prices posted in its online catalog, but it reserved the right to change the catalog prices during the year.

After the parties had ordered and delivered under the agreement for four months, Martha’s Paper House notified Bethany that future orders would be charged at full catalog prices with no 12% discount.

Does Bethany have a viable claim against Martha’s Paper House for breach of contract?', 'Does Bethany have a viable claim against Martha’s Paper House for breach of contract?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ14457.md","source_question_id":"14457","variant_slug":"advent_kits_requirements","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"55020203","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A buyer agrees in writing to buy all of its requirements for goods from a seller for a year; the seller promises a fixed discount from catalog prices but later withdraws the discount. Does the buyer have a breach claim?","review_truth":"A written requirements contract for goods is enforceable without a stated minimum quantity, and a seller that reserved only the right to raise catalog prices must still honor a promised discount.","dominant_trap":"A","anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-CONTRACTS-REQ-01","title":"Requirements contracts are not blanks","signal":"all needs, all requirements, all output, exclusive supply","student_move":"Treat all-requirements language as the quantity term, then ask whether the disputed act breaks an actual contract term.","tiny_rule":"All requirements is enough; good faith polices quantity.","trap":"minimum-quantity myth and offer-revocation wrong frame","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONTRACTS-ADVENT-KITS-01","statement":"An agreement to buy all of the buyer''s requirements for goods is a valid quantity term. Good faith supplies the quantity guardrail, so a separate minimum quantity is not required.","type":"rule","trigger":"a signed goods contract using ''all requirements'' or ''all needs'' language","authority":"UCC § 2-306(1)","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONTRACTS-ADVENT-KITS-01","statement":"Read the array by time slice: once the stem gives a signed agreement and months of performance, offer-revocation answers are aimed at the wrong stage. The live question is breach of a formed requirements contract.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"answers talk about revocation or part-performance after the stem says the parties had already agreed and performed","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"gold_key_recall","target_skill":"requirements-contract quantity term","prompt":"A buyer promises in writing to buy all goods it requires from a seller for a year. Is the absence of a minimum number fatal?","answer":"No. All requirements is a valid quantity term, guarded by good faith."},{"drill_type":"wrong_frame_cut","target_skill":"formation-stage revocation versus formed-contract breach","prompt":"A seller withdraws a discount after months of performance under a signed requirements contract. Is revocation the right frame?","answer":"No. The live frame is breach of a formed contract."},{"drill_type":"answer_array","target_skill":"yes/no reason sorting","prompt":"Two yes answers and two no answers appear; one yes uses part performance, one yes uses requirements language, one no uses revocation, one no uses illusory promise. Which reason should survive?","answer":"The requirements-language yes answer."}]}'),
  ('92564521-5f6d-5da5-8d70-d576a16423df', '14458_verse_wristbands', 'CONTRACTS', 'Performance, Breach, and Discharge', 'Requirement and output contracts / exclusive dealing best efforts', 'specific exclusive-dealing best-efforts duty versus generic risk-allocation reason', 'Under a written agreement, Lydia, an artisan who made glow-in-the-dark Scripture-memory wristbands, promised to sell her entire output of the wristbands at a specified unit price to Daniel, who operated a private Christian gift booth at youth festivals, for one year. Daniel promised that his booth would not sell any other supplier''s Scripture-memory wristbands. Before Lydia tendered any wristbands, Daniel learned that youth groups had abruptly shifted from wristbands to phone lock-screen cards, sharply reducing customer demand for the wristbands. Daniel renounced the agreement, and Lydia sues for breach of contract.', 'Under a written agreement, Lydia, an artisan who made glow-in-the-dark Scripture-memory wristbands, promised to sell her entire output of the wristbands at a specified unit price to Daniel, who operated a private Christian gift booth at youth festivals, for one year. Daniel promised that his booth would not sell any other supplier''s Scripture-memory wristbands. Before Lydia tendered any wristbands, Daniel learned that youth groups had abruptly shifted from wristbands to phone lock-screen cards, sharply reducing customer demand for the wristbands. Daniel renounced the agreement, and Lydia sues for breach of contract.

Which of the following will the court probably decide?', 'Which of the following will the court probably decide?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ14458.md","source_question_id":"14458","variant_slug":"verse_wristbands","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"55020203","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"Seller promised all output of Bible-verse wristbands to an exclusive retailer for one year; the retailer quit before delivery because demand dropped. Who wins?","review_truth":"An exclusive-dealing contract for goods supplies a best-efforts duty to promote the goods, so the exclusive retailer cannot escape merely because demand drops.","dominant_trap":"B","anchor_card":{"card_id":"CON-GK-OUTPUT-EXCLUSIVE-BEST-EFFORTS","title":"Exclusive output deal supplies best efforts","signal":"Seller output plus buyer exclusivity in goods.","student_move":"Do not demand an express buy-all sentence; ask whether exclusivity supplies the action promise.","tiny_rule":"Exclusive dealing imposes best efforts to supply and promote unless otherwise agreed.","trap":"Same-result market-risk answer and no-mutuality answer.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONTRACTS-VERSE-WRISTBANDS-01","statement":"In an exclusive-dealing contract for goods, the law supplies the missing action promise: the seller must use best efforts to supply the goods and the buyer must use best efforts to promote their sale.","type":"rule","trigger":"The seller commits its output and the buyer promises to carry no competing supplier''s goods.","authority":"UCC § 2-306(2).","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONTRACTS-VERSE-WRISTBANDS-01","statement":"When two choices give the seller the win, choose the one whose reason uses the special fact in the stem, not the generic commercial-risk sentence.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"Two plaintiff-win answers, one tied to exclusivity and one tied only to demand or price swings.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"anchor_recall","target_skill":"Recall UCC output/exclusive-dealing best efforts","prompt":"A seller commits all output of devotional stickers to one exclusive retailer; the retailer stops because demand drops. Does lack of a fixed quantity defeat the contract?","answer":"No. Output and exclusive-dealing terms can be enforceable, and exclusivity supplies a best-efforts duty."},{"drill_type":"same_result_reason_check","target_skill":"Separate credited reason from same-result bait","prompt":"Two answers say the seller wins. One says best efforts from exclusivity; one says both parties assumed market risk. Which answer is responsive?","answer":"The best-efforts answer, because it uses the special fact that created the duty."},{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"Cut fabricated express-promise requirements","prompt":"In a goods output contract with exclusivity, an answer says the buyer wins because there was no express promise to buy. Name the defect.","answer":"Fabricated rule; the law can imply best efforts from exclusivity."}]}'),
  ('2149a1fb-84c7-52da-a748-493a94a84f64', '14460_fish_loaves_mosaic', 'CONTRACTS', 'Performance, Breach, and Discharge', 'Excuse of condition / waiver', 'waiver of condition vs alternative remedy labels', 'Before Mary laid a tile, Timothy, Peter’s choir friend and an avid collector of Christian art, said in front of both Peter and Mary that he would not approve Mary’s sketch. That rejection mattered because Peter and Mary had already signed a detailed writing: Mary would cover the side of Peter’s privately owned vintage camper with a fish-and-loaves mosaic according to Mary’s attached sketch, and Peter would pay $18,000 at completion. Peter’s sister Ruth was not a party to the agreement and owned no interest in the camper. At Peter’s insistence, the writing said that neither party would be bound unless Timothy approved Mary’s sketch. After Timothy rejected the sketch, Peter nevertheless told Mary to go ahead with the mosaic. Mary reluctantly began and completed 45% of the work. Peter then repudiated any duty to pay the contract price or any part of it.

If Mary now sues Peter for damages for breach of contract, which concept best supports Mary’s claim?', 'Before Mary laid a tile, Timothy, Peter’s choir friend and an avid collector of Christian art, said in front of both Peter and Mary that he would not approve Mary’s sketch. That rejection mattered because Peter and Mary had already signed a detailed writing: Mary would cover the side of Peter’s privately owned vintage camper with a fish-and-loaves mosaic according to Mary’s attached sketch, and Peter would pay $18,000 at completion. Peter’s sister Ruth was not a party to the agreement and owned no interest in the camper. At Peter’s insistence, the writing said that neither party would be bound unless Timothy approved Mary’s sketch. After Timothy rejected the sketch, Peter nevertheless told Mary to go ahead with the mosaic. Mary reluctantly began and completed 45% of the work. Peter then repudiated any duty to pay the contract price or any part of it.

If Mary now sues Peter for damages for breach of contract, which concept best supports Mary’s claim?

If Mary now sues Peter for damages for breach of contract, which concept best supports Mary''s claim?', 'If Mary now sues Peter for damages for breach of contract, which concept best supports Mary''s claim?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ14460.md","source_question_id":"14460","variant_slug":"fish_loaves_mosaic","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"56050604","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"not_supplied","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"Approval condition fails; the protected party orders performance anyway; the other party performs 45%; the protected party refuses to pay. Which concept supports the breach claim?","review_truth":"A party who orders performance after an express condition fails cannot revoke that waiver after the other party materially relies.","dominant_trap":"C","anchor_card":{"card_id":"CONTRACTS-CALL-14460","title":"Contract lane before backup remedy lane","signal":"The call says breach of contract and the stem gives a signed writing.","student_move":"Cut promissory estoppel and unjust enrichment unless the contract lane is blocked.","tiny_rule":"Use waiver when the protected party gives up a failed condition and the other side relies.","trap":"Choosing the fairest-sounding payment theory instead of the contract concept.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONTRACTS-WAIVER-CONDITION-01","statement":"A party protected by an express condition can waive that condition by ordering performance after the condition fails; after the other party materially relies, the waiver cannot be revoked.","type":"rule","trigger":"Approval condition fails; protected party says proceed; performing party completes a meaningful part of the work.","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts §§ 84, 246; Clark v. West, 193 N.Y. 349, 86 N.E. 1 (1908).","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONTRACTS-WAIVER-CONDITION-01","statement":"Lock the call before the labels: the question asks for the concept supporting a breach-of-contract claim, so backup recovery labels move behind the contract-lane answer unless the contract lane is blocked.","type":"call_focus","trigger":"A signed writing plus a call asking for damages for breach of contract.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"call_focus","prompt":"A signed contract is conditioned on third-person approval; approval fails; the buyer says proceed; the seller performs; the buyer refuses to pay. Cut promissory estoppel or keep it?","answer":"Cut it as not responsive when the call asks for the breach-of-contract concept."},{"drill_type":"anchor","target_skill":"waiver_of_condition","prompt":"A protected party orders performance after a failed condition and the other side materially relies. What anchor controls?","answer":"Irrevocable waiver of condition."},{"drill_type":"trap_spotting","target_skill":"backup_remedy_detection","prompt":"The facts show a valid writing. A choice says unjust enrichment. What is the danger?","answer":"It is a backup recovery label and likely does not answer a breach-of-contract call."}]}'),
  ('f9eeacd8-37b6-5b4a-a64b-dc9a571ddb2f', '14462_mustard_seed_carts', 'CONTRACTS', 'Performance, Breach, and Discharge', 'Conditions / waiver of condition', 'condition occurred vs condition waived', 'Lydia Learning LLC, a private company that runs Bible-verse calligraphy camps, agreed in writing to buy from Ruth twelve hand-painted mobile coffee carts branded for ''Mustard Seed Morning'' events at a fixed price per cart. Lydia Learning was a subsidiary of Emmaus Market Group. Just before the written contract was signed, Martha, Lydia Learning''s purchasing director, said to Ruth, ''Of course, our commitment to buy is subject to approval from Emmaus Market Group.'' Ruth replied, ''Fine. No problem.'' Ruth later refused to deliver the carts, arguing that Lydia Learning had never asked Emmaus Market Group to approve the contract. That was true. Barnabas, the chief executive officer of Emmaus Market Group, is prepared to testify that Emmaus Market Group would have routinely approved the contract if asked. Lydia Learning can also prove that it sold a delivery van to raise money for the coffee-cart purchase, although it admits that it had not anticipated any need to sell the van when it entered the contract.', 'Lydia Learning LLC, a private company that runs Bible-verse calligraphy camps, agreed in writing to buy from Ruth twelve hand-painted mobile coffee carts branded for ''Mustard Seed Morning'' events at a fixed price per cart. Lydia Learning was a subsidiary of Emmaus Market Group. Just before the written contract was signed, Martha, Lydia Learning''s purchasing director, said to Ruth, ''Of course, our commitment to buy is subject to approval from Emmaus Market Group.'' Ruth replied, ''Fine. No problem.'' Ruth later refused to deliver the carts, arguing that Lydia Learning had never asked Emmaus Market Group to approve the contract. That was true. Barnabas, the chief executive officer of Emmaus Market Group, is prepared to testify that Emmaus Market Group would have routinely approved the contract if asked. Lydia Learning can also prove that it sold a delivery van to raise money for the coffee-cart purchase, although it admits that it had not anticipated any need to sell the van when it entered the contract.

If Lydia Learning sues Ruth for breach of contract, is Lydia Learning likely to prevail?', 'If Lydia Learning sues Ruth for breach of contract, is Lydia Learning likely to prevail?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ14462.md","source_question_id":"14462","variant_slug":"mustard_seed_carts","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"56050604","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium","deciding_phase":"CLASH","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A buyer''s purchase duty was conditioned on parent approval; the buyer never requested approval, but the buyer-side parties are ready to close. Can the seller refuse to perform based on that non-occurrence?","review_truth":"A party cannot use the non-occurrence of a condition inserted only for the other side''s protection after the protected side waives it.","dominant_trap":"B","anchor_card":{"card_id":"CON-REM-WAIVE-PROTECTED-CONDITION","title":"Protected condition waiver","signal":"One side says its own commitment depends on approval, but later wants to proceed anyway.","student_move":"Ask whose protection the condition serves before treating nonoccurrence as a defense.","tiny_rule":"The protected side can waive its own condition.","trap":"Treating nonoccurrence as automatically fatal.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONTRACTS-CONDITION-WAIVER-01","statement":"A condition included only to protect one side may be waived by the protected side. After that waiver, the other party cannot use the condition''s non-occurrence as its own escape.","type":"rule","trigger":"The condition is phrased as the buyer''s own commitment and the protected buyer-side parties are ready to close.","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts §§ 84, 225.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONTRACTS-WHOSE-DUTY-01","statement":"Before asking whether the approval happened, ask whose duty the approval condition qualifies. A condition on the buyer''s promise is not automatically a seller''s defense.","type":"clash","trigger":"Choice language turns a buyer-side approval condition into the seller''s reason not to perform.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"condition nonoccurrence versus waiver","prompt":"A buyer-side approval condition did not occur, but the protected side is ready to close. Which answer frame should survive?","answer":"The waiver frame survives over the bare nonoccurrence frame."},{"drill_type":"clash","target_skill":"whose-duty axis","prompt":"One answer says the buyer breached by not seeking approval; another says the buyer-side condition was waived. What axis decides?","answer":"Whose duty the approval condition qualifies and who the condition protects."},{"drill_type":"trap_spotting","target_skill":"reliance without foreseeability","prompt":"The buyer sold assets after contracting but did not anticipate doing so at formation. Why is reliance a weak reason?","answer":"The seller had no reason to foresee that reliance at formation."}]}'),
  ('930865ad-d203-5023-bb91-9cdbb362651b', '14463_calligraphy_supplies', 'CONTRACTS', 'Performance, Breach, and Discharge', 'Performance, Breach, and Discharge', 'third-party-beneficiary status versus contract-level failed-condition defense', 'Ruth, an independent calligrapher who made banners and Scripture-card inserts, turned down a year-long Easter-pageant account after learning that Lydia would send her all of Lydia’s booth-display work for a year. Lydia had promised to do that in a broader deal with Barnabas, Ruth’s brother, who operated a traveling set of Bible-verse projection booths at Christian youth conventions. In that deal, Barnabas promised to buy from Lydia, a merchant seller, all the projector bulbs and portable battery packs his booths required during the year. In the first month, however, Barnabas obtained substantial quantities of those supplies from another supplier. Lydia then told Ruth that Lydia would place no booth-display orders with Ruth.

:', 'Ruth, an independent calligrapher who made banners and Scripture-card inserts, turned down a year-long Easter-pageant account after learning that Lydia would send her all of Lydia’s booth-display work for a year. Lydia had promised to do that in a broader deal with Barnabas, Ruth’s brother, who operated a traveling set of Bible-verse projection booths at Christian youth conventions. In that deal, Barnabas promised to buy from Lydia, a merchant seller, all the projector bulbs and portable battery packs his booths required during the year. In the first month, however, Barnabas obtained substantial quantities of those supplies from another supplier. Lydia then told Ruth that Lydia would place no booth-display orders with Ruth.

:

In Ruth’s action against Lydia for breach of contract, Ruth probably will', 'In Ruth’s action against Lydia for breach of contract, Ruth probably will', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ14463.md","source_question_id":null,"variant_slug":"calligraphy_supplies","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"56050102","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"not_supplied; source_percent_correct=39","deciding_phase":"CLASH","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A supplier promised a buyer to buy a third person’s studio work, but the buyer first bought substantial required goods from another supplier; can the third person recover from the supplier?","review_truth":"A third-party beneficiary cannot enforce a promised benefit when the promisee’s uncured material breach prevents the promisor’s related duty from coming due.","dominant_trap":"C","anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-CONTRACTS-TPB-CONDITION-01","title":"Beneficiary status does not beat a failed condition","signal":"A nonparty sues on a promised benefit, but the stem emphasizes the promisee’s earlier failed performance.","student_move":"Name the status-vs-defense axis before choosing the status answer.","tiny_rule":"Third-party beneficiary rights remain subject to contract-level failed conditions and uncured material breaches.","trap":"Picking the beneficiary label as if it ends the case.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONTRACTS-CALLIGRAPHY-SUPPLIES-01","statement":"A third-party beneficiary’s claim rises no higher than the underlying contract. If the promisee’s uncured material breach or failed condition prevents the promisor’s related duty from coming due, the beneficiary cannot enforce that duty.","type":"rule","trigger":"a beneficiary sues the promisor after the promisee failed to perform the exchange that supported the promised benefit","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts §§ 237 and 309(2); UCC § 2-306 for requirements/exclusive-dealing obligations","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONTRACTS-CALLIGRAPHY-SUPPLIES-01","statement":"Do not stop at the beneficiary label. When one choice names third-party status and another choice names the contracting party’s failed performance, use the status-vs-defense axis and let the earlier performance fact decide.","type":"clash","trigger":"a third person sues, but the stem spends major space on what the contracting buyer did first","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"clash_axis","target_skill":"status-vs-defense axis","prompt":"A contract names a third person to receive a benefit, but the promisee materially breaches before the promisor performs. Which axis decides?","answer":"Beneficiary status versus contract-level failed-condition defense."},{"drill_type":"wrong_element","target_skill":"consideration source","prompt":"A beneficiary gave no consideration, but the promisee gave consideration for the promisor’s promise. Is the beneficiary’s lack of consideration the dispositive reason?","answer":"No. The exchange between promisor and promisee supplies consideration."},{"drill_type":"misfit_cut","target_skill":"agency fact discipline","prompt":"A stem says a contracting party made a deal that benefits a nonparty. One answer says the contracting party acted as the nonparty’s agent. What is the cut?","answer":"Misfit unless the stem supplies agency facts."}]}'),
  ('57ff9b04-6934-5659-8a6a-f6e497ff46a7', '14467_psalm_tutor_installments', 'CONTRACTS', 'Performance, Breach, and Discharge', 'Performance, Breach, and Discharge', 'matured installments only versus full-balance acceleration', 'Mary asked Lydia, a private tutor known for Bible-quiz preparation, to create a Psalm-memorization coaching package for her. Lydia replied that she would do so only after Peter sent a signed reference confirming that Mary had successfully completed Peter’s prior private tutoring course. At Mary’s request, Peter mailed Lydia a signed note: “Mary completed my course and paid me; I recommend working with her.”

On March 3, after reading Peter’s note, Lydia and Mary signed a contract. Lydia immediately delivered the complete coaching package to Mary, and Mary agreed to pay Lydia $1,200 in installments of $120 plus accrued interest on the 20th day of each month beginning April 20. On March 4, Peter left for a six-month wilderness retreat. Later that same day, unaware that Peter had left, Lydia mailed Peter a thank-you postcard saying that she had delivered the coaching package on March 3.

Mary did not pay the installments due on April 20, May 20, or June 20, and she has informed Lydia that she will be unable to make payments in the foreseeable future.', 'Mary asked Lydia, a private tutor known for Bible-quiz preparation, to create a Psalm-memorization coaching package for her. Lydia replied that she would do so only after Peter sent a signed reference confirming that Mary had successfully completed Peter’s prior private tutoring course. At Mary’s request, Peter mailed Lydia a signed note: “Mary completed my course and paid me; I recommend working with her.”

On March 3, after reading Peter’s note, Lydia and Mary signed a contract. Lydia immediately delivered the complete coaching package to Mary, and Mary agreed to pay Lydia $1,200 in installments of $120 plus accrued interest on the 20th day of each month beginning April 20. On March 4, Peter left for a six-month wilderness retreat. Later that same day, unaware that Peter had left, Lydia mailed Peter a thank-you postcard saying that she had delivered the coaching package on March 3.

Mary did not pay the installments due on April 20, May 20, or June 20, and she has informed Lydia that she will be unable to make payments in the foreseeable future.

On July 5, Lydia is entitled to a judgment against Mary for which of the following amounts?', 'On July 5, Lydia is entitled to a judgment against Mary for which of the following amounts?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ14467.md","source_question_id":"14467","variant_slug":"psalm_tutor_installments","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"56050401","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium","deciding_phase":"CLASH","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":"only three installments have matured, and no acceleration term is supplied","distilled_core_question":"A payer owes ten monthly installments after receiving the full service, misses the first three, and says future payment is unlikely; before the rest mature, how much can the provider recover?","review_truth":"A creditor on an installment payment obligation normally recovers only matured unpaid installments unless the contract makes the remaining balance due.","dominant_trap":"A","anchor_card":{"card_id":"CONTRACTS-REMEDY-INSTALLMENT-AMOUNT-LADDER","title":"Installment amount ladder","signal":"The answer choices offer nothing, matured installments, and full balance.","student_move":"Lock the date in the call and count what has matured before considering full-balance remedies.","tiny_rule":"Due installments are collectible now; unmatured installments need an acceleration trigger.","trap":"Upgrading a few missed payments into the whole balance.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONTRACTS-PSALM-TUTOR-INSTALLMENTS-01","statement":"When the only remaining duty is to pay money in separate installments, missing fewer than all installments normally creates only a partial-breach claim for matured payments unless the agreement gives an acceleration right.","type":"rule","trigger":"completed performance plus a monthly payment schedule, missed installments, and no acceleration term supplied","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 243(3); acceleration-clause doctrine","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONTRACTS-PSALM-TUTOR-INSTALLMENTS-01","statement":"Read the choices as an amount ladder: nothing, matured installments, or the full balance. When two choices demand the full balance, ask what fact authorizes acceleration; if the stem gives no such fact, keep the due-installment answer.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"a payment schedule with missed installments and a call asking what amount is recoverable before the whole schedule matures","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"amount_ladder","target_skill":"CLASH","prompt":"A completed service contract is payable in ten monthly installments. The payer misses the first two and says she may never pay. The contract has no acceleration term. Which amount is recoverable today?","answer":"Only the two matured unpaid installments, plus any accrued interest."},{"drill_type":"trap_cut","target_skill":"CUT","prompt":"Cut the answer: ''The creditor gets the whole balance because the debtor appears insolvent.''","answer":"fabricated_rule; the acceleration remedy needs a contractual or legal trigger."},{"drill_type":"gold_key_recall","target_skill":"CALL","prompt":"What fact do full-balance answers need in an installment-payment question?","answer":"An acceleration clause, all installments having matured, or another valid total-breach trigger."}]}'),
  ('91b7396b-ee36-57dd-a417-f685fca50c27', '14477_advent_mugs', 'CONTRACTS', 'Performance, Breach, and Discharge', 'Options and Cooperation Respecting Performance', 'fatal deadline / express condition versus late specification with no material effect', 'By a signed order, Lydia, a private maker of Scripture-themed ceramic mug sets, promised to supply Barnabas’s Christian bookstore with 640 boxed mug sets for its November and December shelves. The order left Barnabas to choose, by August 15, how many boxes would bear each of four verse designs. When August 15 passed without a choice, Lydia still had every design needed and made no inventory commitments on August 15 or August 16. On the morning of August 16, Lydia sent Barnabas a message canceling the order because he had missed the assortment date. That afternoon, after receiving the message, Barnabas sent the full design breakdown and demanded shipment. Lydia refused.', 'By a signed order, Lydia, a private maker of Scripture-themed ceramic mug sets, promised to supply Barnabas’s Christian bookstore with 640 boxed mug sets for its November and December shelves. The order left Barnabas to choose, by August 15, how many boxes would bear each of four verse designs. When August 15 passed without a choice, Lydia still had every design needed and made no inventory commitments on August 15 or August 16. On the morning of August 16, Lydia sent Barnabas a message canceling the order because he had missed the assortment date. That afternoon, after receiving the message, Barnabas sent the full design breakdown and demanded shipment. Lydia refused.

If Barnabas sues Lydia for breach of contract, is Barnabas likely to prevail?', 'If Barnabas sues Lydia for breach of contract, is Barnabas likely to prevail?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ14477.md","source_question_id":"14477","variant_slug":"advent_mugs","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"56050300","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"not_supplied_in_source_row","deciding_phase":"CLASH","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A buyer in a UCC sale gives a required assortment choice one day late; the seller had all goods ready and was not affected by the delay. Can the seller refuse to perform?","review_truth":"In a UCC sale, a late buyer selection defeats the buyer only when the lateness materially affects the seller’s performance.","dominant_trap":"D","anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-CONTRACTS-SPECIFICATION-01","title":"Late specification is not automatically fatal","signal":"goods sale leaves assortment or performance particulars for later","student_move":"Ask whether the late choice materially affected the other party’s performance.","tiny_rule":"No material effect means no refusal to perform.","trap":"deadline language treated as an express condition","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONTRACTS-ADVENT-MUGS-01","statement":"In a UCC sale, leaving assortment particulars for later buyer specification does not invalidate the contract; a late specification matters only if it materially affects the seller’s performance.","type":"rule","trigger":"goods sale with buyer left to choose assortments later","authority":"UCC § 2-311","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONTRACTS-ADVENT-MUGS-01","statement":"When a deadline choice fights a no-effect choice, name the axis before deciding: did the missed specification materially affect the seller’s performance?","type":"clash","trigger":"one-day late specification plus inventory/no-commitment facts","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"clash_axis","target_skill":"name the material-effect axis","prompt":"Buyer sends assortment one day late; seller had inventory ready and no changed commitments. Fatal deadline or no material effect?","answer":"No material effect."},{"drill_type":"gold_key_recall","target_skill":"UCC open particulars","prompt":"Does leaving assortment particulars for later buyer specification invalidate an otherwise definite UCC sale?","answer":"No."},{"drill_type":"trap_cut","target_skill":"condition-language trap","prompt":"An answer says a date for choosing assortment was an express condition. What fact must you check?","answer":"Whether the late specification materially affected the seller’s ability to perform."}]}'),
  ('5ddb6a39-f6a6-50ff-91d6-95a1b257f141', '14492_psalm_lantern_kits', 'CONTRACTS', 'Performance, Breach, and Discharge', 'Installment contracts; perfect-tender exception; cure; cancellation', NULL, 'Lydia operates a private craft workshop that sells devotional event supplies. In a signed writing, Lydia contracted with Daniel, who runs a private Bible-memory retreat, for the sale to Daniel of 40 identical Psalm 23 shadow-lantern kits. The writing called for 20 kits to be delivered on October 10 and the remaining 20 kits on October 24. The writing did not state the place of delivery or the time or place for payment. On October 10, Lydia tendered 19 kits and said, “One kit was crushed in the courier’s truck on its way from my supplier to me, but I will deliver a replacement within four days.”', 'Lydia operates a private craft workshop that sells devotional event supplies. In a signed writing, Lydia contracted with Daniel, who runs a private Bible-memory retreat, for the sale to Daniel of 40 identical Psalm 23 shadow-lantern kits. The writing called for 20 kits to be delivered on October 10 and the remaining 20 kits on October 24. The writing did not state the place of delivery or the time or place for payment. On October 10, Lydia tendered 19 kits and said, “One kit was crushed in the courier’s truck on its way from my supplier to me, but I will deliver a replacement within four days.”

Which statement is correct?', 'Which statement is correct?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ14492.md","source_question_id":"14492","variant_slug":"psalm_lantern_kits","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"56050402","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium","deciding_phase":"CALL","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"Seller owes 20 goods now and 20 later; seller tenders 19 now and promises a replacement in four days. Must buyer accept the current lot, and may buyer cancel the future lot?","review_truth":"In a goods installment contract, a prompt cure promise blocks perfect-tender rejection and whole-contract cancellation unless the nonconformity substantially impairs the whole contract.","dominant_trap":"A","anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-CONTRACTS-INSTALLMENT-CURE","title":"Installment Cure Beats Perfect Tender","signal":"Goods due in separate dated lots plus prompt replacement promise.","student_move":"Do not run ordinary perfect tender; run the installment matrix.","tiny_rule":"A curable nonconforming installment with assurance must be accepted; canceling the whole contract requires substantial impairment of the whole.","trap":"ordinary perfect-tender partial rejection","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONTRACTS-PSALM-LANTERN-KITS-01","statement":"In a UCC installment contract, the perfect-tender rule yields to the installment rule: a curable nonconforming installment with adequate assurance of cure must be accepted, and the whole contract can be canceled only for substantial impairment of the whole.","type":"exception","trigger":"goods contract calls for separate lots on separate dates and the seller promises a prompt replacement","authority":"UCC § 2-612(1)-(3)","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONTRACTS-PSALM-LANTERN-KITS-01","statement":"Read the choices as a 2x2 matrix: current lot accept/reject and future contract cancel/no cancel. The right answer must satisfy both axes, not just one.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"four choices pair the same two remedy axes in every combination","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"answer_array","target_skill":"2x2 remedy matrix","prompt":"A goods seller owes 12 items now and 12 later, delivers 11 now, and promises a replacement in three days. Which axes must be checked?","answer":"Current installment accept/reject and whole-contract cancel/no cancel."},{"drill_type":"trap_spotting","target_skill":"perfect tender wrong-frame","prompt":"A choice says the buyer may accept any units and reject the rest after a short installment with cure promised. What is the trap?","answer":"It imports ordinary perfect tender into an installment contract."},{"drill_type":"call","target_skill":"whole-contract cancellation threshold","prompt":"One small part of the first installment is missing and cure is promised. Can the buyer cancel all remaining installments?","answer":"No, not without substantial impairment of the whole contract."}]}'),
  ('91190ca8-8d63-5c27-9fe0-ca1ac51ceef7', '14493_ark_stage', 'CONTRACTS', 'Performance, Breach, and Discharge', 'Anticipatory repudiation', NULL, 'Two days before a Thursday evening handoff, Stephen accepted $12,400 from Hannah and let Hannah haul away his hand-painted Jonah puppet stage.

Earlier, Ruth and Stephen had signed a written agreement under which Ruth would buy that stage from Stephen for $9,800, with the price due at 7 p.m. Thursday at Stephen’s storage workshop. The agreement provided, and both intended, that time was of the essence.

At 7 p.m. Thursday, Ruth arrived at the workshop with a cashier’s check ready to pay. Stephen was away, so Ruth called to ask where he was. Stephen said that the stage had been sold to Hannah.', 'Two days before a Thursday evening handoff, Stephen accepted $12,400 from Hannah and let Hannah haul away his hand-painted Jonah puppet stage.

Earlier, Ruth and Stephen had signed a written agreement under which Ruth would buy that stage from Stephen for $9,800, with the price due at 7 p.m. Thursday at Stephen’s storage workshop. The agreement provided, and both intended, that time was of the essence.

At 7 p.m. Thursday, Ruth arrived at the workshop with a cashier’s check ready to pay. Stephen was away, so Ruth called to ask where he was. Stephen said that the stage had been sold to Hannah.

If Ruth sues Stephen for breach of contract, is Ruth likely to prevail?', 'If Ruth sues Stephen for breach of contract, is Ruth likely to prevail?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ14493.md","source_question_id":null,"variant_slug":"ark_stage","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"56050403","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"Seller promised a specific item, then sold it to someone else before the payment date; can the buyer sue for breach even without tendering?","review_truth":"A party who voluntarily transfers the promised specific item before performance is due has repudiated, so the other party may sue without tendering.","dominant_trap":"D","anchor_card":{"card_id":"CON-REPUDIATION-ACT-01","title":"Act-as-repudiation vs self-created impossibility","signal":"Promisor transfers the promised specific item before the date for performance.","student_move":"Treat the transfer as the breach trigger, then cut excuses and tender-blame answers.","tiny_rule":"Own-act inability is repudiation, not impossibility excuse.","trap":"Impossibility language and tender language both distract from the seller''s prior transfer.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONTRACTS-ARK-STAGE-01","statement":"A party can anticipatorily repudiate by a voluntary act before performance is due that makes the promised performance impossible or apparently impossible. Self-created impossibility is not an excuse; it is the breach trigger.","type":"distinction","trigger":"The promisor sells or transfers the promised specific item to a third party before performance is due.","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts §250(b); U.C.C. §2-610; Restatement (Second) of Contracts §261.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONTRACTS-ARK-STAGE-01","statement":"For a because-choice, do not stop at the yes/no word. The reason clause must name the operative breach trigger, not a neighboring fact like notice or tender.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"An answer begins with the correct outcome but says ''because'' and points to a different fact than the stem''s strongest breach fact.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"because-clause verification","prompt":"A seller transfers the promised item before performance is due. Which answer survives: yes because of notice, or yes because of the transfer?","answer":"The transfer answer survives."},{"drill_type":"anchor","target_skill":"act-as-repudiation","prompt":"Promisor voluntarily sells the promised specific item before the due date. Is that an excuse or a repudiation?","answer":"Repudiation."},{"drill_type":"wrong_element","target_skill":"tender trap","prompt":"After a seller repudiates, a no answer blames the buyer for not tendering. What filter breaks?","answer":"NOT_RESPONSIVE; it focuses on a non-dispositive tender fact."}]}'),
  ('ae766d85-f33a-594d-abbf-82fa912763fc', '14506_lydia_rooftop_loft', 'CONTRACTS', 'Remedies', 'Specific performance', 'date breach versus time-not-essential partial delay', 'Mary signed a written contract to sell Timothy a downtown parcel known as Psalm Loft, a three-story brick building with a rooftop garden and a narrow parking strip, for $742,000 in cash. The contract called for Mary to deliver the deed and possession, and for Timothy to pay the price, on March 4.

On March 4, Mary told Timothy that Lydia, who had rented one storage bay for a private choir-robe pop-up shop, was wrongfully refusing to leave until March 19. Mary assured Timothy that she would tender the deed and possession on March 19. On March 19, after Lydia left, Mary tendered both the deed and possession. Timothy refused to accept either and refused to pay the $742,000. Throughout March, Psalm Loft’s market value was $756,000, and its fair monthly rental value was $7,200.', 'Mary signed a written contract to sell Timothy a downtown parcel known as Psalm Loft, a three-story brick building with a rooftop garden and a narrow parking strip, for $742,000 in cash. The contract called for Mary to deliver the deed and possession, and for Timothy to pay the price, on March 4.

On March 4, Mary told Timothy that Lydia, who had rented one storage bay for a private choir-robe pop-up shop, was wrongfully refusing to leave until March 19. Mary assured Timothy that she would tender the deed and possession on March 19. On March 19, after Lydia left, Mary tendered both the deed and possession. Timothy refused to accept either and refused to pay the $742,000. Throughout March, Psalm Loft’s market value was $756,000, and its fair monthly rental value was $7,200.

Will Mary probably succeed in an action against Timothy for specific performance?', 'Will Mary probably succeed in an action against Timothy for specific performance?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ14506.md","source_question_id":"14506","variant_slug":"lydia_rooftop_loft","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"51060304","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"not_supplied","deciding_phase":"CLASH","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":"Mary tendered deed and possession 15 days later, and the stem supplies no time-essential signal.","distilled_core_question":"Seller tenders deed and possession for land 15 days after the stated closing date because a holdover occupant delayed possession; buyer refuses to close; can seller get specific performance?","review_truth":"In a land-sale contract, a short late tender does not defeat specific performance unless time was made essential, and land is treated as uniquely suited to specific performance.","dominant_trap":"C gives right result through wrong excuse frame.","anchor_card":{"card_id":"RC-CONTRACTS-LAND-SP-14506","title":"Land-sale late tender: date breach is not automatic walkaway","student_move":"Clash missed date against time-not-essential; then apply land-specific performance anchor."},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONTRACTS-LYDIA-ROOFTOP-01","statement":"A closing date in a land-sale contract is ordinarily not a drop-dead condition by itself; a short late tender is usually a partial breach unless the contract or circumstances make time essential.","type":"distinction","trigger":"land sale, stated closing date, later tender, no facts making that date essential","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts §242(c).","last_minute_review":true},{"id":"GK-CONTRACTS-LYDIA-ROOFTOP-02","statement":"Land-sale contracts are the classic specific-performance setting; money damages are treated as inadequate, and either side may seek specific performance when the other refuses to close.","type":"rule","trigger":"specific performance requested on a contract to sell land","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts §§357, 359, 360; Restatement of Contracts §360.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONTRACTS-LYDIA-ROOFTOP-01","statement":"In a 2-yes/2-no because-clause array, do not pick by outcome. Keep the result separate from the reason, then clash the missed-date answer against the time-not-essential answer.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"two choices say yes, two say no, and each turns on a different reason","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"clash_axis","target_skill":"time-not-essential versus missed-date breach","prompt":"A seller tenders land title 12 days late after a holdover leaves; the buyer refuses. Which fact decides whether the buyer may walk away?","answer":"Whether the date was made essential, not merely whether the date was missed."},{"drill_type":"gold_key_recall","target_skill":"land-specific-performance anchor","prompt":"In a land-sale contract, is money usually treated as an adequate remedy defeating specific performance?","answer":"No. Land is treated as unique, so specific performance is traditionally available."},{"drill_type":"because_clause_cut","target_skill":"right-result wrong-reason detection","prompt":"A yes answer says the seller wins because the late tender was impossible to perform on time. What check should you run?","answer":"Check whether the doctrine is excuse or partial breach/time-not-essential; a right result can have a wrong frame."}]}'),
  ('960bf434-0910-5c89-9bb3-638eb54bfad0', '14510_ark_puppet_stage', 'CONTRACTS', 'Remedies', 'UCC seller remedies', NULL, 'Timothy ran a for-profit storytelling booth that used Bible-themed puppet sets at county fairs. He ordered from Lydia’s Workshop a new fold-up Noah’s Ark puppet-stage kit. Before the kit arrived, Timothy canceled that part of his business. The kit arrived on the promised date and matched the order exactly. Timothy rejected it and sent it back to Lydia’s Workshop. Lydia’s Workshop sells many such ark kits each year, and its production shop was not operating at full capacity.', 'Timothy ran a for-profit storytelling booth that used Bible-themed puppet sets at county fairs. He ordered from Lydia’s Workshop a new fold-up Noah’s Ark puppet-stage kit. Before the kit arrived, Timothy canceled that part of his business. The kit arrived on the promised date and matched the order exactly. Timothy rejected it and sent it back to Lydia’s Workshop. Lydia’s Workshop sells many such ark kits each year, and its production shop was not operating at full capacity.

In an action by Lydia’s Workshop against Timothy for breach of contract, which of the following is NOT a proper measure of Lydia’s Workshop’s damages?', 'In an action by Lydia’s Workshop against Timothy for breach of contract, which of the following is NOT a proper measure of Lydia’s Workshop’s damages?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ14510.md","source_question_id":"14510","variant_slug":"ark_puppet_stage","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"51060101","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"Buyer wrongfully rejects conforming goods and returns them to a seller with unused capacity; on a NOT-proper-measure call, which damages measure is the odd one out?","review_truth":"A seller who receives rejected goods back may not recover the contract price unless the goods were accepted, lost or damaged after risk shifted, or cannot reasonably be resold.","dominant_trap":"C","anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-CONTRACTS-SELLER-PRICE-01","title":"Seller remedies: price is the exception","signal":"Buyer rejects and returns conforming goods.","student_move":"On a NOT-proper-measure call, separate price actions from damages formulas.","tiny_rule":"Price requires acceptance, loss/damage after risk passed, or inability to resell.","trap":"Treating contract price as ordinary expectation damages.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONTRACTS-PRICE-ACTION-01","statement":"A seller’s action for the price is narrow: accepted goods, goods lost or damaged after risk shifted, or identified goods the seller cannot reasonably resell. Returned, resellable goods usually point away from the contract price.","type":"rule","trigger":"buyer rejects and returns conforming goods and the seller sues for a full-price remedy","authority":"UCC § 2-709","last_minute_review":true},{"id":"GK-CONTRACTS-LOST-VOLUME-02","statement":"A seller with unused capacity can be a lost-volume seller: resale does not necessarily replace the breached sale, so lost profit can be a proper measure when market damages undercompensate.","type":"rule","trigger":"seller sells many such goods and was not operating at full capacity","authority":"UCC § 2-708(2)","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONTRACTS-NEGATIVE-REMEDY-01","statement":"When the call asks which measure is NOT proper, cross out the measures that are valid seller remedies. The survivor is the remedy whose required trigger is missing.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"call asks for a measure that is NOT proper","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"negative_stem_cut","target_skill":"call_focus","prompt":"A seller receives rejected goods back. Three choices state valid seller remedies and one states the full price. The call asks which is NOT proper. What is the first move?","answer":"Invert the call and cut the three proper remedies."},{"drill_type":"anchor_recall","target_skill":"seller_price_action","prompt":"Name the three UCC-style triggers that allow a seller to sue for the price.","answer":"Accepted goods, goods lost or damaged after risk passed, or identified goods the seller cannot reasonably resell."},{"drill_type":"trap_diagnosis","target_skill":"lost_volume_seller","prompt":"Seller sells many identical goods and has unused capacity. Why can lost profit remain proper after resale?","answer":"The seller may have lost an additional sale rather than merely substituted one buyer for another."}]}'),
  ('3304382d-5dc5-5344-9607-3e0753d1b068', '14511_worship_studio_delay', 'CONTRACTS', 'Remedies', 'Consequential damages / foreseeability / delay in real-property possession', 'fair rental value only versus rental value plus unforeseeable trailer cost', 'Peter contracted in a signed writing to sell to Paul a converted barn that Peter had been using as a sound-recording studio, together with the surrounding three-acre lot, for $150,000 in cash. The contract provided for exchange of the deed and purchase price on April 15. Possession was to be given to Paul on the same date.

On April 15, Peter notified Paul that because the tenant who leased the studio space wrongfully refused to quit the premises until April 30, Peter would be unable to deliver possession of the barn until then, but he assured Paul that he would tender the deed and possession on that date. Throughout April, the market value of the property was $155,000, and its fair monthly rental value was $2,000.

On April 30, Paul accepted a conveyance and possession of the barn and paid the $150,000 purchase price, but notified Peter that he was reserving any rights he might have to damages caused by Peter''s breach. Paul intended to use the studio to record a worship album and had scheduled a 15-day tracking block with musicians beginning April 15. Because he did not have possession of the barn on that date, he had to rent a mobile sound-recording trailer at a cost of $1,000 for the 15 days. Peter had no reason to know that Paul intended to use the barn as a worship-recording studio or that he had rented the mobile trailer.', 'Peter contracted in a signed writing to sell to Paul a converted barn that Peter had been using as a sound-recording studio, together with the surrounding three-acre lot, for $150,000 in cash. The contract provided for exchange of the deed and purchase price on April 15. Possession was to be given to Paul on the same date.

On April 15, Peter notified Paul that because the tenant who leased the studio space wrongfully refused to quit the premises until April 30, Peter would be unable to deliver possession of the barn until then, but he assured Paul that he would tender the deed and possession on that date. Throughout April, the market value of the property was $155,000, and its fair monthly rental value was $2,000.

On April 30, Paul accepted a conveyance and possession of the barn and paid the $150,000 purchase price, but notified Peter that he was reserving any rights he might have to damages caused by Peter''s breach. Paul intended to use the studio to record a worship album and had scheduled a 15-day tracking block with musicians beginning April 15. Because he did not have possession of the barn on that date, he had to rent a mobile sound-recording trailer at a cost of $1,000 for the 15 days. Peter had no reason to know that Paul intended to use the barn as a worship-recording studio or that he had rented the mobile trailer.

In an action by Paul against Peter for damages, Paul is entitled to recover', 'In an action by Paul against Peter for damages, Paul is entitled to recover', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ14511.md","source_question_id":"14511","variant_slug":"worship_studio_delay","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"51060103","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium","deciding_phase":"CALL","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A seller delayed delivery of possession of real property for 15 days. The buyer accepted late possession, reserved his rights, and now seeks damages, including the cost of a mobile trailer he rented because he could not use the property. Is the buyer limited to the fair rental value for the delay, or can he also recover the trailer cost?","review_truth":"A buyer who accepts late possession of real property may recover only the fair rental value of the delay; special costs are not recoverable unless the seller knew of the buyer''s particular needs at the time of contracting.","dominant_trap":"C","anchor_card":{"card_id":"RC-CONTRACTS-DELAY-DAMAGES-01","title":"Delay in real-property possession — foreseeability of special costs","signal":"Seller delays delivering possession; buyer wants both fair rental value and a special cost the seller did not know about.","student_move":"Cut the wrong-frame and contradiction choices first; then use the Gold Key to strip any unforeseeable special cost from the additive choice.","tiny_rule":"Fair rental value for the delay is recoverable; special costs are recoverable only if the seller knew of the buyer''s particular needs at contracting.","trap":"The additive choice packages the right base amount with a sympathetic extra cost.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONTRACTS-WORSHIP-STUDIO-01","statement":"Consequential damages are limited to losses the breaching party had reason to foresee as a probable result of the breach at the time the contract was made.","type":"rule","trigger":"buyer seeks special costs the seller did not know about at contracting","authority":"Hadley v. Baxendale (1854) and Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 351","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONTRACTS-WORSHIP-STUDIO-01","statement":"When a damages choice adds a second cost to the correct base amount, test whether that second cost was foreseeable before selecting it.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"one choice states the right base amount plus an extra sympathetic cost","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"wrong frame","prompt":"A buyer sues for damages for a 15-day delay in receiving possession of real property. A choice says ''nominal damages only because market value exceeded contract price.'' Why is this cut?","answer":"Market value above price is the wrong frame; the question is loss of use during the delay, measured by fair rental value."},{"drill_type":"anchor","target_skill":"Hadley foreseeability","prompt":"A buyer wants to recover a special cost the seller did not know about at contracting. What is the controlling rule?","answer":"Consequential damages are limited to losses the breaching party had reason to foresee as a probable result of the breach at the time of contracting."},{"drill_type":"call_focus","target_skill":"reservation of rights","prompt":"A buyer accepts late performance, pays, and expressly reserves rights to damages. Has the buyer waived the claim?","answer":"No. Accepting performance and paying with an express reservation of rights preserves the damages claim."}]}'),
  ('e2429e78-106e-527d-9abd-7e165ac0e086', '14521_sea_of_galilee_lantern_walk', 'CONTRACTS', 'Remedies', 'Expectation damages', NULL, 'Daniel contracted to create a privately owned walk-through Bible-story lantern garden for Peter''s family Easter festival. Daniel hired Lydia to build the ''Sea of Galilee'' stone walkway and hidden lighting bed for $260,000. Under the subcontract, Lydia completed work with a reasonable value of $190,000, and Daniel made required progress payments totaling $130,000 for that stage. Lydia then unjustifiably refused to perform further. Daniel reasonably paid Barnabas $155,000 to finish the same work. Lydia sues Daniel for the reasonable value of the benefits conferred, and Daniel counterclaims for breach of contract. Which of the following should be the court''s decision?', 'Daniel contracted to create a privately owned walk-through Bible-story lantern garden for Peter''s family Easter festival. Daniel hired Lydia to build the ''Sea of Galilee'' stone walkway and hidden lighting bed for $260,000. Under the subcontract, Lydia completed work with a reasonable value of $190,000, and Daniel made required progress payments totaling $130,000 for that stage. Lydia then unjustifiably refused to perform further. Daniel reasonably paid Barnabas $155,000 to finish the same work. Lydia sues Daniel for the reasonable value of the benefits conferred, and Daniel counterclaims for breach of contract. Which of the following should be the court''s decision?

Which of the following should be the court''s decision?', 'Which of the following should be the court''s decision?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ14521.md","source_question_id":null,"variant_slug":"sea_of_galilee_lantern_walk","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"51060101","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"PURE_ANCHOR","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A subcontractor is fully paid under the subcontract for the work stage completed, then unjustifiably walks off; the contractor pays more than the remaining subcontract price to finish. Who recovers?","review_truth":"A breaching performer cannot use restitution to exceed the contract allocation, and the nonbreaching party recovers the reasonable completion overrun.","dominant_trap":"A","anchor_card":{"card_id":"REMED-CONTRACTS-REMEDY-CAP-01","title":"Breacher restitution cap versus completion overrun","signal":"Breaching performer sues for reasonable value and the nonbreaching party paid a replacement to finish.","student_move":"Anchor the contract price first, then subtract amounts already paid and reasonable completion cost.","tiny_rule":"The nonbreaching party should not pay more than the contract price for the promised completed performance.","trap":"Netting unpaid reasonable value to the breacher.","confidence":"PURE_ANCHOR"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONTRACTS-BREACHER-RESTITUTION-CAP-01","statement":"A party who materially breaches after part performance cannot turn restitution into a better deal than the contract gave; the recipient''s total required outlay is capped by the contract price, and any reasonable completion overrun is recoverable from the breacher.","type":"rule","trigger":"a breaching performer sues for reasonable value while the nonbreaching party paid another to finish","authority":"Restatement (Third) of Restitution and Unjust Enrichment §36; Restatement (Second) of Contracts §347; United States ex rel. Palmer Construction, Inc. v. Cal State Electric, Inc., 940 F.2d 1260 (9th Cir. 1991)","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONTRACTS-DOLLAR-OUTCOME-ARRAY-01","statement":"In a dollar-outcome array, label who gets paid before trusting arithmetic. A breacher-recovery answer can look precise while pointing the money to the wrong side.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"choices pay Lydia gross, Lydia net, Daniel overrun, or no one","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"calculation","target_skill":"completion-overrun calculation","prompt":"Contract price $300,000; breacher was paid $160,000; reasonable completion cost $170,000. What is the nonbreacher''s overrun?","answer":"$30,000."},{"drill_type":"trap_spotting","target_skill":"breacher restitution cap","prompt":"A breaching performer demands unpaid reasonable value above the progress-payment amount set by the contract. What anchor blocks the demand?","answer":"The breaching performer cannot use restitution to exceed the contract allocation for the performance retained."},{"drill_type":"answer_array","target_skill":"who-gets-paid labeling","prompt":"One option pays the breacher gross, one pays the breacher net, one pays the nonbreacher''s overrun, and one says nobody recovers. What label do you write before math?","answer":"Label the payee first; the likely survivor pays the nonbreaching party''s reasonable overrun."}]}'),
  ('4e90887e-5ce2-5f4e-a80b-32cf705c9e78', '14523_psalm_calligraphy_stipend', 'CONTRACTS', 'Remedies', 'Specific performance', 'Does the reason answer remedy adequacy, or does it answer a nearby burden/no-loss/money-label question?', 'Stem + call: At a Saturday Bible-memory fair in a rented community hall, Martha and Peter, both adults, learned that Hannah, their former Scripture-calligraphy teacher, wanted to hire a private studio aide so she could keep teaching from her backyard workshop. Hannah could afford only half of the aide''s $2,400 monthly fee. Martha and Peter signed a contract with each other providing that, on the first day of May and on the first day of each succeeding month during Hannah''s lifetime, each would pay Hannah $600. Hannah then hired the aide. Martha and Peter made the payments in May, June, and July. In August, Peter refused to make any payment and notified Martha and Hannah that he would make no further payments. The contract between Martha and Peter is valid, but Hannah has declined to sue Peter.', 'Stem + call: At a Saturday Bible-memory fair in a rented community hall, Martha and Peter, both adults, learned that Hannah, their former Scripture-calligraphy teacher, wanted to hire a private studio aide so she could keep teaching from her backyard workshop. Hannah could afford only half of the aide''s $2,400 monthly fee. Martha and Peter signed a contract with each other providing that, on the first day of May and on the first day of each succeeding month during Hannah''s lifetime, each would pay Hannah $600. Hannah then hired the aide. Martha and Peter made the payments in May, June, and July. In August, Peter refused to make any payment and notified Martha and Hannah that he would make no further payments. The contract between Martha and Peter is valid, but Hannah has declined to sue Peter.

Will Martha likely prevail in an action against Peter seeking a court order requiring Peter to continue making the payments to Hannah under the contract?', 'Will Martha likely prevail in an action against Peter seeking a court order requiring Peter to continue making the payments to Hannah under the contract?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ14523.md","source_question_id":"14523","variant_slug":"psalm_calligraphy_stipend","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":null,"outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"source row: 30% correct; no difficulty label supplied","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"Two contracting parties promised to make monthly payments to a third person for that person''s lifetime; one stopped and repudiated; can the other get an order requiring continued payments?","review_truth":"Specific performance can enforce a payment promise when money damages would not adequately protect the promised performance.","dominant_trap":"D","anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-CONTRACTS-SPECIFIC-PERFORMANCE-MONEY-01","title":"Money promise is not an automatic no-SP answer","signal":"Answer says a court will not specifically enforce a promise to pay money.","student_move":"Treat the categorical no as suspect and ask whether damages are adequate.","tiny_rule":"Specific performance turns on adequacy, not on a blanket money/non-money label.","trap":"Confusing personal-service refusal with money-payment promises.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONTRACTS-PSALM-CALLIGRAPHY-STIPEND-01","statement":"Specific performance is not categorically barred merely because the promised act is payment of money. If damages are inadequate because the promised stream is hard to value, the court can order the payment promise performed.","type":"distinction","trigger":"A valid contract requires continuing payments to a third person for an uncertain lifetime duration.","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts §§ 359-360; specific performance when damages are inadequate.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONTRACTS-PSALM-CALLIGRAPHY-STIPEND-01","statement":"For a court-order remedy call, lock the remedy axis first: money damages adequate or inadequate. Do not let choices about moral burden or no pocket loss replace the remedy question.","type":"call_focus","trigger":"The call asks for a court order requiring future payments rather than a damages award.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"Gold Key recall","target_skill":"specific performance money-payment distinction","prompt":"A valid contract requires monthly payments to a third person for that person''s lifetime; damages are difficult to calculate. Is specific performance categorically barred because the promise is to pay money?","answer":"No. Adequacy of damages controls."},{"drill_type":"Issue-Sense wrong-element","target_skill":"remedy-call focus","prompt":"An answer says the enforcing party has no pocket loss. What move should you run?","answer":"Check whether the call asks for damages to that party or an order enforcing promised performance."},{"drill_type":"Overclaim detector","target_skill":"tiered absolute","prompt":"The answer says a court will not grant specific performance of a promise to pay money. What word shape is dangerous?","answer":"The categorical ''will not'' is an overclaim unless a true categorical rule supports it."}]}'),
  ('9c2f9c93-887d-5db6-8d24-27f75563bd7f', '14525_jonah_fish_escape', 'CONTRACTS', 'Remedies', 'Damages', 'per_se_new_business_bar_vs_reasonable_certainty_proof', 'Lydia, a well-known Christian cooking-show host, contracted with Shiloh Arcade Productions, Inc., a private company that runs Bible-themed escape rooms, to host a $7,300 opening-night livestream for its new “Jonah’s Fish Escape” puzzle room. Days before the launch, a video spread online accusing Lydia of shouting at and shoving a costumed mascot at a fish-fry festival. Shiloh canceled the contract because it feared families would stay away if Lydia appeared.

Lydia sued Shiloh for breach of contract. Her manager, Barnabas, testified without contradiction that the cancellation caused Lydia to lose other new livestream, devotional-box promotion, and endorsement contracts. The trial court instructed the jury, in part:

> “If you find for Lydia, you may award damages for losses which at the time of contracting could reasonably have been foreseen by Shiloh as a probable result of its breach. However, the law does not permit recovery for the loss of prospective profits of a new business caused by breach of contract.”

On Lydia’s appeal from a jury verdict for Lydia, and judgment thereon, awarding damages only of the $7,300 fee promised by Shiloh, the judgment will probably be', 'Lydia, a well-known Christian cooking-show host, contracted with Shiloh Arcade Productions, Inc., a private company that runs Bible-themed escape rooms, to host a $7,300 opening-night livestream for its new “Jonah’s Fish Escape” puzzle room. Days before the launch, a video spread online accusing Lydia of shouting at and shoving a costumed mascot at a fish-fry festival. Shiloh canceled the contract because it feared families would stay away if Lydia appeared.

Lydia sued Shiloh for breach of contract. Her manager, Barnabas, testified without contradiction that the cancellation caused Lydia to lose other new livestream, devotional-box promotion, and endorsement contracts. The trial court instructed the jury, in part:

> “If you find for Lydia, you may award damages for losses which at the time of contracting could reasonably have been foreseen by Shiloh as a probable result of its breach. However, the law does not permit recovery for the loss of prospective profits of a new business caused by breach of contract.”

On Lydia’s appeal from a jury verdict for Lydia, and judgment thereon, awarding damages only of the $7,300 fee promised by Shiloh, the judgment will probably be

On Lydia''s appeal from a fee-only judgment after a jury instruction barring new-business lost profits, what will probably happen?', 'On Lydia''s appeal from a fee-only judgment after a jury instruction barring new-business lost profits, what will probably happen?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ14525.md","source_question_id":"14525","variant_slug":"jonah_fish_escape","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"51060103","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"PURE_ANCHOR","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A private company breaches a paid appearance contract, the judge tells the jury that new-business lost profits are never recoverable, and uncontradicted evidence shows lost follow-on contracts; should the fee-only judgment be reversed?","review_truth":"New-business lost profits are not categorically barred; they may be recovered when proved with reasonable certainty.","dominant_trap":"C","anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-CONTRACTS-NEW-BIZ-LOST-PROFITS","title":"New business is not automatic no","student_move":"When an answer or instruction says new-business profits are never recoverable, replace never with proved with reasonable certainty.","trap":"dominant trap C"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONTRACTS-NEW-BIZ-LOST-PROFITS-01","statement":"New-business lost profits are not automatically barred. The modern rule allows them if the loss can be established with reasonable certainty; newness makes proof harder, not impossible.","type":"rule","trigger":"jury instruction says the law does not permit prospective profits of a new business","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 352 and modern majority cases rejecting a per se new-business bar","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONTRACTS-OUTCOME-REASON-01","statement":"In an appeal answer array, do not stop at affirmed or reversed. The credited answer must pair the right appellate result with the right reason.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"choices split by appellate result plus because-clause","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"Gold Key recall","target_skill":"new-business lost profits","prompt":"A judge tells the jury that a new venture can never recover prospective profits. What phrase should replace ''never''?","answer":"Recoverable if proved with reasonable certainty."},{"drill_type":"answer-array read","target_skill":"outcome plus reason","prompt":"Two answers reverse and two affirm. What must you grade after the result word?","answer":"The because-clause."},{"drill_type":"overclaim cut","target_skill":"tiered_absolute","prompt":"Damages are solely a jury question. What word is the tell?","answer":"Solely."},{"drill_type":"wrong-frame cut","target_skill":"foreseeability versus certainty","prompt":"A choice attacks foreseeability when the instruction imposed a new-business-profit bar. What is the skipped issue?","answer":"Reasonable certainty for lost profits."}]}'),
  ('11d7e1b5-26d8-515f-bc6d-d7b23d16d9a4', '14530_lantern_courtyard', 'CONTRACTS', 'Remedies', 'Damages', NULL, 'Ruth wanted to buy the Lantern Courtyard, a small city parcel decorated with Scripture calligraphy for her private hymn-printing studio. On February 18, before Lydia had received any payment or communication from Ruth, Lydia sold and conveyed the Lantern Courtyard to Hannah for $515,000.

Fifteen days earlier, Lydia and Ruth had signed a writing in which Lydia, in consideration of $470 to be paid by Ruth, offered Ruth the right to purchase the Lantern Courtyard for $470,000 within 24 days. The writing stated, “This offer will become effective as an option only if and when the $470 consideration is in fact paid.”

On February 19, Lydia received a courier envelope from Ruth enclosing a cashier’s check for $470 payable to Lydia and stating, “I am hereby exercising my option to purchase the Lantern Courtyard and am prepared to close whenever you’re ready.”', 'Ruth wanted to buy the Lantern Courtyard, a small city parcel decorated with Scripture calligraphy for her private hymn-printing studio. On February 18, before Lydia had received any payment or communication from Ruth, Lydia sold and conveyed the Lantern Courtyard to Hannah for $515,000.

Fifteen days earlier, Lydia and Ruth had signed a writing in which Lydia, in consideration of $470 to be paid by Ruth, offered Ruth the right to purchase the Lantern Courtyard for $470,000 within 24 days. The writing stated, “This offer will become effective as an option only if and when the $470 consideration is in fact paid.”

On February 19, Lydia received a courier envelope from Ruth enclosing a cashier’s check for $470 payable to Lydia and stating, “I am hereby exercising my option to purchase the Lantern Courtyard and am prepared to close whenever you’re ready.”

Assume that, for whatever reason, Ruth prevails in the suit against Lydia. Which of the following is Ruth entitled to recover?', 'Assume that, for whatever reason, Ruth prevails in the suit against Lydia. Which of the following is Ruth entitled to recover?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ14530.md","source_question_id":"14530","variant_slug":"lantern_courtyard","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"51060101","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A buyer is assumed to prevail after a seller conveyed the contracted-for property to someone else; what damages measure does the buyer recover?","review_truth":"A winning buyer''s land-sale damages use the property bargain, not the lost option or resale price: fair market value minus the contract price.","dominant_trap":"C","anchor_card":{"card_id":"REMEDY-OBJECT-51060101","title":"Remedy object before formula","signal":"A remedies call follows a dense formation or option setup.","student_move":"Name what the winner lost before calculating.","tiny_rule":"If buyer wins on the land bargain, use fair market value of the property minus contract price.","trap":"Students price the wrong object or treat specific performance''s unavailability as nominal-only.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONTRACTS-LANTERN-01","statement":"For a winning buyer after a seller''s breach of a land-sale contract, damages track the land bargain: fair market value of the contracted-for property at the relevant breach/performance point minus the contract price, plus proper incidental or consequential losses.","type":"rule","trigger":"The call says the buyer prevails and asks what the buyer may recover after the seller conveyed the property to someone else.","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 347; Davis v. Blast Properties Inc., No. 1:2021cv00218, Document 82 (D. Idaho May 3, 2023).","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONTRACTS-LANTERN-01","statement":"When the stem says to assume the buyer prevails, stop fighting option formation and lock the call: remedies only. Any answer pricing the paper option instead of the property bargain is answering the wrong layer.","type":"call_focus","trigger":"A fact-heavy option setup followed by an assumption of liability and a damages-only call.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"call_focus","target_skill":"Lock the assumed-liability call","prompt":"A stem says assume buyer prevails after a disputed option exercise. The call asks what she may recover. What issue is off the table?","answer":"Option formation or liability; the live issue is remedies."},{"drill_type":"gold_key","target_skill":"Land-bargain damages baseline","prompt":"Seller breaches a land-sale contract; contract price is $300,000 and market value is $340,000. What is the buyer''s basic expectation measure?","answer":"$40,000, before any proper incidental or consequential losses."},{"drill_type":"trap_spotting","target_skill":"Resale-price decoy","prompt":"Seller resells for $330,000 but market value at breach is $350,000 on a $300,000 contract. Which number is the benchmark?","answer":"The contract price remains the benchmark; market value supplies the other side of the comparison."}]}'),
  ('c334233d-63ff-5c09-b57e-e94407592f38', '14546_koine_tutor', 'CONTRACTS', 'Third-Party Rights', 'Third-Party Beneficiary Contracts', 'provider intent versus provider identification/awareness', 'Ruth made an oral promise to Timothy, supported by valid and sufficient consideration, that if Timothy arranged private Koine Greek lessons during the next four months, Ruth would pay the full tutoring fee to whoever taught him. Six weeks later, Timothy hired Lydia, a private tutor, on credit for $4,200. Lydia did not know of Ruth''s promise when she agreed to teach Timothy but learned of it shortly after the tutoring agreement was made.', 'Ruth made an oral promise to Timothy, supported by valid and sufficient consideration, that if Timothy arranged private Koine Greek lessons during the next four months, Ruth would pay the full tutoring fee to whoever taught him. Six weeks later, Timothy hired Lydia, a private tutor, on credit for $4,200. Lydia did not know of Ruth''s promise when she agreed to teach Timothy but learned of it shortly after the tutoring agreement was made.

Can Lydia enforce Ruth''s promise to Timothy?', 'Can Lydia enforce Ruth''s promise to Timothy?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ14546.md","source_question_id":null,"variant_slug":"koine_tutor","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"53070100","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"not_supplied","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"Ruth promised Timothy she would pay whoever taught him Koine Greek; Lydia later taught him on credit without knowing of the promise but learned of it later. Can Lydia enforce Ruth''s promise?","review_truth":"A future, unnamed provider can enforce a promise made for its benefit when the contracting parties intended payment to that provider, even if the provider learned of the promise only after contracting.","dominant_trap":"A","anchor_card":{"card_id":"CON-TPB-01","title":"Future provider beneficiary","signal":"pay whoever provides the item or service","student_move":"Separate provider-intent from provider-knowledge.","tiny_rule":"Intent creates beneficiary status; knowledge/reliance affects other issues.","trap":"unnamed/unaware provider","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONTRACTS-KOINE-TUTOR-01","statement":"A future provider can be an intended beneficiary even if unnamed and unaware at formation when the promise directs payment to whoever provides the goods or services.","type":"distinction","trigger":"promise says to pay whoever provides the thing or service","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts §§302, 304","last_minute_review":true},{"id":"GK-CONTRACTS-KOINE-TUTOR-02","statement":"The suretyship Statute of Frauds frame requires a promise to the creditor or obligee to answer for another''s duty; a promise made to the debtor to discharge that debtor''s bill is a different frame.","type":"distinction","trigger":"promise made to the person who will incur the bill, not to the provider","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts §§112, 123","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONTRACTS-KOINE-TUTOR-01","statement":"When the answer array gives two yes answers and two no answers, sort by rationale before outcome; a highlighted negative fact may answer only a neighboring issue.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"one no answer repeats unnamed/unaware facts","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"wrong_element","prompt":"A promise says ''pay whoever teaches Timothy.'' The tutor was unnamed and unaware when hired. Does that fact alone defeat intended-beneficiary status?","answer":"No. It points to a neighboring timing issue, not status."},{"drill_type":"trap_spotting","target_skill":"bait_doctrine","prompt":"A promise to pay a provider is made to the person who will hire the provider, not to the provider. Is the suretyship clause the first frame?","answer":"No. The promise-to-debtor frame defeats that trap."},{"drill_type":"answer_array","target_skill":"2x2 sorting","prompt":"Two answers say yes and two say no. What should be sorted first?","answer":"Sort by rationale before outcome."}]}'),
  ('7e8033d7-76c1-5621-914a-a854ab71a653', '16127_stewardship_bomb', 'CRIMINAL_LAW', 'Inchoate Crimes', 'Conspiracy and merger with the substantive offense (arson)', 'conspiracy merges with arson versus conspiracy stacks with arson under the non-merger rule and Wharton''s Rule scope', 'Two men were members of a Christian creation-care direct-action network. As a protest against the use of harmful agricultural chemicals in the local watershed, they planned to burn down an industrial chemical-processing plant that produced such chemicals. A few days later, they set off a bomb that burned the plant to the ground. The jurisdiction has a statute that extends the common law definition of arson to buildings other than dwellings.', 'Two men were members of a Christian creation-care direct-action network. As a protest against the use of harmful agricultural chemicals in the local watershed, they planned to burn down an industrial chemical-processing plant that produced such chemicals. A few days later, they set off a bomb that burned the plant to the ground. The jurisdiction has a statute that extends the common law definition of arson to buildings other than dwellings.

Of which of the following crimes may the men be properly convicted?', 'Of which of the following crimes may the men be properly convicted?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ16127.md","source_question_id":"16127","variant_slug":"stewardship_bomb","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"72010400","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium","deciding_phase":"CALL","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"Two members of a group agree to burn down a non-dwelling building to protest its operation, succeed in burning it, and the jurisdiction''s arson statute covers non-dwellings. What crimes can they be convicted of — both, one, or the other?","review_truth":"Conspiracy and the substantive offense are separate crimes; a defendant can be convicted of both for the same conduct unless Wharton''s Rule applies, and arson is not a Wharton''s Rule crime.","dominant_trap":"A","anchor_card":{"card_id":"RC-CRIMINAL-CONSPIRACY-MERGER-01","title":"Conspiracy and the substantive offense — no merger; Wharton''s Rule scope","signal":"Two or more persons agree to commit a substantive crime and succeed; the call is whether they can be convicted of both.","student_move":"Check Wharton''s Rule first. If the substantive crime is one that by definition requires two persons (bigamy, adultery, incest, dueling), conspiracy merges and only the substantive stands. Otherwise, both stack.","tiny_rule":"Conspiracy and the substantive offense are separate crimes; arson is not a Wharton''s Rule crime.","trap":"The merger myth is the dominant trap. Many students half-remember that ''conspiracy merges'' and walk into the but-not-both answer.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CRIMINAL-STEWARDSHIP-BOMB-01","statement":"Conspiracy does not merge with the substantive offense. Wharton''s Rule (the only true no-stack exception) applies only to crimes that by definition require two persons (bigamy, adultery, incest, dueling). Arson is not a Wharton''s Rule crime, so both conspiracy and arson stand.","type":"rule","trigger":"two-person group agrees to commit arson and succeeds; jurisdiction''s arson statute covers non-dwellings; call is concurrent conviction","authority":"Model Penal Code §§ 5.03, 221.1, 1.07(4); Iannelli v. United States, 420 U.S. 770 (1975)","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CRIMINAL-STEWARDSHIP-BOMB-01","statement":"When the call asks ''of which of the following crimes may the defendant be properly convicted,'' check whether both inchoate and substantive elements are present. If yes, both stack unless Wharton''s Rule applies.","type":"call_focus","trigger":"stem describes a group agreement plus a completed substantive crime; the call is concurrent conviction","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"missing-element test (conspiracy vs arson)","prompt":"Two men agree to burn down a warehouse and succeed. The jurisdiction''s arson statute covers non-dwellings. The defendant is charged with arson only. Is that correct?","answer":"No. Two persons agreed to commit arson (conspiracy) and the warehouse was burned (arson under the extended statute). Both stack; charging arson only is incomplete."},{"drill_type":"anchor","target_skill":"non-merger rule and Wharton''s Rule scope","prompt":"A defendant is charged with both conspiracy to commit arson and arson itself for the same conduct. Does the conspiracy merge into the arson?","answer":"No. Conspiracy does not merge with the substantive offense as a general rule. Wharton''s Rule (the only true no-stack exception) applies only to crimes that by definition require two persons (bigamy, adultery, incest, dueling). Arson is not a Wharton''s Rule crime, so both stand."},{"drill_type":"anchor","target_skill":"Wharton''s Rule scope","prompt":"Is bigamy a Wharton''s Rule crime? Is arson?","answer":"Bigamy is a Wharton''s Rule crime (it requires two persons by definition); arson is not (it can be committed by a single person). Conspiracy to commit bigamy merges; conspiracy to commit arson does not."}]}'),
  ('cecb4c45-a886-5e75-859d-7c07fef2fb86', '17018_youth_dodgeball_consent', 'TORTS', 'Privileges and defenses', 'Consent to contact-sport participation', 'yes because within scope versus yes because no intent', 'During a youth-group dodgeball game at church, a player threw a ball that struck another player in the shoulder while both were moving toward the center line to retrieve balls. The throw violated no safety rule and was a customary part of play. The struck player fell and sued the throwing player for battery.', 'During a youth-group dodgeball game at church, a player threw a ball that struck another player in the shoulder while both were moving toward the center line to retrieve balls. The throw violated no safety rule and was a customary part of play. The struck player fell and sued the throwing player for battery.

Can the throwing player rely on consent?', 'Can the throwing player rely on consent?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ17018.md","source_question_id":"17018","variant_slug":"youth_dodgeball_consent","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"64010400","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium","deciding_phase":"CALL","confidence":"HEURISTIC_STRUCTURAL","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"In a contact sport, a player was injured by a customary, in-bounds contact. Can the other player rely on the plaintiff''s implied consent to sport contacts as a defense to battery?","review_truth":"A participant in a contact sport impliedly consents to bodily contacts that are customary and within the ordinary rules of the game.","dominant_trap":"D","anchor_card":{"card_id":"RC-TORTS-SPORT-CONSENT-01","title":"Consent to contact-sport contacts","signal":"Player is injured during a contact sport by a customary, in-bounds contact.","student_move":"Cut the injury-defeats-consent myth and the absolute ''never'' overclaim; in a CLASH of two ''yes'' answers, pick the one that names consent to scope-of-play contacts.","tiny_rule":"A participant in a contact sport impliedly consents to bodily contacts that are customary and within the ordinary rules of the game.","trap":"The absolute ''never covers intentional contact'' sounds like a clean rule.","confidence":"HEURISTIC_STRUCTURAL"},"gold_keys":[],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-TORTS-DODGEBALL-CONSENT-01","statement":"When two ''yes'' choices remain in a consent-to-sport question, pick the one that names the actual defense — consent to contacts within the ordinary scope of the sport — rather than a missing-intent theory.","type":"call_focus","trigger":"one yes choice cites scope of play; the other yes choice cites lack of intent","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"absolute overclaim","prompt":"A choice in a consent-to-sport question says ''consent never covers intentional contact.'' Why is this cut?","answer":"''Never'' overclaims; contact sports involve intentional contacts within the scope of implied consent."},{"drill_type":"call_focus","target_skill":"two-yes CLASH","prompt":"Two choices say ''yes'' in a sport-consent battery question: one says ''yes, because no intent,'' and the other says ''yes, because within ordinary scope.'' Which do you pick and why?","answer":"Pick ''yes, because within ordinary scope'' — that names the actual defense of consent."},{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"stem-fact relation","prompt":"The stem says the contact was customary and within the rules, but the plaintiff was injured. Does injury defeat consent?","answer":"No. Injury alone does not defeat consent when the contact was within the ordinary scope of the sport."}]}'),
  ('e57f6742-af32-5fb1-81a9-415d118ba7ed', '17027_heritage_plow_fraud', 'CIVIL_PROCEDURE', 'Erie Doctrine and Choice of Law', 'State substantive burden of proof on a state-law claim', 'federal procedural rule versus substantive state right on the burden of proof for reliance', 'Mary, a heritage farmer in State X, agreed to buy a hand-forged breaking plow from Daniel, a blacksmith in State Y, for $18,000. Daniel wrote on the invoice that the plow was "high-carbon, work-hardened steel, hand-forged by a fourth-generation smith." Mary paid in full and took delivery. During her first day of spring tillage the moldboard bent and the share cracked, because the steel was ordinary mild stock. Mary sued Daniel in federal district court on the basis of diversity jurisdiction, pleading State X common-law fraud and seeking $18,000 in damages. State X substantive law requires the plaintiff to prove reliance on the defendant''s misrepresentation by clear and convincing evidence. Mary has moved in limine to have the federal court apply a federal preponderance standard to reliance, on the ground that federal procedure controls trial conduct.', 'Mary, a heritage farmer in State X, agreed to buy a hand-forged breaking plow from Daniel, a blacksmith in State Y, for $18,000. Daniel wrote on the invoice that the plow was "high-carbon, work-hardened steel, hand-forged by a fourth-generation smith." Mary paid in full and took delivery. During her first day of spring tillage the moldboard bent and the share cracked, because the steel was ordinary mild stock. Mary sued Daniel in federal district court on the basis of diversity jurisdiction, pleading State X common-law fraud and seeking $18,000 in damages. State X substantive law requires the plaintiff to prove reliance on the defendant''s misrepresentation by clear and convincing evidence. Mary has moved in limine to have the federal court apply a federal preponderance standard to reliance, on the ground that federal procedure controls trial conduct.

If the court rules correctly under Erie and its progeny, which burden of proof will be applied to reliance at trial?', 'If the court rules correctly under Erie and its progeny, which burden of proof will be applied to reliance at trial?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ17027.md","source_question_id":"17027","variant_slug":"heritage_plow_fraud","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"96060100","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium","deciding_phase":"CALL","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A diversity plaintiff sues on a state-law fraud claim, and the forum state requires clear-and-convincing evidence of reliance. The plaintiff argues that the federal court should apply a federal preponderance standard because burdens of proof are procedural. Which burden applies?","review_truth":"Under Erie, a federal court sitting in diversity applies the forum state''s burden of proof on a state-law claim element or defense, including the clear-and-convincing standard for reliance in a state-law fraud action.","dominant_trap":"A","anchor_card":{"card_id":"RC-CIVPRO-ERIE-BURDEN-01","title":"Erie and the burden of proof on a state-law claim","signal":"Diversity suit on a state-law claim; the state has attached a heightened burden to a claim element; the plaintiff argues a federal procedural rule should override.","student_move":"Cut the ''no burden'' misframe and the ''party preference'' misframe on the answer text. Then use the Gold Key to kill the ''all burdens are federal procedural'' trap.","tiny_rule":"Burden of proof on a state-law claim element follows the state right into federal court.","trap":"Federal-court setting cues the student to reach for a federal procedural rule; Erie''s substantive/procedural allocation is the deciding move.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CIVPRO-HERITAGE-PLOW-FRAUD-01","statement":"In a diversity case, the burden of proof on a state-law claim element or defense follows the substantive state right into federal court; federal procedural rules do not override that allocation.","type":"rule","trigger":"diversity suit on a state-law claim, plaintiff invokes federal procedural rule to override a state burden allocation","authority":"Erie Railroad Co. v. Tompkins, 304 U.S. 64 (1938); Guaranty Trust Co. v. York, 326 U.S. 99 (1945)","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CIVPRO-HERITAGE-PLOW-FRAUD-01","statement":"When a call asks which burden applies to a specific claim element in a diversity case, test each answer against the specific element and the specific body of law, not against abstract labels about burdens in general.","type":"call_focus","trigger":"four short procedural-posture labels in a diversity case; one is the state-burden answer","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"anchor","target_skill":"Erie substantive/procedural allocation of the burden of proof","prompt":"A diversity suit is filed on a state-law fraud claim; the forum state requires clear and convincing evidence of reliance. The plaintiff argues the federal preponderance standard controls. Which burden applies?","answer":"The forum-state clear-and-convincing standard applies because the burden is bound up with the substantive state-law claim."},{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"no-burden misframe","prompt":"Does the Due Process Clause eliminate the burden of proof on a state-law claim?","answer":"No. The Due Process Clause requires adequate procedures for the allocation; it does not eliminate the burden."},{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"Erie-as-party-preference misframe","prompt":"Is Erie a party-preference rule that lets the defendant pick the burden?","answer":"No. Erie requires the federal court to apply the state substantive right; it is not a party-preference rule."}]}'),
  ('8f260b6d-cf53-56aa-b1c2-daaa71dc32cc', '17111_parish_newsletter_republication', 'TORTS', 'Defamation', 'Republication and attribution defense', 'attribution as a defense to republication versus republication as itself a publication', 'Naomi is the lead Sunday school teacher at a local church school. Mary runs a Christian parenting newsletter that goes out weekly to several hundred subscribers in the area, most of them parents whose children attend the church school. A parent at the church school emailed Mary alleging that Naomi had been stealing from the classroom supply fund, a charge the parent could not substantiate and that was in fact false. Mary had no independent reason to believe the charge was true but, in her next newsletter, repeated the accusation under the heading, "A parent in our school community writes: this teacher has been stealing from the classroom supply fund." Naomi sues Mary for defamation.', 'Naomi is the lead Sunday school teacher at a local church school. Mary runs a Christian parenting newsletter that goes out weekly to several hundred subscribers in the area, most of them parents whose children attend the church school. A parent at the church school emailed Mary alleging that Naomi had been stealing from the classroom supply fund, a charge the parent could not substantiate and that was in fact false. Mary had no independent reason to believe the charge was true but, in her next newsletter, repeated the accusation under the heading, "A parent in our school community writes: this teacher has been stealing from the classroom supply fund." Naomi sues Mary for defamation.

Is Mary liable to Naomi as a publisher of the defamatory statement?', 'Is Mary liable to Naomi as a publisher of the defamatory statement?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ17111.md","source_question_id":"17111","variant_slug":"parish_newsletter_republication","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"62050401","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium","deciding_phase":"CALL","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"An editor receives a false accusation from a third party, has no independent reason to believe it, and republishes the accusation to a wider audience while attributing it to ''a parent.'' Is the editor a publisher of the defamatory statement?","review_truth":"One who repeats or republishes a defamatory statement is liable as a publisher even when the statement is attributed to someone else; attribution is not a defense to republication.","dominant_trap":"A","anchor_card":{"card_id":"RC-TORTS-REPUBLICATION-ATTRIBUTION-01","title":"Republication and attribution","signal":"An editor or host republishes a defamatory accusation to a wider audience under attribution to the original source.","student_move":"Cut the medium-based and joinder defenses on the answer text. Then use the Gold Key to kill the attribution-as-defense trap.","tiny_rule":"Attribution is not a defense to republication; the republisher is on the hook for the defamatory sting.","trap":"Saying ''a caller said'' or ''a parent said'' feels protective; it does not insulate the republisher.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-TORTS-PARISH-NEWSLETTER-REPUBLICATION-01","statement":"Attribution is not a defense to republication; repeating a defamatory charge to a new audience is itself a publication, and the republisher is liable as a publisher even when the source is named.","type":"rule","trigger":"republisher names the original source and asks whether attribution insulates the republisher","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 578","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-TORTS-PARISH-NEWSLETTER-REPUBLICATION-01","statement":"When the call asks whether a repeater is a publisher of a defamatory statement, test each named defense against the publication element specifically, not against the editor''s intent or the medium used.","type":"call_focus","trigger":"four answer choices that each name a different defense (medium, joinder, attribution, correct republication theory)","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"anchor","target_skill":"republication treated as original publication for liability","prompt":"An editor receives a false accusation from a third party, has no independent reason to believe it, and republishes the accusation to a wider audience attributing it to ''a parent.'' Is the editor a publisher of the defamatory statement?","answer":"Yes. Republication is itself a publication, and attribution is not a defense."},{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"medium-based exception to publication","prompt":"Does a newsletter distribution to several hundred subscribers qualify as publication for defamation?","answer":"Yes. Publication is communication to a third party, regardless of medium."},{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"joinder as a precondition to liability","prompt":"Is a defamation plaintiff required to sue the original speaker before suing a republisher?","answer":"No. A plaintiff may sue the original speaker, the republisher, or both; joinder is a procedural option, not a precondition."}]}'),
  ('8ec43644-ff05-57c7-8602-406eb7f2e3a5', '17114_farmstand_excessive_fine', 'CONSTITUTIONAL_LAW', 'Due Process', 'Incorporation of the Excessive Fines Clause through the Fourteenth Amendment', 'federal-only incorporation theory versus incorporation of the Excessive Fines Clause through the Fourteenth Amendment', 'Daniel runs a small roadside farm stand in a rural county, selling eggs, honey, and produce that he raises on his own land. After a routine inspection, the county health department cited him for a minor technical violation of the county food-code — failing to display a state-issued refrigeration placard on a single small cooler — and the county brought a forfeiture action seeking $400,000. The maximum civil fine under the county ordinance for this kind of citation, if proven, is $250. Daniel concedes the violation but argues that a $400,000 forfeiture for a minor food-code placard violation is grossly excessive under the Excessive Fines Clause of the Eighth Amendment, applied to the county through the Fourteenth Amendment Due Process Clause. The county moves to dismiss on the ground that the Excessive Fines Clause constrains only the federal government.', 'Daniel runs a small roadside farm stand in a rural county, selling eggs, honey, and produce that he raises on his own land. After a routine inspection, the county health department cited him for a minor technical violation of the county food-code — failing to display a state-issued refrigeration placard on a single small cooler — and the county brought a forfeiture action seeking $400,000. The maximum civil fine under the county ordinance for this kind of citation, if proven, is $250. Daniel concedes the violation but argues that a $400,000 forfeiture for a minor food-code placard violation is grossly excessive under the Excessive Fines Clause of the Eighth Amendment, applied to the county through the Fourteenth Amendment Due Process Clause. The county moves to dismiss on the ground that the Excessive Fines Clause constrains only the federal government.

What is the best incorporation route for Daniel''s Excessive Fines claim?', 'What is the best incorporation route for Daniel''s Excessive Fines claim?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ17114.md","source_question_id":"17114","variant_slug":"farmstand_excessive_fine","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"44040201","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium","deciding_phase":"CALL","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A county seeks a $400,000 forfeiture for a minor food-code placard violation. The defendant argues the forfeiture is grossly excessive under the Eighth Amendment Excessive Fines Clause, applied to the county through the Fourteenth Amendment. The county says the Eighth Amendment constrains only the federal government. What is the best incorporation route for the claim?","review_truth":"The Excessive Fines Clause applies to the states (and to local governments acting under state authority) through the Fourteenth Amendment Due Process Clause.","dominant_trap":"A","anchor_card":{"card_id":"RC-CONLAW-INCORPORATION-EXCESSIVE-FINES-01","title":"Incorporation of the Excessive Fines Clause","signal":"A state or local government actor seeks a grossly disproportionate forfeiture for a minor violation; the defendant invokes the Eighth Amendment Excessive Fines Clause through the Fourteenth Amendment; the government moves to dismiss on a federal-only theory.","student_move":"Cut the procedural-due-process misdirection and the automatic-win overclaim on the answer text. Then use the Gold Key to kill the federal-only trap.","tiny_rule":"The Excessive Fines Clause is incorporated against the states (and their political subdivisions) through the Fourteenth Amendment Due Process Clause.","trap":"Some Bill of Rights provisions were historically treated as federal-only; the Excessive Fines Clause is not one of them. Timbs v. Indiana (2019) squarely held that it is incorporated.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONLAW-FARMSTAND-EXCESSIVE-FINE-01","statement":"The Excessive Fines Clause of the Eighth Amendment is incorporated against the states (and their political subdivisions) through the Fourteenth Amendment Due Process Clause; the federal-only incorporation theory fails.","type":"constitutional_principle","trigger":"state or local government actor seeks a grossly disproportionate forfeiture; defendant invokes the Eighth Amendment Excessive Fines Clause through the Fourteenth Amendment; government moves to dismiss on a federal-only theory","authority":"Timbs v. Indiana, 586 U.S. 365 (2019)","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONLAW-FARMSTAND-EXCESSIVE-FINE-01","statement":"When the call asks for the best incorporation route, test each named theory against the incorporation question specifically, not against the merits of the proportionality test.","type":"call_focus","trigger":"four answer choices that each name a different constitutional theory (federal-only, automatic-win, correct incorporation, procedural due process)","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"anchor","target_skill":"incorporation of the Excessive Fines Clause","prompt":"A county seeks a $400,000 forfeiture for a minor food-code placard violation. The defendant argues the forfeiture is grossly excessive under the Eighth Amendment Excessive Fines Clause, applied to the county through the Fourteenth Amendment. The county says the Eighth Amendment constrains only the federal government. What is the best incorporation route for the claim?","answer":"The Excessive Fines Clause is incorporated against the states (and their political subdivisions) through the Fourteenth Amendment Due Process Clause. Timbs v. Indiana (2019)."},{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"substantive vs procedural due process","prompt":"Is a claim that a forfeiture is grossly excessive a substantive or procedural due process claim?","answer":"Substantive. The Excessive Fines Clause is a substantive constraint on the size of the forfeiture, not a procedural constraint on the procedure for imposing it."},{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"automatic-win overclaim","prompt":"Does the Excessive Fines Clause invalidate every forfeiture that exceeds the maximum civil fine?","answer":"No. The Excessive Fines Clause applies a gross-disproportionality test, not a flat rule."}]}'),
  ('75b7b294-2c8d-536d-b9f6-ab0213889edb', '17117_day_school_playground_jmol', 'CIVIL_PROCEDURE', 'JMOL and Trial Motions', 'Rule 50(b) renewed JMOL — preservation of grounds from Rule 50(a)', 'unlimited preservation of Rule 50(b) grounds versus renewal-not-invention rule', 'Mary runs a small Christian day school. She hired Paul, a playground-equipment contractor, to build a new playground on the school grounds for $32,000. Six weeks after completion, a five-year-old child broke her arm when a climber panel gave way. Mary sued Paul in federal district court on diversity grounds, asserting negligence and breach of warranty and seeking $40,000 in damages. Before the case was submitted to the jury, Paul moved for judgment as a matter of law under Rule 50(a) on the single ground that the $40,000 damages figure was not supported by the evidence. The court denied the motion. The jury returned a verdict for Mary and awarded $40,000. After the verdict, Paul filed a renewed motion for judgment as a matter of law under Rule 50(b) — his first time challenging causation — arguing that Mary had failed to introduce legally sufficient evidence that the climber''s failure caused the child''s broken arm.', 'Mary runs a small Christian day school. She hired Paul, a playground-equipment contractor, to build a new playground on the school grounds for $32,000. Six weeks after completion, a five-year-old child broke her arm when a climber panel gave way. Mary sued Paul in federal district court on diversity grounds, asserting negligence and breach of warranty and seeking $40,000 in damages. Before the case was submitted to the jury, Paul moved for judgment as a matter of law under Rule 50(a) on the single ground that the $40,000 damages figure was not supported by the evidence. The court denied the motion. The jury returned a verdict for Mary and awarded $40,000. After the verdict, Paul filed a renewed motion for judgment as a matter of law under Rule 50(b) — his first time challenging causation — arguing that Mary had failed to introduce legally sufficient evidence that the climber''s failure caused the child''s broken arm.

May the court grant the renewed Rule 50(b) motion on the new causation ground?', 'May the court grant the renewed Rule 50(b) motion on the new causation ground?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ17117.md","source_question_id":"17117","variant_slug":"day_school_playground_jmol","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"92080202","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium","deciding_phase":"CALL","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A defendant moved for Rule 50(a) JMOL pre-verdict only on damages; the court denied. The jury found for the plaintiff. After the verdict, the defendant files a Rule 50(b) motion on a new causation ground never raised pre-verdict. May the court grant it?","review_truth":"A Rule 50(b) motion renews, rather than invents, a Rule 50(a) legal-sufficiency challenge; the post-verdict motion cannot introduce a new ground not fairly raised in the pre-verdict Rule 50(a) motion.","dominant_trap":"A","anchor_card":{"card_id":"RC-CIVPRO-RULE50-RENEWAL-01","title":"Rule 50(b) is a renewal, not a new motion","signal":"A defendant files a Rule 50(b) motion on a new ground not raised in the pre-verdict Rule 50(a) motion.","student_move":"Cut the plaintiff-only availability and importance-priority misframes on the answer text. Then use the Gold Key to kill the unlimited-preservation trap.","tiny_rule":"Rule 50(b) renews, rather than invents, a Rule 50(a) legal-sufficiency challenge.","trap":"Filing a Rule 50(a) motion does not preserve every possible Rule 50(b) ground; the post-verdict motion is limited to grounds fairly raised pre-verdict.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CIVPRO-DAY-SCHOOL-PLAYGROUND-JMOL-01","statement":"A Rule 50(b) motion renews, rather than invents, a Rule 50(a) legal-sufficiency challenge; the post-verdict motion is limited to grounds fairly raised in the pre-verdict Rule 50(a) motion.","type":"rule","trigger":"defendant files a Rule 50(b) motion on a new ground not raised in the pre-verdict Rule 50(a) motion","authority":"Unitherm Food Systems, Inc. v. Swift-Eckrich, Inc., 546 U.S. 394, 404 (2006); Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 50(b)","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CIVPRO-DAY-SCHOOL-PLAYGROUND-JMOL-01","statement":"When the call asks whether the court may grant a renewed Rule 50(b) motion on a new ground, test the new ground against the pre-verdict 50(a) record, not against the doctrinal strength of the new ground.","type":"call_focus","trigger":"four answer choices that each name a different procedural theory (unlimited preservation, importance priority, plaintiff-only availability, correct preservation)","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"anchor","target_skill":"Rule 50(b) renewal-not-invention rule","prompt":"A defendant moved for Rule 50(a) JMOL pre-verdict only on damages; the court denied. The jury found for the plaintiff. After the verdict, the defendant files a Rule 50(b) motion on a new causation ground never raised pre-verdict. May the court grant it?","answer":"No. Rule 50(b) renews, rather than invents, a Rule 50(a) legal-sufficiency challenge; the new ground is not properly before the court."},{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"party-availability of Rule 50","prompt":"Is Rule 50(b) available only to plaintiffs after an adverse jury verdict?","answer":"No. Rule 50 is available to any party; a defendant after an adverse jury verdict is the paradigmatic Rule 50(b) movant."},{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"importance-priority fallacy","prompt":"If a new ground is doctrinally more important than the ground the defendant did raise pre-verdict, may the court grant renewed JMOL on the new ground?","answer":"No. Importance does not substitute for preservation; Rule 50(b) is a renewal motion, not an importance-priority vehicle."}]}'),
  ('569c2d1e-5516-5102-b862-8cd7fa34fd99', '17163_permit_fee_ledger', 'CONSTITUTIONAL_LAW', 'Due Process', 'State Deprivation', 'every property loss vs negligence-rule', 'Paul, a pastor at a small church, applied in person for a state concealed-carry permit at a county clerk''s office. He paid the $75 application fee in cash to the clerk, Daniel, who placed the cash envelope in an interoffice pouch to be delivered to the finance office for deposit. While moving the pouch between offices, Daniel accidentally dropped the envelope behind a filing cabinet and never recovered it. Paul''s permit was not issued, and the $75 was never refunded. Paul sued Daniel and the county in federal court under 42 U.S.C. § 1983, alleging a Due Process Clause violation based only on Daniel''s negligence and the loss of the $75 fee.', 'Paul, a pastor at a small church, applied in person for a state concealed-carry permit at a county clerk''s office. He paid the $75 application fee in cash to the clerk, Daniel, who placed the cash envelope in an interoffice pouch to be delivered to the finance office for deposit. While moving the pouch between offices, Daniel accidentally dropped the envelope behind a filing cabinet and never recovered it. Paul''s permit was not issued, and the $75 was never refunded. Paul sued Daniel and the county in federal court under 42 U.S.C. § 1983, alleging a Due Process Clause violation based only on Daniel''s negligence and the loss of the $75 fee.

What is the best due process answer?', 'What is the best due process answer?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ17163.md","source_question_id":"17163","variant_slug":"permit_fee_ledger","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"44040203","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"HARD_STRUCTURAL","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A state employee accidentally loses a small permit fee while moving it between offices. The applicant sues under the Due Process Clause, alleging only negligence and a small property loss. Is that a constitutional violation?","review_truth":"Mere negligent loss of property by a state official is not a substantive due process violation; the clause targets deliberate or reckless deprivation, not carelessness.","dominant_trap":"D","anchor_card":{"card_id":"RC-CONSTITUTIONAL-LAW-NEGLIGENT-LOSS-01","title":"Negligent loss by a state actor is not a due process violation","signal":"A due process claim under § 1983, sole allegation is a state employee''s negligence, the loss is small property.","student_move":"Cut the overclaim (every loss is a violation), the wrong-frame level of review (strict scrutiny), and the wrong-party trap (clerk''s hearing). The negligence rule is the residual answer.","tiny_rule":"Negligence is not a constitutional deprivation; the Due Process Clause targets deliberate or reckless state action.","trap":"The overclaim that every property loss is a due process violation is the dominant trap; the proper remedy is a state tort claim, not § 1983.","confidence":"HARD_STRUCTURAL"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONSTITUTIONAL-LAW-PERMIT-FEE-LEDGER-01","statement":"Mere negligent loss of property by a state official is not a substantive due process violation; the clause targets deliberate or reckless deprivations, not carelessness.","type":"rule","trigger":"due process claim against a state actor, sole allegation is negligence","authority":"County of Sacramento v. Lewis, 523 U.S. 833 (1998); Parratt v. Taylor, 451 U.S. 527 (1981); Hudson v. Palmer, 468 U.S. 517 (1984)","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONSTITUTIONAL-LAW-PERMIT-FEE-LEDGER-01","statement":"On a constitutional item, the government actor stays a government actor; never swap in a church or private actor, and never reframe the call to the defendant''s process.","type":"call_focus","trigger":"constitutional claim where a Christian flavor could tempt a private-actor or church-actor rewrite","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"overclaim recognition","prompt":"A state employee accidentally loses a citizen''s $50 application fee. The citizen sues under the Due Process Clause. Is that a constitutional violation?","answer":"No. Negligent loss by a state official is not a constitutional deprivation; the proper remedy is a state tort claim."},{"drill_type":"call_focus","target_skill":"wrong-party check","prompt":"A due process claim by a citizen against a state clerk. The stem also describes a workplace dispute between the clerk and her supervisor. Which is the call about?","answer":"The call is about the citizen''s due process rights, not the clerk''s employment process."},{"drill_type":"anchor","target_skill":"negligent-loss carve-out","prompt":"What is the controlling rule when a state actor negligently loses property?","answer":"Negligent loss of property by a state official is not a substantive due process violation; only deliberate or reckless deprivations are actionable under § 1983."}]}'),
  ('8fad7ad4-f26c-5bef-8c08-33f6b0a41244', '17179_potluck_lot_rear_end', 'CIVIL_PROCEDURE', 'Joinder', 'Counterclaims and crossclaims (Rule 13)', 'co-party label (crossclaim) versus opposing-party label (counterclaim) versus non-party label (impleader)', 'Mary and Paul, both headed separately to a Saturday-evening church potluck in their own cars, were involved in a rear-end collision in the church parking lot. Hannah, a passenger in Mary''s car, sued both Mary and Paul for the same accident. Mary now asserts that Paul was negligent and must reimburse Mary for any amount Mary pays Hannah.', 'Mary and Paul, both headed separately to a Saturday-evening church potluck in their own cars, were involved in a rear-end collision in the church parking lot. Hannah, a passenger in Mary''s car, sued both Mary and Paul for the same accident. Mary now asserts that Paul was negligent and must reimburse Mary for any amount Mary pays Hannah.

What is Mary''s claim against Paul?', 'What is Mary''s claim against Paul?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ17179.md","source_question_id":"17179","variant_slug":"potluck_lot_rear_end","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"95070301","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A plaintiff sues two drivers for the same accident. One driver asserts an indemnification/contribution claim against the co-driver. What procedural label fits the second driver''s claim?","review_truth":"A claim by one co-defendant against another arising from the same transaction or occurrence is a crossclaim under Rule 13(g), not a counterclaim (which runs against an opposing party).","dominant_trap":"C","anchor_card":{"card_id":"RC-CIVPRO-COUNTERCROSS-01","title":"Counterclaim vs Crossclaim vs Impleader","signal":"One defendant in a multi-defendant suit asserts a claim against another defendant; the caption shows the same side of the ''v.''.","student_move":"Check the caption''s party alignment first. Same-side defendants = co-parties = crossclaim. Opposite sides = counterclaim. Non-party = impleader (Rule 14).","tiny_rule":"Crossclaim = co-party; counterclaim = opposing party.","trap":"Counterclaim is the everyday word; students reach for it on any defendant-asserted claim.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CIVPRO-POTLUCK-LOT-01","statement":"A crossclaim runs against a co-party; a counterclaim runs against an opposing party. Co-defendants on the same side of the caption are co-parties, so the labeled claim between them is a crossclaim under FRCP 13(g).","type":"distinction","trigger":"one defendant asserts a claim against a co-defendant in the same suit","authority":"Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 13(a), 13(g)","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CIVPRO-POTLUCK-LOT-01","statement":"When the second defendant is already named in the caption, do not reach for impleader — impleader exists precisely to pull a *non-party* in.","type":"trap_spotting","trigger":"defendant names a non-plaintiff, but the non-plaintiff is already a co-defendant","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"caption party-alignment check","prompt":"Hannah sues Mary and Paul. Mary asserts a claim against Paul. What procedural label?","answer":"Crossclaim (Rule 13(g)) — Mary and Paul are co-defendants (same side of the caption)."},{"drill_type":"anchor","target_skill":"counterclaim vs crossclaim distinction","prompt":"Mary is sued by Hannah. Mary wants to assert a separate claim against Hannah unrelated to the car wreck. What label?","answer":"Counterclaim (Rule 13(a)) — Mary and Hannah are opposing parties."},{"drill_type":"trap_spotting","target_skill":"Rule 14 impleader precondition","prompt":"Mary is sued by Hannah alone. Mary wants to pull in a non-party mechanic who she says caused the wreck. What device?","answer":"Impleader (Rule 14) — the mechanic is a non-party being brought in."}]}'),
  ('9b002cf9-8745-5e73-b718-e8207de90615', '17225_midnight_utility_fraud', 'CRIMINAL_LAW', 'Property Crimes', 'Burglary — breaking element (constructive breaking by fraud)', 'physical-force-only breaking versus constructive breaking (fraud, threat, trick)', 'At midnight, Paul knocked on Mary''s door and falsely said he was from the electric company and needed to check the meter for a wiring fault. Mary, a private piano teacher who lives alone, let him in. Paul entered intending to steal a pearl necklace and a small wooden Nativity set from Mary''s bedroom.', 'At midnight, Paul knocked on Mary''s door and falsely said he was from the electric company and needed to check the meter for a wiring fault. Mary, a private piano teacher who lives alone, let him in. Paul entered intending to steal a pearl necklace and a small wooden Nativity set from Mary''s bedroom.

Can the breaking element of common-law burglary be satisfied?', 'Can the breaking element of common-law burglary be satisfied?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ17225.md","source_question_id":"17225","variant_slug":"midnight_utility_fraud","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"73031000","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A person knocks at midnight and lies about being a utility worker to gain entry to a home, intending to steal something inside. Does this satisfy the breaking element of common-law burglary?","review_truth":"At common law, the breaking element of burglary is satisfied by constructive breaking, including entry obtained by fraud, when the other elements (dwelling, of another, at night, with intent to commit a felony at the time of entry) are present.","dominant_trap":"A","anchor_card":{"card_id":"RC-CRIMINAL-BURGLARY-BREAKING-01","title":"Breaking element — constructive breaking by fraud","signal":"Entry obtained by a false-pretenses pretext (utility worker, repair person, fellow church member) at night, with intent to commit a felony at the moment of entry.","student_move":"Cut physical-damage and voluntary-opening rationales with the Gold Key; cut intent-after-entry with the timing rule.","tiny_rule":"Constructive breaking includes fraud-induced entry; consent procured by fraud is not effective consent.","trap":"The everyday conception of ''breaking'' fuses it with physical force; the common-law rule is broader.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CRIMINAL-MIDNIGHT-UTILITY-01","statement":"At common law, the breaking element of burglary is satisfied by constructive breaking — entry obtained by fraud, threat, or trick — even without physical damage to the dwelling.","type":"rule","trigger":"false-pretenses entry with intent to commit a felony at the moment of entry","authority":"Common-law burglary doctrine; see LaFave, Substantive Criminal Law § 21.1(c); Model Penal Code § 221.1","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CRIMINAL-MIDNIGHT-UTILITY-01","statement":"On a burglary question, the entry need not be by force — fraud, threat, or trick all count.","type":"trap_spotting","trigger":"entry obtained by deception, threat, or trick at night with felonious intent","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"anchor","target_skill":"constructive breaking by fraud","prompt":"A knock on the door at midnight; the entrant says he is a utility worker and asks to inspect a meter; the homeowner lets him in; he entered with intent to steal jewelry. Is the breaking element of common-law burglary satisfied?","answer":"Yes. Fraud-induced entry is constructive breaking; the breaking element is satisfied at common law."},{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"intent-timing cut","prompt":"A thief enters a home at night by fraud with intent to commit a felony, but is startled and flees before taking anything. Has a burglary occurred?","answer":"Yes. Burglary is complete on entry with felonious intent; the actual completion of the intended felony is not required."},{"drill_type":"trap_spotting","target_skill":"consent vitiated by fraud","prompt":"A homeowner opens the door after being told a false story by the entrant. Does the homeowner''s act of opening defeat the breaking element?","answer":"No. Consent procured by fraud is not effective consent for the constructive-breaking analysis."}]}'),
  ('05b64982-6473-5e84-8c2c-8951e4151a58', '17236_wellness_visit_revocation', 'TORTS', 'Privileges and defenses', 'Consent (revocation before contact)', 'operative-consent defense versus categorical-form defense versus categorical-no-defense', 'Hannah, a private violin teacher, signed a written consent form for a minor cosmetic injection during a visit to a private wellness clinic. Before the injection began, she clearly told the doctor, Daniel, "I changed my mind. Do not inject me." Daniel said she was being silly and intentionally gave the injection anyway. Hannah sues Daniel for battery.', 'Hannah, a private violin teacher, signed a written consent form for a minor cosmetic injection during a visit to a private wellness clinic. Before the injection began, she clearly told the doctor, Daniel, "I changed my mind. Do not inject me." Daniel said she was being silly and intentionally gave the injection anyway. Hannah sues Daniel for battery.

Can Daniel rely on the signed consent form?', 'Can Daniel rely on the signed consent form?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ17236.md","source_question_id":"17236","variant_slug":"wellness_visit_revocation","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"64010400","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A patient signs a consent form for a minor cosmetic injection, then clearly revokes consent before the contact. The doctor proceeds anyway. Is the signed form a defense to the battery claim?","review_truth":"Consent to an intentional touching can be revoked at any time before the contact occurs; an intentional contact after a clear revocation is battery, regardless of any earlier signed consent form.","dominant_trap":"A","anchor_card":{"card_id":"RC-TORTS-CONSENT-REVOCATION-01","title":"Consent to medical contact — revocation before contact","signal":"A patient signs a consent form, then clearly revokes consent before the contact. The doctor proceeds anyway. Battery suit follows.","student_move":"Cut minor-injection with the Silver Key; cut written-consent-cannot-be-withdrawn with the Gold Key; cut consent-never-a-defense with the foundational rule.","tiny_rule":"A signed consent is a present license; the patient can revoke it at any time before the contact.","trap":"The signed form looks like a contract; consent is not a contract — it is a present license.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-TORTS-WELLNESS-VISIT-01","statement":"A signed consent form is a present license that the patient may revoke at any time before the contact occurs, regardless of the form. Revocation does not require written notice or any particular magic words.","type":"rule","trigger":"clear pre-contact revocation of a signed medical/cosmetic consent","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 892A; Mohr v. Williams, 95 Minn. 261 (1905)","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-TORTS-WELLNESS-VISIT-01","statement":"Battery does not require serious injury; an unauthorized harmful or offensive contact is enough — even a minor cosmetic injection.","type":"trap_spotting","trigger":"battery claim with a minor-magnitude contact","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"anchor","target_skill":"consent revocation before contact","prompt":"A patient signs a consent form for a minor procedure, then clearly says, ''I changed my mind. Do not proceed.'' The doctor proceeds anyway. Battery?","answer":"Yes. Consent is a present license revocable before the contact; the signed form is not a defense after a clear revocation."},{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"battery magnitude cut","prompt":"A doctor performs a non-emergency cosmetic injection on a fully conscious patient who has clearly refused. The injection causes no lasting injury. Battery?","answer":"Yes. Battery requires only unauthorized harmful or offensive contact; the contact need not cause serious injury."},{"drill_type":"foundational","target_skill":"consent as a defense","prompt":"Is consent a defense to battery?","answer":"Yes, when valid and unrevoked. The defense fails if consent was not given, was withdrawn, or was exceeded."}]}'),
  ('8f54caef-2561-54b3-b5d7-f2165507bea7', '17264_prayer_parcel_trip', 'TORTS', 'Negligence', 'Duty — particular standard of care (unknown trespasser injured by natural condition)', 'limited duty (unknown trespasser + natural condition) versus invitee inspection duty versus warn-every-natural-condition versus strict liability', 'A hiker, Stephen, ignored posted no-entry signs and crossed onto a remote wooded parcel that Mary, a private landowner who sometimes hosts prayer retreats for friends on the parcel, had not opened to the public. Mary did not know Stephen was on the parcel. Stephen tripped over a naturally fallen branch on an unmaintained footpath and was injured.', 'A hiker, Stephen, ignored posted no-entry signs and crossed onto a remote wooded parcel that Mary, a private landowner who sometimes hosts prayer retreats for friends on the parcel, had not opened to the public. Mary did not know Stephen was on the parcel. Stephen tripped over a naturally fallen branch on an unmaintained footpath and was injured.

Under traditional entrant categories, what duty did Mary likely owe Stephen?', 'Under traditional entrant categories, what duty did Mary likely owe Stephen?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ17264.md","source_question_id":"17264","variant_slug":"prayer_parcel_trip","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"61020202","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A hiker ignores no-entry signs and crosses onto a remote private parcel without the owner''s knowledge and is injured by a natural condition. What duty does the traditional entrant-category regime impose on the owner?","review_truth":"Under traditional entrant categories, an unknown trespasser injured by a natural condition on remote land is owed only limited duties — not the invitee inspection duty, not a duty to warn of every natural condition, and not strict liability.","dominant_trap":"B","anchor_card":{"card_id":"RC-TORTS-ENTRANT-CATEGORIES-01","title":"Traditional entrant categories — unknown trespasser / natural condition","signal":"An entrant ignores posted no-entry signs, crosses onto a remote parcel, and is injured by a naturally fallen branch on an unmaintained footpath. The landowner did not know of the entrant.","student_move":"Cut full-invitee-inspection-duty and warn-every-natural-condition with the Gold Key; cut strict-liability with the Silver Key.","tiny_rule":"Unknown trespasser + natural condition on remote land = limited duty.","trap":"The student fuses any on-land injury with the highest duty; the categories track the owner''s invitation and the hazard type.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-TORTS-PRAYER-PARCEL-01","statement":"Under traditional entrant categories, the duty owed to an unknown trespasser is narrow and is not the invitee inspection duty. The categories track what the landowner *knows* and what the hazard *is*; an unknown trespasser injured by a natural condition receives only the floor-level duty (refrain from willful and wanton conduct).","type":"rule","trigger":"unknown trespasser injured by a natural condition on remote land, with no-entry signs posted","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 333–339; Prosser & Keeton, Torts § 57–60","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-TORTS-PRAYER-PARCEL-01","statement":"Warning signs are evidence of reasonable care, not a basis for strict liability. Premises liability to trespassers is fault-based, not strict.","type":"trap_spotting","trigger":"warning-signs-are-evidence-of-care inference in a premises-liability claim","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"anchor","target_skill":"entrant-category classification","prompt":"An entrant ignores posted no-entry signs and crosses onto a remote private parcel. What is the entrant category under traditional rules?","answer":"Trespasser. No-entry signs are affirmative evidence the owner did not invite the public."},{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"natural-condition rule","prompt":"An unknown trespasser is injured by a naturally fallen branch on an unmaintained footpath. What duty does the traditional rule impose?","answer":"Limited duty. The traditional duty to unknown trespassers is narrow and does not include a duty to warn of every natural condition on remote land."},{"drill_type":"trap_spotting","target_skill":"warning-signs-are-evidence-of-care","prompt":"A landowner has posted no-entry signs. A trespasser is injured. Does strict liability apply?","answer":"No. Premises liability to trespassers is fault-based; warning signs are evidence of reasonable care, not a basis for strict liability."}]}'),
  ('e01dd6df-aa50-58ff-98fe-83706af58b05', '17289_foundation_paneling', 'TORTS', 'Economic torts', 'Fraud', 'every defect vs element-by-element', 'Paul listed his small house in a Texas suburb for sale. Before any showings, an engineer, Daniel, had inspected the foundation and told Paul there were serious termite damage in the floor joists. Paul covered the damaged joists with fresh oak flooring and refinished the surface. When Hannah, a local teacher at a Christian school, came to look at the house, Paul told her, "There are no known structural problems." Hannah relied on the statement, paid full price, and discovered the damage after moving in.', 'Paul listed his small house in a Texas suburb for sale. Before any showings, an engineer, Daniel, had inspected the foundation and told Paul there were serious termite damage in the floor joists. Paul covered the damaged joists with fresh oak flooring and refinished the surface. When Hannah, a local teacher at a Christian school, came to look at the house, Paul told her, "There are no known structural problems." Hannah relied on the statement, paid full price, and discovered the damage after moving in.

Is Hannah likely to establish fraud?', 'Is Hannah likely to establish fraud?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ17289.md","source_question_id":"17289","variant_slug":"foundation_paneling","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"62050200","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"HARD_STRUCTURAL","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A home seller conceals a known structural defect by covering it with new flooring, then makes an affirmative misstatement to a buyer who relies on the statement and pays full price. Is the buyer likely to establish fraud?","review_truth":"Concealing a known material defect and then making an affirmative misstatement to induce the transaction can establish fraud when the buyer justifiably relies and suffers damages.","dominant_trap":"C","anchor_card":{"card_id":"RC-TORTS-FRAUD-CONCEALMENT-MISSTATEMENT-01","title":"Concealment plus misstatement is fraud","signal":"A seller knew of a material defect, covered it up, and made an affirmative misstatement to a buyer who relied and suffered damages.","student_move":"Cut the overclaim (every defect is fraud), the wrong-element economic-loss bar, and the mislabel that fraud can never be concealment. The fraud elements are satisfied.","tiny_rule":"Active concealment plus an affirmative misstatement is actionable fraud.","trap":"The overclaim that every property defect is fraud is the dominant trap; the mislabel that fraud can never be concealment is the second trap.","confidence":"HARD_STRUCTURAL"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-TORTS-FOUNDATION-PANELING-01","statement":"Active concealment of a known material defect, combined with an affirmative misstatement, is actionable fraud; it is not the case that fraud can never be based on concealment, nor that every property defect is fraud.","type":"rule","trigger":"seller''s known material defect + active concealment + affirmative misstatement + buyer''s reliance","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Torts §§ 525, 527, 549","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-TORTS-FOUNDATION-PANELING-01","statement":"Economic damages are exactly what fraud is designed to compensate; do not import the negligence-bar-on-pure-economic-loss rule into fraud.","type":"call_focus","trigger":"fraud call that mentions the buyer''s economic damages","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"overclaim recognition","prompt":"A home seller knowingly concealed a structural defect and told the buyer there were no structural problems. Is every property defect fraud?","answer":"No. Fraud requires the specific elements; not every property defect satisfies all of them."},{"drill_type":"call_focus","target_skill":"wrong-element import","prompt":"A fraud claim where the buyer paid full price. Does the negligence bar on pure economic loss apply?","answer":"No. Fraud is a recognized business tort and economic damages are exactly what it compensates."},{"drill_type":"anchor","target_skill":"concealment plus misstatement","prompt":"A seller conceals a known material defect AND makes an affirmative misstatement to the buyer. Is the buyer likely to establish fraud?","answer":"Yes. Active concealment plus an affirmative misstatement is actionable fraud. Restatement §§ 525, 527, 549."}]}'),
  ('ba9b32ea-cc1b-58a5-974d-cf5d467b142f', '17406_oak_choir_stall', 'CIVIL_PROCEDURE', 'Pleadings, Rule 12 Motions, and Amendments', 'Rule 12(h)(2) preservation of failure-to-state-a-claim defense', 'broad all-12(b)-waived rule versus precise Rule 12(h)(2) preservation of 12(b)(6)', 'Mary contracted in writing with Daniel, a custom woodworker, to fabricate and deliver fifteen hand-carved oak choir stalls for the sanctuary of a small church her family attends. The contract fixed the price at $11,800, with half due on signing and half on delivery. Mary paid the deposit; Daniel delivered the stalls; Mary now sues Daniel in federal court for breach of contract, alleging the stalls fail to match the contract''s specifications. Daniel files a timely answer that admits the contract''s existence and Mary''s payment of the deposit, asserts several affirmative defenses, and nowhere asserts a Rule 12(b)(6) failure-to-state-a-claim defense. After the pleadings close, Daniel moves for judgment on the pleadings under Rule 12(c), arguing that Mary''s complaint still fails to state a claim upon which relief can be granted.', 'Mary contracted in writing with Daniel, a custom woodworker, to fabricate and deliver fifteen hand-carved oak choir stalls for the sanctuary of a small church her family attends. The contract fixed the price at $11,800, with half due on signing and half on delivery. Mary paid the deposit; Daniel delivered the stalls; Mary now sues Daniel in federal court for breach of contract, alleging the stalls fail to match the contract''s specifications. Daniel files a timely answer that admits the contract''s existence and Mary''s payment of the deposit, asserts several affirmative defenses, and nowhere asserts a Rule 12(b)(6) failure-to-state-a-claim defense. After the pleadings close, Daniel moves for judgment on the pleadings under Rule 12(c), arguing that Mary''s complaint still fails to state a claim upon which relief can be granted.

Is Daniel''s failure-to-state-a-claim defense automatically waived by his omission from the answer?', 'Is Daniel''s failure-to-state-a-claim defense automatically waived by his omission from the answer?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ17406.md","source_question_id":"17406","variant_slug":"oak_choir_stall","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"92080104","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium","deciding_phase":"CALL","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A defendant answers a complaint without raising a Rule 12(b)(6) defense, then moves for judgment on the pleadings under Rule 12(c) after the pleadings close, arguing the complaint still fails to state a claim. Has the defense been waived by omission from the answer?","review_truth":"A defendant''s failure to include a Rule 12(b)(6) defense in the answer does not waive it; under Rule 12(h)(2) the defense is preserved for any later pleading, motion, or at trial.","dominant_trap":"D","anchor_card":{"card_id":"RC-CIVPRO-RULE12H-PRESERVATION-01","title":"Rule 12(h) — the (h)(1) vs (h)(2) waiver split","signal":"A defendant omits a Rule 12 defense from the answer and later tries to raise it; the call is whether the defense is preserved.","student_move":"Map the precise defense to the precise Rule 12(h) subsection. 12(b)(2)–(5) and 12(b)(7) are waived under (h)(1); 12(b)(6) and the related defenses are preserved under (h)(2). Subject-matter jurisdiction is a separate non-waivable category under (h)(3).","tiny_rule":"Rule 12(h)(2) preserves 12(b)(6) for any later stage, including a Rule 12(c) motion after the pleadings close.","trap":"The dominant trap treats 12(b)(6) like 12(b)(2) and reads the rule as ''include it or lose it.''","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CIVPRO-OAK-CHOIR-STALL-01","statement":"Rule 12(h)(2) preserves the Rule 12(b)(6) failure-to-state-a-claim defense for any later pleading, motion, or at trial — including a Rule 12(c) motion for judgment on the pleadings after the pleadings close.","type":"rule","trigger":"defendant answers without 12(b)(6) and later moves for judgment on the pleadings under Rule 12(c)","authority":"Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 12(h)(2)","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CIVPRO-OAK-CHOIR-STALL-01","statement":"When the call is ''is the defense waived by omission,'' map the precise defense to the precise Rule 12(h) subsection before saying ''waived.'' Rule 12(h)(1) waives 12(b)(2)–(5) and 12(b)(7); Rule 12(h)(2) preserves 12(b)(6) and a small list of other defenses; Rule 12(h)(3) addresses 12(b)(1) (subject-matter jurisdiction), which is never waived.","type":"cut","trigger":"defendant omits a Rule 12(b) defense from the answer; call is whether the defense is preserved","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"rule_text","target_skill":"subsection mapping","prompt":"A defendant omits a 12(b)(2) personal-jurisdiction defense from the answer and later tries to raise it in a Rule 12(c) motion. Is the defense preserved?","answer":"No. Rule 12(h)(1) waives 12(b)(2)–(5) and 12(b)(7) if not raised in the answer or a pre-answer amendment."},{"drill_type":"rule_text","target_skill":"subsection mapping","prompt":"A defendant omits a 12(b)(6) failure-to-state-a-claim defense from the answer and later tries to raise it at trial. Is the defense preserved?","answer":"Yes. Rule 12(h)(2) preserves 12(b)(6) for any later stage, including at trial."},{"drill_type":"conflation_test","target_skill":"12(b)(1) vs 12(b)(6)","prompt":"A defendant never raises 12(b)(1) subject-matter jurisdiction and the case proceeds to judgment. Can the defendant raise 12(b)(1) on appeal?","answer":"Yes. Rule 12(h)(3) provides that subject-matter jurisdiction can be raised at any time, even on appeal, and is never waived."}]}'),
  ('5527368b-d49e-58d7-9bc3-2044c49b7a4d', '17412_seminary_faculty_apartment', 'CIVIL_PROCEDURE', 'Subject Matter Jurisdiction', 'Diversity - Individual Citizenship (Domicile)', 'residence (Massachusetts) versus domicile (Texas)', 'Lydia grew up in Texas, where she keeps her voter registration, driver''s license, and permanent home. She accepted a one-semester teaching appointment at a small private Christian college in Massachusetts and rents a faculty apartment in Massachusetts for the nine-month term. She plans to return to Texas when the appointment ends. She sues a Massachusetts citizen in federal court for $90,000 under state law for breach of her employment contract.', 'Lydia grew up in Texas, where she keeps her voter registration, driver''s license, and permanent home. She accepted a one-semester teaching appointment at a small private Christian college in Massachusetts and rents a faculty apartment in Massachusetts for the nine-month term. She plans to return to Texas when the appointment ends. She sues a Massachusetts citizen in federal court for $90,000 under state law for breach of her employment contract.

For diversity purposes, what is Lydia''s state citizenship?', 'For diversity purposes, what is Lydia''s state citizenship?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ17412.md","source_question_id":"17412","variant_slug":"seminary_faculty_apartment","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"94010100","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium","deciding_phase":"CALL","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A person keeps her permanent home, voter registration, and driver''s license in State A, accepts a fixed-term nine-month teaching appointment in State B, and rents an apartment in State B. She plans to return to State A when the appointment ends. For diversity purposes, what is her state citizenship?","review_truth":"A natural person''s state citizenship for diversity purposes is the state of domicile, which requires both physical presence and intent to remain indefinitely; a fixed-term temporary stay does not change domicile.","dominant_trap":"B","anchor_card":{"card_id":"RC-CIVPRO-DOMICILE-DIVERSITY-01","title":"Diversity citizenship = domicile (physical presence + intent to remain indefinitely)","signal":"A person has a clear permanent-home state, accepts a fixed-term temporary stay in another state, and plans to return.","student_move":"Translate the citizenship question to a domicile question. Test the two-element definition (physical presence + intent to remain indefinitely). Apply the sticky-domicile rule: a person keeps her existing domicile until acquiring a new one.","tiny_rule":"Domicile requires physical presence AND intent to remain indefinitely. A fixed-term temporary stay does not change domicile.","trap":"The dominant trap treats residence as if it were domicile. A related trap invents dual citizenship or a no-state edge case.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CIVPRO-SEMINARY-FACULTY-APARTMENT-01","statement":"A natural person''s state citizenship for diversity purposes is the state of domicile, defined as physical presence coupled with intent to remain indefinitely. A fixed-term temporary stay does not change domicile; a person keeps her existing domicile until she acquires a new one in a different state.","type":"rule","trigger":"person with clear permanent-home state + fixed-term temporary stay in another state + clear intent to return","authority":"28 U.S.C. § 1332(a)(1); Stifel v. Hopkins, 477 U.S. 407 (1986)","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CIVPRO-SEMINARY-FACULTY-APARTMENT-01","statement":"When the call is ''for diversity purposes, what is her citizenship,'' translate the question to ''where is her domicile'' and test the two-element definition (physical presence + intent to remain indefinitely) before picking.","type":"call_focus","trigger":"call asks for state citizenship in a diversity-jurisdiction fact pattern","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"two_element_test","target_skill":"domicile elements","prompt":"A person physically present in State B for a fixed-term job, with permanent home, voter registration, and driver''s license in State A, plans to return to State A. For diversity purposes, what is her state citizenship?","answer":"State A. Domicile requires both physical presence and intent to remain indefinitely. The intent element is missing; State A domicile persists."},{"drill_type":"sticky_domicile","target_skill":"domicile persistence","prompt":"A person moves out of her State A home to take a job in State B. Before forming the intent to remain in State B indefinitely, what is her domicile?","answer":"State A. A person keeps her existing domicile until acquiring a new one by satisfying both elements in a different state."},{"drill_type":"call_translation","target_skill":"citizenship = domicile","prompt":"When the call is ''for diversity purposes, what is her citizenship,'' what is the operative legal question?","answer":"Where is her domicile. Citizenship for diversity purposes is the state of domicile."}]}'),
  ('64a93868-d801-5727-8145-6b00b45dad15', '17443_devotional_auto_renew', 'CIVIL_PROCEDURE', 'Joinder, Class Actions, and Intervention', 'Rule 23(a) certification prerequisites', 'adequacy subsumed by personal injury versus adequacy as a separate Rule 23(a) prong', 'Two hundred customers of a private devotional-publisher online subscription service sue the publisher over a single auto-renewal-billing policy. The named plaintiff subscribed to the service, claims the same statutory consumer-protection injury as the rest of the customer group, and asks the court to certify a class. The publisher argues that a class cannot be certified until the court analyzes whether the named plaintiff and class counsel can fairly protect the interests of absent class members.', 'Two hundred customers of a private devotional-publisher online subscription service sue the publisher over a single auto-renewal-billing policy. The named plaintiff subscribed to the service, claims the same statutory consumer-protection injury as the rest of the customer group, and asks the court to certify a class. The publisher argues that a class cannot be certified until the court analyzes whether the named plaintiff and class counsel can fairly protect the interests of absent class members.

Which statement best describes the certification inquiry?', 'Which statement best describes the certification inquiry?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ17443.md","source_question_id":"17443","variant_slug":"devotional_auto_renew","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"95070303","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium","deciding_phase":"CALL","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A named plaintiff and a large group of similarly situated customers all suffered the same statutory injury from a single corporate policy. The court is asked to certify a class. What must the court find before certifying the class?","review_truth":"A class action must satisfy all four Rule 23(a) prerequisites — numerosity, commonality, typicality, and adequacy of representation — and the action must also fit within one of the Rule 23(b) categories.","dominant_trap":"A","anchor_card":{"card_id":"RC-CIVPRO-RULE23A-PRONGS-01","title":"Rule 23(a) — all four prongs required; adequacy is separate from typicality and personal injury","signal":"A class-certification inquiry where the defendant argues the court must analyze whether the named plaintiff and class counsel can fairly protect absent class members.","student_move":"Count the prongs. Rule 23(a) has four: numerosity, commonality, typicality, adequacy. All four must be found. Adequacy is a separate prong, distinct from typicality and from the named plaintiff''s personal injury. Then check Rule 23(b) for a category.","tiny_rule":"Rule 23(a) requires all four prongs; adequacy is separate from typicality and personal injury. Rule 23(b) further requires one of three categories.","trap":"The dominant trap treats adequacy as if it were subsumed by the named plaintiff''s personal injury. The two-prong-miss trap counts only numerosity and commonality.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CIVPRO-DEVOTIONAL-AUTO-RENEW-01","statement":"Adequacy of representation is a separate Rule 23(a) prong, distinct from typicality and from the named plaintiff''s personal injury. The named plaintiff and class counsel must fairly protect the interests of absent class members; that is a separate inquiry the court must find in addition to numerosity, commonality, and typicality.","type":"rule","trigger":"defendant argues that the court must analyze whether the named plaintiff and class counsel can fairly protect absent class members; call is which statement best describes the certification inquiry","authority":"Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 23(a)(4)","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CIVPRO-DEVOTIONAL-AUTO-RENEW-01","statement":"When the call is ''which statement best describes the certification inquiry,'' count the prongs. Rule 23(a) has four: numerosity, commonality, typicality, adequacy. All four must be found. Rule 23(b) further requires one of three categories.","type":"cut","trigger":"open-ended ''which statement best describes the certification inquiry'' call","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"prong_counting","target_skill":"Rule 23(a) prongs","prompt":"How many Rule 23(a) prongs must the court find before certifying a class?","answer":"Four: numerosity, commonality, typicality, adequacy."},{"drill_type":"adequacy_separation","target_skill":"adequacy as separate prong","prompt":"Is adequacy of representation subsumed by typicality or by the named plaintiff''s personal injury?","answer":"No. Adequacy is a separate Rule 23(a)(4) prong, distinct from typicality and from the named plaintiff''s personal injury. The named plaintiff and class counsel must fairly protect the interests of absent class members."},{"drill_type":"rule_23b","target_skill":"Rule 23(b) categories","prompt":"After finding all four Rule 23(a) prongs, what else must the court find?","answer":"The action must also fit within one of three Rule 23(b) categories: (b)(1) prejudice from separate actions; (b)(2) injunctive or declaratory relief; (b)(3) common questions predominate and a class action is superior."}]}'),
  ('c98535cb-ca57-5834-9c62-5d4f468e30da', '17521_christian_wedding_cake', 'CONTRACTS', 'Formation of Contracts', 'Acceptance by mail — mailbox rule and the ''properly dispatched'' element', 'mailed-acceptance versus properly-dispatched-acceptance as the testable element of the mailbox rule', 'Paul, a custom cake baker in Tulsa, Oklahoma, mailed Mary, a bride in Norman, Oklahoma, a signed offer to bake and decorate her June wedding cake for $1,200, with delivery to the reception venue. Mary immediately mailed back her acceptance, which was properly addressed to Paul''s bakery and bore the correct amount of postage. The post office lost the letter and Paul never received it. Paul now claims no contract was formed.', 'Paul, a custom cake baker in Tulsa, Oklahoma, mailed Mary, a bride in Norman, Oklahoma, a signed offer to bake and decorate her June wedding cake for $1,200, with delivery to the reception venue. Mary immediately mailed back her acceptance, which was properly addressed to Paul''s bakery and bore the correct amount of postage. The post office lost the letter and Paul never received it. Paul now claims no contract was formed.

Is there a contract?', 'Is there a contract?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ17521.md","source_question_id":"17521","variant_slug":"christian_wedding_cake","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"55020102","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium","deciding_phase":"CALL","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"Offeror mails an offer; offeree mails back an acceptance that is properly addressed and bears the correct postage; the post office loses the letter and the offeror never receives it. Was a contract formed?","review_truth":"An acceptance sent by mail is effective on dispatch only if the acceptance is properly addressed and bears the correct postage; the post office''s subsequent loss of the letter does not undo a properly dispatched acceptance.","dominant_trap":"A","anchor_card":{"card_id":"RC-CONTRACTS-MAILBOX-RULE-01","title":"Mailbox rule — proper dispatch and postal loss","signal":"A mailed acceptance is lost or delayed; the question is whether a contract was formed.","student_move":"Apply the Gold Key: the mailbox rule applies only when the acceptance is properly dispatched (correct address + correct postage). If the acceptance was properly dispatched, the rule applies; the post office''s loss is irrelevant (Silver Key). If the acceptance was misaddressed or unstamped, the rule does not apply.","tiny_rule":"Mailbox rule = acceptance effective on dispatch ONLY when properly dispatched (correct address + correct postage).","trap":"The dominant trap is ''Yes, because the offeree mailed the acceptance'' — it omits the properly dispatched element. The secondary trap is ''No, because the mail was lost'' — it reverses the mailbox rule''s allocation of postal risk.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONTRACTS-CHRISTIAN-WEDDING-CAKE-01","statement":"The mailbox rule makes acceptance effective on dispatch ONLY when the acceptance is properly dispatched (correct address + correct postage); a mailed-but-misaddressed or unstamped acceptance is NOT effective on dispatch.","type":"rule","trigger":"stem mentions a mailed acceptance AND the question tests whether the mailbox rule applies","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 63; sub-element on proper dispatch","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONTRACTS-CHRISTIAN-WEDDING-CAKE-01","statement":"The post office''s loss of a properly dispatched acceptance is irrelevant; the offeror bears the risk of postal loss.","type":"cut","trigger":"stem describes a mailed acceptance that was lost or delayed in transit","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"postal-loss risk-allocation","prompt":"On June 1, A mails B an offer to sell a car. On June 3, B mails A a properly addressed acceptance with correct postage; the post office loses the letter. Is there a contract?","answer":"Yes. The mailbox rule applies because B''s acceptance was properly dispatched; the post office''s loss is irrelevant and the offeror bears the risk of postal loss."},{"drill_type":"anchor","target_skill":"properly dispatched sub-element","prompt":"On June 1, A mails B an offer. On June 3, B mails A an acceptance, but the letter bears no postage. The letter is delivered to A on June 5. Is there a contract?","answer":"No. The acceptance was not properly dispatched (no postage); the mailbox rule does not apply, and the acceptance is not effective until received."},{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"issue frame (formation vs excuse)","prompt":"On June 1, A mails B an offer. On June 3, B mails a properly dispatched acceptance, but it is lost in transit. A claims she is ''excused from performance'' because she never received the acceptance. Is A''s claim correct?","answer":"No. The question is formation, not excuse. The mailbox rule applies; the acceptance is effective on dispatch; A is not excused from performance on these facts."}]}'),
  ('8758e95e-c961-562b-8af1-51a8a8effc6d', '17574_christian_podcast_school_board', 'CONSTITUTIONAL_LAW', 'First Amendment - Freedom of Speech', 'Prior Restraints', 'prior restraint versus red-herring First Amendment doctrines (commercial speech, not the institutional press) on a prior-restraint item with ad-revenue and medium red herrings', 'A state court in Texas entered a pre-trial order in a defamation case brought by a local school-board member named Paul. The order bars Mary, a Christian podcaster in Houston who publishes a weekly commentary podcast about local civic affairs, from publishing any future podcast episodes about Paul unless Paul first reviews and approves the episode. Mary''s podcast earns revenue from third-party ads.', 'A state court in Texas entered a pre-trial order in a defamation case brought by a local school-board member named Paul. The order bars Mary, a Christian podcaster in Houston who publishes a weekly commentary podcast about local civic affairs, from publishing any future podcast episodes about Paul unless Paul first reviews and approves the episode. Mary''s podcast earns revenue from third-party ads.

What First Amendment doctrine is most directly implicated?', 'What First Amendment doctrine is most directly implicated?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ17574.md","source_question_id":"17574","variant_slug":"christian_podcast_school_board","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"44040502","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium","deciding_phase":"CLASH","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A government court order entered before trial in a defamation case requires a private speaker to obtain a government official''s advance approval before publishing any future speech about that official; the speaker''s medium is a podcast and the podcast earns ad revenue. What First Amendment doctrine is most directly implicated?","review_truth":"A court order that requires a speaker to obtain advance approval from a government official before publication is a prior restraint on speech and is presumptively unconstitutional, regardless of the speaker''s medium or whether the speech generates ad revenue.","dominant_trap":"C","anchor_card":{"card_id":"RC-CONSTITUTIONAL-LAW-PRIOR-RESTRAINT-01","title":"Prior restraint — advance approval orders and red-herring doctrines","signal":"A government court order entered before trial in a defamation case requires a private speaker to obtain advance approval from a government official (or a private party under a government order) before publishing any future speech about that official.","student_move":"Apply the Gold Key: any advance-approval requirement imposed by a government order is a prior restraint. Do not be lured by red-herring facts (medium, ad revenue) toward the commercial-speech or ''not the institutional press'' doctrines. Apply Silver Key #1 to cut the commercial-speech answer. Apply Silver Key #2 to cut the ''not the institutional press'' answer.","tiny_rule":"A court order requiring advance approval for speech is a prior restraint, regardless of medium or revenue model.","trap":"The dominant trap is the commercial-speech answer (the ad-revenue fact is salient). The secondary trap is the ''not the institutional press'' answer (the medium is salient). The weakest trap is the rational-basis answer (level-of-scrutiny mismatch).","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONSTITUTIONAL-LAW-CHRISTIAN-PODCAST-01","statement":"A court order that requires a speaker to obtain advance approval from a government official (or a private party acting under a government order) before publication is a prior restraint, regardless of the speaker''s medium or whether the speech generates ad revenue.","type":"constitutional_principle","trigger":"stem describes a court order requiring advance approval for speech AND the speech generates ad revenue","authority":"Near v. Minnesota, 283 U.S. 697 (1931); New York Times Co. v. United States, 403 U.S. 713 (1971)","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONSTITUTIONAL-LAW-CHRISTIAN-PODCAST-01","statement":"Ad revenue does NOT convert political or civic commentary about public officials into commercial speech; commercial speech is speech that does no more than propose a commercial transaction, not political or civic commentary that happens to be monetized.","type":"cut","trigger":"stem describes a speaker who earns ad revenue and is speaking about a public official","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true},{"id":"SK-CONSTITUTIONAL-LAW-CHRISTIAN-PODCAST-02","statement":"The First Amendment protects all speakers, not just institutional newspapers; the medium (podcast, blog, newsletter) does not determine whether the prior-restraint doctrine applies.","type":"trap_spotting","trigger":"stem describes a non-newspaper speaker (blogger, podcaster, YouTuber, newsletter writer) facing a court order","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"anchor","target_skill":"prior-restraint doctrine","prompt":"A federal court enters a pre-trial order in a defamation case requiring a YouTuber to obtain the plaintiff''s advance approval before publishing any future videos about the plaintiff. The YouTuber''s channel earns ad revenue. What First Amendment doctrine is most directly implicated?","answer":"Prior restraint. The order requires advance approval for speech, regardless of medium or revenue model. Near v. Minnesota, 283 U.S. 697 (1931); New York Times Co. v. United States, 403 U.S. 713 (1971)."},{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"commercial-speech red herring","prompt":"A speaker publishes a podcast about a city council member; the podcast earns ad revenue. Is this commercial speech?","answer":"No. Commercial speech is speech that does no more than propose a commercial transaction. Political or civic commentary about public officials is fully protected by the First Amendment, regardless of monetization."},{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"scope-of-speech principle","prompt":"A state court order requires a Substack writer to obtain advance approval from a mayor before publishing future posts about the mayor. The writer argues that Substack is not a newspaper and therefore no First Amendment doctrine applies. Is the writer''s argument correct?","answer":"No. The First Amendment protects all speakers, not just institutional newspapers. The medium (podcast, blog, newsletter) does not determine whether the prior-restraint doctrine applies."}]}'),
  ('fe924d20-d98b-5e66-b27e-309a2c7cc631', '17583_bookshop_securities_followup', 'CIVIL_PROCEDURE', 'Claim and Issue Preclusion', 'Preclusion Law Source (federal-question judgments)', 'Erie-fallacy vs. federal-question preclusion source', 'Mary, the former owner of a chain of Christian bookshops, sold her business to Paul in a stock-for-assets transaction. Mary later sued Paul in federal court alleging violations of the federal securities laws and lost after a final judgment on the merits. She has now filed a second federal action against Paul arising from the same business sale, asserting a different federal statutory claim based on the same underlying transaction. Paul''s lawyer has asked the court to decide the preclusive effect of the earlier federal securities judgment.', 'Mary, the former owner of a chain of Christian bookshops, sold her business to Paul in a stock-for-assets transaction. Mary later sued Paul in federal court alleging violations of the federal securities laws and lost after a final judgment on the merits. She has now filed a second federal action against Paul arising from the same business sale, asserting a different federal statutory claim based on the same underlying transaction. Paul''s lawyer has asked the court to decide the preclusive effect of the earlier federal securities judgment.

Which law generally governs the preclusive effect of the earlier federal securities judgment?', 'Which law generally governs the preclusive effect of the earlier federal securities judgment?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ17583.md","source_question_id":"17583","variant_slug":"bookshop_securities_followup","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"97100300","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A plaintiff lost a federal-question case in federal court after a final judgment on the merits, then filed a second federal action against the same defendant on a different federal claim arising from the same transaction. Which body of preclusion law governs the earlier federal judgment''s preclusive effect?","review_truth":"The preclusive effect of a prior federal-question judgment is governed by federal common law.","dominant_trap":"B","anchor_card":{"card_id":"RC-CIVPRO-FED-QUESTION-PRECLUSION-01","title":"Federal-question preclusion source","signal":"The prior judgment was a federal-question judgment; the second action is also federal; the call asks which preclusion law governs.","student_move":"Cut wrong-frame answers (plaintiff preference, same-judge). Apply the Gold Key: federal-question judgments get federal common-law preclusion.","tiny_rule":"Source of the prior judgment controls: federal-question → federal common law; state-law → rendering state preclusion law.","trap":"The Erie-fallacy answer (''all preclusion is state law'') imports Erie into a federal-question case.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CIVPRO-BOOKSHOP-SECURITIES-FOLLOWUP-01","statement":"The preclusion law that governs a federal-court judgment tracks the source of the substantive claim that produced the judgment: federal-question judgments get federal common-law preclusion; state-law judgments get the preclusion law of the rendering state.","type":"rule","trigger":"prior judgment was a federal-question judgment; second action is also federal; call asks which preclusion law governs","authority":"Semtek International Inc. v. Lockheed Martin Corp., 531 U.S. 497 (2001)","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CIVPRO-BOOKSHOP-SECURITIES-FOLLOWUP-01","statement":"When the call asks which preclusion law governs the earlier judgment, ask first: what was the source of the prior judgment — federal question or state law? Then match: federal question → federal common law; state law → rendering state preclusion law. The Erie posture of either case is a separate question.","type":"call_focus","trigger":"call asks ''which preclusion law governs the earlier judgment''","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"anchor","target_skill":"federal-question preclusion source","prompt":"A federal-question judgment is entered in federal court. The defendant is later sued in federal court on a different federal claim arising from the same transaction. Which preclusion law governs the earlier judgment?","answer":"Federal common law. The preclusive effect of a federal-question judgment is governed by federal common law, not by state preclusion law."},{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"wrong-frame cut","prompt":"Does the plaintiff''s choice of forum or party preference control which preclusion law applies?","answer":"No. Preclusion law is fixed by the source of the prior judgment, not by the plaintiff''s preference."},{"drill_type":"clash","target_skill":"Erie-fallacy distinction","prompt":"A student argues that Erie requires the federal court to apply state preclusion law. Why is the student wrong?","answer":"Erie governs the substantive content of a federal court''s decision in diversity on a state-law claim; it does not control the preclusive effect of a federal-question judgment."}]}'),
  ('3c033739-9421-531a-a737-4db5d0cc2a9d', '17630_blown_bible_orchard', 'TORTS', 'Trespass to Land', 'Qualified privilege to retrieve a chattel; damages from entry', 'causation split between spill-caused and removal-caused damage', 'Daniel, a Christian truck driver, was hauling a box of Bibles in the open back of his pickup truck. A sudden gust of wind blew a Bible out of the truck and onto the adjoining apple orchard owned by Paul, where it came to rest among some young saplings. Daniel entered Paul''s orchard to retrieve the Bible, and in doing so, he trampled a row of saplings and broke several low branches. Hauling Bibles is not an abnormally dangerous activity, and the Bible was not dislodged because of any fault on Daniel''s part. Paul has now asserted a claim against Daniel for trespass to land.', 'Daniel, a Christian truck driver, was hauling a box of Bibles in the open back of his pickup truck. A sudden gust of wind blew a Bible out of the truck and onto the adjoining apple orchard owned by Paul, where it came to rest among some young saplings. Daniel entered Paul''s orchard to retrieve the Bible, and in doing so, he trampled a row of saplings and broke several low branches. Hauling Bibles is not an abnormally dangerous activity, and the Bible was not dislodged because of any fault on Daniel''s part. Paul has now asserted a claim against Daniel for trespass to land.

If the court finds for Paul, which of the following is the correct measure of damages?', 'If the court finds for Paul, which of the following is the correct measure of damages?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ17630.md","source_question_id":"17630","variant_slug":"blown_bible_orchard","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"64010201","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A box falls off a vehicle onto a landowner''s property through no fault of the driver; the driver enters the land to retrieve it and damages crops in doing so. The hauling activity is not abnormally dangerous. What damages can the landowner recover for trespass to land?","review_truth":"One who enters another''s land to retrieve a chattel that got there through no fault of his own has a qualified privilege to enter, but he must compensate the landowner for actual damage caused by the entry and removal.","dominant_trap":"B","anchor_card":{"card_id":"RC-TORTS-CHATTEL-RETRIEVAL-01","title":"Chattel retrieval — qualified privilege + damages measure","signal":"A chattel lands on a landowner''s land through no fault of the actor; the actor enters to retrieve; damage from entry; hauling not abnormally dangerous.","student_move":"Cut under-compensation and full-privilege misreads. Apply the Gold Key: spill-caused damage is not recoverable; removal-caused damage is.","tiny_rule":"Qualified privilege protects the entry, not the damage the entry itself causes.","trap":"Treating the spill as the entry-caused event and stacking all downstream damage onto the actor (the spill-causation overreach).","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-TORTS-BLOWN-BIBLE-ORCHARD-01","statement":"When a chattel lands on a landowner''s land through no fault of the actor, the actor has a qualified privilege to enter to retrieve the chattel, but the privilege protects only the entry — it does not shield the actor from liability for actual damage caused by the entry and removal.","type":"rule","trigger":"chattel on landowner''s land; actor enters to retrieve; damage from entry; hauling not abnormally dangerous; no fault","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Torts §§ 77, 79","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-TORTS-BLOWN-BIBLE-ORCHARD-01","statement":"When a chattel-retrieval case has both spill-caused and removal-caused damage, split the causation: spill-caused damage is not recoverable absent an abnormally dangerous activity; removal-caused damage is recoverable because the entry is a volitional act.","type":"call_focus","trigger":"stem distinguishes between spill-caused and removal-caused damage","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"anchor","target_skill":"qualified privilege to enter for chattel retrieval","prompt":"A gust of wind blows a chattel off a truck onto a landowner''s land. The trucker enters the land to retrieve the chattel and damages crops in doing so. Hauling is not abnormally dangerous. The trucker had no fault. What damages can the landowner recover for trespass to land?","answer":"Only the damages caused by the trucker''s removal of the chattel from the landowner''s land. Spill-caused damage is not recoverable absent an abnormally dangerous activity; removal-caused damage is recoverable because the entry is a volitional act."},{"drill_type":"clash","target_skill":"causation split","prompt":"Distinguish between spill-caused damage and removal-caused damage in a chattel-retrieval case.","answer":"Spill-caused damage is not recoverable absent an abnormally dangerous activity, negligence, or a volitional act. Removal-caused damage is recoverable because the entry is a volitional act; the qualified privilege protects the entry only."},{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"under-compensation cut","prompt":"Is nominal damages the right measure when a privileged entry caused actual damage to crops?","answer":"No. The privilege protects the entry, not the damage the entry itself causes. The actor is liable for actual damage."}]}'),
  ('44e03394-9b47-5e82-b81b-c1ebab087b76', '17680_ministry_housing_permit', 'CONSTITUTIONAL_LAW', 'Equal Protection', 'Rational basis with bite (animus toward a group)', 'animus-to-tier-escalation vs. rational-basis-with-bite', 'Mary, a Christian minister, applied to the town of Cedar Bend for a routine zoning permit to open a small transitional housing ministry for women recovering from addiction. The town planning board held a public hearing, voted, and denied the permit. In the hearing record, board members stated that they wanted to keep "that kind of Christian group" out of the neighborhood and identified no safety, zoning, fiscal, or land-use reason for the denial. Mary has sued in federal court, alleging that the denial violates the Equal Protection Clause.', 'Mary, a Christian minister, applied to the town of Cedar Bend for a routine zoning permit to open a small transitional housing ministry for women recovering from addiction. The town planning board held a public hearing, voted, and denied the permit. In the hearing record, board members stated that they wanted to keep "that kind of Christian group" out of the neighborhood and identified no safety, zoning, fiscal, or land-use reason for the denial. Mary has sued in federal court, alleging that the denial violates the Equal Protection Clause.

Which of the following is the strongest Equal Protection argument on Mary''s behalf?', 'Which of the following is the strongest Equal Protection argument on Mary''s behalf?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ17680.md","source_question_id":"17680","variant_slug":"ministry_housing_permit","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"44040305","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A government actor denies a routine permit to a disfavored group, gives no legitimate interest at the hearing, and instead says the town wants to ''keep those people'' out. What is the strongest Equal Protection argument against the denial?","review_truth":"Even under rational basis review, a law based only on bare animus toward an unpopular group lacks a legitimate government interest.","dominant_trap":"B","anchor_card":{"card_id":"RC-CONLAW-RATIONAL-BASIS-WITH-BITE-01","title":"Rational basis with bite — animus toward a group","signal":"Government actor denies a routine permit; animus on the face of the hearing record; no legitimate interest articulated.","student_move":"Cut tiered-absolute and colloquialism answers. Apply the Gold Key: animus is not a legitimate interest; the discriminator can lose at rational basis without strict scrutiny being triggered.","tiny_rule":"Animus toward a group is not a legitimate government interest, even under rational basis review.","trap":"Reaching for a tier-escalation argument (strict scrutiny) that does not exist.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONLAW-MINISTRY-HOUSING-PERMIT-01","statement":"Even under rational basis review, a government action based only on bare animus toward an unpopular group lacks a legitimate government interest; the discriminator can lose at rational basis without the court elevating the level of scrutiny.","type":"rule","trigger":"government actor denies a routine permit; animus on the face of the hearing record; no legitimate interest articulated; group is not a suspect or quasi-suspect class","authority":"Cleburne v. Cleburne Living Center, 473 U.S. 432 (1985); U.S. Department of Agriculture v. Moreno, 413 U.S. 528 (1973); Romer v. Evans, 517 U.S. 620 (1996)","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONLAW-MINISTRY-HOUSING-PERMIT-01","statement":"When the call asks for the strongest argument, look for the doctrinal move that wins on the actual record (here, animus on the face of the hearing record) rather than the move that escalates the level of scrutiny (strict scrutiny does not apply because the group is not a suspect class).","type":"call_focus","trigger":"open ''strongest argument'' call on a permit-denial record with animus and no legitimate interest","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"anchor","target_skill":"rational basis with bite","prompt":"A government actor denies a routine permit to a disfavored group; the actor cites only a desire to ''keep those people'' out; no legitimate interest is articulated. What is the strongest Equal Protection argument?","answer":"The denial can fail rational basis review because it rests only on bare animus toward the group. Animus is not a legitimate government interest, even under deferential review. The discriminator can lose at rational basis without strict scrutiny being triggered."},{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"tier-escalation cut","prompt":"Does animus toward a group automatically trigger strict scrutiny?","answer":"No. Rational-basis-with-bite cases apply rational basis review but conclude that animus is not a legitimate interest. The group can lose at rational basis without strict scrutiny being triggered."},{"drill_type":"clash","target_skill":"tiered-absolute vs. legitimate interest","prompt":"Does rational basis review require no review at all?","answer":"No. Rational basis review is deferential but still requires a legitimate government interest. Animus is not a legitimate interest."}]}'),
  ('0360e5b9-da14-5d72-8331-76ce3dffbb6b', '17704_fender_shepherd', 'CIVIL_PROCEDURE', 'Claim and Issue Preclusion', 'Source of Preclusion Law', 'rendering-state preclusion law (§ 1738) vs federal common law of preclusion when the prior judgment is state-court and the second court is federal', 'Mary, a citizen of State A, and Paul, a citizen of State B, were involved in a multi-vehicle collision on a State A highway. Mary sued Paul in a State A state court for negligence. After a full trial, the State A court entered judgment for Paul. Later, Mary sued Paul in federal court in State B (sitting in diversity), and Paul raised the State A judgment as preclusive. The parties dispute which preclusion law governs the State A judgment''s effect.', 'Mary, a citizen of State A, and Paul, a citizen of State B, were involved in a multi-vehicle collision on a State A highway. Mary sued Paul in a State A state court for negligence. After a full trial, the State A court entered judgment for Paul. Later, Mary sued Paul in federal court in State B (sitting in diversity), and Paul raised the State A judgment as preclusive. The parties dispute which preclusion law governs the State A judgment''s effect.

Which law should the federal court generally consult?', 'Which law should the federal court generally consult?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ17704.md","source_question_id":"17704","variant_slug":"fender_shepherd","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"97100300","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium","deciding_phase":"CLASH","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A state court in State A enters a negligence judgment; the defendant is sued again in federal court in State B (diversity) and argues the State A judgment is preclusive. Which law governs the State A judgment''s preclusive effect?","review_truth":"For a state-court judgment, the preclusive effect is governed by the preclusion law of the state that rendered the judgment, even when the second suit is in federal court.","dominant_trap":"D","anchor_card":{"card_id":"RC-CIVPRO-SOURCE-OF-PRECLUSION-01","title":"Source of preclusion law — state-court vs federal-court judgment","signal":"A prior judgment is invoked for preclusion in a different court system. Ask: was the prior judgment state-court or federal-court?","student_move":"State-court judgment → § 1738 / Semtek → rendering state''s preclusion law. Federal-court judgment → federal common law of preclusion. The second court''s character is irrelevant; only the prior judgment''s character matters.","tiny_rule":"For a state-court judgment, the rendering state''s preclusion law applies (28 U.S.C. § 1738). For a federal-court judgment, federal common law of preclusion applies.","trap":"The dominant trap is the federal-court-second-suit → federal-law-always assumption. The ''second court is federal'' hook is a red herring when the prior judgment is state-court.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CIVPRO-FENDER-SHEPHERD-01","statement":"For a state-court judgment, the source of preclusion law is the rendering state under 28 U.S.C. § 1738 and Semtek; federal common law of preclusion controls only federal-court judgments.","type":"rule","trigger":"prior judgment is state-court; second court is federal (or vice versa)","authority":"28 U.S.C. § 1738; Semtek International Inc. v. Lockheed Martin Corp., 531 U.S. 497 (2001)","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"tiered_absolute overclaim detection","prompt":"A state-court judgment is raised for preclusion in a federal court. A choice says ''Federal preclusion law always, because the second court is federal.'' Is the ''always'' warranted?","answer":"No. The ''always'' is a tiered_absolute overclaim. The second court''s federal character is irrelevant when the prior judgment is state-court; § 1738 / Semtek directs the federal court to use the rendering state''s preclusion law."},{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"fabricated_rule detection","prompt":"A state-court judgment is raised for preclusion in a federal court. A choice says ''No preclusion law applies unless both cases are in the same court.'' Is that a true rule?","answer":"No. The ''unless both cases are in the same court'' condition is a fabricated rule. § 1738 expressly operates across courts and gives state-court judgments preclusive effect in federal court."},{"drill_type":"anchor","target_skill":"source-of-preclusion-law discriminator","prompt":"A state-court judgment is raised for preclusion in a federal court. Which law governs the prior judgment''s preclusive effect, and which authority controls?","answer":"The rendering state''s preclusion law, under 28 U.S.C. § 1738 and Semtek International Inc. v. Lockheed Martin Corp., 531 U.S. 497 (2001). Federal common law of preclusion controls only federal-court judgments."}]}'),
  ('6108dbb3-cd3b-5d1c-85ab-e58a50727478', '17709_tutoring_tithe', 'CIVIL_PROCEDURE', 'Subject Matter Jurisdiction', 'Diversity - Amount in Controversy Aggregation', 'single-plaintiff aggregation (relatedness irrelevant) vs unrelated-cannot-aggregate when one plaintiff sues one defendant on multiple claims', 'Mary, a citizen of State A, hired Paul, a citizen of State B, to repair her car after a collision. The repair was botched, causing an additional $50,000 in damage to the car. Separately and wholly unrelated, Mary hired Paul to provide private Latin and math tutoring to her children for the school year; Paul failed to perform, causing $35,000 in damages. Mary sues Paul in federal court on both claims, relying on diversity jurisdiction.', 'Mary, a citizen of State A, hired Paul, a citizen of State B, to repair her car after a collision. The repair was botched, causing an additional $50,000 in damage to the car. Separately and wholly unrelated, Mary hired Paul to provide private Latin and math tutoring to her children for the school year; Paul failed to perform, causing $35,000 in damages. Mary sues Paul in federal court on both claims, relying on diversity jurisdiction.

Is the amount in controversy satisfied?', 'Is the amount in controversy satisfied?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ17709.md","source_question_id":"17709","variant_slug":"tutoring_tithe","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"94010100","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium","deciding_phase":"CLASH","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A single plaintiff sues a single defendant in federal court on two unrelated claims totaling $85,000. Is the amount in controversy satisfied?","review_truth":"A single plaintiff may aggregate all claims — related or unrelated — against a single defendant to meet the amount-in-controversy requirement under 28 U.S.C. § 1332.","dominant_trap":"A","anchor_card":{"card_id":"RC-CIVPRO-AGGREGATION-AMOUNT-01","title":"Aggregation for the amount in controversy","signal":"A single plaintiff sues a single defendant in federal court on multiple claims. Question asks whether the amount in controversy is satisfied.","student_move":"Run the ''one plaintiff vs one defendant'' check. If yes, aggregate all claims, related or unrelated, against the single defendant. The ''unrelated'' qualifier is a red herring when one plaintiff sues one defendant. Confirm diversity and that the aggregated sum exceeds $75,000.","tiny_rule":"A single plaintiff may aggregate all claims — related or unrelated — against a single defendant to meet the amount-in-controversy requirement under 28 U.S.C. § 1332.","trap":"The dominant trap is the ''unrelated claims can never be aggregated'' rule. Relatedness IS required for multiple plaintiffs aggregating against a single defendant, but NOT for a single plaintiff aggregating against a single defendant.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CIVPRO-TUTORING-TITHE-01","statement":"A single plaintiff may aggregate all claims — related or unrelated — against a single defendant to meet the amount-in-controversy requirement under 28 U.S.C. § 1332; the ''unrelated'' qualifier is a red herring when one plaintiff sues one defendant.","type":"distinction","trigger":"stem describes a single plaintiff suing a single defendant on multiple claims in federal court","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"tiered_absolute overclaim detection on aggregation","prompt":"A single plaintiff sues a single defendant in federal court on two unrelated claims. A choice says ''No, because unrelated claims can never be aggregated.'' Is the ''never'' warranted?","answer":"No. The ''never'' is a tiered_absolute overclaim. The single-plaintiff-vs-single-defendant rule makes relatedness irrelevant. Relatedness IS required for multiple plaintiffs, not for one plaintiff."},{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"fabricated_rule detection on aggregation","prompt":"A single plaintiff sues a single defendant in federal court on a $50,000 claim and a $35,000 claim. A choice says ''No, because each individual claim must exceed $75,000.'' Is that a true rule for this case?","answer":"No. The ''each individual claim must exceed $75,000'' rule applies to multiple plaintiffs aggregating unrelated claims, not to a single plaintiff. A single plaintiff can aggregate to meet the threshold."},{"drill_type":"anchor","target_skill":"single-plaintiff aggregation discriminator","prompt":"A single plaintiff sues a single defendant in federal court on multiple claims, related or unrelated. May the plaintiff aggregate to meet the $75,000 amount-in-controversy requirement?","answer":"Yes. A single plaintiff may aggregate all claims, related or unrelated, against a single defendant to meet the amount-in-controversy requirement under 28 U.S.C. § 1332. The aggregated sum here is $85,000, which satisfies the threshold."}]}'),
  ('cd10f2ff-b6bf-58ff-99d2-3c2a39b13f92', '17711_mercy_clinic_zoning', 'CIVIL_PROCEDURE', 'Joinder, Class Actions, and Intervention', 'Timeliness under Rule 24', 'untimeliness + prejudice versus mandatory intervention because of public importance', 'A chain of mobile medical clinics called Mary''s Mercy — a 501(c)(3) that provides free ultrasound and Christian counseling in underserved neighborhoods — followed a zoning lawsuit from the day it was filed. After summary judgment briefing closed and trial was scheduled, Mary''s Mercy moved to intervene to present traffic and parking-impact arguments it could have raised earlier. Existing parties object that adding Mary''s Mercy now would require reopening discovery and delaying trial.', 'A chain of mobile medical clinics called Mary''s Mercy — a 501(c)(3) that provides free ultrasound and Christian counseling in underserved neighborhoods — followed a zoning lawsuit from the day it was filed. After summary judgment briefing closed and trial was scheduled, Mary''s Mercy moved to intervene to present traffic and parking-impact arguments it could have raised earlier. Existing parties object that adding Mary''s Mercy now would require reopening discovery and delaying trial.

What is the strongest basis for denying the motion to intervene?', 'What is the strongest basis for denying the motion to intervene?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ17711.md","source_question_id":"17711","variant_slug":"mercy_clinic_zoning","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"95070302","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"HARD_STRUCTURAL","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A non-party that knew of a case from the start moves to intervene at the eve of trial to raise arguments it could have raised earlier, and existing parties object. Is the motion likely to be granted?","review_truth":"A motion to intervene is judged in part by timeliness; when the movant knew of the case from the start, waits until the eve of trial, and granting intervention would require reopening discovery and delay trial, denial on untimeliness and prejudice is the strongest available ground.","dominant_trap":"B","anchor_card":{"card_id":"RC-CIVPRO-RULE-24-TIMELINESS-01","title":"Rule 24 timeliness + prejudice","signal":"A non-party that knew of the case from the start waits until the eve of trial to move to intervene to raise arguments it could have raised earlier, and existing parties object on prejudice grounds.","student_move":"Run the Rule 24 timeliness factors first (how far the case has progressed, the movant''s reason for the delay, the prejudice to existing parties). Do not be drawn into the public-importance argument; the public interest is not a magic override of Rule 24''s timeliness gate.","tiny_rule":"When the movant knew of the case from day 1, the eve-of-trial intervention is denied on untimeliness and prejudice to existing parties.","trap":"The public-importance overreach (B) feels right because zoning IS public. It is not a magic override.","confidence":"HARD_STRUCTURAL"},"gold_keys":[],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CIVPRO-MERCY-CLINIC-ZONING-01","statement":"When the movant knew of the case from day 1, run the Rule 24 timeliness factors first; the public-importance choice is a misstatement of the mandatory-intervention rule (Rule 24(a)(2) requires impairment of a legally protected interest, not a general public concern).","type":"trap_spotting","trigger":"movant knew of the case from the start; the proposed intervention would reopen discovery and delay trial; the ''intervention is required'' framing is on the table","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"trap_spotting","target_skill":"Rule 24 timeliness vs mandatory intervention","prompt":"A non-party that knew of the case from day 1 moves to intervene at the eve of trial on the ground that the underlying subject matter is of public importance. Is the motion likely to be granted?","answer":"No. The public importance of the subject matter is not a magic override of Rule 24''s timeliness gate. The motion is denied on untimeliness and prejudice to existing parties."},{"drill_type":"rule_check","target_skill":"Rule 24(a)(2) interest test","prompt":"A non-profit organization that operates mobile clinics in the affected neighborhood, but does not own property near the zoning site, moves to intervene. Does Rule 24(a)(2) require property ownership?","answer":"No. Rule 24(a)(2) requires an interest that may be impaired by the disposition; it does not require property ownership. A mobile-medical ministry that operates clinics in the affected neighborhood has a cognizable interest in traffic, parking, and patient access."},{"drill_type":"rule_check","target_skill":"Rule 24 contemplates non-parties","prompt":"May a non-party move to intervene under Rule 24?","answer":"Yes. Rule 24(a) and Rule 24(b) both contemplate non-parties as movants. The question is whether the Rule 24 requirements are met, not whether the movant is a named party."}]}'),
  ('82c20f74-3174-5024-81d5-edbccd725674', '17728_illuminated_manuscript', 'CONSTITUTIONAL_LAW', 'Federal Powers', 'Channels of commerce', 'channels-of-commerce power reaches the channels themselves versus stolen goods are outside the Commerce Clause reach because of dirty chain of title', 'A federal statute, the National Stolen Property Act, prohibits knowingly shipping stolen property across state lines. Daniel, who lives in State A, broke into a private collector''s climate-controlled library in State A and stole a 12th-century illuminated Christian gospel lectionary and a 6th-century Coptic gospel codex. He shipped the manuscripts to Paul, a buyer in State B, who paid Daniel $90,000 in cash. Federal authorities charge Daniel under the Act. At trial, Daniel argues Congress cannot punish him because the theft occurred entirely within State A and only the shipment crossed state lines afterward.', 'A federal statute, the National Stolen Property Act, prohibits knowingly shipping stolen property across state lines. Daniel, who lives in State A, broke into a private collector''s climate-controlled library in State A and stole a 12th-century illuminated Christian gospel lectionary and a 6th-century Coptic gospel codex. He shipped the manuscripts to Paul, a buyer in State B, who paid Daniel $90,000 in cash. Federal authorities charge Daniel under the Act. At trial, Daniel argues Congress cannot punish him because the theft occurred entirely within State A and only the shipment crossed state lines afterward.

What is the best response?', 'What is the best response?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ17728.md","source_question_id":"17728","variant_slug":"illuminated_manuscript","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"42010103","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium","deciding_phase":"CALL","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A federal statute prohibits knowingly shipping stolen property across state lines. A defendant steals property in one state and ships it to a buyer in another state. The defendant argues the in-state theft is outside federal reach. Is the statute a valid exercise of Congress''s commerce power?","review_truth":"Congress''s commerce power reaches the channels of interstate commerce themselves, including the power to keep harmful or stolen goods from moving through those channels; the power does not depend on the commercial character of the underlying goods.","dominant_trap":"D","anchor_card":{"card_id":"RC-CONLAW-CHANNELS-OF-COMMERCE-01","title":"Channels-of-commerce power — the channels, not the goods","signal":"A federal statute targets the act of shipping stolen property across state lines; the defendant argues the in-state taking is outside federal reach.","student_move":"Identify the regulated act (the use of a channel), then apply the channels-of-commerce power. The in-state origin of the taking is not a defense.","tiny_rule":"The channels-of-commerce power reaches the channels themselves; the inquiry is whether the statute regulates the use of a channel, not whether the goods are commercially clean.","trap":"The ''stolen goods are outside commerce'' overreach (D) feels right because stolen goods have a dirty chain of title. The channels power is about the channel, not the goods.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONLAW-ILLUMINATED-MANUSCRIPT-01","statement":"Congress''s commerce power reaches the channels of interstate commerce themselves, including the power to keep harmful or stolen goods from moving through those channels; the power does not depend on the commercial character of the underlying goods.","type":"rule","trigger":"federal statute targets the use of a channel; defendant argues the in-state origin of the taking is outside federal reach","authority":"U.S. Const. art. I, § 8, cl. 3; Heart of Atlanta Motel, Inc. v. United States, 379 U.S. 241 (1964); Gibbons v. Ogden, 22 U.S. (9 Wheat.) 1 (1824); cf. United States v. Lopez, 514 U.S. 549, 558-559 (1995); National Stolen Property Act, 18 U.S.C. § 2314","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"anchor","target_skill":"channels-of-commerce power","prompt":"A federal statute prohibits knowingly shipping stolen property across state lines. The defendant argues the in-state taking is outside federal reach. What is the best response?","answer":"Congress''s commerce power reaches the channels of interstate commerce themselves; the regulated act is the interstate shipment, which is itself a use of the channels of interstate commerce. The in-state origin of the taking is not a defense."},{"drill_type":"rule_check","target_skill":"NSPA reach","prompt":"Does the National Stolen Property Act reach only shipping-company operators?","answer":"No. The NSPA reaches any person who knowingly ships stolen property across state lines, whether or not the defendant owns a shipping company."},{"drill_type":"trap_spotting","target_skill":"stolen-goods-outside-commerce overreach","prompt":"A defendant argues that stolen goods are ''outside commerce'' and therefore outside Congress''s commerce power. What is the best response?","answer":"The channels-of-commerce power reaches the channels themselves, not the goods in them. Even if stolen goods have no clean commercial title, Congress can still regulate their movement through the channels of interstate commerce."}]}'),
  ('10a02f66-dabc-542f-aa37-121950557776', '17857_living_nativity_carver', 'TORTS', 'Economic torts', 'Intentional interference with prospective advantage (competition privilege)', 'lawful truthful price competition (privileged) vs. improper means (required for liability)', 'Esther has built a hand-carved outdoor Living Nativity scene for Mary''s small church each December for the past nine years under a hand-shake annual arrangement. Each fall, Mary and Esther would agree on a price and a delivery date for the next season, but no written renewal contract was ever signed. In late summer, Paul, another local woodworker with a similar style, quoted Mary $3,200 for a comparable Living Nativity scene — $700 below Esther''s most recent price — and Mary accepted Paul''s offer. Esther now sues Paul for interference with prospective economic advantage.', 'Esther has built a hand-carved outdoor Living Nativity scene for Mary''s small church each December for the past nine years under a hand-shake annual arrangement. Each fall, Mary and Esther would agree on a price and a delivery date for the next season, but no written renewal contract was ever signed. In late summer, Paul, another local woodworker with a similar style, quoted Mary $3,200 for a comparable Living Nativity scene — $700 below Esther''s most recent price — and Mary accepted Paul''s offer. Esther now sues Paul for interference with prospective economic advantage.

Is Esther likely to prevail against Paul?', 'Is Esther likely to prevail against Paul?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ17857.md","source_question_id":"17857","variant_slug":"living_nativity_carver","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"62050302","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"HARD_STRUCTURAL","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A church had a hand-shake annual renewal habit with its long-time Nativity-set builder, never signed a written renewal, and then accepted a lower price from a competing builder. Can the long-time builder sue the competitor for interference with prospective economic advantage?","review_truth":"Competition for prospective business through truthful pricing is privileged; liability for interference with prospective economic advantage requires improper means, not lawful price competition.","dominant_trap":"B","anchor_card":{"card_id":"RC-TORTS-PROSPECTIVE-PRIVILEGE-01","title":"Prospective advantage vs. competition privilege","signal":"No signed renewal; competitor quotes a truthful lower price; long-time supplier sues the competitor.","student_move":"Cut the ''any lost deal'' and ''privilege only for existing contracts'' answers on visible defects; the privilege answer is residual.","tiny_rule":"Truthful price competition is privileged competition for prospective business; improper means are required for liability.","trap":"Long-time relationship feels protective, but the privilege is the deciding line on these facts.","confidence":"HARD_STRUCTURAL"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-TORTS-LIVING-NATIVITY-CARVER-01","statement":"Competition for prospective business through truthful pricing is privileged; liability for interference with prospective economic advantage requires improper means, not lawful price competition.","type":"rule","trigger":"no signed renewal plus truthful lower price by a competitor","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 768; Restatement (Third) of Torts: Liability for Economic Harm § 4","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-TORTS-LIVING-NATIVITY-CARVER-01","statement":"When a stem pairs ''no signed renewal'' with ''truthful lower price,'' the privilege line is the deciding axis — cut the ''tortious because lost a deal'' answers first.","type":"call_focus","trigger":"no signed renewal plus truthful lower price by a competitor","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"tiered_absolute cut","prompt":"A long-time supplier sues a competitor for taking a deal. The answer says ''any lost expected deal is tortious interference.'' Is that a tiered_absolute cut?","answer":"Yes. The ''any'' overclaims; the rule requires improper means or an existing contract."},{"drill_type":"clash","target_skill":"privilege-scope check","prompt":"No signed renewal; competitor quotes a truthful lower price. Does the competition privilege apply?","answer":"Yes. The privilege protects competition for prospective business, including the renewal-type relationship."},{"drill_type":"anchor","target_skill":"privilege scope","prompt":"What is the deciding rule when a competitor uses truthful price competition to take a prospective deal?","answer":"Truthful price competition is privileged; liability requires improper means."}]}'),
  ('6f80eceb-992b-5a00-a2c0-c4683ca3f7c3', '17859_olive_branch_ornament', 'CRIMINAL_LAW', 'Property Crimes', 'Larceny (asportation element)', 'asportation minimum (any movement suffices) vs. premises removal / sale element', 'Naomi, a customer at "Olive Branch Christian Books," picks up a small hand-painted porcelain Christmas ornament — a shepherd-and-lamb scene — from an open display table, slips it into her tote bag, and turns toward the exit intending to keep it. A clerk named Stephen stops her after she has moved only two steps from the table.', 'Naomi, a customer at "Olive Branch Christian Books," picks up a small hand-painted porcelain Christmas ornament — a shepherd-and-lamb scene — from an open display table, slips it into her tote bag, and turns toward the exit intending to keep it. A clerk named Stephen stops her after she has moved only two steps from the table.

Has the carrying-away element of larceny been satisfied?', 'Has the carrying-away element of larceny been satisfied?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ17859.md","source_question_id":"17859","variant_slug":"olive_branch_ornament","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"73030100","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"HARD_STRUCTURAL","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A customer slips a small item from an open display into her bag, takes two steps toward the exit, and is stopped before leaving the building. Has the carrying-away (asportation) element of larceny been satisfied?","review_truth":"Asportation in larceny is satisfied by even minimal movement of the property; the property need not leave the building and the act of sale is not an element.","dominant_trap":"A","anchor_card":{"card_id":"RC-CRIMINAL-ASPORTATION-MINIMUM-01","title":"Asportation minimum vs. removal / sale","signal":"Customer slips a small item from an open display, takes a few steps, and is stopped before leaving the building.","student_move":"Cut ''must leave the building'' and ''stopped before she sold'' on visible defects; the slight-movement answer is residual.","tiny_rule":"Asportation is satisfied by even minimal movement; the property need not leave the building and sale is not an element.","trap":"Conflating asportation with a successful shoplifting exit, or looking for a sale element that does not exist.","confidence":"HARD_STRUCTURAL"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CRIMINAL-OLIVE-BRANCH-ORNAMENT-01","statement":"Asportation in larceny is satisfied by even minimal movement of the property; the property need not leave the building and the act of sale is not an element.","type":"rule","trigger":"small item, two steps toward the exit, stopped before leaving the building","authority":"Common-law larceny five-element formulation; Model Penal Code § 223.2 (where the jurisdiction has adopted it)","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CRIMINAL-OLIVE-BRANCH-ORNAMENT-01","statement":"When a stem gives a ''small item, two steps, stopped before exit'' pattern, lock the call to the asportation minimum and cut the ''must leave the building'' and ''must have sold it'' distractors first.","type":"call_focus","trigger":"small item, two steps toward the exit, stopped before leaving the building","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"extreme_of_range cut","prompt":"A customer takes a small item from a shelf, slips it into her bag, and is stopped before leaving. The answer says ''no asportation because she did not leave the building.'' Is that an extreme_of_range cut?","answer":"Yes. Asportation requires only minimal movement; leaving the building is not required."},{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"misfit cut","prompt":"A customer takes a small item, slips it into her bag, and is stopped. The answer says ''no larceny because she did not sell it.'' Is that a misfit cut?","answer":"Yes. Sale is not an element of larceny; the offense is complete on trespassory taking + asportation + intent."},{"drill_type":"anchor","target_skill":"asportation minimum","prompt":"What is the asportation minimum in common-law larceny?","answer":"Any movement, however slight, that is part of the taking."}]}'),
  ('2330fe50-a134-5d7a-b85a-6d92f0aefd3f', '17913_summit_klaxon', 'CIVIL_PROCEDURE', 'Erie Doctrine and Choice of Law', 'Contract Choice Clause in Diversity', 'contract clause is self-executing (B) vs. forum''s choice rules apply first under Klaxon (C)', 'Mary, a worship-summit organizer in State A, signed a written speaker agreement with Paul, a Christian conference speaker based in State C, for Paul to deliver the Friday-evening keynote at Mary''s summer worship summit in State A. The agreement contains a clause that states:

> "This agreement shall be governed by the laws of State C, without regard to conflict-of-laws principles."

State A''s choice-of-law rules enforce some contractual choice-of-law clauses but refuse to enforce others when the chosen state has no substantial relationship to the dispute. Paul later cancels the engagement, and Mary sues Paul for breach of contract in federal district court sitting in State A on the basis of diversity jurisdiction.', 'Mary, a worship-summit organizer in State A, signed a written speaker agreement with Paul, a Christian conference speaker based in State C, for Paul to deliver the Friday-evening keynote at Mary''s summer worship summit in State A. The agreement contains a clause that states:

> "This agreement shall be governed by the laws of State C, without regard to conflict-of-laws principles."

State A''s choice-of-law rules enforce some contractual choice-of-law clauses but refuse to enforce others when the chosen state has no substantial relationship to the dispute. Paul later cancels the engagement, and Mary sues Paul for breach of contract in federal district court sitting in State A on the basis of diversity jurisdiction.

How should the federal court decide whether State C law governs Mary''s claim?', 'How should the federal court decide whether State C law governs Mary''s claim?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ17913.md","source_question_id":"17913","variant_slug":"summit_klaxon","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"96060100","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium","deciding_phase":"CALL","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"In a federal diversity contract suit, the contract names a non-forum state''s law. How does the court decide whether that choice-of-law clause is enforceable?","review_truth":"A federal diversity court must apply the forum state''s choice-of-law rules to determine whether a contractual choice-of-law clause is enforceable, even when the contract names the chosen state''s law.","dominant_trap":"B","anchor_card":{"card_id":"RC-CIVPRO-KLAXON-CHOICE-CLAUSE-01","title":"Klaxon - forum''s choice rules govern the choice-of-law clause","signal":"A federal diversity contract suit has a choice-of-law clause naming a non-forum state''s law. The court must decide whether to honor the clause.","student_move":"Cut federal common law (Erie) and plaintiff preference (party preference is not the rule of decision). Then use the Gold Key to defeat the auto-enforce trap: the contract clause is not self-executing - Klaxon sends the court to the forum''s choice rules first.","tiny_rule":"In federal diversity, a contractual choice-of-law clause is not self-executing; the forum state''s choice-of-law rules apply first.","trap":"The contract names a state''s law, so the contract must control. Wrong - Klaxon is the rule of decision.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CIVPRO-SUMMIT-KLAXON-01","statement":"A federal diversity court must apply the forum state''s choice-of-law rules (Klaxon) to determine whether a contractual choice-of-law clause is enforceable, even when the contract names the chosen state''s law. The contract clause is not self-executing.","type":"rule","trigger":"federal diversity case with a contractual choice-of-law clause naming a non-forum state","authority":"Klaxon Co. v. Stentor Electric Mfg. Co., 313 U.S. 487 (1941); Erie R.R. v. Tompkins, 304 U.S. 64 (1938); 28 U.S.C. § 1652; Restatement (Second) of Conflict of Laws § 187(2)","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CIVPRO-SUMMIT-KLAXON-01","statement":"When the call asks how a federal court should decide a choice-of-law question in diversity, the test is the forum-state choice-of-law rules, not the contract''s choice-of-law clause, not federal common law, and not party preference.","type":"clash","trigger":"federal diversity case involving a contractual choice-of-law clause","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"Issue-Sense on federal common law","prompt":"A state-law contract claim between two private parties is filed in federal diversity. Is federal common law the rule of decision?","answer":"No. Erie R.R. v. Tompkins, 304 U.S. 64 (1938) holds that there is no federal general common law. The rule of decision is the substantive law of the state that would apply under the forum''s choice-of-law rules."},{"drill_type":"anchor","target_skill":"Klaxon - forum-state choice rules in diversity","prompt":"A federal diversity contract suit has a choice-of-law clause naming State C. State A is the forum. The court is asked whether State C law governs. What rule of decision applies?","answer":"Klaxon Co. v. Stentor Electric Mfg. Co., 313 U.S. 487 (1941): the federal court applies the forum state''s (State A''s) choice-of-law rules. The contract clause is not self-executing; it operates through State A''s choice rules."},{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"rule-of-decision test (party preference)","prompt":"Plaintiff chose State A as the forum. Does that mean State A''s substantive law applies?","answer":"No. The choice of forum is procedural. The choice of substantive law is governed by the forum''s choice-of-law rules under Klaxon, not by the plaintiff''s preference."}]}'),
  ('ecd1f985-cf56-571c-b9a5-07ae28537d51', '17956_stewards_heavyheart', 'TORTS', 'Economic torts', 'Malicious prosecution — favorable termination element', 'favorable-termination element missing (threshold) vs. secondary defects (overclaim, categorical overstatement, wrong tort frame)', 'Lydia, who runs a small heritage-seed business in Sonoma County, contracted to sell a one-year exclusive on her prized Brandywine tomato seed stock to a wholesale produce distributor run by Paul. When a hard late-spring frost wiped out half the stock, Lydia could not deliver the full exclusive order. Paul promptly sued Lydia for breach of contract in state court. While Paul''s breach suit was still pending, Lydia — believing Paul filed the suit out of spite because she had refused his earlier offer to merge her business into his — filed a separate tort claim against Paul for malicious prosecution, alleging he filed the contract suit without probable cause and with malice. What is Lydia''s strongest problem with her malicious prosecution claim?', 'Lydia, who runs a small heritage-seed business in Sonoma County, contracted to sell a one-year exclusive on her prized Brandywine tomato seed stock to a wholesale produce distributor run by Paul. When a hard late-spring frost wiped out half the stock, Lydia could not deliver the full exclusive order. Paul promptly sued Lydia for breach of contract in state court. While Paul''s breach suit was still pending, Lydia — believing Paul filed the suit out of spite because she had refused his earlier offer to merge her business into his — filed a separate tort claim against Paul for malicious prosecution, alleging he filed the contract suit without probable cause and with malice. What is Lydia''s strongest problem with her malicious prosecution claim?

What is Lydia''s strongest problem with her malicious-prosecution claim?', 'What is Lydia''s strongest problem with her malicious-prosecution claim?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ17956.md","source_question_id":"17956","variant_slug":"stewards_heavyheart","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"62050403","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium","deciding_phase":"CALL","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A buyer sues a seller for breach of contract; the suit is still pending. While the suit is pending, the seller sues the buyer for malicious prosecution, alleging spite and lack of probable cause. The seller wants to know the strongest problem with the malicious prosecution claim.","review_truth":"A malicious-prosecution claim requires that the prior proceeding terminated favorably to the plaintiff; a pending underlying suit is fatal to the claim, even when malice and lack of probable cause are otherwise present.","dominant_trap":"C","anchor_card":{"card_id":"RC-TORTS-MALICIOUS-PROSECUTION-FAVORABLE-TERMINATION-01","title":"Malicious prosecution — favorable-termination threshold","signal":"A plaintiff sues for malicious prosecution while the underlying proceeding is still pending, even though malice and lack of probable cause are alleged.","student_move":"Apply the threshold check: has the prior proceeding terminated in a manner affirming the plaintiff''s innocence of the charge or wrong? If not, the claim is not available.","tiny_rule":"Favorable termination is a threshold element; a pending underlying suit is fatal to malicious prosecution.","trap":"Spite or lack of probable cause is a strong showing on one element, but it cannot substitute for a missing element on another.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-TORTS-STEWARDS-HEAVYHEART-01","statement":"Favorable termination of the prior proceeding is a threshold element of malicious prosecution. A pending underlying suit is fatal to the claim, regardless of how strong the malice or lack-of-probable-cause showing would otherwise be.","type":"threshold","trigger":"underlying suit is still pending; the plaintiff sues for malicious prosecution alleging malice and lack of probable cause","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Torts §§ 674, 681","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-TORTS-STEWARDS-HEAVYHEART-01","statement":"When the call asks for the ''strongest'' problem, pick the threshold-missing-element answer, not a categorical-rule answer. The threshold element controls the analysis; categorical overstatements, overclaim, and wrong-tort-frame distractors name real but secondary defects.","type":"call_focus","trigger":"the call asks for the ''strongest'' problem; one choice targets a threshold/missing-element defect and the others target secondary defects","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"threshold-element check","prompt":"Buyer sues seller for breach of contract; the suit is still pending. The seller sues the buyer for malicious prosecution, alleging spite and lack of probable cause. What is the seller''s strongest problem?","answer":"The favorable-termination element is missing. The contract suit is still pending — no judgment, no favorable dismissal. Favorable termination is a threshold element of malicious prosecution; a pending underlying suit is fatal."},{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"tiered-absolute detection","prompt":"A plaintiff''s malicious-prosecution claim alleges that the defendant acted with spite. Does the spite alone make out the tort?","answer":"No. Spite is one input to malice, but malice is only one element of the claim. The plaintiff must also establish favorable termination, lack of probable cause, and institution of the prior proceeding by the defendant. A strong showing on one element cannot substitute for a missing element on another."},{"drill_type":"anchor","target_skill":"call_focus (strongest problem)","prompt":"A malicious-prosecution claim is filed while the underlying suit is still pending. The defendant argues that (1) the tort never applies to civil suits, (2) the plaintiff''s spite proves the claim, or (3) the plaintiff should relabel the claim as nuisance. Which is the strongest counter?","answer":"Favorable termination is a threshold element; a pending underlying suit is fatal to malicious prosecution. The other arguments name real but secondary defects (jurisdictional, overclaim, wrong tort frame)."}]}'),
  ('6a26642a-6424-56d7-8fbc-5569db590d00', '17996_shepherding_call', 'EVIDENCE', 'Character evidence', 'Impeachment — opinion or reputation evidence on truthfulness (FRE 608(a))', 'trigger-missing (FRE 608(a) ''after attack'' gate) vs. secondary defects (always-bolster overclaim, opinion-never-admissible overclaim, testifying-alone missing trigger)', 'Mary, a member of a small-town congregation in central Oregon, was on trial for spray-painting a sponsored mural of a local charity''s community-outreach program. Mary testified in her own defense that she did not do it. Mary now wants to call her longtime friend and fellow congregant Hannah to testify that Hannah has known Mary for at least 20 years and, in Hannah''s opinion, Mary has always been an honest person.', 'Mary, a member of a small-town congregation in central Oregon, was on trial for spray-painting a sponsored mural of a local charity''s community-outreach program. Mary testified in her own defense that she did not do it. Mary now wants to call her longtime friend and fellow congregant Hannah to testify that Hannah has known Mary for at least 20 years and, in Hannah''s opinion, Mary has always been an honest person.

Is Hannah''s statement admissible?', 'Is Hannah''s statement admissible?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ17996.md","source_question_id":"17996","variant_slug":"shepherding_call","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"31010505","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium","deciding_phase":"CALL","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A defendant in a criminal trial testifies in her own defense that she did not commit the charged act. The defendant wants to call a witness to offer a 20-year-opinion that the defendant is an honest person. Is the opinion evidence admissible?","review_truth":"Under FRE 608(a), opinion or reputation evidence on a witness''s truthfulness is admissible only if the witness''s character for truthfulness has first been attacked; the defendant''s mere testimony does not attack her own character for truthfulness.","dominant_trap":"A","anchor_card":{"card_id":"RC-EVIDENCE-FRE-608A-ATTACK-TRIGGER-01","title":"FRE 608(a) — bolstering gated by ''after attack''","signal":"A party offers reputation or opinion evidence on a witness''s character for truthfulness, but no prior attack on that character appears in the record.","student_move":"Apply the trigger check: has the witness''s character for truthfulness been attacked (e.g., by prior inconsistent statement, bias, conviction of crime, reputation, or contradiction)? If not, the bolstering opinion is not admissible under 608(a).","tiny_rule":"FRE 608(a) authorizes opinion/reputation evidence on truthfulness only after an attack; mere testimony is not an attack.","trap":"The dominant trap is to assume bolstering is always permitted when the witness has taken the stand. It is not — the trigger must be met first.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-EVIDENCE-SHEPHERDING-CALL-01","statement":"Under FRE 608(a), a witness''s character for truthfulness may be supported by reputation or opinion evidence only after that character has been attacked. The defendant''s mere testimony in her own defense is not, by itself, an attack on her own character for truthfulness.","type":"rule","trigger":"criminal defendant testifies in own defense; defense offers a long-acquaintance opinion that the defendant is honest; no prior attack on character is in the record","authority":"Fed. R. Evid. 608(a); United States v. Manso-Portes, 838 F.2d 889 (7th Cir. 1988)","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-EVIDENCE-SHEPHERDING-CALL-01","statement":"FRE 608(a) authorizes opinion (and reputation) evidence on truthfulness; the gate is ''after attack,'' not ''no opinion.'' When the offered evidence is opinion on truthfulness, the discrimination is the attack trigger, not the form.","type":"distinction","trigger":"the offered evidence is opinion on truthfulness; no prior attack is in the record","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"trigger check (FRE 608(a))","prompt":"A criminal defendant testifies in her own defense and denies the charge. The defense offers a witness who would say, ''I have known the defendant for 25 years and she is honest.'' Is the testimony admissible?","answer":"No. FRE 608(a) authorizes opinion evidence on truthfulness only after the witness''s character for truthfulness has been attacked. Mere testimony is not an attack."},{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"tiered-absolute detection","prompt":"Is opinion evidence on truthfulness always admissible when the witness has taken the stand?","answer":"No. FRE 608(a) is conditional: bolstering on truthfulness is admissible only after the character for truthfulness has been attacked."},{"drill_type":"anchor","target_skill":"form vs. trigger distinction","prompt":"Defense offers a witness''s opinion that the defendant is honest. The prosecution objects. The form is opinion; the question is the trigger. Which is the right analytical frame?","answer":"The form (opinion) is exactly what FRE 608(a) authorizes. The gate is the attack trigger. The opinion is admissible only if the witness''s character for truthfulness has been attacked; on these facts (mere denial of the charge), it has not."}]}'),
  ('b568546c-5835-5c5c-aafa-33296f5b4d81', '18077_maranatha_meadow', 'REAL_PROPERTY', 'Estates and conveyancing', 'Future interests — shifting executory interest (third-party condition cutting short another grantee)', 'executory (cutting-short) vs. remainder (natural termination); shifting (third party) vs. springing (grantor) vs. grantor-retained', 'Daniel, a retired organic-wheat farmer in central Washington, conveyed a 60-acre parcel he called Maranatha Meadow "to Barnabas and his heirs, but if Timothy graduates from college before Timothy turns 25, then to Timothy and his heirs." Timothy is Daniel''s 22-year-old grandson; he has not yet graduated. Barnabas is Daniel''s nephew, who has been working the farm for several years and is now in possession.', 'Daniel, a retired organic-wheat farmer in central Washington, conveyed a 60-acre parcel he called Maranatha Meadow "to Barnabas and his heirs, but if Timothy graduates from college before Timothy turns 25, then to Timothy and his heirs." Timothy is Daniel''s 22-year-old grandson; he has not yet graduated. Barnabas is Daniel''s nephew, who has been working the farm for several years and is now in possession.

What interest does Timothy have?', 'What interest does Timothy have?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ18077.md","source_question_id":"18077","variant_slug":"maranatha_meadow","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"82010203","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"calibration","deciding_phase":"CALL","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A grantor conveys land to grantee A and his heirs, but if a third person (a 22-year-old who has not yet met a graduation-before-25 condition) graduates before turning 25, then to the third person and his heirs. A is in possession. What is the third person''s interest?","review_truth":"A future interest created in a third party that cuts short a prior grantee''s estate on a condition subsequent is a shifting executory interest; it is created in the grantor''s original deed and requires no later conveyance.","dominant_trap":"A","anchor_card":{"card_id":"RC-REAL-PROPERTY-EXECUTORY-INTEREST-01","title":"Shifting executory interest — cutting-short axis in a third party","signal":"A conveyance ''to A and his heirs, but if [third person] meets a condition, then to [third person] and his heirs'' — fee simple subject to an executory limitation with a contingent shifting executory interest in a third party.","student_move":"Run the axis check: (1) does the future interest cut short a prior estate? (yes -> executory, not remainder). (2) is the future interest in the grantor or a third party? (third party -> shifting, not springing). Combine: shifting executory interest.","tiny_rule":"Cutting-short + third party = shifting executory interest. Cutting-short + grantor = springing executory interest. No cutting-short + life estate = remainder.","trap":"The dominant trap is to call it a ''vested remainder subject to open'' because there is no life estate to support a remainder. The threshold defect is that the future interest is executory, not a remainder.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-REAL-PROPERTY-MARANATHA-MEADOW-01","statement":"A remainder follows a life estate (or other estate of years); an executory interest follows a fee simple and cuts short the prior estate. A future interest in a third party that cuts short a prior grantee''s estate is a shifting executory interest.","type":"distinction","trigger":"fee simple subject to an executory limitation in the first grantee; cutting-shifting condition in favor of a third party who has not yet satisfied the condition","authority":"Restatement (Third) of Property: future-interest classification","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-REAL-PROPERTY-MARANATHA-MEADOW-01","statement":"Read the original deed for the source of the future interest; if the original deed creates the executory interest, no later deed is required. The dispositive instrument is the original conveyance.","type":"cut","trigger":"the cutting-shifting language is in the original deed; the question is whether the future interest exists","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"prior-estate check (remainder vs. executory)","prompt":"O conveys Blackacre ''to A and his heirs, but if X returns from abroad, then to X and his heirs.'' A is in possession. What is X''s interest?","answer":"Shifting executory interest. The future interest cuts short A''s fee simple (executory, not remainder — no life estate to support a remainder) and is in a third party X (shifting, not springing)."},{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"holder check (grantor vs. third party)","prompt":"O conveys Blackacre ''to A and his heirs, but if X returns from abroad, then to O and his heirs.'' A is in possession. What is O''s interest?","answer":"Springing executory interest. The future interest cuts short A''s fee simple (executory) and is in the grantor (springing, not shifting). Note: if the future interest were in the grantor in a fee simple determinable, the grantor''s interest would be a possibility of reverter; if the future interest were in the grantor in a fee simple subject to condition subsequent, the grantor''s interest would be a right of entry. Here, the future interest is in the grantor and the language cuts short, so it is a springing executory interest."},{"drill_type":"anchor","target_skill":"axis combination (cutting-short + third party)","prompt":"Conveyance ''to A and his heirs, but if B meets a condition, then to B and his heirs.'' A is in possession. What is B''s interest?","answer":"Shifting executory interest. Cutting-short + third party = shifting executory interest."}]}'),
  ('8ed331f4-2d3f-5e42-b8bc-d488f4690e82', '18111_camp_stipend_forgery', 'CRIMINAL_LAW', 'Property Crimes', 'Other Crimes', 'no actual loss means no forgery (B) vs. false making with intent is the actus reus (A)', 'Daniel, a former volunteer at a Christian summer-camp, found Stephen''s checkbook in the parking lot during a Saturday clean-up day at the camp. The checkbook contained unused (blank) personal checks. Daniel wrote a check on one of those checks payable to "cash" for $750, signed Stephen''s name without Stephen''s authority, and planned to cash it at a check-cashing store before the camp week ended.', 'Daniel, a former volunteer at a Christian summer-camp, found Stephen''s checkbook in the parking lot during a Saturday clean-up day at the camp. The checkbook contained unused (blank) personal checks. Daniel wrote a check on one of those checks payable to "cash" for $750, signed Stephen''s name without Stephen''s authority, and planned to cash it at a check-cashing store before the camp week ended.

Has Daniel committed forgery?', 'Has Daniel committed forgery?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ18111.md","source_question_id":"18111","variant_slug":"camp_stipend_forgery","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"73030700","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium","deciding_phase":"CALL","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A person finds another''s unused checks, fills in a check payable to cash for a fixed amount, signs the owner''s name without authority, and plans to cash it. Has that person committed forgery — even if the check is never cashed and the owner never loses money?","review_truth":"Forgery is complete when the defendant falsely makes a writing with apparent legal efficacy with intent to defraud; the victim need not actually lose money, and the forger need not successfully utter the document.","dominant_trap":"B","anchor_card":{"card_id":"RC-CRIMINAL-FORGERY-FALSE-MAKING-01","title":"Forgery - false making is the actus reus","signal":"A finder of unused checks fills in a check, signs the owner''s name without authority, and plans to cash it. The owner has not actually lost money, and the check has not been cashed.","student_move":"Cut tiered-absolute overclaims (checks have apparent legal efficacy) and wrong-element traps (uttering is not the actus reus of forgery). Then apply the Gold Key: the victim''s actual loss is not an element of forgery; the false making is the actus reus.","tiny_rule":"Forgery is complete at the false making of a writing with apparent legal efficacy and intent to defraud; loss and successful uttering are not elements.","trap":"The lay intuition ''no harm, no foul'' is wrong. The victim need not actually lose money, and the forger need not successfully utter the document.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CRIMINAL-CAMP-STIPEND-01","statement":"Forgery is complete when the defendant falsely makes a writing with apparent legal efficacy with intent to defraud; the victim need not actually lose money, and the forger need not successfully utter the document. The ''false making'' is the actus reus.","type":"rule","trigger":"writing falsely made and signed without authority with intent to defraud","authority":"Model Penal Code Section 241.1; common-law forgery (apparent legal efficacy test); state forgery statutes tracking the same elements","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CRIMINAL-CAMP-STIPEND-01","statement":"When the call asks ''has the defendant committed forgery,'' ask whether the false making has occurred. Loss and successful uttering are not elements of forgery.","type":"call","trigger":"question about forgery / uttering / false writing","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"anchor","target_skill":"forgery false-making rule","prompt":"A finder of unused checks fills in a check for $500 payable to ''cash,'' signs the owner''s name without authority, and plans to cash it. The check is never cashed and the owner never loses money. Has the finder committed forgery?","answer":"Yes. Forgery is complete at the false making of a writing with apparent legal efficacy and intent to defraud. The victim''s actual loss is not an element, and successful uttering is not required. Model Penal Code § 241.1; common-law ''apparent legal efficacy'' test."},{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"wrong-element (uttering)","prompt":"Same facts, but the forger is arrested before reaching the check-cashing store. The check is never presented. Has the forger committed forgery?","answer":"Yes. The false making is complete; successful uttering is a separate crime, not an element of forgery."},{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"tiered-absolute overclaim","prompt":"A student argues that a check is not a ''writing with legal significance'' for forgery purposes. Is the student correct?","answer":"No. A check payable to ''cash'' that purports to draw on a depositor''s account has apparent legal efficacy because, if presented and honored, it would transfer funds. The ''apparent legal efficacy'' test is satisfied."}]}'),
  ('ce1d8a4f-bb96-538a-b598-61620bcd8b55', '18135_mountain_lodge_looseboard', 'TORTS', 'Negligence', 'Duty — land possessor and invitee', 'invitee reasonable-care-including-inspection duty versus licensee warn-only-of-known-hazards rule for a paying attendee at a ticketed event', 'A Christian retreat center in the foothills charged $40 admission to a Saturday women''s ministry day-retreat. For several days, a wooden floorboard on the lodge''s front porch had been rocking loose whenever anyone stepped on it. The retreat center had no inspection schedule for the entrance area. Mary, an attendee, walked onto the porch to enter the lodge and tripped on the loose board, injuring her ankle.', 'A Christian retreat center in the foothills charged $40 admission to a Saturday women''s ministry day-retreat. For several days, a wooden floorboard on the lodge''s front porch had been rocking loose whenever anyone stepped on it. The retreat center had no inspection schedule for the entrance area. Mary, an attendee, walked onto the porch to enter the lodge and tripped on the loose board, injuring her ankle.

What duty did the retreat center owe Mary?', 'What duty did the retreat center owe Mary?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ18135.md","source_question_id":"18135","variant_slug":"mountain_lodge_looseboard","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"61020100","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium","deciding_phase":"CALL","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A paying attendee at a Christian retreat center trips on a porch floorboard that had been loose for several days; the retreat center had no inspection schedule. What duty does the retreat center owe the attendee?","review_truth":"A landowner who invites the public onto the premises for a paid event owes invitees reasonable care, including reasonable inspection for hazards the possessor should discover; the narrower ''warn-only-of-known-hazards'' rule is the licensee standard, not the invitee standard.","dominant_trap":"C","anchor_card":{"card_id":"RC-TORTS-INVITEE-DUTY-01","title":"Invitee vs licensee duty — invitees get the inspection duty","signal":"Possessor invites the public onto the premises for a paid event; entrant is injured; call asks what duty the possessor owed.","student_move":"Classify the entrant first. If the entrant paid admission (or otherwise entered for a purpose that benefits the possessor) → invitee → reasonable care including inspection. If the entrant entered for the entrant''s own purpose with permission → licensee → warn only of known hazards.","tiny_rule":"Invitees get the inspection duty; the ''warn only of known'' rule is the licensee standard.","trap":"The dominant trap is the invitee-vs-licensee conflation. Many students half-remember that ''landowners only have to warn'' and pick the narrower licensee rule for paying invitees.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-TORTS-MOUNTAIN-LODGE-LOOSEBOARD-01","statement":"Invitees get the inspection duty; the ''warn only of known'' rule is the licensee standard, not the invitee standard. A paying attendee at a ticketed event is an invitee, and the duty that follows is the broader reasonable-care-including-inspection rule.","type":"rule","trigger":"possessor invites the public onto the premises for a paid event; entrant is injured; call asks what duty the possessor owed","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Torts §§ 332 (invitee), 343 (duty to invitees); §§ 330, 341 (licensees — narrower rule)","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-TORTS-MOUNTAIN-LODGE-LOOSEBOARD-01","statement":"When the call asks ''what duty did the possessor owe,'' first classify the entrant (invitee / licensee / trespasser), then match the duty to the entrant''s status.","type":"call_focus","trigger":"possessor invites the public onto the premises; entrant is injured; call is the duty question","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"entrant classification (invitee / licensee / trespasser)","prompt":"A shopper pays admission to enter a botanical garden conservatory. The conservatory has a wet floor near the entrance with no warning sign. The shopper slips. What duty does the conservatory owe the shopper?","answer":"Reasonable care, including reasonable inspection. The shopper paid admission and entered for a purpose that benefits the conservatory, which makes the shopper an invitee. The ''warn only of known'' rule is the licensee standard and does not apply."},{"drill_type":"anchor","target_skill":"invitee vs licensee duty","prompt":"A guest at a backyard barbecue trips on a loose board on the host''s deck. The host had noticed the loose board a week earlier. The guest sues. What duty did the host owe?","answer":"Reasonable care to warn of known hazards. The guest entered for the guest''s own social purpose with the host''s permission, which makes the guest a licensee. The narrower warn-of-known rule applies. Restatement (Second) of Torts §§ 330, 341."},{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"overclaim test (strict liability for premises)","prompt":"A paying customer is injured by a falling display in a store. The customer sues and argues the store is strictly liable. Is the store strictly liable?","answer":"No. Ordinary premises liability for invitees is a duty of reasonable care, not strict liability. Strict liability is a separate doctrine (abnormally dangerous activities, products liability) and is not the default rule for ordinary premises."}]}'),
  ('91f816a2-20ee-585b-bff4-c2f12b59d477', '18179_campfire_rim_warning', 'TORTS', 'Products Liability', 'Failure to warn - obvious danger', 'tiered-absolute ''always warn'' intuition versus open-and-obvious non-duty rule', 'A steel campfire ring is sold for outdoor use by a small firepit manufacturer in Tennessee. The ring is not defective, and its rim getting hot during use is a property that is visible and ordinary for a steel campfire ring. Stephen, a youth volunteer at a church summer retreat, was roasting marshmallows over the campfire ring and severely burned his hand when it touched the rim. Stephen sues the manufacturer for failure to warn that the rim can burn skin.', 'A steel campfire ring is sold for outdoor use by a small firepit manufacturer in Tennessee. The ring is not defective, and its rim getting hot during use is a property that is visible and ordinary for a steel campfire ring. Stephen, a youth volunteer at a church summer retreat, was roasting marshmallows over the campfire ring and severely burned his hand when it touched the rim. Stephen sues the manufacturer for failure to warn that the rim can burn skin.

Is the failure-to-warn claim likely strong?', 'Is the failure-to-warn claim likely strong?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ18179.md","source_question_id":"18179","variant_slug":"campfire_rim_warning","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"63030100","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium","deciding_phase":"CALL","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A user is injured by an obvious, commonly understood danger of an ordinary product (a steel campfire ring whose rim gets hot during use). He sues the manufacturer for failure to warn. Is the failure-to-warn claim strong?","review_truth":"A manufacturer has no duty to warn of dangers that are open, obvious, and commonly understood by ordinary users of an ordinary product.","dominant_trap":"C","anchor_card":{"card_id":"RC-TORTS-OBVIOUS-DANGER-01","title":"Open and obvious danger — failure to warn does not arise","signal":"An ordinary product, an ordinary-use risk, and a plaintiff sues for failure to warn of that exact risk.","student_move":"Cut the consequence-instead-of-cause and the categorical-exclusion distractors first. Then apply the Gold Key: open-and-obvious risks never trigger the duty to warn.","tiny_rule":"Open and obvious = no duty to warn.","trap":"The dominant trap is the ''every dangerous product needs a warning'' claim (tiered absolute). It is engineered to look like the safe default; the doctrine carves out the obvious risks.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-TORTS-CAMPFIRE-RIM-WARNING-01","statement":"A manufacturer has no duty to warn of dangers that are open, obvious, and commonly understood by ordinary users of an ordinary product. The doctrine operates as a non-duty rule, not as an affirmative defense: the duty never arises for risks a reasonable user can plainly perceive.","type":"rule","trigger":"ordinary product, ordinary-use risk, plaintiff sues for failure to warn","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 402A, cmt. j; Restatement (Third) of Torts: Products Liability § 2, cmt. f","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-TORTS-CAMPFIRE-RIM-WARNING-01","statement":"Run the open-and-obvious check on the SPECIFIC risk the plaintiff claims was undisclosed. The duty to warn fails when the very risk the plaintiff names is the risk any ordinary user would plainly perceive.","type":"call_focus","trigger":"stem names a specific risk in the failure-to-warn allegation","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"missing-element test (injury-claim misframing)","prompt":"A user is injured by an ordinary-use risk of an ordinary product. The plaintiff sues for failure to warn. Is the claim strong because the user suffered physical injury?","answer":"No. Injury alone does not create a warning defect. The duty must be grounded in a non-obvious product risk; the user''s injury is the consequence of the risk, not the trigger for the duty."},{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"misstated-rule test (categorical exclusion)","prompt":"A plaintiff sues the maker of an outdoor firepit for failure to warn. Is the claim defeated because fire rings are never products for products-liability purposes?","answer":"No. A steel campfire ring sold by a manufacturer is a product for § 402A and Restatement (Third) purposes; the ''never a product'' claim is a categorical exclusion the doctrine does not contain."},{"drill_type":"anchor","target_skill":"open-and-obvious non-duty rule","prompt":"A user is injured by an obvious, commonly understood danger of an ordinary product (a steel campfire ring whose rim gets hot during use). He sues the manufacturer for failure to warn. Is the claim strong?","answer":"No. The rim getting hot during use is open, obvious, and commonly understood by ordinary users. The open-and-obvious non-duty rule eliminates the duty to warn for risks a reasonable user can plainly perceive. The claim is weak."}]}'),
  ('6872b1f9-b47e-5f55-8fbe-80f590de2c45', '18182_bookstore_mandamus', 'CIVIL_PROCEDURE', 'Appeals and Finality', 'Mandamus', 'tiered-absolute ''mandamus is available whenever'' intuition versus clear-and-indisputable-right + no-adequate-alternative-means standard', 'Mary and Daniel co-owned a small private bookstore in Tennessee. After they dissolved the partnership, Mary sued Daniel in federal district court for breach of fiduciary duty, alleging that Daniel had diverted partnership opportunities to a side business while still a co-owner. The district court denied Daniel''s ordinary Rule 12(b)(6) motion to dismiss for failure to state a claim. Daniel then seeks a writ of mandamus from the court of appeals, arguing that trial will be expensive and that the judge probably made a legal mistake in denying the motion.', 'Mary and Daniel co-owned a small private bookstore in Tennessee. After they dissolved the partnership, Mary sued Daniel in federal district court for breach of fiduciary duty, alleging that Daniel had diverted partnership opportunities to a side business while still a co-owner. The district court denied Daniel''s ordinary Rule 12(b)(6) motion to dismiss for failure to state a claim. Daniel then seeks a writ of mandamus from the court of appeals, arguing that trial will be expensive and that the judge probably made a legal mistake in denying the motion.

Is mandamus likely available on those facts?', 'Is mandamus likely available on those facts?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ18182.md","source_question_id":"18182","variant_slug":"bookstore_mandamus","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"93110100","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium","deciding_phase":"CALL","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A federal district court denies a defendant''s Rule 12(b)(6) motion to dismiss in a private civil suit, and the defendant seeks a writ of mandamus from the court of appeals on the ground that trial will be expensive and the judge probably made a legal mistake. Is mandamus available?","review_truth":"Mandamus is an extraordinary remedy that requires a clear and indisputable right and no adequate alternative means of relief; ordinary litigation expense and a likely legal error do not satisfy the standard.","dominant_trap":"C","anchor_card":{"card_id":"RC-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-MANDAMUS-STANDARD-01","title":"Mandamus — clear and indisputable right + no adequate alternative means","signal":"A defendant seeks a writ of mandamus to challenge an ordinary interlocutory ruling on weak grounds (litigation expense, likely legal error).","student_move":"Cut the consequence-instead-of-cause and the categorical-abolition distractors first. Then apply the Gold Key: the writ is reserved for extraordinary causes; the eventual final-judgment appeal is an adequate alternative means.","tiny_rule":"Mandamus = clear-and-indisputable-right AND no-adequate-alternative-means. Ordinary legal error does not clear the bar.","trap":"The dominant trap is the ''mandamus is available whenever a party thinks an interlocutory ruling is wrong'' claim (tiered absolute). It is engineered to look like the safe default; the doctrine narrows the writ to extraordinary circumstances.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-BOOKSTORE-MANDAMUS-01","statement":"Mandamus is an extraordinary writ. It requires the petitioner to show a clear and indisputable right to the relief sought AND the absence of any adequate alternative means of relief. The writ is not a substitute for an appeal; ordinary legal error in denying a routine motion does not clear the standard.","type":"rule","trigger":"ordinary interlocutory ruling; defendant seeks mandamus on weak grounds (expense, likely error)","authority":"All Writs Act, 28 U.S.C. § 1651(a); Roche v. Evaporated Milk Ass''n, 319 U.S. 21, 26 (1943); Bankers Life & Casualty Co. v. Holland, 346 U.S. 379 (1953)","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-BOOKSTORE-MANDAMUS-01","statement":"When the call asks whether mandamus is available for an ordinary interlocutory ruling, run the ''clear and indisputable right'' check first. Ordinary legal error in denying a routine motion does not clear the bar; the eventual final-judgment appeal is an adequate alternative means.","type":"call_focus","trigger":"stem describes an ordinary interlocutory ruling and a defendant seeking mandamus on weak grounds","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"missing-element test (expense-alone misframing)","prompt":"A defendant seeks a writ of mandamus to challenge an ordinary interlocutory ruling. Is the writ available because trial will be expensive?","answer":"No. Ordinary litigation expense does not, on its own, supply the clear-and-indisputable-right element; the writ is reserved for extraordinary causes."},{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"misstated-rule test (categorical abolition)","prompt":"Is mandamus abolished in federal civil cases?","answer":"No. The All Writs Act, 28 U.S.C. § 1651(a), still authorizes the writ in civil cases, but only in extraordinary circumstances."},{"drill_type":"anchor","target_skill":"mandamus two-prong standard","prompt":"A federal district court denies a defendant''s Rule 12(b)(6) motion to dismiss, and the defendant seeks a writ of mandamus on the ground that trial will be expensive and the judge probably made a legal mistake. Is mandamus available?","answer":"No. Mandamus requires a clear and indisputable right AND no adequate alternative means of relief. A denial of a Rule 12(b)(6) motion is an ordinary interlocutory ruling; the defendant can challenge the legal error in an appeal after final judgment under 28 U.S.C. § 1291, so the no-adequate-alternative prong is not satisfied."}]}'),
  ('ec43a1f2-6eee-5d0d-8960-371fb22af5f7', '18196_riverstone_citizenship', 'CIVIL_PROCEDURE', 'Subject Matter Jurisdiction', 'Diversity - Corporate Citizenship', 'incorporation-only intuition versus dual-citizenship rule', 'Mary, a citizen of Tennessee, sues RiverStone Logistics, Inc., in federal court for $100,000 under state law for breach of a freight-services contract. RiverStone is incorporated in Nevada, but its headquarters and nerve center are in Tennessee.', 'Mary, a citizen of Tennessee, sues RiverStone Logistics, Inc., in federal court for $100,000 under state law for breach of a freight-services contract. RiverStone is incorporated in Nevada, but its headquarters and nerve center are in Tennessee.

Is complete diversity present?', 'Is complete diversity present?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ18196.md","source_question_id":"18196","variant_slug":"riverstone_citizenship","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"94010100","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium","deciding_phase":"CALL","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A plaintiff sues a corporation that is incorporated in one state and has its principal place of business in the plaintiff''s home state. The amount in controversy exceeds $75,000 and the claim is a state-law claim. Is complete diversity present?","review_truth":"For Section 1332, a corporation is a citizen of BOTH its state of incorporation AND the state of its principal place of business (nerve center); the plaintiff and the corporation cannot both be citizens of the same state for complete diversity.","dominant_trap":"B","anchor_card":{"card_id":"RC-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-CORPORATE-CITIZENSHIP-01","title":"Corporate citizenship — dual citizenship for § 1332","signal":"A corporate defendant is incorporated in one state and has its nerve center in the plaintiff''s home state; amount in controversy exceeds $75,000; claim is state-law.","student_move":"Cut the state-law-claim framing and the one-citizenship misstatement first. Then apply the Gold Key: corporate citizenship is dual, and the principal place of business is also a citizenship.","tiny_rule":"Corporation = state of incorporation + state of nerve center (PPB). Both must differ from every plaintiff''s citizenship for complete diversity.","trap":"The dominant trap is the ''incorporation is the only citizenship'' half-truth. It sounds correct on its face and is the most attractive wrong answer for students who half-remember the incorporation rule.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-RIVERSTONE-CITIZENSHIP-01","statement":"A corporation is a citizen of BOTH its state of incorporation AND the state of its principal place of business (nerve center) for purposes of 28 U.S.C. § 1332. The plaintiff and the corporation cannot both be citizens of the same state for complete diversity to be present.","type":"rule","trigger":"corporate defendant with dual-citizenship structure; plaintiff shares one of the corporate citizenships","authority":"28 U.S.C. § 1332(c)(1); Hertz Corp. v. Friend, 559 U.S. 77 (2010)","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-RIVERSTONE-CITIZENSHIP-01","statement":"When the call asks whether complete diversity is present, run the dual-citizenship check on the corporate defendant first. If the corporation''s nerve center is in the plaintiff''s home state, diversity is destroyed — regardless of the state of incorporation.","type":"call_focus","trigger":"stem describes a corporate defendant with a dual-citizenship structure and a plaintiff sharing one of the corporate citizenships","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"misframing test (state-law-claim framing)","prompt":"A plaintiff sues a corporation in federal court for $100,000 under state law. The corporation is incorporated in one state and has its nerve center in the plaintiff''s home state. Is complete diversity destroyed because the claim is based on state law?","answer":"No. A state-law claim is proper in diversity when § 1332 is otherwise satisfied. The only defect is the destroyed diversity — not the state-law nature of the claim."},{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"misstated-rule test (one-citizenship misstatement)","prompt":"A plaintiff sues a corporation in federal court. The corporation is incorporated in Nevada and has its nerve center in the plaintiff''s home state. Is the corporation a citizen of only one state for § 1332?","answer":"No. A corporation is a citizen of BOTH its state of incorporation AND the state of its principal place of business. 28 U.S.C. § 1332(c)(1). The ''only one citizenship'' claim is a categorical exclusion the doctrine does not contain."},{"drill_type":"anchor","target_skill":"dual-citizenship rule for corporate defendants","prompt":"A plaintiff sues a corporation in federal court. The corporation is incorporated in one state and has its nerve center in the plaintiff''s home state. Is complete diversity present?","answer":"No. A corporation is a citizen of both its state of incorporation and the state of its principal place of business. The plaintiff and the corporation share the nerve-center state''s citizenship; complete diversity is destroyed."}]}'),
  ('97c4197c-4415-5af4-b776-30bb39fe1b3d', '18197_athletics_bus_collision', 'TORTS', 'Other Torts', 'Joint tortfeasors — joint and several liability', 'joint and several liability lets the plaintiff collect 100% from any one defendant versus proportional liability (50% split) based on the defendants'' inter-se contribution', 'A Christian academy operates a school-bus service for its athletic program. Lydia, an employee of the academy, was driving a school bus to a Saturday basketball game at another Christian school when the bus collided with a car driven by Paul. Hannah, a parent volunteer riding on the bus to help supervise the team, was injured. The court held the academy, Lydia, and Paul jointly and severally liable for Hannah''s damages, and the jury found Hannah to be free from fault.', 'A Christian academy operates a school-bus service for its athletic program. Lydia, an employee of the academy, was driving a school bus to a Saturday basketball game at another Christian school when the bus collided with a car driven by Paul. Hannah, a parent volunteer riding on the bus to help supervise the team, was injured. The court held the academy, Lydia, and Paul jointly and severally liable for Hannah''s damages, and the jury found Hannah to be free from fault.

In enforcing the judgment, what portion of her damages is Hannah entitled to collect from the employee, Lydia?', 'In enforcing the judgment, what portion of her damages is Hannah entitled to collect from the employee, Lydia?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ18197.md","source_question_id":"18197","variant_slug":"athletics_bus_collision","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"62060201","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium","deciding_phase":"CALL","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"Three defendants (a Christian academy, the academy''s bus-driver employee, and the other driver) are jointly and severally liable to an injured plaintiff who was found free of fault. What portion of the damages can the plaintiff collect from the bus-driver employee alone?","review_truth":"When tortfeasors are jointly and severally liable, a plaintiff is entitled to collect 100 percent of damages from any one of them; contribution among joint tortfeasors is a separate inter-se problem that does not affect the plaintiff''s right to collect the full judgment from any single defendant.","dominant_trap":"A","anchor_card":{"card_id":"RC-TORTS-JOINT-AND-SEVERAL-01","title":"Joint and several liability — plaintiff collects 100% from any one defendant; contribution is inter-se","signal":"Multiple defendants held jointly and severally liable; plaintiff free of fault; call asks what portion the plaintiff may collect from one defendant.","student_move":"Distinguish the plaintiff''s collection right from the defendants'' inter-se contribution problem. The plaintiff may collect 100% from any one defendant; the defendants'' inter-se contribution claim is a separate question.","tiny_rule":"Joint and several = plaintiff collects 100% from any one defendant; the proportional split is the defendants'' inter-se problem, not the plaintiff''s.","trap":"The dominant trap is the proportional-liability / contribution confusion. Many students half-remember that ''multiple defendants means a proportional split'' and apply the defendants'' inter-se rule to the plaintiff''s collection right.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-TORTS-ATHLETICS-BUS-COLLISION-01","statement":"Joint and several liability lets the plaintiff collect 100% of damages from any one defendant. The proportional split between defendants is the inter-se contribution problem, not the plaintiff''s problem.","type":"rule","trigger":"multiple defendants held jointly and severally liable; plaintiff free of fault; call asks what portion the plaintiff may collect from one defendant","authority":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Apportionment of Liability §§ 1, 4; Uniform Contribution Among Tortfeasors Act (UCATA)","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-TORTS-ATHLETICS-BUS-COLLISION-01","statement":"When the call asks ''what portion can the plaintiff collect from one defendant'' and the stem says ''jointly and severally liable,'' the answer is 100%.","type":"call_focus","trigger":"multiple defendants held jointly and severally liable; plaintiff free of fault; call is the plaintiff''s collection right","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"misfit test (joint and several — plaintiff can collect from any one defendant)","prompt":"Three defendants are held jointly and severally liable to a plaintiff for $300,000 in damages. The plaintiff sues one defendant for the full $300,000. The defendant argues the plaintiff can collect nothing because the other defendants are also liable. Is the defendant correct?","answer":"No. Joint and several liability lets the plaintiff enforce the full judgment against any one defendant. The plaintiff may collect the entire $300,000 from any one of the three defendants. The defendants'' inter-se contribution problem is a separate question."},{"drill_type":"anchor","target_skill":"plaintiff''s-collection-right-vs-defendants''-inter-se-contribution distinction","prompt":"Two defendants are held jointly and severally liable to a plaintiff for $200,000. The plaintiff collects the full $200,000 from Defendant 1. Defendant 1 then sues Defendant 2 for contribution. Defendant 2 argues the plaintiff should have collected $100,000 from each, so Defendant 1 cannot recover. Is Defendant 2 correct?","answer":"No. Joint and several liability let the plaintiff collect the full $200,000 from Defendant 1. The proportional split is the defendants'' inter-se contribution problem, not the plaintiff''s problem. Defendant 1 may recover Defendant 2''s equitable share from Defendant 2 under the Uniform Contribution Among Tortfeasors Act."},{"drill_type":"anchor","target_skill":"comparative-negligence irrelevance when plaintiff is free of fault","prompt":"A plaintiff is found free from fault in a multi-defendant tort case. The defendants are held jointly and severally liable. Does the existence of a comparative-negligence statute change the plaintiff''s collection right?","answer":"No. Comparative negligence affects the plaintiff''s recovery, not the defendants'' inter-se shares. Where the plaintiff is free from fault, comparative negligence is irrelevant to the plaintiff''s collection right; the plaintiff may still collect 100% from any one defendant under joint and several liability."}]}'),
  ('eddf3462-7b66-5e2d-927f-7d5087df6e75', '18240_family_table_refund', 'CIVIL_PROCEDURE', 'Subject Matter Jurisdiction', 'Diversity - Amount in Controversy', 'legal-certainty test (statute or contract cap required) versus probability-of-recovery test (defendant''s discovery prediction)', 'Naomi, a Washington resident, contracted with Paul, a California furniture maker, to hand-build a custom dining table for the family home she shares with her husband. The contract was for a meaningful, hand-forged iron base and a carved Bible-verse inscription on the apron. Naomi paid $90,000 in advance. When Paul delivered the table, the apron had warped so badly that the verse inscription was illegible, and the iron base was unstable. Naomi, who is the plaintiff in the action, is a Washington citizen; Paul, the defendant, is a California citizen. The parties are completely diverse. Naomi sues Paul in federal court for $90,000 in compensatory damages. Paul moves to dismiss for lack of subject-matter jurisdiction, arguing that discovery will probably show her actual damages are only $40,000. No statute or contract provision caps Naomi''s recovery below $75,000.', 'Naomi, a Washington resident, contracted with Paul, a California furniture maker, to hand-build a custom dining table for the family home she shares with her husband. The contract was for a meaningful, hand-forged iron base and a carved Bible-verse inscription on the apron. Naomi paid $90,000 in advance. When Paul delivered the table, the apron had warped so badly that the verse inscription was illegible, and the iron base was unstable. Naomi, who is the plaintiff in the action, is a Washington citizen; Paul, the defendant, is a California citizen. The parties are completely diverse. Naomi sues Paul in federal court for $90,000 in compensatory damages. Paul moves to dismiss for lack of subject-matter jurisdiction, arguing that discovery will probably show her actual damages are only $40,000. No statute or contract provision caps Naomi''s recovery below $75,000.

Should the court dismiss for lack of the amount in controversy?', 'Should the court dismiss for lack of the amount in controversy?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ18240.md","source_question_id":"18240","variant_slug":"family_table_refund","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"94010100","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium","deciding_phase":"CALL","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A diverse plaintiff sues a diverse defendant in federal court for $90,000 in compensatory damages; the defendant argues discovery will probably show only $40,000; no statute or contract caps recovery below $75,000. Should the court dismiss for lack of the amount in controversy?","review_truth":"A good-faith allegation of damages in excess of the amount-in-controversy threshold controls unless it appears to a legal certainty that the plaintiff cannot recover more than that amount.","dominant_trap":"C","anchor_card":{"card_id":"RC-CIVPRO-AMOUNT-CERTAINTY-01","title":"Amount in controversy — legal certainty, not probability","signal":"Diverse parties, good-faith pleading above the $75,000 threshold, defendant argues discovery will show lower damages, no statute or contract caps recovery below the threshold. The call is whether the court will dismiss for lack of amount in controversy.","student_move":"Check the legal-certainty test. If no statute or contract makes it legally impossible to recover more than the threshold, the good-faith pleading controls and the court will not dismiss. A defendant''s prediction of low recovery is a fact, not a legal bar.","tiny_rule":"A good-faith allegation of damages in excess of the amount-in-controversy threshold controls unless it appears to a legal certainty that the plaintiff cannot recover more than that amount.","trap":"The probability-vs-certainty trap is the dominant misconception. Many students substitute ''discovery will probably show lower damages'' for a legal cap and walk into the dismissal answer.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CIVPRO-FAMILY-TABLE-01","statement":"Under the legal-certainty test, a federal court will not dismiss for lack of amount in controversy unless it appears to a legal certainty that the plaintiff cannot recover more than the statutory threshold. A defendant''s prediction that discovery will reduce actual recovery is a factual probability, not a legal bar.","type":"rule","trigger":"diverse parties, good-faith pleading above the threshold, defendant argues discovery will show lower damages, no statute or contract caps recovery below the threshold","authority":"St. Paul Mercury Indemnity Co. v. Red Cab Co., 303 U.S. 283 (1938); 28 U.S.C. § 1332(a)","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CIVPRO-FAMILY-TABLE-01","statement":"A defendant''s contention that the plaintiff''s damages are lower than pleaded does not itself defeat the amount in controversy. Contested damages go to the merits, not to subject-matter jurisdiction.","type":"misfit","trigger":"defendant''s motion to dismiss asserts that discovery will show lower damages (or otherwise disputes the pleaded amount)","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"misfit test (issue-senses)","prompt":"A diverse plaintiff sues a diverse defendant for $90,000 in compensatory damages. The defendant moves to dismiss, arguing that the court lacks federal-question jurisdiction. Is the defendant''s argument a basis for dismissal?","answer":"No. Federal-question jurisdiction is not in play when the suit is grounded in diversity of citizenship. The defendant''s argument is a misfit; the call is the amount question (or the diversity question), not whether a federal claim is pleaded."},{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"merits-vs-jurisdiction distinction","prompt":"A diverse plaintiff sues a diverse defendant for $90,000 in compensatory damages. The defendant moves to dismiss, arguing that discovery will probably show actual damages of $40,000. Is the defendant''s argument a basis for dismissal at the pleading stage?","answer":"No. A defendant''s prediction that discovery will reduce actual recovery is a factual probability, not a legal cap. The damages dispute goes to the merits, not to subject-matter jurisdiction. The court will not dismiss at the pleading stage; the case may be dismissed for lack of proof on the merits if discovery bears out the prediction."},{"drill_type":"anchor","target_skill":"legal-certainty test (St. Paul Mercury)","prompt":"A diverse plaintiff sues a diverse defendant for $90,000 in compensatory damages. No statute or contract caps recovery below $75,000. Will the court dismiss for lack of amount in controversy?","answer":"No. Under St. Paul Mercury, a federal court will not dismiss for lack of amount in controversy unless it appears to a legal certainty that the plaintiff cannot recover more than the threshold. No statute or contract cap exists here, so the legal ceiling is at least $90,000 and the good-faith pleading controls."}]}'),
  ('a261ef50-40f2-538b-95ae-2efcafa91a66', '18287_manger_cradle_collapse', 'TORTS', 'Negligence', 'Breach — Res ipsa loquitur (rented equipment; plaintiff alteration as competing cause)', 'res ipsa clearly applies (never unless the maker was negligent) versus res ipsa is unavailable because the plaintiff''s own post-rental alterations are a plausible alternative cause', 'Mary, the drama-ministry director at a small church, rented a hand-carved olive-wood manger cradle from Daniel, a Christian craftsman who runs a small home-based Nativity-prop rental business, to use as the centerpiece of the church''s outdoor Living Nativity display. The rental agreement was a standard weekly rental at a normal fee. Mary took the cradle home a week before Christmas Eve. To thread a hidden low-voltage LED light cord through the base so the display would not show wires, Mary drilled four small pilot holes in the cradle''s bottom plate. On Christmas Eve, while Mary was setting the carved baby-Jesus figurine on the cradle, the cradle''s right side rail snapped away from the base and the figurine fell. Mary cannot show whether the failure came from a hidden joint defect in Daniel''s carving or from Mary''s drilled pilot holes.', 'Mary, the drama-ministry director at a small church, rented a hand-carved olive-wood manger cradle from Daniel, a Christian craftsman who runs a small home-based Nativity-prop rental business, to use as the centerpiece of the church''s outdoor Living Nativity display. The rental agreement was a standard weekly rental at a normal fee. Mary took the cradle home a week before Christmas Eve. To thread a hidden low-voltage LED light cord through the base so the display would not show wires, Mary drilled four small pilot holes in the cradle''s bottom plate. On Christmas Eve, while Mary was setting the carved baby-Jesus figurine on the cradle, the cradle''s right side rail snapped away from the base and the figurine fell. Mary cannot show whether the failure came from a hidden joint defect in Daniel''s carving or from Mary''s drilled pilot holes.

What is the best res ipsa loquitur analysis of Mary''s res ipsa claim against Daniel?', 'What is the best res ipsa loquitur analysis of Mary''s res ipsa claim against Daniel?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ18287.md","source_question_id":"18287","variant_slug":"manger_cradle_collapse","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"61020304","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium","deciding_phase":"CLASH","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A renter of a hand-crafted item (a wooden display piece) alters it after rental and the item later collapses; the renter cannot tell whether the collapse came from a defect in the item or the renter''s own alteration. Does the res ipsa inference survive?","review_truth":"Res ipsa is weakened or unavailable when a plaintiff''s post-rental alteration is a plausible competing cause of the accident.","dominant_trap":"C","anchor_card":{"card_id":"RC-TORTS-RES-IPSA-ALTERATION-01","title":"Res ipsa and the third-prong plaintiff-contribution test","signal":"The plaintiff has materially altered the instrumentality after leaving the defendant''s control, and the cause of the accident is undetermined between the defendant''s defect and the plaintiff''s alteration.","student_move":"Apply the third prong of the res ipsa test: a post-rental plaintiff alteration is a plausible alternative cause wholly attributable to the plaintiff, and the inference collapses.","tiny_rule":"Res ipsa is unavailable when the plaintiff''s own post-receipt conduct is a plausible alternative cause.","trap":"The ''never unless the maker was negligent'' wording is the dominant trap; carved wooden items can and do fail when a renter drills into the structural base.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-TORTS-MANGER-CRADLE-COLLAPSE-01","statement":"Res ipsa is an evidentiary inference of negligence that requires the absence of voluntary action or contribution by the plaintiff; a post-rental plaintiff alteration is a plausible alternative cause that defeats the inference.","type":"rule","trigger":"rental of a physical item + post-rental plaintiff alteration + undetermined-cause collapse","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 328D (third element and cmt. e)","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-TORTS-MANGER-CRADLE-COLLAPSE-01","statement":"When the call asks for a res ipsa analysis, the third-prong plaintiff-contribution test is the deciding axis; do not pivot to strict-liability or statutory-violation frames.","type":"call_focus","trigger":"call asks for ''best res ipsa analysis'' and one of the choices proposes a strict-liability or statutory-violation answer","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"call focus","prompt":"The call asks for a res ipsa analysis. A choice proposes that res ipsa makes the defendant strictly liable for all damage. Is this responsive to the call?","answer":"No. Res ipsa is an evidentiary inference of negligence, not a strict-liability theory. The choice answers a different liability question."},{"drill_type":"anchor","target_skill":"third-prong plaintiff-contribution test","prompt":"The plaintiff rented an item, altered it after rental, and the item later failed in a way the plaintiff cannot explain. Does res ipsa apply?","answer":"No. The plaintiff''s own post-receipt alteration is a plausible alternative cause wholly attributable to the plaintiff. The third prong of the res ipsa test is not satisfied, and the inference collapses."},{"drill_type":"trap_spotting","target_skill":"absolute-when-uncertain overclaim","prompt":"A choice says ''res ipsa clearly applies because hand-carved X never fails unless the maker was negligent.'' What is the tell?","answer":"The ''never unless'' wording is an absolute-overclaim. Hand-carved items can and do fail when a renter alters them; the ''never unless'' claim is not supported by common experience."}]}'),
  ('75d3bce4-0493-5f32-8454-613042df58f4', '18298_fellowship_hall_grab', 'TORTS', 'Intentional Torts', 'Assault — plaintiff must apprehend imminent contact with herself (not with a third party)', 'bystander wins on transferred intent versus bystander loses because she did not apprehend contact with herself', 'Daniel is a deacon at a small Christian church. On a Sunday morning, an angry stranger walked into the fellowship hall and grabbed Mary, another church member, by the arm and tried to drag her out of the building. Daniel, standing across the room, saw the entire episode. The stranger never looked at Daniel, never made any threatening gesture toward Daniel, and never approached him.', 'Daniel is a deacon at a small Christian church. On a Sunday morning, an angry stranger walked into the fellowship hall and grabbed Mary, another church member, by the arm and tried to drag her out of the building. Daniel, standing across the room, saw the entire episode. The stranger never looked at Daniel, never made any threatening gesture toward Daniel, and never approached him.

Daniel sues the stranger for assault, claiming he was put in apprehension by what he saw. The court should find for', 'Daniel sues the stranger for assault, claiming he was put in apprehension by what he saw. The court should find for', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ18298.md","source_question_id":"18298","variant_slug":"fellowship_hall_grab","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"64010101","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium","deciding_phase":"CALL","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A bystander (the plaintiff) watches a stranger violently grab a third party (the intended victim). The stranger makes no threatening gesture toward the bystander. Does the bystander have an assault claim against the stranger?","review_truth":"Assault requires the plaintiff to be the one who apprehends imminent harmful or offensive contact with herself; transferred intent does not supply the missing plaintiff-side apprehension.","dominant_trap":"C","anchor_card":{"card_id":"RC-TORTS-ASSAULT-THIRD-PARTY-01","title":"Assault — plaintiff-side apprehension and the transferred-intent scope","signal":"A bystander (the plaintiff) sues an attacker for assault over harm the attacker directed at a third party; the attacker never threatened the bystander.","student_move":"Apply the assault elements: the plaintiff must apprehend imminent contact with HERSELF. The transferred-intent doctrine does not supply a missing plaintiff-side apprehension.","tiny_rule":"Transferred intent does not apply to assault.","trap":"The transferred-intent doctrine is a real doctrine; the trap is the doctrinal misapplication, not the doctrine''s existence. Also: ''awareness of fright'' is not ''apprehension of contact with self.''","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-TORTS-FELLOWSHIP-HALL-GRAB-01","statement":"The transferred-intent doctrine applies to battery (and certain other intentional torts against the person) when the defendant intends to commit a tort against one person but commits a different tort against a different person; it does not apply to assault — the assault plaintiff must be the person put in apprehension of contact with herself.","type":"rule","trigger":"bystander claims assault over harm to a third party and the defendant never threatened the bystander","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Torts §§ 16, 21","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-TORTS-FELLOWSHIP-HALL-GRAB-01","statement":"When a bystander claims assault over harm to a third party, the deciding axis is whether the bystander apprehended contact with HERSELF, not whether she perceived injury to the third party or whether the defendant was aware his conduct would frighten her.","type":"call_focus","trigger":"stem describes violence directed at a third party, with the bystander claiming assault","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"anchor","target_skill":"transferred-intent scope","prompt":"A bystander sues an attacker for assault over harm the attacker directed at a third party. The attacker never threatened the bystander. Does transferred intent make the bystander an assault plaintiff?","answer":"No. Transferred intent applies to battery and certain other intentional torts against the person, not to assault. The assault plaintiff must be the person put in apprehension of contact with herself."},{"drill_type":"call_focus","target_skill":"apprehension vs. perception","prompt":"The call asks ''the court should find for'' in a third-party-violence assault pattern. Is the deciding axis whether the bystander perceived injury to a third party, or whether the bystander apprehended contact with herself?","answer":"Whether the bystander apprehended contact with HERSELF. Perception of injury to a third party is not the test."},{"drill_type":"trap_spotting","target_skill":"awareness of fright","prompt":"A choice says the defendant wins because the plaintiff did not perceive an injury. Another says the defendant wins because the defendant was aware his conduct would frighten the plaintiff. Which is the right reason?","answer":"Neither is the right reason on these facts. The right reason is that the plaintiff did not apprehend contact with HERSELF. The ''perception of injury'' and ''awareness of fright'' framings are both wrong reasons, even if they pick the right party."}]}'),
  ('a69d5d50-141d-5273-9ed7-d5b28b1e8d30', '18438_hannah_david_medicaid', 'CONSTITUTIONAL_LAW', 'Equal Protection', 'Fundamental rights — right to interstate travel', 'rational basis applies (public benefits are always discretionary) versus strict scrutiny applies (right to interstate travel is burdened)', 'State A''s Medicaid program provides full Medicaid coverage to long-term residents. For new residents, State A limits the first-year Medicaid coverage to the lower of (a) the amount the new resident would have received in her prior state of residence or (b) State A''s full coverage. Hannah and David, a Christian family who recently moved from State B to State A with their young son who has a chronic medical condition requiring ongoing care, are denied full State A Medicaid coverage for their son''s care during their first year in State A.', 'State A''s Medicaid program provides full Medicaid coverage to long-term residents. For new residents, State A limits the first-year Medicaid coverage to the lower of (a) the amount the new resident would have received in her prior state of residence or (b) State A''s full coverage. Hannah and David, a Christian family who recently moved from State B to State A with their young son who has a chronic medical condition requiring ongoing care, are denied full State A Medicaid coverage for their son''s care during their first year in State A.

Hannah and David sue State A under the Equal Protection Clause. What is the strongest Equal Protection argument?', 'Hannah and David sue State A under the Equal Protection Clause. What is the strongest Equal Protection argument?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ18438.md","source_question_id":"18438","variant_slug":"hannah_david_medicaid","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"44040301","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium","deciding_phase":"CALL","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A state provides full Medicaid coverage to long-term residents but caps new residents'' first-year coverage at the lower of their prior state''s level or the current state''s level. Does this rule violate Equal Protection?","review_truth":"A state rule that disadvantages new residents because they recently moved burdens the fundamental right to interstate travel and triggers strict scrutiny under Equal Protection.","dominant_trap":"A","anchor_card":{"card_id":"RC-CONSTITUTIONAL-LAW-RIGHT-TO-TRAVEL-01","title":"Right to interstate travel and durational-residency rules","signal":"A state durational-residency classification disadvantages new residents in a public benefit (welfare, Medicaid, food assistance, in-state tuition, voting, bar admission).","student_move":"Apply the right-to-travel doctrine: the right to be treated equally to other residents once the traveler has established residence is a fundamental right. Durational-residency rules that burden that right trigger strict scrutiny.","tiny_rule":"Public-benefit rules can still burden fundamental rights; durational-residency rules that disadvantage new residents trigger strict scrutiny.","trap":"The ''public benefits are always discretionary'' framing is the dominant trap; the right to travel is a fundamental right, and the discretionary-benefits reasoning collapses when the eligibility rule operates as a penalty on the exercise of a constitutional right.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONSTITUTIONAL-LAW-HANNAH-DAVID-MEDICAID-01","statement":"Durational-residency rules that disadvantage new residents because they recently moved trigger strict scrutiny under Equal Protection because they burden the fundamental right to interstate travel.","type":"rule","trigger":"state durational-residency classification disadvantaging new residents in a public benefit","authority":"Shapiro v. Thompson, 394 U.S. 618 (1969); Memorial Hospital v. Maricopa County, 415 U.S. 250 (1974); Saenz v. Roe, 526 U.S. 489 (1999)","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONSTITUTIONAL-LAW-HANNAH-DAVID-MEDICAID-01","statement":"The constitutional right at issue is INTERSTATE travel, not international travel; public-benefit rules can still burden fundamental rights.","type":"distinction","trigger":"state durational-residency classification disadvantaging new residents in a public benefit","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"level_of_scrutiny","target_skill":"right-to-travel strict-scrutiny selection","prompt":"A state limits new residents'' first-year welfare benefits to the lower of their prior state''s level or the current state''s level. What level of scrutiny applies under Equal Protection?","answer":"Strict scrutiny. The right to interstate travel is a fundamental right, and durational-residency rules that disadvantage new residents because they recently moved trigger strict scrutiny."},{"drill_type":"distinction","target_skill":"interstate vs. international travel","prompt":"A new resident challenges a durational-residency rule after moving from one U.S. state to another. Is the constitutional right to travel an international-travel right?","answer":"No. The constitutional right to travel includes the right to move from one state to another (interstate travel). The ''international border'' framing is wrong; the right is INTERSTATE travel."},{"drill_type":"trap_spotting","target_skill":"public-benefits-discretion trap","prompt":"A choice says ''rational basis applies because public benefits are always discretionary.'' Is this the right level-of-scrutiny framing for a durational-residency classification that disadvantages new residents in a public benefit?","answer":"No. Public-benefit rules can still burden fundamental rights; the right to interstate travel is a fundamental right, and the discretionary-benefits reasoning collapses when the eligibility rule operates as a penalty on the exercise of a constitutional right."}]}'),
  ('b987af7a-0ffc-50f5-9ba5-16d26951cb48', '18457_lydia_book_club', 'CONSTITUTIONAL_LAW', 'Equal Protection', 'State action requirement', 'strict scrutiny applies because the club uses race or ethnicity versus the constitutional claim fails at the state-action threshold before any scrutiny tier is reached', 'A private Christian book club — a monthly reading-and-discussion group that meets in members'' homes, is not a church or religious institution, and receives no government funding and performs no public function — admits only members of one racial or ethnic group.', 'A private Christian book club — a monthly reading-and-discussion group that meets in members'' homes, is not a church or religious institution, and receives no government funding and performs no public function — admits only members of one racial or ethnic group.

A rejected applicant, who is of a different racial or ethnic background, sues the book club directly under the Equal Protection Clause of the Fourteenth Amendment. What is the book club''s strongest constitutional defense?', 'A rejected applicant, who is of a different racial or ethnic background, sues the book club directly under the Equal Protection Clause of the Fourteenth Amendment. What is the book club''s strongest constitutional defense?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ18457.md","source_question_id":"18457","variant_slug":"lydia_book_club","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"44040100","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium","deciding_phase":"CALL","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A private social group (a Christian book club) with no government funding and no public-function role limits its membership to one racial or ethnic group. A rejected applicant sues the club under the Equal Protection Clause. What is the club''s strongest defense?","review_truth":"The Equal Protection Clause requires government action or a recognized state-action theory; purely private discrimination is not reachable under Equal Protection regardless of the basis.","dominant_trap":"A","anchor_card":{"card_id":"RC-CONSTITUTIONAL-LAW-STATE-ACTION-01","title":"State-action threshold in Equal Protection challenges to private actors","signal":"A constitutional claim (Equal Protection or Due Process) is filed against a private actor with no government funding or public-function role.","student_move":"Run the state-action threshold first. The Equal Protection Clause is, by its terms, a constraint on government action. The scrutiny tier is reached only after state action is established.","tiny_rule":"The threshold problem is state action; the scrutiny tier is reached only after state action is established.","trap":"The strict-scrutiny framing is the dominant trap; the student skips the threshold problem and goes straight to the scrutiny tier. The ''Constitution bans all private discrimination'' overclaim and the ''rational-basis-deference'' framing are also wrong levels of analysis.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONSTITUTIONAL-LAW-LYDIA-BOOK-CLUB-01","statement":"The Equal Protection Clause is, by its terms, a constraint on government action; the threshold problem is state action, and the scrutiny tier is reached only after state action is established.","type":"rule","trigger":"constitutional claim (Equal Protection or Due Process) filed against a private actor with no government funding or public-function role","authority":"The Civil Rights Cases, 109 U.S. 3 (1883); Burton v. Wilmington Parking Authority, 365 U.S. 715 (1961); Jackson v. Metropolitan Edison Co., 419 U.S. 345 (1974); Brentwood Academy v. Tennessee Secondary School Athletic Association, 531 U.S. 288 (2001)","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONSTITUTIONAL-LAW-LYDIA-BOOK-CLUB-01","statement":"When a constitutional claim against a private actor is filed, run the state-action threshold first; do not skip to the scrutiny tier.","type":"threshold_check","trigger":"constitutional claim (Equal Protection or Due Process) filed against a private actor","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"threshold_check","target_skill":"state-action threshold in EP challenges","prompt":"A private social group with no government funding and no public-function role limits its membership to one racial group. A rejected applicant sues the group under the Equal Protection Clause. Does the constitutional claim reach the scrutiny tier?","answer":"No. The Equal Protection Clause requires state action. The scrutiny tier is reached only after state action is established; the constitutional claim fails at the state-action threshold."},{"drill_type":"trap_spotting","target_skill":"strict-scrutiny skipped-threshold trap","prompt":"A choice says ''strict scrutiny applies because the private club uses race.'' Is this the right level of analysis?","answer":"No. Strict scrutiny would apply if state action were present. The scrutiny tier is reached only after state action is established; the constitutional claim fails at the state-action threshold."},{"drill_type":"overclaim","target_skill":"Constitution-bans-all-private-discrimination overclaim","prompt":"A choice says ''the Constitution bans all private discrimination.'' Is this overclaim correct?","answer":"No. The Equal Protection Clause requires state action. Other constitutional provisions (e.g., Thirteenth Amendment) and federal civil-rights statutes (e.g., 42 U.S.C. § 1981) may reach some private discrimination, but the EP claim itself requires state action."}]}'),
  ('65cce141-ed12-5a76-ae85-935cd445547e', '18477_church_craft_fair_quilt', 'CRIMINAL_LAW', 'Property Crimes', 'Receipt of stolen property', 'fact-of-receipt makes the buyer guilty (stolen-before-purchase rule) versus receipt-time knowledge is the dispositive element (no receipt-time knowledge, no offense)', 'Naomi, a member of a local church, attended the church''s annual Christmas craft fair, a benefit event open to the public. At the fair, she bought a hand-stitched quilt that had been entered in the fair''s juried show by Ruth, a fellow church member Naomi has known and trusted for years. Naomi paid the fair''s listed price for the quilt; nothing about Ruth, the quilt, or the transaction suggested the quilt was stolen. The next day, Ruth tearfully confessed to Naomi that she had stolen the fabric she used to make the quilt. Naomi immediately took the quilt to the local police.', 'Naomi, a member of a local church, attended the church''s annual Christmas craft fair, a benefit event open to the public. At the fair, she bought a hand-stitched quilt that had been entered in the fair''s juried show by Ruth, a fellow church member Naomi has known and trusted for years. Naomi paid the fair''s listed price for the quilt; nothing about Ruth, the quilt, or the transaction suggested the quilt was stolen. The next day, Ruth tearfully confessed to Naomi that she had stolen the fabric she used to make the quilt. Naomi immediately took the quilt to the local police.

Is Naomi guilty of receiving stolen property?', 'Is Naomi guilty of receiving stolen property?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ18477.md","source_question_id":"18477","variant_slug":"church_craft_fair_quilt","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"73030801","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium","deciding_phase":"CALL","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A buyer purchases an item at a normal price from a person she has known and trusted for years; nothing suggests the item is stolen. The next day, the seller confesses that she stole the item. The buyer immediately takes the item to the police. Is the buyer guilty of receiving stolen property?","review_truth":"Receiving stolen property requires that the defendant know or believe the property was stolen at the time she received control of it; later knowledge does not retroactively supply the receipt-time mens rea.","dominant_trap":"A","anchor_card":{"card_id":"RC-CRIMINAL-RECEIPT-TIME-KNOWLEDGE-01","title":"Receiving stolen property — receipt-time knowledge is the dispositive element","signal":"Buyer purchases property at a normal price from a trusted seller; nothing suggests the property is stolen; the buyer learns the truth later; the buyer promptly returns the property. The call is whether the buyer is guilty of receiving stolen property.","student_move":"Test whether the defendant had knowledge or belief that the property was stolen at the moment of receipt. If not, the offense fails on the mens rea element and the rest of the analysis is unnecessary. Later knowledge does not retroactively supply the missing mens rea. The fact that the property was stolen before the defendant received it is necessary but not sufficient.","tiny_rule":"Receiving stolen property requires that the defendant know or believe the property was stolen at the time she received control of it; later knowledge does not retroactively supply the receipt-time mens rea.","trap":"The stolen-before-purchase trap is the dominant misconception. Many students reason that because the property was stolen, anyone who received it must be guilty. The error is that the offense also requires knowledge or belief at the time of receipt — a good-faith purchaser is not guilty.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CRIMINAL-CHURCH-CRAFT-FAIR-QUILT-01","statement":"Receiving stolen property requires that the defendant know or believe the property was stolen at the time she received control of it. The fact that the property was stolen before the defendant received it is necessary but not sufficient; the common-law and modern-statutory offense also requires knowledge or belief at the time of receipt. Later knowledge does not retroactively supply the missing mens rea.","type":"rule","trigger":"buyer purchases property at a normal price from a person she has known and trusted for years; nothing suggests the property is stolen; the seller confesses or the buyer learns the truth later; the buyer promptly returns the property","authority":"Model Penal Code § 223.6; Wayne R. LaFave, Substantive Criminal Law § 19.6 (3d ed.)","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CRIMINAL-CHURCH-CRAFT-FAIR-QUILT-01","statement":"When the call is ''guilty of receiving stolen property?'' with a prompt-return fact pattern, the first move is to test whether the defendant had knowledge or belief at the moment of receipt. If not, the offense fails on the mens rea element and the rest of the analysis is unnecessary.","type":"call_focus","trigger":"stem describes a buyer who paid a normal price, saw no red flags, learned the truth later, and promptly returned the property","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"misfit test (issue-senses)","prompt":"A buyer purchases a hand-stitched quilt at a normal price from a trusted seller. Nothing suggests the quilt is stolen. The next day, the seller confesses she stole the fabric. The buyer is charged with receiving stolen property. The defense argues the offense applies only to cash. Is the defense correct?","answer":"No. Receiving stolen property covers all stolen personal property, including quilts, cameras, livestock, vehicles, and electronics. The offense is not limited to cash; the dispositive question is the defendant''s knowledge or belief at receipt, not the type of property."},{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"temporal-mens-rea check","prompt":"A buyer purchases a hand-stitched quilt at a normal price from a trusted seller. Nothing suggests the quilt is stolen. The next day, the seller confesses she stole the fabric. The buyer is charged with receiving stolen property. The prosecution argues the buyer''s later knowledge relates back to the purchase. Is the prosecution correct?","answer":"No. The mens rea must exist at the time the defendant takes possession or control of the property. Later knowledge does not retroactively supply the missing mens rea. Model Penal Code § 223.6."},{"drill_type":"anchor","target_skill":"receipt-time-knowledge requirement (Model Penal Code § 223.6)","prompt":"A buyer purchases a hand-stitched quilt at a normal price from a trusted seller. Nothing suggests the quilt is stolen. The next day, the seller confesses she stole the fabric. The buyer promptly returns the quilt to the police. Is the buyer guilty of receiving stolen property?","answer":"No. The common-law and modern-statutory offense requires that the defendant know or believe the property was stolen at the time she received control of it. The buyer had no such knowledge at the moment of receipt; the next-day confession came too late. The prompt return to the police reinforces the absence of any continuing mens rea or depravity-of-conduct argument."}]}'),
  ('1d89daf9-75ae-5b4e-91aa-9daabb44cbd4', '18488_youth_retreat_trailer', 'CIVIL_PROCEDURE', 'Pleadings, Rule 12 Motions, and Amendments', 'Rule 15(c) Party Change', 'John Doe pleading plus post-limitations identification versus Rule 15(c) notice and identity-mistake requirements', 'Daniel''s pickup truck was rear-ended at a stop sign by an unmarked horse trailer that was pulling away from a church youth-group retreat. The driver of the trailer fled the scene on foot before Daniel could get a name. Daniel later obtained the trailer''s plate number from a witness, but he could not identify the driver before the state limitations period expired. Daniel timely filed suit in federal court (diversity) against "John Doe Driver." Daniel never served the real driver within the 90-day Rule 4(m) period, and the real driver had no actual notice of the action during that period. After limitations had run, Daniel discovered that the real driver was Stephen, who lived in a neighboring county. Daniel moved under Rule 15 to amend the complaint to substitute Stephen for the John Doe defendant. Stephen was served after limitations had expired and now contests the relation-back amendment.', 'Daniel''s pickup truck was rear-ended at a stop sign by an unmarked horse trailer that was pulling away from a church youth-group retreat. The driver of the trailer fled the scene on foot before Daniel could get a name. Daniel later obtained the trailer''s plate number from a witness, but he could not identify the driver before the state limitations period expired. Daniel timely filed suit in federal court (diversity) against "John Doe Driver." Daniel never served the real driver within the 90-day Rule 4(m) period, and the real driver had no actual notice of the action during that period. After limitations had run, Daniel discovered that the real driver was Stephen, who lived in a neighboring county. Daniel moved under Rule 15 to amend the complaint to substitute Stephen for the John Doe defendant. Stephen was served after limitations had expired and now contests the relation-back amendment.

Is relation back likely?', 'Is relation back likely?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ18488.md","source_question_id":"18488","variant_slug":"youth_retreat_trailer","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"95070200","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"HARD_STRUCTURAL","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A plaintiff sues a John Doe driver before limitations, never serves the real driver within Rule 4(m), and the real driver has no actual notice within that period. After limitations expires, the plaintiff learns the real driver''s identity and seeks to amend and relate back under Rule 15(c). Is relation back likely?","review_truth":"Rule 15(c) relation back for a party change requires both that the new party received timely notice of the action and that the new party knew or should have known it would have been named but for a mistake concerning identity; a John Doe pleading plus a lack of identified defendant is not, by itself, a Rule 15(c) mistake.","dominant_trap":"D","anchor_card":{"card_id":"RC-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-RULE15C-NOTICE-01","title":"Rule 15(c) party-change relation back","signal":"John Doe pleading + no service within Rule 4(m) + post-limitations identification + Rule 15(c) motion to substitute","student_move":"Cut the two ''yes'' overclaims (D, B) and the immutability overclaim (C); the residual is the answer that names both required failures.","tiny_rule":"Rule 15(c) needs both timely notice and a shown identity mistake.","trap":"John Doe pleadings exist to bridge the unknown-defendant gap, but they do not by themselves satisfy the identity-mistake element.","confidence":"HARD_STRUCTURAL"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-YOUTH-RETREAT-TRAILER-01","statement":"Rule 15(c) party-change relation back requires both timely notice within the Rule 4(m) period and a shown Rule 15(c) identity mistake; a John Doe pleading plus a lack of identified defendant is not, by itself, the required mistake.","type":"rule","trigger":"John Doe pleading + no service within Rule 4(m) + post-limitations identification + Rule 15(c) motion","authority":"Fed. R. Civ. P. 15(c)(1)(C); 1991 amendment Advisory Committee Notes","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-YOUTH-RETREAT-TRAILER-01","statement":"Read the call to the named party (here, the real driver), then cut the ''yes'' overclaims and the immutability overclaim before reaching for a doctrine label.","type":"call_focus","trigger":"John Doe pleading + post-limitations identification + Rule 15(c) motion","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"overclaim_kill","target_skill":"John Doe placeholder theory","prompt":"A John Doe pleading is filed before limitations; the real defendant is not served within Rule 4(m); the plaintiff moves to substitute after limitations. Is a John Doe pleading, by itself, a Rule 15(c) identity mistake?","answer":"No. A John Doe pleading is not, by itself, a Rule 15(c) identity mistake; the plaintiff must show the new party knew or should have known it would have been named but for a mistake."},{"drill_type":"timing_kill","target_skill":"Rule 4(m) service window","prompt":"The real defendant received notice of the action after limitations. Does that satisfy Rule 15(c)(1)(C)(ii)?","answer":"No. Rule 15(c)(1)(C)(ii) requires notice within the Rule 4(m) period; post-limitations notice is the failure, not the cure."},{"drill_type":"call_focus","target_skill":"immutability overclaim","prompt":"A student says claims against drivers can never be amended. What is the breaker?","answer":"Rule 15(a) freely grants leave to amend, and a proper party-change amendment does relate back; the question is whether this specific amendment satisfies Rule 15(c)."}]}'),
  ('f4a21d38-19bb-5945-8b55-a60ae05eedf1', '18586_hymnsong_records', 'CONSTITUTIONAL_LAW', 'Privileges And Immunities', 'Article IV plaintiff threshold', 'interstate burden triggers Article IV P&I versus Article IV P&I requires a natural-person citizen plaintiff', 'Hymnsong Records Inc. is a Tennessee corporation that produces Christian-music albums. It wants to distribute its catalog in California. California requires out-of-state recording companies to pay an annual distribution license fee of $3,200, while California-incorporated recording companies pay $1,400. Hymnsong sues California in federal court, asserting only an Article IV Privileges and Immunities claim against the higher fee.', 'Hymnsong Records Inc. is a Tennessee corporation that produces Christian-music albums. It wants to distribute its catalog in California. California requires out-of-state recording companies to pay an annual distribution license fee of $3,200, while California-incorporated recording companies pay $1,400. Hymnsong sues California in federal court, asserting only an Article IV Privileges and Immunities claim against the higher fee.

What is California''s strongest response to that claim?', 'What is California''s strongest response to that claim?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ18586.md","source_question_id":"18586","variant_slug":"hymnsong_records","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"44040402","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A corporation challenges a state regulatory fee that burdens out-of-state companies more than in-state ones, suing only under Article IV Privileges and Immunities. Can the corporation invoke the clause at all?","review_truth":"Article IV Privileges and Immunities protects only the citizens of other states; a corporation is not a citizen and cannot use the clause to challenge a state''s regulatory fee.","dominant_trap":"B","anchor_card":{"card_id":"RC-CONSTITUTIONAL_LAW-A4PI-PERSON-CLASS-01","title":"Article IV P&I person-class threshold","signal":"A non-natural-person entity (corporation, LLC, partnership) sues under only Article IV Privileges and Immunities.","student_move":"Cut the merits. The clause''s text and case law require a natural-person citizen of another state; a corporation is not a citizen. The case is resolved at the threshold.","tiny_rule":"Article IV P&I protects only natural-person citizens of other states.","trap":"The student bridges from ''interstate burden'' to ''Article IV applies'' or jumps to a ''fundamental burden'' merits theory. Both miss the person-class threshold.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONSTITUTIONAL_LAW-HYMNSONG-RECORDS-01","statement":"Article IV Privileges and Immunities protects only the natural-person citizens of other states. A corporation is not a citizen for that clause, so a corporate plaintiff cannot use Article IV P&I to challenge a state''s higher regulatory fee.","type":"rule","trigger":"out-of-state non-natural-person entity challenges a higher state fee under Article IV P&I","authority":"Article IV, Section 2, U.S. Constitution; Paul v. Virginia, 75 U.S. (8 Wall.) 168 (1869); United Building & Construction Trades Council v. Mayor of Camden, 465 U.S. 208 (1984)","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONSTITUTIONAL_LAW-HYMNSONG-RECORDS-01","statement":"When a constitutional claim names a ''person-class'' threshold, check the person-class first — the merits never get reached if the plaintiff is outside the protected class.","type":"call_focus","trigger":"a non-natural-person entity sues under a clause that protects only natural-person citizens","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"person-class threshold","prompt":"An out-of-state LLC sues a state under only Article IV Privileges and Immunities, challenging a higher licensing fee. Does the clause reach the LLC?","answer":"No. Article IV P&I protects only natural-person citizens of other states; a corporation or LLC is not a citizen. The case is dismissed at the threshold."},{"drill_type":"call_focus","target_skill":"bridge-from-burden fallacy","prompt":"A non-natural-person entity sues a state under only Article IV P&I, citing a higher fee burden. What is the strongest response?","answer":"The plaintiff is not in the protected class. The merits are never reached."},{"drill_type":"anchor","target_skill":"Article IV P&I scope","prompt":"Article IV P&I protects only natural-person citizens of other states. What kinds of rights does it cover?","answer":"Civil economic and fundamental rights of natural citizen-plaintiffs (e.g., right to practice a livelihood, right to own property). Not criminal-procedure rights; that is the Sixth Amendment."}]}'),
  ('b1fd9ed2-4934-5792-803b-c027099ddbd0', '18592_shiloh_campground', 'TORTS', 'Nuisance', 'Private nuisance defenses', 'complete defense because there first versus factor only, especially after a substantial expansion', 'The Timothy family bought a home next to Shiloh Family Bible Camp, a long-running Christian retreat center that has hosted summer youth retreats for more than twenty years. After the family''s purchase, the camp doubled its summer retreat schedule, adding evening outdoor worship services, amplified gospel-music performances, and heavy bus traffic, producing noise and vibrations that cracked the home''s plaster and made it difficult to occupy the home on summer evenings. The camp argues that the Timothy family "came to the nuisance."', 'The Timothy family bought a home next to Shiloh Family Bible Camp, a long-running Christian retreat center that has hosted summer youth retreats for more than twenty years. After the family''s purchase, the camp doubled its summer retreat schedule, adding evening outdoor worship services, amplified gospel-music performances, and heavy bus traffic, producing noise and vibrations that cracked the home''s plaster and made it difficult to occupy the home on summer evenings. The camp argues that the Timothy family "came to the nuisance."

How should the court treat that argument?', 'How should the court treat that argument?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ18592.md","source_question_id":"18592","variant_slug":"shiloh_campground","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"62050102","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A buyer moves in next to a long-standing activity that subsequently expands its operations, and the buyer sues for private nuisance. The defendant argues the buyer ''came to the nuisance.'' Is that a complete defense, irrelevant, or just a factor?","review_truth":"Coming to the nuisance is a factor in the reasonableness and remedy analysis, but it is not a complete defense to a private nuisance claim, especially when the defendant''s activity has substantially expanded.","dominant_trap":"B","anchor_card":{"card_id":"RC-TORTS-COMING-TO-NUISANCE-01","title":"Coming to the nuisance is a factor, not a defense","signal":"Defendant argues ''coming to the nuisance'' after a substantial expansion of the activity following the plaintiff''s move-in.","student_move":"Cut the complete-defense overclaim. The doctrine is a reasonableness factor, not a complete defense. An *expansion* after the plaintiff''s arrival cuts strongly against the ''was there first'' intuition.","tiny_rule":"Coming to the nuisance is a factor, not a complete defense; an expansion of the activity after the plaintiff moved in makes the doctrine weakest.","trap":"The ''was there first'' intuition pulls toward the complete-defense overclaim.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-TORTS-SHILOH-CAMPGROUND-01","statement":"Coming to the nuisance is a reasonableness factor, not a complete defense. An *expansion* of the activity after the plaintiff moved in cuts strongly against the ''was there first'' intuition.","type":"rule","trigger":"defendant argues ''coming to the nuisance'' after a substantial expansion of the activity following the plaintiff''s move-in","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Torts §§ 826, 833(1); Spano v. Perini & Sons, 250 N.E.2d 31 (N.Y. 1969)","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-TORTS-SHILOH-CAMPGROUND-01","statement":"When the defendant''s activity has *expanded* after the plaintiff moved in, the ''coming to the nuisance'' argument is at its weakest as a complete defense.","type":"call_focus","trigger":"defendant argues ''coming to the nuisance'' after a substantial expansion of the activity following the plaintiff''s move-in","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"tiered-absolute overclaim","prompt":"A family moves in next to a long-operating quarry. The quarry doubles its blasting after the family''s move-in. The quarry argues ''coming to the nuisance.'' Is that a complete defense?","answer":"No. Coming to the nuisance is a reasonableness factor, not a complete defense. An *expansion* of the activity after the plaintiff''s arrival cuts strongly against the ''was there first'' intuition."},{"drill_type":"call_focus","target_skill":"splitting fact for coming-to-the-nuisance","prompt":"When is the ''coming to the nuisance'' doctrine at its weakest as a complete defense?","answer":"When the defendant''s activity has substantially *expanded* after the plaintiff''s arrival. The expansion is treated as a fresh nuisance-causing activity for analytical purposes."},{"drill_type":"anchor","target_skill":"Restatement § 826 balancing","prompt":"Under Restatement § 826, what factors go into the private-nuisance balancing?","answer":"The gravity of the harm, the utility of the conduct, the suitability of the particular use, and the damage to the plaintiff. Coming to the nuisance can affect these factors but does not, by itself, defeat the claim."}]}'),
  ('1ec9548c-b487-503b-b666-0dc120fc7a5e', '18669_mary_negligence_survival', 'CIVIL_PROCEDURE', 'Erie Doctrine and Choice of Law', 'Survival of State Claims', 'merits-preference intuition versus Erie command; substantive survival versus procedural diversity', 'Mary, a Christian school teacher, was severely injured in a collision with a delivery truck driven by Paul in County X. Mary filed a negligence suit in federal court (diversity) against Paul''s company. Mary died while the suit was pending. The forum state has a statute that says personal-injury negligence claims do not survive the plaintiff''s death. Mary''s estate asks the federal court to continue the claim under general federal procedure.', 'Mary, a Christian school teacher, was severely injured in a collision with a delivery truck driven by Paul in County X. Mary filed a negligence suit in federal court (diversity) against Paul''s company. Mary died while the suit was pending. The forum state has a statute that says personal-injury negligence claims do not survive the plaintiff''s death. Mary''s estate asks the federal court to continue the claim under general federal procedure.

What should the federal court do?', 'What should the federal court do?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ18669.md","source_question_id":"18669","variant_slug":"mary_negligence_survival","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"96060100","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A state-law personal-injury plaintiff dies while a federal diversity suit is pending, and the forum state''s survival statute says the claim does not survive. Should the federal court apply the state''s no-survival rule, or continue the claim under federal procedure?","review_truth":"Under Erie, a federal diversity court applies state law that defines the existence, scope, and survival of a state-created claim, including whether the claim survives the plaintiff''s death.","dominant_trap":"A","anchor_card":{"card_id":"RC-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-ERIE-SURVIVAL-01","title":"Erie and survival of state claims","signal":"A state-law plaintiff dies during a federal diversity suit; the forum state''s survival statute says the claim does not survive.","student_move":"Apply Erie. The survival of a state-created claim is a substantive question; the forum state''s no-survival rule governs. The merits-preference intuition does not override the Erie command.","tiny_rule":"Under Erie, the survival of a state-created claim is substantive; the forum state''s survival statute governs.","trap":"The merits-preference intuition pulls toward ''always continue.'' The citizenship-mismatch intuition pulls toward ''dismiss if diversity destroyed.'' Both miss the Erie analysis.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-MARY-NEGLIGENCE-SURVIVAL-01","statement":"Under Erie, a federal diversity court applies state substantive law. The survival of a state-created claim is a substantive question for Erie purposes, so the forum state''s survival statute — including a no-survival rule — governs.","type":"rule","trigger":"state-law plaintiff dies during federal diversity litigation; forum-state survival statute says the claim does not survive","authority":"Erie R.R. Co. v. Tompkins, 304 U.S. 64 (1938); Klaxon Co. v. Stentor Electric Manufacturing Co., 313 U.S. 487 (1941); Wheeling Steel Corp. v. Fox, 298 U.S. 193 (1936)","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-MARY-NEGLIGENCE-SURVIVAL-01","statement":"When the call asks ''what should the federal court do?'' in a diversity case involving a state-law claim, the answer is the Erie command, not a merits-preference or diversity-impact analysis.","type":"call_focus","trigger":"federal court, diversity jurisdiction, state-law claim, plaintiff dies during litigation","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"merits-preference overclaim","prompt":"A state-law plaintiff dies during a federal diversity suit. The forum state''s survival statute says the claim does not survive. The estate asks the federal court to continue the claim. Should the federal court continue?","answer":"No. Under Erie, the survival of a state-created claim is a substantive question; the forum state''s no-survival rule governs. The merits-preference intuition does not override the Erie command."},{"drill_type":"call_focus","target_skill":"Erie vs merits-preference","prompt":"A federal court, sitting in diversity, is asked to apply general federal procedure to continue a state-law claim whose survival is governed by a state no-survival statute. What should the court do?","answer":"Apply Erie. The survival of a state-created claim is substantive; the forum state''s no-survival rule governs."},{"drill_type":"anchor","target_skill":"Klaxon choice-of-law","prompt":"A federal court sitting in diversity must apply state law. Whose choice-of-law rules does it follow?","answer":"The forum state''s choice-of-law rules. Klaxon Co. v. Stentor Electric Manufacturing Co., 313 U.S. 487 (1941). Not the defendant''s home-state rule automatically."}]}'),
  ('1ad7129e-cb3e-5de9-ae09-afec91247e99', '18676_mary_wellness_appropriation', 'TORTS', 'Privacy', 'Appropriation and consent scope', 'permanent and unlimited consent versus limited-by-scope consent; appropriation versus false light or flattering-photo theories', 'Mary, a Christian fitness instructor, licensed her photo to Antioch Wellness Co. for one printed brochure distributed in January. In March, Antioch used the same photo in online ads for a new line of supplements. Mary did not consent to online ads or to the later use.', 'Mary, a Christian fitness instructor, licensed her photo to Antioch Wellness Co. for one printed brochure distributed in January. In March, Antioch used the same photo in online ads for a new line of supplements. Mary did not consent to online ads or to the later use.

Can Antioch rely on the January license?', 'Can Antioch rely on the January license?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ18676.md","source_question_id":"18676","variant_slug":"mary_wellness_appropriation","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"62050402","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A person licenses a photo for a limited use (one printed brochure in January), and the licensee later uses the photo for a different medium and a later time period (online ads in March for a new product line). Does the original license authorize the later use, or is the licensee liable for appropriation of likeness?","review_truth":"Consent or a license to use a person''s likeness is limited by its scope; use outside the agreed time, medium, or purpose can support a claim for appropriation of likeness.","dominant_trap":"A","anchor_card":{"card_id":"RC-TORTS-APPROPRIATION-SCOPE-01","title":"Appropriation and scope of consent","signal":"A person licenses a photo for a limited use, and the licensee later uses the photo for a different medium and a later time period.","student_move":"Compare the original license''s scope to the later use on each scope dimension (time, medium, purpose). If any dimension is exceeded, the use is unauthorized and the appropriation claim survives.","tiny_rule":"A license to use a likeness is limited by its scope; use outside the agreed scope can support an appropriation claim.","trap":"The ''once-consented-always-consented'' intuition pulls toward ''permanent and unlimited.'' The ''flattering photo'' intuition is irrelevant. The ''false fact'' theory belongs to a different privacy tort.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-TORTS-MARY-WELLNESS-APPROPRIATION-01","statement":"A license to use a likeness is limited by its scope — time, medium, and purpose — and use outside the agreed scope can support an appropriation claim.","type":"rule","trigger":"licensee uses a photo for a different medium or later time period than the original license authorized","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Torts §§ 652C, 652E, 652F","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-TORTS-MARY-WELLNESS-APPROPRIATION-01","statement":"When the call asks ''Can the licensee rely on the original license?'' compare the original license''s scope to the later use on each scope dimension (time, medium, purpose).","type":"call_focus","trigger":"licensee uses a photo for a different medium or later time period than the original license authorized","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"permanent-and-unlimited overclaim","prompt":"A person licenses a photo for one printed brochure in January. The licensee uses the same photo in online ads in March for a new product line. Is the original license sufficient?","answer":"No. A license to use a likeness is limited by its scope — time, medium, and purpose. The March use exceeded the January license on all three scope dimensions."},{"drill_type":"call_focus","target_skill":"scope-comparison analysis","prompt":"When the call asks ''Can the licensee rely on the original license?'' what is the analysis?","answer":"Compare the original license''s scope to the later use on each scope dimension (time, medium, purpose). If any dimension is exceeded, the use is unauthorized."},{"drill_type":"anchor","target_skill":"tort-category discipline","prompt":"The defendant used a photo without consent, but did not make a false statement. Which privacy tort is implicated?","answer":"Appropriation of likeness. The ''false fact'' theory belongs to defamation or false light, not to appropriation."}]}'),
  ('d1dfedb5-a515-5889-bf7d-fe8d740b8893', '18747_vintage_bible_embezzlement', 'CRIMINAL_LAW', 'Property Crimes', 'Other Crimes', 'colloquial ''every wrongful taking is larceny'' versus the lawful-possession re-routing to embezzlement', 'Timothy entrusted his late grandfather''s leather-bound 1898 family Bible — with a hand-tooled cover, raised hub, and gold-leaf edges — to a small leather bookbinding and restoration shop run by Stephen, a popular bookbinder in a farming community. The Bible had been in the family for four generations. Stephen accepted the Bible for a leather-cover restoration and edge repair, quoted $380, and told Timothy the work would be ready in six weeks. Before completing the work, Stephen sold the Bible to a private collector at an online auction for $4,200 and kept the proceeds. Timothy learned the Bible had been sold when he returned to the shop to pick it up. The state has not codified a separate embezzlement statute; the modern bar treats the conversion of lawfully possessed entrusted property as embezzlement.', 'Timothy entrusted his late grandfather''s leather-bound 1898 family Bible — with a hand-tooled cover, raised hub, and gold-leaf edges — to a small leather bookbinding and restoration shop run by Stephen, a popular bookbinder in a farming community. The Bible had been in the family for four generations. Stephen accepted the Bible for a leather-cover restoration and edge repair, quoted $380, and told Timothy the work would be ready in six weeks. Before completing the work, Stephen sold the Bible to a private collector at an online auction for $4,200 and kept the proceeds. Timothy learned the Bible had been sold when he returned to the shop to pick it up. The state has not codified a separate embezzlement statute; the modern bar treats the conversion of lawfully possessed entrusted property as embezzlement.

What is the best theft offense?', 'What is the best theft offense?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ18747.md","source_question_id":"18747","variant_slug":"vintage_bible_embezzlement","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"73030500","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"HARD_STRUCTURAL","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"An owner entrusts property to a repair or restoration shop, the shop lawfully accepts possession, and the shop then sells the property to a third party and keeps the proceeds. Which theft offense fits?","review_truth":"Conversion of lawfully possessed entrusted property is embezzlement, not larceny or false pretenses; the lawful possession is what makes the later conversion embezzlement.","dominant_trap":"A","anchor_card":{"card_id":"RC-CRIMINAL-EMBEZZLEMENT-LAWFUL-POSSESSION-01","title":"Embezzlement vs larceny vs false pretenses","signal":"owner entrusts property to a repair/restoration shop, shop lawfully accepts, shop then sells to a third party and keeps the proceeds","student_move":"Cut the colloquial-larceny overclaim (A) and the consent-coverage overclaim (B) and the false-pretenses misfit (D); the residual is embezzlement (C).","tiny_rule":"Lawful possession at the outset re-routes the offense from larceny to embezzlement.","trap":"Everyday English treats ''theft'' as a synonym for larceny, but the theft-offense distinction turns on the manner in which the defendant first came into possession.","confidence":"HARD_STRUCTURAL"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CRIMINAL-VINTAGE-BIBLE-EMBEZZLEMENT-01","statement":"The lawful possession at the outset re-routes the offense from larceny to embezzlement; the conversion of entrusted property is the embezzlement element, and the larceny trespassory-taking requirement is not met.","type":"distinction","trigger":"owner entrusts property to repair/restoration shop, shop lawfully accepts, shop then sells to a third party and keeps the proceeds","authority":"Common-law embezzlement doctrine; Restatement (Third) of Restitution and Unjust Enrichment; Model Penal Code § 223.4","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CRIMINAL-VINTAGE-BIBLE-EMBEZZLEMENT-01","statement":"When the call asks for the ''best'' theft offense, do not stop at the colloquial ''theft = larceny'' answer — check the manner in which the defendant first came into possession.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"entrustment plus later sale-to-third-party fact pattern","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"overclaim_kill","target_skill":"colloquial larceny theory","prompt":"An owner entrusts a camera to a repair shop, the shop lawfully accepts possession, then sells the camera to a third party and keeps the proceeds. Is this larceny because every wrongful taking is larceny?","answer":"No. Larceny requires a trespassory taking, and here the possession was lawful. The later conversion of lawfully possessed entrusted property is embezzlement."},{"drill_type":"consent_coverage_kill","target_skill":"consent-coverage scope","prompt":"An owner voluntarily hands over a family Bible to a bookbinder for restoration. The bookbinder sells the Bible and keeps the proceeds. Is consent a defense?","answer":"No. Consent to entrust for one purpose (restoration) does not authorize fraudulent conversion to a different purpose (sale for the bailee''s own benefit). The offense is embezzlement."},{"drill_type":"misfit_kill","target_skill":"false-pretenses element check","prompt":"A repair shop owner sells an entrusted camera to a third party and keeps the proceeds. Is this false pretenses?","answer":"No. False pretenses requires a false representation of a material fact that induces the victim to pass title. Here the shop owner never made a false representation to the owner, and the owner did not pass title. The offense is conversion of entrusted property, not obtaining title by fraud."}]}'),
  ('58b73d68-eb85-54be-9001-66bf8f0e104b', '18784_fellowship_lot_used_car', 'TORTS', 'Economic torts', 'Fraud', NULL, 'After a Sunday service, Daniel, a longtime member of the congregation, told Ruth, a fellow churchgoer, that the 2012 Civic he was selling from the church parking lot "has no mechanical problems and runs like new." The one-page bill of sale, in bold type directly above Ruth''s signature line, stated "SOLD AS-IS — NO WARRANTIES OF ANY KIND." Ruth signed the bill of sale without reading the printed terms and later sued Daniel for fraud, claiming she relied on his oral assurance.', 'After a Sunday service, Daniel, a longtime member of the congregation, told Ruth, a fellow churchgoer, that the 2012 Civic he was selling from the church parking lot "has no mechanical problems and runs like new." The one-page bill of sale, in bold type directly above Ruth''s signature line, stated "SOLD AS-IS — NO WARRANTIES OF ANY KIND." Ruth signed the bill of sale without reading the printed terms and later sued Daniel for fraud, claiming she relied on his oral assurance.

Which of the following is Daniel''s strongest defense?', 'Which of the following is Daniel''s strongest defense?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ18784.md","source_question_id":"18784","variant_slug":"fellowship_lot_used_car","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":null,"outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"core (carried from source row; not separately scored)","deciding_phase":"CALL","confidence":"HARD_STRUCTURAL","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A seller makes an oral assurance about the property, but the buyer signs a writing that plainly contradicts the assurance. The buyer sues in fraud based only on the oral assurance. Does the buyer win?","review_truth":"Fraud requires justifiable reliance, and reliance is usually not justified when an oral assurance is plainly contradicted by an obvious written term.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"RC-TORTS-FRAUD-AS-IS-01","title":"AS-IS / obvious writing kills justifiable reliance","signal":"Oral assurance + signed contradictory writing in obvious form (bold type, above signature)","student_move":"Spot the visible writing. Cut all ''fraud applies'' / ''reliance presumed'' overclaim distractors. Lock the ''justifiable reliance defeated by obvious writing'' answer.","tiny_rule":"Justifiable reliance requires the reliance to be reasonable in light of plainly visible contradictory information the plaintiff signed.","trap":"The contract-integration misframe (parol evidence lane) and the broad-absolute ''fraud never applies'' overclaim.","confidence":"HARD_STRUCTURAL"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-TORTS-FRAUD-RELIANCE-01","statement":"A plaintiff cannot justifiably rely on an oral assurance that is plainly contradicted by a written term the plaintiff signed, where the writing is in obvious form (here, bold type above the signature line).","type":"distinction","trigger":"When the stem has a signed writing that flatly contradicts an alleged oral representation, and the plaintiff sues in tort based on the oral representation, ask first whether the reliance element can survive the obviousness of the writing.","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Torts §§ 525, 537A, 541 (1977); Holcomb v. Hoffman, 695 N.W.2d 65 (Wis. 2005) (oral assurances contradicted by unambiguous integration clause are not justifiably relied upon).","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-TORTS-FRAUD-OVERCLAIM-01","statement":"When a wrong answer carries a universal-quantifier (''never,'' ''always,'' ''presumed''), it is a tiered_absolute overclaim — the kind of phrasing that loses to a single counterexample. Cut it on the word, then look for the element-based residual.","type":"trap_spotting","trigger":"Any fraud call with a ''never'' or ''presumed'' distractor.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"element drill","target_skill":"Identify which fraud element fails on the facts","prompt":"A buyer signs a one-page receipt in bold type that says ''AS-IS, NO WARRANTIES'' and then sues in fraud for an oral assurance made before signing. Which element of fraud is most likely to fail?","answer":"Justifiable reliance — the obvious writing defeats the reasonableness of reliance under Restatement (Second) of Torts § 541."},{"drill_type":"overclaim-spotting drill","target_skill":"Cut universal-quantifier distractors on the word","prompt":"A wrong answer choice says ''Fraud never applies to [X] contexts.'' What is the overclaim marker, and what is the correct doctrinal move?","answer":"The overclaim marker is ''never.'' Fraud applies in every commercial context where the elements are met; the [X] status is irrelevant. Cut on the word, then look for the element-based residual."}]}'),
  ('22806851-8d57-5693-a77d-bf6c0464f963', '18811_elevator_access_class', 'CIVIL_PROCEDURE', 'Joinder, Class Actions, and Intervention', 'Rule 23(b)(2) injunctive or declaratory classes', NULL, 'Hannah, Stephen, and Lydia are students at a public state university. Each of them uses a wheelchair and lives in an upper-floor dormitory room. For three months, the only elevator serving their dormitory building has been out of service, and the university has not repaired it. The students, on behalf of themselves and a class of more than forty similarly situated dormitory residents who use wheelchairs, sue the university. They seek a single injunction requiring the university to repair the elevator or to provide accessible alternative housing. They do not seek individualized damages in the class claim.', 'Hannah, Stephen, and Lydia are students at a public state university. Each of them uses a wheelchair and lives in an upper-floor dormitory room. For three months, the only elevator serving their dormitory building has been out of service, and the university has not repaired it. The students, on behalf of themselves and a class of more than forty similarly situated dormitory residents who use wheelchairs, sue the university. They seek a single injunction requiring the university to repair the elevator or to provide accessible alternative housing. They do not seek individualized damages in the class claim.

Which Rule 23(b) category is the most natural fit?', 'Which Rule 23(b) category is the most natural fit?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ18811.md","source_question_id":"18811","variant_slug":"elevator_access_class","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":null,"outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"core (carried from source row; not separately scored)","deciding_phase":"CALL","confidence":"HARD_STRUCTURAL","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A class of plaintiffs sues a single defendant, alleging a single uniform policy or course of conduct, and seeks a single injunction. They do not seek individualized damages. Which Rule 23(b) category fits?","review_truth":"Rule 23(b)(2) applies when the defendant has acted or refused to act on grounds generally applicable to the class, making final injunctive or declaratory relief appropriate for the class as a whole.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"RC-CIVPRO-RULE23-23B2-01","title":"Rule 23(b)(2) — single defendant, single conduct, single injunction","signal":"One defendant + one uniform conduct or policy + one injunctive remedy + no individualized damages","student_move":"Spot the 1-1-1-0 pattern. Default to Rule 23(b)(2). Cut 23(b)(3) overclaim (''every class action'') on the word. Cut 23(b)(1) on the mandatory-class fit. Cut ''no class can be certified'' on the True & Responsive axiom.","tiny_rule":"Rule 23(b)(2) applies when the defendant has acted or refused to act on grounds generally applicable to the class, making final injunctive or declaratory relief appropriate for the class as a whole.","trap":"The 23(b)(3) default (''every class action needs predominance'') and the 23(b)(1) misframe.","confidence":"HARD_STRUCTURAL"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CIVPRO-RULE23-23B2-01","statement":"Rule 23(b)(2) is the natural fit when a class claim targets a single defendant for a single uniform policy or course of conduct and seeks a single injunctive or declaratory remedy that would provide classwide relief — no predominance, no superiority, no opt-out.","type":"rule","trigger":"When the stem has one defendant, one uniform conduct, one injunctive remedy, and no individualized damages, ask ''is this a Rule 23(b)(2) case?'' first, before defaulting to Rule 23(b)(3).","authority":"Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 23(b)(2); Wal-Mart Stores, Inc. v. Dukes, 564 U.S. 338 (2011) (cohesion test for Rule 23(b)(2) classes).","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CIVPRO-RULE23-1-1-1-0-01","statement":"When a Rule 23 call has a 1-1-1-0 pattern (one defendant + one uniform conduct + one injunctive remedy + no individualized damages), default to Rule 23(b)(2) before reaching for Rule 23(b)(3). The 1-1-1-0 pattern is the textbook Rule 23(b)(2) shape.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"A class claim with one defendant, one conduct, one injunction, no damages.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"pattern drill","target_skill":"Identify the Rule 23(b) subsection that fits a class claim","prompt":"A class of tenants sues a single landlord for a single uniform policy of refusing to repair broken heating in any of the class members'' apartments, and seeks a single injunction requiring repairs. They do not seek damages. Which Rule 23(b) subsection fits?","answer":"Rule 23(b)(2) — single defendant, single uniform conduct, single injunctive remedy, no individualized damages."},{"drill_type":"overclaim-spotting drill","target_skill":"Cut universal-quantifier distractors on the word","prompt":"A wrong answer choice says ''Rule 23(b)(3) is the only class category that works for any case involving many plaintiffs.'' What is the overclaim marker, and what is the correct doctrinal move?","answer":"The overclaim marker is ''only.'' Rule 23(b)(2) is a separate category that does not require predominance or superiority; it requires only that the defendant has acted on grounds generally applicable to the class and that final injunctive or declaratory relief is appropriate for the class as a whole. Cut on the word, then look for the rule-fit residual."}]}'),
  ('9e8f10c3-1f33-5603-b36c-6aa705e2345a', '18843_patio_pressure_washer', 'CRIMINAL_LAW', 'Property Crimes', 'Larceny — temporary use (no intent to permanently deprive)', 'Intent-to-permanently-deprive element vs. the temporary-use-and-return fact pattern.', 'Naomi owns a heavy-duty electric pressure washer. Without asking first, Lydia takes the pressure washer from Naomi''s unlocked garage, intending to use it to clean the side patio of her home in preparation for a Saturday family gathering. She plans to return it the next day. Police stop her while she is loading the pressure washer into her car to take it home.', 'Naomi owns a heavy-duty electric pressure washer. Without asking first, Lydia takes the pressure washer from Naomi''s unlocked garage, intending to use it to clean the side patio of her home in preparation for a Saturday family gathering. She plans to return it the next day. Police stop her while she is loading the pressure washer into her car to take it home.

Is Lydia guilty of common-law larceny?', 'Is Lydia guilty of common-law larceny?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ18843.md","source_question_id":"18843","variant_slug":"patio_pressure_washer","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"73030100","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"core (carried from source row; not separately scored)","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A person takes another person''s personal property without permission, uses it briefly for a stated purpose, and plans to return it the next day. Is that common-law larceny?","review_truth":"Common-law larceny requires the intent to permanently deprive; a taking for short, safe, temporary use and return is not larceny.","dominant_trap":"C","anchor_card":{"card_id":"RC-CRIMINAL-LARCENY-TEMPORARY-01","title":"Larceny — temporary-use and return is not larceny","signal":"Taking of another''s personal property without permission, defined short-term use, actual return plan, police stop before return","student_move":"Lock the intent-to-permanently-deprive element on the temporary-use-and-return fact pattern. Cut C (any unauthorized use) on the universal-quantifier marker. Cut D (asportation) on the missing-intent element. Cut B (power tools not personal property) on the obvious-chattel counterexample.","tiny_rule":"Larceny requires the intent to permanently deprive; short, safe, return-planned borrowing is not larceny.","trap":"The ''any unauthorized use'' shortcut that fuses trespass with the common-law definition.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CRIMINAL-LARCENY-INTENT-01","statement":"Unauthorized borrowing is not common-law larceny unless the borrower''s intended use creates a substantial risk of permanent loss or otherwise shows intent to permanently deprive.","type":"rule","trigger":"When the stem shows a defined short-term use and an actual return plan (no permanent-loss risk), the intent-to-permanently-deprive element fails, and the larceny claim fails with it.","authority":"Perkins & Boyce, Criminal Law 305-06 (3d ed. 1982); cf. Model Penal Code § 223.0(1), § 223.1.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CRIMINAL-LARCENY-INTENT-01","statement":"On a larceny question, look for the intent-to-permanently-deprive anchor before locking any ''Yes'' answer. The ''Yes'' answer that omits the intent element is the one to suspect.","type":"call_focus","trigger":"A larceny call with a defined short-term use and an actual return plan.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"anchor drill","target_skill":"Identify the intent-to-permanently-deprive element of common-law larceny","prompt":"A student borrows her friend''s bicycle without asking, rides it two blocks to a coffee shop, locks it to a rack, and plans to return it within an hour. Is she guilty of common-law larceny?","answer":"No. Common-law larceny requires the intent to permanently deprive. A taking for short, safe, temporary use and return is not larceny, even if the use was unauthorized. Apply the Gold Key: unauthorized borrowing is not larceny unless the borrower''s intended use creates a substantial risk of permanent loss."},{"drill_type":"overclaim drill","target_skill":"Cut universal-quantifier distractors on the word","prompt":"A wrong answer choice says ''any unauthorized use of another''s property is larceny.'' What is the overclaim marker, and what is the correct doctrinal move?","answer":"The overclaim marker is ''any.'' Common-law larceny requires the intent to permanently deprive, not merely unauthorized use. Safe, short, return-planned borrowing is a counterexample. Cut on the word, then look for the intent-element fit."},{"drill_type":"element-mismatch drill","target_skill":"Spot element-mismatch distractors on the larceny elements","prompt":"A wrong answer choice says ''larceny is satisfied because the defendant''s use of the property involved movement from one place to another.'' What is the element-mismatch, and what is the correct doctrinal move?","answer":"Movement (asportation) is one element of larceny, not the whole thing. The intent-to-permanently-deprive element is independently required, and the stem shows clear temporary-use and intent-to-return facts that defeat the intent element on their face. Lock the intent element on the temporary-use-and-return fact pattern."}]}'),
  ('47ebef8d-353b-58b7-8cea-6feee7b049bf', '18890_hanna_erie_threshold', 'CIVIL_PROCEDURE', 'Erie Doctrine and Choice of Law', 'Erie Sequence (Hanna two-track analysis)', 'Federal-directive threshold (Hanna first-step) vs. the outcome-determinative test (Guaranty Trust).', 'In a federal diversity action, Hannah, a second-year law clerk, writes a memo to the supervising partner arguing that the Erie answer is always simple: if applying the state rule would change the outcome, the state rule applies; if it would not, federal practice applies. The case is a personal-injury claim governed by state negligence law, and the central dispute is whether an out-of-court statement is admissible against the defendant. Federal Rule of Evidence 801 directly addresses whether that statement is hearsay and excluded from evidence.', 'In a federal diversity action, Hannah, a second-year law clerk, writes a memo to the supervising partner arguing that the Erie answer is always simple: if applying the state rule would change the outcome, the state rule applies; if it would not, federal practice applies. The case is a personal-injury claim governed by state negligence law, and the central dispute is whether an out-of-court statement is admissible against the defendant. Federal Rule of Evidence 801 directly addresses whether that statement is hearsay and excluded from evidence.

What is wrong with Hannah''s statement?', 'What is wrong with Hannah''s statement?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ18890.md","source_question_id":"18890","variant_slug":"hanna_erie_threshold","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"96060100","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"core (carried from source row; not separately scored)","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A law clerk says the Erie answer is just an outcome-determinative test (state rule controls if it changes the outcome; federal practice applies if it does not). The case has a Federal Rule directly on point. Is the law clerk''s framing of Erie correct?","review_truth":"Under Hanna v. Plumer, the first step in any Erie analysis is to ask whether a valid Federal Rule of Civil Procedure or valid federal statute directly controls the issue; if it does, the federal directive applies, and the outcome-determinative test does not come into play.","dominant_trap":"B","anchor_card":{"card_id":"RC-CIVPRO-ERIE-HANNA-01","title":"Erie sequence — Hanna first-step (federal-directive threshold)","signal":"Diversity action; a Federal Rule directly on point; an over-simplified Erie framing","student_move":"Lock the federal-directive threshold on the visible stem fact (a Federal Rule directly on point). Cut B (outcome-test-only) on the universal-quantifier marker ''every.'' Cut C (state-only) on the overclaim marker ''always.'' Cut D (federal-only) on the overclaim marker ''always.''","tiny_rule":"Under Hanna, the Erie sequence begins with the federal-directive threshold; the outcome test runs only if no valid federal directive directly controls.","trap":"The ''Erie is the outcome test'' shortcut and the state-only / federal-only cliches.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CIVPRO-ERIE-HANNA-01","statement":"Under Hanna v. Plumer, the first step in any Erie analysis is to ask whether a valid Federal Rule of Civil Procedure or valid federal statute directly controls the issue; if it does, the federal directive applies, and the outcome-determinative test does not come into play.","type":"rule","trigger":"When the stem names a specific Federal Rule directly on point (e.g., FRE 801 hearsay), lock the federal-directive threshold first; do not reach for the outcome test.","authority":"Hanna v. Plumer, 380 U.S. 460 (1965); Erie Railroad Co. v. Tompkins, 304 U.S. 64 (1938); cf. Guaranty Trust Co. v. York, 326 U.S. 99 (1945); Byrd v. Blue Ridge Rural Electric Cooperative, 356 U.S. 525 (1958); Rules Enabling Act, 28 U.S.C. § 2072.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CIVPRO-ERIE-HANNA-01","statement":"When the stem names a specific Federal Rule directly on point, lock the federal-directive threshold first; do not reach for the outcome test.","type":"call_focus","trigger":"A diversity case with a Federal Rule directly on point.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"anchor drill","target_skill":"Identify the Hanna first-step (federal-directive threshold) in any Erie analysis","prompt":"In a federal diversity action, the central dispute is the scope of discovery under FRCP 26. A student argues that Erie is just the outcome-determinative test. Is the student correct?","answer":"No. Under Hanna v. Plumer, the first step in any Erie analysis is to ask whether a valid Federal Rule directly controls the issue. FRCP 26 directly controls the scope of discovery in federal court; the federal directive applies; the outcome-determinative test does not come into play. Apply the Gold Key: under Hanna, the Erie sequence begins with the federal-directive threshold; the outcome test runs only if no valid federal directive directly controls."},{"drill_type":"overclaim drill","target_skill":"Cut universal-quantifier distractors on the word","prompt":"A wrong answer choice says ''Erie is the outcome-determinative test in every case.'' What is the overclaim marker, and what is the correct doctrinal move?","answer":"The overclaim marker is ''every.'' Under Hanna v. Plumer, the Erie sequence begins with the federal-directive threshold; the outcome-determinative test runs only if no valid federal directive directly controls. The ''Erie is the outcome test in every case'' framing is a tiered_absolute overclaim that loses to the Hanna counterexample (a valid Federal Rule directly on point). Cut on the word, then look for the federal-directive threshold."},{"drill_type":"state-federal cliche drill","target_skill":"Cut state-only and federal-only cliches on the word","prompt":"A wrong answer choice says ''federal law always applies in diversity.'' What is the overclaim marker, and what is the correct doctrinal move?","answer":"The overclaim marker is ''always.'' Erie holds that state substantive law applies in diversity; federal law applies only to the extent a valid federal directive directly controls the issue. The ''federal law always applies'' framing is a tiered_absolute overclaim that loses to the Erie counterexample (state substantive law applies in diversity). Cut on the word, then look for the federal-directive threshold."}]}'),
  ('4e6ae8ca-5294-505b-a812-f5e8ebb1cfb8', '18900_wedding_garden_spill', 'TORTS', 'Negligence damages', 'Economic loss parasitic to physical injury', NULL, 'Boaz and Ruth run "Boaz & Ruth''s Wedding Garden," a Christian-owned wedding venue on a converted farm outside a small Texas town. Esther, a self-employed wedding photographer, was working a Saturday reception in the venue''s main barn. While Esther was setting up a second camera near the open bar, an empty cocktail glass slipped out of a guest''s hand and shattered on the wood floor, leaving a wet, slick patch. A venue employee had just mopped the same area minutes earlier and had not placed any warning cones. Esther stepped on the wet patch, fell, and broke her right ankle. She missed three weeks of wedding-season bookings during recovery — one engagement shoot and two paid wedding-day shoots she had already contracted to photograph. The venue argues that the missed-booking income is pure economic loss and therefore cannot be recovered in negligence.', 'Boaz and Ruth run "Boaz & Ruth''s Wedding Garden," a Christian-owned wedding venue on a converted farm outside a small Texas town. Esther, a self-employed wedding photographer, was working a Saturday reception in the venue''s main barn. While Esther was setting up a second camera near the open bar, an empty cocktail glass slipped out of a guest''s hand and shattered on the wood floor, leaving a wet, slick patch. A venue employee had just mopped the same area minutes earlier and had not placed any warning cones. Esther stepped on the wet patch, fell, and broke her right ankle. She missed three weeks of wedding-season bookings during recovery — one engagement shoot and two paid wedding-day shoots she had already contracted to photograph. The venue argues that the missed-booking income is pure economic loss and therefore cannot be recovered in negligence.

How should the court treat Esther''s lost-booking claim?', 'How should the court treat Esther''s lost-booking claim?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ18900.md","source_question_id":"18900","variant_slug":"wedding_garden_spill","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"61020500","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"core","deciding_phase":"CALL","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A customer slips on a slick spill at a business, breaks an ankle, and misses three weeks of work. The business argues the lost wages are pure economic loss and therefore unrecoverable in negligence. Should the court allow the lost-wage recovery?","review_truth":"Lost wages are recoverable economic consequences of a tort-caused physical injury; they are not standalone pure economic loss.","dominant_trap":"B","anchor_card":{"card_id":"RC-TORTS-LOST-WAGES-PARASITIC-01","title":"Lost wages are parasitic damages when they flow from a tort-caused physical injury","signal":"Negligence claim, plaintiff has a physical injury, plaintiff seeks lost wages or lost self-employed contract income, defendant invokes ''pure economic loss.''","student_move":"Cut the ''all financial loss is pure economic loss'' overclaim, the nominal-only remedy, and the contract-theory frame. Apply the Gold Key: financial harm that flows from a tort-caused physical injury is recoverable parasitic damages.","tiny_rule":"Lost wages are parasitic on a tort-caused physical injury; the standalone pure economic loss bar does not apply when the plaintiff has a physical injury.","trap":"The dominant trap is treating ''pure economic loss'' as an absolute bar to financial recovery in negligence. The bar is a no-physical-injury bar; once a physical injury is present, the financial harm rides in on the personal-injury claim.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-TORTS-WEDDING-GARDEN-SPILL-01","statement":"Economic damages that flow from a tort-caused physical injury are recoverable; they are not standalone pure economic loss.","type":"rule","trigger":"negligence suit with a physical injury and a request for lost wages or lost self-employed contract income","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 924; McDougald v. Perry, 146 So. 2d 408 (Fla. Dist. Ct. App. 1962); Robins Dry Dock & Repair Co. v. Flint, 275 U.S. 303 (1927)","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-TORTS-WEDDING-GARDEN-SPILL-01","statement":"When the defendant''s argument uses the phrase ''pure economic loss,'' check whether the plaintiff also has a physical injury — if yes, the bar does not apply and the financial harm rides in on the personal-injury claim.","type":"call_focus","trigger":"defendant in a negligence suit invokes ''pure economic loss'' as a defense to a lost-wage claim","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"spot-the-overclaim","target_skill":"recognize the ''always pure economic loss'' overclaim in negligence suits with a physical injury","prompt":"Identify the overclaim pattern in: ''Bar it, because missed-booking income in a negligence case is always pure economic loss.''","answer":"The ''always'' framing is the tell. The standalone pure economic loss bar applies only to plaintiffs with no physical injury; it does not bar financial harm that flows from a tort-caused physical injury."},{"drill_type":"wrong-remedy cut","target_skill":"separate damages quantification from damages remedy category","prompt":"Why is ''nominal only'' the wrong remedy when a negligence plaintiff with a broken ankle seeks lost future bookings?","answer":"Contingent future bookings are a damages-quantification issue (certainty, mitigation), not a remedy-category issue. Once causation and a reasonable degree of certainty are established, lost future earnings are recoverable as compensatory damages."},{"drill_type":"frame-match","target_skill":"match the call to the cause of action","prompt":"Why is ''bar it unless contract'' the wrong frame when the suit is in tort for a premises-injury negligence claim?","answer":"The suit is in tort, not on a contract for the missed bookings. The relevant contracts measure the loss, but they are not the cause of action against the venue."}]}'),
  ('e97f7fd0-cf65-563c-8004-3fb889865b75', '18914_rare_bible_provenance', 'TORTS', 'Economic torts', 'Fraud - reliance', NULL, 'Esther, a retired Christian schoolteacher in New England who had been quietly selling down her late husband''s collection of antique books, orally told Daniel, a seminary student and serious Bible collector, that a 1699 Geneva Bible she was offering had "all of its original pages." Before paying, Daniel received in the mail an unsigned single-page provenance letter from a well-known third-party biblical-authentication firm; the letter was printed on firm letterhead and plainly stated on its first page: "PAGES 412-448 WERE REPLACED IN 1948; REMAINDER OF VOLUME CONSISTENT WITH 1699 ISSUE." Daniel did not read the letter. He paid Esther $4,800 for the Bible, later noticed the replaced pages, and now sues Esther for fraud.', 'Esther, a retired Christian schoolteacher in New England who had been quietly selling down her late husband''s collection of antique books, orally told Daniel, a seminary student and serious Bible collector, that a 1699 Geneva Bible she was offering had "all of its original pages." Before paying, Daniel received in the mail an unsigned single-page provenance letter from a well-known third-party biblical-authentication firm; the letter was printed on firm letterhead and plainly stated on its first page: "PAGES 412-448 WERE REPLACED IN 1948; REMAINDER OF VOLUME CONSISTENT WITH 1699 ISSUE." Daniel did not read the letter. He paid Esther $4,800 for the Bible, later noticed the replaced pages, and now sues Esther for fraud.

What is Esther''s strongest defense?', 'What is Esther''s strongest defense?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ18914.md","source_question_id":"18914","variant_slug":"rare_bible_provenance","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"62050200","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"core","deciding_phase":"CALL","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A seller orally represents that an antique Bible has all original pages; before the sale, the buyer receives a third-party provenance letter plainly stating that specific pages were replaced; the buyer does not read the letter and later sues for fraud. What is the seller''s strongest defense?","review_truth":"Fraud requires justifiable reliance; reliance is not justified when the plaintiff ignores an obvious contradiction in materials provided before the transaction.","dominant_trap":"C","anchor_card":{"card_id":"RC-TORTS-FRAUD-JUSTIFIABLE-RELIANCE-01","title":"Fraud requires justifiable reliance; reliance is defeated by ignoring an obvious contradiction","signal":"Fraud suit, pre-sale written document plainly contradicts the oral assurance, plaintiff did not read the document.","student_move":"Cut the private-law contract trope (oral overrides written), the no-reliance-element misstatement, and the fact/opinion confusion. Apply the Gold Key: ignoring an obvious, plainly-worded contradiction can defeat justifiable reliance as a matter of law.","tiny_rule":"Justifiable reliance is a flexible reasonableness standard; reliance on an obvious written contradiction of an oral assurance is not justified.","trap":"The dominant trap is the fact/opinion confusion: the student treats a verifiable historical fact (page replacement) as if it were a value judgment or connoisseur''s opinion. The other strong trap is the private-law contract trope (oral overrides written).","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-TORTS-RARE-BIBLE-PROVENANCE-01","statement":"Justifiable reliance is defeated when the plaintiff ignores an obvious, plainly-worded contradiction in materials provided before the transaction.","type":"rule","trigger":"fraud suit, pre-sale written document plainly contradicts the oral assurance, plaintiff did not read the document","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 525; § 545A and comments; Prosser & Keeton, The Law of Torts § 108","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-TORTS-RARE-BIBLE-PROVENANCE-01","statement":"Lock the call to the seller''s strongest defense — that is the lack-of-reliance defense, not the opinion defense or the no-reliance defense.","type":"call_focus","trigger":"the call asks ''what is the seller''s strongest defense'' in a fraud suit where the buyer ignored a pre-sale written contradiction","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"frame-match","target_skill":"match the call to the cause of action (tort vs. contract)","prompt":"Why is ''oral overrides written'' the wrong frame when the suit is in tort for fraud?","answer":"The parol evidence rule and the ''oral overrides written'' framing are private-law contract doctrines; they do not control the reliance analysis in tort. The relevant question is what a reasonable buyer would have noticed, not which version of a statement ''wins'' as a matter of form."},{"drill_type":"fact-vs-opinion","target_skill":"separate verifiable historical fact from value judgment","prompt":"Why is ''the originality of Bible pages is always opinion'' a fact/opinion confusion in fraud?","answer":"The originality of specific pages in a specific book is a verifiable historical fact, not a value judgment or connoisseur''s opinion. The ''always opinion'' framing is the tell."},{"drill_type":"elements-check","target_skill":"list the elements of fraud and prove each independently","prompt":"Why is ''fraud does not require reliance if the statement was intentional'' a flat misstatement of the elements of fraud?","answer":"Reliance is an independent element of fraud; proving the defendant''s scienter does not waive the reliance requirement. Restatement (Second) of Torts § 525."}]}'),
  ('4566cda2-0326-5f9a-8cdd-1fb34622244f', '18974_subscription_meal_kit_23f', 'CIVIL_PROCEDURE', 'Appeals and Finality', 'Class Certification Appeals', 'importance-as-of-right jurisdiction versus permission-based Rule 23(f) review', 'A national subscription home-meal-kit service bills its customers on a recurring monthly basis. A group of consumers sues the company in federal court, alleging a hidden-fee billing scheme. The district court certifies a consumer class. The meal-kit company immediately files a notice of appeal and argues that class certification is so consequential that the court of appeals must hear it as of right.', 'A national subscription home-meal-kit service bills its customers on a recurring monthly basis. A group of consumers sues the company in federal court, alleging a hidden-fee billing scheme. The district court certifies a consumer class. The meal-kit company immediately files a notice of appeal and argues that class certification is so consequential that the court of appeals must hear it as of right.

What is the correct appellate route?', 'What is the correct appellate route?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ18974.md","source_question_id":"18974","variant_slug":"subscription_meal_kit_23f","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"93110100","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium","deciding_phase":"CLASH","confidence":"HARD_STRUCTURAL","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A district court certifies a consumer class; the defendant immediately files a notice of appeal and argues that the importance of the class-certification question entitles it to appeal as of right. What appellate route is available?","review_truth":"A district court''s class-certification order is not appealable as of right; the route to the court of appeals is a Rule 23(f) petition for permission to appeal, and importance of the issue does not change that.","dominant_trap":"B","anchor_card":{"card_id":"RC-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-RULE-23F-INTERLOCUTORY-01","title":"Class-certification orders are not appealable as of right; the route is Rule 23(f)","signal":"A district court grants or denies class certification; the losing party files an immediate notice of appeal. The call is the appellate route.","student_move":"When the call asks ''what is the appellate route'' for a non-final class-certification order, the answer is a Rule 23(f) petition for permission to appeal — never an as-of-right appeal. Importance of the issue does not change the route.","tiny_rule":"A class-certification order is not appealable as of right; the route is a Rule 23(f) discretionary petition.","trap":"Importance-as-jurisdiction bait. Many students accept the importance argument and pick ''automatic appeal as of right.'' The final-judgment rule is the default, and Rule 23(f) is permission-based, not as-of-right.","confidence":"HARD_STRUCTURAL"},"gold_keys":[],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-SUBSCRIPTION-MEAL-KIT-23F-01","statement":"When the call asks ''what is the appellate route'' for a non-final order, scan for a permission-based statute or rule (Rule 23(f), 28 U.S.C. § 1292(b), mandamus, collateral order). The default is no interlocutory appeal as of right.","type":"cut","trigger":"defendant files an immediate notice of appeal from a non-final class-certification order and argues that the importance of the question entitles it to appeal as of right","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"false-rule / overclaim detection in appellate-route calls","prompt":"The district court grants a plaintiff''s motion to dismiss. The defendant immediately files a notice of appeal. The defendant argues that the order is so important that the court of appeals must hear it as of right. What is the correct appellate route?","answer":"None as of right. A motion-to-dismiss order is interlocutory; the default is the final-judgment rule of 28 U.S.C. § 1291. If the order is not within a permission-based carve-out (e.g., 28 U.S.C. § 1292(b), mandamus, collateral order), the defendant''s only route is to wait for final judgment."},{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"importance-as-jurisdiction bait detection","prompt":"The district court denies a motion to certify a class. The defendant immediately files a notice of appeal and argues that the class-certification question is so consequential that the court of appeals must hear it as of right. What is the correct appellate route?","answer":"Rule 23(f) petition for permission to appeal. Rule 23(f) covers both grants and denials of class certification. The court of appeals has discretion; importance of the question does not change the route."},{"drill_type":"anchor","target_skill":"Rule 23(f) timing and discretion","prompt":"When must a Rule 23(f) petition be filed, and what is the standard for granting it?","answer":"The petition must be filed within fourteen days of the class-certification order. The court of appeals has discretion whether to grant the petition; the decision is reviewed for abuse of discretion."}]}'),
  ('6e7b71c9-56a8-5ba8-8814-9e5d8a6fcb21', '18982_homeschool_coop_month', 'TORTS', 'Defamation', 'Falsity and substantial truth', 'any-inaccuracy myth versus substantial-truth doctrine (gist or sting)', 'A faith-based homeschool-co-op newsletter stated that a co-op member''s childcare license was revoked in May for failing a state safety inspection. The license was actually revoked in June for the same safety-inspection failure. The member sued the newsletter for defamation based only on the wrong month.', 'A faith-based homeschool-co-op newsletter stated that a co-op member''s childcare license was revoked in May for failing a state safety inspection. The license was actually revoked in June for the same safety-inspection failure. The member sued the newsletter for defamation based only on the wrong month.

What is the best defense?', 'What is the best defense?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ18982.md","source_question_id":"18982","variant_slug":"homeschool_coop_month","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"62050401","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium","deciding_phase":"CALL","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A private publication says X''s license was revoked in May for failing a state safety inspection. The license was actually revoked in June for the same failure. X sues for defamation based only on the wrong month. What is the best defense?","review_truth":"A statement is not defamatory if the gist or sting is substantially true, even if minor details (such as the exact month) are inaccurate.","dominant_trap":"D","anchor_card":{"card_id":"RC-TORTS-DEFAMATION-SUBSTANTIAL-TRUTH-01","title":"Defamation requires falsity; substantial truth tolerates minor inaccuracies","signal":"A private publication misstates a date (month) of an otherwise-true defamatory fact (license revocation, criminal charge, professional sanction, etc.); the plaintiff sues based only on the wrong date. The call is the best defense.","student_move":"Identify the defamatory sting. If the sting is true — even if a minor detail (month, day, year) is wrong — substantial truth is the defense. The ''any mistake defeats truth'' framing is the any-inaccuracy myth.","tiny_rule":"A statement is not defamatory if the gist or sting is substantially true, even if minor details are inaccurate.","trap":"The any-inaccuracy myth is the dominant trap. Many students half-remember that ''defamation requires truth'' and reason that any mistake (even a one-month error) makes the statement false.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-TORTS-HOMESCHOOL-COOP-MONTH-01","statement":"Defamation requires falsity, but a statement is not false if the gist or sting is substantially true. Minor inaccuracies that do not change the gist or sting (such as a wrong month) are tolerated.","type":"rule","trigger":"private publication misstates a date (month) of an otherwise-true defamatory fact; the plaintiff sues based only on the wrong date","authority":"Milkovich v. Lorain Journal Co., 497 U.S. 1, 19-20 (1990); Masson v. New Yorker Magazine, Inc., 501 U.S. 496, 513-17 (1991)","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"anchor","target_skill":"substantial-truth doctrine (gist or sting)","prompt":"A newspaper states that a city council member was indicted in March for accepting a bribe. The council member was actually indicted in April for accepting a bribe. The council member sues for defamation based only on the wrong month. What is the best defense?","answer":"Substantial truth. The defamatory sting is ''indicted for accepting a bribe''; the sting is true; the wrong month is a minor inaccuracy that does not change the sting."},{"drill_type":"anchor","target_skill":"absolute privilege scope","prompt":"A state legislator makes a defamatory statement on the floor of the legislature about a private citizen. The citizen sues. Is the legislator''s statement absolutely privileged?","answer":"Yes, as to statements made on the floor of the legislature in the legislator''s official capacity. The privilege is reserved for institutional speakers speaking in the relevant capacity. A private newsletter is not within that set."},{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"opinion defense scope (verifiable facts are not opinion)","prompt":"A blogger writes that a doctor ''had his license revoked for malpractice.'' The license was actually revoked for failing a state inspection, not malpractice. Is the blogger''s statement protected opinion?","answer":"No. The statement asserts a verifiable fact (license revocation) and a verifiable reason (malpractice). The opinion defense requires a non-verifiable statement. The statement is actionable if the reason is materially false."}]}'),
  ('387ce434-0464-558d-a8a2-43e17d64fb24', '19379_hymn_candle_invoice_van', 'EVIDENCE', 'III.B.2 — Marital Communications Privilege', 'Marital Communications Privilege', 'adverse spousal testimonial privilege vs confidential marital communications privilege', 'In a federal wire-fraud prosecution against Timothy over online sales from his small Christian-craft booth, the prosecutor plans to call Lydia. Timothy and Lydia finalized their divorce eighteen months ago. Years earlier, while they were married, the two were sitting alone in Timothy’s parked delivery van after a city winter fair when Timothy quietly told Lydia, “I forged the online invoices for those hymn-themed candle orders.” Timothy objects to Lydia’s testimony about that private statement on spousal-privilege grounds.', 'In a federal wire-fraud prosecution against Timothy over online sales from his small Christian-craft booth, the prosecutor plans to call Lydia. Timothy and Lydia finalized their divorce eighteen months ago. Years earlier, while they were married, the two were sitting alone in Timothy’s parked delivery van after a city winter fair when Timothy quietly told Lydia, “I forged the online invoices for those hymn-themed candle orders.” Timothy objects to Lydia’s testimony about that private statement on spousal-privilege grounds.

How should the court rule?', 'How should the court rule?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ19379.md","source_question_id":"19379","variant_slug":"hymn_candle_invoice_van","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"35030202","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"discriminator","deciding_phase":"CLASH","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":"divorced now; private statement during marriage","distilled_core_question":"Former spouse is called in a federal criminal case to testify about a private statement made during the marriage. Does divorce defeat the privilege objection?","review_truth":"Divorce ends the adverse-testimonial route, not the confidential-communications route for private statements made during marriage.","dominant_trap":"C","anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-EVIDENCE-SPOUSAL-19379-01","title":"Do not collapse the two spousal privileges","signal":"Divorced spouse asked about a private statement from the marriage.","student_move":"Separate testimonial privilege from communications privilege before choosing sustain or overrule.","tiny_rule":"Current marriage matters for testimonial privilege; private communication during marriage matters for communications privilege.","trap":"Divorce ends everything.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-EVIDENCE-SPOUSAL-01","statement":"Spousal testimonial privilege and confidential marital communications privilege are separate. Testimonial privilege requires a current marriage; communications privilege can protect confidential private communications made during the marriage even after divorce.","type":"distinction","trigger":"Divorced former spouse + private statement made during marriage.","authority":"FRE 501; Trammel v. United States; Pereira v. United States.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-EVIDENCE-SPOUSAL-01","statement":"When the answer set splits sustain/overrule and testimonial/communications, pick the route that matches the timing facts: divorced now, private communication then.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"Two sustain answers with different privilege labels.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"Gold Key recall","target_skill":"spousal privilege distinction","prompt":"A divorced former spouse is called to testify about a private statement from the marriage. Which privilege route is live?","answer":"Confidential marital communications privilege."},{"drill_type":"Overclaim cut","target_skill":"Ear: tiered_absolute","prompt":"Cut the defect: ''Divorce ends every spousal privilege.''","answer":"Tiered absolute; divorce does not end every spousal privilege."},{"drill_type":"Wrong-frame clash","target_skill":"Issue-Sense: bait_doctrine","prompt":"Two answers say sustain. One says adverse testimonial privilege; one says confidential marital communications privilege. The parties are divorced, but the statement was private and made during marriage. Which route wins?","answer":"Confidential marital communications privilege."}]}'),
  ('72a48c04-e965-5332-a035-431e62baad85', '19381_shepherd_olive_press_deed', 'REAL_PROPERTY', 'Conveyance by Deed — Requirements for Valid Deed', 'B1', 'recording as validity condition vs recording as notice/priority protection', 'Naomi owned a small olive-press parcel behind her bakery. After Lydia completed a private hymn-calligraphy commission for Naomi, Naomi signed and handed Lydia a deed conveying the parcel to Lydia. The deed contained all required deed terms, and Lydia accepted it. Lydia tucked the deed inside a Psalms hymnal and never recorded it. No later buyer, lender, creditor, or other third party claims the parcel. Naomi later says the deed did not work because no recorder’s stamp was ever placed on it.', 'Naomi owned a small olive-press parcel behind her bakery. After Lydia completed a private hymn-calligraphy commission for Naomi, Naomi signed and handed Lydia a deed conveying the parcel to Lydia. The deed contained all required deed terms, and Lydia accepted it. Lydia tucked the deed inside a Psalms hymnal and never recorded it. No later buyer, lender, creditor, or other third party claims the parcel. Naomi later says the deed did not work because no recorder’s stamp was ever placed on it.

Who owns the olive-press parcel?', 'Who owns the olive-press parcel?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ19381.md","source_question_id":"19381","variant_slug":"shepherd_olive_press_deed","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"84040201","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"calibration","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":"No third party claims the parcel.","distilled_core_question":"Grantor signed, delivered, and grantee accepted a valid deed, but the grantee never recorded it. No third party claims the land. Who owns it?","review_truth":"Recording protects against outsiders; it is not required for a valid deed to pass title between grantor and grantee.","dominant_trap":"A","anchor_card":{"card_id":"RP-ANCHOR-RECORDING-VALIDITY-01","title":"Recording vs Validity","signal":"Valid deed delivered and accepted, but not recorded.","student_move":"Ask: original parties or outsider priority?","tiny_rule":"No outsider: recording is not required for title to pass between grantor and grantee.","trap":"Treating the recorder’s office as the title-transfer event.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-REAL_PROPERTY-RECORDING-VALIDITY-01","statement":"A valid deed delivered and accepted passes title between grantor and grantee even if unrecorded; recording protects notice and priority against outsiders.","type":"distinction","trigger":"Valid deed + delivery + acceptance + no recording + no third-party claimant.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-REAL_PROPERTY-RECORDING-VALIDITY-01","statement":"When three choices make missing recording decide ownership, first ask whether the stem has an outsider. No outsider means the array is testing validity between the original parties.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"All distractors point to nonrecording.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"anchor_recall","target_skill":"Recording vs validity","prompt":"A valid deed is delivered and accepted but not recorded. No third party appears. Who owns?","answer":"The grantee owns; recording is not required between grantor and grantee."},{"drill_type":"answer_array","target_skill":"3-vs-1 recording trap","prompt":"Three choices give the grantor title because the deed was not recorded. One gives the grantee title because delivery and acceptance occurred. What is the array testing?","answer":"Recording vs validity."},{"drill_type":"trigger_fact","target_skill":"Outsider check","prompt":"What fact would change this into a recording-act priority question?","answer":"A later buyer, lender, creditor, or other protected third-party claimant."}]}'),
  ('2c26c157-512d-509e-b5fc-7b272d09e2e2', '19386_scripture_audio_cap', 'CONTRACTS', 'Third-Party Beneficiary Contracts', 'VII-A', 'same-contract cap travels with intended-beneficiary right vs nonparty escape from the cap', 'Hannah spent $26,000 replacing unusable Scripture-memory audio tracks after Lydia, a freelance sound engineer, delivered corrupted masters. The work had been commissioned under Peter''s written service contract with Lydia. That contract required Lydia to prepare the tracks for Hannah''s private tutoring program and stated, "Lydia''s total liability for any audio-work claim is capped at $12,000." Hannah sues Lydia as an intended beneficiary and seeks $26,000.', 'Hannah spent $26,000 replacing unusable Scripture-memory audio tracks after Lydia, a freelance sound engineer, delivered corrupted masters. The work had been commissioned under Peter''s written service contract with Lydia. That contract required Lydia to prepare the tracks for Hannah''s private tutoring program and stated, "Lydia''s total liability for any audio-work claim is capped at $12,000." Hannah sues Lydia as an intended beneficiary and seeks $26,000.

Which argument is Lydia''s strongest response?', 'Which argument is Lydia''s strongest response?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ19386.md","source_question_id":"19386","variant_slug":"scripture_audio_cap","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"53070100","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"core diagnostic","deciding_phase":"CLASH","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":"The beneficiary promise and liability cap are in the same written contract.","distilled_core_question":"A written service contract directly benefits a named third party and caps liability. The third party sues as an intended beneficiary for more than the cap. Is the beneficiary limited by the cap?","review_truth":"A third-party beneficiary can enforce the contract only as the contract gives it; the beneficiary does not get the benefit without the cap.","dominant_trap":"D","anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-CONTRACTS-TPB-PACKAGE-01","title":"Third-party beneficiary package deal","signal":"A nonparty enforces a contract that also limits the claim.","student_move":"Do not split the contract into benefit-only and cap-free pieces.","tiny_rule":"TPB right travels with the contract terms.","trap":"Nonparty status feels like freedom from the cap.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONTRACTS-TPB-CAP-01","statement":"An intended beneficiary takes the contract as written. If the same contract creates the beneficiary''s right and caps liability, the beneficiary''s claim is capped.","type":"rule","trigger":"A nonparty sues as an intended beneficiary and seeks more than a limit in the same contract.","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts §§ 302, 309.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONTRACTS-TPB-CAP-01","statement":"In a no-recovery / capped-recovery / full-recovery / assignment answer set, compare the source of the right with the source of the limit. Same source means package answer.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"A beneficiary claims more than a contract limit.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"overclaim","prompt":"A choice says intended beneficiaries can never enforce service contracts. What is the visible breaker?","answer":"The categorical word ''never'' overclaims."},{"drill_type":"CLASH","target_skill":"source-of-right/source-of-limit axis","prompt":"A beneficiary sues under a contract that also caps the promisor''s liability. What axis decides cap vs full recovery?","answer":"Whether the right and the cap come from the same contract."},{"drill_type":"ANCHOR","target_skill":"TPB scope","prompt":"State the Gold Key for an intended beneficiary and a contract cap.","answer":"An intended beneficiary takes the contract as written; same-contract caps apply."},{"drill_type":"ISSUE-SENSE","target_skill":"TPB vs assignment","prompt":"The stem says the plaintiff sues as an intended beneficiary. A choice says recovery requires assignment. What is the mold?","answer":"Bait doctrine / wrong frame."}]}'),
  ('aa0bdc43-9381-5cdf-9cb6-fb3eb072ffeb', '19430_harp_instructor_olive_grove', 'REAL_PROPERTY', 'Conveyance by Deed — Requirements for Valid Deed', 'B1', 'ascertainable role-holder vs formalities/entity traps', 'Lydia owned a fenced olive grove outside Capernaum. Before leaving for a psalm-singing festival, she signed and delivered a deed stating, “I convey the olive grove to the harp instructor of Capernaum Hymn School today.” At that moment, Daniel was the only harp instructor of Capernaum Hymn School. Daniel accepted the deed.', 'Lydia owned a fenced olive grove outside Capernaum. Before leaving for a psalm-singing festival, she signed and delivered a deed stating, “I convey the olive grove to the harp instructor of Capernaum Hymn School today.” At that moment, Daniel was the only harp instructor of Capernaum Hymn School. Daniel accepted the deed.

Who owns the olive grove?', 'Who owns the olive grove?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ19430.md","source_question_id":"19430","variant_slug":"harp_instructor_olive_grove","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"84040201","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"calibration","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A deed conveys land to a role-holder; one person held that role at delivery and accepted. Who owns?","review_truth":"A role description can identify the grantee if the person holding the role is ascertainable at delivery.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"RP-DEED-ROLE-GRANTEE-01","title":"Role Description as Grantee","signal":"Deed conveys to “the [role holder] of [organization].”","student_move":"Ask whether one person fit that role at delivery.","tiny_rule":"Role description is enough if it identifies one person at delivery.","trap":"Do not require the personal name or grantee signature.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-REAL_PROPERTY-ASCERTAINABLE-GRANTEE-01","statement":"A deed does not fail merely because the grantee is described by role rather than personal name. If one person fits the description at delivery, that person is ascertainable and can take title.","type":"rule","trigger":"Deed says “to the [office/role holder]” and the stem says one person held that role at delivery.","authority":"Vineyard v. O’Connor, 36 S.W. 424 (Tex. 1896), as cited in ConocoPhillips Co. v. Hahn.","last_minute_review":true},{"id":"GK-REAL_PROPERTY-GRANTEE-SIGNATURE-01","statement":"A grantee generally need not sign the deed to take title; delivery by the grantor and acceptance by the grantee do the work.","type":"rule","trigger":"Answer says the named recipient loses because the recipient did not sign.","authority":"Tex. Prop. Code § 5.021 requires the conveyance to be subscribed and delivered by the conveyor or authorized agent.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-REAL_PROPERTY-ROLE-GRANTEE-01","statement":"When a deed names a role, separate the role-holder from the organization named inside the role phrase before checking formalities.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"Choice set includes the person, the grantor, and the organization as possible owners.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"anchor_recall","target_skill":"ascertainable grantee by role","prompt":"A deed says “to the choir director of a school today.” One person held that role at delivery. Who is the grantee?","answer":"The role-holder."},{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"fabricated deed formalities","prompt":"Cut the choice: “Grantor keeps title because the grantee did not sign the deed.”","answer":"NOT_TRUE; fabricated_rule; grantee signature generally not required."},{"drill_type":"answer_array","target_skill":"role-holder vs entity","prompt":"A deed says “to the treasurer of Mercy Hall.” Does that automatically make Mercy Hall the grantee?","answer":"No; first ask who the role-holder was."}]}'),
  ('8b887187-31c3-5c86-8525-494ad62dd6e6', '19434_hymn_hands_stimulator', 'TORTS', 'Products liability', 'Failure to warn - learned intermediary', NULL, 'Before Lydia played handbells at a community hymn festival, her physician, Stephen, prescribed a prescription-only wrist nerve stimulator to control hand tremors. The stimulator carried a rare risk of finger numbness that could make fine hand movement difficult. Before Stephen ordered it, Barnabas Biotech sent Stephen an updated professional safety notice that clearly described the risk, symptoms, and alternative treatments. Stephen read the notice, discussed the risk with Lydia, and ordered the stimulator because alternatives were unsuitable. Lydia later suffered finger numbness and sued Barnabas Biotech, arguing that Barnabas did not send the same warning directly to her. The jurisdiction follows the learned-intermediary rule.

Call:', 'Before Lydia played handbells at a community hymn festival, her physician, Stephen, prescribed a prescription-only wrist nerve stimulator to control hand tremors. The stimulator carried a rare risk of finger numbness that could make fine hand movement difficult. Before Stephen ordered it, Barnabas Biotech sent Stephen an updated professional safety notice that clearly described the risk, symptoms, and alternative treatments. Stephen read the notice, discussed the risk with Lydia, and ordered the stimulator because alternatives were unsuitable. Lydia later suffered finger numbness and sued Barnabas Biotech, arguing that Barnabas did not send the same warning directly to her. The jurisdiction follows the learned-intermediary rule.

Call:

Is Lydia likely to prevail on a strict products liability claim for failure to warn against Barnabas Biotech?', 'Is Lydia likely to prevail on a strict products liability claim for failure to warn against Barnabas Biotech?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ19434.md","source_question_id":"19434","variant_slug":"hymn_hands_stimulator","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"63030100","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium_high_anchor","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A prescription-only product had a clear risk warning sent to the prescribing physician, who read it and discussed it with the patient. The patient suffered the warned-of risk and sues because the manufacturer did not warn her directly. Does the failure-to-warn claim succeed?","review_truth":"In a learned-intermediary jurisdiction, an adequate warning to the prescribing physician can defeat a direct-to-patient failure-to-warn theory for a prescription product.","dominant_trap":"C","anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-TORTS-LI-01","title":"Warned risk is not warning defect","signal":"The answer says the plaintiff suffered the exact risk described.","student_move":"Ask whether the call is about injury or about warning duty.","tiny_rule":"Injury completes harm; it does not prove the warning was defective.","trap":"Wrong-element answer on failure-to-warn claims.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-TORTS-LEARNED-INTERMEDIARY-01","statement":"For prescription products, an adequate warning to the prescribing healthcare provider can satisfy the manufacturer''s warning duty; the manufacturer is not automatically required to warn the patient directly.","type":"distinction","trigger":"Prescription-only product + professional warning to prescriber + jurisdiction follows learned-intermediary rule.","authority":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Products Liability § 6(d); learned-intermediary case law.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-TORTS-WARNED-RISK-01","statement":"On a failure-to-warn call, an answer saying the warned-of risk happened proves injury, not warning defect.","type":"trap_spotting","trigger":"Choice points to injury by the described risk instead of the adequacy or audience of the warning.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"wrong_element","prompt":"Failure-to-warn claim. The answer says the plaintiff suffered the exact risk listed in the warning. What filter breaks?","answer":"NOT_RESPONSIVE: it proves injury, not warning defect."},{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"overclaim","prompt":"An answer says prescription products are exempt from all products liability claims. What mold?","answer":"tiered_absolute."},{"drill_type":"ANCHOR","target_skill":"learned intermediary","prompt":"Prescription-only product, adequate warning to prescribing physician, no direct patient warning. What Gold Key?","answer":"Learned intermediary: adequate warning to prescriber can satisfy the manufacturer''s warning duty."},{"drill_type":"PREDICT","target_skill":"call lock","prompt":"A patient sues for failure to warn after the doctor received and discussed a warning. What is the precise call?","answer":"Whether the manufacturer breached the warning duty, not whether the patient was injured."}]}'),
  ('0cb5c216-ffe2-5b1c-bb99-f3e3c2da0744', '19448_praise_night_tshirt_cannon', 'TORTS', 'Negligence', 'Duty - negligent entrustment', 'control/supply versus formal ownership', 'At a rented civic auditorium, Lydia organized a private Christian music night. A vendor left a compressed-air T-shirt cannon for authorized staff to use between songs. Lydia was not the cannon''s owner, but under the event agreement she had the key and authority to decide who could handle it during the event. During setup, Lydia handed the cannon to Barnabas, a visibly intoxicated friend who had never used one, so he could "make the praise-team singers laugh." Barnabas fired wildly, knocked over two music stands, and then struck Ruth, a visiting photographer, in the face. Ruth sues Lydia for negligence.', 'At a rented civic auditorium, Lydia organized a private Christian music night. A vendor left a compressed-air T-shirt cannon for authorized staff to use between songs. Lydia was not the cannon''s owner, but under the event agreement she had the key and authority to decide who could handle it during the event. During setup, Lydia handed the cannon to Barnabas, a visibly intoxicated friend who had never used one, so he could "make the praise-team singers laugh." Barnabas fired wildly, knocked over two music stands, and then struck Ruth, a visiting photographer, in the face. Ruth sues Lydia for negligence.

Is Ruth likely to establish a negligent entrustment duty against Lydia?', 'Is Ruth likely to establish a negligent entrustment duty against Lydia?', 3, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ19448.md","source_question_id":"19448","variant_slug":"praise_night_tshirt_cannon","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"61020103","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium-hard","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A person with authority to control a dangerous chattel gives it to a visibly intoxicated, inexperienced user, who injures a third party. Can the controller face negligent entrustment liability even though she is not the owner?","review_truth":"For negligent entrustment, control/supply plus known unsafe user beats formal title.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"RC-TORTS-NE-001","title":"Control Beats Title","signal":"Non-owner had authority over who could use the chattel.","student_move":"Ask who supplied or permitted use, then ask whether the user was visibly unsafe.","tiny_rule":"For negligent entrustment, supplier/control can be enough; title is not the gate.","trap":"Owner-only answer.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-TORTS-NEGLIGENT-ENTRUSTMENT-01","statement":"Negligent entrustment looks to control or supply of the chattel plus known unsafe use; formal legal title is not the gate.","type":"distinction","trigger":"Defendant is not owner but has key, permission authority, or control and hands the chattel to an impaired or inexperienced user.","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Torts §§ 308, 390; Delana v. CED Sales, Inc.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-TORTS-NEGLIGENT-ENTRUSTMENT-01","statement":"Lock the target defendant first: the call asks about Lydia''s duty, so an answer focused only on Barnabas''s operation is adjacent-call bait.","type":"call_focus","trigger":"The injured plaintiff sues the person who handed over the chattel, not the person who physically used it.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"overclaim","prompt":"Negligent entrustment makes the entrustor strictly liable for any injury caused by the chattel.","answer":"CUT: tiered_absolute / wrong_standard."},{"drill_type":"ANCHOR","target_skill":"control versus title","prompt":"The defendant is not the owner but has authority to decide who uses the chattel.","answer":"Use Gold Key: supplier/control can satisfy the negligent entrustment gate."},{"drill_type":"CALL_FOCUS","target_skill":"target defendant","prompt":"The injured plaintiff sues the person who handed over the chattel, but a choice says the user alone operated it.","answer":"Lock the call to the entrustor''s duty; do not answer only the user''s negligence."}]}'),
  ('961ae7f9-0caf-5951-94cf-4179781ff7c7', '19499_jericho_chariot_night', 'CRIMINAL_LAW', 'Inchoate Crimes and Parties', 'Inchoate Crimes', 'minimum required participants vs extra participants', 'A state criminalizes an unlicensed paid chariot race between two racers. Peter and John agree to drive such a race at a private Bible-themed harvest fair. Lydia, Ruth, and Barnabas agree to rent a fairground track, sell admission wristbands, and handle the cashbox and livestream fees. Prosecutors charge all five with conspiracy to conduct the illegal chariot race. The defendants invoke Wharton’s Rule.', 'A state criminalizes an unlicensed paid chariot race between two racers. Peter and John agree to drive such a race at a private Bible-themed harvest fair. Lydia, Ruth, and Barnabas agree to rent a fairground track, sell admission wristbands, and handle the cashbox and livestream fees. Prosecutors charge all five with conspiracy to conduct the illegal chariot race. The defendants invoke Wharton’s Rule.

What is the best response?', 'What is the best response?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ19499.md","source_question_id":"19499","variant_slug":"jericho_chariot_night","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"71040200","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium-high","deciding_phase":"CLASH","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A two-person target offense has two required participants, but three extra people join the plan. Does Wharton’s Rule bar conspiracy?","review_truth":"Wharton’s Rule does not bar conspiracy when extra participants beyond the target offense’s minimum join the agreement.","dominant_trap":"D","anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-CRIMINAL-WHARTON-COUNT","title":"Wharton Count Check","signal":"Wharton’s Rule plus extra people in the agreement","student_move":"Count required participants first; count actual agreement participants second.","tiny_rule":"Extra participants defeat Wharton’s Rule.","trap":"Stopping at the two-party offense and missing the extra-party exception.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CRIMINAL-WHARTON-EXTRA-PARTY-01","statement":"Wharton’s Rule can bar conspiracy only when the target offense already requires the same minimum parties. If extra people beyond that minimum join the agreement, the conspiracy charge survives.","type":"exception","trigger":"two-person target offense plus organizers or other extra participants","authority":"Iannelli v. United States, 420 U.S. 770 (1975)","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CRIMINAL-WHARTON-COUNT-01","statement":"When Wharton’s Rule appears, count twice: how many people the target offense needs, then how many people joined the plan.","type":"clash","trigger":"a two-party offense with extra planners, sellers, organizers, or facilitators","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"anchor_recall","target_skill":"Wharton''s Rule extra-party exception","prompt":"Two required participants plus one unnecessary helper join the plan. Does Wharton''s Rule bar conspiracy?","answer":"No. Extra participants defeat the bar."},{"drill_type":"clash_axis","target_skill":"participant counting","prompt":"Name the axis: two-party offense, five-person plan.","answer":"Minimum required participants vs actual agreement participants."},{"drill_type":"overclaim_cut","target_skill":"tiered_absolute","prompt":"Wharton''s Rule always bars conspiracy whenever the target offense involves any agreement.","answer":"Cut as overclaim."},{"drill_type":"wrong_element","target_skill":"red-herring fact","prompt":"The organizers expected payment, so Wharton''s Rule bars conspiracy.","answer":"Wrong fact axis; payment is not the trigger."}]}'),
  ('395b28ab-4aa7-5fae-8a9d-35bb89de4779', '19502_manna_festival_finals_tax', 'CONSTITUTIONAL_LAW', 'Constitutional Law', 'Separation of Powers', 'taxing power revenue frame vs imported constitutional defect frames', 'After several costly nationwide flood-control projects, Congress enacted the Manna Festival Finals Revenue Act as part of a broader plan to retire federal disaster-recovery debt. For six years, the Act imposed a 40% excise tax on the price of paid admission wristbands to final-round artisan food competitions. Covered events included the Manna Bread Cup, the Loaves-and-Fishes Fish-Fry Crown, the Ruth Harvest Pie Finals, and similar paid championship food contests.', 'After several costly nationwide flood-control projects, Congress enacted the Manna Festival Finals Revenue Act as part of a broader plan to retire federal disaster-recovery debt. For six years, the Act imposed a 40% excise tax on the price of paid admission wristbands to final-round artisan food competitions. Covered events included the Manna Bread Cup, the Loaves-and-Fishes Fish-Fry Crown, the Ruth Harvest Pie Finals, and similar paid championship food contests.

This federal tax is probably', 'This federal tax is probably', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ19502.md","source_question_id":null,"variant_slug":"manna_festival_finals_tax","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"42010101","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"2","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"Congress imposes a high temporary federal excise tax on paid admission to one category of final-round events to raise revenue. Is the tax likely constitutional despite possible economic harm to the taxed events?","review_truth":"A facially revenue-raising federal excise tax is usually valid even if it hurts the taxed activity; do not jump to strict scrutiny or equal protection without a trigger.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-CONLAW-TAX-REVENUE-01","title":"Tax-power frame before scrutiny frame","signal":"Federal excise tax + revenue purpose + answer choices invoking strict scrutiny or equal protection.","student_move":"Ask whether the stem gives a protected trigger. If not, stay in the revenue-tax frame.","tiny_rule":"Bad economic effects do not by themselves invalidate a facial revenue tax.","trap":"Picking the dramatic constitutional standard instead of the tax-power answer.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONLAW-TAX-REVENUE-01","statement":"A federal excise tax that is facially revenue-raising is usually valid even if it burdens, discourages, or economically harms the taxed activity.","type":"rule","trigger":"federal excise tax + revenue/debt purpose + argument that the tax hurts the taxed activity","authority":"U.S. Const. art. I, §8; Sonzinsky v. United States; Fernandez v. Wiener","last_minute_review":true},{"id":"GK-CONLAW-TAX-CLASS-02","statement":"Ordinary economic and tax classifications do not get strict scrutiny unless the stem gives a suspect class, fundamental right, or other protected trigger.","type":"burden","trigger":"tax classification + compelling-interest or equal-protection answer with no protected trigger","authority":"Regan v. Taxation With Representation; FCC v. Beach Communications","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONLAW-TAX-ARRAY-01","statement":"When a Con Law tax array mixes compelling interest, rational relation, equal protection, and economic-impact language, first lock the question as taxing power before liking the strongest-sounding constitutional phrase.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"revenue tax stem with multiple constitutional standards in the answer set","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"strict-scrutiny trigger discipline","prompt":"A federal excise tax funds debt retirement. One answer says it is valid because of a compelling national interest. What is the first C3 cut?","answer":"Cut the strict-scrutiny frame unless the stem gives a protected trigger."},{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"economic-impact non sequitur","prompt":"A tax may reduce attendance at the taxed events. Does that alone defeat the tax''s revenue relationship?","answer":"No. Economic burden does not erase the revenue-tax frame."},{"drill_type":"ANCHOR","target_skill":"taxing-power Gold Key","prompt":"State the Gold Key for a facial revenue excise tax with adverse economic consequences.","answer":"A facial revenue tax is usually valid even if it burdens or discourages the taxed activity."},{"drill_type":"ANSWER_ARRAY","target_skill":"tax array navigation","prompt":"The array contains compelling interest, rational relation, equal protection, and adverse economic consequences. What frame should you lock first?","answer":"Taxing power."}]}'),
  ('662ba579-8e62-574b-9b19-cf3fb2dae6c2', '19554_road_to_damascus_cashbox', 'CRIMINAL_LAW', 'Property Crimes', 'Other Crimes', 'custody versus lawful possession versus title', 'Lydia owns a private Bible-themed escape room called Road to Damascus. For one evening, she assigns Peter to the entry table for the “shipwreck puzzle” room. Peter may take visitors’ $18 admission fees and drop the bills through the slot of Lydia’s wooden ark-shaped cash box. During his shift, Peter pulls $360 from the box and hides it in his costume satchel, intending to keep it.', 'Lydia owns a private Bible-themed escape room called Road to Damascus. For one evening, she assigns Peter to the entry table for the “shipwreck puzzle” room. Peter may take visitors’ $18 admission fees and drop the bills through the slot of Lydia’s wooden ark-shaped cash box. During his shift, Peter pulls $360 from the box and hides it in his costume satchel, intending to keep it.

What is the best common-law classification?', 'What is the best common-law classification?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ19554.md","source_question_id":"19554","variant_slug":"road_to_damascus_cashbox","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"73030100","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"high","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A worker may collect business payments and put them in the owner’s cash box; he pockets some intending to keep it. What common-law theft offense is it?","review_truth":"A low-level worker who only handles the owner’s cash has custody; pocketing it is larceny, not embezzlement.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"RC-CRIM-CUSTODY-POSSESSION-01","title":"Custody is not possession","signal":"Low-level worker handles owner cash.","student_move":"Ask whether the worker had custody only, lawful possession, title by deception, or no taking.","tiny_rule":"Custody + pocketing owner cash = larceny.","trap":"Worker theft automatically means embezzlement.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CRIM-CUSTODY-POSSESSION-01","statement":"A low-level worker who merely handles the owner’s cash usually has custody, not lawful possession. If the worker pockets it intending to keep it, the common-law classification is larceny.","type":"distinction","trigger":"Worker is authorized to collect or handle money for the owner, then secretly pockets it.","authority":"Lawyer-confirmation only: common-law larceny and embezzlement authorities.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CRIM-THEFT-CLASSIFICATION-01","statement":"On a theft-classification array, do not start with the job title. Sort the property path: custody, lawful possession, title, or no offense.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"The answer choices are competing common-law theft labels.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"overclaim","prompt":"Choice says all worker theft from a business is embezzlement. Name the mold.","answer":"tiered_absolute"},{"drill_type":"ANCHOR","target_skill":"custody versus possession","prompt":"A low-level worker collects owner cash and pockets it. Classify.","answer":"larceny"},{"drill_type":"ISSUE_SENSE","target_skill":"wrong element","prompt":"Choice says no theft because the worker could handle the money. What element is missing?","answer":"permission to keep or appropriate the money"}]}'),
  ('395da092-7f26-5e9c-8323-6d898d6e35a3', '19607_cana_hills_bethany_flats', 'CONSTITUTIONAL_LAW', 'Equal Protection', 'Rational basis — non-fundamental right / education', 'heightened-review trigger vs default rational basis', 'The State of Galilee funds its public schools with a statewide foundation grant plus whatever each local district raises through local property taxes. In Cana Hills, a lakeside district with expensive homes and commercial property, the public schools spend about $22,400 per student each year. In Bethany Flats, an old cannery district with a small tax base, the public schools spend about $7,300 per student each year. Mary, Peter, and Lydia, parents of children in Bethany Flats, bring an Equal Protection challenge. They argue that the funding plan denies their children an equal public education. The state responds that the plan preserves local control of schools and rationally connects local tax revenue to local school spending.', 'The State of Galilee funds its public schools with a statewide foundation grant plus whatever each local district raises through local property taxes. In Cana Hills, a lakeside district with expensive homes and commercial property, the public schools spend about $22,400 per student each year. In Bethany Flats, an old cannery district with a small tax base, the public schools spend about $7,300 per student each year. Mary, Peter, and Lydia, parents of children in Bethany Flats, bring an Equal Protection challenge. They argue that the funding plan denies their children an equal public education. The state responds that the plan preserves local control of schools and rationally connects local tax revenue to local school spending.

Under Equal Protection analysis, which standard of review applies, and how should the court likely rule?', 'Under Equal Protection analysis, which standard of review applies, and how should the court likely rule?', 3, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ19607.md","source_question_id":"19607","variant_slug":"cana_hills_bethany_flats","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"44040305","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium-hard anchor","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A state public-school funding plan creates large district spending gaps because local property-tax bases differ. Parents in the lower-funded district sue under Equal Protection. What review applies, and does the plan likely survive?","review_truth":"School-funding disparities tied to local property-tax wealth get rational basis review under federal Equal Protection unless a real suspect-class, quasi-suspect-class, or fundamental-right trigger is present.","dominant_trap":"B","anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-CONLAW-TIER-TRIGGER-01","title":"Tier trigger before tier label","signal":"Answer names strict or intermediate scrutiny.","student_move":"Ask what trigger the answer supplies.","tiny_rule":"No valid trigger means default rational basis.","trap":"Picking the strongest-sounding tier because the facts feel unfair.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONLAW-SCHOOL-FUNDING-01","statement":"Public education is not a federal fundamental right, and poverty/local district wealth is not a suspect class for school-funding disparities; rational basis applies.","type":"constitutional_principle","trigger":"public-school funding gap caused by local property-tax bases","authority":"San Antonio Independent School District v. Rodriguez, 411 U.S. 1 (1973)","last_minute_review":true},{"id":"GK-CONLAW-DISPARATE-IMPACT-01","statement":"A facially neutral policy’s racial disparate impact does not by itself trigger heightened Equal Protection scrutiny; look for discriminatory purpose or a racial classification.","type":"threshold","trigger":"neutral funding formula plus racial overlap language","authority":"Washington v. Davis, 426 U.S. 229 (1976)","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONLAW-SCHOOL-FUNDING-01","statement":"In a scrutiny-tier array, read each heightened-review answer by its trigger, not by how morally serious the inequality feels.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"Strict / intermediate / rational-basis answer spread","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"Gold Key recall","target_skill":"school-funding tier trigger","prompt":"A local-property-tax public-school funding plan creates richer and poorer districts. Is education a federal fundamental right for strict scrutiny?","answer":"No. Rational basis applies under the school-funding Gold Key."},{"drill_type":"Silver Key answer-array scan","target_skill":"tier trigger before tier label","prompt":"Three choices name strict or intermediate scrutiny and one names rational basis. What is the first move?","answer":"Check the trigger each heightened-review choice names."},{"drill_type":"Disparate-impact threshold","target_skill":"impact vs purpose","prompt":"A neutral public policy disproportionately affects racial-minority communities. Does impact alone trigger heightened Equal Protection scrutiny?","answer":"No. Look for discriminatory purpose or a racial classification."}]}'),
  ('f4e3156d-a251-589d-bf50-78621933b904', '19721_jonah_whale_float', 'CIVIL_PROCEDURE', 'Motions', 'Motions to dismiss', 'claim sufficiency vs amount-in-controversy / notice pleading distractions', 'Mary, a freelance scenic painter and citizen of State F, sued MannaJoy Props, Inc., a company incorporated in State G with its principal place of business in State H, in federal district court. Mary demanded $130,000 for false imprisonment. Her complaint stated that “Mary was shut inside the whale-belly compartment of a Jonah parade float from late cleanup until sunrise when a MannaJoy technician negligently latched the service hatch without realizing Mary was still painting inside.” Mary suffered no physical harm from being in the float. MannaJoy filed a motion to dismiss. How should the court rule?', 'Mary, a freelance scenic painter and citizen of State F, sued MannaJoy Props, Inc., a company incorporated in State G with its principal place of business in State H, in federal district court. Mary demanded $130,000 for false imprisonment. Her complaint stated that “Mary was shut inside the whale-belly compartment of a Jonah parade float from late cleanup until sunrise when a MannaJoy technician negligently latched the service hatch without realizing Mary was still painting inside.” Mary suffered no physical harm from being in the float. MannaJoy filed a motion to dismiss. How should the court rule?

How should the court rule?', 'How should the court rule?', 3, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ19721.md","source_question_id":"19721","variant_slug":"jonah_whale_float","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"92080104","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium-hard","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A federal complaint claims false imprisonment but pleads negligent confinement. On a motion to dismiss, should the court dismiss for failure to state a claim?","review_truth":"A motion-to-dismiss answer must match the defect in the complaint; negligent confinement does not state false imprisonment.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-CIV-12B6-INTENT-01","title":"Complaint label vs complaint facts","signal":"Claim label says an intentional tort, but the pleaded facts say negligence.","student_move":"Use the claim label and the pleaded fact together; do not stop at notice.","tiny_rule":"A complaint can defeat itself by pleading facts inconsistent with an element of the selected claim.","trap":"Notice pleading or amount-in-controversy distractors.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-JONAH-WHALE-FLOAT-01","statement":"False imprisonment requires intentional confinement. A complaint that pleads only negligent confinement defeats that claim at the motion-to-dismiss stage.","type":"distinction","trigger":"Complaint labels false imprisonment but says the confinement happened negligently.","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Torts §35; Fed. R. Civ. P. 12(b)(6); Ashcroft v. Iqbal.","last_minute_review":true},{"id":"GK-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-JONAH-WHALE-FLOAT-02","statement":"Amount in controversy is not a likely-verdict test. The pleaded amount controls unless it is legally certain the claim is below the jurisdictional threshold.","type":"threshold","trigger":"Answer choice talks about unlikely recovery above $75,000.","authority":"28 U.S.C. §1332; St. Paul Mercury Indem. Co. v. Red Cab Co.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-JONAH-WHALE-FLOAT-01","statement":"On a motion-to-dismiss array, match the reason to the motion. Same outcome plus wrong procedural reason is a trap.","type":"call_focus","trigger":"Two choices talk about amount in controversy while one choice talks about failure to state a claim.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"same outcome wrong reason","prompt":"A motion should be granted. One answer grants on the wrong jurisdictional reason; another grants on failure to state a claim. Which survives?","answer":"The answer with the correct motion ground survives."},{"drill_type":"Gold Key recall","target_skill":"false imprisonment intent","prompt":"Complaint says false imprisonment but pleads accidental or negligent confinement. Does it state false imprisonment?","answer":"No. False imprisonment requires intentional confinement."},{"drill_type":"Issue-Sense","target_skill":"notice pleading lure","prompt":"Complaint gives notice but pleads facts defeating one element. Is notice enough?","answer":"No. Notice does not cure facts that defeat the claim."},{"drill_type":"Anchor contrast","target_skill":"amount-in-controversy legal-certainty rule","prompt":"Is federal amount in controversy defeated merely because recovery over $75,000 is unlikely?","answer":"No. The likely recovery is not the standard."}]}'),
  ('b02ac29a-e5bf-5b67-8343-809e2e5a20c2', '19793_dove_candle_ledgers', 'CIVIL_PROCEDURE', 'Appeals and Finality', 'Collateral Order Narrowness', 'review now vs review after final judgment', 'Mary sued Peter in federal district court over a private contract for dove-shaped Christmas Eve candles sold through Peter’s Light of the World craft booth. During discovery, the judge ordered Peter to produce nonprivileged wax-batch logs, shipping receipts, and supplier invoices stored in a rented warehouse. Peter says paying workers to locate, box, and scan the papers will be expensive, so he seeks an immediate appeal under the collateral order doctrine.', 'Mary sued Peter in federal district court over a private contract for dove-shaped Christmas Eve candles sold through Peter’s Light of the World craft booth. During discovery, the judge ordered Peter to produce nonprivileged wax-batch logs, shipping receipts, and supplier invoices stored in a rented warehouse. Peter says paying workers to locate, box, and scan the papers will be expensive, so he seeks an immediate appeal under the collateral order doctrine.

Is the court of appeals likely to hear the appeal now?', 'Is the court of appeals likely to hear the appeal now?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ19793.md","source_question_id":"19793","variant_slug":"dove_candle_ledgers","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"93110101","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A party is ordered to produce ordinary nonprivileged documents in discovery and says compliance is expensive. Is that discovery order immediately appealable under the collateral order doctrine?","review_truth":"Ordinary discovery orders generally wait for final-judgment review; cost does not make them collateral orders.","dominant_trap":"A","anchor_card":{"card_id":"RC-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-COLLATERAL-ORDER-NARROWNESS-01","title":"Finality Gate: Ordinary Discovery Waits","signal":"Immediate appeal from a discovery order.","student_move":"Treat cost as bait; check whether later review is enough.","tiny_rule":"Collateral order is narrow; ordinary discovery orders usually wait.","trap":"Turning expense or separateness into immediate appeal.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-COLLATERAL-ORDER-NARROWNESS-01","statement":"Collateral-order review is narrow. Ordinary discovery orders generally wait for final judgment; expense alone is not effective unreviewability.","type":"threshold","trigger":"Immediate appeal from a discovery order under the collateral order doctrine.","authority":"28 U.S.C. §1291; Cohen; Mohawk; Cunningham.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-DOVE-CANDLE-LEDGERS-01","statement":"In an appealability array, sort answers as review-now or review-later. Cut the extreme words first, then ask whether final judgment review is enough.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"Immediate appeal + discovery order + collateral order doctrine.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"overclaim absolutes","prompt":"Classify: ''An expensive discovery order automatically qualifies as a collateral order.''","answer":"NOT_TRUE; tiered_absolute; cost is not automatic appealability."},{"drill_type":"anchor","target_skill":"collateral order narrowness","prompt":"A party must produce ordinary nonprivileged records and wants immediate appeal because production is costly. Appeal now?","answer":"No. Ordinary discovery waits for final judgment."},{"drill_type":"answer_array","target_skill":"review now vs review later","prompt":"Two choices say yes-now, two choices say no-now. What axis should you name?","answer":"Review now vs review after final judgment."},{"drill_type":"recovery","target_skill":"one-prong bait","prompt":"Why is ''separate from the merits'' not enough?","answer":"It is only one collateral-order requirement."}]}'),
  ('df8697a4-1b71-5eb3-a89b-77bb3ebb3166', '19840_red_sea_bridge_bolts', 'CIVIL_PROCEDURE', 'Discovery', 'Attorney-Client Privilege', 'fact itself vs communication about the fact', 'At Lydia''s privately owned Christian bookstore, Ruth was injured when a portable wooden "Red Sea crossing" bridge used for a paid Bible-story craft night shifted under her. Ruth sues Lydia in federal court for negligence. In an interrogatory, Ruth asks Lydia to identify who tightened the bridge''s bolts earlier that day. Lydia refuses, saying the answer is privileged because she gave that name to her lawyer during a confidential meeting seeking legal advice.', 'At Lydia''s privately owned Christian bookstore, Ruth was injured when a portable wooden "Red Sea crossing" bridge used for a paid Bible-story craft night shifted under her. Ruth sues Lydia in federal court for negligence. In an interrogatory, Ruth asks Lydia to identify who tightened the bridge''s bolts earlier that day. Lydia refuses, saying the answer is privileged because she gave that name to her lawyer during a confidential meeting seeking legal advice.

Is the identity of the person who tightened the bolts protected from discovery on that ground?', 'Is the identity of the person who tightened the bolts protected from discovery on that ground?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ19840.md","source_question_id":"19840","variant_slug":"red_sea_bridge_bolts","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"95070405","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"Can a party refuse to identify a relevant fact in an interrogatory just because the party told that fact to a lawyer while seeking legal advice?","review_truth":"Privilege can protect the lawyer conversation, but it does not hide the underlying fact.","dominant_trap":"C","anchor_card":{"card_id":"CIV-DISC-PRIV-01","title":"Fact, not chat","signal":"Discovery asks for a fact; privilege objection points to a lawyer conversation.","student_move":"Separate the underlying fact from the communication that reported it.","tiny_rule":"Facts do not become privileged by being told to counsel.","trap":"Every fact discussed with a lawyer becomes privileged.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-UNDERLYING-FACTS-01","statement":"Attorney-client privilege protects confidential legal-advice communications, not the underlying facts. A fact does not become privileged just because the client told it to a lawyer.","type":"distinction","trigger":"The discovery request asks for a fact, but the refusal says the fact was told to counsel.","authority":"Upjohn Co. v. United States, 449 U.S. 383 (1981); Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(b)(1); Fed. R. Civ. P. 33(a)(2).","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-FACT-NOT-CHAT-01","statement":"Before reading privilege choices, name the object of discovery. If the other side asks for the fact itself, do not let an answer switch you to the lawyer conversation.","type":"trap_spotting","trigger":"Interrogatory asks for an identity or event fact; objection says the fact was told to counsel.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"Overclaim detection","prompt":"A choice says every fact discussed with counsel becomes privileged. Name the mold.","answer":"tiered_absolute"},{"drill_type":"ISSUE_SENSE","target_skill":"Wrong-frame detection","prompt":"A privilege choice turns on whether the lawyer later repeated the fact in court, but the stem asks whether the fact was privileged in the first place. Cut or keep?","answer":"Cut as NOT_RESPONSIVE / bait_doctrine."},{"drill_type":"ANCHOR","target_skill":"Underlying facts rule","prompt":"A client tells a lawyer the name of a witness. Can the client refuse to disclose the witness''s name solely for that reason?","answer":"No. The fact is not privileged merely because it was communicated to counsel."},{"drill_type":"CALIBRATION","target_skill":"Residual after three named cuts","prompt":"You cut every-fact, never-civil, and wrong-frame waiver. What confidence band?","answer":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED high; commit after read-check."}]}'),
  ('72e5bbb0-ceb0-5ae2-b29d-0de3882c03a2', '19904_psalm_orchard_foreclosure_notice', 'REAL_PROPERTY', 'Mortgages', 'Foreclosure - parties and priorities', 'process threshold versus sale-scope merits', 'Ruth owned a private orchard cottage where she made Scripture memory cards for sale at weekend markets. To buy a new printing press, Ruth borrowed $74,000 from Barnabas and gave Barnabas a promissory note and a mortgage on the orchard parcel. Barnabas immediately and properly recorded the mortgage. Six weeks later, Ruth borrowed $41,000 from Lydia and gave Lydia a promissory note and a mortgage on the same parcel. Lydia immediately and properly recorded the mortgage. Ruth died soon afterward. Her will left the orchard parcel to Stephen for life, then to Mary. Stephen moved into the cottage. Neither Barnabas nor Lydia received the payments due on their notes. Without notifying or serving Stephen or Mary, Barnabas asks a court to order a foreclosure sale of the orchard parcel.', 'Ruth owned a private orchard cottage where she made Scripture memory cards for sale at weekend markets. To buy a new printing press, Ruth borrowed $74,000 from Barnabas and gave Barnabas a promissory note and a mortgage on the orchard parcel. Barnabas immediately and properly recorded the mortgage. Six weeks later, Ruth borrowed $41,000 from Lydia and gave Lydia a promissory note and a mortgage on the same parcel. Lydia immediately and properly recorded the mortgage. Ruth died soon afterward. Her will left the orchard parcel to Stephen for life, then to Mary. Stephen moved into the cottage. Neither Barnabas nor Lydia received the payments due on their notes. Without notifying or serving Stephen or Mary, Barnabas asks a court to order a foreclosure sale of the orchard parcel.

The court should:', 'The court should:', 3, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ19904.md","source_question_id":"19904","variant_slug":"psalm_orchard_foreclosure_notice","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"83050603","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium-hard","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A senior mortgagee seeks judicial foreclosure without notifying or serving the life tenant or remainderman. Should the court order a sale or deny the request?","review_truth":"Foreclosure sale waits for process: present and future interest holders must receive notice before their interests can be cut off.","dominant_trap":"D","anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-RP-FORECLOSURE-NOTICE-01","title":"Foreclosure: bring interest holders into court","signal":"Foreclosure request + life estate/remainder + no notice/service","student_move":"Cut every sale-scope answer until the service threshold is satisfied.","tiny_rule":"No notice to affected estate holders, no sale order.","trap":"Choosing the estate that can be sold instead of the process answer.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-REAL_PROPERTY-FORECLOSURE-NOTICE-01","statement":"In judicial foreclosure, holders of present or future property interests that may be cut off by the sale must receive notice and be made parties before the court orders the sale.","type":"threshold","trigger":"Foreclosure requested without notice or service on a life tenant or remainderman.","authority":"Mullane v. Central Hanover Bank & Trust Co.; Mennonite Board of Missions v. Adams.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-REAL_PROPERTY-FORECLOSURE-NOTICE-01","statement":"When a foreclosure stem says the lender did not notify or serve the estate holders, lock the call as process first and sale scope second.","type":"call_focus","trigger":"without notifying or serving","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"procedural_frame","prompt":"A foreclosure stem says the lender did not notify the life tenant or remainderman. Three choices order a sale. What is the first cut?","answer":"Cut the sale-scope choices as premature; choose the process answer."},{"drill_type":"CALL","target_skill":"threshold before merits","prompt":"Which comes first in foreclosure: identifying the estate to sell or serving the holders of affected estates?","answer":"Serving the affected estate holders."},{"drill_type":"anchor","target_skill":"foreclosure necessary parties","prompt":"Who must receive notice when a judicial foreclosure may cut off a life estate and remainder?","answer":"Both the life tenant and the remainderman."}]}'),
  ('623874e0-d6be-54d0-8302-65aded0e9261', '19982_manna_warehouse', 'CONSTITUTIONAL_LAW', 'Federal Powers', 'Spending conditions', NULL, 'After Lydia, a state emergency-services director, signed up for a federal Good Samaritan pantry-vans grant program, the federal relief agency announced a new requirement: every participating state must build refrigerated ''manna warehouses'' beside the vans. The statute authorizing the grant had said only that a state receiving the money must obey ''whatever later requirements the agency finds fitting.'' The warehouse requirement was not listed in the statute when the state took the money.', 'After Lydia, a state emergency-services director, signed up for a federal Good Samaritan pantry-vans grant program, the federal relief agency announced a new requirement: every participating state must build refrigerated ''manna warehouses'' beside the vans. The statute authorizing the grant had said only that a state receiving the money must obey ''whatever later requirements the agency finds fitting.'' The warehouse requirement was not listed in the statute when the state took the money.

What is the state''s strongest Spending Clause objection?', 'What is the state''s strongest Spending Clause objection?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ19982.md","source_question_id":"19982","variant_slug":"manna_warehouse","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"42010101","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium_anchor","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"PURE_ANCHOR","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A state accepts federal grant money under a statute reserving unspecified future agency conditions, and the agency later adds a costly condition. What is the Spending Clause objection?","review_truth":"Spending Clause grants need clear terms before the state accepts; hidden future conditions are the trap.","dominant_trap":"B","anchor_card":{"card_id":"REMED-CONLAW-SPENDING-CLEAR-NOTICE","title":"Hidden grant terms","signal":"Federal money plus states plus later undisclosed condition.","student_move":"Ask whether the state could know the condition before accepting.","tiny_rule":"No clear notice, no knowing acceptance.","trap":"Treating federal spending as a blank check.","confidence":"PURE_ANCHOR"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONLAW-SPENDING-CLEAR-NOTICE-01","statement":"Spending Clause conditions must be stated clearly enough that a state can knowingly decide whether to accept the money and the obligations. A blank-check future-condition clause is the red flag.","type":"constitutional_principle","trigger":"state accepts federal money before the concrete condition appears","authority":"Pennhurst State School & Hospital v. Halderman; South Dakota v. Dole; Arlington Central School District Board of Education v. Murphy.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONLAW-MANNA-WAREHOUSE-01","statement":"When the call asks for the state’s Spending Clause objection, hunt for the answer that attacks the condition’s clarity at the time of acceptance.","type":"trap_spotting","trigger":"state took grant money before the costly condition was listed","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"anchor_recall","target_skill":"Spending Clause clear notice","prompt":"A state accepts grant money before a concrete condition is stated. What objection should fire?","answer":"No clear notice for knowing acceptance."},{"drill_type":"ear_falsity","target_skill":"backwards rule","prompt":"Classify: Congress may add any undisclosed condition after a state accepts federal funds.","answer":"NOT_TRUE; backwards."},{"drill_type":"trap_spotting","target_skill":"call lock","prompt":"The call asks for a Spending Clause objection. Which facts matter most?","answer":"State acceptance, unclear condition, later costly mandate."}]}'),
  ('5944fabb-fcf2-50fe-8db9-2f05e986ff7d', '20004_dove_tokens_manna_game', 'CONTRACTS', 'Gap-Fillers, Interpretation, and the Parol Evidence Rule', 'IV', NULL, 'Stephen sued Ruth for the profit he expected from 72,000 wooden dove tokens. The written twelve-month deal had said that Ruth''s private Christian board-game company would buy from Stephen''s woodworking shop ''all wooden dove tokens required for the Manna Trail game line'' and listed 72,000 tokens as an estimate. Five months into the deal, a new child-safety rule made that small-token board-game line unprofitable. Ruth permanently canceled the line and bought no wooden dove tokens from any other source for it.', 'Stephen sued Ruth for the profit he expected from 72,000 wooden dove tokens. The written twelve-month deal had said that Ruth''s private Christian board-game company would buy from Stephen''s woodworking shop ''all wooden dove tokens required for the Manna Trail game line'' and listed 72,000 tokens as an estimate. Five months into the deal, a new child-safety rule made that small-token board-game line unprofitable. Ruth permanently canceled the line and bought no wooden dove tokens from any other source for it.

Which statement best states the parties'' rights?', 'Which statement best states the parties'' rights?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20004.md","source_question_id":"20004","variant_slug":"dove_tokens_manna_game","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"55020203","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"trap repair","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A buyer in a requirements contract estimated a quantity, then in good faith shut down the covered product line and bought none elsewhere. Does the estimate force a minimum purchase?","review_truth":"A requirements-contract estimate is not a purchase floor; good-faith zero requirements can be zero.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"CON-REQ-01","title":"Requirements estimate is not a floor","signal":"all requirements + estimate + shutdown","student_move":"Stay in the quantity/good-faith lane before considering excuse or writing rules.","tiny_rule":"Estimate guides policing; it is not automatically a minimum.","trap":"Treating the estimate as the quantity promised.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONTRACTS-REQUIREMENTS-SHUTDOWN-01","statement":"In a requirements contract, a stated estimate is not a fixed minimum. Actual good-faith requirements can be zero if the buyer truly shuts down the covered operation and does not shift the same requirements elsewhere.","type":"rule","trigger":"all requirements + stated estimate + covered line shuts down + no substitute purchases","authority":"UCC 2-306(1); official-comment material on good-faith discontinuance.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONTRACTS-REQUIREMENTS-LANE-01","statement":"When the stem says ''all requirements'' plus an estimate, first ask whether the buyer still had covered requirements. Cut answers that jump to fixed quantity, excuse, or parol-evidence frames.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"requirements term with estimate and later shutdown","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"gold_key_recall","target_skill":"requirements-contract estimate","prompt":"A buyer promises to buy all requirements for one product line, estimates 40,000 units, then shuts that line down in good faith and buys none elsewhere. Is 40,000 a minimum?","answer":"No. The estimate is not automatically a floor; actual good-faith requirements may be zero."},{"drill_type":"wrong_frame_cut","target_skill":"excuse-doctrine drift","prompt":"The answer says the buyer wins only if it proves frustration. The stem asks about actual requirements after a covered-line shutdown. What is the C3 label?","answer":"NOT_RESPONSIVE / bait_doctrine / wrong_frame."},{"drill_type":"falsity_direction","target_skill":"parol evidence timing","prompt":"A later shutdown fact is offered to measure actual requirements. Does the parol evidence rule bar it as varying the estimate?","answer":"No. That is a backwards timing use of the parol-evidence rule."}]}'),
  ('98908f9c-4937-5dfd-8db1-50868c24d0aa', '20024_loaves_fishes_crate_count', 'TORTS', 'Negligence damages', 'Negligent misrepresentation and economic loss', 'open-ended foreseeability vs known/limited class for a specific transaction', 'At a for-profit Bible-camp supply expo, Martha sold off the remaining stock from her “Loaves & Fishes” craft-kit shop. Weeks earlier, Lydia, a professional inventory consultant, had prepared a crate-count sheet solely so Martha could plan warehouse space and packing for the expo. The sheet mistakenly listed 900 sealed craft kits instead of 90. Lydia was not told that the sheet would be shown to buyers or used in any sale. Martha later slipped a copy of the sheet into a sale packet on her booth table. Timothy, who had never dealt with Lydia, used the sheet to buy the remaining kits for his own resale booth and lost money when the count proved wrong.', 'At a for-profit Bible-camp supply expo, Martha sold off the remaining stock from her “Loaves & Fishes” craft-kit shop. Weeks earlier, Lydia, a professional inventory consultant, had prepared a crate-count sheet solely so Martha could plan warehouse space and packing for the expo. The sheet mistakenly listed 900 sealed craft kits instead of 90. Lydia was not told that the sheet would be shown to buyers or used in any sale. Martha later slipped a copy of the sheet into a sale packet on her booth table. Timothy, who had never dealt with Lydia, used the sheet to buy the remaining kits for his own resale booth and lost money when the count proved wrong.

In Timothy''s negligent-misrepresentation claim against Lydia, how should the court rule?', 'In Timothy''s negligent-misrepresentation claim against Lydia, how should the court rule?', 3, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20024.md","source_question_id":null,"variant_slug":"loaves_fishes_crate_count","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"62050200","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"hard","deciding_phase":"CLASH","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":"The count sheet was made only for Martha''s internal planning, and Lydia was not told it would be used for buyers or any sale.","distilled_core_question":"A professional prepared an inaccurate count sheet only for a client’s internal planning. An unknown later buyer relied on a copy in a sale packet and lost money. Is the professional liable to that buyer for negligent misrepresentation?","review_truth":"For negligent misrepresentation causing economic loss, do not stop at foreseeable reader; ask whether the plaintiff was in the known or limited group for the transaction the information was meant to guide.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-TORTS-NMISREP-01","title":"Foreseeability is not the whole duty gate.","signal":"Professional information + economic loss + unknown later reader.","student_move":"Ask: targeted person/class and targeted transaction?","tiny_rule":"Foreseeability alone loses to targeted-use duty.","trap":"General negligence reflex.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-TORTS-LIMITED-CLASS-01","statement":"For negligent misrepresentation by a professional information supplier, economic-loss duty is limited to a known person or limited group for a specific transaction or substantially similar transaction. It does not run to every foreseeable later reader.","type":"threshold","trigger":"professional report, economic loss, unknown third-party reader","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 552; Bily v. Arthur Young & Co.; Ultramares Corp. v. Touche.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-TORTS-TARGETED-USE-01","statement":"When one finalist says ''foreseeable reader'' and another says ''known or limited class for a transaction,'' name the axis as targeted-use duty, not general foreseeability.","type":"clash","trigger":"unknown later reader relying on a client-republished report","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"overclaim","prompt":"No, because pure economic loss is never recoverable for negligent misrepresentation.","answer":"CUT: tiered_absolute / NOT_TRUE."},{"drill_type":"CLASH","target_skill":"targeted-use axis","prompt":"Foreseeable later reader vs known/limited class for a transaction. Which fact decides?","answer":"Whether the professional supplied the information for that person/class and transaction."},{"drill_type":"ANCHOR","target_skill":"Gold Key recall","prompt":"Professional report, economic loss, unknown third-party reader. What tiny rule?","answer":"Duty is limited to a known or limited group for a specific transaction; foreseeability alone is not enough."}]}'),
  ('c935f630-8cfc-57f8-9db0-c24b10a258fa', '20100_fish_and_loaves_keychains', 'CONTRACTS', 'Statute of Frauds', 'D', 'alleged quantity versus admitted quantity', 'Barnabas operates a private online shop that sells glow-in-the-dark ''fish and loaves'' keychains to Christian retreat organizers. He sues Lydia, alleging that she orally agreed to buy 2,400 keychains for a Bible-quiz tournament at a total price over $500. No signed writing exists. At trial, Lydia testifies, ''I did agree to buy keychains, but 750 of them, not 2,400.'' Barnabas asks the court to enforce the alleged deal for all 2,400 keychains.', 'Barnabas operates a private online shop that sells glow-in-the-dark ''fish and loaves'' keychains to Christian retreat organizers. He sues Lydia, alleging that she orally agreed to buy 2,400 keychains for a Bible-quiz tournament at a total price over $500. No signed writing exists. At trial, Lydia testifies, ''I did agree to buy keychains, but 750 of them, not 2,400.'' Barnabas asks the court to enforce the alleged deal for all 2,400 keychains.

What is the strongest Statute of Frauds result?', 'What is the strongest Statute of Frauds result?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20100.md","source_question_id":"20100","variant_slug":"fish_and_loaves_keychains","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"54030400","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"discriminator","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"No signed writing. Buyer admits in court to buying 750 goods, not the seller''s alleged 2,400. How much is enforceable under the UCC Statute of Frauds admission exception?","review_truth":"Under UCC 2-201(3)(b), an in-court admission can satisfy the Statute of Frauds only up to the quantity admitted.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-CONTRACTS-SOF-ADMISSION-QUANTITY-CAP","title":"Admission exception quantity cap","signal":"No writing plus in-court admission of a smaller quantity.","student_move":"Lock the admitted quantity before evaluating all-or-none answers.","tiny_rule":"Admission opens enforcement only up to the quantity admitted.","trap":"Some admitted contract means all alleged goods are enforceable.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONTRACTS-FISH-KEYCHAINS-01","statement":"For a UCC sale-of-goods oral contract, an in-court admission can satisfy the Statute of Frauds, but only for the quantity the party admits.","type":"exception","trigger":"No signed writing plus testimony admitting a smaller quantity than the seller alleges.","authority":"UCC 2-201(3)(b)","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONTRACTS-FISH-KEYCHAINS-01","statement":"When the answers fight over all, none, and only the admitted amount, circle the exact admitted number first; the right answer usually sits on that number.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"A party admits one quantity but denies the larger alleged quantity.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"number_lock","target_skill":"Gold Key recall","prompt":"No writing. Buyer admits 300 units but denies 900. How many units are enforceable under the UCC admission exception?","answer":"300"},{"drill_type":"mold_identification","target_skill":"extreme_of_range","prompt":"Buyer admits 300 but seller seeks 900. Choice says all 900 because buyer admitted a contract existed. Name the mold.","answer":"extreme_of_range"},{"drill_type":"answer_array","target_skill":"all/none/admitted-number scan","prompt":"Choices say all, none, market reasonable, or admitted amount. What number do you lock first?","answer":"The quantity admitted in court."}]}'),
  ('b5c7682e-9942-5089-b41f-e2efb3777e56', '20123_prayer_card_threat_writers', 'TORTS', 'Privacy', 'False light and constitutional limits', NULL, 'A Christian streaming channel released a public-concern episode about anonymous threat letters sent to several disaster-relief centers. In the episode’s opening graphic, the channel showed a close-up of a handwritten prayer card signed by Hannah under the words, “Threat writers from the mercy team.” Before release, the producers had received the graphic designer’s note stating that Hannah’s card was only a thank-you note from a volunteer lunch and that Hannah had not written any threat letter. The producers used the graphic anyway. Hannah sues for false light.', 'A Christian streaming channel released a public-concern episode about anonymous threat letters sent to several disaster-relief centers. In the episode’s opening graphic, the channel showed a close-up of a handwritten prayer card signed by Hannah under the words, “Threat writers from the mercy team.” Before release, the producers had received the graphic designer’s note stating that Hannah’s card was only a thank-you note from a volunteer lunch and that Hannah had not written any threat letter. The producers used the graphic anyway. Hannah sues for false light.

What mental-state showing most directly supports the claim?', 'What mental-state showing most directly supports the claim?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20123.md","source_question_id":"20123","variant_slug":"prayer_card_threat_writers","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"62050402","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium-high","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A public-concern false-light publication falsely implies a volunteer wrote threats, and the publisher had a note contradicting that implication before publication. What mental-state showing supports the claim?","review_truth":"Public-concern false light points to actual malice when the publisher had facts showing the implication was false and published anyway.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-TORTS-FALSE-LIGHT-FAULT-01","title":"False-light public concern: fault before feelings","signal":"False-light claim plus public-concern publication.","student_move":"Lock the call. If it asks mental state, find the publisher''s knowledge or serious doubt.","tiny_rule":"Actual malice is knowledge of falsity or reckless disregard.","trap":"Mistaking embarrassment, special damages, or misleading graphics for the required mental-state showing.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-TORTS-PUBLIC-FALSE-LIGHT-01","statement":"Public-concern false light points to actual malice: the publisher knew the false implication was false or recklessly disregarded that risk. Strict liability is the trap.","type":"constitutional_principle","trigger":"false-light claim + public-concern publication + pre-publication contradiction","authority":"Time, Inc. v. Hill, 385 U.S. 374 (1967); New York Times Co. v. Sullivan, 376 U.S. 254 (1964)","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-TORTS-PRAYER-CARD-01","statement":"On a mental-state call, cut choices that describe harm, remedy, tort category, or no-fault liability before judging doctrine.","type":"call_focus","trigger":"call asks ''what mental-state showing''","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"mental-state call focus","prompt":"A false-light call asks for mental state. Choice says the plaintiff was humiliated. Cut or keep?","answer":"Cut: harm, not mental state."},{"drill_type":"ANCHOR","target_skill":"public-concern false-light fault","prompt":"Public-concern false light; publisher had contrary note and published anyway. What fault phrase?","answer":"Actual malice."},{"drill_type":"TRAP_SPOTTING","target_skill":"strict liability shortcut","prompt":"Choice says strict liability because the graphic misled readers. What trap?","answer":"No-fault shortcut; public-concern false light needs constitutional fault."}]}'),
  ('7aca671e-e9cb-543b-a50f-66dd5eb7822e', '20127_jericho_trumpet', 'CRIMINAL_LAW', 'Fifth Amendment', 'Constitutional Protection of Accused Persons', 'violation layer vs remedy layer; statement evidence vs physical evidence', 'At Paul''s theft trial, the prosecutor plans to introduce a gilded ram''s-horn trumpet prop recovered from a private rehearsal studio. The recovery traced back to police questioning: after Lydia reported that the trumpet had been taken from her Bible-themed escape-room booth, officers arrested Paul and asked him about the item before giving Miranda warnings. Paul voluntarily said, "It is inside the hollow lectern we use for the Esther skit at my rehearsal studio." Using that statement, officers obtained a valid search warrant for the studio and found the trumpet. The prosecution does not offer Paul''s statement, only the trumpet.', 'At Paul''s theft trial, the prosecutor plans to introduce a gilded ram''s-horn trumpet prop recovered from a private rehearsal studio. The recovery traced back to police questioning: after Lydia reported that the trumpet had been taken from her Bible-themed escape-room booth, officers arrested Paul and asked him about the item before giving Miranda warnings. Paul voluntarily said, "It is inside the hollow lectern we use for the Esther skit at my rehearsal studio." Using that statement, officers obtained a valid search warrant for the studio and found the trumpet. The prosecution does not offer Paul''s statement, only the trumpet.

Should the trumpet be suppressed as fruit of the Miranda violation?', 'Should the trumpet be suppressed as fruit of the Miranda violation?', 3, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20127.md","source_question_id":"20127","variant_slug":"jericho_trumpet","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"75080101","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"hard","deciding_phase":"CALL","confidence":"PURE_ANCHOR","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"Police use a voluntary unwarned statement to find physical evidence and offer only the physical evidence at trial. Suppress?","review_truth":"Miranda can suppress the voluntary unwarned statement, but not the physical object found because of it.","dominant_trap":"B","anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-CRIM-MIRANDA-FRUIT-01","title":"Miranda Fruit Is Not Fourth-Amendment Fruit","signal":"Unwarned but voluntary statement leads police to a physical object.","student_move":"Pin the offered evidence: statement or object.","tiny_rule":"Statement out; voluntary-statement physical fruit generally in.","trap":"Importing all-fruits suppression from Fourth Amendment doctrine.","confidence":"PURE_ANCHOR"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CRIM-MIRANDA-FRUIT-01","statement":"A voluntary unwarned statement is generally kept out of the prosecution''s case-in-chief, but physical evidence discovered from that statement need not be suppressed.","type":"exception","trigger":"The prosecution offers a physical object found through a voluntary unwarned statement.","authority":"United States v. Patane, 542 U.S. 630 (2004)","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CRIM-MIRANDA-REMEDY-LAYER-01","statement":"Separate the warning violation from the suppression remedy: first ask what evidence the prosecution is offering.","type":"call_focus","trigger":"The call says ''suppressed as fruit'' and the prosecution offers only a physical object.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"anchor_recall","target_skill":"Miranda physical-fruit distinction","prompt":"Police get a voluntary unwarned statement naming a weapon location and offer only the weapon. Suppress?","answer":"No, not solely on Miranda grounds."},{"drill_type":"bait_architecture","target_skill":"violation_vs_remedy","prompt":"The call asks whether evidence is suppressed after a violation. What must you identify first?","answer":"The evidence being offered and the remedy rule."},{"drill_type":"overclaim_cut","target_skill":"tiered_absolute","prompt":"Classify: all evidence derived from an unwarned statement is suppressed.","answer":"NOT_TRUE; tiered_absolute."},{"drill_type":"backwards_cut","target_skill":"voluntary does not erase Miranda","prompt":"Classify: voluntary means there was no Miranda violation.","answer":"NOT_TRUE; backwards."}]}'),
  ('8a1a1119-9877-5077-9322-c649dd57a4d3', '20153_lampstand_labs', 'CONSTITUTIONAL_LAW', 'Individual Rights', 'First Amendment Protections - Freedom of religion', 'automatic invalidity of aid to religious schools vs neutral secular-use aid', 'Congress enacts the Lampstand Labs Act, which authorizes federal tax funds to buy robotics kits, digital microscopes, and portable planetarium projectors for qualifying private schools. The kits are available on identical terms to private schools with religious missions and to secular private schools. The Act states that every donated kit must be used only for secular instruction and secular academic activities. Mary and Peter, federal taxpayers whose children attend public schools in the state, sue in federal court for an order enjoining the federal government from spending tax funds on kits that will go to Christian and other religious private schools. They argue that the spending is unconstitutional. Should the court issue the requested injunction?', 'Congress enacts the Lampstand Labs Act, which authorizes federal tax funds to buy robotics kits, digital microscopes, and portable planetarium projectors for qualifying private schools. The kits are available on identical terms to private schools with religious missions and to secular private schools. The Act states that every donated kit must be used only for secular instruction and secular academic activities. Mary and Peter, federal taxpayers whose children attend public schools in the state, sue in federal court for an order enjoining the federal government from spending tax funds on kits that will go to Christian and other religious private schools. They argue that the spending is unconstitutional. Should the court issue the requested injunction?

Should the court issue the requested injunction?', 'Should the court issue the requested injunction?', 3, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20153.md","source_question_id":"20153","variant_slug":"lampstand_labs","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"44040501","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium-hard anchor item","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"Congress funds secular-use educational equipment for religious and nonreligious private schools on equal terms. Federal taxpayer-parents seek an injunction. Should the court stop the spending?","review_truth":"Neutral, secular-use educational aid available to religious and secular schools on equal terms is not automatically an Establishment Clause violation.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-CONLAW-LAMPSTAND-01","title":"Neutral aid / secular-use restriction","signal":"Religious and secular schools get the same educational aid, and the statute limits use to secular instruction.","student_move":"Do not make religious-school aid per se unconstitutional. Look for neutrality and use restriction.","tiny_rule":"Neutral + secular-use school aid usually defeats the per se Establishment Clause trap.","trap":"Public money plus religious school feels automatically invalid.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONLAW-LAMPSTAND-01","statement":"Neutral educational aid available to religious and secular private schools on equal terms, restricted to secular use, is not automatically an Establishment Clause violation.","type":"constitutional_principle","trigger":"Public funds buy school equipment for religious and nonreligious schools on identical terms, with secular-use limits.","authority":"Mitchell v. Helms, 530 U.S. 793 (2000)","last_minute_review":true},{"id":"GK-CONLAW-LAMPSTAND-02","statement":"Federal taxpayers can have standing to challenge congressional taxing-and-spending measures alleged to violate the Establishment Clause.","type":"threshold","trigger":"Federal taxpayers sue to stop congressional spending on Establishment Clause grounds.","authority":"Flast v. Cohen, 392 U.S. 83 (1968)","last_minute_review":true},{"id":"GK-CONLAW-LAMPSTAND-03","statement":"Congress is not categorically barred from subsidizing education; the taxing-and-spending power reaches general-welfare education spending.","type":"constitutional_principle","trigger":"Answer says education is solely for states.","authority":"U.S. Const. art. I, sec. 8, cl. 1","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONLAW-LAMPSTAND-01","statement":"In a 2-yes / 2-no constitutional array, do not pick by result. Grade the reason after because; same result plus false reason still dies.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"Two choices say No but only one uses the statute''s secular-use fact.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"Gold Key recall","target_skill":"neutral aid","prompt":"A federal program gives secular-use science equipment to religious and secular private schools on equal terms. Automatic Establishment Clause violation?","answer":"No. Neutral + secular-use aid is not automatically invalid."},{"drill_type":"answer-array read","target_skill":"same-result wrong-reason","prompt":"Two choices say No. One says no standing; one says secular-use restriction. What do you check?","answer":"Grade the because; same result is not enough."},{"drill_type":"overclaim detector","target_skill":"states-only language","prompt":"Answer says education is solely a state function. What is the C3 signal?","answer":"Solely is the overclaim signal; check spending-power anchor."},{"drill_type":"threshold trap","target_skill":"Flast standing","prompt":"Federal taxpayers challenge congressional spending as violating the Establishment Clause. Standing absent?","answer":"No. Flast supplies the taxpayer-standing anchor."}]}'),
  ('1d311504-36ec-57f8-933a-a96fe30b646c', '20186_road_to_emmaus_royalty_ledgers', 'CONSTITUTIONAL_LAW', 'Separation of Powers', 'Congressional subpoenas', 'automatic outcome vs calibrated review', 'Before taking office, President Daniel licensed a scripture-themed family board game, Road to Emmaus, and a devotional planner to Lydia''s Lamp Games LLC. A House committee, saying it is studying legislation requiring federal officials to disclose outside royalty streams and business entanglements, subpoenas the company for twelve years of Daniel''s licensing agreements, royalty statements, vendor invoices, and payment ledgers. The subpoena is sweeping. Daniel objects that the committee mainly wants to parade private details from a Christian-themed side project and that the search would burden the presidency.', 'Before taking office, President Daniel licensed a scripture-themed family board game, Road to Emmaus, and a devotional planner to Lydia''s Lamp Games LLC. A House committee, saying it is studying legislation requiring federal officials to disclose outside royalty streams and business entanglements, subpoenas the company for twelve years of Daniel''s licensing agreements, royalty statements, vendor invoices, and payment ledgers. The subpoena is sweeping. Daniel objects that the committee mainly wants to parade private details from a Christian-themed side project and that the search would burden the presidency.

What is the best description of the court''s task?', 'What is the best description of the court''s task?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20186.md","source_question_id":"20186","variant_slug":"road_to_emmaus_royalty_ledgers","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"42010105","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"high","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A House committee subpoenas a third party for broad personal records of the President while claiming a legislative-disclosure purpose. What should a court do?","review_truth":"Presidential personal records are neither automatically immune nor automatically obtainable; courts check valid legislative purpose, tailoring, and separation-of-powers burdens.","dominant_trap":"C","anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-CONLAW-20186-MAZARS-01","title":"Presidential subpoena = calibrated review","signal":"Congress seeks President''s personal information from a third party.","student_move":"Cut automatic extremes; then apply the Gold Key.","tiny_rule":"No automatic enforcement, no automatic immunity; court checks purpose, tailoring, and burdens.","trap":"Choosing the branch slogan instead of the court''s task.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONSTITUTIONAL_LAW-MAZARS-PRESIDENTIAL-01","statement":"When Congress subpoenas a President''s personal information, there is no automatic enforcement and no automatic immunity. Courts check valid legislative purpose, need, reasonable tailoring, and burdens on the presidency.","type":"constitutional_principle","trigger":"congressional subpoena for President''s personal records held by a third party","authority":"Trump v. Mazars USA, LLP, 591 U.S. ___ (2020)","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONSTITUTIONAL_LAW-AUTOMATIC-EXTREMES-01","statement":"When three choices give automatic outcomes and one describes a judicial evaluation, lock the call before choosing. The court-task call usually wants the calibrated evaluation, not a branch-power slogan.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"automatically / never / unlimited / only if","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"overclaim","prompt":"Congressional subpoena answer says the court must enforce automatically because Congress has unlimited investigative power. Name the mold.","answer":"tiered_absolute / NOT_TRUE"},{"drill_type":"CALL","target_skill":"answer_array","prompt":"Three answers give automatic outcomes; one answer gives a tailored judicial evaluation. What is the navigation move?","answer":"Use the answer-array Silver Key and prefer the calibrated court-task answer."},{"drill_type":"ANCHOR","target_skill":"Mazars trigger","prompt":"What Gold Key triggers when Congress subpoenas a President''s personal records from a third party?","answer":"No automatic enforcement and no automatic immunity; court checks purpose, need, tailoring, and burden."}]}'),
  ('2a55da74-d9d9-5573-8a8a-b267decd7049', '20213_mercy_pool_reliction', 'REAL_PROPERTY', 'Estates and conveyancing', 'O-4', 'adjoining owner vs public/far-side/no-private owner', 'Ruth owns a private olive-grove retreat along the east edge of Mercy Pool, a spring-fed oasis lake where her family holds Sunday hymn picnics. Barnabas owns land on the far west edge. Over 11 years, Mercy Pool’s waterline naturally crept back from Ruth’s side, little by little, leaving a dry crescent of ground between Ruth’s old waterline and the current waterline. The change was natural and gradual.', 'Ruth owns a private olive-grove retreat along the east edge of Mercy Pool, a spring-fed oasis lake where her family holds Sunday hymn picnics. Barnabas owns land on the far west edge. Over 11 years, Mercy Pool’s waterline naturally crept back from Ruth’s side, little by little, leaving a dry crescent of ground between Ruth’s old waterline and the current waterline. The change was natural and gradual.

Under the usual rule, who owns the newly exposed crescent of land?', 'Under the usual rule, who owns the newly exposed crescent of land?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20213.md","source_question_id":"20213","variant_slug":"mercy_pool_reliction","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"82010000","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"core diagnostic","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"Water gradually and naturally recedes from the edge of a private littoral parcel, exposing attached dry land. Who owns the exposed land?","review_truth":"Gradual natural reliction moves the water boundary; the adjoining littoral owner gets the exposed land.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"RP-ANCHOR-RELICTION-01","title":"Reliction: gradual water retreat adds land to the adjoining parcel","signal":"Water naturally and gradually recedes, exposing dry land.","student_move":"Ask which parcel touches the exposed strip.","tiny_rule":"Gradual natural reliction generally gives exposed land to the adjoining littoral owner.","trap":"Public-forever or far-side-owner answers.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-REAL_PROPERTY-RELICTION-01","statement":"Gradual natural recession of water generally moves the water boundary; the exposed land belongs to the adjoining riparian or littoral owner.","type":"rule","trigger":"Natural, gradual recession exposing dry land attached to a water-adjacent parcel.","authority":"Cornell Wex reliction; Nebraska v. Iowa gradual-change boundary principle.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-REAL_PROPERTY-RELICTION-01","statement":"For water-boundary ownership arrays, lock two facts before reading policy-sounding answers: gradual or sudden, and which parcel the exposed land touches.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"Four choices name different owners of newly exposed waterfront land.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"anchor_recall","target_skill":"reliction owner-axis","prompt":"Water naturally and gradually recedes from a private lakefront parcel and exposes land attached to that parcel. Who owns the exposed land?","answer":"The adjoining littoral owner."},{"drill_type":"overclaim_cut","target_skill":"public-forever absolute","prompt":"Cut or keep: all land once covered by a lake remains public forever.","answer":"Cut: tiered_absolute overclaim."},{"drill_type":"answer_array","target_skill":"adjacency vs direction","prompt":"Two owners are on opposite sides of a lake. Water recedes from Owner 1’s side and exposes a strip attached to Owner 1’s land. Does the far-side owner get it because the water moved that direction?","answer":"No. The adjoining owner gets it under the reliction anchor."}]}'),
  ('aed2312b-e91f-52de-bc2b-a23d67832426', '20231_crown_above_caesars', 'CONSTITUTIONAL_LAW', 'First Amendment Religion', 'Free Exercise Clause', 'belief-as-such punishment vs conduct regulation', 'During a private Bible-study art class in a rented storefront, Esther teaches a creed she calls "Crown Above Caesars": that Christ''s authority is higher than any earthly government. A state statute makes it a misdemeanor to hold or teach that creed. After charging Esther, the state argues that because it may regulate unlawful acts committed by people who invoke the creed, it may also forbid the creed itself.', 'During a private Bible-study art class in a rented storefront, Esther teaches a creed she calls "Crown Above Caesars": that Christ''s authority is higher than any earthly government. A state statute makes it a misdemeanor to hold or teach that creed. After charging Esther, the state argues that because it may regulate unlawful acts committed by people who invoke the creed, it may also forbid the creed itself.

What is the best constitutional response?', 'What is the best constitutional response?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20231.md","source_question_id":"20231","variant_slug":"crown_above_caesars","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"44040501","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"moderate-anchor","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"Can a state criminalize holding or teaching a religious belief because conduct inspired by that belief can be regulated?","review_truth":"Free Exercise protects religious belief as such; conduct is the separate regulatory lane.","dominant_trap":"B","anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-CONLAW-BELIEF-CONDUCT-01","title":"Belief lane vs conduct lane","signal":"A religion question says the state punishes holding, teaching, professing, or affirming a belief.","student_move":"Cut answers that switch to conduct, churches only, or a different Religion Clause.","tiny_rule":"Belief as such is protected; conduct is separate.","trap":"The state justifies a belief ban by pointing to regulable conduct.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONLAW-BELIEF-CONDUCT-01","statement":"Free Exercise protects religious belief and profession as such. Conduct inspired by belief is a separate lane and may be regulated under the applicable constitutional standard.","type":"distinction","trigger":"A statute punishes holding, teaching, professing, or affirming a religious belief.","authority":"Reynolds v. United States; Cantwell v. Connecticut; Employment Division v. Smith.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONLAW-BELIEF-CONDUCT-01","statement":"First lock the object of the law: belief/teaching or conduct. If the answer changes the object from belief to unlawful acts, cut it as the adjacent-call answer.","type":"cut","trigger":"Stem says hold or teach, while an answer says unlawful acts or conduct.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"belief/conduct object lock","prompt":"A statute punishes teaching a religious creed. One answer defends it by citing the state''s power to regulate unlawful acts inspired by the creed. What is the C3 cut?","answer":"NOT_RESPONSIVE; bait_doctrine; wrong_frame."},{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"only-if overclaim","prompt":"A religion-clause answer says the law is invalid only if it also violates Establishment. What is the mold?","answer":"NOT_TRUE; tiered_absolute."},{"drill_type":"ANCHOR","target_skill":"Free Exercise tiny anchor","prompt":"State the Gold Key for a statute that punishes holding or teaching a religious belief.","answer":"Belief/profession as such is protected; conduct is separate."},{"drill_type":"SILVER_KEY","target_skill":"adjacent-call defense","prompt":"What is the Silver Key when the stem says hold or teach but the answer says unlawful acts?","answer":"Lock the object of the law; cut object-switch answers."}]}'),
  ('96c7f15d-367b-5e28-a913-73dfdd4262ed', '20247_bible_memory_kit', 'CIVIL_PROCEDURE', 'Appeals and Finality', 'Rule 54(b)', 'Partial dismissal alone vs Rule 54(b) certification gate.', 'Ruth filed a federal civil action after a private Bible-memory tournament kit launch fell apart. Her complaint asserted four unrelated claims against Timothy, Lydia, and Stephen. The district court dismissed every claim against Timothy and wrote, “Timothy is no longer part of this case.” The order did not direct entry of judgment, and it did not state that there was no just reason for delay. Several claims against Lydia and Stephen remain.', 'Ruth filed a federal civil action after a private Bible-memory tournament kit launch fell apart. Her complaint asserted four unrelated claims against Timothy, Lydia, and Stephen. The district court dismissed every claim against Timothy and wrote, “Timothy is no longer part of this case.” The order did not direct entry of judgment, and it did not state that there was no just reason for delay. Several claims against Lydia and Stephen remain.

May Ruth immediately appeal the dismissal under Rule 54(b)?', 'May Ruth immediately appeal the dismissal under Rule 54(b)?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20247.md","source_question_id":"20247","variant_slug":"bible_memory_kit","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"93110101","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium-high","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A federal civil case still has claims pending. One defendant is fully dismissed, but the order lacks Rule 54(b)’s judgment direction and no-just-reason determination. Is the dismissal immediately appealable?","review_truth":"Partial dismissal is not immediately appealable under Rule 54(b) unless the district court directs entry of judgment and finds no just reason for delay.","dominant_trap":"D","anchor_card":{"card_id":"CIV-REM-54B-CERT-GATE","title":"Rule 54(b): partial dismissal is not automatic finality","signal":"Fewer than all claims or parties resolved.","student_move":"Ask whether the court both directed entry of judgment and found no just reason for delay.","tiny_rule":"No certification, no immediate Rule 54(b) appeal.","trap":"Full dismissal of one defendant feels final.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-RULE54B-CERT-01","statement":"Rule 54(b) is not automatic. A partial judgment in a multi-claim or multi-party civil action is immediately appealable only if the district court directs entry of judgment and determines that there is no just reason for delay.","type":"threshold","trigger":"Fewer than all claims or parties resolved; claims remain; order lacks certification language.","authority":"Fed. R. Civ. P. 54(b).","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-RULE54B-CERT-01","statement":"When the stem goes out of its way to say the order lacks formal Rule 54(b) language, do not let a partial-dismissal fact do the work by itself.","type":"trap_spotting","trigger":"One party is out, but other claims remain and formal certification is missing.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"overclaim detection","prompt":"Rule 54(b) applies automatically in every multi-party case.","answer":"CUT: tiered_absolute."},{"drill_type":"anchor","target_skill":"Rule 54(b) certification","prompt":"A multi-party civil case dismisses one defendant; claims remain; no no-just-reason finding. Immediate appeal?","answer":"No."},{"drill_type":"issue_sense","target_skill":"wrong-element procedural answer","prompt":"Yes, because one defendant is fully dismissed.","answer":"CUT: true fact, incomplete Rule 54(b) gate."}]}'),
  ('397918a8-a061-512f-b055-8029b8ac070e', '20258_psalter_pressure', 'CRIMINAL_LAW', 'Fifth Amendment', 'Constitutional Protection of Accused Persons', NULL, 'The source row is BARMATRIX Q# 20258, a Criminal Procedure / Fifth Amendment item testing whether coercive police threats and interrogation conditions render a confession involuntary and inadmissible in the prosecution’s case-in-chief. 

Christian divergent variation — `20258_psalter_pressure`

Investigators were questioning Martha about the theft of Stephen’s rare hand-illustrated psalter from a booth at a Christian arts festival. Before questioning, the investigators gave Martha Miranda warnings, and Martha said she understood them. Later, the investigators falsely told Martha that unless she admitted taking the psalter, they would have Barnabas, her brother, arrested that night and would call social services to remove Ruth, the niece Martha was raising. The questioning continued through the dinner hour for eight hours without food. Martha then confessed to the theft.', 'The source row is BARMATRIX Q# 20258, a Criminal Procedure / Fifth Amendment item testing whether coercive police threats and interrogation conditions render a confession involuntary and inadmissible in the prosecution’s case-in-chief. 

Christian divergent variation — `20258_psalter_pressure`

Investigators were questioning Martha about the theft of Stephen’s rare hand-illustrated psalter from a booth at a Christian arts festival. Before questioning, the investigators gave Martha Miranda warnings, and Martha said she understood them. Later, the investigators falsely told Martha that unless she admitted taking the psalter, they would have Barnabas, her brother, arrested that night and would call social services to remove Ruth, the niece Martha was raising. The questioning continued through the dinner hour for eight hours without food. Martha then confessed to the theft.

In Martha’s prosecution for theft, should the confession be admitted in the prosecution’s case-in-chief?', 'In Martha’s prosecution for theft, should the confession be admitted in the prosecution’s case-in-chief?', 3, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20258.md","source_question_id":"20258","variant_slug":"psalter_pressure","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"75080101","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium-hard","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"Police gave warnings, then used family threats and long questioning without food; is the confession admissible in the prosecution’s case-in-chief?","review_truth":"Warnings do not save a confession if police pressure overbears the suspect’s will.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"CRIM-REM-20258-01","title":"Warnings are not a pressure shield","signal":"Stem gives Miranda warnings and then coercive pressure facts.","student_move":"Separate warning/form facts from voluntariness facts.","tiny_rule":"Coercion can defeat admissibility even after warnings.","trap":"Picking the warning answer because it sounds procedurally clean.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CRIMINAL-PSALTER-PRESSURE-01","statement":"A confession produced by coercive police threats or conditions that overbear the suspect’s will is involuntary and cannot be used in the prosecution’s case-in-chief.","type":"rule","trigger":"Warnings plus threats against family plus deprivation","authority":"Lynumn v. Illinois; Colorado v. Connelly; Mincey v. Arizona","last_minute_review":true},{"id":"GK-CRIMINAL-MIRANDA-SEPARATE-02","statement":"Miranda waiver and due-process voluntariness are separate gates; warnings or a waiver form do not cure a coerced confession.","type":"distinction","trigger":"The stem gives warnings, then pressure facts","authority":"Mincey v. Arizona; North Carolina v. Butler","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CRIMINAL-PSALTER-PRESSURE-01","statement":"When a stem gives one clean procedure fact and then piles on pressure facts, do not stop at the procedure fact; the answer must account for the pressure facts.","type":"trap_spotting","trigger":"Warnings appear before coercion facts","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true},{"id":"SK-CRIMINAL-PSALTER-PRESSURE-02","statement":"In a yes/no answer set, a no answer with the wrong reason still gets cut; match both the result and the reason.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"Two no answers appear","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"overclaim detector","prompt":"Choice says police may use any deception during interrogation. What is the C3 cut?","answer":"NOT_TRUE / tiered_absolute."},{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"bait doctrine","prompt":"Choice says the confession is admissible because warnings were given and understood. The stem adds threats and deprivation. What is the C3 cut?","answer":"NOT_RESPONSIVE / bait_doctrine; warnings answer the wrong layer."},{"drill_type":"answer_array","target_skill":"no-but-wrong-reason","prompt":"Two choices say no. One says no because coercion; one says no because no written waiver. Which survives?","answer":"The coercion answer; the form answer gives the wrong reason."},{"drill_type":"anchor","target_skill":"confession voluntariness","prompt":"What Gold Key unlocks warnings-plus-pressure confession questions?","answer":"Coercion can make a confession involuntary and inadmissible even after warnings."}]}'),
  ('9f879ab2-ed02-5049-8067-38adcd91200a', '20271_lantern_handbell_evening', 'CONTRACTS', 'Expectation damages', 'A1', NULL, 'Daniel agreed to create and lead a private Scripture-themed lantern-and-handbell evening for Lydia’s family Easter celebration for $48,000. Daniel reasonably expected to spend $31,000 on lantern rentals, music assistants, and supplies. Before Daniel rented anything or began preparing, Lydia repudiated the contract without excuse. Daniel had no other losses and avoided all performance costs.', 'Daniel agreed to create and lead a private Scripture-themed lantern-and-handbell evening for Lydia’s family Easter celebration for $48,000. Daniel reasonably expected to spend $31,000 on lantern rentals, music assistants, and supplies. Before Daniel rented anything or began preparing, Lydia repudiated the contract without excuse. Daniel had no other losses and avoided all performance costs.

What is Daniel’s proper expectation recovery?', 'What is Daniel’s proper expectation recovery?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20271.md","source_question_id":"20271","variant_slug":"lantern_handbell_evening","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"51060101","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"calibration","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A service provider had a $48,000 contract, expected $31,000 in performance costs, and the client repudiated before any costs were incurred. What expectation damages does the provider recover?","review_truth":"Expectation recovery does not pay the gross price or the costs saved; it pays the net bargain lost.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-CONTRACTS-EXPECTATION-SAVED-COSTS-01","title":"Do not pay avoided costs.","signal":"The stem gives contract price and expected cost, then says the contractor avoided all costs.","student_move":"Subtract the avoided cost from the price.","tiny_rule":"Expectation protects the net bargain, not the gross price.","trap":"price/cost/zero answer array","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONTRACTS-COSTS-SAVED-01","statement":"When a service contract is repudiated before the contractor spends money, expectation recovery is the profit the contractor lost: contract price minus costs avoided, plus any other proven loss.","type":"rule","trigger":"fixed service price + expected costs + repudiation before performance + avoided all costs","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 347","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONTRACTS-PROFIT-MATH-01","statement":"When the choices are gross price, expected cost, net profit, and zero, treat the array as a formula test: keep the answer that uses price and subtracts saved costs.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"one choice equals price, one equals cost, one equals price-minus-cost, one equals zero","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"number_array","target_skill":"cost-saved offset","prompt":"Contract price $70,000; saved costs $52,000; no other loss. Recovery?","answer":"$18,000"},{"drill_type":"wrong_element","target_skill":"gross price trap","prompt":"Why is the full contract price not the recovery when all costs were avoided?","answer":"It ignores saved costs and overcompensates."},{"drill_type":"fabricated_rule","target_skill":"no-work myth","prompt":"Does a contractor lose all profit recovery because work had not begun?","answer":"No. If the contract was repudiated and profit is proved, saved costs are subtracted."}]}'),
  ('22a1dd96-613f-54ca-9810-3584de3fae19', '20291_nativity_prop_rental_freeze', 'CIVIL_PROCEDURE', 'Appeals and Finality', 'Injunction Appeals', 'caption label vs practical command', 'In a federal contract dispute between two private owners of a Nativity-prop rental business, the district court enters an order captioned “trial-preparation protocol.” The order directs Ruth not to rent out the disputed angel wings, shepherd cloaks, or manger displays to any Christmas pageant customers until trial. Ruth immediately appeals, arguing that the order operates as an injunction despite its caption.', 'In a federal contract dispute between two private owners of a Nativity-prop rental business, the district court enters an order captioned “trial-preparation protocol.” The order directs Ruth not to rent out the disputed angel wings, shepherd cloaks, or manger displays to any Christmas pageant customers until trial. Ruth immediately appeals, arguing that the order operates as an injunction despite its caption.

Is Ruth’s characterization likely right?', 'Is Ruth’s characterization likely right?', 3, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20291.md","source_question_id":"20291","variant_slug":"nativity_prop_rental_freeze","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"93110100","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium-hard","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A district court order with a non-injunction caption tells a party not to rent disputed property until trial. Is it likely treated as an injunction for immediate appeal?","review_truth":"For injunction appealability, read practical effect; a stop-command can be treated as an injunction even if the caption says something else.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"CIV-REM-20291-FUNCTIONAL-INJUNCTION","title":"Caption vs command","signal":"A district-court order has a neutral label but tells someone to do or stop doing something.","student_move":"Ignore the caption first; identify the operative command.","tiny_rule":"Function beats label for injunction appealability.","trap":"Magic-word caption rule; every-management-order overclaim.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-FUNCTIONAL-INJUNCTION-01","statement":"For immediate appeal, an order can count as an injunction by practical effect. A command to do or stop doing something may be injunctive even when the caption uses another label.","type":"distinction","trigger":"Non-injunction caption plus a command to stop conduct until trial.","authority":"28 U.S.C. §1292(a)(1); Carson v. American Brands; Abbott v. Perez.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-FUNCTIONAL-INJUNCTION-01","statement":"In a label-vs-function appealability question, read the verb in the order before reading the caption. A stop-command is the navigation signal.","type":"trap_spotting","trigger":"Order caption says one thing; operative sentence commands a party not to act.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"overclaim scan","prompt":"An order is called a scheduling order. One answer says every scheduling order is immediately appealable. What is the cut?","answer":"tiered_absolute; every is too broad."},{"drill_type":"anchor","target_skill":"functional injunction","prompt":"A district court labels an order a protocol but tells a party not to sell inventory until trial. What Gold Key applies?","answer":"Function beats label for injunction appealability."},{"drill_type":"trap_spotting","target_skill":"caption vs command","prompt":"The caption says protocol; the text says do not rent. Which fact controls the student move?","answer":"The command controls the navigation move."},{"drill_type":"calibration","target_skill":"anchor-assisted cut","prompt":"You cut every, only, and never, but two cuts use a Gold Key. What confidence bucket?","answer":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED."}]}'),
  ('209e8d61-21c6-57c9-b5e5-f3e9d97cc6fe', '20307_bethany_banners_transcript', 'EVIDENCE', 'Evidence Law', 'Hearsay and Circumstances of Its Admissibility', NULL, 'At a private “Songs of the Psalms” merchandise expo, Ruth was walking through an aisle when she was struck by a motorized pallet cart driven by Peter, an employee of Bethany Banners, Inc. Peter was moving boxed stage banners for Bethany Banners to its rented booth.

After the incident, Peter was charged with reckless operation of equipment and pleaded not guilty. At that criminal trial, Peter testified in his own defense. He stated that, at the time of the incident, he had looked down at the booth map to find the “Road to Emmaus” display area, and as a result he did not see Ruth walking in the aisle.

Ruth later brought a personal-injury action against Bethany Banners under a respondeat superior theory, alleging that Peter’s negligence caused her injuries. At the civil trial, Ruth proved that Peter remained employed by Bethany Banners until Peter later died from causes unrelated to the incident. Ruth then offered a transcript of Peter’s testimony from the reckless-operation trial.

Upon objection by Bethany Banners’s attorney, the transcript should be:', 'At a private “Songs of the Psalms” merchandise expo, Ruth was walking through an aisle when she was struck by a motorized pallet cart driven by Peter, an employee of Bethany Banners, Inc. Peter was moving boxed stage banners for Bethany Banners to its rented booth.

After the incident, Peter was charged with reckless operation of equipment and pleaded not guilty. At that criminal trial, Peter testified in his own defense. He stated that, at the time of the incident, he had looked down at the booth map to find the “Road to Emmaus” display area, and as a result he did not see Ruth walking in the aisle.

Ruth later brought a personal-injury action against Bethany Banners under a respondeat superior theory, alleging that Peter’s negligence caused her injuries. At the civil trial, Ruth proved that Peter remained employed by Bethany Banners until Peter later died from causes unrelated to the incident. Ruth then offered a transcript of Peter’s testimony from the reckless-operation trial.

Upon objection by Bethany Banners’s attorney, the transcript should be:

Upon objection by Bethany Banners''s attorney, the transcript should be:', 'Upon objection by Bethany Banners''s attorney, the transcript should be:', 3, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20307.md","source_question_id":"20307","variant_slug":"bethany_banners_transcript","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"33040202","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"hard","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"An employee, still employed when he testified in an earlier criminal case, admitted job-related careless conduct; the injured plaintiff later sues the employer and offers the court transcript. Is the transcript admissible?","review_truth":"Transcript questions are layer questions: clear the speaker layer and the transcript layer.","dominant_trap":"No measured dominant trap supplied; former-testimony answer is treated as the highest-risk trap by structure.","anchor_card":{"card_id":"REMED-EVIDENCE-DOUBLE-HEARSAY-TRANSCRIPT","title":"Transcript = two layers","signal":"A transcript carries a statement from an earlier proceeding.","student_move":"Ask: what admits the speaker''s statement, and what admits the transcript?","tiny_rule":"Both layers need paths.","trap":"Picking the first familiar exception name.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-EVIDENCE-DOUBLE-HEARSAY-01","statement":"For hearsay within hearsay, each layer needs its own path. In an employee-transcript item, check the employee statement layer and the transcript/public-record layer separately.","type":"rule","trigger":"Transcript of earlier testimony offered for its truth.","authority":"FRE 805; FRE 801(d)(2)(D); FRE 803(8).","last_minute_review":true},{"id":"GK-EVIDENCE-FORMER-TESTIMONY-01","statement":"Former testimony needs the current opponent, or a civil predecessor, to have had an opportunity and similar motive to develop the earlier testimony.","type":"threshold","trigger":"Earlier criminal trial; later civil defendant was not a party.","authority":"FRE 804(b)(1).","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-EVIDENCE-TRANSCRIPT-LAYERS-01","statement":"When a choice says ''transcript,'' split the evidence into speaker layer and document layer before choosing an exception.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"Transcript offered in a later trial.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"Gold Key recall","target_skill":"Double-hearsay layer split","prompt":"A transcript of earlier testimony is offered in a later civil trial. What two layers must be cleared?","answer":"The speaker''s statement layer and the transcript/document layer."},{"drill_type":"Wrong-frame cut","target_skill":"Former testimony threshold","prompt":"Prior testimony is offered against a party who was not present in the earlier case. What threshold should you check?","answer":"Whether that party or a civil predecessor had an opportunity and similar motive to develop the testimony."},{"drill_type":"Exception distinction","target_skill":"Recorded recollection vs transcript","prompt":"Why is a court transcript usually not a recorded recollection?","answer":"It is not the witness''s made-or-adopted memory record."}]}'),
  ('c4682e6a-9b89-5fca-88eb-44613808b2d0', '20310_psalms_wax_lamp', 'TORTS', 'Negligence', 'Breach', 'later-installation use: breach proof vs limited feasibility use', 'Lydia runs a paid private Psalms calligraphy workshop in her garage studio. During the class, Ruth reached across the worktable for a sheet of parchment and brushed her forearm against an exposed brass warming lamp used to soften sealing wax. Ruth was burned. The next morning, Lydia installed a small wire guard around the lamp. Lydia admits that the guard could have been installed before Ruth was burned, but she denies that reasonable care required a guard under the conditions that existed during the class.', 'Lydia runs a paid private Psalms calligraphy workshop in her garage studio. During the class, Ruth reached across the worktable for a sheet of parchment and brushed her forearm against an exposed brass warming lamp used to soften sealing wax. Ruth was burned. The next morning, Lydia installed a small wire guard around the lamp. Lydia admits that the guard could have been installed before Ruth was burned, but she denies that reasonable care required a guard under the conditions that existed during the class.

For breach, what is the best use of the later installation?', 'For breach, what is the best use of the later installation?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20310.md","source_question_id":"20310","variant_slug":"psalms_wax_lamp","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"61020301","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium-high","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"After a workshop injury, the defendant added a safety guard and admits the guard was feasible before the injury. For breach, does the later guard prove prior negligence, or is its only possible use feasibility if genuinely disputed?","review_truth":"A later safety fix does not prove earlier negligence; at most, it can serve a limited purpose such as feasibility if that point is genuinely disputed.","dominant_trap":"later fix equals conclusive prior negligence","anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-TORTS-SRM-01","title":"Later Fix Is Not Hindsight Liability","signal":"Post-injury repair, added guard, new warning, new traction, or changed procedure.","student_move":"Separate prior breach from later feasibility.","tiny_rule":"Later fix is not breach proof; feasibility only if genuinely disputed.","trap":"They fixed it later, so they must have breached earlier.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-TORTS-LATER-REPAIR-01","statement":"A later safety fix is not proof that the earlier condition was negligent. Its limited use is a non-breach purpose such as feasibility, and only if that purpose is genuinely disputed.","type":"distinction","trigger":"Defendant changes the condition after the injury.","authority":"Fed. R. Evid. 407; Restatement (Third) of Torts: Liability for Physical and Emotional Harm §3.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-TORTS-LATER-REPAIR-01","statement":"Lock the call to use, not hindsight. First cut the two extremes: later fix proves everything, later fix proves nothing.","type":"call_focus","trigger":"A post-injury safety change appears in a breach question.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"overclaim detection","prompt":"A defendant repairs a hazard after an injury. One choice says the repair conclusively proves breach. Cut or keep?","answer":"Cut. Conclusive proof is an overclaim."},{"drill_type":"CALL_LOCK","target_skill":"call focus","prompt":"The call asks breach by the defendant. A choice proves plaintiff contributory negligence. What filter breaks?","answer":"NOT_RESPONSIVE."},{"drill_type":"ANCHOR","target_skill":"later remedial measure limited use","prompt":"A later guard may be used for what limited purpose if genuinely disputed?","answer":"Feasibility, not breach proof."},{"drill_type":"TRAP_SPOTTING","target_skill":"hindsight bias","prompt":"What is the trap in ''they fixed it later, so they were negligent before''?","answer":"It uses hindsight as a substitute for breach analysis."}]}'),
  ('7847a157-2386-5de5-ba4d-82eeec2f608d', '20312_loaves_fishes_retreat', 'REAL_PROPERTY', 'Ownership', 'Life estates; remainders; encumbrance payment allocation', 'interest/current-use burden vs principal/future-equity burden', 'Martha holds a life estate in a private lakeside parcel called the Loaves-and-Fishes Retreat, where families rent small cabins during nearby Bible-study weekends. Stephen holds the remainder. Before the life estate was created, the parcel was already subject to a recorded mortgage securing a loan on the parcel. The parcel’s reasonable rental value is greater than the total currently needed to cover both principal and interest on that secured obligation.', 'Martha holds a life estate in a private lakeside parcel called the Loaves-and-Fishes Retreat, where families rent small cabins during nearby Bible-study weekends. Stephen holds the remainder. Before the life estate was created, the parcel was already subject to a recorded mortgage securing a loan on the parcel. The parcel’s reasonable rental value is greater than the total currently needed to cover both principal and interest on that secured obligation.

Which statement correctly allocates the payment obligation, as between Martha and Stephen, for the obligation secured by the mortgage?', 'Which statement correctly allocates the payment obligation, as between Martha and Stephen, for the obligation secured by the mortgage?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20312.md","source_question_id":"20312","variant_slug":"loaves_fishes_retreat","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"82010103","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium_high","deciding_phase":"CALL","confidence":"PURE_ANCHOR","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A life tenant is in possession. The property’s rental value can cover principal and interest on a mortgage. Who pays interest, and who pays principal?","review_truth":"Current-use burden goes to the life tenant: interest. Future-equity burden goes to the remainderman: principal.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"RP-REM-LIFE-ENCUMBRANCE-01","title":"Life tenant pays interest; remainderman pays principal","signal":"life tenant + remainderman + mortgage/encumbrance","student_move":"Name the two payment components before choosing.","tiny_rule":"Interest is current-use cost; principal is future-equity cost.","trap":"Choosing whoever seems more morally responsible for the whole mortgage.","confidence":"PURE_ANCHOR"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-REAL_PROPERTY-LIFE-ENCUMBRANCE-01","statement":"For an existing mortgage or encumbrance on land held in life estate, the life tenant pays interest up to the reasonable rental value; the remainderman pays principal.","type":"distinction","trigger":"life tenant + remainderman + mortgage/encumbrance + principal and interest","authority":"Thompson v. Watkins, 285 N.C. 616 (1974); N.Y. Real Property Law § 269.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-REAL_PROPERTY-LIFE-ENCUMBRANCE-01","statement":"When every choice assigns principal and interest between two estate holders, read the answers as a 2x2 allocation grid before using the anchor.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"choices distribute principal and interest between life tenant and remainderman","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"anchor_recall","target_skill":"life estate encumbrance allocation","prompt":"Life tenant in possession; remainderman waiting; mortgage has principal and interest due. Allocate the burdens.","answer":"Life tenant pays interest up to rental value; remainderman pays principal."},{"drill_type":"answer_array","target_skill":"2x2 allocation grid","prompt":"Four choices assign two costs to two estate holders. What is the first move?","answer":"Read it as a grid: identify each cost and each holder before applying the anchor."},{"drill_type":"wrong_answer_recovery","target_skill":"avoid over-assigning burdens","prompt":"Why is ''life tenant pays both principal and interest'' tempting and why does it fail?","answer":"Tempting because the life tenant is in possession; fails because possession maps to interest, not principal."}]}'),
  ('265b586e-c0c3-5f41-a6ad-555f53f31ae4', '20313_bethlehem_olivewood_crosses', 'EVIDENCE', 'III.B.5 — Attorney-client', 'Attorney-client privilege / crime-fraud boundary', 'compliance advice versus furthering fraud', 'Lydia runs a small Bible-study gift cart at weekend retreats. Before selling a shipment of wooden crosses, she privately told her lawyer, Paul, “I am thinking about advertising these as carved from Bethlehem olivewood. Would that be legal? If that would be fraudulent, I will not use that description.” Paul researched the law and advised Lydia not to use the description.', 'Lydia runs a small Bible-study gift cart at weekend retreats. Before selling a shipment of wooden crosses, she privately told her lawyer, Paul, “I am thinking about advertising these as carved from Bethlehem olivewood. Would that be legal? If that would be fraudulent, I will not use that description.” Paul researched the law and advised Lydia not to use the description.

Are Lydia’s communications protected by attorney-client privilege?', 'Are Lydia’s communications protected by attorney-client privilege?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20313.md","source_question_id":"20313","variant_slug":"bethlehem_olivewood_crosses","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"35030205","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"discriminator","deciding_phase":"CLASH","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":"If that would be fraudulent, I will not use that description.","distilled_core_question":"Client asks lawyer whether planned conduct would be fraudulent and says she will not do it if unlawful. Lawyer advises against it. Are the communications privileged?","review_truth":"Suspicious subject matter does not defeat attorney-client privilege unless the client seeks legal advice to carry out or cover up wrongdoing.","dominant_trap":"B","anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-EVIDENCE-PRIV-CRIME-FRAUD-20313","title":"Purpose beats suspicious topic","signal":"Client asks whether a proposed act would be fraudulent.","student_move":"Ask whether the client is seeking compliance advice or help carrying out wrongdoing.","tiny_rule":"Crime-fraud needs furtherance; compliance advice remains privileged.","trap":"Fraud word = automatic exception.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-EVIDENCE-CRIME-FRAUD-01","statement":"Crime-fraud is purpose-based, not topic-based: privilege fails only when legal advice is sought to commit, continue, or cover up crime or fraud.","type":"exception","trigger":"Client asks whether planned conduct would be fraudulent and says she will not do it if unlawful.","authority":"FRE 501 common law; United States v. Zolin; Clark v. United States.","last_minute_review":true},{"id":"GK-EVIDENCE-WORKPRODUCT-DISTINCTION-01","statement":"Substantial need is a work-product concept; it is not the test for overriding attorney-client communications.","type":"distinction","trigger":"A privilege answer says ''substantial need.''","authority":"Federal privilege and work-product doctrine distinction.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-EVIDENCE-CRIME-FRAUD-01","statement":"When a privilege answer says ''fraud,'' do not stop at the topic. Name the axis: compliance advice versus using the lawyer to further fraud.","type":"clash","trigger":"Client says she will not proceed if the conduct is fraudulent.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"Gold Key recall","target_skill":"crime-fraud boundary","prompt":"Client asks lawyer whether a planned act would be fraudulent and says she will not do it if unlawful. Does crime-fraud automatically apply?","answer":"No. The purpose is compliance advice, not furtherance."},{"drill_type":"bait-doctrine cut","target_skill":"privilege versus work product","prompt":"A privilege answer says disclosure depends on substantial need. What is the C3 label?","answer":"bait_doctrine / wrong_frame."},{"drill_type":"Clash axis","target_skill":"purpose axis","prompt":"Two answers fight: fraud topic kills privilege versus legal advice to avoid fraud. What fact decides?","answer":"Client says she will not proceed if the conduct is fraudulent."}]}'),
  ('c9cdaca4-bcf9-57c0-9f70-9aef62c0666e', '20328_bethlehem_star_drop', 'TORTS', 'Negligence', 'Breach proof - res ipsa loquitur', 'precise defect proof required vs res ipsa inference without direct defect proof', 'Lydia owns a for-profit Bible-story dinner theater that sells tickets to a Nativity show. During a performance, an overhead Bethlehem star prop that was supposed to glide slowly above the stage suddenly dropped from its track and struck Martha, a ticketed guest standing in the marked viewing area. Lydia had hired Timothy''s rigging company to inspect and service the overhead track, but Lydia kept responsibility for deciding whether the star prop would be used during each show and received all inspection reports. Martha did not touch the rigging, controls, or stage equipment. Martha sues Lydia for negligence but cannot prove the exact clamp, cable, or motor failure that caused the star to drop.', 'Lydia owns a for-profit Bible-story dinner theater that sells tickets to a Nativity show. During a performance, an overhead Bethlehem star prop that was supposed to glide slowly above the stage suddenly dropped from its track and struck Martha, a ticketed guest standing in the marked viewing area. Lydia had hired Timothy''s rigging company to inspect and service the overhead track, but Lydia kept responsibility for deciding whether the star prop would be used during each show and received all inspection reports. Martha did not touch the rigging, controls, or stage equipment. Martha sues Lydia for negligence but cannot prove the exact clamp, cable, or motor failure that caused the star to drop.

Will res ipsa loquitur likely be available in Martha''s negligence claim against Lydia?', 'Will res ipsa loquitur likely be available in Martha''s negligence claim against Lydia?', 3, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20328.md","source_question_id":"20328","variant_slug":"bethlehem_star_drop","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"61020304","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium-hard","deciding_phase":"CLASH","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A maintained overhead prop suddenly drops during normal use; the defendant kept operational responsibility, the plaintiff did not cause it, and the plaintiff lacks direct defect proof. Is res ipsa likely available?","review_truth":"Res ipsa can supply a negligence inference from an abnormal event without direct defect proof, but it does not force judgment.","dominant_trap":"precise-defect requirement","anchor_card":{"card_id":"RC-TORTS-RES-IPSA-01","title":"Res ipsa: inference, not proof shortcut to judgment","signal":"Abnormal malfunction, defendant responsibility, plaintiff non-contribution, no direct proof.","student_move":"Do not punish the plaintiff for lacking the exact defect; ask whether the event itself can speak.","tiny_rule":"No exact defect needed; no automatic judgment.","trap":"Missing direct proof feels fatal.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-TORTS-RES-IPSA-INFERENCE-01","statement":"Res ipsa is an inference bridge. It can apply when the plaintiff lacks direct proof of the exact defect, and it permits but does not compel a negligence finding.","type":"rule","trigger":"The call asks whether res ipsa is available and the stem says the plaintiff lacks direct defect proof.","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 328D","last_minute_review":true},{"id":"GK-TORTS-RES-IPSA-CONTROL-01","statement":"Contractor involvement does not automatically erase res ipsa responsibility when the defendant retained operational responsibility for the instrumentality.","type":"distinction","trigger":"The stem says a contractor serviced the mechanism but the defendant retained operational responsibility and received reports.","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 328D responsibility/control formulation","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-TORTS-RES-IPSA-ARRAY-01","statement":"Cut the absolute contractor and automatic-judgment answers first. Then resolve the residual pair by asking whether the missing exact defect is fatal or is the reason res ipsa matters.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"One answer says no exact defect, one says inference without exact defect.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"gold_key_recall","target_skill":"res ipsa direct-proof trap","prompt":"Plaintiff lacks proof of the exact defect but the instrumentality fails abnormally under defendant responsibility. Fatal to res ipsa?","answer":"No. Missing exact defect proof is compatible with res ipsa."},{"drill_type":"overclaim_cut","target_skill":"absolute contractor trap","prompt":"Hiring a contractor always defeats res ipsa responsibility.","answer":"Cut. Always overclaims."},{"drill_type":"remedy_precision","target_skill":"inference versus judgment","prompt":"Res ipsa requires judgment for the plaintiff once an accident happens.","answer":"Cut. It permits an inference; it does not require judgment."},{"drill_type":"answer_array","target_skill":"residual pair recognition","prompt":"No exact defect proof vs inference without exact defect proof. Name the axis.","answer":"Whether res ipsa requires direct proof of the precise defect."}]}'),
  ('aec55d9c-5747-5295-a4ec-284c1b2d27bb', '20351_red_sea_mini_golf', 'CONTRACTS', 'Mistake / Lack of Mutual Assent', 'F', 'mutual-mistake fact vs contractual risk allocation', 'Barnabas owns a privately run Bible-story mini-golf course called The Exodus Course. He sells it to Ruth. The sale contract says that Ruth buys the course "as is, including all hidden subsurface drainage defects, whether known or unknown." Both Barnabas and Ruth believe the underground drainage tunnels beneath the "Red Sea Crossing" hole are intact. Before signing, however, those tunnels had already collapsed from storm erosion. Ruth later discovers the collapse and seeks rescission for mutual mistake. How should the court rule?', 'Barnabas owns a privately run Bible-story mini-golf course called The Exodus Course. He sells it to Ruth. The sale contract says that Ruth buys the course "as is, including all hidden subsurface drainage defects, whether known or unknown." Both Barnabas and Ruth believe the underground drainage tunnels beneath the "Red Sea Crossing" hole are intact. Before signing, however, those tunnels had already collapsed from storm erosion. Ruth later discovers the collapse and seeks rescission for mutual mistake. How should the court rule?

How should the court rule?', 'How should the court rule?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20351.md","source_question_id":"20351","variant_slug":"red_sea_mini_golf","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"54030600","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"trap repair","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"Buyer signs an as-is clause covering hidden unknown defects. Both parties are wrong about a hidden preexisting defect. Buyer seeks mutual-mistake rescission. Who wins?","review_truth":"A mutual mistake does not support rescission when the contract assigned that mistake-risk to the party seeking rescission.","dominant_trap":"C","anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-CONTRACTS-RISK-BEFORE-RESCISSION-01","title":"Risk before rescission","signal":"Mutual mistake plus as-is / unknown-defect clause.","student_move":"Do not stop at both mistaken. Ask who took the risk.","tiny_rule":"If the claimant accepted the mistake-risk, rescission is denied.","trap":"Picking the mutual-mistake-only grant answer.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONTRACTS-AS-IS-MISTAKE-01","statement":"A mutual mistake does not support rescission if the party seeking rescission bore the risk. An as-is clause can allocate unknown hidden-defect risk to that party.","type":"rule","trigger":"A mutual mistake stem plus an express as-is or unknown-defect risk-allocation term.","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts §§ 152, 154.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONTRACTS-AS-IS-MISTAKE-01","statement":"In a grant/deny rescission matrix, separate the mistake fact from the risk-allocation fact. The answer that engages the explicit risk term beats the answer that only says both parties were mistaken.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"Two choices grant rescission, two deny; one denial tracks the as-is clause.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"Overclaim detection","prompt":"Hidden defects are always outside an as-is clause.","answer":"Cut: tiered_absolute."},{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"Fabricated rule detection","prompt":"Deny rescission only if the buyer inspected every hidden component personally.","answer":"Cut: fabricated_rule."},{"drill_type":"ANCHOR","target_skill":"Risk allocation in mutual mistake","prompt":"Both parties were mistaken, but the buyer agreed to take unknown hidden-defect risk. Rescission?","answer":"No rescission."},{"drill_type":"ANSWER_ARRAY","target_skill":"2x2 grant/deny matrix","prompt":"Two choices grant because of mistake; two deny because of risk or a proof condition. Which axis decides?","answer":"Risk allocation."}]}'),
  ('42d71c1c-8d9f-573d-9a77-6792c5eb46af', '20353_baptism_tank_burst', 'CIVIL_PROCEDURE', 'Subject Matter Jurisdiction', 'Supplemental Jurisdiction - Defendant Claims', NULL, 'In a federal diversity action, Lydia, a citizen of State A, sues Barnabas, a citizen of State B, for $120,000 after Barnabas''s privately owned portable baptism-tank display burst at a Christian handbell expo and ruined Lydia''s sound equipment. Barnabas counterclaims against Lydia for $18,000 for damage to the tank display from the same burst. The counterclaim has no independent amount in controversy.', 'In a federal diversity action, Lydia, a citizen of State A, sues Barnabas, a citizen of State B, for $120,000 after Barnabas''s privately owned portable baptism-tank display burst at a Christian handbell expo and ruined Lydia''s sound equipment. Barnabas counterclaims against Lydia for $18,000 for damage to the tank display from the same burst. The counterclaim has no independent amount in controversy.

May the federal court hear the counterclaim?', 'May the federal court hear the counterclaim?', 3, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20353.md","source_question_id":null,"variant_slug":"baptism_tank_burst","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"94010300","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium_hard","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"In a diversity case with a proper $120,000 anchor claim, may the court hear a defendant''s $18,000 counterclaim against the plaintiff when it arises from the same incident?","review_truth":"A same-event defendant counterclaim can use supplemental jurisdiction even if it does not independently exceed $75,000.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"CIV-REM-20353-DEFENDANT-COUNTERCLAIM","title":"Defendant counterclaim under supplemental jurisdiction","signal":"Diversity anchor claim plus low-value defendant counterclaim from the same incident.","student_move":"Check whether the low-value claim is defendant-side and same occurrence before applying §1367(b).","tiny_rule":"Same-event defendant counterclaims may ride §1367(a); §1367(b) is plaintiff-side.","trap":"Treating every low-value diversity claim as independently barred.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-DEFENDANT_COUNTERCLAIM-01","statement":"In a diversity case, a defendant''s same-transaction counterclaim can use supplemental jurisdiction even below $75,000 unless a specific plaintiff-side §1367(b) bar applies.","type":"rule","trigger":"Defendant counterclaim + same incident + no independent amount in controversy.","authority":"28 U.S.C. §1367(a)-(b); Fed. R. Civ. P. 13(a)(1).","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-SUPP_COUNTERCLAIM_ARRAY-01","statement":"Read the array as result plus jurisdictional reason. A ''yes'' answer is not enough; the reason must be same-event supplemental jurisdiction, not federal question.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"A counterclaim in a diversity case with no independent amount.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"tiered_absolute","prompt":"A diversity anchor claim is for $110,000. The defendant asserts a $12,000 same-incident counterclaim. Cut: ''No, each claim must independently exceed $75,000.''","answer":"CUT as tiered_absolute after Gold Key."},{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"misfit","prompt":"A counterclaim arises from the same incident but no federal statute appears. Cut: ''Yes, because the counterclaim presents a federal question.''","answer":"CUT as misfit / wrong_frame."},{"drill_type":"anchor","target_skill":"supplemental_jurisdiction_defendant_counterclaim","prompt":"State the Gold Key for a same-event defendant counterclaim below $75,000 in a diversity case.","answer":"It can use supplemental jurisdiction unless a specific plaintiff-side §1367(b) bar applies."},{"drill_type":"answer_array","target_skill":"result_reason_match","prompt":"Two answers say yes; one says federal question, one says same Article III case. The stem gives only same-incident facts. Which yes survives?","answer":"The same Article III case answer."}]}'),
  ('563044e0-872c-539a-b9d8-760f2b1333ab', '20370_dove_mug_notice', 'CONTRACTS', 'UCC sale of goods', 'Acceptance, rejection, notice of breach, and seller action for price', 'notice-after-acceptance vs defect-alone defense', 'Paul, a devotional-merchandise wholesaler, sued Lydia on August 9 for the price of 360 ceramic “Faith, Hope, Love” mugs. The sale began on March 7, when Lydia, who operates a Christian gift shop, ordered mugs finished with a gold dove decal, for delivery by March 18. On March 18, the mugs reached Lydia’s shop. Timothy, Lydia’s stockroom manager, opened the cartons and told Lydia both that the order had arrived and that the mugs were missing the gold dove decals. Lydia meant to text Paul that afternoon, but while preparing a children’s choir sale she forgot. On April 27, Lydia received Paul’s invoice but neither paid nor contacted him. In defense to Paul’s action, Lydia asserted that the mugs did not conform to the contract of sale.', 'Paul, a devotional-merchandise wholesaler, sued Lydia on August 9 for the price of 360 ceramic “Faith, Hope, Love” mugs. The sale began on March 7, when Lydia, who operates a Christian gift shop, ordered mugs finished with a gold dove decal, for delivery by March 18. On March 18, the mugs reached Lydia’s shop. Timothy, Lydia’s stockroom manager, opened the cartons and told Lydia both that the order had arrived and that the mugs were missing the gold dove decals. Lydia meant to text Paul that afternoon, but while preparing a children’s choir sale she forgot. On April 27, Lydia received Paul’s invoice but neither paid nor contacted him. In defense to Paul’s action, Lydia asserted that the mugs did not conform to the contract of sale.

The court should rule for', 'The court should rule for', 3, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20370.md","source_question_id":"20370","variant_slug":"dove_mug_notice","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"56050402","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium-hard","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A buyer learns on delivery that goods are nonconforming, says nothing for months, and raises the defect only after the seller sues for the price. Who wins?","review_truth":"In a goods sale, a buyer who accepts goods and sits on a known defect without timely notice loses the defect remedy.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"RC-CONTRACTS-NOTICE-AFTER-ACCEPTANCE-01","title":"Accepted goods: notice keeps the remedy alive","signal":"Buyer knows goods are defective, keeps silent, then raises defect after being sued.","student_move":"Ask whether the buyer notified the seller within a reasonable time.","tiny_rule":"Known defect plus no seasonable notice means no defect remedy after acceptance.","trap":"Picking the nonconformity answer because the goods were genuinely defective.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONTRACTS-DOVE-NOTICE-01","statement":"After accepting goods, a buyer who discovers a defect must notify the seller within a reasonable time or lose the defect remedy.","type":"rule","trigger":"Known nonconformity on delivery plus months of silence before a price suit.","authority":"UCC §§2-602, 2-606, 2-607, 2-709","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONTRACTS-DOVE-ARRAY-01","statement":"Read the 2x2 by reason, not outcome: the seller-wins choice with the notice fact beats the seller-wins choice with acceptance alone.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"Two choices name the seller and two name the buyer.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"same_outcome_wrong_reason","prompt":"Seller sues for price after buyer accepted defective goods. Choice says seller wins because acceptance alone always binds buyer to contract price. Cut or keep?","answer":"Cut; acceptance alone is incomplete because timely notice can preserve defect remedies."},{"drill_type":"Issue-Sense","target_skill":"wrong_element","prompt":"Buyer knew goods were defective on delivery and stayed silent for months. Choice says buyer wins because goods were defective. What is the skipped element?","answer":"Notice after discovery; defect alone is not responsive."},{"drill_type":"Anchor","target_skill":"accepted_goods_notice_rule","prompt":"After accepting goods, what must a buyer do after discovering a defect to preserve remedies?","answer":"Notify the seller within a reasonable time."},{"drill_type":"Trap Spotting","target_skill":"fabricated_rule","prompt":"Choice says seller loses the price if a reasonable pre-shipment inspection would have found the defect. What mold is this?","answer":"fabricated_rule."}]}'),
  ('b73bc1ad-be11-5921-a287-7dd3bbc81a60', '20387_psalms_chess_indictment', 'CRIMINAL_LAW', 'Exclusionary rule', 'Remedies and limits', 'violation versus remedy', 'Detectives investigating the theft of Stephen’s personal olivewood chess set from a Christian bookstore already had the shop’s security video and written statements from two shoppers identifying Peter. Three weeks later, officers saw Peter outside the store after a Psalms-and-coffee night and arrested him without a warrant or probable cause. The arrest produced no physical evidence and no statement. Prosecutors later indicted Peter for the theft using the video and shopper statements. Peter moves to dismiss the indictment solely because the arrest was unlawful.', 'Detectives investigating the theft of Stephen’s personal olivewood chess set from a Christian bookstore already had the shop’s security video and written statements from two shoppers identifying Peter. Three weeks later, officers saw Peter outside the store after a Psalms-and-coffee night and arrested him without a warrant or probable cause. The arrest produced no physical evidence and no statement. Prosecutors later indicted Peter for the theft using the video and shopper statements. Peter moves to dismiss the indictment solely because the arrest was unlawful.

How should the court rule?', 'How should the court rule?', 3, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20387.md","source_question_id":"20387","variant_slug":"psalms_chess_indictment","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"75100000","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"hard","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"Police make an unlawful arrest, get no evidence or statement from it, and indict using evidence already in the file. The defendant moves to dismiss solely because of the arrest. Should dismissal be granted?","review_truth":"A bad arrest can suppress tainted evidence, but it does not automatically erase a prosecution supported by independent evidence.","dominant_trap":"C","anchor_card":{"card_id":"CRIM-REM-ARREST-REMEDY-01","title":"Bad arrest is not automatic dismissal","signal":"Unlawful arrest + motion to dismiss + independent evidence","student_move":"Separate the violation from the requested remedy.","tiny_rule":"Suppress tainted evidence when applicable; do not dismiss charges supported by independent evidence.","trap":"Treating illegality as automatic case erasure.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CRIMINAL-ARREST-REMEDY-01","statement":"An unlawful arrest is not itself a prosecution-ending defect. The remedy targets evidence obtained through the illegality; charges supported by untainted evidence are not dismissed merely because the arrest was bad.","type":"rule","trigger":"unlawful arrest + no arrest-derived evidence + motion to dismiss","authority":"United States v. Crews, Gerstein v. Pugh, Frisbie v. Collins.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CRIMINAL-ARREST-REMEDY-01","statement":"Lock the remedy before applying Fourth Amendment exceptions. If the call asks for dismissal and the stem gives no arrest-produced evidence, suppression exceptions are side streets.","type":"call_focus","trigger":"motion to dismiss solely because arrest was unlawful","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"violation_vs_remedy","prompt":"Unlawful arrest; no evidence or statement obtained; independent evidence supports indictment. Motion to dismiss?","answer":"Deny; dismissal is not required."},{"drill_type":"trap_spotting","target_skill":"good-faith side street","prompt":"A choice says dismissal depends on officer good faith. What is the C3 signal?","answer":"Good faith is an evidence-admissibility lane; lock the dismissal call."},{"drill_type":"anchor_recall","target_skill":"unlawful arrest remedy","prompt":"What is the remedy for an unlawful arrest when no evidence was obtained from it?","answer":"No automatic dismissal; suppress tainted evidence only when applicable."},{"drill_type":"overclaim","target_skill":"automatic indictment nullity","prompt":"A choice says an unlawful arrest voids any later indictment. Name the mold.","answer":"tiered_absolute."}]}'),
  ('2469bc75-26b4-5c2d-8e41-90ec8867bd88', '20404_acts_axes_cash_loads', 'EVIDENCE', 'Materiality and probative value', 'A4', NULL, 'Mary operates a private “Acts & Axes” booth that sells scripture-engraved toy arks at summer fairs. She sues Stephen, her weekend cash-box clerk, for taking booth receipts. Before the records evidence is offered, Stephen testifies that Mary paid him $3,200 for the season and that he never took any receipts. Mary calls a prepaid-card records custodian, who will testify that during that same season Stephen made $18,600 in cash loads onto his personal prepaid card. Stephen objects that the cash loads could have come from birthday gifts or family loans.', 'Mary operates a private “Acts & Axes” booth that sells scripture-engraved toy arks at summer fairs. She sues Stephen, her weekend cash-box clerk, for taking booth receipts. Before the records evidence is offered, Stephen testifies that Mary paid him $3,200 for the season and that he never took any receipts. Mary calls a prepaid-card records custodian, who will testify that during that same season Stephen made $18,600 in cash loads onto his personal prepaid card. Stephen objects that the cash loads could have come from birthday gifts or family loans.

The custodian’s testimony is:', 'The custodian’s testimony is:', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20404.md","source_question_id":"20404","variant_slug":"acts_axes_cash_loads","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"31010104","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"trap repair","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A civil-theft defendant denies taking receipts and claims low seasonal pay; the plaintiff offers records showing much larger same-season cash deposits. Is that admissible even though the deposits have possible innocent explanations?","review_truth":"A clue can be admissible even when it is not a confession; weakness usually goes to weight, not admissibility.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"EVI-REM-ANY-TENDENCY-01","title":"Relevance is a clue threshold","signal":"Opponent says the evidence has innocent explanations or is not conclusive.","student_move":"Ask what fact the evidence tends to make more likely.","tiny_rule":"A clue need not be a confession.","trap":"demanding certainty before admissibility","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-EVIDENCE-ANY-TENDENCY-01","statement":"Relevant evidence does not need to prove the whole case by itself. If it has any tendency to make a consequential fact more probable, it clears the relevance threshold.","type":"threshold","trigger":"The opponent says the evidence could have another explanation.","authority":"Fed. R. Evid. 401, 402, 403; see pass-1 legal verification.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-EVIDENCE-RELEVANCE-ARRAY-01","statement":"In a relevance array, cut ''conclusive,'' ''always,'' and ''only'' before weighing the story. The survivor usually states what the evidence tends to show.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"Three choices narrow admissibility; one states the fact relation.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"overclaim","prompt":"Classify: inadmissible because financial records are always unfairly prejudicial.","answer":"tiered_absolute; NOT_TRUE."},{"drill_type":"ANCHOR","target_skill":"relevance threshold","prompt":"Does circumstantial evidence need to conclusively prove the fact before it is admissible?","answer":"No. Any tendency is enough if the fact matters."},{"drill_type":"BAIT_ARCHITECTURE","target_skill":"offered_for_purpose","prompt":"Records contradict a witness and also support the claim. Is an only-impeachment answer safe?","answer":"No. It underclaims the substantive use."}]}'),
  ('b353cc43-edc3-5b5a-af74-6a94f7a62811', '20423_galilee_props_bias', 'EVIDENCE', 'Opinion or reputation', 'E5', 'religious-belief truthfulness use versus organizational-affiliation bias use', 'At a federal civil trial over a broken Christmas-pageant stage wagon, Peter sues Galilee Props, Inc., a private prop-rental company. Ruth testifies for Galilee. On cross-examination, Peter’s lawyer asks Ruth whether she is the paid treasurer of New Covenant Fellowship, the religious organization that owns Galilee’s voting shares and appoints its managers. Galilee objects under the rule barring religious-belief credibility evidence. Peter says he offers the question to show Ruth’s possible bias toward Galilee, not to suggest that her religious views make her truthful or untruthful. How should the court rule?', 'At a federal civil trial over a broken Christmas-pageant stage wagon, Peter sues Galilee Props, Inc., a private prop-rental company. Ruth testifies for Galilee. On cross-examination, Peter’s lawyer asks Ruth whether she is the paid treasurer of New Covenant Fellowship, the religious organization that owns Galilee’s voting shares and appoints its managers. Galilee objects under the rule barring religious-belief credibility evidence. Peter says he offers the question to show Ruth’s possible bias toward Galilee, not to suggest that her religious views make her truthful or untruthful. How should the court rule?

How should the court rule?', 'How should the court rule?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20423.md","source_question_id":"20423","variant_slug":"galilee_props_bias","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"31010505","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"trap","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A witness has a paid role in a religious organization that controls a party. The question is offered to show bias, not religious truthfulness. Should the objection be sustained?","review_truth":"Religion evidence is barred for truthfulness character, not for bias from affiliation or interest.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-EVIDENCE-R610-BIAS-01","title":"Religion Evidence: Purpose Split","signal":"Religious affiliation appears, but the proponent says bias.","student_move":"Do not stop at the religious-evidence objection. Ask the offered purpose.","tiny_rule":"Belief-as-truthfulness is barred; affiliation-for-bias is not.","trap":"Blanket religious-affiliation ban.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-EVIDENCE-RELIGION-BIAS-01","statement":"Rule 610 blocks religion-as-truthfulness evidence. It does not block religious affiliation or organizational ties when offered to show bias, interest, or relationship to a party.","type":"distinction","trigger":"religion objection plus paid role or organizational tie to a party","authority":"Federal Rule of Evidence 610 Advisory Committee Note","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-EVIDENCE-PURPOSE-AXIS-01","statement":"When an Evidence answer turns on the same fact being used for different credibility purposes, name the offered purpose first and grade only that lane.","type":"trap_spotting","trigger":"The stem says ''offered to show bias'' and ''not to show truthfulness.''","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"purpose_axis","target_skill":"offered_for_purpose","prompt":"A witness belongs to a religious organization connected to a party. The question is offered to show bias. Is Rule 610 a blanket bar?","answer":"No. Bias purpose is outside the belief-as-truthfulness ban."},{"drill_type":"overclaim_cut","target_skill":"tiered_absolute","prompt":"Cut the flaw: any reference to religious affiliation is inadmissible.","answer":"Overclaim. Any erases the bias-purpose lane."},{"drill_type":"bait_doctrine_sort","target_skill":"bait_doctrine","prompt":"Truthful character may be supported only after attack. Does that govern a bias question?","answer":"No. That is Rule 608(a) truthfulness-character doctrine, not bias."},{"drill_type":"fabricated_rule_cut","target_skill":"fabricated_rule","prompt":"Bias may be shown only by reputation evidence.","answer":"Cut. The only limit is invented."}]}'),
  ('1123b3fb-ff74-5bdb-8150-29e0ff95c7ea', '20439_floating_star_driveway', 'TORTS', 'Negligence', 'Standard of Due Care / Best Argument', NULL, 'Timothy was walking on the public sidewalk past Lydia’s house on Saturday morning. Lydia, a professional director of children’s Christmas pageants, was rehearsing a ''floating star'' scene for a neighborhood Advent program. She tested a rented battery-powered cable rig in her open driveway beside the sidewalk. A defect in the rig caused a plywood star to swing outside the driveway and strike Timothy.', 'Timothy was walking on the public sidewalk past Lydia’s house on Saturday morning. Lydia, a professional director of children’s Christmas pageants, was rehearsing a ''floating star'' scene for a neighborhood Advent program. She tested a rented battery-powered cable rig in her open driveway beside the sidewalk. A defect in the rig caused a plywood star to swing outside the driveway and strike Timothy.

If Timothy asserts a claim against Lydia, Timothy’s most effective argument in support of his claim would be that', 'If Timothy asserts a claim against Lydia, Timothy’s most effective argument in support of his claim would be that', 3, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20439.md","source_question_id":"20439","variant_slug":"floating_star_driveway","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":null,"outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"moderate-hard","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A professional pageant director uses a defective stage rig in an exposed driveway and a passerby is hurt. Which argument best supports the passerby’s tort claim?","review_truth":"A tort claim needs an operative liability basis; unreasonable conduct can supply negligence, while product defect, causation, or professional status alone does not.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-TORTS-20439-01","title":"Cause/status/product are not the claim","signal":"A Torts answer names only cause, status, or product defect.","student_move":"Ask whether the answer supplies a liability basis.","tiny_rule":"For negligence, look for unreasonable conduct.","trap":"Choosing a legally flavored fragment instead of the operative ground.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-TORTS-USER-SUPPLIER-01","statement":"Strict products liability for a defective product is aimed at commercial sellers or distributors, not a person who merely uses the product.","type":"distinction","trigger":"The defendant is using, testing, or operating a product rather than selling or distributing it.","authority":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Products Liability § 1.","last_minute_review":true},{"id":"GK-TORTS-FAULT-BASIS-01","statement":"Cause and status are not enough; the plaintiff’s argument must supply negligence, intent, or a strict-liability category. Unreasonable conduct supplies the negligence route.","type":"threshold","trigger":"A best-argument call with choices that name cause, status, or product defect.","authority":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Liability for Physical and Emotional Harm § 3.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-TORTS-BEST-ARGUMENT-01","statement":"On a best-argument call, pick the answer that gives the plaintiff a legal route to liability; do not require the answer to prove the whole case.","type":"call_focus","trigger":"The call asks for the claimant’s most effective argument.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"wrong_element","prompt":"A Torts answer says only that the defendant’s act was a concurring cause. Cut or keep?","answer":"Cut unless another part of the answer supplies negligence, intent, or strict liability."},{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"bait_doctrine","prompt":"A passerby is injured by a defective device used by a pageant director. The answer says the director is strictly liable for using a defective product. Cut or keep?","answer":"Cut; strict products liability targets commercial sellers or distributors, not mere users."},{"drill_type":"CALL","target_skill":"call_focus","prompt":"The call asks for the plaintiff’s most effective argument. One answer gives possible unreasonable conduct; another gives only causation. Which wins?","answer":"The unreasonable-conduct answer."},{"drill_type":"ANCHOR","target_skill":"negligence reasonableness","prompt":"Which phrase best supplies negligence: professional status, concurring cause, unreasonable conduct, or product defect?","answer":"Unreasonable conduct."}]}'),
  ('f9fb9885-1afb-5e65-b06f-987ee4dd5da4', '20461_lydia_beeswax_requirements', 'CONTRACTS', 'Offer / valid offer requirements', 'II.A.1.a', NULL, 'Barnabas Wax Co. promptly accepted a signed email from Lydia, who runs a Bible-verse candle studio. The email said: “I offer to buy all the beeswax my studio requires for the next nine months at $18 per slab, shipment every other Friday.” Lydia later asserted that no contract had formed because the email did not list a fixed number of slabs.', 'Barnabas Wax Co. promptly accepted a signed email from Lydia, who runs a Bible-verse candle studio. The email said: “I offer to buy all the beeswax my studio requires for the next nine months at $18 per slab, shipment every other Friday.” Lydia later asserted that no contract had formed because the email did not list a fixed number of slabs.

Which statement is most accurate?', 'Which statement is most accurate?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20461.md","source_question_id":"20461","variant_slug":"lydia_beeswax_requirements","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"55020203","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"trap","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"Buyer offers to buy all goods her business requires at a set unit price and delivery schedule; seller accepts; buyer says no fixed number means no contract. Is the quantity too indefinite?","review_truth":"A requirements term is a quantity term when it measures the buyer''s actual good-faith needs.","dominant_trap":"D","anchor_card":{"card_id":"RC-CONTRACTS-REQ-QTY-01","title":"Flexible quantity is not empty quantity","signal":"all goods the buyer requires","student_move":"Do not equate no fixed number with no quantity.","tiny_rule":"Requirements quantity is measured by good-faith needs.","trap":"Illusory-promise overread.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONTRACTS-REQUIREMENTS-01","statement":"A requirements term is a quantity term: it measures the buyer''s quantity by actual good-faith needs, not by a fixed number.","type":"rule","trigger":"all goods the buyer requires","authority":"UCC 2-306(1)","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONTRACTS-REQUIREMENTS-ARRAY-01","statement":"When three choices say no contract and the stem gives a requirements phrase, cut the no-contract reasons one at a time before assuming the missing number is fatal.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"No fixed number objection after an ''all I require'' phrase.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"gold_key_recall","target_skill":"requirements quantity","prompt":"A buyer offers to purchase all widgets her shop requires for six months. No fixed number is stated. Is the quantity automatically too indefinite?","answer":"No. Requirements quantity can be measured by good-faith needs."},{"drill_type":"overclaim_cut","target_skill":"tiered_absolute","prompt":"Cut or keep: ''Requirements quantities are always illusory.''","answer":"Cut: tiered_absolute."},{"drill_type":"wrong_frame","target_skill":"price quote vs offer","prompt":"Stem says buyer wrote ''I offer to buy'' and seller accepted. Choice says the writing was only a price quote. What is the C3 label?","answer":"NOT_RESPONSIVE / bait_doctrine / wrong_frame."},{"drill_type":"anchor_assisted_cut","target_skill":"adequacy review","prompt":"Choice says no contract unless the court finds the stated price adequate. What is the problem?","answer":"Fabricated adequacy gate; anchor-assisted cut."}]}'),
  ('ac21ec1c-a564-5e0b-a708-f4e3d5344c03', '20476_road_to_bethlehem_sash', 'CRIMINAL_LAW', 'Fourth Amendment', 'Constitutional Protection of Accused Persons', 'extra manipulation/immediate apparentness vs no-warrant-after-pocket-entry', 'After a city Christmas pageant called Road to Bethlehem, Officer Hannah lawfully stopped Peter on a public plaza near vendor tents where officers had recently seen illegal pill sales. Peter was still wearing his shepherd costume, including a robe with a small sash pocket. During a pat-down of Peter’s outer clothing for weapons, Hannah felt a pea-sized hard tablet in the sash pocket. She knew it was not a weapon. Curious, Hannah rolled and rubbed the tablet through the fabric for a few moments until she decided it felt like an illegal opioid pill. She then reached into the pocket and seized it. Peter moves to suppress.', 'After a city Christmas pageant called Road to Bethlehem, Officer Hannah lawfully stopped Peter on a public plaza near vendor tents where officers had recently seen illegal pill sales. Peter was still wearing his shepherd costume, including a robe with a small sash pocket. During a pat-down of Peter’s outer clothing for weapons, Hannah felt a pea-sized hard tablet in the sash pocket. She knew it was not a weapon. Curious, Hannah rolled and rubbed the tablet through the fabric for a few moments until she decided it felt like an illegal opioid pill. She then reached into the pocket and seized it. Peter moves to suppress.

Was the officer’s warrantless seizure of the pill valid under the plain-feel doctrine?', 'Was the officer’s warrantless seizure of the pill valid under the plain-feel doctrine?', 3, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20476.md","source_question_id":"20476","variant_slug":"road_to_bethlehem_sash","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"75070202","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"hard","deciding_phase":"CLASH","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":"The officer rolled and rubbed the tablet before deciding it was contraband.","distilled_core_question":"During a lawful weapons frisk, an officer feels a nonweapon object, manipulates it to identify contraband, then seizes it. Is the seizure valid under plain feel?","review_truth":"Plain feel allows seizure only when contraband is immediately apparent during the lawful frisk; extra manipulation of a nonweapon item breaks the search.","dominant_trap":"D","anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-CRIMINAL-PLAIN-FEEL-01","title":"Plain feel: immediate apparentness, not tactile investigation","signal":"Lawful frisk; nonweapon object; officer keeps feeling it to identify it.","student_move":"Stop the frisk at the moment the officer knows the item is not a weapon unless contraband is already immediately apparent.","tiny_rule":"Manipulation to identify contraband breaks plain feel.","trap":"Treating Terry frisk as permission to search for drugs.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CRIMINAL-PLAIN-FEEL-01","statement":"Plain feel works only when the contraband identity is immediately apparent during the lawful weapons frisk. If the officer must squeeze, roll, rub, or otherwise manipulate a nonweapon item to identify it, the frisk has become a new search.","type":"distinction","trigger":"Lawful frisk + nonweapon object + extra manipulation before identification","authority":"Minnesota v. Dickerson, 508 U.S. 366 (1993); Terry v. Ohio, 392 U.S. 1 (1968)","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CRIMINAL-PLAIN-FEEL-01","statement":"When two choices both say the seizure is invalid, ask which one names the first unlawful move. Here, the first unlawful move is the extra manipulation, not the later hand-in-pocket act.","type":"clash","trigger":"Two ''No'' answers with different defect rationales","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"overclaim","prompt":"During a lawful frisk, the answer says any item found may be seized. Name the mold.","answer":"tiered_absolute"},{"drill_type":"CLASH","target_skill":"same-result pair","prompt":"Two answers both say suppression. One names manipulation before identification; one names lack of warrant before pocket entry. Which fact resolves it?","answer":"The officer manipulated the nonweapon object before identifying contraband."},{"drill_type":"ANCHOR","target_skill":"plain feel immediate apparentness","prompt":"What must be true before an officer may seize contraband felt during a lawful frisk?","answer":"Its contraband character must be immediately apparent by touch."},{"drill_type":"CALL","target_skill":"call focus","prompt":"A choice justifies a drug search because the area was known for drug sales. Why is it off-call?","answer":"The call asks about plain-feel seizure validity, not drug-investigation suspicion."}]}'),
  ('f83f9b86-2b38-50ad-8f92-bbe89b6dbd99', '20493_fish_mark_picnic_lot', 'REAL_PROPERTY', 'Conveyance by Deed — Requirements for Valid Deed', 'B1', NULL, 'Esther owned a small vacant lot that she used for neighborhood Bible-memory picnics. After one picnic, she pulled a clean paper lunch wrapper from the basket and wrote, “I convey my vacant lot at 48 Galilee Court to Timothy.” Because Esther’s hands were too stiff to write her name, she drew a small fish symbol at the bottom intending it as her signature. She gave the wrapper to Timothy and said, “This little lot belongs to you today.” Timothy accepted. Timothy paid nothing, and nobody notarized or recorded the wrapper.', 'Esther owned a small vacant lot that she used for neighborhood Bible-memory picnics. After one picnic, she pulled a clean paper lunch wrapper from the basket and wrote, “I convey my vacant lot at 48 Galilee Court to Timothy.” Because Esther’s hands were too stiff to write her name, she drew a small fish symbol at the bottom intending it as her signature. She gave the wrapper to Timothy and said, “This little lot belongs to you today.” Timothy accepted. Timothy paid nothing, and nobody notarized or recorded the wrapper.

Who owns the lot?', 'Who owns the lot?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20493.md","source_question_id":"20493","variant_slug":"fish_mark_picnic_lot","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"84040201","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"core diagnostic","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"PURE_ANCHOR","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A landowner writes present transfer words identifying herself, the grantee, and the land, signs by an intended mark, delivers the writing, and the grantee accepts. No money is paid, and the writing is not notarized or recorded. Who owns the land?","review_truth":"A deed can be valid between grantor and grantee without a printed form, consideration, notarization, or recording if the minimal transfer formalities are present.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"RP-ANCHOR-01","title":"Minimal Deed Formalities","signal":"Informal writing, present transfer words, no money, no notarization, no recording.","student_move":"Ask whether the writing hits the minimum transfer elements before adding extra gates.","tiny_rule":"Writing + parties + land + present transfer + grantor authentication + delivery + acceptance is enough between the parties.","trap":"Contract or courthouse instincts add requirements the tested rule does not require.","confidence":"PURE_ANCHOR"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-REAL_PROPERTY-MINIMAL-DEED-FORMALITIES-01","statement":"A deed can be valid between grantor and grantee if it is a writing that identifies the grantor, grantee, land, and present transfer, is signed or otherwise authenticated by the grantor, and is delivered and accepted. No printed deed form, consideration, notarization, or recording is required for baseline validity between them.","type":"rule","trigger":"Who owns land after an informal written transfer with present words, mark, handoff, and acceptance?","authority":"Common-law/bar baseline; representative authorities include statutes and cases recognizing writing, subscription, delivery, signature by mark, no consideration requirement, and validity of unrecorded instruments between parties.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-REAL_PROPERTY-EXTRA-GATE-ARRAY-01","statement":"When three deed answers add formal extra gates and one answer lists the minimal transfer elements, treat the array as an extra-gate sweep after the deed-formality anchor.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"An informal writing plus present transfer words is followed by three formal-sounding objections.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"anchor_recall","target_skill":"minimal deed formalities","prompt":"A landowner writes present transfer words on ordinary paper, signs by an intended mark, delivers it, and the grantee accepts. No money is paid. Does title pass between them?","answer":"Yes, if the writing identifies grantor, grantee, land, present transfer, and is delivered and accepted."},{"drill_type":"trap_spotting","target_skill":"extra-gate sweep","prompt":"Which added gate is not required for baseline deed validity: printed deed form, consideration, recording, or all three?","answer":"All three are not required under the tested baseline."},{"drill_type":"crossover","target_skill":"recording acts separation","prompt":"What does recording usually protect that deed delivery already accomplished between the parties?","answer":"Recording protects notice/priority against third parties; delivery and acceptance transfer between the parties."}]}'),
  ('b2a91b44-8dc8-5e17-9624-e558967e5fdf', '20496_loaves_prop_cart', 'TORTS', 'Negligence', 'Damages / Collateral Source Rule', 'Who paid?', 'At Esther’s backyard “Loaves and Fishes” game night, Lydia was standing beside a table of Bible-verse prize cards when Daniel negligently lost control of a wheeled prop cart stacked with foam bread baskets. The cart struck Lydia and caused injuries that required emergency treatment. Lydia’s medical bills were substantial, but they were paid in full under a health-insurance policy that Lydia had purchased before the game night.

Lydia sued Daniel for negligence. The jury found for Lydia and awarded her $64,000. Before judgment was entered, Daniel’s lawyer made a proper motion asking the court to reduce the award by the amount Lydia had already received from her health insurer.

The motion to reduce Lydia’s damage award should be:', 'At Esther’s backyard “Loaves and Fishes” game night, Lydia was standing beside a table of Bible-verse prize cards when Daniel negligently lost control of a wheeled prop cart stacked with foam bread baskets. The cart struck Lydia and caused injuries that required emergency treatment. Lydia’s medical bills were substantial, but they were paid in full under a health-insurance policy that Lydia had purchased before the game night.

Lydia sued Daniel for negligence. The jury found for Lydia and awarded her $64,000. Before judgment was entered, Daniel’s lawyer made a proper motion asking the court to reduce the award by the amount Lydia had already received from her health insurer.

The motion to reduce Lydia’s damage award should be:

The motion to reduce Lydia''s damage award should be:', 'The motion to reduce Lydia''s damage award should be:', 3, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20496.md","source_question_id":"20496","variant_slug":"loaves_prop_cart","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"61020500","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium_hard","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"The plaintiff''s own insurer paid her medical bills before she won a negligence verdict. May the negligent defendant reduce the verdict by that insurance payment?","review_truth":"Plaintiff-side insurance is collateral; it does not reduce the negligent defendant''s damages judgment.","dominant_trap":"D","anchor_card":{"card_id":"TOR-REM-COLLATERAL-SOURCE-01","title":"Who paid?","signal":"Insurance payment before or after tort verdict.","student_move":"Sort the payer: defendant-side, joint-tortfeasor-side, or plaintiff-side.","tiny_rule":"Plaintiff-side insurance does not reduce the judgment.","trap":"Double recovery feels fair but points the wrong way.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-TORTS-COLLATERAL-SOURCE-01","statement":"Plaintiff-side insurance is collateral. A negligence defendant gets no credit for benefits paid by the plaintiff''s own insurer; credit is for payments by the defendant, someone acting for the defendant, or another actor subject to the same tort liability.","type":"rule","trigger":"Defendant asks to reduce a tort award because plaintiff''s own insurer already paid.","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 920A.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-TORTS-COLLATERAL-SOURCE-01","statement":"For setoff questions, ask who paid and whose liability the payer was tied to before reacting to double-recovery language.","type":"trap_spotting","trigger":"Motion to reduce damages after insurance or settlement payment.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"Gold Key recall","target_skill":"Collateral source rule","prompt":"A plaintiff''s own insurer paid medical bills before a tort verdict. Can the defendant reduce the verdict by that amount?","answer":"No. Plaintiff-side insurance is collateral."},{"drill_type":"Source sorting","target_skill":"Setoff payer classification","prompt":"Classify the payer: defendant, joint tortfeasor, plaintiff''s own insurer.","answer":"Only plaintiff''s own insurer is collateral."},{"drill_type":"Trap language","target_skill":"Fairness bait detection","prompt":"A choice says ''no double recovery.'' What question must you ask first?","answer":"Who paid, and whose liability was the payer tied to?"}]}'),
  ('28565236-896f-5b44-8503-93ec5ca156a9', '20516_manna_games_duress', 'CRIMINAL_LAW', 'Defenses', 'General Principles', 'imminent threshold vs real-but-incomplete fear/family facts', 'Timothy works the evening shift in the shipping room at Manna Games, a private company that sells Bible-themed board games and collector study-Bible sets. On Monday night, Barnabas, a collector with a reputation for using hired muscle, sends Timothy a video message from a retreat center three states away: ''Before the warehouse closes on Friday, take the locked case of gold-edition study Bibles worth $18,000 and leave it behind the Noah’s Ark mini-golf sign, or after next Sunday’s hymn-sing I will have your sister Hannah badly hurt.'' Timothy believes Barnabas is serious and is terrified for Hannah. On Thursday, Timothy takes the case. Charged with theft, Timothy asserts duress.', 'Timothy works the evening shift in the shipping room at Manna Games, a private company that sells Bible-themed board games and collector study-Bible sets. On Monday night, Barnabas, a collector with a reputation for using hired muscle, sends Timothy a video message from a retreat center three states away: ''Before the warehouse closes on Friday, take the locked case of gold-edition study Bibles worth $18,000 and leave it behind the Noah’s Ark mini-golf sign, or after next Sunday’s hymn-sing I will have your sister Hannah badly hurt.'' Timothy believes Barnabas is serious and is terrified for Hannah. On Thursday, Timothy takes the case. Charged with theft, Timothy asserts duress.

Should the court accept Timothy’s duress defense?', 'Should the court accept Timothy’s duress defense?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20516.md","source_question_id":"20516","variant_slug":"manna_games_duress","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"72060206","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium_high","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A worker commits theft after a remote person threatens to hurt his sister at a future time. Does duress succeed?","review_truth":"Duress is not fear alone; it requires an imminent human threat and no reasonable legal alternative.","dominant_trap":"D","anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-CRIM-DURESS-IMMINENCE","title":"Duress requires now-pressure.","signal":"Future threat, remote threatener, time to seek help.","student_move":"Cut fear-only and family-only answers; keep the answer that checks imminence.","tiny_rule":"Duress is immediate-threat plus no reasonable escape, not fear alone.","trap":"Sympathy for a believable family threat.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CRIMINAL-DURESS-IMMINENCE-01","statement":"Common-law duress needs an imminent human threat of death or serious bodily harm, directed at the defendant or a close family member, with no reasonable legal alternative.","type":"threshold","trigger":"Threat scheduled for later, threatener absent, time to seek help.","authority":"Common-law duress; United States v. Bailey and federal duress formulations.","last_minute_review":true},{"id":"GK-CRIMINAL-DURESS-SCOPE-02","statement":"Duress is not a violent-crime-only defense; theft is not excluded if the duress elements are met.","type":"exception","trigger":"Answer says duress is only for violent crimes.","authority":"Common-law duress scope; homicide limitation.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CRIMINAL-DURESS-ARRAY-01","statement":"When two Yes answers each name one sympathetic fact and a No answer names a missing threshold, treat the array as an element-completion test.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"Defense-success call with multiple partial-element answers.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"Gold Key recall","target_skill":"duress threshold","prompt":"A defendant steals after a threatener far away promises to hurt a sibling next week. Does duress succeed?","answer":"No. The threat is future and remote, so imminence is missing."},{"drill_type":"wrong_element cut","target_skill":"partial defense elements","prompt":"Choice says: ''Yes, because the threat was to a close family member.'' Cut or keep?","answer":"Cut as NOT_RESPONSIVE / wrong_element unless imminence and no escape are also satisfied."},{"drill_type":"tiered_absolute cut","target_skill":"scope overclaim","prompt":"Choice says: ''No, because duress is only for violent crimes.'' Cut or keep?","answer":"Cut as NOT_TRUE / tiered_absolute."}]}'),
  ('eb31e2d3-0f2f-5f47-9a19-c735af54fba0', '20523_easter_stage_guaranty', 'CONTRACTS', 'Statute of Frauds', 'D', 'extra formalities versus party-to-be-charged memorandum', 'After Peter failed to pay a private event-supply company for the portable stage and speakers used at a ticketed Easter concert at his bookstore café, the company sued Ruth. Before the rental, Ruth had orally told the company that she would pay Peter’s $18,400 rental bill if Peter did not. That evening, Ruth signed a note to Lydia, Peter’s concert scheduler, stating: “I told Galilee Gear Rentals I would cover Peter’s $18,400 stage-and-speaker rental if Peter does not pay it.” Ruth argues that the signed note cannot satisfy the Statute of Frauds because it was sent to Lydia, not to Galilee Gear Rentals.', 'After Peter failed to pay a private event-supply company for the portable stage and speakers used at a ticketed Easter concert at his bookstore café, the company sued Ruth. Before the rental, Ruth had orally told the company that she would pay Peter’s $18,400 rental bill if Peter did not. That evening, Ruth signed a note to Lydia, Peter’s concert scheduler, stating: “I told Galilee Gear Rentals I would cover Peter’s $18,400 stage-and-speaker rental if Peter does not pay it.” Ruth argues that the signed note cannot satisfy the Statute of Frauds because it was sent to Lydia, not to Galilee Gear Rentals.

Can Ruth’s signed note potentially satisfy the suretyship writing requirement?', 'Can Ruth’s signed note potentially satisfy the suretyship writing requirement?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20523.md","source_question_id":"20523","variant_slug":"easter_stage_guaranty","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"54030400","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"trap repair","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A surety signs a note to someone other than the creditor admitting she promised to pay another person’s debt if that person defaults. Can that note potentially satisfy the Statute of Frauds?","review_truth":"The Statute of Frauds memorandum test looks to the signer and content, not whether the writing was addressed to the enforcing party.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"CONTRACTS-ANCHOR-SOF-MEMO-ADDRESSEE-01","title":"SOF memorandum: signer beats addressee","signal":"A signed writing admits a guaranty but was sent to someone other than the enforcing creditor.","student_move":"Map who is being sued; then ask whether that person signed a writing identifying the promise.","tiny_rule":"The memorandum need not be addressed to the enforcing party.","trap":"extra formality","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONTRACTS-SURETY-MEMO-THIRD-PERSON-01","statement":"For a common-law Statute of Frauds memorandum, the writing need not be addressed or delivered to the enforcing party. Ask whether it is signed by the party to be charged and whether it identifies the promise, parties, and essential terms.","type":"distinction","trigger":"signed note admits a guaranty but was sent to someone other than the creditor","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts §§ 131, 133; lawyer-confirmation only","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONTRACTS-SURETY-MEMO-ARRAY-01","statement":"When three answers add formalities and one answer tracks the signer-plus-content path, group the formalities first. Then cut the addressee and extra-signature conditions as the same trap family.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"choices say must be addressed, must always be signed by both, or only if debtor signed","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"Gold Key recall","target_skill":"SOF memorandum sufficiency","prompt":"A guarantor signs a note to the debtor admitting the guaranty. The creditor sues. Does the note fail solely because it was addressed to the debtor?","answer":"No. Addressee is not the test; signer and content are."},{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"extra-formality detection","prompt":"Cut the answer: ''No, because both the debtor and guarantor must always sign the guaranty memorandum.''","answer":"CUT as tiered_absolute after the Gold Key; the party being charged must sign."},{"drill_type":"answer_array","target_skill":"formalities grouping","prompt":"Choices add creditor-addressing, both-signature, and debtor-signature requirements. What is the shared trap?","answer":"Extra formalities beyond the party-to-be-charged signed memorandum."}]}'),
  ('7405f59f-9110-5e72-a456-4505ca50bfbe', '20542_praise_festival_cashbox', 'CRIMINAL_LAW', 'Sixth Amendment', 'Constitutional Protection of Accused Persons', 'same-offense substance versus different label', 'Peter is indicted for armed robbery of Lydia''s traveling Scripture-calligraphy booth at a private praise-music festival, and counsel is appointed. Later, while counsel is absent, detectives visit Peter at his apartment. They say they only want to ask whether he committed ''the unarmed theft of the cash box from that same booth during the closing-night festival rush.'' They obtain no waiver. Peter admits that he took Lydia''s cash box during that incident.', 'Peter is indicted for armed robbery of Lydia''s traveling Scripture-calligraphy booth at a private praise-music festival, and counsel is appointed. Later, while counsel is absent, detectives visit Peter at his apartment. They say they only want to ask whether he committed ''the unarmed theft of the cash box from that same booth during the closing-night festival rush.'' They obtain no waiver. Peter admits that he took Lydia''s cash box during that incident.

Should Peter''s statement be suppressed under the Sixth Amendment?', 'Should Peter''s statement be suppressed under the Sixth Amendment?', 3, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20542.md","source_question_id":"20542","variant_slug":"praise_festival_cashbox","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"75090400","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"hard","deciding_phase":"CLASH","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":"The question concerned the same booth, same cash box, and same festival incident.","distilled_core_question":"After indictment and counsel, police ask without waiver about a relabeled lesser version of the same charged incident. Suppress?","review_truth":"After indictment, the Sixth Amendment follows the charged offense in substance, not the police''s new label.","dominant_trap":"D","anchor_card":{"card_id":"CRIM-REM-20542-SAME-OFFENSE","title":"Same offense, new label","signal":"Police say they only want to ask about a smaller version of the charged incident.","student_move":"Do not stop at the label. Compare the factual event and offense substance.","tiny_rule":"Post-charge Sixth Amendment protection follows the same offense in substance.","trap":"The label-only answer sounds precise but is too shallow.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CRIMINAL-MASSIAH-SAME-OFFENSE-01","statement":"After formal charge, the Sixth Amendment bars deliberate elicitation about the charged offense without counsel or waiver; a lesser-included or same-Blockburger relabeling stays inside the charged offense.","type":"distinction","trigger":"indictment/counsel appointed + police ask about a renamed version of the same incident","authority":"Massiah; Fellers; McNeil; Texas v. Cobb; Brown v. Ohio","last_minute_review":true},{"id":"GK-CRIMINAL-MASSIAH-CUSTODY-01","statement":"Sixth Amendment post-charge deliberate elicitation is not the same test as Fifth Amendment custodial interrogation.","type":"distinction","trigger":"Sixth Amendment call + post-indictment questioning","authority":"Fellers; Massiah","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CRIMINAL-LABEL-VS-SUBSTANCE-01","statement":"When the choices fight over crime labels, go back to the stem and ask whether the officers are really asking about the same charged event in a smaller costume.","type":"clash","trigger":"charged armed robbery followed by police asking about unarmed theft from the same event","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"Gold Key recall","target_skill":"Sixth Amendment attachment and same-offense boundary","prompt":"After indictment, police ask without counsel about a relabeled lesser version of the charged incident. What is the trigger?","answer":"Same charged offense in substance after attachment; suppress without counsel or waiver."},{"drill_type":"Wrong-frame CUT","target_skill":"Miranda vs Massiah","prompt":"A choice says no suppression because the defendant was not in custody. The call is Sixth Amendment after indictment. Cut or keep?","answer":"Cut as bait_doctrine / wrong_frame."},{"drill_type":"CLASH axis","target_skill":"same offense versus different label","prompt":"One answer says same charged offense; another says different labels. What fact decides?","answer":"Whether the questioning targets the same incident and same offense in substance."},{"drill_type":"Overclaim scan","target_skill":"tiered_absolute","prompt":"Police may not question an indicted defendant about any unrelated offense. What is the mold?","answer":"tiered_absolute; the Sixth Amendment right is offense-specific."}]}'),
  ('bfc302af-90b9-5aa8-b232-f6d984d4af83', '20546_jericho_wall_studio', 'TORTS', 'Negligence', 'Premises liability / invitee dangerous condition', 'named-defendant premises breach vs adjacent product/contractor/status facts', 'Lydia owned Selah Studio, a private Bible-verse calligraphy studio open to the public for paid lettering workshops and sales of framed Scripture cards. After closing, Lydia hired Barnabas to install a motorized “Jericho Wall” display in the front room. When a participant stepped on a pressure mat, wooden panels were supposed to slide apart to reveal the day’s featured verse.

Barnabas read the manufacturer’s instructions but disregarded the warnings. When he finished, he did not test the display by stepping on the pressure mat, but told Ruth, Lydia’s night manager, that the installation was complete. The next morning, when Selah Studio opened, Mary entered and was struck by one of the moving panels. Mary asserted a claim for her injuries against Lydia.', 'Lydia owned Selah Studio, a private Bible-verse calligraphy studio open to the public for paid lettering workshops and sales of framed Scripture cards. After closing, Lydia hired Barnabas to install a motorized “Jericho Wall” display in the front room. When a participant stepped on a pressure mat, wooden panels were supposed to slide apart to reveal the day’s featured verse.

Barnabas read the manufacturer’s instructions but disregarded the warnings. When he finished, he did not test the display by stepping on the pressure mat, but told Ruth, Lydia’s night manager, that the installation was complete. The next morning, when Selah Studio opened, Mary entered and was struck by one of the moving panels. Mary asserted a claim for her injuries against Lydia.

Which one of the following additional facts or inferences, if it was the only one true, would be most likely to result in a judgment for Mary?', 'Which one of the following additional facts or inferences, if it was the only one true, would be most likely to result in a judgment for Mary?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20546.md","source_question_id":"20546","variant_slug":"jericho_wall_studio","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"61020101","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium_high","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A public business visitor is injured by a newly installed moving device. Which added fact most supports judgment against the owner: defect, contractor non-negligence, reasonable owner inspection, or completed payment?","review_truth":"The winning premises-liability fact links the owner to a danger discoverable by reasonable inspection.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-TORTS-NAMED-DEFENDANT-INSPECTION-01","title":"Owner-inspection fact beats product/contractor/status bait","signal":"Premises owner is sued after a third party installed a dangerous device.","student_move":"Ask which choice supplies breach by the named owner.","tiny_rule":"For invitees, reasonable inspection can be the breach path.","trap":"Product defect and contractor conduct feel causal but do not answer owner breach.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-TORTS-JERICHO-WALL-01","statement":"For a business invitee, owner liability turns on dangerous conditions the owner knew about or would discover by reasonable care, plus failure to protect.","type":"rule","trigger":"public-facing business premises plus customer injury from a condition on the premises","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Torts §§ 332, 343","last_minute_review":true},{"id":"GK-TORTS-PRODUCT-SELLER-01","statement":"A product defect alone points to strict products liability only when the defendant is in the business of selling that type of product.","type":"threshold","trigger":"injury from a defective component but defendant is sued as premises owner","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 402A","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-TORTS-JERICHO-WALL-01","statement":"Lock the named defendant before reading the added facts: the winning fact must create liability for Lydia, not for Barnabas or the product chain.","type":"cut","trigger":"one defendant named in the call, but choices mention product defect, contractor conduct, and customer payment","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"named-defendant filtering","prompt":"Premises owner is sued; one choice says the contractor was careful. Cut or keep?","answer":"Cut as NOT_RESPONSIVE unless it supplies owner breach."},{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"wrong_frame detection","prompt":"Premises owner is sued; one choice says the device had a manufacturing defect. What is the trap?","answer":"Product frame; it needs seller status or owner-discoverability."},{"drill_type":"ANCHOR","target_skill":"invitee inspection duty","prompt":"What owner-side fact most supports an invitee premises claim?","answer":"The owner knew or reasonable inspection would have discovered the dangerous condition."},{"drill_type":"CALIBRATION","target_skill":"anchor-assisted clean cut","prompt":"Three choices miss named-defendant breach; one gives reasonable inspection by owner. Confidence?","answer":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED; commit after call/read check."}]}'),
  ('51038e24-e533-5032-bcb7-c1daf5e985cc', '20556_mustard_seed_puppet_caravan', 'CONTRACTS', 'Performance, Breach, and Discharge', 'Prospective inability to perform; reliance before cure', 'later readiness / label-only answer vs reliance before cure', 'Martha owns and operates Mustard Seed Puppet Caravan, a private for-profit company that tours Bible-story puppet shows for families. The caravan hires only one full-time performer-driver for each tour season because that person travels with the caravan, sleeps in its bunk compartment, receives meals, drives between towns, and maintains the puppets and sound equipment. On September 3, Stephen entered into a valid written contract with Martha. Under the contract, Stephen was to begin traveling with the caravan on January 10 and continue until July 10. Martha was to pay Stephen $18,500 at the end of the season, and Stephen was to receive meals and bunk space during the tour. On September 18, Stephen signed a three-year contract to join an overseas gospel recording tour. That tour was scheduled to begin on December 20. A notice of Stephen''s new tour contract appeared in the "Praise Notes" column of a local arts newsletter and was read by Martha''s managers. Martha immediately began looking for another performer-driver. On December 5, Martha entered into a valid written contract with Barnabas on terms identical to Stephen''s contract. On December 12, Stephen failed the overseas tour''s health-clearance requirement and was released from that contract. On January 10, Stephen arrived at Martha''s garage ready to travel with the caravan, but Martha told him that his services were not needed.', 'Martha owns and operates Mustard Seed Puppet Caravan, a private for-profit company that tours Bible-story puppet shows for families. The caravan hires only one full-time performer-driver for each tour season because that person travels with the caravan, sleeps in its bunk compartment, receives meals, drives between towns, and maintains the puppets and sound equipment. On September 3, Stephen entered into a valid written contract with Martha. Under the contract, Stephen was to begin traveling with the caravan on January 10 and continue until July 10. Martha was to pay Stephen $18,500 at the end of the season, and Stephen was to receive meals and bunk space during the tour. On September 18, Stephen signed a three-year contract to join an overseas gospel recording tour. That tour was scheduled to begin on December 20. A notice of Stephen''s new tour contract appeared in the "Praise Notes" column of a local arts newsletter and was read by Martha''s managers. Martha immediately began looking for another performer-driver. On December 5, Martha entered into a valid written contract with Barnabas on terms identical to Stephen''s contract. On December 12, Stephen failed the overseas tour''s health-clearance requirement and was released from that contract. On January 10, Stephen arrived at Martha''s garage ready to travel with the caravan, but Martha told him that his services were not needed.

If Stephen asserts a claim against Martha for damages resulting from breach of contract, the court should find for whom?', 'If Stephen asserts a claim against Martha for damages resulting from breach of contract, the court should find for whom?', 3, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20556.md","source_question_id":"20556","variant_slug":"mustard_seed_puppet_caravan","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"56050403","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"hard","deciding_phase":"CLASH","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":"Martha hired Barnabas on December 5; Stephen was released on December 12.","distilled_core_question":"A service contractor made himself apparently unable to perform, the other party reasonably hired a replacement before the obstacle disappeared, and the contractor later appeared ready. Who wins the breach claim?","review_truth":"Later readiness does not save the claimant when the other party reasonably relied on apparent future inability before the obstacle disappeared.","dominant_trap":"B","anchor_card":{"card_id":"CONTRACTS-ANCHOR-PI-01","title":"Prospective inability: reliance before cure","signal":"Future performance appears impossible, then later becomes possible.","student_move":"Ask whether the other party changed position before the cure.","tiny_rule":"Reliance before cure locks the defendant-side discharge.","trap":"Later readiness feels decisive, but it is late.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONTRACTS-PROSPECTIVE-INABILITY-01","statement":"If a party''s own conduct makes future performance reasonably appear impossible, the other side can be discharged when it materially changes position in reliance before the obstacle disappears.","type":"distinction","trigger":"Outside commitment begins before contract performance; replacement hired before later release.","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts §§ 250, 253, 256.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONTRACTS-RELIANCE-TIMING-01","statement":"In a future-performance clash, prefer the answer that states the reliance/timing fact over the answer that gives only a legal label.","type":"clash","trigger":"Two defendant answers remain; one says only ''anticipatory repudiation,'' the other says reliance.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"timeline_clash","target_skill":"CLASH","prompt":"Replacement hired December 5; outside commitment released December 12. Which date controls?","answer":"December 5 controls because reliance came before cure."},{"drill_type":"label_vs_fact","target_skill":"Issue-Sense","prompt":"One answer says ''repudiation''; another says ''hired replacement in reliance.'' Which is stronger?","answer":"The reliance answer."},{"drill_type":"wrong_timing_cut","target_skill":"CUT","prompt":"A performer is ready on the start date after the company already hired a replacement. What is the trap?","answer":"Later readiness."},{"drill_type":"notice_lure","target_skill":"CUT","prompt":"The performer never personally warned the company, but the company learned from a reliable notice. What is the trap?","answer":"Direct-notice wrong element."}]}'),
  ('ecd6b6fc-0a55-5b04-86a7-6a03e19d75e9', '20565_purple_gear_hymnbook_tote', 'EVIDENCE', 'Laying a Foundation', 'II.B', 'foundation gate vs jury weight', 'In a federal products-liability action over a battery-powered fog machine used in a private Christmas pageant, Lydia offers a cracked purple plastic gear that Stephen found three weeks later inside a hymnbook tote at Martha’s rented banquet hall. Stephen did not see it come from the fog machine; no witness recognizes it; no logo, serial number, or marking ties it to the fog machine; and no evidence shows how it got into the tote. Lydia says the jury can decide what to make of it. The manufacturer objects. How should the court rule?', 'In a federal products-liability action over a battery-powered fog machine used in a private Christmas pageant, Lydia offers a cracked purple plastic gear that Stephen found three weeks later inside a hymnbook tote at Martha’s rented banquet hall. Stephen did not see it come from the fog machine; no witness recognizes it; no logo, serial number, or marking ties it to the fog machine; and no evidence shows how it got into the tote. Lydia says the jury can decide what to make of it. The manufacturer objects. How should the court rule?

How should the court rule?', 'How should the court rule?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20565.md","source_question_id":"20565","variant_slug":"purple_gear_hymnbook_tote","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"32020200","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"core","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"Plaintiff offers an unidentified object with no witness, marking, or circumstance connecting it to the product or accident. Should the court admit it for the jury to weigh, or exclude it for lack of foundation?","review_truth":"A jury cannot weigh a physical object until the proponent supplies a threshold link connecting it to the case.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"RM-EVI-FOUNDATION-GATE-01","title":"Gate Before Weight","signal":"Unidentified object; no witness; no marking; no chain/source fact.","student_move":"Cut weight arguments until a threshold link exists.","tiny_rule":"A jury weighs weak evidence; it does not supply the first connection.","trap":"Let the jury decide.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-EVIDENCE-FOUNDATION-GATE-01","statement":"A physical object does not reach the jury on ''maybe.'' The proponent must first supply some link supporting a finding that the object is what it claims or is connected to the case.","type":"threshold","trigger":"An object is offered, but the stem gives no identifying witness, no marks, and no source facts.","authority":"FRE 901(a); FRE 104(b).","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-EVIDENCE-FOUNDATION-GATE-01","statement":"When a choice says the jury can weigh it, first ask whether the item crossed the foundation gate. No gate, no weight.","type":"call_focus","trigger":"The proponent says the jury can decide what to make of an unidentified object.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"weight-before-foundation","prompt":"An unidentified object is offered with no witness, marking, or source fact. One answer says admit because the opponent can argue weight. Cut or keep?","answer":"Cut. Weight comes after foundation."},{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"burden direction","prompt":"An answer says exclude only if the opponent proves the object came from somewhere else. What is the problem?","answer":"Backwards. The proponent must first show a connection."},{"drill_type":"EAR","target_skill":"tiered absolute","prompt":"An answer says any physical object may be considered by the jury. What is the mold?","answer":"tiered_absolute; it erases the foundation requirement."},{"drill_type":"CALL","target_skill":"threshold before weight","prompt":"Two choices survive: one says weight, one says no foundation. Which layer controls?","answer":"No foundation controls before weight."}]}'),
  ('8205c8ec-4a00-5531-9635-6921258bc689', '20567_psalm_panels_venue', 'CIVIL_PROCEDURE', 'Erie Doctrine and Choice of Law', 'Federal Statute Directly Controls', 'valid federal statute directly controls vs Erie/state-law-policy bait', 'Mary, a citizen of State Jordan, runs a private business that rents hand-painted “Psalms & Parables” stage panels for paid community programs. She sued Barnabas, a citizen of State Carmel, in the U.S. District Court for the Northern District of State Carmel for breach of a paid restoration contract involving those panels. A State Carmel statute provides that a civil action may be filed only in the county where the defendant resides. Barnabas resides in a county located in the Southern District of State Carmel. Federal venue statute § 1391 provides that venue is proper in a federal district where a substantial part of the events or omissions giving rise to the claim occurred. The design meetings, payment, restoration work, and disputed delivery all occurred in the Northern District.', 'Mary, a citizen of State Jordan, runs a private business that rents hand-painted “Psalms & Parables” stage panels for paid community programs. She sued Barnabas, a citizen of State Carmel, in the U.S. District Court for the Northern District of State Carmel for breach of a paid restoration contract involving those panels. A State Carmel statute provides that a civil action may be filed only in the county where the defendant resides. Barnabas resides in a county located in the Southern District of State Carmel. Federal venue statute § 1391 provides that venue is proper in a federal district where a substantial part of the events or omissions giving rise to the claim occurred. The design meetings, payment, restoration work, and disputed delivery all occurred in the Northern District.

Which venue rule should the federal court apply?', 'Which venue rule should the federal court apply?', 3, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20567.md","source_question_id":"20567","variant_slug":"psalm_panels_venue","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"96060100","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium-hard","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"Diversity case in federal court. State venue rule points to defendant’s county. § 1391 points to the federal district where the events occurred. The events occurred there. Which venue rule controls?","review_truth":"When a valid federal statute directly governs federal venue, apply that statute before Erie forum-shopping analysis.","dominant_trap":"D","anchor_card":{"card_id":"CIV-REM-ERIE-FED-STATUTE-01","title":"Federal statute on point beats Erie balancing","signal":"Diversity + state rule + federal statute directly addressing procedure.","student_move":"Ask whether the federal statute directly controls before discussing forum shopping.","tiny_rule":"Federal statute on point first; Erie twin aims only after no controlling federal law.","trap":"State-law claim language makes the state procedural rule feel controlling.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-FED-STATUTE-VENUE-01","statement":"When a valid federal statute directly controls a federal-court procedural issue, apply the federal statute before Erie twin-aims balancing.","type":"rule","trigger":"Diversity case + state procedural rule + federal statute on point.","authority":"28 U.S.C. § 1391; Hanna v. Plumer; Stewart Organization v. Ricoh.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-RULE-SOURCE-CALL-01","statement":"On a ''which rule controls'' call, do not pick the rule that creates the preferred outcome. First identify whether a federal statute directly answers the procedural question.","type":"call_focus","trigger":"Answer choices compare state rule, federal statute, and forum-shopping policy.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"Federal-statute-on-point anchor","prompt":"Diversity case; state service rule conflicts with valid Federal Rule. Which source controls?","answer":"Federal rule/statute on point controls if valid."},{"drill_type":"trap_spotting","target_skill":"State-law-claim bait","prompt":"Choice says state procedure controls because the claim arises under state law. Name the trap.","answer":"Bait doctrine / wrong frame."},{"drill_type":"call_focus","target_skill":"Rule-source call","prompt":"Call asks which rule controls. One answer picks the rule that would dismiss. Cut or keep?","answer":"Cut if it answers outcome instead of source-of-rule."},{"drill_type":"anchor_recall","target_skill":"§ 1391 venue","prompt":"What federal district is proper under § 1391(b)(2)?","answer":"A district where a substantial part of the events or omissions giving rise to the claim occurred."}]}'),
  ('30a7b8e0-c813-5d32-a2fe-104e935f29aa', '20569_ark_float', 'CONTRACTS', 'Conditions — Express conditions', 'V-A2', 'exact stated terminating event vs neighboring contract doctrines', 'Lydia rented her hand-painted Noah’s Ark parade float to Ruth for a private neighborhood Bible-verse parade. The rental agreement stated, “The float rental remains effective unless the city engineer limits the float’s permitted load below 1,200 pounds before parade day.” The day before the parade, the city engineer issued an order limiting the float’s permitted load to 700 pounds. Lydia refused to provide the float.', 'Lydia rented her hand-painted Noah’s Ark parade float to Ruth for a private neighborhood Bible-verse parade. The rental agreement stated, “The float rental remains effective unless the city engineer limits the float’s permitted load below 1,200 pounds before parade day.” The day before the parade, the city engineer issued an order limiting the float’s permitted load to 700 pounds. Lydia refused to provide the float.

Which statement is most accurate?', 'Which statement is most accurate?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20569.md","source_question_id":"20569","variant_slug":"ark_float","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"56050102","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"core diagnostic","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A contract says an existing rental ends if a specified official order lowers a safety limit below a set threshold before the event. The order happens. Did the owner breach by refusing to provide the item?","review_truth":"An existing contract duty can be discharged by a later ''unless'' event stated in the contract.","dominant_trap":"A","anchor_card":{"card_id":"RM-CONTRACTS-CONDITION-SUBSEQUENT-01","title":"Unless-trigger cuts off duty","signal":"Contract says ''remains effective unless'' or similar.","student_move":"Track the exact trigger and timing before defaulting to breach, impossibility, or substantial performance.","tiny_rule":"If the terminating event happened, the duty is cut off.","trap":"treating initial effectiveness as permanent","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONTRACTS-ARK-FLOAT-01","statement":"A condition subsequent is a later event that cuts off an existing contractual duty when the contract says that event will end the duty.","type":"rule","trigger":"contract says remains effective unless a later event occurs","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts §230; condition subsequent / event that terminates a duty","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONTRACTS-ARK-FLOAT-01","statement":"When a contract gives an exact ''unless'' trigger, do not let choices swap in a broader doctrine like impossibility or substantial performance.","type":"trap_spotting","trigger":"answer uses a different doctrine than the clause''s stated event","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"wrong-frame trigger swap","prompt":"A contract says a duty remains effective unless Event X happens. Event X happens. A choice says breach unless performance was impossible. Cut or keep?","answer":"Cut. It swaps Event X for impossibility."},{"drill_type":"anchor","target_skill":"condition subsequent","prompt":"What does a later ''unless'' event do to an existing duty when the contract says it ends the duty?","answer":"It discharges the duty."},{"drill_type":"call_prediction","target_skill":"timeline discipline","prompt":"The contract was effective when signed, but the stated later terminating event occurred before performance. Which fact is dispositive?","answer":"The later terminating event."}]}'),
  ('c5f29e7c-8277-5dcf-bbeb-a986a8ef9652', '20586_psalms_hormone_use', 'CONSTITUTIONAL_LAW', 'Equal Protection', 'Medical-use classifications', NULL, 'At a private ''Psalms-and-Paintbrushes'' youth retreat, Hannah brings her 16-year-old son Timothy to Dr. Luke after Timothy''s endocrinologist recommends a particular hormone medication for a diagnosis listed in the state medical code. A state statute prohibits physicians from prescribing that hormone medication to minors for that listed diagnosis, but it permits physicians to prescribe the same medication to minors for other diagnoses. The prohibition applies to every minor seeking the barred use. The text of the statute does not divide patients into male and female categories. Timothy challenges the statute under the Equal Protection Clause.', 'At a private ''Psalms-and-Paintbrushes'' youth retreat, Hannah brings her 16-year-old son Timothy to Dr. Luke after Timothy''s endocrinologist recommends a particular hormone medication for a diagnosis listed in the state medical code. A state statute prohibits physicians from prescribing that hormone medication to minors for that listed diagnosis, but it permits physicians to prescribe the same medication to minors for other diagnoses. The prohibition applies to every minor seeking the barred use. The text of the statute does not divide patients into male and female categories. Timothy challenges the statute under the Equal Protection Clause.

What is the key Equal Protection threshold question?', 'What is the key Equal Protection threshold question?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20586.md","source_question_id":"20586","variant_slug":"psalms_hormone_use","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"44040305","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium_high","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"HARD_STRUCTURAL","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A state bars one hormone medication for minors for one medical use, allows the same medication for other uses, and does not classify male/female on its face. What classification does the Equal Protection analysis start with?","review_truth":"On an Equal Protection threshold question, classify the line the law actually draws before arguing about scrutiny.","dominant_trap":"D","anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-CONLAW-CLASSIFICATION-FIRST-20586","title":"Classification First, Scrutiny Second","signal":"Answer choices jump to strict scrutiny, rights in every context, or automatic discrimination.","student_move":"Name the line the law draws before choosing a tier.","tiny_rule":"Equal Protection starts with classification.","trap":"Policy-disagreement and same-medication facts are not automatic scrutiny triggers.","confidence":"HARD_STRUCTURAL"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONSTITUTIONAL_LAW-MEDICAL-USE-01","statement":"A statute that bars the same medication only for a specified medical use and age group is not automatically a sex classification. Start with the classification the law actually draws.","type":"distinction","trigger":"same medication allowed for some diagnoses but barred for minors for one diagnosis","authority":"United States v. Skrmetti, 605 U.S. 495 (2025); Massachusetts Board of Retirement v. Murgia; Vacco v. Quill","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONSTITUTIONAL_LAW-PSALMS-HORMONE-01","statement":"On an Equal Protection threshold call, cut answers that jump straight to automatic scrutiny. The answer that names the classification actually drawn is the responsive lane.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"call asks key Equal Protection threshold question","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"overclaim_cut","target_skill":"automatic-trigger spotting","prompt":"Which phrase makes this answer structurally suspect: ''bad medical policy automatically triggers strict scrutiny''?","answer":"automatically triggers"},{"drill_type":"classification_first","target_skill":"Equal Protection threshold framing","prompt":"Before choosing scrutiny, what must you identify in an Equal Protection question?","answer":"The classification the law actually draws."},{"drill_type":"gold_key_recall","target_skill":"medical-use versus sex classification","prompt":"Same medication, different permitted uses. Does that fact alone automatically prove sex discrimination?","answer":"No. Start with the classification drawn by the law."}]}'),
  ('12e57f2b-e3e4-5efe-bd60-91c1c5bea738', '20594_daniels_whale_float', 'TORTS', 'Negligence', 'Duty; foreseeable rescuers; rescue doctrine', 'foreseeable rescue versus actual awareness', 'For a private Bible-story block party in his driveway, Daniel built a wheeled “Jonah’s whale” float. The night before the party, Daniel climbed inside the float to test the hatch on his sloped driveway, even though he had not set the brake or chocked the wheels. The float began rolling downhill toward a retention pond with Daniel trapped inside. Lydia saw the float rolling, ran to pull open the hatch before it reached the water, and the hatch spring snapped back and broke her wrist. Daniel did not see Lydia approach before she was hurt.', 'For a private Bible-story block party in his driveway, Daniel built a wheeled “Jonah’s whale” float. The night before the party, Daniel climbed inside the float to test the hatch on his sloped driveway, even though he had not set the brake or chocked the wheels. The float began rolling downhill toward a retention pond with Daniel trapped inside. Lydia saw the float rolling, ran to pull open the hatch before it reached the water, and the hatch spring snapped back and broke her wrist. Daniel did not see Lydia approach before she was hurt.

If Lydia asserts a negligence claim against Daniel for the injury she sustained, the court should find for:', 'If Lydia asserts a negligence claim against Daniel for the injury she sustained, the court should find for:', 3, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20594.md","source_question_id":"20594","variant_slug":"daniels_whale_float","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"61020102","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium-hard","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A defendant carelessly creates danger to himself; a rescuer is injured while trying to save him. Can the rescuer recover from the defendant?","review_truth":"Danger invites rescue: negligent danger to human life can make the rescuer a foreseeable plaintiff.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-TORTS-RESCUE-20594","title":"Danger invites rescue, not automatic indemnity","signal":"Rescuer injured while responding to apparent human peril.","student_move":"Ask whether the defendant''s unreasonable conduct created the danger; then reject actual-awareness and automatic-payment traps.","tiny_rule":"Rescue is foreseeable when negligent danger creates apparent human peril.","trap":"Treating rescue as either meddling or automatic reimbursement.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-TORTS-DANGER-INVITES-RESCUE-01","statement":"Danger invites rescue: when a person negligently creates apparent human peril, even to himself, a reasonable rescue attempt is foreseeable and the rescuer may recover for rescue injuries caused by that negligence.","type":"rule","trigger":"rescuer injured while trying to save someone from danger the defendant negligently created","authority":"Wagner v. International Railway Co., 232 N.Y. 176 (1921); self-peril applications recognized in later rescue-doctrine cases.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-TORTS-BECAUSE-CLAUSE-01","statement":"In a party-result answer set, test the because-clause before choosing the winner. A correct party with a wrong reason is still out.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"choices split between plaintiff/defendant and each uses ''because''","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"Gold Key recall","target_skill":"rescue doctrine","prompt":"A negligent defendant creates apparent danger to himself, and a rescuer is injured. State the rescue rule.","answer":"Danger invites rescue; reasonable rescue is foreseeable, and the rescuer may recover for injury caused by the negligent peril."},{"drill_type":"answer-array drill","target_skill":"because-clause testing","prompt":"Two answers choose the same party but give different reasons. What do you test first?","answer":"The because-clause."},{"drill_type":"wrong-element drill","target_skill":"actual awareness versus foreseeability","prompt":"The defendant did not know a rescuer would appear. Is that the rescue-doctrine element?","answer":"No. The key is whether rescue was foreseeable from the danger."}]}'),
  ('70c4dded-cb25-5940-8e6f-e80c599f237a', '20597_olivewood_nativity_craft_swap', 'CRIMINAL_LAW', 'Defenses', 'General Principles', 'fact mistake versus law mistake', 'At a community Advent craft swap, Stephen sees a hand-carved olivewood nativity figurine on a table beside small giveaway cards printed with Bible verses. A sign on the table says, “Free verse cards—take one.” Stephen honestly believes the figurine itself has been left as part of the giveaway and takes it home intending to keep it. The figurine actually belongs to Ruth, who had set it down briefly while stacking programs. Stephen is charged with larceny, a specific intent crime requiring intent to permanently deprive another of property.

Later, at the same event, Mary sees Paul set down a similar olivewood figurine while he helps lead the closing song. Mary knows the figurine belongs to someone at the event, but she mistakenly believes state law permits a person to keep any item left unattended on a public craft-swap table after the closing song. Mary takes it intending to keep it. Mary is also charged with larceny; her belief about the law was wrong.', 'At a community Advent craft swap, Stephen sees a hand-carved olivewood nativity figurine on a table beside small giveaway cards printed with Bible verses. A sign on the table says, “Free verse cards—take one.” Stephen honestly believes the figurine itself has been left as part of the giveaway and takes it home intending to keep it. The figurine actually belongs to Ruth, who had set it down briefly while stacking programs. Stephen is charged with larceny, a specific intent crime requiring intent to permanently deprive another of property.

Later, at the same event, Mary sees Paul set down a similar olivewood figurine while he helps lead the closing song. Mary knows the figurine belongs to someone at the event, but she mistakenly believes state law permits a person to keep any item left unattended on a public craft-swap table after the closing song. Mary takes it intending to keep it. Mary is also charged with larceny; her belief about the law was wrong.

Which of the following correctly analyzes both prosecutions?', 'Which of the following correctly analyzes both prosecutions?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20597.md","source_question_id":"20597","variant_slug":"olivewood_nativity_craft_swap","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"72060103","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium-high anchor-dependent","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"One larceny defendant honestly mistakes property as abandoned/giveaway; another knows it belongs to someone but misunderstands the law. Which one has a defense?","review_truth":"For specific-intent larceny, an honest fact mistake can negate intent; a law mistake usually cannot.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"CRIM-REM-MISTAKE-FACT-LAW-01","title":"Fact mistake / law mistake split","signal":"One actor misunderstands ownership facts; another understands facts but misunderstands legality.","student_move":"First label each mistake as fact or law, then apply the specific-intent Gold Key.","tiny_rule":"Specific-intent fact mistake can negate intent; law mistake generally cannot.","trap":"Treating all sincere mistakes the same or requiring reasonableness for every mistake.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CRIMINAL-MISTAKE-FACT-LAW-01","statement":"For a specific intent crime, an honest mistake of fact can negate the required specific intent even if the mistake is unreasonable.","type":"distinction","trigger":"The stem says specific intent and the defendant honestly thinks the property is abandoned or free.","authority":"Morissette v. United States; DOJ Justice Manual larceny formulation; Cornell Wex mistake-of-fact summary.","last_minute_review":true},{"id":"GK-CRIMINAL-MISTAKE-FACT-LAW-02","statement":"Mistake of law generally does not excuse criminal conduct; sincerity alone is not enough.","type":"exception","trigger":"The defendant knows the object belongs to someone but misunderstands whether taking it is legal.","authority":"Cheek v. United States; Justia mistake-of-law summary.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CRIMINAL-FACT-LAW-MATRIX-01","statement":"Read the answer array as two switches: Stephen''s mistake is about a fact; Mary''s mistake is about the law. The credited answer must split them, not treat both defendants the same.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"Two scenarios plus choices that say both guilty, both not guilty, and two split answers.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"matrix_read","target_skill":"answer_array","prompt":"One defendant mistakenly thinks property is free; another knows it belongs to someone but misunderstands the law. Which outcome pair should you expect?","answer":"Fact-mistake defendant not guilty if intent is negated; law-mistake defendant guilty."},{"drill_type":"gold_key_recall","target_skill":"anchor","prompt":"For a specific-intent crime, must an honest fact mistake be reasonable?","answer":"No. It must honestly negate the specific intent."},{"drill_type":"trap_cut","target_skill":"wrong_frame","prompt":"Cut: ''Both are guilty because only reasonable mistakes count.''","answer":"Wrong frame. Specific-intent fact mistake does not require reasonableness."}]}'),
  ('a509b787-a938-5a66-9779-fbb8aa1aa3be', '20598_write_it_on_your_heart', 'TORTS', 'Privacy', 'Appropriation of identity', 'identity-use/commercial-consent answer vs extra-condition traps', 'Source row: BARMATRIX Q# 20598, Torts, Privacy, appropriation of identity. The source row tests whether unauthorized commercial use of a clearly recognizable identity can support appropriation even without exact name or photograph. 

Final Christian variation — `20598_write_it_on_your_heart`

Ruth is a nationally known Christian calligraphy teacher whose short devotional videos are instantly recognized by her turquoise headscarf, sand-timer necklace, whispery cadence, spiral hand-lettering flourish, and catchphrase, “write it on your heart.” Peter’s private Bible-memory app company hired Lydia, an actress and animator, to recreate those traits in a paid social-media ad for the app. The ad never used Ruth’s legal name or photograph, and Ruth did not consent. Commenters overwhelmingly understood the ad’s guide character to be Ruth. Ruth sues Peter’s company for appropriation.

Call:', 'Source row: BARMATRIX Q# 20598, Torts, Privacy, appropriation of identity. The source row tests whether unauthorized commercial use of a clearly recognizable identity can support appropriation even without exact name or photograph. 

Final Christian variation — `20598_write_it_on_your_heart`

Ruth is a nationally known Christian calligraphy teacher whose short devotional videos are instantly recognized by her turquoise headscarf, sand-timer necklace, whispery cadence, spiral hand-lettering flourish, and catchphrase, “write it on your heart.” Peter’s private Bible-memory app company hired Lydia, an actress and animator, to recreate those traits in a paid social-media ad for the app. The ad never used Ruth’s legal name or photograph, and Ruth did not consent. Commenters overwhelmingly understood the ad’s guide character to be Ruth. Ruth sues Peter’s company for appropriation.

