Should a court rule for Ruth?', 'Should a court rule for Ruth?', 3, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20598.md","source_question_id":"20598","variant_slug":"write_it_on_your_heart","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"62050402","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium-hard","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A company uses a clearly recognizable imitation of a person’s identity in a product ad without consent and without using the person’s name or photograph. Does appropriation lie?","review_truth":"Appropriation can rest on unauthorized commercial use of recognizable identity; exact name/photo, insult, and false statement are not required.","dominant_trap":"D","anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-TORTS-APPROPRIATION-IDENTITY-01","title":"Name/photo trap in appropriation","signal":"The stem says no exact name or photograph, but many identity cues are copied.","student_move":"Do not require exact name/photo. Ask whether the ad clearly uses the plaintiff’s identity for commercial advantage without consent.","tiny_rule":"Recognizable commercial evocation can be enough.","trap":"Exact-name/photo-only answer.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-TORTS-APPROPRIATION-IDENTITY-01","statement":"Appropriation is not limited to exact name or photograph. A clearly recognizable, unauthorized commercial evocation of the plaintiff’s identity can be enough.","type":"distinction","trigger":"Ad uses a lookalike, soundalike, persona, or identity cues to sell a product.","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 652C; Midler v. Ford Motor Co., 849 F.2d 460 (9th Cir. 1988).","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-TORTS-APPROPRIATION-IDENTITY-01","statement":"For appropriation calls, sort the array by the three trigger facts: identity use, commercial advantage, and no consent. Extra requirements like exact name/photo, insult, or false fact are trap add-ons.","type":"trap_spotting","trigger":"One answer mirrors identity-use facts while others add conditions.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"fabricated_rule","prompt":"A commercial uses a distinctive soundalike and costume to evoke a celebrity but avoids the celebrity’s name and photo. Cut the answer: ''No, because no exact name or photograph was used.''","answer":"CUT as fabricated_rule once the Gold Key is applied."},{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"colloquialism","prompt":"A commercial respectfully imitates a performer’s identity to sell a product. Cut the answer: ''No, because the imitation was flattering.''","answer":"CUT as colloquialism; tone is not the appropriation trigger."},{"drill_type":"wrong_frame","target_skill":"bait_doctrine","prompt":"A product ad uses a person’s recognizable identity without consent. Cut the answer: ''Yes, but only if the ad stated a false fact.''","answer":"CUT as bait_doctrine / wrong_frame; false fact belongs to neighboring reputation torts."},{"drill_type":"residual","target_skill":"Gold Key application","prompt":"Pick the survivor: exact name/photo requirement, flattering-use answer, false-fact answer, or identity-use/commercial/no-consent answer.","answer":"Identity-use/commercial/no-consent answer."}]}'),
  ('40fcbd7e-d2fb-5c4b-a921-de85208e3bfa', '20599_advent_handbell_integration', 'CONTRACTS', 'CM-B — Parol evidence rule core', 'Complete integration / merger clause / consistent additional term barred', 'complete-and-exclusive integration vs mere non-contradiction', 'Stephen agreed to write and rehearse a Scripture-themed puppet play for Martha’s family Advent party. Before anyone signed, Stephen allegedly said he would also prepare a handbell cue sheet at no extra charge. The signed agreement that followed listed the fee, scenes to be written, rehearsal dates, delivery deadline, and payment schedule. It also provided, “This writing is the complete and exclusive statement of the parties’ agreement.” When a dispute arose, Martha offered evidence of the earlier handbell promise. The agreement says nothing about handbells. How should the court rule?', 'Stephen agreed to write and rehearse a Scripture-themed puppet play for Martha’s family Advent party. Before anyone signed, Stephen allegedly said he would also prepare a handbell cue sheet at no extra charge. The signed agreement that followed listed the fee, scenes to be written, rehearsal dates, delivery deadline, and payment schedule. It also provided, “This writing is the complete and exclusive statement of the parties’ agreement.” When a dispute arose, Martha offered evidence of the earlier handbell promise. The agreement says nothing about handbells. How should the court rule?

How should the court rule?', 'How should the court rule?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20599.md","source_question_id":"20599","variant_slug":"advent_handbell_integration","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"52040300","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"trap repair","deciding_phase":"CLASH","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":"The writing says it is the complete and exclusive statement.","distilled_core_question":"A signed service contract says it is the complete and exclusive agreement. One party offers a prior oral extra term that does not conflict with the writing. Admit or exclude the oral term?","review_truth":"Complete-and-exclusive beats merely consistent: a prior oral add-on inside the same deal is excluded.","dominant_trap":"B","anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-CONTRACTS-COMPLETE-INTEGRATION-01","title":"Complete beats consistent","signal":"Signed writing says complete and exclusive; offered term came before signing.","student_move":"Do not ask only whether the oral term conflicts; ask whether the writing is complete.","tiny_rule":"Complete integration excludes prior consistent add-ons.","trap":"Consistency trap","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONTRACTS-COMPLETE-INTEGRATION-01","statement":"A complete-and-exclusive writing is a complete integration. Prior or contemporaneous oral additions inside the same deal are excluded even if they do not contradict the writing.","type":"distinction","trigger":"complete and exclusive statement + prior oral extra term","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts §§ 210, 213, 216","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONTRACTS-INTEGRATION-CLASH-01","statement":"When one choice says the oral term does not contradict and another points to complete/exclusive language, clash them on integration level before judging consistency.","type":"clash","trigger":"not conflict vs complete and exclusive","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"wrong-object Issue-Sense","prompt":"A party offers an oral side promise, but an answer excludes the signed contract. What filter breaks?","answer":"NOT_RESPONSIVE / misfit."},{"drill_type":"CLASH","target_skill":"integration-level axis","prompt":"One answer says the oral term is consistent; another says the writing is complete and exclusive. What is the axis?","answer":"Complete integration versus mere non-contradiction."},{"drill_type":"ANCHOR","target_skill":"complete integration Gold Key","prompt":"A complete-and-exclusive writing is silent about a prior oral add-on. Does consistency alone admit it?","answer":"No. Complete integration excludes prior consistent add-ons."}]}'),
  ('e8804f9d-2dd5-5307-9b98-c05259d12527', '20602_davids_harp_projector', 'EVIDENCE', 'III.C.1 — Subsequent Remedial Measures', 'Rule 407 product defect, design defect, and need for warning or instruction', 'Barred defect/warning purpose versus general relevance/feasibility/negligence-only drift.', 'Naomi bought a portable “David’s Harp” hymn-projector kit for a neighborhood praise-night in her garage. As she unfolded the aluminum tripod, the hinge bracket snapped shut and crushed her hand. In a federal products-liability action governed by the Federal Rules of Evidence, Naomi offers evidence that, after her injury, the manufacturer replaced the hinge with a spring-loaded locking hinge and added a bold “keep hands clear / load limit” warning tag. Naomi offers the evidence to prove that the original hinge design was defective and that the original warning was inadequate.', 'Naomi bought a portable “David’s Harp” hymn-projector kit for a neighborhood praise-night in her garage. As she unfolded the aluminum tripod, the hinge bracket snapped shut and crushed her hand. In a federal products-liability action governed by the Federal Rules of Evidence, Naomi offers evidence that, after her injury, the manufacturer replaced the hinge with a spring-loaded locking hinge and added a bold “keep hands clear / load limit” warning tag. Naomi offers the evidence to prove that the original hinge design was defective and that the original warning was inadequate.

How should the court rule under Rule 407?', 'How should the court rule under Rule 407?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20602.md","source_question_id":"20602","variant_slug":"davids_harp_projector","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"35030301","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"trap repair","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"After a product injury, the plaintiff offers a later product redesign and stronger warning to prove the original design defect and warning defect. Under Rule 407, admissible?","review_truth":"Rule 407 blocks later fixes offered to prove defect or warning need; feasibility only matters when that is the actual disputed purpose.","dominant_trap":"No measured pick rates supplied; likely diagnostic trap is negligence-only Rule 407 scope.","anchor_card":{"card_id":"REMED-EVIDENCE-407-PURPOSE","title":"Rule 407 Purpose Lock","signal":"Post-injury fix plus answer choices about relevance, feasibility, or negligence-only scope.","student_move":"Name the offered purpose before reading exceptions.","tiny_rule":"Defect/warning purpose is barred; feasibility is not automatic.","trap":"Treating feasibility or relevance as a universal admission route.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-EVIDENCE-RULE407-PURPOSE-01","statement":"Rule 407 is not negligence-only: it bars later fixes offered to prove product defect, design defect, or a need for warning or instruction. Feasibility is only a separate permitted purpose when it is genuinely disputed and actually offered for that purpose.","type":"distinction","trigger":"Post-injury redesign or stronger warning offered in a product case.","authority":"Federal Rule of Evidence 407; 1997 Advisory Committee note.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-EVIDENCE-RULE407-PURPOSE-01","statement":"When the stem tells you why evidence is offered, hold that purpose fixed. Relevance and exception words do not matter until they match the stated purpose.","type":"call_focus","trigger":"The stem says the later measure is offered to prove defect or inadequate warning.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"purpose_pin","target_skill":"offered_for_purpose","prompt":"A plaintiff offers a later product redesign to prove the original product was defective. Name the offered purpose.","answer":"Product/design defect; barred under Rule 407."},{"drill_type":"gold_key_recall","target_skill":"Rule 407 product scope","prompt":"True or false: Rule 407 applies only to negligence and culpable conduct.","answer":"False; it also covers product defect, design defect, and warning/instruction need."},{"drill_type":"overclaim_cut","target_skill":"tiered_absolute","prompt":"Cut the phrase: ''A later redesign always proves feasibility.''","answer":"Always is the overclaim; feasibility is only a permitted purpose when disputed and actually offered."},{"drill_type":"relevance_layer","target_skill":"specific exclusion over general relevance","prompt":"Why is ''relevant evidence is generally admissible'' a bad answer to a Rule 407 call?","answer":"It answers the wrong layer; relevance does not override a specific exclusion."}]}'),
  ('68bb59a3-7ac6-5d50-94b1-38ff6ee5046e', '20603_road_to_emmaus_escape_room', 'TORTS', 'Torts', 'Negligence', 'Daniel''s negligent creation of peril vs Ruth-centered rescue facts', 'During a private, for-profit Bible-themed escape-room contest called ''The Road to Emmaus,'' Daniel, although acting with reasonable care, crawled into a narrow prop tunnel as part of the puzzle and became wedged, unconscious, and critically injured in a compartment that was difficult to reach. Ruth, an experienced stagehand who was attending the event, was seriously injured while trying to free Daniel. Ruth''s attempt failed, and Daniel died before he could be reached. Ruth brought an action against Daniel''s estate for compensation for her injuries. In this jurisdiction, traditional common-law rules relating to contributory negligence and assumption of risk remain in effect.', 'During a private, for-profit Bible-themed escape-room contest called ''The Road to Emmaus,'' Daniel, although acting with reasonable care, crawled into a narrow prop tunnel as part of the puzzle and became wedged, unconscious, and critically injured in a compartment that was difficult to reach. Ruth, an experienced stagehand who was attending the event, was seriously injured while trying to free Daniel. Ruth''s attempt failed, and Daniel died before he could be reached. Ruth brought an action against Daniel''s estate for compensation for her injuries. In this jurisdiction, traditional common-law rules relating to contributory negligence and assumption of risk remain in effect.

Is Ruth likely to prevail against Daniel''s estate?', 'Is Ruth likely to prevail against Daniel''s estate?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20603.md","source_question_id":"20603","variant_slug":"road_to_emmaus_escape_room","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":null,"outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"2","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A careful person gets trapped and needs rescue. The rescuer is injured in a failed rescue. Can the rescuer recover from the careful person''s estate?","review_truth":"A rescuer''s claim needs a negligent peril-maker; noble rescue alone does not create liability against a careful person.","dominant_trap":"A","anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-TORTS-RESCUE-ACTOR-FAULT-01","title":"Rescue Doctrine: Actor-Fault Gate","signal":"Rescuer is hurt and sues the person rescued or that person''s estate.","student_move":"Target the defendant''s negligence before praising the rescue.","tiny_rule":"No negligent peril-maker, no rescuer recovery from that defendant.","trap":"Noble rescue facts feel dispositive.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-TORTS-RESCUE-NEGLIGENT-PERIL-01","statement":"Rescue doctrine needs a negligent peril-maker. A rescuer''s injury is charged to the person whose negligence created the peril, not merely to the person who needed help.","type":"threshold","trigger":"rescuer sues the imperiled person''s estate","authority":"Wagner v. International Railway, 232 N.Y. 176, 133 N.E. 437 (N.Y. 1921); Cornell Wex rescue doctrine","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-TORTS-TARGET-ACTOR-01","statement":"Lock the target actor before judging the rescue. If the rescuer sues the imperiled person''s estate, ask what that imperiled person did wrong.","type":"call_focus","trigger":"choices discuss the rescuer''s reasonableness, public policy, or failed benefit","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"colloquialism","prompt":"An answer says, ''Yes, because the law should encourage rescue.'' Cut or keep?","answer":"Cut. It is policy language, not the operative legal test."},{"drill_type":"issue_sense","target_skill":"wrong_element","prompt":"A rescuer sues the rescued person''s estate. One answer says the rescuer acted reasonably. What is missing?","answer":"The rescued person''s negligent creation of the peril."},{"drill_type":"anchor","target_skill":"rescue doctrine threshold","prompt":"When can a rescuer recover from the person rescued?","answer":"Only when that person negligently created the peril that led to the rescue injury."},{"drill_type":"call_focus","target_skill":"target actor","prompt":"The choices discuss the rescuer, policy, and failed rescue. The suit is against the estate. What is the first move?","answer":"Lock the target actor and ask what the estate''s decedent did wrong."}]}'),
  ('29c3fb4f-4f52-5084-944b-98cb02fc2153', '20605_scripture_quiz_whisper', 'TORTS', 'Privacy', 'False light', 'publicity threshold vs false-impression-only / wrong privacy-tort frames', 'Daniel first learned of Martha’s accusation when Martha later apologized to him. At a privately run Bible-character board-game tournament, Martha had pulled Lydia behind the prize table and falsely whispered, “Daniel is secretly swapping the Scripture-quiz answer cards.” Lydia kept the accusation to herself. Daniel sued Martha for false light.', 'Daniel first learned of Martha’s accusation when Martha later apologized to him. At a privately run Bible-character board-game tournament, Martha had pulled Lydia behind the prize table and falsely whispered, “Daniel is secretly swapping the Scripture-quiz answer cards.” Lydia kept the accusation to herself. Daniel sued Martha for false light.

Is Daniel likely to prevail on false light?', 'Is Daniel likely to prevail on false light?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20605.md","source_question_id":"20605","variant_slug":"scripture_quiz_whisper","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"62050402","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium-high","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A defendant falsely accused the plaintiff to one person in private; that person did not repeat it. Can the plaintiff win false light?","review_truth":"False light needs public-style publicity; a private one-listener accusation does not create false-light liability.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"TOR-ANCHOR-FALSE-LIGHT-PUBLICITY","title":"False light publicity gate","signal":"False-light claim after a private accusation.","student_move":"Ask whether the matter reached the public or a broad audience.","tiny_rule":"One private listener is not false-light publicity.","trap":"Treating falsity or defamation overlap as enough.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-TORTS-FALSE-LIGHT-PUBLICITY-01","statement":"False light needs publicity to the public or a broad audience. A private one-listener accusation is not enough, even if it is false.","type":"threshold","trigger":"false light claim + one private listener + no repeat","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Torts §652E; Restatement publicity commentary.","last_minute_review":true},{"id":"GK-TORTS-PRIVACY-TORT-HOMES-02","statement":"Commercial use of a person''s identity is appropriation, not false light. Do not borrow the neighboring privacy tort''s element.","type":"distinction","trigger":"false light choice using identity, likeness, or commercial use","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Torts §652C and §652E.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-TORTS-FALSE-LIGHT-NO-PAIR-01","statement":"When two answers say No, keep the No reason that matches the stem''s decisive fact. Here, private audience beats commercial-use vocabulary.","type":"trap_spotting","trigger":"two No choices in a privacy-tort element question","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"Gold Key recall","target_skill":"false-light publicity threshold","prompt":"A false accusation is made privately to one person and not repeated. False light?","answer":"No; no public-style publicity."},{"drill_type":"Wrong-frame sort","target_skill":"privacy tort homes","prompt":"Commercial use of name or likeness belongs to which privacy tort home?","answer":"Appropriation, not false light."},{"drill_type":"Wrong-element cut","target_skill":"false-impression-only trap","prompt":"Choice says yes because the statement created a false impression. What element is missing?","answer":"Publicity to the public or a broad audience."}]}'),
  ('90356905-7c86-5293-bb6c-a26dc9a13849', '20606_lydia_festival_venue', 'CIVIL_PROCEDURE', 'Civil Procedure', 'Jurisdiction and Venue', 'complete venue buckets vs incomplete/poisoned venue list', 'Source row: BarMatrix Q# 20606 is a federal diversity venue question with the official credited answer listing the accident district plus both defendants’ residence districts. 

Final Christian variation

Lydia, a worship harpist domiciled in the Northern District of State G, was injured when a propane coffee cart exploded during a privately run Christian music festival in the Central District of State H. Lydia filed a federal action based on diversity against two individuals involved in setting up the cart. Peter, a stage electrician, is domiciled in the Eastern District of State J. Ruth, a carpenter who built the cart platform, is domiciled in the Western District of State J.

In which judicial districts would venue be proper for Lydia’s action?', 'Source row: BarMatrix Q# 20606 is a federal diversity venue question with the official credited answer listing the accident district plus both defendants’ residence districts. 

Final Christian variation

Lydia, a worship harpist domiciled in the Northern District of State G, was injured when a propane coffee cart exploded during a privately run Christian music festival in the Central District of State H. Lydia filed a federal action based on diversity against two individuals involved in setting up the cart. Peter, a stage electrician, is domiciled in the Eastern District of State J. Ruth, a carpenter who built the cart platform, is domiciled in the Western District of State J.

In which judicial districts would venue be proper for Lydia’s action?

In which judicial districts would venue be proper for Lydia''s action?', 'In which judicial districts would venue be proper for Lydia''s action?', 3, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20606.md","source_question_id":"20606","variant_slug":"lydia_festival_venue","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"94050100","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium-hard","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A federal diversity tort suit has one event district and two individual defendants domiciled in different districts of the same state. Which districts are proper venue?","review_truth":"Venue list questions are bucket checks: include the event district and defendant-residence districts when all defendants reside in one state; do not add the plaintiff''s home district.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"CIV-REMEDY-VENUE-BUCKETS-20606","title":"Venue bucket checklist","signal":"The call asks which judicial districts are proper.","student_move":"Build the complete bucket list before reading combinations.","tiny_rule":"Event district plus qualifying defendant-residence districts; plaintiff home is a lure.","trap":"Stopping after the loudest venue fact.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-VENUE-BUCKETS-01","statement":"For ordinary federal civil actions, venue can sit in the event district or in a defendant-residence district when all defendants reside in that same state; the plaintiff''s home is not a venue bucket.","type":"rule","trigger":"The call asks which judicial districts are proper and gives accident location plus defendants from different districts of one state.","authority":"28 U.S.C. § 1391(b)(1), (b)(2), (c)(1)","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-VENUE-BUCKETS-01","statement":"Read list answers as bucket checklists: keep every valid bucket and reject any answer that adds a false bucket or omits a true one.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"Choices mix event district, defendant districts, and plaintiff home district.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"bucket_check","target_skill":"venue source sorting","prompt":"Event in District X; two defendants reside in Districts Y and Z of the same state; plaintiff resides in District P. Which buckets count?","answer":"X, Y, and Z; not P."},{"drill_type":"trap_spotting","target_skill":"plaintiff-home lure rejection","prompt":"A venue answer includes the plaintiff''s home district in an ordinary private-defendant tort suit. What is the trap?","answer":"Plaintiff residence is not a venue bucket here."},{"drill_type":"answer_array","target_skill":"complete-list selection","prompt":"One choice gives event only, one gives defendants only, one gives plaintiff plus event, one gives event plus defendants. Which shape usually survives?","answer":"The complete event-plus-defendants list."}]}'),
  ('008eb633-4b60-5811-960f-7a7000a2b9b5', '20627_ruth_hymn_poster_contract', 'CIVIL_PROCEDURE', 'Subject Matter Jurisdiction', 'Diversity - Time of Filing', 'filing-day citizenship snapshot vs later domicile facts', 'Daniel was packing his private Scripture-verse printing booth for a Colorado craft fair when Ruth sued him in federal district court. The lawsuit, filed on April 4, was a state-law contract action seeking $144,000 for allegedly ruined illuminated hymn posters Ruth had commissioned for her private Easter pageant. When the complaint was filed, Ruth was domiciled in Arizona, and Daniel was also domiciled in Arizona. On April 25, before filing any answer, Daniel permanently moved to Colorado and changed his domicile.', 'Daniel was packing his private Scripture-verse printing booth for a Colorado craft fair when Ruth sued him in federal district court. The lawsuit, filed on April 4, was a state-law contract action seeking $144,000 for allegedly ruined illuminated hymn posters Ruth had commissioned for her private Easter pageant. When the complaint was filed, Ruth was domiciled in Arizona, and Daniel was also domiciled in Arizona. On April 25, before filing any answer, Daniel permanently moved to Colorado and changed his domicile.

Did Daniel’s Colorado move create diversity jurisdiction?', 'Did Daniel’s Colorado move create diversity jurisdiction?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20627.md","source_question_id":"20627","variant_slug":"ruth_hymn_poster_contract","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"94010100","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"2","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"PURE_ANCHOR","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"P and D were citizens of the same state when P filed a $144,000 state-law case in federal court. D later moved to another state before answering. Does the move create diversity jurisdiction?","review_truth":"Diversity is a filing-day snapshot; a later domicile change does not create diversity that was missing when the complaint was filed.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-CIV-DIVERSITY-FILING-SNAPSHOT","title":"Diversity snapshot","signal":"Filing-day citizenship plus later move.","student_move":"Freeze citizenship at filing before reading later procedural facts.","tiny_rule":"Diversity citizenship is checked at filing.","trap":"response-date and before-trial timing choices","confidence":"PURE_ANCHOR"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-DIVERSITY-FILING-01","statement":"For ordinary diversity jurisdiction, citizenship is measured when the complaint is filed. A later move does not create diversity that was missing at filing.","type":"rule","trigger":"A stem gives citizenship at filing and a later domicile change.","authority":"28 U.S.C. § 1332(a); Grupo Dataflux v. Atlas Global Group, L.P.; Mollan v. Torrance","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-DIVERSITY-SNAPSHOT-01","statement":"When a jurisdiction stem gives filing-day citizenship and later domicile facts, freeze the snapshot at filing before judging the later facts.","type":"call_focus","trigger":"same-state parties at filing plus a later move before answer or trial","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"anchor_recall","target_skill":"diversity time of filing","prompt":"P and D are both Texas citizens when P files a $90,000 state-law case. D moves to Oklahoma before answering. Diversity?","answer":"No. Citizenship is measured at filing."},{"drill_type":"overclaim_cut","target_skill":"never / any detector","prompt":"Cut the answer: ''No, because state-law claims can never be heard under diversity.''","answer":"Cut. Never is an overclaim."},{"drill_type":"call_focus","target_skill":"freeze the filing snapshot","prompt":"A stem gives citizenship at filing and then a later domicile change. What fact do you check first?","answer":"Citizenship when the complaint was filed."}]}'),
  ('a702ba1d-f4ed-5a98-91c5-1c06dd99a9ae', '20638_advent_rescue_beacons', 'CONSTITUTIONAL_LAW', 'Relations Between Federal and State Governments', 'Supremacy Clause', 'practical interference vs geography/formality', 'Source row: QID 20638, Constitutional Law, official key B. 

Under authority granted by Congress, the United States Department of Defense hired Barnabas Metalworks, a private Advent-themed shop owned by Lydia, to manufacture rugged rescue-beacon casings for military search teams. The contract required all casting and polishing to occur at Barnabas’s old rail-depot workshop beside Mercy Brook, and it allowed the shop to release no more than four ounces per day of a polishing solvent into the brook from that production.

While producing the beacon casings under the contract, Barnabas routinely released between three and four ounces per day of the solvent into Mercy Brook. The state later prosecuted Barnabas under a state clean-water statute that prohibits any person or business entity from releasing more than three ounces per day of that solvent into any creek located in the state.

Barnabas defended on the ground that, as a federal contractor, it was immune from state regulation. Which one of the following additional facts or inferences, if it were the only one true, would most effectively support the state’s argument?', 'Source row: QID 20638, Constitutional Law, official key B. 

Under authority granted by Congress, the United States Department of Defense hired Barnabas Metalworks, a private Advent-themed shop owned by Lydia, to manufacture rugged rescue-beacon casings for military search teams. The contract required all casting and polishing to occur at Barnabas’s old rail-depot workshop beside Mercy Brook, and it allowed the shop to release no more than four ounces per day of a polishing solvent into the brook from that production.

While producing the beacon casings under the contract, Barnabas routinely released between three and four ounces per day of the solvent into Mercy Brook. The state later prosecuted Barnabas under a state clean-water statute that prohibits any person or business entity from releasing more than three ounces per day of that solvent into any creek located in the state.

Barnabas defended on the ground that, as a federal contractor, it was immune from state regulation. Which one of the following additional facts or inferences, if it were the only one true, would most effectively support the state’s argument?

Which additional fact or inference, if it were the only one true, would most effectively support the state''s argument?', 'Which additional fact or inference, if it were the only one true, would most effectively support the state''s argument?', 3, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20638.md","source_question_id":"20638","variant_slug":"advent_rescue_beacons","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"41030300","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium-hard","deciding_phase":"CLASH","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A federal contractor violates a stricter state discharge limit. Which fact best supports the state? The stricter limit does not increase federal production cost.","review_truth":"For a federal contractor, the state’s best fact is the one showing the state rule does not interfere with the federal project.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-CONLAW-FED-CONTRACTOR-INTERFERENCE","title":"Federal contractor: practical interference axis","signal":"state regulation of a private federal contractor","student_move":"Ask whether the state rule burdens the federal job.","tiny_rule":"No interference, no immunity win.","trap":"geography or express-exemption formalism","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONSTITUTIONAL_LAW-FEDERAL-CONTRACTOR-01","statement":"A state may regulate a federal contractor unless applying the state rule would interfere with federal purposes or policies.","type":"constitutional_principle","trigger":"state prosecutes or regulates a private contractor performing a federal contract","authority":"Supremacy Clause; Leslie Miller; Goodyear Atomic","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONSTITUTIONAL_LAW-FEDERAL-CONTRACTOR-01","statement":"When the call asks which fact best supports the state against federal-contractor immunity, look for the fact about practical effect on the federal job.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"choices split between location facts, express wording, and contract-performance cost","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"clash_axis","target_skill":"name the immunity axis","prompt":"State regulation of a federal contractor: express exemption or no project burden?","answer":"No project burden is the stronger state-support fact."},{"drill_type":"wrong_frame_cut","target_skill":"cut geography bait","prompt":"Federal contractor claims immunity; choice says the creek is wholly intrastate.","answer":"Cut as not responsive after federal authority is given."},{"drill_type":"gold_key_recall","target_skill":"federal-contractor anchor","prompt":"What makes state regulation of a federal contractor vulnerable?","answer":"Interference with federal purposes or policies."}]}'),
  ('1ba61517-0bd5-5767-9c87-66876bef7172', '20649_christmas_pageant_platform', 'TORTS', 'Negligence', 'Standard of Due Care / Contributory Negligence / Causation', 'defendant no-breach/foreseeability vs plaintiff avoidability vs other-cause blame', 'Naomi worked for Lydia''s private pageant-production company, which was staging a Christian Christmas pageant. The company hired Barnabas, an independent contractor, to adjust the idle setting on a motorized rolling platform that carried a large manger prop. Barnabas adjusted the idle setting too high. Later, Naomi stood beside the platform and pressed the start switch to move it; because the platform lacked a roll-stop interlock, it lurched and injured her. Naomi asserted a negligence claim against Barnabas.', 'Naomi worked for Lydia''s private pageant-production company, which was staging a Christian Christmas pageant. The company hired Barnabas, an independent contractor, to adjust the idle setting on a motorized rolling platform that carried a large manger prop. Barnabas adjusted the idle setting too high. Later, Naomi stood beside the platform and pressed the start switch to move it; because the platform lacked a roll-stop interlock, it lurched and injured her. Naomi asserted a negligence claim against Barnabas.

Which one of the following additional facts or inferences, if it were the only one true, would be most likely to result in a judgment for Barnabas in a jurisdiction that applies the all-or-nothing rule of contributory negligence?', 'Which one of the following additional facts or inferences, if it were the only one true, would be most likely to result in a judgment for Barnabas in a jurisdiction that applies the all-or-nothing rule of contributory negligence?', 3, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20649.md","source_question_id":"20649","variant_slug":"christmas_pageant_platform","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"61020201","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium-hard","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A contractor adjusted equipment in a way that caused injury only because a safety device was missing. Which added fact most helps the contractor win: normal safety-device custom, plaintiff avoidability, product defect, or another party''s notice failure?","review_truth":"A defendant wins a negligence claim by breaking the plaintiff''s proof of the defendant''s own unreasonable foreseeable-risk conduct, not by merely pointing to avoidability or another possible cause.","dominant_trap":"B","anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-TORTS-20649-01","title":"Avoidability is not contributory negligence","signal":"Choice says plaintiff would have avoided injury if she had acted differently.","student_move":"Ask whether the choice says the actual conduct was negligent.","tiny_rule":"No plaintiff negligence, no contributory-negligence bar.","trap":"Confusing safer hypothetical with legal fault.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-TORTS-FORESEEABLE-RISK-01","statement":"Negligence turns on unreasonable conduct in light of a foreseeable risk. A fact that makes the specific risk not foreseeable can defeat breach.","type":"rule","trigger":"The added fact says the missing safety device is usually present.","authority":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Liability for Physical and Emotional Harm § 3; negligence factors include foreseeable likelihood/severity and burden of precautions.","last_minute_review":true},{"id":"GK-TORTS-CONTRIB-AVOIDABILITY-01","statement":"Contributory negligence requires negligent plaintiff conduct that contributed to the harm. A fact that the plaintiff would have avoided injury by doing something else is not enough by itself.","type":"distinction","trigger":"The answer says the plaintiff would not have been hurt from another position.","authority":"Common-law contributory negligence rule; see Cornell Wex summary.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-TORTS-PAGEANT-ARRAY-01","statement":"In an added-fact defense question, sort each option by what it attacks: defendant breach, plaintiff negligence, other cause, or other party. The best defense fact usually attacks an element of the plaintiff''s claim against this defendant.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"Choices point in four different blame directions.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"wrong-element","prompt":"A plaintiff would not have been injured if she had used a safer position. Does that alone prove contributory negligence?","answer":"No. It shows avoidability, not negligent conduct."},{"drill_type":"CLASH","target_skill":"tension axis","prompt":"Two answers survive: one says the device is usually present; one says the plaintiff could have stood elsewhere. Name the axis.","answer":"Defendant no-breach / unforeseeable risk versus plaintiff avoidability."},{"drill_type":"CALL","target_skill":"answer-array sorting","prompt":"In an added-fact defense call, which fact is stronger: one blaming a manufacturer, or one making the defendant''s risk unforeseeable?","answer":"The unforeseeable-risk fact, because it attacks the plaintiff''s claim against this defendant."},{"drill_type":"ANCHOR","target_skill":"contributory negligence","prompt":"What must the added fact show before contributory negligence bars recovery?","answer":"Plaintiff negligence that contributed to the harm."}]}'),
  ('5791d081-b55a-588d-85ab-ab4ef9bbfd45', '20654_nativity_ornaments_volition', 'CRIMINAL_LAW', 'Defenses', 'General Principles', 'cognition vs volition', 'Stem / Fact Pattern

Lydia has a severe compulsion disorder that psychiatrists recognize as a legitimate mental disease. She has been examined and found to understand fully that damaging another person’s property is illegal and wrong. Even so, whenever she visits a privately run Christian craft market and sees ceramic nativity ornaments for sale, she experiences overwhelming uncontrollable urges to snap the shepherd staffs off the ornaments. She does this on repeated visits and is charged with multiple counts of criminal mischief.

The jurisdiction uses both M’Naghten and the irresistible-impulse supplement. Lydia’s expert testifies that, because of her mental disease, her urges were genuinely uncontrollable and she could not conform her conduct to the law.

Call of Question

Which of the following best states Lydia’s insanity claim?', 'Stem / Fact Pattern

Lydia has a severe compulsion disorder that psychiatrists recognize as a legitimate mental disease. She has been examined and found to understand fully that damaging another person’s property is illegal and wrong. Even so, whenever she visits a privately run Christian craft market and sees ceramic nativity ornaments for sale, she experiences overwhelming uncontrollable urges to snap the shepherd staffs off the ornaments. She does this on repeated visits and is charged with multiple counts of criminal mischief.

The jurisdiction uses both M’Naghten and the irresistible-impulse supplement. Lydia’s expert testifies that, because of her mental disease, her urges were genuinely uncontrollable and she could not conform her conduct to the law.

Call of Question

Which of the following best states Lydia’s insanity claim?

Which of the following best states Lydia''s insanity claim?', 'Which of the following best states Lydia''s insanity claim?', 3, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20654.md","source_question_id":"20654","variant_slug":"nativity_ornaments_volition","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"72060101","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium_hard","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A defendant knows the act is wrong but, due to mental disease, cannot conform conduct in a M''Naghten-plus-irresistible-impulse jurisdiction. Which insanity test may apply?","review_truth":"Knowing wrongfulness kills only the M''Naghten cognitive route; irresistible impulse can still matter when mental disease prevents control.","dominant_trap":"C","anchor_card":{"card_id":"CRIM-ANCHOR-INSANITY-01","title":"Insanity tests: cognition vs volition","signal":"Stem gives M''Naghten plus irresistible impulse.","student_move":"Match facts to lanes before picking.","tiny_rule":"Know-wrong = cognition; cannot-conform = volition.","trap":"Treating one lane as the whole insanity defense.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CRIMINAL-IRRESISTIBLE-IMPULSE-01","statement":"M''Naghten is cognition: did the defendant know the nature or wrongfulness of the act? Irresistible impulse is volition: the defendant may know wrong but still be excused if mental disease prevents control.","type":"distinction","trigger":"The stem says the jurisdiction uses M''Naghten plus irresistible impulse and gives both knowledge-of-wrongfulness and inability-to-conform facts.","authority":"M''Naghten''s Case; Model Penal Code section 4.01 conform-conduct analogue; Cornell Wex summaries.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CRIMINAL-IRRESISTIBLE-IMPULSE-01","statement":"Read the array as three buckets: diagnosis alone, cognition, and volition. When the stem gives both ''knows wrong'' and ''cannot conform,'' the answer must choose the volition bucket.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"Choices separately mention mental disease, knew wrong, M''Naghten, and irresistible impulse.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"anchor_recall","target_skill":"cognition_vs_volition","prompt":"A defendant knows an act is wrong but cannot control the act due to mental disease. Which lane is this: M''Naghten or irresistible impulse?","answer":"Irresistible impulse."},{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"wrong_element","prompt":"Cut the choice: ''The defendant is insane because she has a recognized mental disease.''","answer":"Wrong element: diagnosis is threshold, not sufficient."},{"drill_type":"answer_array","target_skill":"bucket_sorting","prompt":"Sort these facts into lanes: mental disease; knew wrong; could not conform.","answer":"Mental disease = threshold; knew wrong = cognition; could not conform = volition."}]}'),
  ('e2782e23-8183-5f9e-a192-9abc4930cb31', '20690_bethlehem_star_drone', 'CIVIL_PROCEDURE', 'Personal Jurisdiction', 'Rule 12 waiver after amended pleading', NULL, 'Mary runs a private Christmas-market design studio in State A. She sued Daniel, a State B drone-show contractor, in federal court after Daniel''s ''Bethlehem Star'' drone display failed during a paid rehearsal for Mary''s Advent market. Daniel served an answer that did not object to personal jurisdiction, although he already knew every fact supporting that objection. Seven weeks later, Mary amended the complaint against Daniel only to add a lost-profit damages theory from the same failed drone display. Daniel then moved to dismiss for lack of personal jurisdiction.', 'Mary runs a private Christmas-market design studio in State A. She sued Daniel, a State B drone-show contractor, in federal court after Daniel''s ''Bethlehem Star'' drone display failed during a paid rehearsal for Mary''s Advent market. Daniel served an answer that did not object to personal jurisdiction, although he already knew every fact supporting that objection. Seven weeks later, Mary amended the complaint against Daniel only to add a lost-profit damages theory from the same failed drone display. Daniel then moved to dismiss for lack of personal jurisdiction.

How should the court rule?', 'How should the court rule?', 3, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20690.md","source_question_id":"20690","variant_slug":"bethlehem_star_drone","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"94020201","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium-hard","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A defendant knew all facts supporting a personal-jurisdiction objection, answered without raising it, then faced a same-defendant amended complaint adding only a new damages theory from the same transaction. Should the later personal-jurisdiction motion be granted?","review_truth":"Personal jurisdiction is waivable; a same-defendant amended complaint does not automatically revive an available personal-jurisdiction defense omitted from the first answer.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"CIV-REMEDY-R12-WAIVER-20690","title":"First response freezes waivable Rule 12 defenses","signal":"Known personal-jurisdiction facts + answer or first Rule 12 motion without PJ objection.","student_move":"Check whether the later pleading created a new defect. If not, cut fresh-start answers.","tiny_rule":"Available PJ objections are use-it-or-lose-it; same-defect amendments do not restart them.","trap":"Amended complaint = fresh Rule 12 slate.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-R12-PJ-WAIVER-01","statement":"Personal jurisdiction is a waivable Rule 12(b)(2) defense. If it was available and the defendant omitted it from the first answer or first Rule 12 motion, it is waived.","type":"rule","trigger":"A defendant answers or files a Rule 12 motion without objecting to personal jurisdiction.","authority":"Fed. R. Civ. P. 12(b)(2), 12(g)(2), 12(h)(1).","last_minute_review":true},{"id":"GK-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-AMENDED-COMPLAINT-NO-REVIVAL-02","statement":"An amended complaint does not automatically revive a Rule 12(b)(2)-(5) defense that was available and waived earlier unless the amendment first creates the defense.","type":"distinction","trigger":"Plaintiff amends after defendant already failed to raise a known threshold defense.","authority":"Gilmore v. Shearson/American Express, Inc.; State Auto Ins. Co. v. Thomas Landscaping & Constr., Inc.; Gnotec Reftele AB order.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-JURISDICTION-WORD-01","statement":"Match the jurisdiction word to the call before doing doctrine. Personal jurisdiction is defendant-court power; supplemental jurisdiction is subject-matter power over claims.","type":"trap_spotting","trigger":"A choice switches from personal jurisdiction to supplemental jurisdiction.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"wrong_frame","prompt":"The call asks whether a late personal-jurisdiction motion should be granted. A choice says the plaintiff must establish supplemental jurisdiction. Cut or keep?","answer":"Cut: NOT_RESPONSIVE / misfit."},{"drill_type":"anchor","target_skill":"Rule 12 waiver","prompt":"A defendant knew all personal-jurisdiction facts and answered without raising PJ. What happens to the PJ defense?","answer":"Waived."},{"drill_type":"anchor","target_skill":"amended complaint no revival","prompt":"Plaintiff later amends only to add damages from the same transaction against the same defendant. Does that automatically revive waived PJ?","answer":"No."},{"drill_type":"trap_spotting","target_skill":"absolute words","prompt":"A choice says every amended complaint restarts all Rule 12 defenses. What is the C3 tell?","answer":"Every/all overclaim plus fabricated Rule 12 reset."}]}'),
  ('e9d8bc59-c4f2-594d-88d2-39ad44638808', '20714_prayer_robotics_kits', 'CONSTITUTIONAL_LAW', 'Judicial Review', 'Standing', 'Is this a general taxpayer grievance, or the narrow spending-power/Establishment-Clause exception?', 'After reports that teenagers at weekend robotics competitions are injuring themselves while soldering circuit boards, Congress enacts a provision distributing free soldering-safety kits to participants in public programs and private youth academies. The kits contain goggles, burn pads, and safety cards; they contain no prayer, worship material, or religious instruction. In many states, most private youth academies are church-affiliated. Lydia, a federal taxpayer, challenges the provision, arguing that it unconstitutionally supports religious entities.', 'After reports that teenagers at weekend robotics competitions are injuring themselves while soldering circuit boards, Congress enacts a provision distributing free soldering-safety kits to participants in public programs and private youth academies. The kits contain goggles, burn pads, and safety cards; they contain no prayer, worship material, or religious instruction. In many states, most private youth academies are church-affiliated. Lydia, a federal taxpayer, challenges the provision, arguing that it unconstitutionally supports religious entities.

Does Lydia have standing to make the challenge?', 'Does Lydia have standing to make the challenge?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20714.md","source_question_id":"20714","variant_slug":"prayer_robotics_kits","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"43020201","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"3","deciding_phase":"CALL","confidence":"PURE_ANCHOR","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A federal taxpayer challenges a congressional spending program that gives neutral materials to public and church-affiliated private recipients, claiming an Establishment Clause violation. Does the taxpayer have standing?","review_truth":"Federal taxpayers usually lack standing, but a challenge to congressional spending under the Establishment Clause fits the narrow Flast exception.","dominant_trap":"B","anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-CONLAW-FLAST-STANDING-01","title":"Taxpayer Standing: Narrow Door, Not Open Gate","signal":"Federal taxpayer challenges religious spending.","student_move":"Cut ''any taxpayer'' overclaims, then check the narrow spending-power/Establishment trigger.","tiny_rule":"General no standing; Flast exception for congressional spending plus Establishment Clause.","trap":"Treating all taxpayer challenges as allowed, or treating merits proof as standing proof.","confidence":"PURE_ANCHOR"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONSTITUTIONAL_LAW-FLAST_SPENDING-01","statement":"Federal taxpayers generally lack standing, but they may challenge a congressional taxing-and-spending enactment when the claim alleges violation of a specific constitutional limit on that spending power; the Establishment Clause is the classic trigger.","type":"exception","trigger":"Federal taxpayer + congressional spending + Establishment Clause claim.","authority":"Flast v. Cohen, 392 U.S. 83 (1968).","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONSTITUTIONAL_LAW-PRAYER_ROBOTICS_KITS-01","statement":"On a taxpayer-standing call, separate entry-to-court from whether the aid is unconstitutional.","type":"call_focus","trigger":"The call asks whether the taxpayer has standing.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"Gold Key recall","target_skill":"taxpayer standing exception","prompt":"Federal taxpayer challenges a congressional spending program as violating the Establishment Clause. Standing?","answer":"Yes, under the narrow Flast exception."},{"drill_type":"overclaim cut","target_skill":"tiered_absolute","prompt":"Choice says: ''Any federal taxpayer may challenge any congressional spending.'' Cut or keep?","answer":"Cut: overclaim."},{"drill_type":"call focus","target_skill":"standing versus merits","prompt":"Choice denies standing because no religious use has been proved. What is the skipped move?","answer":"The choice answers merits proof, not standing."}]}'),
  ('1e84ae4b-d832-5ade-b7e7-3d41b806c052', '20717_ruths_hymn_recording_procedure', 'TORTS', 'Torts', 'Negligence', 'breach evidence vs breach-caused-injury evidence', 'Ruth, an adult soprano preparing a private Christmas hymns recording, had been treated by an allergist for severe medication sensitivities for four years before undergoing an elective outpatient vocal-cord procedure performed by Dr. Peter, a throat surgeon. Three days after the procedure, Ruth suffered respiratory collapse from a delayed drug reaction that developed after the procedure. The collapse put her in a coma. When doctors concluded that she was in a permanent vegetative state with no hope of recovery, the ventilator and feeding support were withdrawn at her family''s request after court approval, and Ruth died later that evening. Dr. Peter was not involved in that decision or its execution. Ruth''s estate filed a wrongful death action against Dr. Peter, alleging that he was negligent in failing to consult an allergist before the procedure. At trial, the estate offered evidence that accepted medical practice required an allergist to examine a patient before this type of procedure. In this action, the estate should', 'Ruth, an adult soprano preparing a private Christmas hymns recording, had been treated by an allergist for severe medication sensitivities for four years before undergoing an elective outpatient vocal-cord procedure performed by Dr. Peter, a throat surgeon. Three days after the procedure, Ruth suffered respiratory collapse from a delayed drug reaction that developed after the procedure. The collapse put her in a coma. When doctors concluded that she was in a permanent vegetative state with no hope of recovery, the ventilator and feeding support were withdrawn at her family''s request after court approval, and Ruth died later that evening. Dr. Peter was not involved in that decision or its execution. Ruth''s estate filed a wrongful death action against Dr. Peter, alleging that he was negligent in failing to consult an allergist before the procedure. At trial, the estate offered evidence that accepted medical practice required an allergist to examine a patient before this type of procedure. In this action, the estate should

In this action, the estate should', 'In this action, the estate should', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20717.md","source_question_id":"20717","variant_slug":"ruths_hymn_recording_procedure","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"61020401","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"2","deciding_phase":"CLASH","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A malpractice plaintiff proves a required specialist consult was omitted but offers no evidence that the consult would have changed the outcome. Should the plaintiff prevail?","review_truth":"Breach evidence does not win malpractice unless the plaintiff also links that breach to the injury.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"TOR-REM-CAUSATION-LINK-01","title":"Breach is not causation","signal":"The stem proves what the doctor should have done but not what would have happened if the doctor had done it.","student_move":"Ask: what evidence links this specific missed step to this specific injury?","tiny_rule":"No link, no negligence win.","trap":"Choosing the answer that proves breach or the answer that proves the procedure caused harm.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-TORTS-MEDMAL-CAUSATION-01","statement":"In malpractice, proof that the doctor broke a professional rule is not enough; the plaintiff must prove that the broken rule caused the injury.","type":"threshold","trigger":"Stem gives standard-of-care evidence but no evidence the missed consult would change the outcome.","authority":"Restatement (Third) of Torts §§ 26, 29; medical-malpractice causation doctrine.","last_minute_review":true},{"id":"GK-TORTS-LIFE-SUPPORT-01","statement":"When the alleged tort creates the condition requiring life support, later withdrawal of support normally does not cut off causation.","type":"distinction","trigger":"Family/court withdraws support after the medical injury creates a permanent vegetative state.","authority":"State v. Pelham; State v. Patterson.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-TORTS-CAUSATION-LINK-01","statement":"Track the alleged breach, not the whole medical timeline. The question is not procedure-to-death; it is missed-consult-to-injury.","type":"clash","trigger":"One answer proves breach while another says no evidence the omitted consult would change the outcome.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"wrong_element","prompt":"A malpractice stem proves accepted practice required a specialist consult. No evidence says the consult would have changed the outcome. Cut the breach-only answer.","answer":"Cut as wrong_element: breach without causation."},{"drill_type":"CLASH","target_skill":"tension_axis","prompt":"One answer says the doctor was negligent for omitting a consult. Another says no proof shows the consult would change the result. Name the axis.","answer":"Breach evidence vs breach-caused-injury evidence."},{"drill_type":"ANCHOR","target_skill":"life_support_superseding_cause","prompt":"The injury causes a vegetative state; family and court approve withdrawal of support. Does that usually break causation?","answer":"No, if the original breach caused the condition requiring support."}]}'),
  ('683eb2ff-5ffb-54bd-ac9a-bd1a82b387b8', '20725_emmaus_laser_cutter', 'TORTS', 'Business torts', 'Negligent misrepresentation', 'known-recipient duty boundary: no contract vs every foreseeable reader vs named recipient', 'Paul owned a small private company, Emmaus Games, that made Bible-themed board games. To decide whether to buy a used industrial laser cutter from Paul, Ruth asked for an inspection report. Paul hired Daniel, a professional equipment inspector, and told him that the report would be sent to Ruth for that purchase. Daniel prepared a report stating that the laser cutter had no major functional defects, but he carelessly failed to include that its control board was failing. Ruth reasonably relied on the report, bought the cutter, and lost money when it broke down soon after.', 'Paul owned a small private company, Emmaus Games, that made Bible-themed board games. To decide whether to buy a used industrial laser cutter from Paul, Ruth asked for an inspection report. Paul hired Daniel, a professional equipment inspector, and told him that the report would be sent to Ruth for that purchase. Daniel prepared a report stating that the laser cutter had no major functional defects, but he carelessly failed to include that its control board was failing. Ruth reasonably relied on the report, bought the cutter, and lost money when it broke down soon after.

Is Ruth likely to prevail on a negligent misrepresentation claim against Daniel?', 'Is Ruth likely to prevail on a negligent misrepresentation claim against Daniel?', 3, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20725.md","source_question_id":null,"variant_slug":"emmaus_laser_cutter","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"62050200","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"moderate-hard","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A professional supplies careless false information for a specifically known person''s reliance in a business transaction. The known person relies and loses money. Can that person recover for negligent misrepresentation?","review_truth":"Known recipient is enough; every foreseeable reader is too many; knowledge of falsity is fraud territory, not negligence.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-TORTS-NM-CLASS-BOUNDARY-01","title":"Known recipient, not no one and not everyone","signal":"Professional report prepared for a named recipient''s business reliance.","student_move":"Cut the extremes: no-contract is too narrow; every-foreseeable is too broad.","tiny_rule":"Known recipient or limited known group can recover for careless false business information.","trap":"Students choose either strict privity or unlimited foreseeability.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-TORTS-KNOWN-RECIPIENT-01","statement":"For negligent misrepresentation by a professional information supplier, no contract privity is required when the plaintiff is the known person, or one of a limited known group, expected to rely on the information in the transaction.","type":"rule","trigger":"professional report + named recipient or limited known group + business reliance","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Torts §552; Private Mortgage Investment Services, Inc. v. Hotel & Club Associates, Inc.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-TORTS-BRACKET-CLASS-01","statement":"When the array swings from no contract to every foreseeable reader, bracket both extremes and look for the named-recipient middle lane.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"choices include no contract, every foreseeable, and named-recipient reliance","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"Overclaim class boundary","prompt":"Choice says negligent misrepresentation reaches every foreseeable reader of a professional report. Cut or keep?","answer":"Cut: tiered_absolute / too broad."},{"drill_type":"anchor","target_skill":"Known recipient rule","prompt":"Professional knows a named buyer will rely on a careless report. Buyer is not in contract with professional. Does no contract alone defeat the claim?","answer":"No. Use the known-recipient Gold Key."},{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"Mental-state mismatch","prompt":"Choice says negligent misrepresentation requires knowledge of falsity. Stem says carelessly false information. Cut or keep?","answer":"Cut: flat_misstatement / wrong mental state."},{"drill_type":"answer_array","target_skill":"Bracket extremes","prompt":"Array contains no contract, every foreseeable reader, and known recipient. Which lane is structurally favored after the Gold Key?","answer":"Known recipient."}]}'),
  ('a06c6b79-2a41-5f98-a315-38212d3caa4e', '20727_easter_garden_blast_hose', 'TORTS', 'Strict liability', 'Abnormally dangerous activities - harms outside abnormal risk', 'special-risk injury link vs activity/site/timing shortcut', 'Esther owned a private hillside garden where she planned to stage a Bible-themed outdoor drama. She hired Daniel’s blasting company to use explosives to carve a stone backdrop for the ''empty tomb'' scene. While the crew was checking the perimeter and before any explosive charge had been set, Lydia arrived to drop off palm-branch props. Near the refreshment tent, an ordinary folding costume rack tipped over and fractured Lydia’s ankle.', 'Esther owned a private hillside garden where she planned to stage a Bible-themed outdoor drama. She hired Daniel’s blasting company to use explosives to carve a stone backdrop for the ''empty tomb'' scene. While the crew was checking the perimeter and before any explosive charge had been set, Lydia arrived to drop off palm-branch props. Near the refreshment tent, an ordinary folding costume rack tipped over and fractured Lydia’s ankle.

Lydia sues Daniel''s company for strict liability based on the blasting. Is the company strictly liable?', 'Lydia sues Daniel''s company for strict liability based on the blasting. Is the company strictly liable?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20727.md","source_question_id":"20727","variant_slug":"easter_garden_blast_hose","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"63040100","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium_high","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":"The injury came from a folding costume rack, not blasting danger.","distilled_core_question":"A plaintiff is hurt by an ordinary site hazard during preparation for blasting. The injury does not come from blasting danger. Is the blaster strictly liable?","review_truth":"Strict liability for an abnormally dangerous activity covers only injuries caused by the special danger that makes the activity abnormal.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-TORTS-SPECIAL-RISK-LINK","title":"Strict Liability Needs the Risk Link","signal":"Dangerous activity plus ordinary injury.","student_move":"Ask what hurt the plaintiff before choosing a strict-liability answer.","tiny_rule":"Activity label is not enough; injury must flow from the abnormal risk.","trap":"Picking the dangerous-activity label.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-TORTS-SPECIAL-RISK-01","statement":"Strict liability for an abnormally dangerous activity follows the special danger, not every accident near the activity. The injury must be the kind of harm that made the activity abnormal.","type":"distinction","trigger":"The stem gives an abnormally dangerous activity plus an ordinary injury mechanism.","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Torts §519(2)","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-TORTS-RISK-LINK-01","statement":"In a 2-yes / 2-no strict-liability array, do not pick the outcome first. Pick the reason that links this injury to the risk named 