  ('a956243f-bdf7-5c09-b771-81da86cbabbe', '20730_shepherd_sling_fair', 'TORTS', 'Torts', 'Intentional Torts', 'contact intent vs injury/risk/motive mental-state distractors', 'At a privately run Bible-story craft fair held in a rented community center, Mary brought her seven-year-old son Peter. When Lydia, a volunteer judge, gave the blue ribbon to another child''s model of Noah''s ark instead of Peter''s painted shepherd-sling display, Peter shouted, ran across the aisle, and slammed his shoulder into Lydia. Lydia fell against a table and fractured her elbow.', 'At a privately run Bible-story craft fair held in a rented community center, Mary brought her seven-year-old son Peter. When Lydia, a volunteer judge, gave the blue ribbon to another child''s model of Noah''s ark instead of Peter''s painted shepherd-sling display, Peter shouted, ran across the aisle, and slammed his shoulder into Lydia. Lydia fell against a table and fractured her elbow.

In Lydia''s battery action against Peter, which argument most strongly supports liability?', 'In Lydia''s battery action against Peter, which argument most strongly supports liability?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20730.md","source_question_id":"20730","variant_slug":"shepherd_sling_fair","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"64010102","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"2","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A child deliberately makes body contact with an adult, causing a fall and injury. In the adult''s battery action against the child, which fact most strongly supports liability?","review_truth":"For battery, aim intent at the contact, not the injury, risk, or motive.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-TORTS-BATTERY-CONTACT-INTENT","title":"Battery mental-state target","signal":"Battery answer set offers contact, injury, risk, and motive.","student_move":"Lock the call, then pick the answer that targets contact.","tiny_rule":"Contact intent beats injury/risk/motive.","trap":"Choosing the more serious-sounding injury or risk answer.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-TORTS-BATTERY-CONTACT-INTENT-01","statement":"Battery intent points at harmful/offensive contact or apprehension of that contact. It does not require intent to injure, appreciation of risk, or bad motive; a child can be liable if the child formed that contact-level intent.","type":"distinction","trigger":"Battery call plus choices that talk about injury, risk, age, or anger.","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Torts §§ 13, 18; Garratt v. Dailey.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-TORTS-BATTERY-ANSWER-ARRAY-01","statement":"When the answer array gives four mental states, choose the one aimed at the tort named in the call. For battery, the contact-target answer beats injury, risk, and motive answers.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"Battery call with choices about contact, injury, risk, and anger.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"choice_sort","target_skill":"mental-state target sorting","prompt":"Battery call: one choice says contact intent, one says injury intent, one says risk appreciation, one says bad motive. Which target belongs to battery?","answer":"Contact intent."},{"drill_type":"gold_key_recall","target_skill":"battery anchor","prompt":"Does battery require intent to injure?","answer":"No. It requires intent to cause harmful/offensive contact or apprehension."},{"drill_type":"crossover","target_skill":"battery vs negligence","prompt":"A child is old enough to appreciate risk. Is that the strongest battery argument?","answer":"No. Risk appreciation is a negligence-style frame; battery turns on contact intent."},{"drill_type":"motive_filter","target_skill":"motive vs element","prompt":"The defendant was unjustifiably angry before making contact. Is that the battery element?","answer":"No. It may explain motive, but the element is intentional contact."}]}'),
  ('7e606a45-314d-5e94-a04c-df1cfa2af7a5', '20733_galilee_trails_death_notice', 'TORTS', 'Negligence damages', 'Special NIED categories', 'special erroneous-death-notice category vs ordinary NIED physical-danger default', 'Galilee Trails, a private company that runs paid Christian wilderness retreats, required each participant to list an emergency contact. During a dawn prayer hike, Lydia became separated from her group. Peter, the company’s trip coordinator, mixed up two radio reports and sent Lydia’s husband, Timothy, an emergency text saying that Lydia had been found dead near the overlook. Lydia was actually alive and waiting in a ranger cabin with Ruth, a first-aid volunteer. Timothy suffered severe emotional distress from the death notice. The jurisdiction recognizes negligent erroneous death notice as a special NIED category.', 'Galilee Trails, a private company that runs paid Christian wilderness retreats, required each participant to list an emergency contact. During a dawn prayer hike, Lydia became separated from her group. Peter, the company’s trip coordinator, mixed up two radio reports and sent Lydia’s husband, Timothy, an emergency text saying that Lydia had been found dead near the overlook. Lydia was actually alive and waiting in a ranger cabin with Ruth, a first-aid volunteer. Timothy suffered severe emotional distress from the death notice. The jurisdiction recognizes negligent erroneous death notice as a special NIED category.

If Timothy sues Galilee Trails, how should a court likely rule on the emotional-distress claim?', 'If Timothy sues Galilee Trails, how should a court likely rule on the emotional-distress claim?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20733.md","source_question_id":"20733","variant_slug":"galilee_trails_death_notice","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"61020203","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium_high","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A spouse receives a negligent false death notice, suffers severe emotional distress, and the jurisdiction recognizes erroneous death notice as a special NIED category. Is the claim viable?","review_truth":"A recognized negligent erroneous-death-notice category can support NIED without ordinary physical-danger limits.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"RC-TORTS-NIED-SPECIAL-CATEGORY-01","title":"Special category beats ordinary NIED gate","signal":"Stem gives a recognized emotional-distress category.","student_move":"Do not import zone-of-danger or bystander requirements unless the call asks for the ordinary rule.","tiny_rule":"Recognized erroneous death notice is its own NIED lane.","trap":"Physical danger sounds like the default NIED screen.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-TORTS-ERRONEOUS-DEATH-NOTICE-01","statement":"When a jurisdiction recognizes negligent erroneous death notice, the claim does not fail merely because the plaintiff was outside physical danger or because the reported death was false.","type":"exception","trigger":"False death notice to close family + severe distress + jurisdiction recognizes the category.","authority":"Johnson v. State of New York, 37 N.Y.2d 378 (1975); Restatement (Third) of Torts: Liability for Physical and Emotional Harm § 47(b).","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-TORTS-SPECIAL-CATEGORY-CUT-01","statement":"When the stem says the jurisdiction recognizes a special category, cut choices that answer the ordinary default lane instead of the special lane.","type":"cut","trigger":"Stem phrase: jurisdiction recognizes negligent erroneous death notice as a special NIED category.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"overclaim detection","prompt":"A choice says any negligent message that causes grief creates liability. What is the mold?","answer":"tiered_absolute"},{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"bait doctrine","prompt":"The stem gives an erroneous-death-notice category, and a choice denies recovery for lack of physical danger. What is the skipped move?","answer":"Special-category lane check."},{"drill_type":"anchor","target_skill":"NIED special category","prompt":"A spouse receives a negligent false death notice and suffers severe distress. The jurisdiction recognizes the category. Does lack of physical danger defeat the claim?","answer":"No."}]}'),
  ('a69c5057-5a86-5753-ba35-9c2429335cf7', '20735_bethlehem_donkey_blower', 'TORTS', 'Nuisance', 'Injunction, damages, and avoidable consequences', 'all-or-nothing remedies versus tailored abatement and avoidable damages', 'Barnabas ran a private Christmas-nativity petting-zoo business on land next to Ruth’s small prayer-retreat cottages. To dry animal bedding overnight, Barnabas installed a compost-air blower behind the donkey shed. The blower pushed a sour odor and low rumble across Ruth’s cottages, and several guests lost sleep. Ruth sued for private nuisance and requested a permanent injunction closing the petting-zoo business entirely. Before the hearing, Barnabas proved that, for a moderate cost, he could move the blower into a sealed feed room and add a scrubber, which would end nearly all odor and vibration without closing the business. Ruth refused to discuss that change and demanded closure because the guests had been miserable for months.', 'Barnabas ran a private Christmas-nativity petting-zoo business on land next to Ruth’s small prayer-retreat cottages. To dry animal bedding overnight, Barnabas installed a compost-air blower behind the donkey shed. The blower pushed a sour odor and low rumble across Ruth’s cottages, and several guests lost sleep. Ruth sued for private nuisance and requested a permanent injunction closing the petting-zoo business entirely. Before the hearing, Barnabas proved that, for a moderate cost, he could move the blower into a sealed feed room and add a scrubber, which would end nearly all odor and vibration without closing the business. Ruth refused to discuss that change and demanded closure because the guests had been miserable for months.

What is the most likely remedy in Ruth’s private nuisance claim against Barnabas?', 'What is the most likely remedy in Ruth’s private nuisance claim against Barnabas?', 3, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20735.md","source_question_id":"20735","variant_slug":"bethlehem_donkey_blower","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"62050102","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium-hard","deciding_phase":"CLASH","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":"moderate-cost relocation and scrubber would end nearly all interference","distilled_core_question":"A private nuisance can be mostly fixed by reasonable abatement at moderate cost. The plaintiff wants total closure and refused to discuss the fix. What remedy is most likely?","review_truth":"A nuisance remedy is usually tailored to reasonable abatement; mitigation can limit damages, but it does not erase the nuisance or require total shutdown.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-TORTS-NUISANCE-ABATEMENT-01","title":"Nuisance Remedy Oversteer","signal":"The answer says total shutdown, no remedy, or punitive-only.","student_move":"Ask which remedy fits the abatement fact.","tiny_rule":"Tailor the fix; limit avoidable damages.","trap":"All-or-nothing remedy language.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-TORTS-NUISANCE-ABATEMENT-01","statement":"In nuisance remedies, a court can tailor abatement to a reasonable fix. Avoidable-consequences principles can limit damages, but they do not erase the nuisance or require total closure.","type":"distinction","trigger":"The stem gives both a substantial interference and a moderate-cost abatement option.","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Torts §§ 822, 826; Boomer v. Atlantic Cement Co., 26 N.Y.2d 219 (1970).","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-TORTS-BETHLEHEM-01","statement":"When the choices are shutdown, no remedy, punitive-only, and tailored abatement, treat the first three as remedy oversteers. 