  ('ab9688a5-3f0e-577a-84bb-092a97570d24', '20737_jonahs_whale_lyre', 'CONTRACTS', 'Remedies', 'Specific performance for unique or highly unusual goods', 'Which fact triggers specific performance?', 'At a Bible-camp art fair, Lydia arrived on March 5 with $3,700 to collect a hand-carved olive-wood lyre shaped like Jonah’s whale. Two days earlier, Paul, who operated a booth selling Christian folk art, had agreed to sell that lyre to Lydia for that price. Paul refused to hand it over.', 'At a Bible-camp art fair, Lydia arrived on March 5 with $3,700 to collect a hand-carved olive-wood lyre shaped like Jonah’s whale. Two days earlier, Paul, who operated a booth selling Christian folk art, had agreed to sell that lyre to Lydia for that price. Paul refused to hand it over.

If Lydia sues Paul, is Lydia entitled to specific performance of the sale?', 'If Lydia sues Paul, is Lydia entitled to specific performance of the sale?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20737.md","source_question_id":"20737","variant_slug":"jonahs_whale_lyre","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"51060304","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium-high","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A buyer agreed to buy a highly unusual chattel, and the seller refused to deliver it. Can the buyer get specific performance?","review_truth":"Specific performance for goods turns on uniqueness or high unusualness, not merchant status, ordinary personalty labels, or the seller’s hypothetical remedy.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-CONTRACTS-SP-UNIQUE-GOODS-01","title":"Specific performance for goods: find the uniqueness trigger","signal":"Buyer asks for specific performance of a chattel.","student_move":"Ask whether the object is unique or highly unusual; ignore merchant status and reciprocity.","tiny_rule":"Unique/highly unusual goods can support buyer specific performance.","trap":"Ordinary personalty damages default overstated into a never rule.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONTRACTS-UNIQUE-GOODS-SP-01","statement":"For goods, specific performance is exceptional but available to a buyer when the goods are unique or highly unusual.","type":"exception","trigger":"The stem describes an unusual object and asks for specific performance.","authority":"UCC 2-716(1)","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONTRACTS-JONAH-LYRE-01","statement":"In a 3-Yes/1-No remedies array, do not accept the first Yes. Pick the answer tied to the stem’s remedy-trigger fact.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"Multiple Yes answers give different reasons for specific performance.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"anchor_recall","target_skill":"Gold Key recall","prompt":"Buyer seeks specific performance of a highly unusual chattel. What fact matters?","answer":"The goods are unique or highly unusual."},{"drill_type":"wrong_element_cut","target_skill":"Issue-Sense","prompt":"Choice says yes because the seller is a merchant. What filter breaks?","answer":"NOT_RESPONSIVE; merchant status is not the remedy trigger."},{"drill_type":"overclaim_cut","target_skill":"Ear plus anchor","prompt":"Choice says no specific performance is ever available for personalty. What mold?","answer":"tiered_absolute; it omits the unique-goods exception."},{"drill_type":"answer_array_navigation","target_skill":"Silver Key","prompt":"Three answers say yes for different reasons. What move?","answer":"Pick the Yes tied to the stem’s remedy-trigger fact."}]}'),
  ('68d29415-78b6-5385-91db-1b7f22744e73', '20744_bethlehem_loading_lane', 'TORTS', 'Negligence', 'Duty / Foreseeability Target', 'foreseeability target: plaintiff/risk in the lane versus set-stage cause, exact chain, or defendant''s own breach', 'Source row used: BARMATRIX Q#20744, Torts, official key C, asking which foreseeability target controls the taxi driver’s negligence liability. 

Christian variation — final locked version

During setup for a private Christmas Bible-pageant at a rented fairground, Timothy was pushing a handcart stacked with manger scenery when he clipped Lydia and hurried away, leaving her sprawled in a marked loading lane. A few minutes later, Stephen, drowsy after knowingly taking a strong sleep aid, drove a small electric forklift down the lane and ran over Lydia’s leg.

If Lydia brings a negligence action against Stephen for damages resulting from her fractured leg, Stephen’s liability will most probably turn on whether it was foreseeable that', 'Source row used: BARMATRIX Q#20744, Torts, official key C, asking which foreseeability target controls the taxi driver’s negligence liability. 

Christian variation — final locked version

During setup for a private Christmas Bible-pageant at a rented fairground, Timothy was pushing a handcart stacked with manger scenery when he clipped Lydia and hurried away, leaving her sprawled in a marked loading lane. A few minutes later, Stephen, drowsy after knowingly taking a strong sleep aid, drove a small electric forklift down the lane and ran over Lydia’s leg.

If Lydia brings a negligence action against Stephen for damages resulting from her fractured leg, Stephen’s liability will most probably turn on whether it was foreseeable that

If Lydia brings a negligence action against Stephen for damages resulting from her fractured leg, Stephen''s liability will most probably turn on whether it was foreseeable that', 'If Lydia brings a negligence action against Stephen for damages resulting from her fractured leg, Stephen''s liability will most probably turn on whether it was foreseeable that', 3, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20744.md","source_question_id":"20744","variant_slug":"bethlehem_loading_lane","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"61020101","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"hard","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A vehicle operator injures a person already in the travel path. Which foreseeability target matters: a person in the path, the prior event that put the person there, the exact second accident, or the operator''s own negligence?","review_truth":"Foreseeability targets the plaintiff/risk created by the defendant''s conduct, not the exact set-stage event and not the defendant''s own negligence.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"TOR-FORESEEABILITY-TARGET-01","title":"Foreseeability target scan","signal":"All answers ask what had to be foreseeable.","student_move":"Name the object after ''foreseeable that'' before judging law.","tiny_rule":"Pick the plaintiff/risk target, not the old cause, exact chain, or defendant breach.","trap":"Students pick the most dramatic fact instead of the legal target.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-TORTS-FORESEEABILITY-TARGET-01","statement":"For negligence by an active operator, the foreseeability target is the plaintiff/class/risk created by the defendant''s conduct, not the exact prior event that put the plaintiff there and not whether the defendant''s own careless act was foreseeable.","type":"distinction","trigger":"answer choices all complete ''foreseeable that ___''","authority":"Palsgraf v. Long Island R.R.; Restatement (Third) of Torts §§ 7, 29","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-TORTS-BETHLEHEM-LANE-01","statement":"When every choice starts from ''foreseeable that,'' compare the object of foreseeability first. Set-stage cause, exact chain, defendant breach, and plaintiff risk are different targets.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"same grammatical stem repeated across all choices","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"target_scan","target_skill":"identify foreseeability object","prompt":"In a negligence answer choice, underline the object after ''foreseeable that.'' Is it plaintiff/risk, exact chain, prior cause, or defendant breach?","answer":"plaintiff/risk is the target"},{"drill_type":"wrong_element","target_skill":"separate breach from foreseeability","prompt":"A choice says liability turns on whether the defendant would drive while impaired. What element is it chasing?","answer":"defendant breach, not foreseeability target"},{"drill_type":"set_stage","target_skill":"identify preexisting condition lure","prompt":"A person is already in a lane before defendant drives through it. Does liability turn on whether the earlier cause of that position was foreseeable?","answer":"No; focus on whether a person in the lane was foreseeable."}]}'),
  ('dbacf306-9c18-5779-bbdc-f0d0fcde539f', '20760_rubber_stamp_mail_fraud', 'EVIDENCE', 'Types of Evidence', 'C1', NULL, 'At Daniel’s federal mail-fraud trial over forged Bible-study supply invoices, the prosecutor offers a rubber stamp marked ''PAID'' that was found in a clearance bin at Lydia’s Christian Bookshop twenty miles from the post office used in the alleged scheme. No witness connects the stamp to Daniel, any invoice, or the charged mailing. Daniel objects.', 'At Daniel’s federal mail-fraud trial over forged Bible-study supply invoices, the prosecutor offers a rubber stamp marked ''PAID'' that was found in a clearance bin at Lydia’s Christian Bookshop twenty miles from the post office used in the alleged scheme. No witness connects the stamp to Daniel, any invoice, or the charged mailing. Daniel objects.

How should the court rule?', 'How should the court rule?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20760.md","source_question_id":"20760","variant_slug":"rubber_stamp_mail_fraud","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"31010300","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"trap","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"HARD_STRUCTURAL","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"At a federal trial, the prosecution offers a physical object with no connection to the defendant, the charged event, or any relevant issue. Should it be admitted?","review_truth":"A physical object is not relevant just because it belongs to a category that could matter; it needs a connection to a fact that matters in this case.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"EVI-REMEDY-REAL-OBJECT-CONNECTION-01","title":"Real object needs a bridge","signal":"Physical object offered, but no fact connects it to the charged event or issue.","student_move":"Ask: what fact does this object make more likely?","tiny_rule":"Object category is not a bridge.","trap":"The crime label makes the object feel relevant.","confidence":"HARD_STRUCTURAL"},"gold_keys":[],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-EVIDENCE-RUBBER-STAMP-01","statement":"In Evidence, start with the bridge: what fact does this item help prove in this case? If the stem gives no bridge, do not jump to object category, demonstrative use, or prejudice.","type":"call_focus","trigger":"A physical object is offered, but the stem says no witness connects it to the defendant, document, event, or issue.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"overclaim","prompt":"A physical object is offered because all objects of that kind are relevant in this type of case. What is the C3 cut?","answer":"Tiered-absolute overclaim; category alone is not a case connection."},{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"offered_purpose","prompt":"The proponent offers an object as real evidence. A choice admits it as a demonstrative aid, but the stem gives no such purpose. What is the cut?","answer":"NOT_RESPONSIVE; bait_doctrine / offered_for_purpose."},{"drill_type":"CALL_FOCUS","target_skill":"layer_order","prompt":"An object has no link to the charged event. One choice excludes under prejudice only; another excludes for no probative value. Which layer comes first?","answer":"Connection/relevance comes before prejudice."}]}'),
  ('baa7935e-f4fe-5f3f-9941-ebfe015c6722', '20805_psalm_lanterns_confirmation', 'CONTRACTS', 'Statute of Frauds', 'D', 'timely oral objection versus required written objection', 'Martha and Timothy are both merchants who wholesale supplies for Christian camp stores. At a trade fair, Timothy orally agrees to buy 180 brass lanterns with Psalm-themed labels from Martha for $18,000. Later that week, Martha mails Timothy a signed written confirmation of the deal. On the eighth day after receiving it, Timothy leaves Martha a voicemail: “I object to your confirmation.” Timothy sends no written objection and later refuses the lanterns.', 'Martha and Timothy are both merchants who wholesale supplies for Christian camp stores. At a trade fair, Timothy orally agrees to buy 180 brass lanterns with Psalm-themed labels from Martha for $18,000. Later that week, Martha mails Timothy a signed written confirmation of the deal. On the eighth day after receiving it, Timothy leaves Martha a voicemail: “I object to your confirmation.” Timothy sends no written objection and later refuses the lanterns.

Which statement best states the result?', 'Which statement best states the result?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20805.md","source_question_id":"20805","variant_slug":"psalm_lanterns_confirmation","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"54030400","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"trap repair","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"PURE_ANCHOR","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"Two merchants make an oral goods deal; the sender sends a signed confirmation; the recipient objects orally within 10 days but sends no writing. Does the confirmation still work against the recipient?","review_truth":"Between merchants, a confirming writing is not defeated by an oral objection; the objection must be written within 10 days.","dominant_trap":"D","anchor_card":{"card_id":"CONTRACTS-ANCHOR-MERCHANT-CONFIRMATION-01","title":"Merchant confirmation: oral objection trap","signal":"Two merchants, oral goods deal, signed confirmation, oral objection.","student_move":"Check whether the recipient sent written notice of objection within 10 days.","tiny_rule":"Written objection required; oral objection is not enough.","trap":"Treating any timely objection as enough.","confidence":"PURE_ANCHOR"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONTRACTS-MERCHANT-CONFIRMATION-01","statement":"Between merchants, a signed written confirmation sufficient against the sender can satisfy the UCC Statute of Frauds against the recipient if the recipient has reason to know its contents and does not give written notice of objection within 10 days. Oral objection is not enough.","type":"exception","trigger":"two merchants + oral goods deal + signed written confirmation + oral/no written objection","authority":"UCC §2-201(2)","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONTRACTS-TIMING-VS-FORM-01","statement":"When a choice says the objection was within 10 days, ask a second question: was it in the required form? Timing does not cure the wrong method of objection.","type":"trap_spotting","trigger":"oral objection inside a time window","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"anchor_recall","target_skill":"merchant-confirmation written objection","prompt":"Two merchants make an oral goods deal. Seller sends a signed confirmation. Buyer calls within 10 days to object but sends no writing. Does the call defeat the confirmation?","answer":"No. Written objection is required."},{"drill_type":"trap_spotting","target_skill":"timing versus form","prompt":"A choice says: ''Any objection within 10 days defeats the confirmation.'' Name the trap.","answer":"Timing-swallows-form trap; objection must be written."},{"drill_type":"mold_label","target_skill":"fabricated_rule","prompt":"A choice says the sender must prove the recipient read every word of a merchant confirmation. Mold?","answer":"fabricated_rule."},{"drill_type":"signature_myth","target_skill":"recipient signature under merchant confirmation","prompt":"A choice says the confirmation fails because the recipient merchant did not sign it. Why cut?","answer":"The merchant-confirmation rule can work without recipient signature if no timely written objection is sent."}]}'),
  ('81aea214-8e50-5183-b56e-6f5133e18d6a', '20808_jericho_press', 'CONTRACTS', 'Formation', 'Consideration - preexisting legal duty', 'new legal detriment vs preexisting duty', 'Hannah owned a vintage hand-cranked letterpress called the “Jericho Press,” which Timothy liked to use to print private Bible-verse cards. Hannah transferred title to the press to Lydia, but only after Lydia agreed that Timothy could use the press whenever he wished. Lydia agreed but did not tell Timothy about that promise.

Mary lived in a coastal town and had long wanted Timothy to move there for the town’s private Scripture-calligraphy fairs. Mary wrote Timothy a letter saying that, if he moved to the town, she would leave him her estate when she died.

Timothy knew that Lydia now owned the Jericho Press. He told Lydia that, if she would let him use the press until Mary died, he would give Lydia half of whatever he received from Mary. Lydia agreed, and Timothy moved to the town and used the press.

Mary died shortly afterward. Her will left her entire estate to Timothy, but Timothy refused to share any of it with Lydia.', 'Hannah owned a vintage hand-cranked letterpress called the “Jericho Press,” which Timothy liked to use to print private Bible-verse cards. Hannah transferred title to the press to Lydia, but only after Lydia agreed that Timothy could use the press whenever he wished. Lydia agreed but did not tell Timothy about that promise.

Mary lived in a coastal town and had long wanted Timothy to move there for the town’s private Scripture-calligraphy fairs. Mary wrote Timothy a letter saying that, if he moved to the town, she would leave him her estate when she died.

Timothy knew that Lydia now owned the Jericho Press. He told Lydia that, if she would let him use the press until Mary died, he would give Lydia half of whatever he received from Mary. Lydia agreed, and Timothy moved to the town and used the press.

Mary died shortly afterward. Her will left her entire estate to Timothy, but Timothy refused to share any of it with Lydia.

If Lydia asserts a claim against Timothy for a share of the estate that Timothy received from Mary, a court should find for:', 'If Lydia asserts a claim against Timothy for a share of the estate that Timothy received from Mary, a court should find for:', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20808.md","source_question_id":"20808","variant_slug":"jericho_press","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"55020204","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"high","deciding_phase":"CLASH","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":"Lydia had already agreed that Timothy could use the press whenever he wished.","distilled_core_question":"A claimant promises to allow a use she already owed, in exchange for half of a later estate. Is the estate-sharing promise enforceable?","review_truth":"A promise to do what you already legally must do is not consideration for a new promise.","dominant_trap":"A","anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-CONTRACTS-PREEXISTING-DUTY-01","title":"Existing duty is not new consideration","signal":"A party promises to do the same thing already owed.","student_move":"Ask what new legal detriment appears in the later exchange.","tiny_rule":"Already owed is not newly paid for.","trap":"Promise-for-promise wording looks like consideration.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONTRACTS-PRESS-DUTY-01","statement":"A promise to do what the promisee is already legally obligated to do is not consideration for a new promise, unless the new performance is materially different or the old duty is honestly disputed.","type":"rule","trigger":"A later promise is exchanged for permission or performance already owed under an earlier deal.","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts §§ 71, 73.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONTRACTS-PRESS-DUTY-01","statement":"When one answer says ''promise-for-promise'' and the other says ''no consideration,'' ask what new legal position the plaintiff gave up in the later deal.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"A surface bargain repeats an earlier duty.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"Gold Key recall","target_skill":"preexisting duty","prompt":"A promises B to do the exact act A already owes B under an earlier contract. Is A''s promise consideration for B''s new promise?","answer":"No. Existing duty is not new consideration."},{"drill_type":"CLASH axis","target_skill":"new legal detriment vs preexisting duty","prompt":"Choice 1 says promise-for-promise. Choice 2 says no consideration. What fact decides?","answer":"Whether the promisee gave up a new legal right or only repeated an existing duty."},{"drill_type":"Wrong-element cut","target_skill":"promissory estoppel reliance","prompt":"A choice says estoppel applies, but the plaintiff did not change position because of the promise. Cut or keep?","answer":"Cut. Estoppel needs reliance."},{"drill_type":"Wrong-frame cut","target_skill":"condition-precedent bait","prompt":"A choice uses a hidden earlier promise to make a condition in a later promise by another person. What is the C3 label?","answer":"Wrong-frame bait doctrine."}]}'),
  ('4b2e2804-0686-5dc6-865f-546f63aa0d22', '20811_illuminated_psalms_solvent', 'CIVIL_PROCEDURE', 'Personal Jurisdiction', 'Specific jurisdiction - contacts before fairness', 'defendant-created contacts vs plaintiff-side fairness/effects', 'Lydia, a Maine resident, paid to attend Barnabas’s private “Illuminated Psalms” calligraphy workshop in New Mexico. During the workshop, Barnabas handed students a solvent that he had prepared for the class. All instruction, materials use, and alleged negligent conduct occurred in New Mexico. After Lydia returned to Maine, her hands became inflamed and she missed several weeks of work on paid Advent-card commissions.

Lydia sued Barnabas in Maine. She argues that Maine is the fairest forum because she is recovering there, her family can help her there, and traveling back to New Mexico would be difficult. Barnabas has never taught workshops, advertised, shipped supplies, or taken commissions in Maine.', 'Lydia, a Maine resident, paid to attend Barnabas’s private “Illuminated Psalms” calligraphy workshop in New Mexico. During the workshop, Barnabas handed students a solvent that he had prepared for the class. All instruction, materials use, and alleged negligent conduct occurred in New Mexico. After Lydia returned to Maine, her hands became inflamed and she missed several weeks of work on paid Advent-card commissions.

Lydia sued Barnabas in Maine. She argues that Maine is the fairest forum because she is recovering there, her family can help her there, and traveling back to New Mexico would be difficult. Barnabas has never taught workshops, advertised, shipped supplies, or taken commissions in Maine.

Can fairness considerations alone create specific jurisdiction in Maine?', 'Can fairness considerations alone create specific jurisdiction in Maine?', 3, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ20811.md","source_question_id":"20811","variant_slug":"illuminated_psalms_solvent","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"94020302","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"hard","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A plaintiff travels to another state for a paid service, all defendant conduct occurs there, the harm is later felt at home, and the defendant has no home-state contacts. Can plaintiff hardship and forum fairness alone create specific jurisdiction?","review_truth":"Fairness can make jurisdiction unreasonable after contacts exist, but it cannot create minimum contacts by itself.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"CIV-REM-PJ-CONTACTS-FIRST","title":"Contacts First, Fairness Second","signal":"Plaintiff argues home forum is fairest, but defendant did all conduct elsewhere.","student_move":"Ask for defendant-created forum contacts before weighing fairness.","tiny_rule":"Fairness can defeat jurisdiction; it cannot create minimum contacts.","trap":"Letting sympathy for plaintiff hardship substitute for purposeful availment.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-CONTACTS_BEFORE_FAIRNESS-01","statement":"Specific jurisdiction starts with defendant-created forum contacts. Fairness or convenience can defeat jurisdiction after contacts exist, but it cannot create contacts by itself.","type":"threshold","trigger":"The plaintiff felt harm at home or argues the home forum is fair, while the defendant''s conduct all occurred elsewhere.","authority":"International Shoe; World-Wide Volkswagen; Walden; Ford.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-ILLUMINATED_PSALMS_SOLVENT-01","statement":"In a specific-jurisdiction array, sort contacts first and fairness second. A plaintiff-side hardship fact cannot do the work of a defendant-side contact fact.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"Choices mention hardship, injury felt in the forum, and no forum-directed defendant conduct.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"wrong_element","prompt":"A defendant did all conduct in State X; plaintiff feels injury in State Y. Is injury felt in State Y the defendant contact?","answer":"No. It is a plaintiff-side effects fact."},{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"misfit","prompt":"The call asks personal jurisdiction; an answer discusses federal-court exclusion. What is the cut?","answer":"Misfit: wrong procedural gate."},{"drill_type":"CALL","target_skill":"threshold ordering","prompt":"A personal-jurisdiction answer relies on fairness before defendant contacts. What is the ordered move?","answer":"Contacts first, fairness second."},{"drill_type":"trap_spotting","target_skill":"fairness-alone trap","prompt":"Plaintiff is ill and travel is hard. Defendant has no forum contacts. Can hardship create contacts?","answer":"No. Fairness cannot create minimum contacts."}]}'),
  ('e8e3e28b-b160-5f31-b8fb-4b51f858b164', '21711_foam_whale_nativity_attachment', 'CIVIL_PROCEDURE', 'Personal Jurisdiction', 'Quasi-in-rem limits and relatedness', 'court power over defendant vs plaintiff/property/collection lures', 'At a privately rented Bible-story obstacle-course party in State A, Lydia and Daniel, both State A citizens, are helping set up a giant foam whale prop. Daniel negligently bumps the prop, which falls and injures Lydia. Daniel owns a hand-carved olive-wood Nativity set stored in a private storage locker in State B, but Daniel has no other contacts with State B. Lydia sues Daniel in a State B court for the prop injury and attaches the Nativity set, arguing that the property gives State B power to decide the whole injury claim.', 'At a privately rented Bible-story obstacle-course party in State A, Lydia and Daniel, both State A citizens, are helping set up a giant foam whale prop. Daniel negligently bumps the prop, which falls and injures Lydia. Daniel owns a hand-carved olive-wood Nativity set stored in a private storage locker in State B, but Daniel has no other contacts with State B. Lydia sues Daniel in a State B court for the prop injury and attaches the Nativity set, arguing that the property gives State B power to decide the whole injury claim.

What is the best analysis?', 'What is the best analysis?', 3, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ21711.md","source_question_id":null,"variant_slug":"foam_whale_nativity_attachment","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"94020302","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium-hard","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":"The State B property is unrelated to the State A injury claim, and Daniel has no other State B contacts.","distilled_core_question":"Defendant has unrelated property in State B and no other State B contacts. Plaintiff sues there on a State A injury claim and attaches the property. Does State B have personal jurisdiction over the whole claim?","review_truth":"Unrelated forum property is not a magic key to personal jurisdiction over an unrelated claim.","dominant_trap":"D","anchor_card":{"card_id":"CIV-REM-QIR-RELATEDNESS-01","title":"Unrelated property is not forum power.","signal":"Plaintiff attaches defendant''s forum property for a claim arising elsewhere.","student_move":"Ask whether the property connects the defendant, forum, and litigation.","tiny_rule":"Unrelated property alone does not create jurisdiction over the personal claim.","trap":"Any-property / asset-value shortcut.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-QIR-01","statement":"Forum property is not a magic key. If the claim is unrelated to the attached property, the forum still needs a defendant-forum-litigation connection.","type":"constitutional_principle","trigger":"A plaintiff attaches a defendant''s forum property to sue on an unrelated personal claim.","authority":"Shaffer v. Heitner, 433 U.S. 186; International Shoe Co. v. Washington, 326 U.S. 310.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-JURISDICTION-ASSET-VALUE-01","statement":"Separate court power from collection. An asset''s value may matter after a valid judgment, but it does not answer whether this court can decide the claim.","type":"trap_spotting","trigger":"An answer asks whether the attached property can satisfy the whole judgment.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"wrong_element","prompt":"A plaintiff sues in State B because she was injured and prefers that forum. What is the C3 break?","answer":"NOT_RESPONSIVE: plaintiff preference is not defendant-forum power."},{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"violation_vs_remedy","prompt":"An answer says the attached property is valuable enough to satisfy the judgment. What is the trap?","answer":"It answers collection, not jurisdiction."},{"drill_type":"ANCHOR","target_skill":"quasi_in_rem_relatedness","prompt":"A defendant''s unrelated property is attached in the forum for a claim arising elsewhere. Does the property alone support jurisdiction?","answer":"No. Unrelated property alone is not enough."},{"drill_type":"TRAP_SPOTTING","target_skill":"tiered_absolute","prompt":"Choice says any property in the forum creates personal jurisdiction. What word should ring?","answer":"Any."}]}'),
  ('12419e26-273d-5337-976c-0e398eff5f6f', '21752_olive_oil_ramp', 'TORTS', 'Vicarious liability', 'Independent contractors - nondelegable duty', 'independent-contractor default vs nondelegable-duty exception', 'Lydia operates a private, for-profit Bible-themed candle-dipping studio called Lampstand Studio. A statute requires businesses that invite the public into hands-on workshops to keep their customer walkway reasonably safe. Before opening for a paid ''Psalms and Candles'' class, Lydia hires Barnabas, an outside floor-care contractor, to cover and clean a slick olive-oil spill on the only ramp customers use to enter the studio. Barnabas negligently leaves the slick oil on the ramp, and Peter slips and falls while entering.', 'Lydia operates a private, for-profit Bible-themed candle-dipping studio called Lampstand Studio. A statute requires businesses that invite the public into hands-on workshops to keep their customer walkway reasonably safe. Before opening for a paid ''Psalms and Candles'' class, Lydia hires Barnabas, an outside floor-care contractor, to cover and clean a slick olive-oil spill on the only ramp customers use to enter the studio. Barnabas negligently leaves the slick oil on the ramp, and Peter slips and falls while entering.

What is Peter''s best argument against Lampstand Studio?', 'What is Peter''s best argument against Lampstand Studio?', 3, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ21752.md","source_question_id":"21752","variant_slug":"olive_oil_ramp","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"62060101","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium-hard","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"PURE_ANCHOR","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A public-facing business has a statutory duty to keep its customer walkway reasonably safe, hires an outside contractor to make the walkway safe, the contractor negligently leaves a hazard, and a customer falls. What is the customer''s best argument against the business?","review_truth":"A hirer may remain liable for an independent contractor''s negligence when the breached duty is nondelegable.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-TORTS-NONDELEGABLE-01","title":"Independent contractor is not the end of the story","signal":"Hirer has a statutory/public safety duty and hires someone else to perform it.","student_move":"Ask whether the duty itself can be delegated.","tiny_rule":"Nondelegable duty can keep liability with the hirer.","trap":"Contractor status as total shield.","confidence":"PURE_ANCHOR"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-TORTS-NONDELEGABLE-01","statement":"A statutory or otherwise nondelegable safety duty stays with the hirer even when the negligent work is done by an independent contractor.","type":"exception","trigger":"statutory/public safety duty plus outside contractor","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Torts §§ 409, 424","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-TORTS-NONDELEGABLE-01","statement":"When the stem gives a legal safety duty and an outside contractor, do not choose the all-or-nothing contractor answers; look for the duty-stays-with-the-hirer route.","type":"trap_spotting","trigger":"statutory duty + hired contractor + injured public entrant","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"anchor_recall","target_skill":"nondelegable-duty exception","prompt":"A business has a statutory duty to keep an entrance safe and hires an independent contractor to clear it. The contractor is negligent. What argument keeps the business in the case?","answer":"The duty may be nondelegable."},{"drill_type":"overclaim_cut","target_skill":"tiered_absolute","prompt":"Cut this answer: ''Every outside contractor working at a business is treated as the business''s employee.''","answer":"Cut as tiered_absolute; nondelegable duty is narrower than employee status."},{"drill_type":"trap_spotting","target_skill":"contractor-shield exception check","prompt":"Stem signal: statutory safety duty + independent contractor + public entrant injured. What trap should you expect?","answer":"The trap is treating contractor status as a total shield."}]}'),
  ('22ee3011-70a1-513f-a5d5-93b4a07141fd', '21848_manna_house_family_integrity', 'CONSTITUTIONAL_LAW', 'Due Process', 'Family Liberty', 'family-integrity route vs ordinary zoning route', 'In the city of New Capernaum, the One Hearth residential code permits a one-unit rowhouse to be occupied by a parent and that parent’s children, but it does not allow an adult aunt to live in that same rowhouse with her niece and the niece’s children. Naomi, Lydia, Timothy, and Ruth share a privately owned rowhouse they call Manna House. The city orders Naomi to move out under the code. The household challenges the code as a direct interference with their family living arrangement.', 'In the city of New Capernaum, the One Hearth residential code permits a one-unit rowhouse to be occupied by a parent and that parent’s children, but it does not allow an adult aunt to live in that same rowhouse with her niece and the niece’s children. Naomi, Lydia, Timothy, and Ruth share a privately owned rowhouse they call Manna House. The city orders Naomi to move out under the code. The household challenges the code as a direct interference with their family living arrangement.

What is the best constitutional route?', 'What is the best constitutional route?', 3, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ21848.md","source_question_id":"21848","variant_slug":"manna_house_family_integrity","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"44040202","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"hard","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A city directly excludes a close relative from living with family in one home. What constitutional route should the family use?","review_truth":"Direct government exclusion of close relatives from a shared household routes to substantive due process family integrity, not generic zoning or commerce.","dominant_trap":"D","anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-CONLAW-FAMILY-HOME-01","title":"Close-family household ≠ ordinary zoning","signal":"A city rule excludes one close relative from a shared household.","student_move":"Treat it as family-integrity due process after cutting wrong constitutional homes.","tiny_rule":"Family household first; zoning only after the family trigger is absent.","trap":"Treating every occupancy ordinance as ordinary rational-basis zoning.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONSTITUTIONAL_LAW-FAMILY-INTEGRITY-01","statement":"When a government housing rule directly excludes close relatives from living together as a household, route the claim to substantive due process family integrity, not ordinary zoning.","type":"constitutional_principle","trigger":"A residential rule slices a close relative out of a family household.","authority":"Moore v. City of East Cleveland, 431 U.S. 494 (1977).","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONSTITUTIONAL_LAW-ROUTE-CALL-01","statement":"On a route call, pick the constitutional home before grading policy strength. First cut homes that have no facts in the stem.","type":"call_focus","trigger":"The call asks for the best constitutional route and the choices name different constitutional homes.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"route-call cut","target_skill":"claim-home first","prompt":"A city occupancy rule excludes an aunt from living with her niece and the niece''s children. Which route: commerce, ordinary zoning, or family-integrity due process?","answer":"Family-integrity due process."},{"drill_type":"overclaim detector","target_skill":"tiered_absolute","prompt":"Classify: ''Aunts are never family for constitutional purposes.''","answer":"tiered_absolute; use the family-integrity Gold Key."},{"drill_type":"misfit detector","target_skill":"wrong constitutional home","prompt":"A local family-household ordinance is challenged. The answer says Dormant Commerce Clause only. Cut or keep?","answer":"Cut as misfit / NOT_RESPONSIVE."},{"drill_type":"bait-doctrine detector","target_skill":"neighboring context","prompt":"A residential code excludes a close relative from a household. The answer treats it as ordinary zoning rational basis with no family liberty concern. What is the breaker?","answer":"Neighboring zoning frame; direct family exclusion triggers the Gold Key."}]}'),
  ('8549fbd3-3cc3-59d1-9764-0eb65f4233c7', '21867_psalm_puzzle_booth', 'CRIMINAL_LAW', 'Homicide', 'Homicide', 'misdemeanor label vs cause link', 'At a Christian homeschool co-op fair in a rented convention hall, Peter ran a paid Bible-verse puzzle booth without the required county amusement permit, a misdemeanor. Ruth bought a ticket, completed the puzzle, collected a bookmark, and left. Later that afternoon, while helping Stephen hang a banner at a bakery for a separate youth-group fundraiser, Ruth stood on a defective stepstool, fell, and died. Peter was charged with involuntary manslaughter based on operating the booth without the permit. Is Peter guilty of misdemeanor manslaughter?', 'At a Christian homeschool co-op fair in a rented convention hall, Peter ran a paid Bible-verse puzzle booth without the required county amusement permit, a misdemeanor. Ruth bought a ticket, completed the puzzle, collected a bookmark, and left. Later that afternoon, while helping Stephen hang a banner at a bakery for a separate youth-group fundraiser, Ruth stood on a defective stepstool, fell, and died. Peter was charged with involuntary manslaughter based on operating the booth without the permit. Is Peter guilty of misdemeanor manslaughter?

Is Peter guilty of misdemeanor manslaughter?', 'Is Peter guilty of misdemeanor manslaughter?', 2, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ21867.md","source_question_id":"21867","variant_slug":"psalm_puzzle_booth","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"74020102","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"moderate","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A person commits a misdemeanor by running an unpermitted paid booth. The customer later dies in a separate accident. Is that misdemeanor enough for misdemeanor manslaughter?","review_truth":"Misdemeanor manslaughter needs a cause link; a misdemeanor that merely came first is not enough.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"CRIM-REM-CAUSE-LINK-01","title":"Misdemeanor label is not a death cause","signal":"A misdemeanor is followed by a death from a separate event.","student_move":"Ask whether the misdemeanor produced the death, not merely whether it came first.","tiny_rule":"No cause link, no misdemeanor manslaughter.","trap":"Counting chronology as causation.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CRIM-MISDEMEANOR-MANSLAUGHTER-01","statement":"Misdemeanor manslaughter still needs actual and proximate causation. A misdemeanor that merely happens before a death is not enough.","type":"threshold","trigger":"unlawful act followed by a separate death mechanism","authority":"MPC §2.03; People v. Penny; Logan/Ceasar line reflected in Oklahoma jury-instruction commentary","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CRIM-MISDEMEANOR-MANSLAUGHTER-01","statement":"When the array gives you a misdemeanor-label answer and a cause-link answer, lock the cause link before debating the label.","type":"cut","trigger":"one choice says the misdemeanor happened; another choice says the misdemeanor did not cause the death","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"wrong_element","prompt":"A defendant commits a permit misdemeanor. The victim later dies in an unrelated accident. Is the misdemeanor label enough?","answer":"No; check actual and proximate cause."},{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"overclaim","prompt":"Classify: ''Any death after a misdemeanor is manslaughter.''","answer":"tiered_absolute; NOT_TRUE."},{"drill_type":"anchor","target_skill":"misdemeanor manslaughter causation","prompt":"State the Gold Key for misdemeanor manslaughter.","answer":"The misdemeanor must actually and proximately cause the death."},{"drill_type":"trap_spotting","target_skill":"cause-link residual","prompt":"One answer says the misdemeanor happened. One answer says the misdemeanor did not cause the death. Which one is the safer residual?","answer":"The cause-link answer."}]}'),
  ('d70c2a6e-311a-5f75-bf94-cb8a56006557', '21869_tiny_house_hymn_sheet', 'CRIMINAL_LAW', 'Fourth Amendment', 'Arrest warrants and home entry', NULL, 'At Lydia’s tiny house, a private psalm-singing rehearsal has turned into a hymn-sheet stapling contest. Police, holding an arrest warrant for Timothy, get a tip that Timothy is helping there. They have no search warrant for Lydia’s home, no consent, and no emergency. They enter the tiny house and arrest Timothy in the prayer nook.', 'At Lydia’s tiny house, a private psalm-singing rehearsal has turned into a hymn-sheet stapling contest. Police, holding an arrest warrant for Timothy, get a tip that Timothy is helping there. They have no search warrant for Lydia’s home, no consent, and no emergency. They enter the tiny house and arrest Timothy in the prayer nook.

Was the entry lawful?', 'Was the entry lawful?', 3, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ21869.md","source_question_id":"21869","variant_slug":"tiny_house_hymn_sheet","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"75070201","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium-hard","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"Police have an arrest warrant for a suspect but enter someone else’s home to arrest him without a search warrant, consent, or exigency. Was the entry lawful?","review_truth":"An arrest warrant is not a search warrant for someone else’s home.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"CRIM-REM-THIRD-PARTY-HOME-01","title":"Person warrant vs place warrant","signal":"Arrest warrant for suspect + suspect inside someone else''s home.","student_move":"Ask whose home is being entered.","tiny_rule":"Own home may be Payton; third-party home needs Steagald search warrant or exception.","trap":"Arrest-warrant portability.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CRIM-THIRD-PARTY-HOME-01","statement":"An arrest warrant lets police seize the named suspect; it does not by itself authorize entry into someone else’s home. For a third party’s home, look for a search warrant for that home, consent, or exigency.","type":"distinction","trigger":"arrest warrant + suspect located in a third party''s home","authority":"Steagald v. United States, 451 U.S. 204 (1981); Payton v. New York, 445 U.S. 573 (1980)","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CRIM-ENTRY-FIRST-01","statement":"When the call asks whether entry into a home was lawful, decide the door before the room. A bedroom, prayer nook, or living room fact cannot supply authority to cross the threshold.","type":"cut","trigger":"answer turns on where the suspect was found after entry","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"anchor_recall","target_skill":"Gold Key recall","prompt":"Police have an arrest warrant for Paul and enter Ruth’s home to find him without a search warrant, consent, or exigency. Lawful entry?","answer":"No. Third-party home requires a search warrant or valid exception."},{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"Overclaim detection","prompt":"Cut: ''Yes, because an arrest warrant follows the suspect into any home.''","answer":"tiered_absolute; arrest warrant does not authorize entry into any third-party home."},{"drill_type":"issue_sense","target_skill":"Wrong element / wrong timing","prompt":"Call asks whether entry into Naomi’s home was lawful. Choice says no only because suspect was found in the pantry.","answer":"NOT_RESPONSIVE; room fact comes after entry."}]}'),
  ('110d352f-2867-5318-b660-cc816b2d45b5', '21872_scripturequest_price_gate', 'CONSTITUTIONAL_LAW', 'Dormant Commerce Clause', 'Extraterritorial regulation', 'in-state sales condition vs out-of-state price control', 'Ruth’s Workshop, Inc., a private company founded by Ruth, makes ScriptureQuest, a Bible-trivia board game sold through ordinary retail stores around the country. A state allows board-game makers to sell games in the state only if each month they certify that the price charged for the same game in every other state during that month is not lower than the in-state price. The state insists that it is regulating only sales inside its borders.', 'Ruth’s Workshop, Inc., a private company founded by Ruth, makes ScriptureQuest, a Bible-trivia board game sold through ordinary retail stores around the country. A state allows board-game makers to sell games in the state only if each month they certify that the price charged for the same game in every other state during that month is not lower than the in-state price. The state insists that it is regulating only sales inside its borders.

What is the company''s strongest dormant Commerce Clause objection?', 'What is the company''s strongest dormant Commerce Clause objection?', 3, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ21872.md","source_question_id":"21872","variant_slug":"scripturequest_price_gate","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"41030402","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium-hard","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A state says: sell here only if your out-of-state prices this month are not lower than your in-state price. What is the strongest DCC objection?","review_truth":"A state cannot condition in-state sales on the seller’s promise not to charge lower prices in other states.","dominant_trap":"D","anchor_card":{"card_id":"CONLAW-PRICE-GATE-01","title":"Price Gate Across State Lines","signal":"Sell here only if prices elsewhere satisfy this state’s rule.","student_move":"Follow the price lever across the border.","tiny_rule":"In-state access tied to out-of-state prices points to DCC extraterritorial price control.","trap":"Accepting the state’s in-state label.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONSTITUTIONAL_LAW-PRICE-AFFIRMATION-01","statement":"A state may not condition in-state sales on a seller’s prices charged wholly outside the state. Price-affirmation laws tying in-state access to out-of-state prices are classic dormant Commerce Clause danger zones.","type":"rule","trigger":"in-state sales permission depends on prices in other states","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONSTITUTIONAL_LAW-PRICE-LEVER-01","statement":"Ignore the state’s label and follow the lever. If the in-state sales gate moves when out-of-state prices move, the DCC objection is extraterritorial control.","type":"trap_spotting","trigger":"certify prices in every other state","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"overclaim","prompt":"Cut the choice: ''The state may control all nationwide prices once the seller enters its market.''","answer":"extreme_of_range; local sales power is being pushed to nationwide prices."},{"drill_type":"cut","target_skill":"misfit","prompt":"Call asks for a DCC objection. Choice says Article IV privileges and immunities is the only doctrine. Cut or keep?","answer":"Cut: misfit / wrong doctrine home."},{"drill_type":"anchor","target_skill":"DCC price-affirmation Gold Key","prompt":"State conditions in-state sales on prices charged in other states. Name the DCC objection.","answer":"Extraterritorial price control / price affirmation."},{"drill_type":"trap_spotting","target_skill":"state label vs practical effect","prompt":"The state says it regulates only in-state sales. What fact should you follow?","answer":"The certification lever tied to prices in every other state."}]}'),
  ('c7b198d2-209a-514e-a43d-e47f8db35f59', '21879_noahs_ark_puppet_set', 'CONTRACTS', 'Formation of Contracts', 'Mutual Assent / Offer and Acceptance', 'offer vs acceptance by required performance', 'On April 3, after hearing that Lydia was organizing a neighborhood Bible-story night, Peter sent Lydia a note offering to sell his handmade Noah’s Ark puppet set. Lydia wrote back on April 10 and told Peter that she would buy the puppet set for $175 if Peter delivered it to her courtyard studio on May 6. On May 5, Lydia decided that she would borrow puppets from Naomi instead and wrote Peter a note telling him that she was no longer interested in buying the puppet set. She was about to take the note to the mail drop when Peter knocked at her door. As soon as Lydia opened the door, Peter said, “I’ll bring you that puppet set tomorrow. I’ll just have to borrow Barnabas’s cargo tricycle to carry it.” Lydia said, “Never mind. I don’t want it,” and handed Peter the note she had written but not mailed.', 'On April 3, after hearing that Lydia was organizing a neighborhood Bible-story night, Peter sent Lydia a note offering to sell his handmade Noah’s Ark puppet set. Lydia wrote back on April 10 and told Peter that she would buy the puppet set for $175 if Peter delivered it to her courtyard studio on May 6. On May 5, Lydia decided that she would borrow puppets from Naomi instead and wrote Peter a note telling him that she was no longer interested in buying the puppet set. She was about to take the note to the mail drop when Peter knocked at her door. As soon as Lydia opened the door, Peter said, “I’ll bring you that puppet set tomorrow. I’ll just have to borrow Barnabas’s cargo tricycle to carry it.” Lydia said, “Never mind. I don’t want it,” and handed Peter the note she had written but not mailed.

Peter’s statement on May 5 that “I’ll bring you that puppet set tomorrow” was probably', 'Peter’s statement on May 5 that “I’ll bring you that puppet set tomorrow” was probably', 3, 'active', 'ccg_transform', 1, '{"source_file":"CQ21879.md","source_question_id":null,"variant_slug":"noahs_ark_puppet_set","batch":"cq-2026-06-12","outline_code":"55020101","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium-hard","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"Buyer says she will buy only if seller delivers on a date; seller says the day before that he will bring it tomorrow. What is seller’s statement?","review_truth":"A promise to deliver later is not delivery; if delivery is the required acceptance, the promise is best labeled an offer.","dominant_trap":"acceptance-family trap; measured rates unavailable","anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-CONTRACTS-OFFER-ACCEPTANCE-MODE-01","title":"Mode-of-Acceptance Label Drill","signal":"One party says “I will buy if you deliver,” and the other says “I will deliver.”","student_move":"Ask what act the first statement required and whether that act has happened.","tiny_rule":"Words promising future delivery are not the delivery itself.","trap":"Calling the future promise an acceptance or commencement.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONTRACTS-MODE-ACCEPTANCE-01","statement":"If an offer requires acceptance by a specified performance or tender, a later promise to perform is not the invited acceptance; it is at most a new offer or counteroffer.","type":"distinction","trigger":"A buyer says “I will buy if you deliver,” and the seller later says “I will deliver.”","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts §§30, 45, 50; UCC §2-206 language/circumstances caveat.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONTRACTS-NOAHS-ARK-01","statement":"Classify the sentence before debating doctrine: future-tense words are not performance already begun.","type":"cut","trigger":"Choice labels a promise to bring later as performance now.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"label_classification","target_skill":"offer_acceptance_mode","prompt":"Buyer: “I will buy if you deliver Friday.” Seller Thursday: “I will deliver Friday.” Classify seller’s words.","answer":"Offer, not acceptance, if delivery was the required acceptance."},{"drill_type":"verb_tense_cut","target_skill":"future_words_vs_performance","prompt":"Choice says “commencement of performance.” Stem says “I will bring it tomorrow.” Cut or keep?","answer":"Cut; future words are not present performance."},{"drill_type":"wrong_frame","target_skill":"ratification_trap","prompt":"Can a party ratify an acceptance that never legally occurred?","answer":"No."}]}')
