Gold Key: A complete integration blocks prior negotiations used to add or contradict terms, but it does not block prior negotiations used to explain an ambiguity in the writing.

Silver Key: First label the proffer’s purpose. Here the purpose is interpretation of signer capacity, not addition of a side term.', NULL, 'REM-CONTRACTS-PER-AMBIGUITY-01'),
  ('b5b5f067-b155-5777-80cc-770eee7fa133', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14379_psalm_lightshow'), 'D', 'Yes, because the evidence would not contradict either the recital or the way Lydia signed the contract.', 0, '["wrong_element"]', '["integration_bars_all_context","noncontradiction_is_enough","prior_understandings_always_change_legal_effect"]', 'The student notices that the offered evidence does not contradict the paper. The skipped move is that complete integration requires an ambiguity hook, not merely a non-contradiction hook.', 'No contradiction is not the controlling permission point in a complete-integration question; the controlling point is ambiguity.', 'Require the ambiguity reason, not just a no-contradiction reason.', 'REM-CONTRACTS-PER-AMBIGUITY-01'),
  ('1fbb4887-64be-5b2a-8d89-6ebd01330a17', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14382_bethlehem_lanterns'), 'A', 'Since Peter’s began distributing Barnabas lantern kits, sales of Bethlehem Star lantern kits have fallen 4% in the eleven-town district.', 0, '["wrong_element"]', '["direct_word_match_controls","business_decline_equals_breach","competitor_conduct_equals_distributor_breach"]', 'The student sees a sales decline and treats business harm as proof of breach. The breaker is that harm is not the missing contract term or the breach fact.', 'The sales drop is an effect fact. It does not supply the missing contract restriction or show the distributor breached the written promise.', 'Ask what fact creates the missing restriction, not what fact shows downstream harm.', 'RC-CONTRACTS-TRADE-USAGE-SOURCE'),
  ('8fbaec56-a2fc-5f79-a224-19b8dfeb48e6', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14382_bethlehem_lanterns'), 'B', 'For many years in the private Christian craft-kit industry, it has been a uniform practice for regional distributors to handle only one brand of devotional lantern kit.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'It gives objective trade usage that can supply or qualify the silent term.', NULL, 'RC-CONTRACTS-TRADE-USAGE-SOURCE'),
  ('0a829b22-28e1-5bb0-ae51-bd88dcc2498a', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14382_bethlehem_lanterns'), 'C', 'Barnabas Bright Crafts’ national advertising campaign disparages Bethlehem Star lantern kits by saying, “Faith does not need batteries, and neither should your lantern.”', 0, '["wrong_element"]', '["direct_word_match_controls","business_decline_equals_breach","competitor_conduct_equals_distributor_breach"]', 'The student sees a competitor attacking the product and treats market hostility as breach. The breaker is that the ad is the competitor''s act, not the distributor''s promise or contract meaning.', 'The competitor''s ad is a wrong-actor fact. It does not show what the distributor promised or how the written agreement should be read.', 'Tie every fact back to the distributor''s promise or to a source for the missing term.', 'RC-CONTRACTS-TRADE-USAGE-SOURCE'),
  ('90e619b0-3749-52f3-84d8-f62f8dd29461', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14382_bethlehem_lanterns'), 'D', 'Before the written agreement was signed, a representative of Peter’s told Lydia Lantern Works that the arrangement would be “exclusive.”', 0, '["bait_doctrine","wrong_frame"]', '["direct_word_match_controls","business_decline_equals_breach","competitor_conduct_equals_distributor_breach"]', 'The student sees the exact word ''exclusive'' and overvalues the direct quote. The breaker is the Gold Key: prior oral exclusivity is the wrong source when the UCC source comparison favors consistent trade usage.', 'The oral exclusivity phrase is attractive because it speaks directly to exclusivity. The Gold Key keeps the source frame straight: prior oral contradiction is not the same as admissible trade usage.', 'Use the Gold Key: trade usage can enter to explain a silence; prior oral contradiction is vulnerable.', 'RC-CONTRACTS-TRADE-USAGE-SOURCE'),
  ('fff753c4-007b-5172-b504-ce3f340e40f7', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14391_noble_fir_nativity'), 'A', 'If a contract was formed, it is voidable because of mutual mistake.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'A contract was formed (acceptance dispatched before any rejection or revocation was received). The discharge is framed by the NCBE as mutual mistake of a basic assumption (legality); supervening impracticability is also defensible per the official scoring note.', NULL, 'RC-CONTRACTS-SUPERVENING-ILLEGALITY-01'),
  ('3c95daa0-d365-5f3c-acf7-f23ea4f59a5a', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14391_noble_fir_nativity'), 'B', 'No contract was formed, because Mary''s June 11 rejection was effective on dispatch.', 0, '["wrong_element","wrong_frame"]', '["mailbox rule applies to all mailed documents","resale to a third party always revokes the offer","supervening illegality only triggers impracticability"]', 'The student sees ''mailed'' and reaches for the famous mailbox rule, but the rule covers acceptance, not rejection. The breaker is the Silver Key: rejection is effective on receipt, and Mary retrieved the rejection the same hour so it never reached Paul.', 'The mailbox rule covers acceptance (dispatch), not rejection (receipt). Mary retrieved the rejection the same hour, so the rejection never reached Paul; the acceptance dispatched the same hour was effective on dispatch.', 'Ask whether the rule applies to the act (rejection) or the counterpart (acceptance). Mailbox = acceptance only; rejection = receipt.', 'RC-CONTRACTS-SUPERVENING-ILLEGALITY-01'),
  ('60f4dab3-f6ad-51a0-96f4-bf6bbd2b5aac', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14391_noble_fir_nativity'), 'C', 'No contract was formed, because Paul''s June 10 sale of the firs to another merchant revoked the offer to Mary.', 0, '["backwards"]', '["mailbox rule applies to all mailed documents","resale to a third party always revokes the offer","supervening illegality only triggers impracticability"]', 'The student sees a resale to a third party and calls it revocation. The breaker is the notice element: revocation requires receipt of notice by the offeree, and the stem says Mary was unaware of the resale when she dispatched her acceptance.', 'Revocation of an offer requires receipt of notice by the offeree. The resale to a third party is not, by itself, notice to Mary; the stem states she was unaware of the resale when she dispatched her acceptance.', 'Ask whether the original offeree actually learned of the resale before accepting. If not, no revocation.', 'RC-CONTRACTS-SUPERVENING-ILLEGALITY-01'),
  ('145ca458-57e3-5ce0-a811-c8a6c0d314c4', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14391_noble_fir_nativity'), 'D', 'If a contract was formed, it is unenforceable because of supervening impracticability.', 0, '["bait_doctrine"]', '["mailbox rule applies to all mailed documents","resale to a third party always revokes the offer","supervening illegality only triggers impracticability"]', 'The student identifies a clean ground for discharge (federal quarantine makes performance illegal) and picks the most intuitive theory. The breaker is the Gold Key: supervening criminal statute triggers BOTH mistake and impracticability, and the NCBE framed key is mutual mistake; the official scoring note acknowledged impracticability was also credited.', 'The federal PPA quarantine does make performance illegal and supervening impracticability (Restatement § 261) does discharge the duty; the NCBE scoring note for QID 14311 acknowledged that this answer was also credited. The discrimination from A is the NCBE''s framed key, not a structural cut on D.', 'Apply the Gold Key: supervening criminal statute triggers BOTH mistake and impracticability; the NCBE framed key is mutual mistake, with impracticability co-credited per the official scoring note.', 'RC-CONTRACTS-SUPERVENING-ILLEGALITY-01'),
  ('5632a689-5a6e-5362-b338-883d37670b94', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14394_lydia_puppet_stage'), 'A', 'The loan to Lydia was made without any agreement concerning the applicable interest rate.', 0, '["wrong_element","wrong_frame"]', '["consideration_must_benefit_promisor","one_year_sof_overgrab","missing_interest_rate_fatal","family_guaranty_informal"]', 'The missing interest rate sounds like a concrete contract defect. The breaker is that Paul is sued on his guaranty, and the loan can be enforced with a reasonable supplied term.', 'The interest-rate detail attacks the loan’s term, not the enforceability of Paul’s oral guaranty.', 'Ask whether the choice attacks Paul’s guaranty or Lydia’s loan.', 'CONTRACTS-SOF-SURETY-MAIN-PURPOSE'),
  ('4f45eb64-40b0-57d8-9221-fe88a2e24829', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14394_lydia_puppet_stage'), 'B', 'Paul’s conditional promise to Barnabas was not made for the primary purpose of benefiting Paul.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'B is correct. Gold Key: An oral promise to pay another person’s debt is usually in the Statute of Frauds. The main-purpose exception removes the promise from the writing requirement only when the guarantor’s leading purpose is the guarantor’s own economic advantage.

Paul’s words create a secondary promise: if Lydia does not repay Barnabas, Paul will. The facts show the money went to Lydia for her puppet stage. Nothing in the stem gives Paul a personal economic stake. Silver Key: sort the defenses by whose promise they attack. The lawsuit is against Paul, so the best defense is the one attacking Paul’s oral guaranty, not Lydia’s loan terms.', NULL, 'CONTRACTS-SOF-SURETY-MAIN-PURPOSE'),
  ('40a0fe14-556e-54ac-a99a-73f40cc15157', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14394_lydia_puppet_stage'), 'C', 'Paul received no consideration for his conditional promise to Barnabas.', 0, '["wrong_element","wrong_frame"]', '["consideration_must_benefit_promisor","one_year_sof_overgrab","missing_interest_rate_fatal","family_guaranty_informal"]', 'The guarantor received no cash, so students treat benefit to the guarantor as the consideration test. The breaker is that bargained-for performance can be given to the borrower instead.', 'The no-personal-benefit point targets the wrong consideration question; Barnabas’s cash to Lydia can be consideration for Paul’s promise.', 'Check whether Barnabas gave bargained-for value after Paul’s promise.', 'CONTRACTS-SOF-SURETY-MAIN-PURPOSE'),
  ('e70e0b49-980a-52b3-955b-f008a17b7b1f', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14394_lydia_puppet_stage'), 'D', 'Paul’s conditional promise to Barnabas was not to be performed in less than one year from the time it was made.', 0, '["bait_doctrine","wrong_frame"]', '["consideration_must_benefit_promisor","one_year_sof_overgrab","missing_interest_rate_fatal","family_guaranty_informal"]', 'The one-year phrase sounds like a familiar Statute of Frauds category. The breaker is that the best defense is the suretyship writing gate, not the neighboring one-year lane.', 'The one-year Statute of Frauds lane is not Paul’s best defense; the suretyship writing lane is the responsive defense.', 'Use the phrase ''If she does not pay, I will'' to route to suretyship.', 'CONTRACTS-SOF-SURETY-MAIN-PURPOSE'),
  ('9a37ab6f-50c9-52e1-8f63-6a2bea63d562', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14396_easter_handbell_plan'), 'A', 'Yes, because the written protection agreement will be strictly construed against Barnabas as the drafter.', 0, '["bait_doctrine","wrong_frame"]', '["all statutory violations void contracts","yes outcome makes the reason safe","drafter construction applies to every insurance-like contract dispute"]', 'This choice gives the correct yes outcome and uses a familiar drafter-construction rule. The breaker is that the call asks enforceability after a statutory defect, not interpretation of uncertain words.', 'This sells the correct outcome through a familiar drafting rule. The breaker is the call: there is no interpretation fight, so strict construction answers a different question.', 'Say the call aloud: enforceability after statutory violation, not construction of ambiguous language.', 'REM-CONTRACTS-PROTECTED-STATUTE-01'),
  ('2e2e47c3-1901-56d8-96dd-3afc80721a74', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14396_easter_handbell_plan'), 'B', 'Yes, because Lydia belongs to the class of persons the statute was intended to protect.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'B is the only answer that is both true under the Gold Key and responsive to the enforceability call.', NULL, 'REM-CONTRACTS-PROTECTED-STATUTE-01'),
  ('36485390-0d57-56e3-82d8-e29e624a3d0d', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14396_easter_handbell_plan'), 'C', 'No, because the protection agreement is not a divisible contract.', 0, '["misfit","wrong_frame"]', '["all statutory violations void contracts","yes outcome makes the reason safe","drafter construction applies to every insurance-like contract dispute"]', 'This choice sounds like a contract-law gate because divisibility can matter in partial-performance disputes. The breaker is that no one is trying to split the agreement into enforceable and unenforceable parts.', 'This sells a lawyerly contract doctrine. The breaker is the call: divisibility does not answer whether a statutory violation blocks enforcement by the protected party.', 'Cut doctrines with no matching stem fact.', 'REM-CONTRACTS-PROTECTED-STATUTE-01'),
  ('1c0bf46f-2c4b-5eda-ad03-ea7e7297d509', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14396_easter_handbell_plan'), 'D', 'No, because the protection agreement violated the statute.', 0, '["half_truth","violation_vs_remedy"]', '["all statutory violations void contracts","yes outcome makes the reason safe","drafter construction applies to every insurance-like contract dispute"]', 'This choice correctly notices the statutory violation and treats that as the end of the case. The breaker is the protected-class exception: the violation does not automatically defeat the protected party’s enforcement claim.', 'This sells the general illegality rule. The breaker is the Gold Key: violation alone is not enough when nonenforcement would injure the class the statute protects.', 'Ask who the statute was meant to protect before treating violation as forfeiture.', 'REM-CONTRACTS-PROTECTED-STATUTE-01'),
  ('5c12dcbd-9312-5db0-ab77-a5a3a0bf6d13', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14397_fish_keychains'), 'A', 'Yes, because Lydia relied on an implied promise to pay when she brought the keychains to Daniel.', 0, '["wrong_element","wrong_frame"]', '["missing_price_kills_goods_contract","delivery_equals_enforceability","formation_equals_enforceability"]', 'Delivery makes the seller look like it changed position in reliance on the phone call. The breaker is that the call asks enforceability against a buyer who refused to receive and accept the goods.', 'The delivery/reliance story feels like conduct that should count, but it does not answer the writing gate. The buyer refused to receive and accept the goods, so the partial-performance path is not triggered.', 'Return to the call and test the UCC writing gate first.', 'CONTRACTS-SOF-OPEN-PRICE-01'),
  ('35d0dbc3-ac9f-51ad-9198-1296cb1bf9f9', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14397_fish_keychains'), 'B', 'No, because the parties never agreed on the price of the keychains.', 0, '["flat_misstatement"]', '["missing_price_kills_goods_contract","delivery_equals_enforceability","formation_equals_enforceability"]', 'A missing price looks like a missing essential term. The breaker is the Gold Key that an open price can be filled when the parties otherwise intended a sale.', 'The missing-price fact is visible and tempting. The Gold Key separates price formation from the writing requirement, so missing price is not the breaker.', 'Recall that UCC § 2-305 can supply a reasonable price.', 'CONTRACTS-SOF-OPEN-PRICE-01'),
  ('1ef08239-38ba-5a57-93f6-f996f52df44e', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14397_fish_keychains'), 'C', 'Yes, because leaving the price open does not prevent a valid sale-of-goods contract when the parties otherwise intended to contract.', 0, '["bait_doctrine","wrong_frame"]', '["missing_price_kills_goods_contract","delivery_equals_enforceability","formation_equals_enforceability"]', 'This is the dominant trap because it states a real UCC open-price rule. The breaker is that formation does not remove the separate writing requirement for a goods deal over $500.', 'The open-price rule is true and therefore highly attractive. Silver Key: it answers formation but not enforceability, so it misses the writing gate.', 'Use the Silver Key: lock enforceable, then check the writing gate.', 'CONTRACTS-SOF-OPEN-PRICE-01'),
  ('32820189-7476-5e22-b7a5-a416e4582519', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14397_fish_keychains'), 'D', 'No, because the parties did not put their agreement in writing.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'D is correct. This is a sale of ordinary goods for about $9,600, and no writing exists. Gold Key: a sale-of-goods deal priced at $500 or more must satisfy the UCC writing requirement unless an exception applies. Lydia’s delivery did not solve that problem because Daniel refused to receive and accept the keychains. Silver Key: lock the call word enforceable before rewarding a Yes answer; formation and enforceability are separate gates.', NULL, 'CONTRACTS-SOF-OPEN-PRICE-01'),
  ('e1773321-5472-5d72-b775-766d832a2c09', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14400_mechanical_camel'), 'A', 'After the camel had completed four Advent tours for Daniel, it was discovered that, because of a clerical error unknown to both parties, the maker’s certificate listed an obscure prop shop as the builder rather than the famous Jerusalem Stageworks workshop that had actually built it.', 0, '["wrong_element","wrong_frame"]', '["all_hidden_facts_support_rescission","worst_harm_equals_best_answer","pedigree_errors_are_always_material","side_use_equals_basic_assumption"]', 'A student sees a certificate error and thinks authenticity or pedigree automatically matters. The breaker is direction and purpose: the fact helps the buyer and does not attack the main performance use.', 'The maker-certificate fact tempts a pedigree frame, but it gives Daniel a better builder than the paperwork suggested and does not supply an adverse buyer-side mistake.', 'Ask whether the buyer got a worse thing or a better thing than believed.', 'GK-CONTRACTS-MECHANICAL-CAMEL-01'),
  ('7a14e142-8481-59c6-86d1-72efe9939bf7', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14400_mechanical_camel'), 'B', 'A week after the sale, while Daniel was testing the camel, it suddenly jerked, threw him from its platform, and injured him so severely that he was hospitalized for seven months and needed eighteen months for full recovery.', 0, '["wrong_element","violation_vs_remedy"]', '["all_hidden_facts_support_rescission","worst_harm_equals_best_answer","pedigree_errors_are_always_material","side_use_equals_basic_assumption"]', 'A student sees severe injury and treats the worst harm as the best legal answer. The breaker is risk allocation: the injury flows from the warned-about jerking trait, not from an undisclosed basic-assumption mistake.', 'The injury fact tempts a damages frame, but it comes from the same jerking trait Daniel was warned about and therefore does not support mistake-based rescission.', 'Match the later harm to the exact trait disclosed before sale.', 'GK-CONTRACTS-MECHANICAL-CAMEL-01'),
  ('dec7802f-9e85-5c7f-84f9-14e52486085b', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14400_mechanical_camel'), 'C', 'In its debut pageant after the sale, the camel rolled smoothly to the center mark but shut down permanently during the final scene because of a rare internal crack in the main drive shaft that was undiscoverable except by complete disassembly.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'C is correct. Daniel’s best rescission scenario is the hidden internal crack. The defect existed at the time of sale, could not be found without complete disassembly, and destroyed the camel’s core expected use in its debut pageant.

Gold Key: A mistake supports rescission only when the mistaken fact existed at contracting, went to a basic assumption with material adverse effect, and the party seeking rescission did not bear that risk. The hidden drive-shaft crack fits that pattern. Silver Key: On a buyer-rather-than-seller rescission call, sort each scenario by direction and purpose. C is the only scenario where the buyer is harmed by a hidden defect that defeats the main bargain.', NULL, 'GK-CONTRACTS-MECHANICAL-CAMEL-01'),
  ('18235357-698b-505f-951b-e14a01d002ff', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14400_mechanical_camel'), 'D', 'The camel earned Daniel $4.8 million over a four-year touring career, but when retired it was found to have nonstandard internal electronics and to be useless as a template for manufacturing replica camels.', 0, '["wrong_element","wrong_frame"]', '["all_hidden_facts_support_rescission","worst_harm_equals_best_answer","pedigree_errors_are_always_material","side_use_equals_basic_assumption"]', 'A student sees a post-retirement defect and treats every hidden disappointment as a rescission fact. The breaker is that the prop already performed the purchased use, and replica manufacture is a collateral side use.', 'The replica-template fact tempts a side-use frame, but the prop already served the touring-use purpose for three years and the side-use defect does not hit the purchase''s basic assumption.', 'Look for whether the item already delivered the main expected value.', 'GK-CONTRACTS-MECHANICAL-CAMEL-01'),
  ('577a86bb-b75d-5558-8623-effd56b47025', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14406_advent_lanterns'), 'A', 'No contract was formed, because Lydia Supply’s purchase order was merely a revocable offer.', 0, '["wrong_element","wrong_frame"]', '["revocable_offer_means_no_contract","one_sided_cancellation_equals_option","either_party_overread"]', 'The student sees ''revocable offer'' and treats revocability as the reason no contract exists. The breaker is that the stem gives an acceptance and no cancellation, so revocability before acceptance does not answer the formation call.', 'Revocability before acceptance is not the dispositive point after the seller''s acceptance and with no cancellation fact.', 'Say: revocable is not revoked; move to the cancellation-reservation issue.', 'GK-CONTRACTS-ILLUSORY-CANCEL-01'),
  ('a7d5267e-2d69-5596-9dd2-ac0e9c2b2bdc', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14406_advent_lanterns'), 'B', 'A contract was formed, but before August 12 it was an option contract cancelable only at Lydia Supply’s election.', 0, '["bait_doctrine","wrong_frame"]', '["revocable_offer_means_no_contract","one_sided_cancellation_equals_option","either_party_overread"]', 'The student sees a one-sided cancellation power and reaches for the familiar option-contract label. The breaker is the Gold Key: a one-sided cancellation escape makes the buyer''s promise illusory instead of creating a binding option contract.', 'The option-contract label is a neighboring contract concept, but this one-sided cancellation term creates an illusory promise problem instead.', 'Ask whether the party with the cancellation right made any real commitment before the cutoff.', 'GK-CONTRACTS-ILLUSORY-CANCEL-01'),
  ('6628facb-fce3-5350-b7c2-e3002aacba6e', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14406_advent_lanterns'), 'C', 'No contract was formed, because Lydia Supply reserved the right to cancel the order.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'Only C gives the no-contract result and ties it to the one-sided cancellation reservation.', NULL, 'GK-CONTRACTS-ILLUSORY-CANCEL-01'),
  ('81c0b96d-ac83-5554-a49f-2042b50201e0', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14406_advent_lanterns'), 'D', 'A contract was formed, but before August 12 it was terminable at the election of either party.', 0, '["flat_misstatement"]', '["revocable_offer_means_no_contract","one_sided_cancellation_equals_option","either_party_overread"]', 'The student turns one cancellation right into a balanced mutual-termination term. The breaker is that the stem gives the cancellation power only to Lydia Supply, not to both parties.', 'The stem gives the cancellation power only to Lydia Supply, not to both parties.', 'Underline who has the cancellation power; if only one side has it, cut any ''either party'' answer.', 'GK-CONTRACTS-ILLUSORY-CANCEL-01'),
  ('77a2035f-74f0-5f57-8532-222e5c240c24', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14409_sunken_ark_hall'), 'A', 'Barnabas is excused, because the contract is voidable on account of the parties’ mutual mistake concerning an essential underlying fact.', 0, '["half_truth","wrong_frame"]', '["mutual_mistake_without_risk_check","any_major_cost_is_impracticability","actual_ignorance_controls"]', 'A student sees both parties lacked the same subsurface fact and reaches for mutual mistake. The breaker is the risk bucket: the specialist contractor treated limited site knowledge as enough.', 'The mutual-mistake frame is attractive because both sides lacked the same subsurface fact. Gold Key: the missing condition is risk; a specialist who took the fixed-price excavation job and skipped testing carries the site-condition risk.', 'Add the Gold Key: mutual mistake fails if the contractor bore the risk.', 'REM-CONTRACTS-RISK-BUCKET-01'),
  ('46ab681e-6544-5fb3-aa8e-b04eea848811', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14409_sunken_ark_hall'), 'B', 'Lydia prevails, because Barnabas assumed the risk of encountering subsurface limestone that was unknown to Lydia.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'B is the only answer that is both true and responsive because it identifies the risk allocation that defeats the excuse theories.', NULL, 'REM-CONTRACTS-RISK-BUCKET-01'),
  ('d4a43bee-beaf-5930-ac57-1438808af4fc', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14409_sunken_ark_hall'), 'C', 'Lydia prevails, because Barnabas did not know that subsurface limestone was present in the area.', 0, '["wrong_element"]', '["mutual_mistake_without_risk_check","any_major_cost_is_impracticability","actual_ignorance_controls"]', 'A student notices the true fact that the contractor did not actually know about the limestone. The breaker is that actual ignorance is not the winning reason when assumption of risk controls.', 'The ignorance fact is true but points to the wrong element. Silver Key: check the because-clause; the owner wins because risk was allocated to the contractor, not because the contractor lacked actual knowledge.', 'Ask whether the reason explains owner victory; replace actual knowledge with risk allocation.', 'REM-CONTRACTS-RISK-BUCKET-01'),
  ('ea364d86-b124-5986-8d79-64dca3158be0', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14409_sunken_ark_hall'), 'D', 'Barnabas is excused under the modern doctrine of supervening impossibility, which includes severe impracticability.', 0, '["extreme_of_range","wrong_frame"]', '["mutual_mistake_without_risk_check","any_major_cost_is_impracticability","actual_ignorance_controls"]', 'A student sees a large new bill and reaches for impracticability. The breaker is that this is still ordinary fixed-price construction risk rather than extreme impracticability.', 'Impracticability is attractive because the project became more expensive. The added cost is a fixed-price construction risk on these facts, not the kind of extreme event that discharges the duty.', 'Check whether the cost increase is extreme and outside the risk of the bargain.', 'REM-CONTRACTS-RISK-BUCKET-01'),
  ('55446416-524e-5d37-823d-b36032f6f579', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14412_gospel_puzzle_invoice'), 'A', 'Yes, because a promise allowing Lydia to postpone payment of a past-due fee is enforceable without consideration.', 0, '["flat_misstatement"]', '["bargain_language_equals_consideration","delay_equals_new_consideration","wrong_promise_supported"]', 'A student remembers that parties can rearrange payment timing and treats delay as naturally enforceable. The breaker is that an already-due fixed debt needs new consideration to make the creditor’s no-suit promise enforceable.', 'The delay-without-consideration statement invents a no-consideration exception for an already-due fixed debt.', 'Mark the debt as already due and ask what new consideration the debtor gave.', 'CONTRACTS-ANCHOR-PREEXISTING-DUTY-01'),
  ('10004637-6651-553f-9994-ac34c3ddc6e1', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14412_gospel_puzzle_invoice'), 'B', 'No, because there was no consideration to support Stephen’s promise not to sue.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'It is the only answer that identifies the enforceability problem with Stephen’s no-suit promise.', NULL, 'CONTRACTS-ANCHOR-PREEXISTING-DUTY-01'),
  ('a2a6dc7e-cf15-575c-9971-19a4af2f1291', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14412_gospel_puzzle_invoice'), 'C', 'Yes, because Lydia bargained for Stephen’s forbearance.', 0, '["wrong_element","wrong_frame"]', '["bargain_language_equals_consideration","delay_equals_new_consideration","wrong_promise_supported"]', 'A student sees the word bargained and assumes any bargain language supplies consideration. The breaker is that the motion depends on enforcing Stephen’s no-suit promise, and Lydia gave only what she already owed.', 'The bargain language proves what Lydia wanted, not what new consideration she gave to support Stephen’s no-suit promise.', 'Identify the promise the debtor is trying to enforce: the creditor’s no-suit promise.', 'CONTRACTS-ANCHOR-PREEXISTING-DUTY-01'),
  ('fb000f09-2650-5208-99e1-50dc08eaf0dd', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14412_gospel_puzzle_invoice'), 'D', 'No, because there was no consideration to support Lydia’s promise to pay $1,840 on November 15.', 0, '["backwards","wrong_frame"]', '["bargain_language_equals_consideration","delay_equals_new_consideration","wrong_promise_supported"]', 'A student correctly senses that no consideration exists somewhere in the new exchange. The breaker is that the choice points to the wrong promise: Stephen’s forbearance can support Lydia’s promise, not the reverse.', 'The consideration-support direction is reversed; Stephen’s forbearance can support Lydia’s promise, while Lydia’s old duty does not support Stephen’s promise.', 'Put arrows on the exchange: forbearance supports debtor’s promise; same old debt does not support creditor’s promise.', 'CONTRACTS-ANCHOR-PREEXISTING-DUTY-01'),
  ('09c3ade2-a2d6-5c48-9b4b-b213aec9fffe', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14418_jericho_lamp_credit'), 'A', 'By providing the goods to Lydia on credit, Barnabas effectively accepted Paul''s offer for a unilateral contract.', 0, '["wrong_element"]', '["not_call_misread","consideration_must_flow_to_promisor","notice_never_required_for_performance_acceptance","signed_writing_equals_answer"]', 'The credit extension looks like a concrete event that should form the deal, and that instinct is right. The negative call makes that accurate statement a trap because it asks for the NOT accurate statement.', 'Choice A is an accurate unilateral-contract statement, so it answers the affirmative version of the call instead of the NOT-accurate call.', 'Say out loud: I am picking the inaccurate statement, so accurate formation rules are out.', 'REM-CONTRACTS-NEGATIVE-NOTICE-01'),
  ('2f129050-46cc-5878-9106-1e85f505a418', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14418_jericho_lamp_credit'), 'B', 'Paul''s promise is enforceable by Barnabas whether or not Barnabas gave Paul seasonable notice of the extension of credit to Lydia.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'Only B is inaccurate under the notice rule, and the negative call asks for the inaccurate statement.', NULL, 'REM-CONTRACTS-NEGATIVE-NOTICE-01'),
  ('23b1f5a7-60ca-59d6-9a67-97c349b573fd', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14418_jericho_lamp_credit'), 'C', 'It was important to the enforceability of Paul''s promise to Barnabas that it be embodied in a signed writing.', 0, '["wrong_element"]', '["not_call_misread","consideration_must_flow_to_promisor","notice_never_required_for_performance_acceptance","signed_writing_equals_answer"]', 'The signed note feels like formal legal magic, and for a suretyship promise that instinct is right. The breaker is the call inversion: accurate statements are not responsive to a NOT accurate question.', 'Choice C is an accurate signed-writing statement for a suretyship promise, so it is not the inaccurate statement requested.', 'Confirm that the signed-writing statement is accurate, then eliminate it because the call asks for NOT accurate.', 'REM-CONTRACTS-NEGATIVE-NOTICE-01'),
  ('d2c2c18c-20a9-5c22-908a-3fc236326eb3', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14418_jericho_lamp_credit'), 'D', 'Although Paul received no consideration from Lydia, Paul''s promise is enforceable by Barnabas.', 0, '["wrong_element"]', '["not_call_misread","consideration_must_flow_to_promisor","notice_never_required_for_performance_acceptance","signed_writing_equals_answer"]', 'The guarantor received nothing from the buyer, so the choice sounds like a no-consideration flaw. The creditor''s extension of credit is the requested performance, making the statement accurate and therefore not the negative-stem target.', 'Choice D is an accurate consideration statement because the seller''s credit extension supplies the requested exchange, so it is not the negative-stem target.', 'Look for the creditor''s requested performance; extending credit is the exchange.', 'REM-CONTRACTS-NEGATIVE-NOTICE-01'),
  ('afe167d6-2dc4-58fd-9e77-d3fa0ab71c04', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14424_christmas_pageant_lantern_bid'), 'A', 'Paul was negligent in erroneously calculating the amount of his sub-bid.', 0, '["wrong_element"]', '["mitigation proves liability","fault makes an offer irrevocable","clean hands substitute for reliance"]', 'A student sees Paul caused the mistake and treats blame as the best recovery fact. The breaker is that blame does not show Esther was legally locked into relying on the bid.', 'Negligent calculation sounds like blame, but the recovery theory needs a fact showing the bid was temporarily irrevocable.', 'Ask whether the fact made the bid temporarily irrevocable before acceptance.', 'REM-CONTRACTS-SUB_BID_LOCKIN-01'),
  ('ce419657-c580-58dc-b90e-bbaed0ac9d79', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14424_christmas_pageant_lantern_bid'), 'B', 'After Paul''s notice of revocation, Esther made a reasonable effort to subcontract with another lighting installer at the lowest possible price.', 0, '["wrong_element"]', '["mitigation proves liability","fault makes an offer irrevocable","clean hands substitute for reliance"]', 'A student sees a responsible substitute-contract search and thinks mitigation strengthens damages. The breaker is that mitigation comes after revocation and does not make the original sub-bid temporarily irrevocable.', 'Mitigation after revocation sounds responsible, but it addresses damages rather than the lock-in fact that makes the bid enforceable.', 'Separate recovery right from damages cleanup; choose the fact that creates the recovery right.', 'REM-CONTRACTS-SUB_BID_LOCKIN-01'),
  ('f052ceb5-edf5-5038-a1c7-ad6a99057575', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14424_christmas_pageant_lantern_bid'), 'C', 'Lydia had required Esther to submit a bid bond, and Esther could not have withdrawn or amended her prime bid without forfeiting that bond.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'It is the only choice that supplies the lock-in reliance fact needed for recovery.', NULL, 'REM-CONTRACTS-SUB_BID_LOCKIN-01'),
  ('9642e971-a745-5d13-a944-d123d7d20d40', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14424_christmas_pageant_lantern_bid'), 'D', 'Esther dealt with all of her subcontractors in good faith and without seeking to renegotiate lower prices they had bid.', 0, '["wrong_element"]', '["mitigation proves liability","fault makes an offer irrevocable","clean hands substitute for reliance"]', 'A student sees fair dealing and no bid shopping as morally clean conduct by Esther. The breaker is that general good faith with subcontractors does not show she could not escape the prime bid after Paul''s revocation.', 'General good faith with other subcontractors sounds clean, but it does not show that the prime bid was locked before Paul revoked.', 'Look for a fact tying reliance to the prime bid, not broad good conduct.', 'REM-CONTRACTS-SUB_BID_LOCKIN-01'),
  ('cb35aded-c5f4-52be-9885-c06e787991ce', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14430_psalm_room_option'), 'A', 'When the September 3 writing was signed, Daniel said to Timothy, "Don''t worry about the $75; the recital of ''$75 to be paid'' makes this binding."', 0, '["flat_misstatement","wrong_frame"]', '["no_option_means_no_offer","recital_equals_payment","dealer_status_changes_common_law","preparation_equals_acceptance"]', 'The recital sounds like formal contract language that makes the option binding. The breaker is the written actual-payment condition.', 'The choice tries to make an unpaid recital override the written actual-payment condition.', 'Underline ''only if and when the $75 option payment is actually received'' before reading choices.', 'CON-FORM-OPTION-REVOCATION-01'),
  ('dcfac03e-f5ac-58d2-b81f-b67f89ffd482', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14430_psalm_room_option'), 'B', 'Timothy did not know Daniel had leased the Psalm Room to Naomi when Daniel received Timothy''s letter and check on September 15.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'B alone supplies the notice fact that keeps Daniel''s inconsistent lease from terminating Timothy''s power of acceptance before Timothy accepted.', NULL, 'CON-FORM-OPTION-REVOCATION-01'),
  ('3ec81123-4c3b-5e2c-a092-7046dd999a09', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14430_psalm_room_option'), 'C', 'Daniel and Timothy both regularly arrange paid retreats and studio leases for Christian events.', 0, '["misfit","wrong_frame"]', '["no_option_means_no_offer","recital_equals_payment","dealer_status_changes_common_law","preparation_equals_acceptance"]', 'Professional status sounds like it could make a firm offer special. The breaker is that this is not a UCC goods problem and the call is acceptance before revocation.', 'The parties'' professional status answers a different lane and does not resolve acceptance before revocation.', 'Ask whether the choice answers acceptance before revocation.', 'CON-FORM-OPTION-REVOCATION-01'),
  ('841ea3f6-2b63-5402-8028-80badaecf538', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14430_psalm_room_option'), 'D', 'On September 10, Timothy decided to lease the Psalm Room, and he applied for and obtained a commitment from a credit union for a $2,700 loan to help pay the rent.', 0, '["wrong_element"]', '["no_option_means_no_offer","recital_equals_payment","dealer_status_changes_common_law","preparation_equals_acceptance"]', 'The loan commitment makes Timothy look committed and reliant. The breaker is that private preparation is not acceptance and does not show lack of revocation notice.', 'The loan commitment shows preparation, not acceptance before effective revocation.', 'Check whether the fact changes Daniel''s power to revoke or Timothy''s power to accept.', 'CON-FORM-OPTION-REVOCATION-01'),
  ('6b16c18f-3748-5d9e-9afc-ad607e3218c3', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14434_crossed_handbells'), 'A', 'As to both formation and consideration, identical cross-offers are functionally equivalent to an offer followed by a responsive acceptance.', 0, '["flat_misstatement","wrong_frame"]', '["cross_offers_equal_acceptance","merchant_status_creates_estoppel","mirror_image_rule_always_relevant"]', 'It rewards the familiar offer-and-acceptance template and treats matching letters as a clean acceptance story. The breaker is that the matching-crossed-letter label overstates the formation event; the safer support is the rule allowing agreement even when the exact moment is uncertain.', 'The choice sells the dominant trap by treating matching crossed letters as if one were a responsive acceptance. The Gold Key keeps the better path: flexible agreement despite an uncertain formation moment.', 'Use the Gold Key and choose the answer that says agreement can exist even when the exact moment is undetermined.', 'CON-REM-CROSSED-FORMATION-01'),
  ('86d0e0c1-f38d-5e6d-b44c-eb7221384ef1', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14434_crossed_handbells'), 'B', 'Because Daniel was a merchant seller and Hannah was not a merchant buyer, Daniel is estopped to deny that their writings created a binding contract.', 0, '["fabricated_rule","wrong_frame"]', '["cross_offers_equal_acceptance","merchant_status_creates_estoppel","mirror_image_rule_always_relevant"]', 'It turns the merchant/nonmerchant contrast into a formal-sounding estoppel shortcut. The breaker is that status alone does not answer whether these writings show agreement.', 'The choice invents a status-to-estoppel shortcut from merchant and nonmerchant labels. Those labels do not supply the formation ground.', 'Cut status-to-estoppel shortcuts unless the stem gives an actual estoppel basis.', 'CON-REM-CROSSED-FORMATION-01'),
  ('5de69cf6-1c1c-5531-b5f6-9d1bef8ca045', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14434_crossed_handbells'), 'C', 'A sale-of-goods contract may be made in any manner sufficient to show agreement, even if the exact moment of formation cannot be determined.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'It is true and responsive: same goods, price, and delivery writings show agreement, and the exact formation moment need not be identified.', NULL, 'CON-REM-CROSSED-FORMATION-01'),
  ('b62f2b77-959f-5488-9247-493ddd8429a3', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14434_crossed_handbells'), 'D', 'A sale-of-goods contract does not require the acceptance to mirror the offer exactly.', 0, '["bait_doctrine","wrong_frame"]', '["cross_offers_equal_acceptance","merchant_status_creates_estoppel","mirror_image_rule_always_relevant"]', 'It invokes a real UCC idea that acceptances do not always have to be mirror images. The breaker is that the stem gives matching terms, so mirror-image relief answers a different problem.', 'The choice invokes a true UCC move for nonmatching acceptance terms. These writings match, so the mirror-image issue is not the problem assigned by the call.', 'Match the answer to the exact defect; mirror-image relief is for mismatched terms, not matching crossed writings.', 'CON-REM-CROSSED-FORMATION-01'),
  ('4db775ae-df69-5978-9120-9f9f3d243bad', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14437_lydias_verse_card_boxes'), 'A', 'Yes, because Barnabas’s letter created a one-year option contract.', 0, '["extreme_of_range"]', '["three_month_cap_means_expired","firm_offer_equals_option_contract","four_month_order_always_unreasonable"]', 'It borrows the correct yes outcome and gives it a familiar option-contract label. The breaker is that the unpaid merchant assurance creates only limited firm-offer irrevocability, not a one-year option.', 'The option-contract label overextends the unpaid firm-offer assurance into one-year irrevocability.', 'Label it an unpaid firm offer, not a one-year option.', 'CON-FORM-FIRM-CAP-REVOCABLE-NOT-REVOKED'),
  ('b9cbcd59-f56f-5818-89f2-5823b626fa69', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14437_lydias_verse_card_boxes'), 'B', 'No, because Lydia did not accept Barnabas’s offer within a reasonable time.', 0, '["flat_misstatement"]', '["three_month_cap_means_expired","firm_offer_equals_option_contract","four_month_order_always_unreasonable"]', 'It sounds cautious because four months feels late. The breaker is that the stem gives a next-calendar-year ordering window and no revocation.', 'The stem’s own year-long ordering window and lack of revocation make the four-month order timely enough.', 'Check whether the stem gives an express time period and whether revocation occurred.', 'CON-FORM-FIRM-CAP-REVOCABLE-NOT-REVOKED'),
  ('07f149dc-5f2b-50ae-93ce-9b889486369a', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14437_lydias_verse_card_boxes'), 'C', 'Yes, because Barnabas had not revoked the offer before March 29.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'C is the best answer. Lydia’s email came after the three-month firm-offer protection had expired, so Barnabas was free to revoke. But Barnabas had not revoked, and the offer itself still invited orders during the next calendar year.

Gold Key: The three-month firm-offer cap ends irrevocability only. It does not automatically kill the offer.

Silver Key: Separate “irrevocable” from “open.” The call asks whether the March 29 order accepted before revocation or lapse, not whether the offer was still firm.', NULL, 'CON-FORM-FIRM-CAP-REVOCABLE-NOT-REVOKED'),
  ('57f85956-620c-57e3-ae07-e34add0dded9', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14437_lydias_verse_card_boxes'), 'D', 'No, because under applicable law the irrevocability of Barnabas’s offer was limited to three months.', 0, '["wrong_element","violation_vs_remedy"]', '["three_month_cap_means_expired","firm_offer_equals_option_contract","four_month_order_always_unreasonable"]', 'It remembers the three-month cap, which is the hardest rule in the item. The breaker is that the cap ends only irrevocability, not the offer itself.', 'The three-month cap is true but answers only irrevocability, not whether the offer was revoked or lapsed before acceptance.', 'Run the Gold Key: after three months, ask whether the offer was revoked or lapsed.', 'CON-FORM-FIRM-CAP-REVOCABLE-NOT-REVOKED'),
  ('50a7600f-cb4a-5d0f-a6cd-bc599ce31631', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14438_nativity_ornament_kits'), 'A', 'Because Lydia reserved the right to cancel the order before May 1, Martha’s May 12 shipment was only an offer.', 0, '["contradiction"]', '["cancellation clause always prevents contract","shipment turns order into option","condition subsequent creates enforceability"]', 'This answer treats the original cancellation clause as if it still controlled the later shipment. The date check breaks it because the shipment occurred after the cancellation window closed.', 'The choice relies on a cancellation right that ended before the shipment date.', 'Underline before May 1, then underline May 12 and June 6.', 'REM-CONTRACTS-ILLUSORY-CUTOFF-01'),
  ('bdbe411d-fbe1-547f-9380-d9f23e6abcf9', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14438_nativity_ornament_kits'), 'B', 'Lydia’s cancellation right was a condition subsequent, and its failure made the contract enforceable.', 0, '["backwards","wrong_frame"]', '["cancellation clause always prevents contract","shipment turns order into option","condition subsequent creates enforceability"]', 'This answer gives a sophisticated label to the missed cancellation deadline. The Gold Key breaks it because the missed deadline is a cutoff for an illusory promise, not a condition subsequent creating the contract.', 'The choice recasts the missed cancellation deadline as a condition subsequent and points the consequence in the wrong direction.', 'Ask whether the clause is a deadline for cancellation or a later event that cuts off duty.', 'REM-CONTRACTS-ILLUSORY-CUTOFF-01'),
  ('f9497398-43a0-5d50-954f-4b639a49e9f7', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14438_nativity_ornament_kits'), 'C', 'Lydia’s order for the ornament kits, even if initially illusory, became a binding promise to accept and pay for them.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'Only C states the parties’ status after the cancellation deadline passed unused.', NULL, 'REM-CONTRACTS-ILLUSORY-CUTOFF-01'),
  ('6ae05d71-11e0-5b21-88b9-70119f941028', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14438_nativity_ornament_kits'), 'D', 'Lydia’s order was an offer that became an option after Martha shipped the kits.', 0, '["bait_doctrine","wrong_frame"]', '["cancellation clause always prevents contract","shipment turns order into option","condition subsequent creates enforceability"]', 'This answer reaches for the familiar option-contract label after seeing an order and later shipment. The status call breaks it because the question asks about the parties after the cancellation deadline passed, not about keeping an offer open.', 'The choice uses the nearby option-contract label instead of answering the status after the cancellation date passed.', 'Answer the status as of June 6, not the status before the cancellation deadline.', 'REM-CONTRACTS-ILLUSORY-CUTOFF-01'),
  ('c2456593-d7d0-5607-8a36-4fdcda223adf', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14447_jericho_trumpet_deposit'), 'A', 'Reliance by the promisee on a promise to make a future gift does not make the promise enforceable unless that reliance also results in an economic benefit to the promisor.', 0, '["fabricated_rule"]', '["gift_promises_never_enforced","reliance_must_equal_promise","reliance_must_benefit_promisor","reliance_alone_always_enough"]', 'A student remembers that reliance can substitute for consideration but looks for a benefit flowing back to the promisor. The breaker is that promissory estoppel tests expected reliance, actual reliance, and injustice, not economic benefit to the promisor.', 'This adds an economic-benefit-to-promisor gate that is not part of the reliance rule.', 'Ask whether the answer names one of the actual reliance gates: expected reliance, actual reliance, injustice.', 'REM-CONTRACTS-PE-GIFT-GATES-01'),
  ('9561e062-e118-59ab-8788-4096310b405a', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14447_jericho_trumpet_deposit'), 'B', 'Reliance by the promisee on a promise to make a future gift does not make the promise enforceable unless injustice can be avoided only by such enforcement.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'B is correct. The answer names the real promissory-estoppel gate: reliance on a future gift promise matters only if enforcement is needed to avoid injustice.

Gold Key: A future gift promise can be enforceable by promissory estoppel, but only through the actual reliance gates: expected reliance, actual reliance, and injustice. No “equivalent loss” gate and no “benefit to promisor” gate is part of the rule.

Silver Key: Read the array as a gate list. Three choices either delete the reliance exception or invent extra gates. B is the only choice that names the real gate.

Timothy did rely by signing the rental contract. But his reliance—renting a $58,000 Jericho-trumpet rig right after receiving a promise meant to help him get financially steady—cuts against the claim that justice requires enforcing Lydia’s promise. That makes B Lydia’s best defense.', NULL, 'REM-CONTRACTS-PE-GIFT-GATES-01'),
  ('a6b9442a-141a-53ca-8896-cf7f6ba76ee4', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14447_jericho_trumpet_deposit'), 'C', 'A promise to make a gift in the future is not enforceable.', 0, '["tiered_absolute"]', '["gift_promises_never_enforced","reliance_must_equal_promise","reliance_must_benefit_promisor","reliance_alone_always_enough"]', 'A student remembers the baseline rule that gratuitous future promises usually are not bargained-for contracts. The breaker is the Gold Key: reliance can create an enforcement path, so a no-exceptions gift rule overclaims.', 'This turns the gift-promise baseline into a no-exceptions rule and deletes the reliance path.', 'Treat future-gift-is-never-enforceable as an overclaim once reliance appears in the stem.', 'REM-CONTRACTS-PE-GIFT-GATES-01'),
  ('38e0411e-f1ab-5158-b4a4-2df9f5490b9e', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14447_jericho_trumpet_deposit'), 'D', 'Reliance by the promisee on a promise to make a future gift does not make the promise enforceable unless the value of the promised gift is substantially equivalent to the promisee''s loss by reliance.', 0, '["fabricated_rule"]', '["gift_promises_never_enforced","reliance_must_equal_promise","reliance_must_benefit_promisor","reliance_alone_always_enough"]', 'A student tries to measure reliance with a compensation-like equivalence test. The breaker is that the rule asks whether enforcement is necessary to avoid injustice, not whether the promised amount equals the reliance loss.', 'This adds a substantial-equivalence gate that is not part of the reliance rule.', 'Replace the equivalence question with the injustice question.', 'REM-CONTRACTS-PE-GIFT-GATES-01'),
  ('dbf3eb67-32f3-5f34-a713-cae0703c2a49', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14451_psalm_trail_quitclaim'), 'A', 'lose, because Barnabas suffered no legal detriment in executing the quitclaim deed.', 0, '["flat_misstatement"]', '["worthless_claim_means_no_consideration","legal_detriment_requires_good_title","first_payment_creates_estoppel"]', 'The legal-detriment wording sounds like the formal consideration test students memorized. The breaker is that executing the deed is the bargained-for act.', 'The choice denies legal detriment in the very act the buyer bargained to receive.', 'Mark the executed quitclaim as the promised performance.', 'CON-REMEDY-PSALM-QUITCLAIM'),
  ('192c4566-7d91-5a17-861f-90c230e813e9', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14451_psalm_trail_quitclaim'), 'B', 'lose, because Barnabas knew before the agreement with Lydia that his claimed access right was doubtful.', 0, '["backwards"]', '["worthless_claim_means_no_consideration","legal_detriment_requires_good_title","first_payment_creates_estoppel"]', 'The later zero-value ruling makes the doubtful claim feel worthless from the start. The breaker is that the bargain is graded at the exchange and a doubtful claim can be enough.', 'The choice treats doubt in the claim as destroying consideration, while the anchor makes the doubtful claim the reason the deed can matter.', 'Time-slice the bargain at execution and delivery.', 'CON-REMEDY-PSALM-QUITCLAIM'),
  ('1f2c96e0-fa3f-5ab4-847b-1855c51754ef', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14451_psalm_trail_quitclaim'), 'C', 'win, because Lydia, by paying the first $1,500 installment, is estopped to deny that her agreement with Barnabas is an enforceable contract.', 0, '["bait_doctrine","wrong_frame"]', '["worthless_claim_means_no_consideration","legal_detriment_requires_good_title","first_payment_creates_estoppel"]', 'The first payment makes enforceability feel like an estoppel case. The breaker is that the valid consideration path already answers the breach call.', 'The choice reaches for estoppel even though the call is resolved by bargained-for consideration.', 'Choose the win answer that uses the bargain actually in the facts.', 'CON-REMEDY-PSALM-QUITCLAIM'),
  ('74149b0c-1ca6-5061-9205-24218474e556', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14451_psalm_trail_quitclaim'), 'D', 'win, because Lydia bargained for, and received in exchange, a quitclaim deed from Barnabas.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'D is correct. Lydia promised to pay for Barnabas''s quitclaim deed. Barnabas delivered exactly that deed. Gold Key: a bargained-for quitclaim, release, or written surrender of a doubtful claim can supply consideration even if the claim later proves worthless. Silver Key: keep the reason attached to the result; the winning answer must say why Barnabas wins through the bargain itself, not through a substitute theory.', NULL, 'CON-REMEDY-PSALM-QUITCLAIM'),
  ('1bf95369-91e9-5dbd-81c3-f9c49010e3ee', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14457_advent_kits_requirements'), 'A', 'No, because Martha’s Paper House could, and did, revoke its offer as to later deliveries.', 0, '["bait_doctrine","wrong_frame"]', '["no_number_no_contract","future_delivery_revocation","part_performance_overuse"]', 'The choice turns the case into an offer-revocation problem because future deliveries sound like future offers. The breaker is the signed requirements agreement and performance, which put the case in formed-contract breach territory.', 'Revocation is a formation-stage frame; the stem has a signed agreement and months of performance.', 'Mark the signed agreement and four months of performance; then test breach, not revocation.', 'REM-CONTRACTS-REQ-01'),
  ('2d990a65-eea1-5c7a-94eb-1cde58fc2153', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14457_advent_kits_requirements'), 'B', 'No, because no stated minimum quantity made Bethany’s promise illusory.', 0, '["fabricated_rule"]', '["no_number_no_contract","future_delivery_revocation","part_performance_overuse"]', 'The choice sells the common myth that no number means no quantity term. The breaker is the Gold Key that all-requirements language supplies the quantity term under the requirements-contract rule.', 'The choice invents a minimum-quantity requirement that the requirements-contract rule does not impose.', 'Treat ''all requirements'' as the quantity term and apply the good-faith guardrail.', 'REM-CONTRACTS-REQ-01'),
  ('0dae6efe-d63b-5001-b1d3-1fa106fa4797', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14457_advent_kits_requirements'), 'C', 'Yes, because Bethany’s agreement to buy all of its kit-supply requirements from Martha’s Paper House made the agreement enforceable.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'It is the only choice that treats the signed all-requirements writing as an enforceable contract and addresses the seller’s withdrawal of the discount.', NULL, 'REM-CONTRACTS-REQ-01'),
  ('b2c52579-a2a9-5ef5-bdb1-d09b5d226255', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14457_advent_kits_requirements'), 'D', 'Yes, because four months of performance by both sides made the agreement enforceable for the entire year.', 0, '["bait_doctrine","wrong_frame"]', '["no_number_no_contract","future_delivery_revocation","part_performance_overuse"]', 'The choice reaches for a familiar rescue idea because performance by both sides feels like the reason the year should bind. The breaker is that part performance answers a different enforceability problem, while the requirements promise already supplies the enforceability ground.', 'Part performance is a rescue path for a different enforceability problem; this contract is enforceable through the requirements promise.', 'Use the requirements promise as the enforceability hook; do not spend the performance fact on the wrong issue.', 'REM-CONTRACTS-REQ-01'),
  ('29504801-6656-5cdf-9556-9ba5c84e026a', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14458_verse_wristbands'), 'A', 'Daniel wins, because the agreement was void for indefiniteness of quantity and total price for the year.', 0, '["fabricated_rule"]', '["no_fixed_quantity_means_no_contract","express_promise_required","right_result_wrong_reason","demand_drop_excuses_performance"]', 'The choice sells the familiar idea that a contract with no fixed annual quantity looks too open-ended to enforce. The breaker is the output and exclusivity frame, which is the exact frame where the best-efforts anchor supplies the missing obligation.', 'Indefiniteness bait. The output/exclusive-dealing frame supplies a quantity and obligation through the UCC anchor.', 'Circle the output-plus-exclusivity fact before judging quantity definiteness.', 'CON-GK-OUTPUT-EXCLUSIVE-BEST-EFFORTS'),
  ('fd0abe2b-41c9-5e2a-ad9f-9613f6677b81', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14458_verse_wristbands'), 'B', 'Lydia wins, because both parties to a sale-of-goods contract impliedly assume the risk of changes in price and customer demand.', 0, '["bait_doctrine","wrong_frame"]', '["no_fixed_quantity_means_no_contract","express_promise_required","right_result_wrong_reason","demand_drop_excuses_performance"]', 'The choice gives Lydia the win and speaks to the demand-crash fact, so it feels commercially fair and exam-plausible. The breaker is that it does not use the legal hook created by Daniel''s exclusivity promise.', 'Dominant same-result trap. It uses a generic market-risk frame instead of the exclusive-dealing best-efforts rule.', 'When two answers reach the same result, choose the reason tied to the special contract structure.', 'CON-GK-OUTPUT-EXCLUSIVE-BEST-EFFORTS'),
  ('f8511b20-1137-5fd2-bd67-f5b2af0b5d10', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14458_verse_wristbands'), 'C', 'Daniel wins, because there was no mutuality of obligation since Daniel made no express promise to buy any of Lydia''s Scripture-memory wristbands.', 0, '["fabricated_rule"]', '["no_fixed_quantity_means_no_contract","express_promise_required","right_result_wrong_reason","demand_drop_excuses_performance"]', 'The choice sells the surface grammar: Daniel never wrote the words ''I will buy all of them.'' The breaker is the best-efforts anchor supplied by the exclusive-dealing output arrangement.', 'Express-promise bait. The absence of an express buy-all sentence does not defeat an exclusive-output arrangement when the law supplies best efforts.', 'Treat exclusivity as a signal that the law may supply an implied promotional duty.', 'CON-GK-OUTPUT-EXCLUSIVE-BEST-EFFORTS'),
  ('8fdbbd8f-cf63-5a9c-886f-b1a6a59d3be6', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14458_verse_wristbands'), 'D', 'Lydia wins, because Daniel''s promise to retail Lydia''s Scripture-memory wristbands exclusively, if he sold such wristbands at all, implied a promise to use his best efforts to sell Lydia''s one-year output of wristbands.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'D is correct. Lydia wins because the written deal did more than leave Daniel free to buy nothing. The agreement gave Daniel an exclusive retail position for Lydia’s wristbands. Gold Key: in an exclusive-dealing sale-of-goods contract, the law supplies a best-efforts duty. Daniel’s exclusivity promise implied that he would use best efforts to promote the sale of Lydia’s one-year output.

Silver Key: when two answers give Lydia the win, choose the reason that uses the special fact in the stem. The special fact is Daniel’s promise not to sell any other supplier’s Scripture-memory wristbands. D uses that fact. B gives the right winner but not the right legal hook.', NULL, 'CON-GK-OUTPUT-EXCLUSIVE-BEST-EFFORTS'),
  ('27423947-d671-5261-80b2-45bfc8eaa539', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14460_fish_loaves_mosaic'), 'A', 'Promissory estoppel.', 0, '["bait_doctrine","wrong_frame"]', '["reliance_always_means_promissory_estoppel","benefit_received_means_unjust_enrichment","partial_work_means_substantial_performance"]', 'This choice grabs the reliance fact and turns it into a familiar promise-reliance label. The breaker is the call: the writing gives a contract lane, so reliance only explains why the waiver cannot be pulled back.', 'Promissory estoppel tracks reliance, but the call asks for a breach-of-contract concept where a signed writing supplies the contract lane.', 'Ask whether the stem already gives a signed contract lane.', 'CONTRACTS-CALL-14460'),
  ('fae24d8a-9867-5b4b-b68b-7609256d110d', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14460_fish_loaves_mosaic'), 'B', 'Irrevocable waiver of condition.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'It is the only concept that accounts for the failed approval condition, Peter''s order to proceed, Mary''s reliance, and the breach-of-contract call.', NULL, 'CONTRACTS-CALL-14460'),
  ('888d1b07-5ee1-5430-9769-7f716c25cf1e', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14460_fish_loaves_mosaic'), 'C', 'Unjust enrichment.', 0, '["bait_doctrine","wrong_frame"]', '["reliance_always_means_promissory_estoppel","benefit_received_means_unjust_enrichment","partial_work_means_substantial_performance"]', 'This choice feels fair because Peter received part of the mosaic before refusing to pay. The breaker is the writing: unjust enrichment is a backup lane, not the best support for a breach-of-contract claim.', 'Unjust enrichment tracks the benefit received, but it is a backup lane rather than the best support for a breach-of-contract claim on a valid writing.', 'Use unjust enrichment only when the contract lane is unavailable or not the assigned call.', 'CONTRACTS-CALL-14460'),
  ('a2ab5fc5-c3be-5b79-b969-bd3510be8c8b', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14460_fish_loaves_mosaic'), 'D', 'Substantial performance.', 0, '["bait_doctrine","wrong_frame"]', '["reliance_always_means_promissory_estoppel","benefit_received_means_unjust_enrichment","partial_work_means_substantial_performance"]', 'This choice grabs the partial-work fact and sounds like a performance doctrine. The breaker is the condition lane: the problem is failed approval followed by an order to proceed, not whether 45% is enough performance.', 'Substantial performance points to a performance-completion lane, but the stem turns on an express approval condition waived by the protected party.', 'When a named approval condition fails and the protected party says proceed, test waiver first.', 'CONTRACTS-CALL-14460'),
  ('775836a8-9685-521e-b6b6-e0def6d28b7f', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14462_mustard_seed_carts'), 'A', 'No, because the express condition of Emmaus Market Group''s approval had not occurred before the lawsuit.', 0, '["bait_doctrine","wrong_frame"]', '["conditions_never_waivable","buyer_control_equals_breach","any_reliance_is_enough"]', 'It sounds precise because the approval event really did not happen before suit. The breaker is that the protected side waived the condition, so nonoccurrence is the wrong frame.', 'It applies the default condition-nonoccurrence frame while skipping the waiver frame supplied by the protected side.', 'Name the waiver axis before committing to the nonoccurrence answer.', 'CON-REM-WAIVE-PROTECTED-CONDITION'),
  ('9dec3246-1224-5f8d-8ae6-c5af99ae0d43', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14462_mustard_seed_carts'), 'B', 'No, because obtaining Emmaus Market Group''s approval was within Lydia Learning''s control, and Lydia Learning''s failure to obtain it was itself a material breach of contract.', 0, '["wrong_element","wrong_frame"]', '["conditions_never_waivable","buyer_control_equals_breach","any_reliance_is_enough"]', 'It feels strong because the buyer controlled the approval request and neglected it. The breaker is that the answer converts a buyer-protective condition into a seller-side breach defense.', 'It turns the buyer-side approval condition into an asserted buyer breach, which answers the wrong layer of the seller''s refusal.', 'Use the Silver Key and sort condition-on-buyer from seller-defense.', 'CON-REM-WAIVE-PROTECTED-CONDITION'),
  ('aa0cabdf-6a6c-5202-99d1-e3ad37ee80c5', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14462_mustard_seed_carts'), 'C', 'Yes, because the approval condition, being designed to protect only Lydia Learning and Emmaus Market Group, can be and has been waived by those entities.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'The approval condition protected the buyer-side parties, and those parties can waive it.', NULL, 'CON-REM-WAIVE-PROTECTED-CONDITION'),
  ('878fcc7a-c85c-5459-af4e-4f3a575ce57f', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14462_mustard_seed_carts'), 'D', 'Yes, because Lydia Learning detrimentally relied on Ruth''s commitment by selling its delivery van to finance the coffee-cart purchase.', 0, '["wrong_element"]', '["conditions_never_waivable","buyer_control_equals_breach","any_reliance_is_enough"]', 'It uses a real reliance-sounding fact, the sale of other assets. The breaker is that the stem withholds a foreseeability link, so reliance does not answer the call.', 'It relies on financing conduct while skipping the missing foreseeability link that would make reliance carry the claim.', 'Require a foreseeable reliance link before using reliance as the reason the buyer wins.', 'CON-REM-WAIVE-PROTECTED-CONDITION'),
  ('0b55d575-2201-5b89-8bdb-f796cf8696a1', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14463_calligraphy_supplies'), 'A', 'not succeed, because Ruth did not give any consideration for Lydia’s promise to order the banners and inserts.', 0, '["wrong_element","wrong_frame"]', '["beneficiary_status_equals_win","beneficiary_must_give_consideration","family_relationship_implies_agency"]', 'The answer sells the familiar idea that a promise needs consideration and Ruth herself gave none. The breaker is that Ruth is not the party who had to furnish the exchange; Barnabas’s promise supplied the consideration.', 'Focuses on Ruth’s lack of consideration, but the consideration issue runs through Barnabas’s exchange with Lydia.', 'Ask whether the answer attacks the beneficiary or the underlying exchange between the contracting parties.', 'REM-CONTRACTS-TPB-CONDITION-01'),
  ('dd859695-08af-58e1-8a94-7b0b2b631e3e', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14463_calligraphy_supplies'), 'B', 'not succeed, because Barnabas’s failure to satisfy a constructive condition precedent excused Lydia’s duty to order the banners and inserts from Ruth.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'Barnabas’s failure of the exchange prevents Lydia’s related duty to Ruth from coming due.', NULL, 'REM-CONTRACTS-TPB-CONDITION-01'),
  ('ef94576a-52de-5e0c-8c75-fae167892f61', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14463_calligraphy_supplies'), 'C', 'succeed, because Ruth is a third-party beneficiary of the Barnabas-Lydia contract.', 0, '["wrong_element","wrong_frame"]', '["beneficiary_status_equals_win","beneficiary_must_give_consideration","family_relationship_implies_agency"]', 'The answer sells the visible beneficiary label and Ruth’s reliance on the promised studio work. The breaker is the Gold Key: beneficiary status does not outrun Barnabas’s uncured material breach.', 'Names beneficiary status but skips the contract-level defense created by Barnabas’s earlier failure of performance.', 'After status, ask whether the promisor has a contract-level defense against the promisee.', 'REM-CONTRACTS-TPB-CONDITION-01'),
  ('966e8bc6-52e5-55a2-a43b-aaec3a7c4c17', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14463_calligraphy_supplies'), 'D', 'succeed, because Barnabas was acting as Ruth’s agent when he made the contract with Lydia.', 0, '["misfit","wrong_frame"]', '["beneficiary_status_equals_win","beneficiary_must_give_consideration","family_relationship_implies_agency"]', 'The answer sells a clean path to recovery by making Barnabas Ruth’s contracting agent. The breaker is that the stem gives no agency facts and frames Ruth as a third person, not a party through an agent.', 'Imports an agency theory not supplied by the stem and answers a different route to recovery.', 'Mark agency as unavailable unless the stem shows control or authorization.', 'REM-CONTRACTS-TPB-CONDITION-01'),
  ('1165424f-7460-5157-b662-a89fbe3f65b7', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14467_psalm_tutor_installments'), 'A', '$1,200 plus the accrued interest, because Mary’s unexcused failure to pay three installments is a material breach.', 0, '["extreme_of_range"]', '["three_misses_equals_total_breach","insolvency_equals_acceleration","suit_for_due_installments_equals_claim_splitting"]', 'A student sees three missed payments plus a statement of future inability and upgrades the breach to a full-balance claim. The breaker is that the amount ladder still needs an acceleration fact before the unmatured balance becomes collectible.', 'Full-balance recovery overextends the breach from three matured installments to the whole contract.', 'Ask whether the stem gives an acceleration clause or all installments have matured.', 'CONTRACTS-REMEDY-INSTALLMENT-AMOUNT-LADDER'),
  ('c5d9d92c-5831-510b-8d5c-acb487caac27', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14467_psalm_tutor_installments'), 'B', '$1,200 plus the accrued interest, because the failure to pay her obligations as they come due indicates that Mary is insolvent and Lydia is thereby entitled to accelerate payment of the balance.', 0, '["fabricated_rule"]', '["three_misses_equals_total_breach","insolvency_equals_acceleration","suit_for_due_installments_equals_claim_splitting"]', 'A student treats inability to pay as a shortcut to immediate collection of everything. The breaker is that the answer supplies an acceleration remedy the stem did not give.', 'The choice invents an acceleration entitlement from suspected insolvency.', 'Look for an express acceleration term; if none appears, cut the full-balance insolvency answer.', 'CONTRACTS-REMEDY-INSTALLMENT-AMOUNT-LADDER'),
  ('ea69c67b-cefd-5eb9-afde-1740b691381b', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14467_psalm_tutor_installments'), 'C', '$360 plus the accrued interest, because Mary’s breach is only a partial breach.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'It answers the call date and amount without accelerating unmatured installments.', NULL, 'CONTRACTS-REMEDY-INSTALLMENT-AMOUNT-LADDER'),
  ('63d7b493-3257-5829-8691-4ce71bae2396', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14467_psalm_tutor_installments'), 'D', 'Nothing, because if Lydia sues before the entire amount is due, she will be splitting her cause of action.', 0, '["fabricated_rule"]', '["three_misses_equals_total_breach","insolvency_equals_acceleration","suit_for_due_installments_equals_claim_splitting"]', 'A student remembers claim-splitting and fears suing before all installments mature. The breaker is that the call asks for matured installments already due, not for later installments.', 'The choice turns a permissible suit for matured installments into forbidden claim-splitting.', 'Recover what is already due and leave later installments for later maturity.', 'CONTRACTS-REMEDY-INSTALLMENT-AMOUNT-LADDER'),
  ('8bd164f0-e54e-5ae3-8dce-65f50d7d500d', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14477_advent_mugs'), 'A', 'Yes, because once Barnabas missed the date for choosing the assortments, Lydia was required to choose a reasonable assortment.', 0, '["fabricated_rule","violation_vs_remedy"]', '["missed_date_ends_contract","open_assortment_no_contract","seller_must_choose_after_buyer_delay"]', 'It feels practical because someone must choose the assortment after the buyer misses the date. The breaker is that the seller-selection remedy is not automatic when the late choice does not materially affect performance.', 'The choice invents an automatic seller-selection duty when the material-effect trigger is absent.', 'Ask whether the late choice materially changed the seller’s performance position.', 'REM-CONTRACTS-SPECIFICATION-01'),
  ('0299830e-dd8e-5a65-a935-dbdfcdd37c8e', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14477_advent_mugs'), 'B', 'Yes, because the one-day delay in choosing the assortment did not materially affect Lydia’s ability to perform.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'B is the only answer that is true and responsive to the material-effect axis.', NULL, 'REM-CONTRACTS-SPECIFICATION-01'),
  ('ed9758dd-20f9-58b3-9acc-5c005f2c8c94', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14477_advent_mugs'), 'C', 'No, because no contract existed until Barnabas chose the particular assortments, and Lydia withdrew before that choice.', 0, '["flat_misstatement","wrong_frame"]', '["missed_date_ends_contract","open_assortment_no_contract","seller_must_choose_after_buyer_delay"]', 'It feels safe because a late choice sounds like no deal was complete. The breaker is the signed-order frame plus the Gold Key that open assortment terms do not invalidate a UCC sale.', 'The choice treats an open assortment detail as fatal to contract formation.', 'Start from the signed goods-sale order, not from offer withdrawal.', 'REM-CONTRACTS-SPECIFICATION-01'),
  ('a281a762-88f8-5fe6-b076-1fb28f4d83b3', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14477_advent_mugs'), 'D', 'No, because choosing the assortments by August 15 was an express condition to Lydia’s duty to perform.', 0, '["flat_misstatement","wrong_frame"]', '["missed_date_ends_contract","open_assortment_no_contract","seller_must_choose_after_buyer_delay"]', 'It is the dominant trap because deadline language sounds fatal and the phrase express condition sounds precise. The breaker is the no-material-effect fact that resolves the CLASH for B.', 'The choice treats deadline language as an express condition and skips the no-material-effect facts.', 'Compare the fatal-deadline answer against the no-material-effect answer and use the inventory facts.', 'REM-CONTRACTS-SPECIFICATION-01'),
  ('54316563-86b4-5d31-8209-5711d4dda54c', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14492_psalm_lantern_kits'), 'A', 'Daniel is entitled to accept any number of the 19 kits and reject the rest, but is not entitled to cancel the contract as to any rejected kits or the lot due on October 24.', 0, '["bait_doctrine","wrong_frame"]', '["perfect_tender_applies_to_installments","partial_rejection_available","one_short_lot_cancels_future_lots"]', 'This choice sells the ordinary perfect-tender instinct: a buyer who receives fewer goods than promised can accept some and reject the rest. The breaker is the Gold Key: separate delivery lots plus a prompt cure promise move the item to the installment rule.', 'It imports ordinary perfect-tender partial rejection into an installment-contract setting with prompt cure assurance.', 'Circle the separate dated lots, then apply the Gold Key before choosing a remedy.', 'REM-CONTRACTS-INSTALLMENT-CURE'),
  ('a5ec035b-8a0b-5cd9-86bd-a027a95d3a33', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14492_psalm_lantern_kits'), 'B', 'Daniel must accept the 19 kits and is not entitled to cancel the rest of the contract.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'B is the only choice that satisfies both axes after the installment-contract Gold Key is applied.', NULL, 'REM-CONTRACTS-INSTALLMENT-CURE'),
  ('395d612b-3398-5bbb-9050-2c58b50ea302', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14492_psalm_lantern_kits'), 'C', 'Daniel is entitled to accept any number of the 19 kits, reject the rest, and cancel the contract both as to any rejected kits and the lot due on October 24.', 0, '["bait_doctrine","wrong_frame"]', '["perfect_tender_applies_to_installments","partial_rejection_available","one_short_lot_cancels_future_lots"]', 'This choice feels powerful because it combines partial acceptance with a broad buyer remedy. The breaker is the same wrong frame: the current lot and future lot are governed by the installment-contract cure standard, not ordinary perfect tender.', 'It applies ordinary perfect-tender cancellation logic to the current lot and future lot instead of the installment-contract rule.', 'Use the Silver Key matrix: present installment first, whole contract second.', 'REM-CONTRACTS-INSTALLMENT-CURE'),
  ('a5cd0528-b26f-585a-9efd-fbfaaf6b0d29', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14492_psalm_lantern_kits'), 'D', 'Daniel must accept the 19 kits but is entitled to cancel the rest of the contract.', 0, '["extreme_of_range","violation_vs_remedy"]', '["perfect_tender_applies_to_installments","partial_rejection_available","one_short_lot_cancels_future_lots"]', 'This choice rewards the first half of the matrix by making Daniel accept the current lot. The breaker is the second half: one curable short lot does not justify canceling the future lot without whole-contract substantial impairment.', 'It gets the current-lot acceptance axis right but overextends the remedy to whole-contract cancellation without whole-contract substantial impairment.', 'Ask whether the facts show substantial impairment of the whole contract; if not, no cancellation.', 'REM-CONTRACTS-INSTALLMENT-CURE'),
  ('9b3ae594-1ea0-5e58-8fc3-145d52b764f9', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14493_ark_stage'), 'A', 'No, because Ruth did not tender her performance at 7 p.m. Thursday.', 0, '["wrong_element","violation_vs_remedy"]', '["buyer_must_still_tender","impossibility_excuses_own_sale","notice_required_for_act_repudiation"]', 'The answer sells the familiar instinct that a buyer who has not handed over the money cannot complain. The breaker is that the seller''s prior repudiation suspends the buyer''s performance duty.', 'This choice blames the buyer''s non-tender after the seller''s own pre-date transfer already supplied the breach trigger.', 'Ask which party made performance fail first.', 'CON-REPUDIATION-ACT-01'),
  ('691f04ae-3c8a-57c3-a6ea-1e926aace5b7', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14493_ark_stage'), 'B', 'Yes, because Stephen anticipatorily repudiated the contract when he sold the puppet stage to Hannah.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'It is the only answer that says yes and names Stephen''s pre-date sale as the breach trigger.', NULL, 'CON-REPUDIATION-ACT-01'),
  ('a2941c03-a96e-5355-863f-47ad9d238caa', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14493_ark_stage'), 'C', 'No, because the contractual obligations were discharged on the ground of impossibility.', 0, '["backwards","wrong_frame"]', '["buyer_must_still_tender","impossibility_excuses_own_sale","notice_required_for_act_repudiation"]', 'The answer uses the word impossibility, which sounds right because the seller no longer has the item. The breaker is that the seller created the inability himself, so the move is repudiation rather than excuse.', 'This choice turns self-created inability into an excuse, when the anchor treats it as repudiation.', 'Ask whether the alleged impossibility came from outside the promisor or from the promisor''s own act.', 'CON-REPUDIATION-ACT-01'),
  ('95f12850-0d1a-58b7-8412-cff9cd0dcd59', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14493_ark_stage'), 'D', 'Yes, because Stephen did not inform Ruth of his repudiation before 7 p.m. Thursday.', 0, '["bait_doctrine","wrong_frame"]', '["buyer_must_still_tender","impossibility_excuses_own_sale","notice_required_for_act_repudiation"]', 'The answer gives the correct yes outcome and then tempts the student to accept any contract-flavored reason. The breaker is the because-clause: notice is not the operative breach trigger here.', 'This choice has the right yes/no outcome but makes notice the reason instead of the seller''s transfer of the promised item.', 'Underline the because-clause and force it to match the operative fact.', 'CON-REPUDIATION-ACT-01'),
  ('092a69cd-dbfb-5fbd-9508-ee88bfd67b94', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14506_lydia_rooftop_loft'), 'A', 'No, because Mary breached by failing to tender the deed and possession on March 4.', 0, '["wrong_element","violation_vs_remedy"]', '["missed date equals total breach","holdover equals impossibility","money damages always adequate in contracts"]', 'It spots that Mary did not perform on the named date. The breaker is that a date breach is not the same as a total breach defeating specific performance.', 'This choice proves only a date breach. It skips the deciding question whether that breach was material enough to end Timothy''s duty to close.', 'Ask whether the fact proves only breach or also proves the buyer may refuse the whole deal.', 'RC-CONTRACTS-LAND-SP-14506'),
  ('9150cdd3-18d5-5a21-a430-e27a9a284321', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14506_lydia_rooftop_loft'), 'B', 'No, because Mary has an adequate remedy at law for money damages.', 0, '["flat_misstatement"]', '["missed date equals total breach","holdover equals impossibility","money damages always adequate in contracts"]', 'It uses the ordinary contract instinct that money fixes contract breaches. The breaker is the land-specific Gold Key that treats damages as inadequate for specific performance analysis.', 'This choice treats money damages as adequate for a land-sale contract. The Gold Key makes land the classic specific-performance setting.', 'Tag land sale plus specific performance as a Gold Key before comparing money remedies.', 'RC-CONTRACTS-LAND-SP-14506'),
  ('6f5998f4-d177-59ab-a80d-a23e941db8c0', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14506_lydia_rooftop_loft'), 'C', 'Yes, because the court will excuse Mary''s late tender as temporary impossibility caused by Lydia''s wrongful holdover.', 0, '["bait_doctrine","wrong_frame"]', '["missed date equals total breach","holdover equals impossibility","money damages always adequate in contracts"]', 'It sees the holdover obstacle and turns it into an excuse doctrine. The breaker is that the credited path does not excuse Mary; it classifies the delay as partial rather than total.', 'This choice reaches the right outcome through an excuse frame. The correct route treats the late tender as a partial breach, not as excused nonperformance.', 'Separate answer result from answer reason; cut right-result/wrong-frame choices.', 'RC-CONTRACTS-LAND-SP-14506'),
  ('a280e262-67e4-59f8-924e-d8b08d4026cc', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14506_lydia_rooftop_loft'), 'D', 'Yes, because time is ordinarily not of the essence in a land-sale contract.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'It is true and responsive after the excuse-frame, damages-adequacy, and missed-date-only choices are cut.', NULL, 'RC-CONTRACTS-LAND-SP-14506'),
  ('bfa4d2c2-3f26-5825-a5a9-da3a6de4db48', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14510_ark_puppet_stage'), 'A', 'The profit Lydia’s Workshop would have made on Timothy’s purchase.', 0, '["wrong_element"]', '["contract_price_is_ordinary_expectation","resale_always_fully_mitigates","familiar_formula_beats_call"]', 'Lost profit feels like a windfall because the seller got the kit back. The unused-capacity fact makes lost profit a proper lost-volume measure, so it fails the NOT-proper call.', 'This is a proper lost-volume measure, so it answers the positive version of the call.', 'When unused capacity appears, test whether resale replaced the sale or whether the seller lost an additional sale.', 'REM-CONTRACTS-SELLER-PRICE-01'),
  ('02d57b77-e946-57b5-90fb-3eb00033db9d', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14510_ark_puppet_stage'), 'B', 'The contract price of the puppet-stage kit.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'The price action is not proper because the goods were rejected and returned and no price-action trigger is present.', NULL, 'REM-CONTRACTS-SELLER-PRICE-01'),
  ('26dda78b-8022-5ecd-bfc6-47044695011a', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14510_ark_puppet_stage'), 'C', 'The difference between the contract price and the market price of the puppet-stage kit.', 0, '["wrong_element"]', '["contract_price_is_ordinary_expectation","resale_always_fully_mitigates","familiar_formula_beats_call"]', 'Market-minus-contract price is the familiar default seller formula and looks safely doctrinal. The call asks for the measure that is NOT proper, so a valid default formula answers the opposite question.', 'This is a proper market-damages measure, so it is the dominant positive-call trap.', 'Mark each formula as proper or not proper; then choose the single not-proper formula.', 'REM-CONTRACTS-SELLER-PRICE-01'),
  ('cd823d26-b48e-5d10-9481-d3d98c87b681', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14510_ark_puppet_stage'), 'D', 'The difference between the contract price and the price obtained from a proper resale of the puppet-stage kit.', 0, '["wrong_element"]', '["contract_price_is_ordinary_expectation","resale_always_fully_mitigates","familiar_formula_beats_call"]', 'The returned goods make resale damages feel directly matched to the facts. A proper resale formula is still a proper measure, so it cannot be the NOT-proper answer.', 'This is a proper resale-damages measure, so it does not satisfy the NOT-proper call.', 'A proper resale formula is a reason to eliminate the choice on a NOT-proper-measure call.', 'REM-CONTRACTS-SELLER-PRICE-01'),
  ('77110598-3ff0-5681-8e37-8abf159bb07c', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14511_worship_studio_delay'), 'A', 'Nominal damages only, because the market value of the property exceeded the contract price.', 0, '["misfit","wrong_frame"]', '["market value above price means no damages","closing and paying waives all claims","all costs caused by the breach are recoverable"]', 'The student sees the property appreciated above the contract price and assumes no loss or only nominal damages. The breaker is that the question is about loss of use during the delay, not loss of the bargain as a whole.', 'The choice uses market value above contract price, which is the wrong frame for damages for a 15-day delay in possession.', 'Ask whether the choice answers the loss-of-use-for-15-days question.', 'RC-CONTRACTS-DELAY-DAMAGES-01'),
  ('ec2b3eb9-af59-5b05-97f7-393def9261fd', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14511_worship_studio_delay'), 'B', '$1,000 only (the fair rental value of the barn for 15 days).', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'It is the only choice that gives the correct, foreseeability-limited measure of damages for the 15-day delay.', NULL, 'RC-CONTRACTS-DELAY-DAMAGES-01'),
  ('44adb22f-2d8c-5ee5-9b43-f3a7eed5b2e8', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14511_worship_studio_delay'), 'C', '$1,000 (the fair rental value of the barn for 15 days), plus $1,000 (the cost of the mobile recording trailer for 15 days).', 0, '["wrong_element"]', '["market value above price means no damages","closing and paying waives all claims","all costs caused by the breach are recoverable"]', 'The student sees the correct rental-value base and a sympathetic second cost and adds them. The breaker is the Gold Key: the trailer cost was not foreseeable to Peter at the time of contracting.', 'The choice proves the correct rental-value element but adds a special cost that fails the foreseeability test because Peter had no reason to know of the worship-album plans or trailer rental.', 'Ask whether the seller knew of the buyer''s special need at the time of contracting.', 'RC-CONTRACTS-DELAY-DAMAGES-01'),
  ('132f892d-a196-5026-b613-568e52ce5f63', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14511_worship_studio_delay'), 'D', 'Nothing, because by paying the purchase price on April 30, Paul waived whatever cause of action he may have had.', 0, '["contradiction"]', '["market value above price means no damages","closing and paying waives all claims","all costs caused by the breach are recoverable"]', 'The student sees that Paul closed and paid the full price and assumes acceptance waives the claim. The breaker is the explicit reservation of rights in the stem.', 'The choice says Paul waived by paying, but the stem states he reserved his rights when he accepted the deed and paid.', 'Look for an express reservation of rights before concluding waiver.', 'RC-CONTRACTS-DELAY-DAMAGES-01'),
  ('5b7e3d3c-cf3b-5d59-b2ba-b438dd01eef7', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14521_sea_of_galilee_lantern_walk'), 'A', 'Lydia recovers $35,000, the benefit Lydia conferred on Daniel minus the $25,000 in damages incurred by Daniel.', 0, '["bait_doctrine","wrong_frame"]', '["reasonable_value_controls","breacher_can_net_restitution","both_sides_wash_out","ignore_progress_payment_allocation"]', 'Students net the two visible numbers and feel the answer accounts for both lawsuits. The Gold Key blocks the move because restitution for the breaching performer cannot make the nonbreaching party pay beyond the subcontract price.', 'A imports the attractive net-restitution frame and pays the breaching performer $35,000. The Gold Key redirects the calculation to the contract-price ceiling and the contractor''s $25,000 completion overrun.', 'Apply the Gold Key: the breaching performer cannot use restitution to exceed the contract allocation.', 'REMED-CONTRACTS-REMEDY-CAP-01'),
  ('0fe9029d-1ede-554d-a3e9-4cd2b9a85679', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14521_sea_of_galilee_lantern_walk'), 'B', 'Daniel recovers $25,000, the excess over the subcontract price that Daniel paid for the performance he had bargained to receive from Lydia.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'B is the correct answer. Daniel bargained to receive the completed walkway-and-lighting work for $260,000. Because Lydia unjustifiably stopped, Daniel had to pay a replacement $155,000 after already paying Lydia $130,000. That means Daniel’s total outlay for the promised completed performance is $285,000. The overrun is $25,000.

Gold Key: A breaching performer cannot use restitution to collect more than the contract allocation for the performance retained; the nonbreaching party is protected against paying more than the bargain price. Silver Key: In a dollar-outcome array, label who gets paid before trusting arithmetic. Here, the survivor pays Daniel, not Lydia.', NULL, 'REMED-CONTRACTS-REMEDY-CAP-01'),
  ('413294eb-d19a-59ec-9f0b-b7f863158270', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14521_sea_of_galilee_lantern_walk'), 'C', 'Neither party recovers anything, because Lydia committed a material, unexcused breach and Daniel received a $60,000 benefit from Lydia for which Lydia has not been paid.', 0, '["even_split"]', '["reasonable_value_controls","breacher_can_net_restitution","both_sides_wash_out","ignore_progress_payment_allocation"]', 'Students see one fact favoring each side and convert the problem into a wash. The breaker is that the contractor''s reasonable completion overrun is still a contract damage.', 'C turns two opposing money facts into a no-recovery compromise. The array move asks which side the law pays, and the completion overrun points to Daniel.', 'Compute the completion overrun: $130,000 paid plus $155,000 completion cost minus $260,000 contract price equals $25,000.', 'REMED-CONTRACTS-REMEDY-CAP-01'),
  ('607b274d-5d76-52ab-9ff4-ef1749312437', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14521_sea_of_galilee_lantern_walk'), 'D', 'Lydia recovers $60,000, the benefit conferred on Daniel for which Lydia has not been paid.', 0, '["wrong_element","wrong_frame"]', '["reasonable_value_controls","breacher_can_net_restitution","both_sides_wash_out","ignore_progress_payment_allocation"]', 'Students focus on the $60,000 unpaid reasonable value and stop before the contract ceiling. The breaker is that the completed-stage contract allocation has already been paid.', 'D counts Lydia''s unpaid reasonable value but omits the contract allocation and Daniel''s completion overrun. The Gold Key blocks a breaching performer from collecting above the contract allocation.', 'Use the progress-payment fact to see Lydia has already received the contract amount due for that stage.', 'REMED-CONTRACTS-REMEDY-CAP-01'),
  ('a1eff792-360a-5ac1-be63-baf974732e66', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14523_psalm_calligraphy_stipend'), 'A', 'Yes, because Martha''s burden of keeping Hannah''s aide will be increased if Peter does not contribute his share.', 0, '["wrong_element"]', '["specific_performance_never_for_money","no_pocket_loss_no_remedy","moral_burden_equals_legal_burden"]', 'The student feels that Martha will have to rescue Hannah if Peter stops paying. The breaker is that the call asks for legal remedy adequacy, not moral pressure on Martha.', 'This choice sells sympathy for Martha''s increased burden, but the court-order remedy call turns on Peter''s promise and adequacy of legal relief.', 'Ask whether the answer explains inadequacy of legal damages.', 'REM-CONTRACTS-SPECIFIC-PERFORMANCE-MONEY-01'),
  ('c3afe817-fab0-56dc-bbc8-7023c325c459', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14523_psalm_calligraphy_stipend'), 'B', 'No, because Peter''s breach of contract has caused no economic harm to Martha.', 0, '["wrong_element","violation_vs_remedy"]', '["specific_performance_never_for_money","no_pocket_loss_no_remedy","moral_burden_equals_legal_burden"]', 'The student sees no money moving from Peter to Martha and thinks no pocket loss means no remedy. The breaker is that equitable enforcement can protect the promised performance even when Martha''s immediate loss is not the measure.', 'This choice measures Martha''s present pocket loss, but the question asks whether performance should be ordered.', 'Match the remedy to the promised performance, not just to the plaintiff''s wallet.', 'REM-CONTRACTS-SPECIFIC-PERFORMANCE-MONEY-01'),
  ('243cdcdd-80b6-5a5a-828e-cf376cace299', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14523_psalm_calligraphy_stipend'), 'C', 'Yes, because Martha''s remedy at law is inadequate.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'C is correct. Peter promised to make continuing monthly payments to Hannah for the rest of Hannah''s life. Martha is not asking for a one-time damages award; she is asking the court to make Peter perform the continuing payment promise. Silver Key: first lock the remedy call. The question is whether a legal remedy is adequate, not whether Martha feels pressure or has already lost money. Gold Key: a payment promise is not automatically outside specific performance. Where damages are not an adequate substitute, a court may order the promised performance.', NULL, 'REM-CONTRACTS-SPECIFIC-PERFORMANCE-MONEY-01'),
  ('148d08e0-72ef-5249-8985-34f8b3d4810f', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14523_psalm_calligraphy_stipend'), 'D', 'No, because a court will not grant specific performance of a promise to pay money.', 0, '["tiered_absolute","wrong_frame"]', '["specific_performance_never_for_money","no_pocket_loss_no_remedy","moral_burden_equals_legal_burden"]', 'The student remembers that courts usually prefer damages for money obligations. The breaker is the Gold Key: no categorical bar exists when damages are inadequate.', 'This choice turns a usual damages preference into a categorical no-money-specific-performance rule.', 'Replace ''money promise means no SP'' with ''adequacy controls.''', 'REM-CONTRACTS-SPECIFIC-PERFORMANCE-MONEY-01'),
  ('60bff498-7def-5d21-97f9-8695e4b7bec7', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14525_jonah_fish_escape'), 'A', 'reversed, because the test for limiting damages is what the breaching party could reasonably have foreseen at the time of the breach.', 0, '["bait_doctrine","wrong_frame"]', '["new_business_lost_profits_never_recoverable","correct_appellate_result_is_enough","damages_is_only_for_jury","foreseeability_is_the_only_contract_damages_limiter"]', 'The choice gives the appealing appellate result and invokes foreseeability, a familiar contract-damages concept. The breaker is that the challenged defect is the new-business-profit certainty instruction, not a breach-time foreseeability test.', 'Foreseeability is a real contract-damages limiter, but this choice attacks the wrong limiter and uses the wrong timing. Recovery here turns on the per se new-business-profit instruction and the reasonable-certainty standard.', 'Circle the challenged sentence and ask which answer attacks that sentence.', 'REM-CONTRACTS-NEW-BIZ-LOST-PROFITS'),
  ('80ee11ee-10f3-54e2-93dc-d02f60062aac', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14525_jonah_fish_escape'), 'B', 'reversed, because under the prevailing modern view, lost profits of a new business are recoverable if they are established with reasonable certainty.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'It is the only choice that both reverses and identifies the instruction''s legal error.', NULL, 'REM-CONTRACTS-NEW-BIZ-LOST-PROFITS'),
  ('07572449-b59b-50c4-aeee-3f63e3193dbe', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14525_jonah_fish_escape'), 'C', 'affirmed, because the trial court stated the law correctly.', 0, '["flat_misstatement"]', '["new_business_lost_profits_never_recoverable","correct_appellate_result_is_enough","damages_is_only_for_jury","foreseeability_is_the_only_contract_damages_limiter"]', 'The choice sounds safe because the trial judge did recite ordinary foreseeability language before the new-business sentence. The breaker is the Gold Key: a categorical new-business-profit bar is not the modern rule.', 'This is the dominant trap. It treats the trial instruction as correct even though the instruction imposed a categorical new-business-profit bar.', 'Replace ''never recoverable'' with ''harder to prove'' and re-run the choice.', 'REM-CONTRACTS-NEW-BIZ-LOST-PROFITS'),
  ('c91c62f8-4b7e-5f18-ad9a-24b0b275f615', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14525_jonah_fish_escape'), 'D', 'affirmed, because the issue of damages for breach of contract was solely a jury question.', 0, '["tiered_absolute","procedural_frame"]', '["new_business_lost_profits_never_recoverable","correct_appellate_result_is_enough","damages_is_only_for_jury","foreseeability_is_the_only_contract_damages_limiter"]', 'The choice leans on the familiar idea that juries decide damages. The breaker is the absolute word ''solely,'' because appellate courts review legal errors in jury instructions.', 'The word ''solely'' overclaims. Damages can involve jury fact-finding, but an appellate court can review an erroneous legal instruction.', 'Separate jury fact-finding from appellate review of legal instructions.', 'REM-CONTRACTS-NEW-BIZ-LOST-PROFITS'),
  ('d272388e-873c-5ea7-a180-d0c0204b0da5', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14530_lantern_courtyard'), 'A', 'The fair market value, if any, of an assignable option to purchase the Lantern Courtyard for $470,000.', 0, '["wrong_element","violation_vs_remedy"]', '["no_specific_performance_means_nominal_only","option_value_after_exercise","resale_price_sets_damages"]', 'The option setup is long and tempting, so students price the option paper. The call assumes the buyer wins and asks for the land-bargain remedy.', 'Prices the option paper rather than the property bargain after the stem assumes the buyer wins.', 'Ask what the buyer lost after winning: the property bargain.', 'REMEDY-OBJECT-51060101'),
  ('3d90638c-35c9-5a8d-a844-d4a95c960d63', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14530_lantern_courtyard'), 'B', 'The amount, if any, by which the fair market value of the Lantern Courtyard on the date of Lydia''s breach exceeded $470,000.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'Only B answers the assumed-liability remedies call with the property-bargain benchmark.', NULL, 'REMEDY-OBJECT-51060101'),
  ('15cca7e2-74ed-545a-95e0-de159318a71c', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14530_lantern_courtyard'), 'C', 'Nominal damages only, because the remedy of specific performance was not available to Ruth.', 0, '["tiered_absolute","violation_vs_remedy"]', '["no_specific_performance_means_nominal_only","option_value_after_exercise","resale_price_sets_damages"]', 'The seller already conveyed the property, so nominal-only sounds like a practical remedial limit. The absolute word ''only'' collapses the damages remedy that survives when specific performance is unavailable.', 'Says nominal damages only, but the remedy layer allows expectation damages even when specific performance is unavailable.', 'Reject nominal-only and calculate the expectancy baseline.', 'REMEDY-OBJECT-51060101'),
  ('2022c1be-5156-59e9-8203-23669bae2548', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14530_lantern_courtyard'), 'D', '$45,000, plus the amount, if any, by which the fair market value of the Lantern Courtyard on the date of Lydia''s breach exceeded $515,000.', 0, '["fabricated_rule","violation_vs_remedy"]', '["no_specific_performance_means_nominal_only","option_value_after_exercise","resale_price_sets_damages"]', 'The third-party sale price is concrete and invites subtraction. The breaker is that resale price is not the independent benchmark for the buyer''s expectation damages.', 'Uses the third-party resale price as a second damages baseline instead of using the contract price as the single bargain baseline.', 'Use fair market value minus contract price, not a resale-price hybrid.', 'REMEDY-OBJECT-51060101'),
  ('c2f661b2-15ce-5d5e-8892-3b4bebd3e541', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14546_koine_tutor'), 'A', 'No, because Lydia was neither identified when Ruth''s promise was made nor aware of it when the tutoring agreement was made.', 0, '["wrong_element","wrong_frame"]', '["beneficiary must be named","beneficiary must know before contracting","oral payment promise is automatically suretyship","yes outcome can be justified by any familiar enforcement doctrine"]', 'The student sees the highlighted negative facts that Lydia was unnamed and unaware and treats them as disqualifying. The breaker is that those facts answer a neighboring timing or vesting issue, not whether the provider was intended.', 'This choice answers the neighboring identification/awareness issue instead of the intended-beneficiary status issue.', 'Ask whether the promise points payment to a provider class.', 'CON-TPB-01'),
  ('4fa411fb-0737-550e-84e8-c853e90cbad7', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14546_koine_tutor'), 'B', 'No, because Ruth''s promise to Timothy is unenforceable under the suretyship clause of the Statute of Frauds.', 0, '["bait_doctrine","wrong_frame"]', '["beneficiary must be named","beneficiary must know before contracting","oral payment promise is automatically suretyship","yes outcome can be justified by any familiar enforcement doctrine"]', 'The student sees an oral promise to pay another person''s bill and reaches for the Statute of Frauds. The breaker is that the promise was made to Timothy rather than to Lydia as creditor.', 'This choice imports the suretyship Statute of Frauds frame even though the promise was made to Timothy rather than Lydia.', 'Check whether the promise was made to the creditor or to the debtor.', 'CON-TPB-01'),
  ('4a8ca569-8516-5a70-90a1-3ee999d72d3f', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14546_koine_tutor'), 'C', 'Yes, because Lydia is an intended beneficiary of the Ruth-Timothy contract.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'After the three neighboring-rationale answers are cut, C is the only answer that is both true and responsive.', NULL, 'CON-TPB-01'),
  ('7060dda7-2825-5270-ac80-4158242f932d', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '14546_koine_tutor'), 'D', 'Yes, under the doctrine of promissory estoppel.', 0, '["bait_doctrine","wrong_frame"]', '["beneficiary must be named","beneficiary must know before contracting","oral payment promise is automatically suretyship","yes outcome can be justified by any familiar enforcement doctrine"]', 'The student wants a yes answer and reaches for a familiar reliance doctrine. The breaker is that Lydia did not know of Ruth''s promise when she agreed to teach.', 'This choice uses promissory estoppel even though the stem blocks reliance by saying Lydia did not know of the promise when she agreed to teach.', 'Confirm knowledge and reliance before using promissory estoppel.', 'CON-TPB-01'),
  ('ed0dba3c-4f7b-5766-bc48-5c8d265626c3', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '16127_stewardship_bomb'), 'A', 'Arson or conspiracy, but not both.', 0, '["bait_doctrine"]', '["conspiracy always merges with the substantive offense","Wharton''s Rule applies to any crime committed by two persons","arson is always a Wharton''s Rule crime"]', 'The student half-remembers ''conspiracy merges'' and picks the but-not-both answer. The breaker is the Gold Key: Wharton''s Rule applies only to crimes that by definition require two persons (bigamy, adultery, incest, dueling); arson is not such a crime, and the general rule is non-merger.', 'Merger-myth dominant trap. Conspiracy does not merge with the substantive offense as a general rule; Wharton''s Rule applies only to crimes that by definition require two persons, and arson is not such a crime. Both stack.', 'Ask whether the substantive crime is one that by definition requires two persons (bigamy, adultery, incest, dueling). Arson is not such a crime; both stack.', 'RC-CRIMINAL-CONSPIRACY-MERGER-01'),
  ('09dd6e08-6bca-56f5-b952-ce80373e7780', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '16127_stewardship_bomb'), 'B', 'Arson only.', 0, '["misfit"]', '["conspiracy always merges with the substantive offense","Wharton''s Rule applies to any crime committed by two persons","arson is always a Wharton''s Rule crime"]', 'The student focuses on the completed burning and never registers the group agreement that triggers conspiracy. The breaker is the agreement-plus-overt-act structure of conspiracy; the bombing is the overt act, and the two men agreed to commit arson.', 'Missing-element cut. The stem describes a two-person agreement to commit arson — exactly what conspiracy requires — but the answer asserts arson only.', 'Ask whether two or more persons agreed to commit a substantive crime. If yes, conspiracy exists in addition to the substantive offense.', 'RC-CRIMINAL-CONSPIRACY-MERGER-01'),
  ('05cfd5cb-9ba2-5474-bd1f-24ecf399da59', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '16127_stewardship_bomb'), 'C', 'Both arson and conspiracy.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'The arson elements are met (the statute extends the definition; the men wanted the plant burned and succeeded), and the conspiracy elements are met (two persons agreed to commit arson). Conspiracy does not merge with the substantive offense; Wharton''s Rule does not apply to arson.', NULL, 'RC-CRIMINAL-CONSPIRACY-MERGER-01'),
  ('b36d9d1c-29e9-53e8-9de1-2295be738a84', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '16127_stewardship_bomb'), 'D', 'Conspiracy only.', 0, '["misfit"]', '["conspiracy always merges with the substantive offense","Wharton''s Rule applies to any crime committed by two persons","arson is always a Wharton''s Rule crime"]', 'The student registers the agreement but treats the substantive offense as if absorbed by the agreement. The breaker is the completed burning — arson under the extended statute is a separate crime satisfied by the bombing.', 'Mirror missing-element cut. The stem describes a completed burning — exactly what arson under the extended statute requires — but the answer asserts conspiracy only.', 'Ask whether the substantive offense was actually completed. The bombing is both the conspiracy overt act and the arson actus reus.', 'RC-CRIMINAL-CONSPIRACY-MERGER-01'),
  ('f72d91d0-797a-501c-9782-865a10ca545c', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17018_youth_dodgeball_consent'), 'A', 'No, because the plaintiff was injured.', 0, '["flat_misstatement"]', '["consent never covers intentional contact","injury automatically defeats consent","no intent means no battery"]', 'The student sees the plaintiff fell and assumes injury defeats consent. The breaker is that the contact was customary and within the rules, so injury alone is not enough.', 'The choice says injury defeats consent, but the stem frames the contact as customary and within the rules, so injury alone is not enough.', 'Remember that injury does not defeat consent to contacts within the ordinary scope of the sport.', 'RC-TORTS-SPORT-CONSENT-01'),
  ('10a96175-3790-5bd3-b03d-a32204b022bf', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17018_youth_dodgeball_consent'), 'B', 'Yes, but only because the player did not intend any contact.', 0, '["wrong_element","wrong_frame"]', '["consent never covers intentional contact","injury automatically defeats consent","no intent means no battery"]', 'The student wants to say yes and finds a reason that sounds legally safe: no intent means no battery. The breaker is that the privilege comes from consent, not lack of intent.', 'The choice reaches the right result but relies on lack of intent rather than the actual defense of consent to contacts within the ordinary scope of the sport.', 'Identify whether the privilege comes from consent or from absence of intent.', 'RC-TORTS-SPORT-CONSENT-01'),
  ('86690fbb-89cf-5d93-94d6-e4bfe0371dfb', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17018_youth_dodgeball_consent'), 'C', 'Yes, because the contact was within the ordinary scope of the sport as played.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'It is the only choice that correctly names the consent defense and aligns with the stem facts of a customary, in-bounds contact.', NULL, 'RC-TORTS-SPORT-CONSENT-01'),
  ('d31a13b9-b48e-565b-9524-25ab93329a35', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17018_youth_dodgeball_consent'), 'D', 'No, because consent to a sport never covers intentional contact.', 0, '["tiered_absolute"]', '["consent never covers intentional contact","injury automatically defeats consent","no intent means no battery"]', 'The student treats ''intentional contact'' as the core of battery and reasons that consent can never cover it. The breaker is that contact sports involve intentional contacts within the scope of implied consent.', 'The choice states an absolute rule that ''never'' covers intentional contact, but contact sports involve intentional contacts that are within the scope of implied consent.', 'Spot the ''never'' and recall that contact sports involve intentional contacts covered by implied consent.', 'RC-TORTS-SPORT-CONSENT-01'),
  ('42d44411-c311-54fa-9e47-dc91b598438d', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17027_heritage_plow_fraud'), 'A', 'A federal preponderance-of-the-evidence standard, because all burdens of proof in federal court are governed by federal procedural rules.', 0, '["flat_misstatement"]', '["all burdens of proof in federal court are procedural","Erie is a forum-shopping / party-preference rule","juries decide facts freely with no burden of proof"]', 'The student sees a federal-court setting and reaches for a federal procedural rule. The breaker is the Gold Key: the burden of proof on a state-law claim element follows the state right into federal court.', 'Asserts the doctrinal overclaim that all burdens of proof in federal court are procedural. The overclaim is not student-accessibly false without the Erie / York anchor.', 'Ask whether the claim is a state-law claim; if so, the state burden of proof on a state-law claim element follows the right into federal court.', 'RC-CIVPRO-ERIE-BURDEN-01'),
  ('39167211-28ea-5773-8297-8e1f660515db', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17027_heritage_plow_fraud'), 'B', 'Whatever standard the defendant prefers, because Erie exists to prevent forum shopping between state and federal court.', 0, '["misfit","wrong_frame"]', '["all burdens of proof in federal court are procedural","Erie is a forum-shopping / party-preference rule","juries decide facts freely with no burden of proof"]', 'The student hears Erie and reaches for the forum-shopping / party-preference rationale. The breaker is name-the-rule: Erie is a state-law-application rule, not a party-preference rule.', 'Recharacterizes Erie as a forum-shopping or party-preference rule. Erie''s actual function is to require the federal court to apply state substantive law; it is not a party-preference rule.', 'Erie is a state-law-application rule, not a party-preference rule.', 'RC-CIVPRO-ERIE-BURDEN-01'),
  ('42a19b13-3e96-561e-a169-b0b428c45940', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17027_heritage_plow_fraud'), 'C', 'The State X clear-and-convincing-evidence standard, because the burden is bound up with the substantive State X fraud claim.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'It is the only answer that maps the Erie substantive/procedural allocation to the specific burden on a state-law claim element.', NULL, 'RC-CIVPRO-ERIE-BURDEN-01'),
  ('3ceb1a51-37f5-5f41-90af-26c05cb723d1', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17027_heritage_plow_fraud'), 'D', 'No burden of proof at all, because the jury evaluates the evidence freely and reaches its own conclusion.', 0, '["fabricated_rule"]', '["all burdens of proof in federal court are procedural","Erie is a forum-shopping / party-preference rule","juries decide facts freely with no burden of proof"]', 'The student reads ''burden'' and pictures a free jury. The breaker is structural: ''no burden'' is not a recognized standard in any American court system.', 'Asserts the fabricated rule that there is no burden of proof at all. The cut is structural: every American civil claim has a burden of proof on each element.', 'Every claim has a burden; the question is which body of law supplies it.', 'RC-CIVPRO-ERIE-BURDEN-01'),
  ('673311b7-0463-528f-86ee-6b5b76c8040f', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17111_parish_newsletter_republication'), 'A', 'No, because attributing the accusation to "a parent in our school community" insulates the newsletter editor from liability for the underlying charge.', 0, '["fabricated_rule"]', '["attribution insulates the republisher","newsletter/podcast is not publication","you must sue the original speaker before suing a republisher"]', 'The student hears ''I said it was someone else'' and treats attribution as a shield. The breaker is the Gold Key: attribution is not a defense to republication.', 'Asserts the fabricated rule that attribution insulates the republisher from liability. The doctrinal overclaim is not student-accessibly false without the § 578 anchor.', 'Attribution goes to damages mitigation, not to liability; the republisher is on the hook for the defamatory sting.', 'RC-TORTS-REPUBLICATION-ATTRIBUTION-01'),
  ('25a0abbe-00a5-502c-960b-5bd34d8b0efe', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17111_parish_newsletter_republication'), 'B', 'Yes, but only if the parent who wrote the original email is also joined as a defendant in the suit.', 0, '["misfit","procedural_frame"]', '["attribution insulates the republisher","newsletter/podcast is not publication","you must sue the original speaker before suing a republisher"]', 'The student half-remembers joint-and-several liability and converts it into a joinder rule. The breaker is structural: defamation plaintiffs may sue any one publisher they choose.', 'Converts a joint-and-several-liability observation into a joinder rule. Defamation plaintiffs may sue any one publisher they choose; joinder is not a precondition to liability.', 'Defamation plaintiffs may sue any one publisher they choose; the original speaker''s presence is not a precondition.', 'RC-TORTS-REPUBLICATION-ATTRIBUTION-01'),
  ('48cffd76-e7a1-5c25-8ef4-a98a960440ba', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17111_parish_newsletter_republication'), 'C', 'Yes, because republishing a defamatory accusation to the newsletter''s subscribers is publication, even though the editor attributed the charge to the parent.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'It is the only answer that treats republication as itself a publication and rejects attribution as a defense.', NULL, 'RC-TORTS-REPUBLICATION-ATTRIBUTION-01'),
  ('2d94a7c0-dcaa-5122-b56c-cf689396d674', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17111_parish_newsletter_republication'), 'D', 'No, because a newsletter is not the kind of medium that counts as "publication" for defamation purposes.', 0, '["misfit"]', '["attribution insulates the republisher","newsletter/podcast is not publication","you must sue the original speaker before suing a republisher"]', 'The student tries to draw a medium-based exception. The breaker is structural: publication is communication to a third party, regardless of medium.', 'Asserts a medium-based exception that does not exist. Publication is communication to a third party, regardless of medium.', 'Publication does not depend on medium; email, podcast, broadcast, and print all qualify.', 'RC-TORTS-REPUBLICATION-ATTRIBUTION-01'),
  ('1f51f8cd-b800-51b7-9b08-8394d0c8f4c3', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17114_farmstand_excessive_fine'), 'A', 'The claim fails because the Excessive Fines Clause of the Eighth Amendment applies only to the federal government, not to states or their political subdivisions.', 0, '["fabricated_rule"]', '["the Excessive Fines Clause applies only to the federal government","every large fine is automatically unconstitutional","excessive fines is a procedural due process issue"]', 'The student treats the Excessive Fines Clause as federal-only. The breaker is the Gold Key: Timbs v. Indiana (2019) squarely held that the Excessive Fines Clause is incorporated through the Fourteenth Amendment Due Process Clause.', 'Asserts the fabricated rule that the Excessive Fines Clause applies only to the federal government. The doctrinal overclaim is not student-accessibly false without the Timbs v. Indiana anchor.', 'Timbs v. Indiana (2019) squarely held that the Excessive Fines Clause is incorporated through the Fourteenth Amendment Due Process Clause.', 'RC-CONLAW-INCORPORATION-EXCESSIVE-FINES-01'),
  ('718f4fe3-551c-5f6c-a8fc-520ec4da3a02', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17114_farmstand_excessive_fine'), 'B', 'The claim wins automatically because every forfeiture larger than the maximum statutory fine is unconstitutional, regardless of the violation.', 0, '["tiered_absolute"]', '["the Excessive Fines Clause applies only to the federal government","every large fine is automatically unconstitutional","excessive fines is a procedural due process issue"]', 'The student converts a substantive Excessive Fines claim into a flat rule. The breaker is the call: the question asks about incorporation, not about merits; the Excessive Fines Clause applies a gross-disproportionality test, not a flat rule.', 'Asserts the flat rule that every forfeiture larger than the maximum civil fine is unconstitutional. The overclaim is visible in the answer text: it ignores the gross-disproportionality analysis that the Excessive Fines Clause requires.', 'The call asks about incorporation, not about merits; the Excessive Fines Clause does not invalidate every forfeiture that exceeds the maximum civil fine.', 'RC-CONLAW-INCORPORATION-EXCESSIVE-FINES-01'),
  ('7151f02e-37aa-5db1-8255-9c9fcb191a20', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17114_farmstand_excessive_fine'), 'C', 'The claim can proceed because the Excessive Fines Clause of the Eighth Amendment is incorporated against the states (and their political subdivisions) through the Fourteenth Amendment Due Process Clause.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'It is the only answer that names the correct incorporation route for the Excessive Fines Clause.', NULL, 'RC-CONLAW-INCORPORATION-EXCESSIVE-FINES-01'),
  ('df32d1a1-e971-544f-a87d-1fb8c2c1b933', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17114_farmstand_excessive_fine'), 'D', 'The claim belongs only under the procedural due process rules requiring notice and a hearing before a forfeiture is imposed.', 0, '["misfit","procedural_frame"]', '["the Excessive Fines Clause applies only to the federal government","every large fine is automatically unconstitutional","excessive fines is a procedural due process issue"]', 'The student reduces the substantive claim to procedural due process. The breaker is structural: the Excessive Fines Clause is a substantive constraint, not a procedural one.', 'Converts a substantive Eighth Amendment claim into a procedural due process claim. The Excessive Fines Clause is a substantive constraint on the size of the forfeiture, not a procedural constraint on the procedure for imposing it.', 'Procedural due process addresses how the government takes a protected interest; the Excessive Fines Clause addresses the kind of interest the government can take.', 'RC-CONLAW-INCORPORATION-EXCESSIVE-FINES-01'),
  ('99ca21af-0692-5ad1-89d3-fc0f8d4eda95', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17117_day_school_playground_jmol'), 'A', 'Yes, because any pre-verdict Rule 50(a) motion preserves every possible post-verdict Rule 50(b) ground, regardless of whether the ground was raised before submission.', 0, '["tiered_absolute"]', '["any Rule 50(a) motion preserves every Rule 50(b) ground","Rule 50(b) is for plaintiffs only","causation is more important than damages, so the court can grant renewed JMOL on a new causation ground"]', 'The student hears ''Rule 50(a) was filed'' and assumes any post-verdict challenge is preserved. The breaker is the Gold Key: Rule 50(b) renews, rather than invents, a Rule 50(a) legal-sufficiency challenge.', 'Asserts the flat rule that any pre-verdict Rule 50(a) motion preserves every possible post-verdict Rule 50(b) ground. The doctrinal overclaim is not student-accessibly false without the Unitherm anchor.', 'Rule 50(b) renews, rather than invents, a Rule 50(a) legal-sufficiency challenge; new grounds are not properly before the court.', 'RC-CIVPRO-RULE50-RENEWAL-01'),
  ('b888d631-32fa-5c85-9144-bff810758760', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17117_day_school_playground_jmol'), 'B', 'No, because Paul did not fairly raise the causation ground in his pre-verdict Rule 50(a) motion, and a Rule 50(b) motion may not introduce a new legal-sufficiency ground.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'It is the only answer that names the renewal-not-invention rule for Rule 50(b).', NULL, 'RC-CIVPRO-RULE50-RENEWAL-01'),
  ('04b7607a-676c-5042-b70d-39f9cceb820d', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17117_day_school_playground_jmol'), 'C', 'No, because Rule 50(b) is available only to plaintiffs, not to defendants, after an adverse jury verdict.', 0, '["misfit","procedural_frame"]', '["any Rule 50(a) motion preserves every Rule 50(b) ground","Rule 50(b) is for plaintiffs only","causation is more important than damages, so the court can grant renewed JMOL on a new causation ground"]', 'The student reverses the party-availability rule. The breaker is structural: Rule 50 is available to any party; a defendant after an adverse jury verdict is the paradigmatic Rule 50(b) movant.', 'Asserts a party-availability rule that is the inverse of the actual rule. Rule 50 is available to any party; a defendant after an adverse jury verdict is the paradigmatic Rule 50(b) movant.', 'Rule 50 is available to any party; a defendant after an adverse jury verdict is the paradigmatic Rule 50(b) movant.', 'RC-CIVPRO-RULE50-RENEWAL-01'),
  ('55028ad8-7142-5443-83b1-d561af742c72', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17117_day_school_playground_jmol'), 'D', 'Yes, because causation is more important than damages, and a court may grant renewed JMOL on any ground that is more important than the ground the defendant did raise pre-verdict.', 0, '["misfit"]', '["any Rule 50(a) motion preserves every Rule 50(b) ground","Rule 50(b) is for plaintiffs only","causation is more important than damages, so the court can grant renewed JMOL on a new causation ground"]', 'The student converts a doctrinal-strength observation into a preservation rule. The breaker is structural: importance does not substitute for preservation.', 'Converts a doctrinal-strength observation into a preservation rule. Importance does not substitute for preservation; Rule 50(b) is a renewal motion, not an importance-priority vehicle.', 'Importance does not substitute for preservation; Rule 50(b) is a renewal motion, not an importance-priority vehicle.', 'RC-CIVPRO-RULE50-RENEWAL-01'),
  ('d73c6596-5c36-5d0c-8bbc-6791e44cac06', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17163_permit_fee_ledger'), 'A', 'Strict scrutiny applies, because permit fees burden travel within the state.', 0, '["misfit","wrong_frame"]', '["due process makes every property loss by a state employee a constitutional violation","strict scrutiny applies to any permit fee","the defendant''s process is the question"]', 'The student hears ''permit'' and reaches for strict scrutiny. The breaker is that strict scrutiny is reserved for fundamental rights and suspect classifications, and a $75 permit fee is neither.', 'Strict scrutiny is for fundamental rights and suspect classifications; a $75 permit fee is neither. Wrong-frame level of review.', 'Ask whether the burdened right is fundamental or the classification is suspect. Neither is true here.', 'RC-CONSTITUTIONAL-LAW-NEGLIGENT-LOSS-01'),
  ('3ada0249-3837-51bb-a753-ada84421c5df', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17163_permit_fee_ledger'), 'B', 'The clerk must receive a hearing before the applicant can sue.', 0, '["misfit","wrong_frame"]', '["due process makes every property loss by a state employee a constitutional violation","strict scrutiny applies to any permit fee","the defendant''s process is the question"]', 'The student redirects the call to the clerk''s employment process. The breaker is that the call asks about the applicant''s due process rights, not the clerk''s.', 'The call asks about the applicant''s due process rights, not the clerk''s employment process. Wrong party.', 'Ask whose due process rights the call is about. The applicant''s, not the clerk''s.', 'RC-CONSTITUTIONAL-LAW-NEGLIGENT-LOSS-01'),
  ('7eed5d6b-fb85-5e79-929a-f79a0659a369', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17163_permit_fee_ledger'), 'C', 'The Due Process Clause is not ordinarily violated by mere negligence.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'The fact pattern alleges only an accidental, single-employee loss of a $75 fee — paradigmatic negligence, not wrongful state action. The Due Process Clause is not a federal tort statute; the proper remedy is a state-law claim, not a § 1983 suit.', NULL, 'RC-CONSTITUTIONAL-LAW-NEGLIGENT-LOSS-01'),
  ('02cc4de8-5ab5-5cd8-8db6-a466df77dce7', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17163_permit_fee_ledger'), 'D', 'Every property loss by a state employee is a due process violation.', 0, '["tiered_absolute"]', '["due process makes every property loss by a state employee a constitutional violation","strict scrutiny applies to any permit fee","the defendant''s process is the question"]', 'The student treats the Due Process Clause as a make-tort-whole theory: a property loss by a state employee is a constitutional violation. The breaker is the Gold Key: negligent loss is not a constitutional deprivation; the proper remedy is a state tort claim, not § 1983.', 'The Due Process Clause does not make every careless loss by a state employee a constitutional deprivation; the clause targets wrongful state action, not negligence.', 'Ask whether the deprivation is wrongful state action or mere negligence. Only the former is a constitutional deprivation.', 'RC-CONSTITUTIONAL-LAW-NEGLIGENT-LOSS-01'),
  ('81871de1-19b8-5203-b7d2-853f19098518', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17179_potluck_lot_rear_end'), 'A', 'A crossclaim, because it is a claim by one co-party against another arising from the same accident.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'It is the only procedural label that matches Mary''s claim against a co-defendant arising from the same transaction or occurrence.', NULL, 'RC-CIVPRO-COUNTERCROSS-01'),
  ('4b7fe315-2ec6-5426-bd78-224eeed4aecf', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17179_potluck_lot_rear_end'), 'B', 'A required-party motion, because Paul''s fault must be adjudicated.', 0, '["misfit","wrong_frame"]', '["any defendant-asserted claim is a counterclaim","Rule 14 impleader is a free-floating device for contribution","Rule 19 governs any multi-defendant situation"]', 'The student hears ''fault must be adjudicated'' and reaches for Rule 19 compulsory joinder. The breaker is caption-level: Paul is already a party, and Rule 19 governs absent persons, not labeled claims.', 'Rule 19 governs joinder of absent persons; Paul is already a party, and the question asks for the procedural label on Mary''s claim, not a motion to add someone.', 'Ask whether anyone is missing from the case. If not, Rule 19 is not in play; the question asks for a labeled claim, not a motion.', 'RC-CIVPRO-COUNTERCROSS-01'),
  ('f5014ee5-753c-576d-80cc-6b5d01f5943c', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17179_potluck_lot_rear_end'), 'C', 'A counterclaim, because it is a claim asserted by a defendant.', 0, '["bait_doctrine","wrong_frame"]', '["any defendant-asserted claim is a counterclaim","Rule 14 impleader is a free-floating device for contribution","Rule 19 governs any multi-defendant situation"]', 'Counterclaim is the everyday word for any defendant-asserted claim. The breaker is the Gold Key: counterclaim runs against an opposing party, and Paul is on Mary''s side of the caption.', 'Counterclaim is the everyday label for any defendant-asserted claim, but Rule 13(a) defines it as a claim against an opposing party. Paul is a co-defendant (same side of the ''v.''), not an opposing party.', 'Crossclaim = co-party; counterclaim = opposing party. Co-defendants on the same side of the ''v.'' are co-parties.', 'RC-CIVPRO-COUNTERCROSS-01'),
  ('4756f982-532b-5e1b-8aa9-6aa88eb83e39', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17179_potluck_lot_rear_end'), 'D', 'An impleader claim, because Paul may be liable for the plaintiff''s damages.', 0, '["bait_doctrine","wrong_frame"]', '["any defendant-asserted claim is a counterclaim","Rule 14 impleader is a free-floating device for contribution","Rule 19 governs any multi-defendant situation"]', 'The student hears ''third-party practice'' and grabs Rule 14. The breaker is caption-level: impleader brings in non-parties, and Paul is already a named co-defendant.', 'Rule 14 impleader exists to bring a non-party into the case. Paul is already a named co-defendant, so impleader is unavailable; the labeled claim is a crossclaim.', 'Impleader brings in a non-party. When the second defendant is already named in the caption, impleader is unavailable.', 'RC-CIVPRO-COUNTERCROSS-01'),
  ('7c6895e7-135d-510c-bdb9-89ef1a54de40', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17225_midnight_utility_fraud'), 'A', 'No, because breaking always requires physical damage.', 0, '["flat_misstatement"]', '["burglary requires physical force","voluntary opening defeats consent","burglary requires completed theft"]', 'The student fuses ''breaking'' with physical force, the everyday conception. The breaker is the Gold Key: at common law, breaking includes constructive breaking (fraud, threat, trick), with no physical damage required.', 'The choice states that breaking always requires physical damage. At common law, breaking is satisfied by constructive breaking — fraud, threat, or trick — even without physical damage. The ''always'' formulation misstates the rule.', 'Ask whether the entry was by force, fraud, threat, or trick. Fraud is constructive breaking at common law.', 'RC-CRIMINAL-BURGLARY-BREAKING-01'),
  ('4e877744-f9ca-52d4-b217-f591794ab13d', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17225_midnight_utility_fraud'), 'B', 'No, because the homeowner opened the door voluntarily.', 0, '["wrong_element"]', '["burglary requires physical force","voluntary opening defeats consent","burglary requires completed theft"]', 'The student treats the homeowner''s act of opening the door as effective consent that defeats the breaking element. The breaker is the Gold Key: consent procured by fraud is not effective consent for the constructive-breaking analysis.', 'The choice assumes voluntary opening defeats consent for the breaking element. Consent procured by fraud is not effective consent for the constructive-breaking analysis; Mary''s apparent consent was induced by deception.', 'Ask whether the consent was procured by deception. If yes, the consent is not effective for the constructive-breaking analysis.', 'RC-CRIMINAL-BURGLARY-BREAKING-01'),
  ('2e1d6357-f249-51eb-acb6-0ff2564bea51', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17225_midnight_utility_fraud'), 'C', 'No, because burglary requires theft after entry, not intent at entry.', 0, '["wrong_element"]', '["burglary requires physical force","voluntary opening defeats consent","burglary requires completed theft"]', 'The student conflates common-law burglary with completed theft and reads the call as asking whether the theft happened. The breaker is the timing rule: burglary is complete on entry with felonious intent, even if the intended felony is not carried out.', 'The choice requires a completed theft. Common-law burglary is complete on entry with felonious intent; the intended felony need not actually occur.', 'Ask whether the intent existed at the moment of entry. If yes, the intended felony need not actually occur for burglary to be complete.', 'RC-CRIMINAL-BURGLARY-BREAKING-01'),
  ('9cce797d-d009-5ec6-b192-56cc1998543b', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17225_midnight_utility_fraud'), 'D', 'Yes, because entry obtained by fraud can be constructive breaking.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'It is the only choice that correctly states the constructive-breaking rule and matches the call.', NULL, 'RC-CRIMINAL-BURGLARY-BREAKING-01'),
  ('8a4cc692-fc33-54c9-9fcd-ce0fe70e75e7', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17236_wellness_visit_revocation'), 'A', 'Yes, because written consent can never be withdrawn.', 0, '["tiered_absolute"]', '["a signed consent form is binding","consent is never a defense to battery","battery requires serious injury"]', 'The student treats the signed form as binding, like a contract. The breaker is the Gold Key: consent is a present license revocable before contact, regardless of the form.', 'The choice treats the signed form as binding. Consent is a present license; revocation is effective at any time before the contact, regardless of the form. The ''never withdrawn'' formulation is a tiered absolute that misstates the rule.', 'Ask whether the patient said anything that withdrew the consent before the contact. If yes, revocation defeats the form defense.', 'RC-TORTS-CONSENT-REVOCATION-01'),
  ('25e87f4a-1357-5a1b-9aba-bdc6f714613a', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17236_wellness_visit_revocation'), 'B', 'No, because consent is never a defense to battery.', 0, '["flat_misstatement"]', '["a signed consent form is binding","consent is never a defense to battery","battery requires serious injury"]', 'The student overcorrects, hears ''revoked before contact'' and concludes that consent is categorically not a defense. The breaker is the foundational rule: consent is a defense when valid and unrevoked; the defense fails here because of revocation, not because consent is never a defense.', 'The choice overcorrects, treating consent as categorically not a defense. Consent is a defense to battery when valid and unrevoked; the defense fails here because the consent was withdrawn.', 'Ask whether consent is a defense when valid and unrevoked. Yes; the defense fails here because of revocation, not because consent is categorically unavailable.', 'RC-TORTS-CONSENT-REVOCATION-01'),
  ('0fe8be0a-354f-5cc9-aa7a-8faa1d8924ce', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17236_wellness_visit_revocation'), 'C', 'No, because the patient revoked consent before the contact occurred.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'It is the only choice that correctly identifies the operative defense and matches the call.', NULL, 'RC-TORTS-CONSENT-REVOCATION-01'),
  ('2881d668-4eb4-5812-ab34-d047ae62b6be', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17236_wellness_visit_revocation'), 'D', 'Yes, because the injection was minor.', 0, '["extreme_of_range"]', '["a signed consent form is binding","consent is never a defense to battery","battery requires serious injury"]', 'The student maps battery onto a serious-injury requirement (the everyday-negligence frame). The breaker is the Silver Key: battery requires only an unauthorized harmful or offensive contact; the contact itself can be minor, cosmetic, or trivial.', 'The choice fuses battery with a serious-injury requirement. Battery requires only an unauthorized harmful or offensive contact; the contact itself can be minor, cosmetic, or trivial.', 'Ask whether battery requires serious injury. No; unauthorized harmful or offensive contact is enough.', 'RC-TORTS-CONSENT-REVOCATION-01'),
  ('6b5831d2-258a-5bc7-9835-c10baf3ab5d5', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17264_prayer_parcel_trip'), 'A', 'Only limited duties, because the hiker was an unknown trespasser injured by a natural condition.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'It is the only choice that correctly identifies the entrant category, the hazard type, and the resulting limited-duty rule.', NULL, 'RC-TORTS-ENTRANT-CATEGORIES-01'),
  ('be525d87-aab6-5c89-a376-f7c8b94d8dbe', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17264_prayer_parcel_trip'), 'B', 'The full invitee inspection duty for all hazards on the land.', 0, '["misfit","wrong_frame"]', '["any on-land injury triggers the highest duty","owners must warn of every natural condition","warning signs create strict liability"]', 'The student overgeneralizes: ''someone came onto the land, so the highest duty runs.'' The breaker is the Gold Key: the invitee inspection duty runs only to invitees, and no-entry signs are affirmative evidence the owner did not invite the public.', 'The student overgeneralizes: ''someone came onto the land, so the highest duty runs.'' The invitee inspection duty runs only to invitees; no-entry signs are affirmative evidence the owner did not invite the public. Stephen is a trespasser, not an invitee.', 'Ask whether the owner held the land out to the public. No-entry signs are the inverse; the entrant is a trespasser, not an invitee.', 'RC-TORTS-ENTRANT-CATEGORIES-01'),
  ('e70594e0-5fcf-510b-9a87-d54e3cac19da', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17264_prayer_parcel_trip'), 'C', 'A duty to warn of every natural condition on remote land.', 0, '["extreme_of_range"]', '["any on-land injury triggers the highest duty","owners must warn of every natural condition","warning signs create strict liability"]', 'The student over-extends the artificial/natural condition distinction into a warn-of-everything rule. The breaker is the Gold Key: the traditional duty to unknown trespassers is narrow; ''every natural condition'' is not the standard.', 'The student over-extends the artificial/natural condition distinction into a warn-of-everything rule. The traditional duty to unknown trespassers is narrow; ''every natural condition on remote land'' is not the standard.', 'Ask whether the entrant was discovered and whether the hazard was artificial. Both are no; the limited-duty rule applies.', 'RC-TORTS-ENTRANT-CATEGORIES-01'),
  ('c0012935-aa06-5c41-8701-18e490d490c2', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17264_prayer_parcel_trip'), 'D', 'Strict liability because no-entry signs prove the owner expected injury.', 0, '["flat_misstatement"]', '["any on-land injury triggers the highest duty","owners must warn of every natural condition","warning signs create strict liability"]', 'The student reads warning signs as evidence of expected injury and infers strict liability. The breaker is the Silver Key: warning signs are evidence of reasonable care, not a basis for strict liability.', 'The student reads warning signs as evidence of expected injury and infers strict liability. Premises liability to trespassers is fault-based; warning signs are evidence of reasonable care, not a basis for strict liability.', 'Ask whether premises liability to trespassers is fault-based. Yes; warning signs are evidence of reasonable care, not a basis for strict liability.', 'RC-TORTS-ENTRANT-CATEGORIES-01'),
  ('bf9ee1e2-486b-5e3f-a51a-97b15040a37b', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17289_foundation_paneling'), 'A', 'No, because the buyer''s damages are economic.', 0, '["wrong_element","wrong_frame"]', '["every property defect is fraud","economic damages are not recoverable in fraud","fraud can never be based on concealment"]', 'The student imports the negligence-bar-on-pure-economic-loss rule into fraud. The breaker is that fraud is a recognized business tort and economic damages are exactly what it compensates.', 'Fraud is a recognized business tort; economic damages are exactly what it compensates. Wrong element (economic-loss import from negligence).', 'Ask what tort is at issue. Fraud, not negligence. Economic damages are recoverable in fraud.', 'RC-TORTS-FRAUD-CONCEALMENT-MISSTATEMENT-01'),
  ('a8065d23-ca3a-58b2-a331-c6c8d484c269', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17289_foundation_paneling'), 'B', 'Yes, because the seller concealed and misstated a known material fact to induce reliance.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'All five fraud elements are satisfied: Paul''s misstatement is false, he knew it, he intended to induce reliance, Hannah reasonably relied, and she suffered damages by paying full price.', NULL, 'RC-TORTS-FRAUD-CONCEALMENT-MISSTATEMENT-01'),
  ('47841740-d70f-5558-a219-277db8e7687a', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17289_foundation_paneling'), 'C', 'Yes, because every defect in sold property is fraud.', 0, '["tiered_absolute"]', '["every property defect is fraud","economic damages are not recoverable in fraud","fraud can never be based on concealment"]', 'The student treats every property defect as fraud. The breaker is the Gold Key: fraud requires the specific elements (falsity, scienter, intent, reliance, damages) and not every property defect satisfies all of them.', 'Fraud requires the specific elements; a latent defect the seller did not know about is not fraud.', 'Ask whether the seller knew (scienter) and whether the buyer relied. Both are required.', 'RC-TORTS-FRAUD-CONCEALMENT-MISSTATEMENT-01'),
  ('bbca271f-b501-5c76-a3af-826b5155ff06', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17289_foundation_paneling'), 'D', 'No, because fraud can never be based on concealment.', 0, '["flat_misstatement"]', '["every property defect is fraud","economic damages are not recoverable in fraud","fraud can never be based on concealment"]', 'The student treats concealment as a forbidden fraud theory, missing that the seller also made an affirmative misstatement. The breaker is that the misstatement alone satisfies falsity, and active concealment is independently actionable.', 'Active concealment of a known material defect, plus an affirmative misstatement, is actionable. The misstatement alone satisfies falsity.', 'Ask whether the seller also made an affirmative misstatement. The misstatement alone satisfies falsity.', 'RC-TORTS-FRAUD-CONCEALMENT-MISSTATEMENT-01'),
  ('f514a79e-5433-5843-ba08-ebb1d137ca76', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17406_oak_choir_stall'), 'A', 'No, failure to state a claim can be raised by Rule 12(c) after the pleadings close.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'Rule 12(h)(2) preserves the 12(b)(6) defense for any later stage. The omitted 12(b)(6) defense is not waived, and a Rule 12(c) motion for judgment on the pleadings after the pleadings close is the precise procedural vehicle the rule preserves.', NULL, 'RC-CIVPRO-RULE12H-PRESERVATION-01'),
  ('a428acf6-77bf-539d-b2a7-3d05a4842ca2', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17406_oak_choir_stall'), 'B', 'Yes, all Rule 12 defenses are waived if omitted from the first response.', 0, '["tiered_absolute"]', '["all Rule 12 defenses are waived by omission from the answer","12(b)(6) is treated like 12(b)(2) and must be raised in the answer","subject-matter jurisdiction and failure to state a claim are the same defense"]', 'The student treats Rule 12 as one big ''include-it-or-lose-it'' rule. The breaker is the Silver Key: Rule 12(h)(1) waives 12(b)(2)–(5) and 12(b)(7); Rule 12(h)(2) preserves 12(b)(6).', 'Overclaim cut. The Silver Key forces the precise-defense mapping: Rule 12(h)(1) waives 12(b)(2)–(5) and 12(b)(7); Rule 12(h)(2) preserves 12(b)(6). The ''all 12 defenses waived'' framing does not match the (h)(1)/(h)(2) split.', 'Read Rule 12(h)(1) and (h)(2) as separate subsections with different treatment for different defenses.', 'RC-CIVPRO-RULE12H-PRESERVATION-01'),
  ('3af694bb-b9b7-5cbb-816a-cb1f95394fb0', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17406_oak_choir_stall'), 'C', 'No, because subject-matter jurisdiction and failure to state a claim are the same.', 0, '["flat_misstatement"]', '["all Rule 12 defenses are waived by omission from the answer","12(b)(6) is treated like 12(b)(2) and must be raised in the answer","subject-matter jurisdiction and failure to state a claim are the same defense"]', 'The student sees that both defenses can be raised late and concludes they are the same. The breaker is the conflation test: 12(b)(1) is a non-waivable constitutional limit; 12(b)(6) is a merits-based pleading challenge.', 'Conflation cut. Subject-matter jurisdiction (12(b)(1)) and failure to state a claim (12(b)(6)) are separately treated in the rule text; both can be raised late but are not the same defense.', 'Separate the two defenses; subject-matter jurisdiction is a non-waivable constitutional limit; failure to state a claim is a merits-based pleading challenge.', 'RC-CIVPRO-RULE12H-PRESERVATION-01'),
  ('25268f8f-0b1a-5663-889f-4ce762438ab5', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17406_oak_choir_stall'), 'D', 'Yes, failure to state a claim must be raised before answering or never.', 0, '["tiered_absolute"]', '["all Rule 12 defenses are waived by omission from the answer","12(b)(6) is treated like 12(b)(2) and must be raised in the answer","subject-matter jurisdiction and failure to state a claim are the same defense"]', 'The student treats 12(b)(6) as if it were 12(b)(2) (personal jurisdiction, which is waived). The breaker is the Gold Key: Rule 12(h)(2) is the explicit textual carve-out that preserves 12(b)(6) for any later stage.', 'Dominant trap. The student treats 12(b)(6) as if it were 12(b)(2) (personal jurisdiction, which is waived). The Gold Key tells the student Rule 12(h)(2) is the explicit textual carve-out that preserves 12(b)(6) for any later stage, including a Rule 12(c) motion after the pleadings close.', 'Apply the Gold Key: Rule 12(h)(2) is the explicit textual carve-out that preserves 12(b)(6) for any later stage.', 'RC-CIVPRO-RULE12H-PRESERVATION-01'),
  ('03199de6-a26b-547b-807d-c6788b2d35ba', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17412_seminary_faculty_apartment'), 'A', 'Texas, because she remains domiciled there despite her temporary residence in Massachusetts.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'A natural person''s state citizenship for diversity purposes is the state of domicile, which requires physical presence and intent to remain indefinitely. Lydia is physically present in Massachusetts for a fixed-term purpose, but her intent is to return to Texas. Her Texas domicile persists.', NULL, 'RC-CIVPRO-DOMICILE-DIVERSITY-01'),
  ('6ac17624-4e46-5084-adda-c1cefa05681d', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17412_seminary_faculty_apartment'), 'B', 'Massachusetts, because she currently resides there.', 0, '["colloquialism"]', '["an individual can hold two state citizenships at once for diversity","physical absence from the permanent home destroys the domicile","residence alone is enough to establish state citizenship"]', 'The student sees ''currently resides'' and picks the current-residence state. The breaker is the Gold Key: domicile requires both elements, and Lydia''s intent is to return to Texas.', 'Dominant trap. The student sees ''currently resides'' and picks the current-residence state. Domicile requires both physical presence and intent to remain indefinitely; Lydia''s intent is to return to Texas.', 'Apply the Gold Key: domicile requires both elements, and Lydia''s intent is to return to Texas.', 'RC-CIVPRO-DOMICILE-DIVERSITY-01'),
  ('2eebebd0-4d19-5274-ae4a-68e017260e19', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17412_seminary_faculty_apartment'), 'C', 'Both Texas and Massachusetts, because individuals have citizenship wherever they live during a year.', 0, '["fabricated_rule"]', '["an individual can hold two state citizenships at once for diversity","physical absence from the permanent home destroys the domicile","residence alone is enough to establish state citizenship"]', 'The student half-remembers that diversity has residency concepts and assumes dual state citizenship. The breaker is the structural test: an individual has one domicile at a time.', 'Dual-citizenship trap. The choice asserts dual state citizenship, which the diversity rule does not allow. An individual has one domicile at a time.', 'An individual has one domicile at a time for diversity purposes.', 'RC-CIVPRO-DOMICILE-DIVERSITY-01'),
  ('8d6dad02-99b3-5905-8471-b7f35521f6fe', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17412_seminary_faculty_apartment'), 'D', 'No state, because she is away from her permanent home.', 0, '["flat_misstatement"]', '["an individual can hold two state citizenships at once for diversity","physical absence from the permanent home destroys the domicile","residence alone is enough to establish state citizenship"]', 'The student reads ''away from her permanent home'' and concludes there is no state. The breaker is the sticky-domicile rule: domicile persists through physical absence until a new domicile is acquired.', 'No-state edge case. The choice asserts that physical absence from the permanent home destroys the domicile. Domicile is sticky and persists through physical absence until a new domicile is acquired.', 'Domicile persists through physical absence until a new domicile is acquired.', 'RC-CIVPRO-DOMICILE-DIVERSITY-01'),
  ('6d431f25-6552-58a0-aa44-1bce56ea5618', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17443_devotional_auto_renew'), 'A', 'Adequacy of representation is unnecessary because the named plaintiff personally suffered the same injury as the class.', 0, '["wrong_element"]', '["adequacy is subsumed by typicality or by the named plaintiff''s personal injury","numerosity and commonality are the only relevant Rule 23(a) prongs","a single corporate policy makes class certification automatic"]', 'The student treats adequacy as if it were subsumed by the named plaintiff''s personal injury. The breaker is the Gold Key: adequacy is a separate Rule 23(a) prong, distinct from typicality and from the named plaintiff''s personal injury.', 'Dominant trap. The student treats adequacy as if it were subsumed by the named plaintiff''s personal injury. The Gold Key tells the student adequacy is a separate Rule 23(a) prong, distinct from typicality and from the named plaintiff''s personal injury.', 'Apply the Gold Key: adequacy is a separate Rule 23(a) prong, distinct from typicality and from the named plaintiff''s personal injury.', 'RC-CIVPRO-RULE23A-PRONGS-01'),
  ('2530fa07-90e5-53ed-a8ea-dbe7ff508688', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17443_devotional_auto_renew'), 'B', 'The court must find numerosity, commonality, typicality, and adequacy, and the action must also fit within Rule 23(b).', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 23(a) requires the court to find numerosity, commonality, typicality, and adequacy of representation. Rule 23(b) further requires that the action fit within one of three categories. Adequacy of representation is a separate prong, distinct from typicality and from the named plaintiff''s personal injury.', NULL, 'RC-CIVPRO-RULE23A-PRONGS-01'),
  ('6cbcd880-6044-52d4-a679-7e72a1ae0b14', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17443_devotional_auto_renew'), 'C', 'Numerosity and commonality are enough because the challenged policy affected hundreds of customers.', 0, '["wrong_element"]', '["adequacy is subsumed by typicality or by the named plaintiff''s personal injury","numerosity and commonality are the only relevant Rule 23(a) prongs","a single corporate policy makes class certification automatic"]', 'The student reads ''hundreds of customers'' and concludes that numerosity and commonality are the only relevant prongs. The breaker is the Silver Key: Rule 23(a) has four prongs, all required.', 'Two-prong-miss cut. The student reads ''hundreds of customers'' and concludes that numerosity and commonality are the only relevant prongs. The Silver Key forces the student to count the prongs: Rule 23(a) has four, all required.', 'Count the prongs: Rule 23(a) has four, all required.', 'RC-CIVPRO-RULE23A-PRONGS-01'),
  ('46f5338e-f1f5-50d8-8526-4cada5f39c43', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17443_devotional_auto_renew'), 'D', 'Certification is automatic if the company used one policy, because commonality is the main Rule 23 requirement.', 0, '["tiered_absolute"]', '["adequacy is subsumed by typicality or by the named plaintiff''s personal injury","numerosity and commonality are the only relevant Rule 23(a) prongs","a single corporate policy makes class certification automatic"]', 'The student reads ''single policy'' and concludes that commonality is dispositive. The breaker is the Silver Key: commonality is one prong, not the only prong; certification is never ''automatic.''', 'Automatic-certification cut. The student reads ''single policy'' and concludes that commonality is dispositive. The Silver Key forces the student to count the prongs: commonality is one prong, not the only prong; certification is never ''automatic.''', 'Commonality is one prong, not the only prong; certification is never ''automatic.''', 'RC-CIVPRO-RULE23A-PRONGS-01'),
  ('1d4f4ed7-1cd2-56b6-9ef8-a9f9ca6cd24d', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17521_christian_wedding_cake'), 'A', 'Yes, because Mary mailed her acceptance.', 0, '["wrong_element","wrong_frame"]', '["mailbox rule applies to any mailed acceptance","postal loss undoes a mailed acceptance","non-receipt of a mailed acceptance is an excuse from performance"]', 'The student hears ''mailed'' and reaches for the mailbox rule, then applies it without checking the properly dispatched element. The breaker is the Gold Key: the rule applies only when the acceptance is properly dispatched, not merely when it was mailed.', 'The mailbox rule applies only when the acceptance is properly dispatched (correct address + correct postage). A mailed-but-misaddressed or unstamped acceptance is NOT effective on dispatch. The choice states the correct outcome but omits the properly dispatched element.', 'Ask whether the rule applies when the acceptance was merely mailed, or only when the acceptance was properly dispatched. The rule requires proper dispatch — correct address + correct postage.', 'RC-CONTRACTS-MAILBOX-RULE-01'),
  ('0d26c181-fd18-5101-a767-1ca6d93b9a99', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17521_christian_wedding_cake'), 'B', 'No, because the post office lost the letter.', 0, '["flat_misstatement"]', '["mailbox rule applies to any mailed acceptance","postal loss undoes a mailed acceptance","non-receipt of a mailed acceptance is an excuse from performance"]', 'The student sees the postal loss (the most salient fact) and asserts the opposite of the mailbox rule. The breaker is the Silver Key: the offeror bears the risk of postal loss of a properly dispatched acceptance.', 'The mailbox rule exists precisely to allocate the risk of postal loss to the offeror. The choice reverses the rule.', 'Ask whether the mailbox rule exists to allocate postal risk to the offeror. Yes — and the offeror bears the risk of postal loss of a properly dispatched acceptance.', 'RC-CONTRACTS-MAILBOX-RULE-01'),
  ('4f531593-4bc7-571b-a3ac-e1ea9b2c39ee', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17521_christian_wedding_cake'), 'C', 'Yes, because the acceptance was properly addressed with the correct amount of postage.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'Mary''s acceptance was properly addressed to Paul''s bakery and bore the correct amount of postage, so the mailbox rule applies; the acceptance was effective on dispatch when Mary deposited it in the mail, and the post office''s loss of the letter is irrelevant.', NULL, 'RC-CONTRACTS-MAILBOX-RULE-01'),
  ('877da374-f351-536c-adde-21e2289174bd', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17521_christian_wedding_cake'), 'D', 'No, because Paul was excused from performance since he did not receive the letter.', 0, '["misfit","procedural_frame"]', '["mailbox rule applies to any mailed acceptance","postal loss undoes a mailed acceptance","non-receipt of a mailed acceptance is an excuse from performance"]', 'The student reaches for an ''excuse from performance'' framework that does not apply to a formation question. The breaker is the issue-frame check: the question is formation, not excuse.', 'The question is formation, not excuse. An offeror''s failure to receive a mailed acceptance does not, by itself, constitute an excuse from performance. The excuse doctrines require a supervening event or changed circumstances.', 'Ask whether the question is formation or excuse. Formation — the mailbox rule is dispositive. The excuse doctrines require a supervening event or changed circumstances.', 'RC-CONTRACTS-MAILBOX-RULE-01'),
  ('39b10e71-be85-56a0-8181-913bbc5f7094', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17574_christian_podcast_school_board'), 'A', 'Rational basis, because defamation is a tort.', 0, '["misfit","procedural_frame"]', '["rational basis applies to speech restrictions","ad revenue converts political commentary into commercial speech","First Amendment protects only institutional newspapers","prior restraint is a tort doctrine, not a constitutional doctrine"]', 'The student reaches for the most deferential level of scrutiny (rational basis) without checking the doctrinal fit. The breaker is the level-of-scrutiny check: rational basis is for economic and social-welfare legislation, not First Amendment prior restraint.', 'Rational basis is the deferential review for economic and social-welfare legislation; speech restrictions are reviewed under the First Amendment standards (strict or intermediate scrutiny), not rational basis. The question is First Amendment prior restraint, not economic regulation.', 'Ask whether the question is economic/social-welfare legislation or speech restriction. Speech restriction — apply the First Amendment standards (strict or intermediate scrutiny), not rational basis.', 'RC-CONSTITUTIONAL-LAW-PRIOR-RESTRAINT-01'),
  ('8803298b-617b-5332-ab9d-27146b67b652', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17574_christian_podcast_school_board'), 'B', 'Prior restraint, because the order requires advance approval before publication.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'The order is a content-based restraint imposed before publication, requiring a speaker to obtain advance approval from a party (Paul) under a government order. That is the textbook definition of a prior restraint, regardless of the speaker''s medium or whether the speech generates ad revenue.', NULL, 'RC-CONSTITUTIONAL-LAW-PRIOR-RESTRAINT-01'),
  ('53dd021b-0984-54e4-b58b-83023cf1ba82', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17574_christian_podcast_school_board'), 'C', 'Commercial speech, because the podcaster earns ad revenue.', 0, '["bait_doctrine","wrong_frame"]', '["rational basis applies to speech restrictions","ad revenue converts political commentary into commercial speech","First Amendment protects only institutional newspapers","prior restraint is a tort doctrine, not a constitutional doctrine"]', 'The student sees the ad-revenue fact (the most salient red herring) and reaches for the commercial-speech doctrine. The breaker is Silver Key #1: ad revenue does not convert civic commentary about public officials into commercial speech.', 'Ad revenue does not convert political or civic commentary about public officials into commercial speech; commercial speech is speech that does no more than propose a commercial transaction, not political or civic commentary that happens to be monetized.', 'Ask whether the speech proposes a commercial transaction or is civic commentary about a public official. Civic commentary — the commercial-speech frame does not apply.', 'RC-CONSTITUTIONAL-LAW-PRIOR-RESTRAINT-01'),
  ('a1aaba37-2773-5018-8340-35ba83d72ed2', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17574_christian_podcast_school_board'), 'D', 'No First Amendment doctrine, because podcasts are not newspapers.', 0, '["bait_doctrine","wrong_frame"]', '["rational basis applies to speech restrictions","ad revenue converts political commentary into commercial speech","First Amendment protects only institutional newspapers","prior restraint is a tort doctrine, not a constitutional doctrine"]', 'The student reaches for the ''not the institutional press'' argument. The breaker is Silver Key #2: the First Amendment protects all speakers, not just institutional newspapers; the medium does not determine whether the prior-restraint doctrine applies.', 'The First Amendment protects all speakers, not just institutional newspapers; the medium (podcast, blog, newsletter) does not determine whether the prior-restraint doctrine applies.', 'Ask whether the First Amendment protects only institutional newspapers. No — it protects all speakers, regardless of medium.', 'RC-CONSTITUTIONAL-LAW-PRIOR-RESTRAINT-01'),
  ('072e6834-9217-56cc-b170-9e5ccb52d1e4', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17583_bookshop_securities_followup'), 'A', 'Federal common law, because the earlier judgment was a federal-question judgment.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'The earlier judgment was a federal-question judgment. The preclusive effect of a federal-question judgment is governed by federal common law.', NULL, 'RC-CIVPRO-FED-QUESTION-PRECLUSION-01'),
  ('ec659933-d1d1-5db6-912d-2bba1014f3d8', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17583_bookshop_securities_followup'), 'B', 'The law of the forum state automatically, because all preclusion is state law.', 0, '["bait_doctrine","wrong_frame"]', '["all preclusion is state law","Erie controls federal-question preclusion","plaintiff chooses preclusion law","preclusion requires same judge"]', 'The student half-remembers Erie and assumes all preclusion is state law. The breaker is the Gold Key: federal-question judgments get federal common-law preclusion; Erie does not control.', 'Imports Erie into a federal-question preclusion case. Erie governs federal-court decision-making in diversity on a state-law claim; it does not control the preclusive effect of a federal-question judgment.', 'Ask which law governed the prior judgment — federal question or state law? Erie does not control federal-question preclusion.', 'RC-CIVPRO-FED-QUESTION-PRECLUSION-01'),
  ('cd14c841-466a-5195-9a0a-5901d37d9748', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17583_bookshop_securities_followup'), 'C', 'The law of whichever state Mary prefers.', 0, '["misfit","wrong_frame"]', '["all preclusion is state law","Erie controls federal-question preclusion","plaintiff chooses preclusion law","preclusion requires same judge"]', 'The student invents a plaintiff-control rule. The breaker is structural: preclusion law is fixed by the source of the prior judgment, not by the plaintiff''s preference.', 'Wrong-frame: preclusion law is fixed by the source of the prior judgment, not by the plaintiff''s preference. The plaintiff does not select the preclusion law by choice.', 'Preclusion law is fixed by the source of the prior judgment, not by the plaintiff''s preference.', 'RC-CIVPRO-FED-QUESTION-PRECLUSION-01'),
  ('fc7338af-0dde-5afe-8e4a-0c493ff594c5', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17583_bookshop_securities_followup'), 'D', 'No law applies until the same judge hears both cases.', 0, '["misfit","wrong_frame"]', '["all preclusion is state law","Erie controls federal-question preclusion","plaintiff chooses preclusion law","preclusion requires same judge"]', 'The student invents a same-judge rule. The breaker is structural: preclusion applies across judges and courts; the prior judgment''s preclusive effect does not depend on judge identity.', 'Wrong-frame: preclusion does not require the same judge. The second federal court must apply the preclusion law fixed by the source of the prior judgment.', 'Preclusion applies across judges and courts; the prior judgment''s preclusive effect does not depend on judge identity.', 'RC-CIVPRO-FED-QUESTION-PRECLUSION-01'),
  ('45073ca1-4c2a-5882-b8e0-20d75753f153', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17630_blown_bible_orchard'), 'A', 'Only the damages caused by Daniel''s removal of the Bible from Paul''s land.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'Daniel has a qualified privilege to enter Paul''s land to retrieve a chattel that got there through no fault of his own, but the privilege protects the entry only. Daniel is liable for the actual damage caused by his entry and removal (the trampled saplings and broken branches), not for damage caused by the spill.', NULL, 'RC-TORTS-CHATTEL-RETRIEVAL-01'),
  ('fb895cd3-63b9-5348-bf7c-4490a5b7ac56', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17630_blown_bible_orchard'), 'B', 'All damages resulting from the Bible''s being blown onto Paul''s land.', 0, '["bait_doctrine","wrong_frame"]', '["spill-caused damage is always recoverable","qualified privilege is absolute","nominal is the right measure when entry is privileged"]', 'The student treats the spill as the entry-caused event and stacks all downstream damage onto Daniel. The breaker is the Gold Key: spill-caused damage is not recoverable absent an abnormally dangerous activity.', 'Treats the spill as the entry-caused event and stacks all downstream damage onto Daniel. Spill-caused damage is not recoverable absent an abnormally dangerous activity.', 'Split the causation: spill-caused damage is not recoverable absent abnormally dangerous activity; removal-caused damage is.', 'RC-TORTS-CHATTEL-RETRIEVAL-01'),
  ('d0ce75c2-e977-52af-ac30-793685c432a4', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17630_blown_bible_orchard'), 'C', 'Nominal damages only.', 0, '["half_truth","wrong_frame"]', '["spill-caused damage is always recoverable","qualified privilege is absolute","nominal is the right measure when entry is privileged"]', 'The student treats the qualified privilege as a complete shield and stops at nominal. The breaker is the Silver Key: the privilege protects the entry, not the damage the entry itself causes.', 'Treats the qualified privilege as a complete shield. The privilege protects the entry, not the damage the entry itself causes.', 'The privilege protects the entry, not the damage the entry itself causes.', 'RC-TORTS-CHATTEL-RETRIEVAL-01'),
  ('f7ccad9f-93f9-5008-bf12-4f7facdffce4', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17630_blown_bible_orchard'), 'D', 'No damages.', 0, '["half_truth","wrong_frame"]', '["spill-caused damage is always recoverable","qualified privilege is absolute","nominal is the right measure when entry is privileged"]', 'The student treats the qualified privilege as absolute. The same Silver Key handles the axis: the privilege is qualified, and the actor is liable for damage caused by the entry itself.', 'Treats the qualified privilege as absolute. The privilege is qualified, and the actor is liable for damage caused by the entry itself.', 'The privilege is qualified, and the actor is liable for damage caused by the entry itself.', 'RC-TORTS-CHATTEL-RETRIEVAL-01'),
  ('6cde6bf6-4908-5070-bac8-43ad9d3f813b', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17680_ministry_housing_permit'), 'A', 'The denial can fail rational basis review because it rests only on bare animus toward the group.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'The board''s stated rationale was bare animus, and the board gave no legitimate interest. Animus is not a legitimate government interest, even under rational basis review.', NULL, 'RC-CONLAW-RATIONAL-BASIS-WITH-BITE-01'),
  ('2d7976aa-d55a-5fe2-b701-9d77ee1dc6fd', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17680_ministry_housing_permit'), 'B', 'Strict scrutiny applies automatically because the group is unpopular.', 0, '["backwards","wrong_frame"]', '["animus triggers strict scrutiny","rational basis means no review","majority dislike is a legitimate interest"]', 'The student reaches for a tier-escalation argument that does not exist. The breaker is the Gold Key: rational-basis-with-bite cases do not convert the group to a suspect class; they apply rational basis review but conclude that animus is not a legitimate interest.', 'Reaches for a tier-escalation argument that does not exist. Rational-basis-with-bite cases do not convert the group to a suspect class; they apply rational basis review but conclude that animus is not a legitimate interest.', 'Ask whether the group is a suspect or quasi-suspect class. If not, strict scrutiny does not apply; the rational-basis-with-bite argument is the strongest move.', 'RC-CONLAW-RATIONAL-BASIS-WITH-BITE-01'),
  ('2aba42de-e0cc-5722-8c15-24ba356404c1', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17680_ministry_housing_permit'), 'C', 'The denial is valid because rational basis review means no review at all.', 0, '["tiered_absolute"]', '["animus triggers strict scrutiny","rational basis means no review","majority dislike is a legitimate interest"]', 'The student treats rational basis as rubber-stamp. The breaker is the Silver Key: rational basis review is deferential but still requires a legitimate interest. Animus is not a legitimate interest.', 'Treats rational basis as rubber-stamp. Rational basis review is deferential but still requires a legitimate interest. Animus is not a legitimate interest.', 'Rational basis is deferential but still requires a legitimate interest. Animus is not a legitimate interest.', 'RC-CONLAW-RATIONAL-BASIS-WITH-BITE-01'),
  ('0ac8b350-9536-54bf-926c-39e01793134e', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17680_ministry_housing_permit'), 'D', 'The denial is valid if any resident dislikes the group.', 0, '["colloquialism"]', '["animus triggers strict scrutiny","rational basis means no review","majority dislike is a legitimate interest"]', 'The student treats majority disfavor as the test. The breaker is structural: Equal Protection asks about legitimate interest, not about resident dislike. Bare dislike is not a legitimate interest.', 'Treats majority disfavor as the test. Equal Protection asks about legitimate interest, not about resident dislike.', 'Equal Protection asks about legitimate interest, not about resident dislike.', 'RC-CONLAW-RATIONAL-BASIS-WITH-BITE-01'),
  ('7c2242e0-28ec-55d1-9040-1553c5b1227d', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17704_fender_shepherd'), 'A', 'State B preclusion law, because the second case is pending there.', 0, '["tiered_absolute"]', '["second court is federal → federal preclusion law always","preclusion is court-internal and cannot cross court systems","the forum''s preclusion law applies whenever the second suit is there"]', 'The student anchors on the second court''s location and assumes the forum''s preclusion law applies. The breaker is the rendering-state rule: the source of preclusion law is the rendering court, not the forum. The ''always'' is a tiered_absolute overclaim.', 'The ''always'' + ''because the second case is pending there'' is a tiered_absolute overclaim. Preclusion law does not follow the forum; it follows the rendering court.', 'Ask which court rendered the prior judgment. That court — not the forum — is the source under § 1738 / Semtek.', 'RC-CIVPRO-SOURCE-OF-PRECLUSION-01'),
  ('6ff4eeda-e7e9-5426-b868-425372aac963', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17704_fender_shepherd'), 'B', 'No preclusion law applies unless both cases are in the same court.', 0, '["fabricated_rule"]', '["second court is federal → federal preclusion law always","preclusion is court-internal and cannot cross court systems","the forum''s preclusion law applies whenever the second suit is there"]', 'The student assumes preclusion is a court-internal doctrine and cannot cross court systems. The breaker is § 1738''s express cross-court operation; the ''unless both cases are in the same court'' condition is a fabricated rule.', 'The ''unless both cases are in the same court'' condition is a fabricated rule. § 1738 expressly operates across courts and gives state-court judgments preclusive effect in federal court.', 'Cite § 1738''s express cross-court operation; preclusion routinely operates across court systems.', 'RC-CIVPRO-SOURCE-OF-PRECLUSION-01'),
  ('851a547d-92e1-5034-92b7-316ca2616bb9', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17704_fender_shepherd'), 'C', 'State A preclusion law, because State A rendered the judgment.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'Under 28 U.S.C. § 1738 and Semtek, a federal court must give a state-court judgment the same preclusive effect the rendering state would give. The second court''s federal character is irrelevant when the prior judgment is state-court.', NULL, 'RC-CIVPRO-SOURCE-OF-PRECLUSION-01'),
  ('eec909aa-321e-5d47-8c98-e0beb9e3cca5', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17704_fender_shepherd'), 'D', 'Federal preclusion law always, because the second court is federal.', 0, '["tiered_absolute"]', '["second court is federal → federal preclusion law always","preclusion is court-internal and cannot cross court systems","the forum''s preclusion law applies whenever the second suit is there"]', 'The student anchors on ''second court is federal'' and infers that federal preclusion law must govern. The breaker is the Gold Key: § 1738 / Semtek controls state-court judgments, and federal common law of preclusion applies only to federal-court judgments. The ''always'' is a tiered_absolute overclaim.', 'The ''always'' is a tiered_absolute overclaim. The second court''s federal character is irrelevant when the prior judgment is state-court; § 1738 / Semtek directs the federal court to use the rendering state''s preclusion law. Federal common law of preclusion applies only to federal-court judgments.', 'Apply the Gold Key: § 1738 / Semtek controls state-court judgments; federal common law of preclusion applies only to federal-court judgments. The ''second court is federal'' hook is a red herring.', 'RC-CIVPRO-SOURCE-OF-PRECLUSION-01'),
  ('8b76d3bb-b427-5f92-bd7a-4ecd05719d1f', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17709_tutoring_tithe'), 'A', 'No, because unrelated claims can never be aggregated.', 0, '["tiered_absolute"]', '["relatedness is required for all aggregation","each individual claim must independently exceed $75,000","federal question is the basis whenever the answer is yes"]', 'The student assumes relatedness is required and reads the ''wholly unrelated'' phrase as a bar to aggregation. The breaker is the Silver Key: the single-plaintiff-vs-single-defendant rule makes relatedness irrelevant. The ''never'' is a tiered_absolute overclaim.', 'The ''never'' is a tiered_absolute overclaim. The aggregation rule does NOT require relatedness when one plaintiff sues one defendant. The ''relatedness required'' rule applies to multiple plaintiffs, not to one plaintiff.', 'Apply the Silver Key: the single-plaintiff-vs-single-defendant rule makes relatedness irrelevant. Relatedness is required only for multiple plaintiffs.', 'RC-CIVPRO-AGGREGATION-AMOUNT-01'),
  ('b16bd171-ce32-5797-b3b3-e466343894d6', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17709_tutoring_tithe'), 'B', 'No, because each individual claim must exceed $75,000.', 0, '["fabricated_rule"]', '["relatedness is required for all aggregation","each individual claim must independently exceed $75,000","federal question is the basis whenever the answer is yes"]', 'The student applies a non-existent ''each individual claim must exceed'' requirement. The breaker is the aggregation rule: a single plaintiff can aggregate to meet the threshold. The ''each must exceed'' condition is a fabricated rule; it is the multi-plaintiff rule, not the single-plaintiff rule.', 'The ''each individual claim must exceed $75,000'' condition is a fabricated rule. A single plaintiff can aggregate to meet the threshold; the rule that each claim must independently exceed $75,000 is the multi-plaintiff rule, not the single-plaintiff rule.', 'Run the ''one plaintiff vs one defendant'' check. If one plaintiff, aggregate to meet the threshold. The ''each claim must exceed'' rule is the multi-plaintiff rule, not the single-plaintiff rule.', 'RC-CIVPRO-AGGREGATION-AMOUNT-01'),
  ('a0727ded-83bf-588b-8b84-e52b9b23edb2', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17709_tutoring_tithe'), 'C', 'Yes, but only because the claims arise under federal law.', 0, '["wrong_element"]', '["relatedness is required for all aggregation","each individual claim must independently exceed $75,000","federal question is the basis whenever the answer is yes"]', 'The student gets the answer right (yes) but for the wrong reason (federal question). The breaker is the basis check: this is diversity, not federal question. The ''but only because'' condition makes the federal-question basis dispositive when it is not.', 'The student gets the answer right (yes) but for the wrong reason (federal question). The basis here is diversity, not federal question, and the ''but only because'' condition makes the federal-question basis dispositive when it is not.', 'Check the basis. Diversity of citizenship + aggregated amount, not federal question. The ''but only because'' condition makes the federal-question basis dispositive when it is not.', 'RC-CIVPRO-AGGREGATION-AMOUNT-01'),
  ('fd757cbc-3ce0-5cae-84eb-322d5415b85e', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17709_tutoring_tithe'), 'D', 'Yes, because one plaintiff may aggregate multiple claims against one defendant to exceed $75,000.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'A single plaintiff can aggregate all claims against a single defendant, related or unrelated, to meet the $75,000 amount-in-controversy requirement. The aggregated amount is $85,000, which satisfies the threshold. Diversity is satisfied (different states).', NULL, 'RC-CIVPRO-AGGREGATION-AMOUNT-01'),
  ('f7ab1fed-141c-5c8c-b305-8d3d575725bf', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17711_mercy_clinic_zoning'), 'A', 'The motion is untimely because Mary''s Mercy waited despite knowing about the case, and late intervention would prejudice existing parties.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'Rule 24(a) and Rule 24(b) both require timeliness; the movant knew of the case from the start, waited until the eve of trial, and the proposed intervention would require reopening discovery and delay trial. Untimeliness combined with prejudice to existing parties is the strongest available ground for denial.', NULL, 'RC-CIVPRO-RULE-24-TIMELINESS-01'),
  ('ebf1673f-92dd-5d57-8f6f-26606e1721a5', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17711_mercy_clinic_zoning'), 'B', 'Intervention is required because zoning decisions affect the public.', 0, '["bait_doctrine"]', '["public interest is a magic override of Rule 24","Rule 24 requires property ownership","only named parties can argue"]', 'The student hears ''zoning'' and ''public'' and assumes intervention is mandatory. The breaker is the Silver Key: the public importance of zoning is not a magic override of Rule 24''s timeliness requirement; ''intervention is required'' misstates the mandatory-intervention rule.', 'Dominant trap. The public importance of zoning is real but is not a magic override of Rule 24''s timeliness gate; ''intervention is required'' misstates the mandatory-intervention rule, which requires a separate Rule 24(a)(2) showing of impairment of a legally protected interest, not merely a general public concern.', 'Separate the public importance of zoning from the Rule 24(a)(2) mandatory-intervention rule. The public interest is not itself a magic override of the timeliness gate.', 'RC-CIVPRO-RULE-24-TIMELINESS-01'),
  ('eab0da04-e3a3-5fce-936d-fa072b51befd', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17711_mercy_clinic_zoning'), 'C', 'Intervention must be denied because only original parties may raise traffic-impact arguments.', 0, '["misfit"]', '["public interest is a magic override of Rule 24","Rule 24 requires property ownership","only named parties can argue"]', 'The student assumes only named parties can argue. The breaker is Rule 24''s plain text: non-parties may move to intervene; the question is whether the Rule 24 requirements are met.', 'Wrong-rule cut. Rule 24 expressly allows non-parties to raise arguments; the question is whether the Rule 24 requirements are met, not whether the movant is a named party.', 'Ask whether Rule 24 contemplates non-parties as movants. It does; the question is whether the Rule 24 requirements are met.', 'RC-CIVPRO-RULE-24-TIMELINESS-01'),
  ('5ae8d866-fb22-5bc4-8501-785f6ecc8e5a', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17711_mercy_clinic_zoning'), 'D', 'Mary''s Mercy has no possible interest because it is not a property owner.', 0, '["misfit"]', '["public interest is a magic override of Rule 24","Rule 24 requires property ownership","only named parties can argue"]', 'The student assumes property ownership is required. The breaker is Rule 24(a)(2): the interest test is functional, and a mobile-medical ministry has a cognizable interest in traffic, parking, and patient access.', 'Wrong-rule cut. Rule 24(a)(2) requires an interest that may be impaired by the disposition; it does not require property ownership. A mobile-medical ministry that operates clinics in the affected neighborhood has a cognizable interest in traffic, parking, and patient access.', 'Ask whether Rule 24(a)(2) requires property ownership. It does not; the interest test is functional, and a mobile-medical ministry has a cognizable interest in traffic, parking, and patient access.', 'RC-CIVPRO-RULE-24-TIMELINESS-01'),
  ('bbd9b348-077f-58bb-864c-9590a669b4d8', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17728_illuminated_manuscript'), 'A', 'Congress lacks power unless the theft itself happened in multiple states.', 0, '["backwards"]', '["stolen goods are outside commerce","Commerce Clause requires multi-state character of the underlying offense","only shipping-company operators are reached by NSPA"]', 'The student assumes the multi-state character of the underlying offense is required. The breaker is the regulated-act / taking distinction: the regulated act is the interstate shipment, not the underlying taking.', 'Wrong-rule cut. The Commerce Clause, including the channels-of-commerce power, is not conditioned on the multi-state character of the underlying offense. The regulated act is the interstate shipment, which is itself a use of the channels of interstate commerce.', 'Separate the regulated act (the interstate shipment) from the underlying taking (state-law crime). The Commerce Clause is not conditioned on the multi-state character of the underlying offense.', 'RC-CONLAW-CHANNELS-OF-COMMERCE-01'),
  ('6de67ce0-775a-5860-9b19-632c75e46ed1', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17728_illuminated_manuscript'), 'B', 'Congress may regulate use of interstate channels to move stolen goods.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'The National Stolen Property Act targets the act of shipping stolen property across state lines — the use of the channels of interstate commerce. Congress''s commerce power reaches the channels themselves; the in-state origin of the taking is not a defense to the federal charge of knowingly shipping stolen property across state lines.', NULL, 'RC-CONLAW-CHANNELS-OF-COMMERCE-01'),
  ('37d7f62b-e6c4-5e6c-8b0e-7909f0f03ff6', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17728_illuminated_manuscript'), 'C', 'Congress may regulate only if the defendant personally owns a shipping company.', 0, '["fabricated_rule"]', '["stolen goods are outside commerce","Commerce Clause requires multi-state character of the underlying offense","only shipping-company operators are reached by NSPA"]', 'The student assumes only shipping-company operators are reached. The breaker is the NSPA''s plain text: the statute reaches any person who knowingly ships stolen property across state lines.', 'Wrong-rule cut. The National Stolen Property Act reaches any person who knowingly ships stolen property across state lines, whether or not the defendant owns a shipping company. The statute targets the act of using the channels, not the defendant''s occupational status.', 'Read the NSPA''s plain text; the statute reaches any person who knowingly ships stolen property across state lines.', 'RC-CONLAW-CHANNELS-OF-COMMERCE-01'),
  ('80636146-8a07-5c28-892a-9ae2d189d665', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17728_illuminated_manuscript'), 'D', 'Congress lacks power because stolen goods are outside commerce.', 0, '["bait_doctrine"]', '["stolen goods are outside commerce","Commerce Clause requires multi-state character of the underlying offense","only shipping-company operators are reached by NSPA"]', 'The student hears ''stolen goods'' and ''outside commerce'' and assumes Congress cannot reach them. The breaker is the Gold Key: the channels-of-commerce power reaches the channels themselves, not the goods in them.', 'Dominant trap. The ''stolen goods are outside commerce'' framing misstates the channels-of-commerce power. The Gold Key tells the student the channels power reaches the channels themselves, not the goods in them. Even if stolen goods have no clean commercial title, Congress can still regulate their movement through the channels of interstate commerce.', 'Apply the Gold Key: the channels-of-commerce power reaches the channels themselves, not the goods in them.', 'RC-CONLAW-CHANNELS-OF-COMMERCE-01'),
  ('573f9927-18f2-5b4c-8982-b1e079cf5caa', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17857_living_nativity_carver'), 'A', 'No, because truthful price competition without improper means is generally privileged.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'The other three answers all carry a visible defect (overclaim or reversed rule); A is the only true-and-responsive answer and matches the call focus (is Esther likely to prevail).', NULL, 'RC-TORTS-PROSPECTIVE-PRIVILEGE-01'),
  ('1957cdd9-5cc3-57c9-91d0-6486221d66b8', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17857_living_nativity_carver'), 'B', 'Yes, because any expected annual commission is tortious interference.', 0, '["tiered_absolute"]', '["lost a deal equals tortious interference","competition privilege is only for existing contracts","expectancies are never protected"]', 'The student sees Esther lose the deal and concludes that losing a deal must be tortious. The breaker is the privilege: lawful price competition is privileged, and ''any lost deal'' is an overclaim of the rule.', 'The ''any expected commission'' overclaim misstates the prospective-advantage rule. The rule requires improper means, not just any lost expectancy; lawful price competition is privileged.', 'Ask whether the competitor''s means were improper; truthful pricing is privileged.', 'RC-TORTS-PROSPECTIVE-PRIVILEGE-01'),
  ('a594caf5-d8ef-5b71-ab60-9ccd08b21f8a', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17857_living_nativity_carver'), 'C', 'Yes, because the competition privilege applies only to existing contracts.', 0, '["backwards"]', '["lost a deal equals tortious interference","competition privilege is only for existing contracts","expectancies are never protected"]', 'The student half-remembers that there is a competition privilege and inverts the scope. The breaker is the rule: the privilege applies to competition for prospective business, not only to existing contracts.', 'The competition privilege applies to competition for prospective business, including the renewal-type relationship. Reversing the rule''s scope to ''only existing contracts'' is a misstatement.', 'Read the rule — the privilege covers competition for prospective advantage, not just existing contracts.', 'RC-TORTS-PROSPECTIVE-PRIVILEGE-01'),
  ('9bf3e569-19a8-5592-b9d2-b8a7150bc71a', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17857_living_nativity_carver'), 'D', 'No, because business expectancies are never protected.', 0, '["extreme_of_range"]', '["lost a deal equals tortious interference","competition privilege is only for existing contracts","expectancies are never protected"]', 'The student tries to explain why Esther loses by saying expectancies are never protected. The breaker is the overclaim: expectancies can be protected when interference is by improper means or with an existing contract.', 'Business expectancies are not categorically unprotected; they can support a claim when interference is by improper means or with an existing contract. ''Never'' is an overclaim.', 'Ask whether the absolute holds; expectancies can be protected when means are improper.', 'RC-TORTS-PROSPECTIVE-PRIVILEGE-01'),
  ('5df372cf-051a-50c2-89ff-9840e07c900f', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17859_olive_branch_ornament'), 'A', 'No, because the property must be removed from the building.', 0, '["extreme_of_range"]', '["larceny requires leaving the building","store consent covers all handling","sale is an element of larceny"]', 'The student conflates asportation with a successful shoplifting exit. The breaker is the asportation minimum: any movement, however slight, suffices; the property need not leave the building.', 'The ''must be removed from the building'' formulation pushes the asportation rule too far. Asportation requires only minimal movement, not removal from the building.', 'Ask what the asportation minimum actually is; any movement, however slight, satisfies it.', 'RC-CRIMINAL-ASPORTATION-MINIMUM-01'),
  ('d9012a57-ed51-55c6-a6c6-d2d2288f05d9', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17859_olive_branch_ornament'), 'B', 'No, because picking up merchandise in a store is always permitted until the customer leaves.', 0, '["tiered_absolute"]', '["larceny requires leaving the building","store consent covers all handling","sale is an element of larceny"]', 'The student overclaims the store-consent rule. The breaker is the consent scope: consent covers inspection, not concealment with intent to steal.', 'The ''always permitted until exit'' framing overclaims the store-consent rule. Consent covers ordinary inspection, not concealment with intent to steal.', 'Ask what the consent covers; inspection, not concealment with intent to steal.', 'RC-CRIMINAL-ASPORTATION-MINIMUM-01'),
  ('f254fe00-ef3c-5948-a13d-0cdc23dffa09', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17859_olive_branch_ornament'), 'C', 'Yes, because even slight movement of the property is enough.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'The other three answers all carry a visible defect (extreme of range, tiered absolute, or wrong element); C is the only true-and-responsive answer and matches the call focus (has the asportation element been satisfied).', NULL, 'RC-CRIMINAL-ASPORTATION-MINIMUM-01'),
  ('42b79fbb-e0ae-596b-89e8-3e11bb383799', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17859_olive_branch_ornament'), 'D', 'No, because the clerk stopped Naomi before she sold the ornament.', 0, '["wrong_element"]', '["larceny requires leaving the building","store consent covers all handling","sale is an element of larceny"]', 'The student looks for a sale element that does not exist. The breaker is the elements of larceny: trespassory taking + asportation + intent to permanently deprive. Sale is not an element.', 'Sale is not an element of larceny. The clerk''s interception affects apprehension, not the offense elements of trespassory taking + asportation + intent to permanently deprive.', 'Ask which element of larceny the answer proves; sale is not one.', 'RC-CRIMINAL-ASPORTATION-MINIMUM-01'),
  ('3143ad10-2629-534f-863c-4d0995f29531', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17913_summit_klaxon'), 'A', 'Use federal common law because speaker agreements are interstate.', 0, '["misfit"]', '["the contract''s choice-of-law clause is self-executing in federal diversity","interstate commerce generates federal common law for state-law diversity claims","plaintiff''s choice of forum dictates the choice of substantive law"]', 'The student hears ''interstate'' and reaches for federal common law. The breaker is Issue-Sense: federal common law is not the rule of decision for state-law claims in diversity. Erie controls.', 'Federal common law is not the rule of decision for state-law contract claims in a federal diversity court. Erie R.R. v. Tompkins, 304 U.S. 64 (1938) controls.', 'Apply Erie: federal courts in diversity apply state substantive law, not federal common law. Interstate commerce does not, on its own, generate federal common law.', 'RC-CIVPRO-KLAXON-CHOICE-CLAUSE-01'),
  ('2c5f6370-ddf2-57aa-a519-56f97b78c1e0', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17913_summit_klaxon'), 'B', 'Enforce State C law automatically because the contract names it.', 0, '["autonomy_appeal"]', '["the contract''s choice-of-law clause is self-executing in federal diversity","interstate commerce generates federal common law for state-law diversity claims","plaintiff''s choice of forum dictates the choice of substantive law"]', 'The student reads the contract clause and treats it as the rule of decision. The breaker is the Gold Key: the contract clause is not self-executing; Klaxon sends the court to the forum''s choice rules first.', 'The contract''s choice-of-law clause is not self-executing. Klaxon Co. v. Stentor Electric Mfg. Co., 313 U.S. 487 (1941) sends the court to the forum state''s choice-of-law rules first.', 'Apply the Gold Key: Klaxon sends the court to the forum''s choice rules first; the contract clause is not self-executing.', 'RC-CIVPRO-KLAXON-CHOICE-CLAUSE-01'),
  ('489200ba-940b-5da7-8186-6c6decde82ba', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17913_summit_klaxon'), 'C', 'Use State A''s choice-of-law rules to determine the effect of the clause.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'Klaxon binds a federal diversity court to apply the forum state''s choice-of-law rules. The contract''s choice clause is not self-executing; it operates through the forum''s choice rules.', NULL, 'RC-CIVPRO-KLAXON-CHOICE-CLAUSE-01'),
  ('65528578-f4d2-5a99-80bd-c0ebf40c92fd', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17913_summit_klaxon'), 'D', 'Use the plaintiff''s preferred law because the plaintiff chose the forum.', 0, '["flat_misstatement"]', '["the contract''s choice-of-law clause is self-executing in federal diversity","interstate commerce generates federal common law for state-law diversity claims","plaintiff''s choice of forum dictates the choice of substantive law"]', 'The student reads plaintiff''s forum choice as substantive-law choice. The breaker is the rule-of-decision test: party preference is not the rule of decision; Klaxon is.', 'Plaintiff''s choice of forum is procedural and does not dictate the choice of substantive law. The rule of decision is the forum''s choice-of-law rules, not the plaintiff''s preference.', 'Plaintiff''s choice of forum is procedural; the rule of decision is the forum''s choice-of-law rules under Klaxon.', 'RC-CIVPRO-KLAXON-CHOICE-CLAUSE-01'),
  ('6c958d66-e07b-5810-aef9-487b6db2603f', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17956_stewards_heavyheart'), 'A', 'The prior contract proceeding has not yet terminated favorably for Lydia.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'The favorable-termination element is a threshold requirement of malicious prosecution. The contract suit is still pending — no judgment, no favorable dismissal. Without favorable termination, the claim is not available regardless of how strong the malice or lack-of-probable-cause showing would otherwise be.', NULL, 'RC-TORTS-MALICIOUS-PROSECUTION-FAVORABLE-TERMINATION-01'),
  ('056681ba-1998-5013-99ba-6e760dcff52c', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17956_stewards_heavyheart'), 'B', 'Malicious prosecution never applies to a civil suit, regardless of jurisdiction.', 0, '["extreme_of_range"]', '["malicious prosecution never applies to civil suits","spite alone proves a malicious-prosecution claim","a pending suit is enough to start a malicious-prosecution claim"]', 'The student treats a jurisdictional nuance as a categorical bar. The breaker is that the modern Restatement permits civil malicious-prosecution claims in many jurisdictions; the missing element is favorable termination, not the civil/criminal distinction.', 'Categorical overstatement. The modern Restatement permits civil malicious-prosecution claims in many jurisdictions; the missing element is favorable termination, not the civil/criminal distinction.', 'Ask whether the rule is categorical or jurisdictional. The modern Restatement permits civil claims; the missing element is favorable termination, not the civil/criminal distinction.', 'RC-TORTS-MALICIOUS-PROSECUTION-FAVORABLE-TERMINATION-01'),
  ('bab93919-4eea-569e-823a-c59f703b434f', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17956_stewards_heavyheart'), 'C', 'Paul''s alleged spite by itself automatically proves the malicious-prosecution claim.', 0, '["tiered_absolute"]', '["malicious prosecution never applies to civil suits","spite alone proves a malicious-prosecution claim","a pending suit is enough to start a malicious-prosecution claim"]', 'The student assumes that a strong showing on malice cures everything. The breaker is the Gold Key: a strong showing on one element cannot substitute for a missing element on another. The ''automatically'' phrasing is a tiered-absolute tell.', 'Spite-alone-proves-claim dominant trap. Spite is one input to malice, not the whole tort; the ''automatically'' phrasing is a tiered-absolute tell. The Gold Key tells the student that a strong showing on one element cannot substitute for a missing element on another.', 'Check every element independently. A strong showing on one element cannot substitute for a missing element on another.', 'RC-TORTS-MALICIOUS-PROSECUTION-FAVORABLE-TERMINATION-01'),
  ('53f374fe-85e0-55c0-b471-aca321d90c58', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17956_stewards_heavyheart'), 'D', 'Lydia should relabel the claim as a private nuisance action against Paul.', 0, '["bait_doctrine","wrong_frame"]', '["malicious prosecution never applies to civil suits","spite alone proves a malicious-prosecution claim","a pending suit is enough to start a malicious-prosecution claim"]', 'The student confuses the tort frame. The breaker is that nuisance concerns interference with the use and enjoyment of land; a pending contract suit is litigation conduct, not land-use interference. Relabeling also does not cure the favorable-termination defect.', 'Wrong tort frame. Nuisance concerns interference with the use and enjoyment of land; a pending contract suit is litigation conduct, not land-use interference. Relabeling also does not cure the favorable-termination defect.', 'Ask whether the wrong describes litigation conduct or land-use interference. A pending contract suit is litigation conduct, not land-use interference.', 'RC-TORTS-MALICIOUS-PROSECUTION-FAVORABLE-TERMINATION-01'),
  ('655a24a2-7f07-58a7-970e-144447685e4e', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17996_shepherding_call'), 'A', 'Yes, because it is admissible to prove Mary''s credibility as a witness in her own defense.', 0, '["tiered_absolute"]', '["bolstering is always permitted when the witness has taken the stand","opinion is never admissible for truthfulness","a denial of the charge attacks the witness''s character for truthfulness"]', 'The student assumes bolstering is always permitted when the witness has taken the stand. The breaker is the Gold Key: bolstering is foreclosed by 608(a) until the character for truthfulness has been attacked. The ''admissible to prove credibility'' phrasing is a tiered-absolute tell.', 'Always-bolster dominant trap. Bolstering on truthfulness is foreclosed by FRE 608(a) until the character for truthfulness has been attacked. The ''admissible to prove credibility'' phrasing is a tiered-absolute tell.', 'Apply the trigger check. Bolstering is conditional on a prior attack; mere testimony is not an attack. 608(a) is conditional, not categorical.', 'RC-EVIDENCE-FRE-608A-ATTACK-TRIGGER-01'),
  ('16f2b1f0-0e30-58b1-819c-9289206e5434', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17996_shepherding_call'), 'B', 'No, because Hannah is merely stating her opinion regarding Mary''s truthfulness.', 0, '["tiered_absolute"]', '["bolstering is always permitted when the witness has taken the stand","opinion is never admissible for truthfulness","a denial of the charge attacks the witness''s character for truthfulness"]', 'The student reads ''opinion'' as a categorical disqualifier. The breaker is that 608(a) authorizes opinion, subject to the attack trigger. The ''merely'' framing is a tiered-absolute tell.', 'Opinion-never-admissible overclaim. FRE 608(a) specifically authorizes opinion (and reputation) evidence on truthfulness; the ''merely'' framing is a tiered-absolute tell. The gate is ''after attack,'' not ''no opinion.''', '608(a) authorizes opinion, subject to the attack trigger. The discrimination is the attack, not the form.', 'RC-EVIDENCE-FRE-608A-ATTACK-TRIGGER-01'),
  ('3725ec9c-f4f0-521a-a89d-eb057063c41b', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17996_shepherding_call'), 'C', 'No, because Mary''s character for truthfulness has not been attacked.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'FRE 608(a) authorizes opinion (and reputation) evidence on truthfulness only after the witness''s character for truthfulness has been attacked. Mary testified in her own defense and denied the charge, but a mere denial is not an attack on her own character for truthfulness. The trigger has not been met; the bolstering opinion is not admissible.', NULL, 'RC-EVIDENCE-FRE-608A-ATTACK-TRIGGER-01'),
  ('eb17f590-28fa-5817-b774-d2846dcb5928', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '17996_shepherding_call'), 'D', 'Yes, because Mary testified that she did not spray-paint the mural.', 0, '["wrong_element"]', '["bolstering is always permitted when the witness has taken the stand","opinion is never admissible for truthfulness","a denial of the charge attacks the witness''s character for truthfulness"]', 'The student treats a denial of the charge as an attack on truthfulness. The breaker is that a denial of the charge is the substance of the testimony, not an attack on the witness''s character. The trigger requires a separate assault on the witness''s character.', 'Testifying-alone-opens-door missing-trigger cut. Mere testimony is not an attack on truthfulness. The trigger for FRE 608(a) requires a separate assault on the witness''s character (e.g., prior inconsistent statement, bias, conviction, contradiction).', 'A denial of the charge is the substance of the testimony, not an attack on the witness''s character. The trigger requires a separate assault on the witness''s character (prior inconsistent statement, bias, conviction, contradiction).', 'RC-EVIDENCE-FRE-608A-ATTACK-TRIGGER-01'),
  ('4bcfec21-bf3d-5f93-9711-c727f6016e07', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18077_maranatha_meadow'), 'A', 'A vested remainder subject to open.', 0, '["wrong_element"]', '["any future interest after a fee simple is a vested remainder","possibility of reverter applies whenever a fee simple is followed by a condition","an executory interest requires a later deed to be created"]', 'The student remembers ''remainder'' and ''vested'' and reaches for this answer. The breaker is that a remainder must wait for the natural termination of a prior possessory estate (typically a life estate); Barnabas received a fee simple, not a life estate. There is no life estate to support a remainder; the future interest must be executory, not a remainder.', 'Vested-remainder-subject-to-open dominant trap. A remainder must wait for the natural termination of a prior possessory estate (typically a life estate). Barnabas received a fee simple, not a life estate. There is no life estate to support a remainder; the future interest must be executory, not a remainder.', 'Ask what prior possessory estate supports a remainder. Barnabas received a fee simple, not a life estate. The future interest must be executory, not a remainder.', 'RC-REAL-PROPERTY-EXECUTORY-INTEREST-01'),
  ('914770e7-7c7d-57e5-b4fd-3e7cf1f69df9', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18077_maranatha_meadow'), 'B', 'A possibility of reverter.', 0, '["wrong_element"]', '["any future interest after a fee simple is a vested remainder","possibility of reverter applies whenever a fee simple is followed by a condition","an executory interest requires a later deed to be created"]', 'The student associates ''future interest + fee simple + condition'' with grantor-retained interests. The breaker is that a possibility of reverter is the grantor''s interest; the future interest here is in a third party (Timothy), not the grantor (Daniel).', 'Possibility-of-reverter wrong-holder cut. A possibility of reverter is the grantor''s retained interest in a fee simple determinable. The future interest here is in a third party (Timothy), not the grantor (Daniel).', 'Ask who holds the future interest. Timothy is a third party, not the grantor. A future interest in a third party that cuts short a prior grantee is a shifting executory interest.', 'RC-REAL-PROPERTY-EXECUTORY-INTEREST-01'),
  ('bcac2f16-074d-5496-be0f-181679e1f7ae', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18077_maranatha_meadow'), 'C', 'A shifting executory interest.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'The future interest cuts short a prior grantee''s estate (executory) and is in a third party (shifting). Timothy is a third party, not the grantor; the cutting-shifting language in Daniel''s deed creates a contingent shifting executory interest in Timothy. If the condition never occurs, the executory interest is extinguished and Barnabas''s fee simple becomes absolute.', NULL, 'RC-REAL-PROPERTY-EXECUTORY-INTEREST-01'),
  ('4359cea7-442d-5490-8a57-43c90403ffe0', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18077_maranatha_meadow'), 'D', 'No interest unless Barnabas later signs another deed.', 0, '["fabricated_rule"]', '["any future interest after a fee simple is a vested remainder","possibility of reverter applies whenever a fee simple is followed by a condition","an executory interest requires a later deed to be created"]', 'The student assumes a later deed is required. The breaker is that the cutting-shifting language is in Daniel''s original deed; the executory interest is created in the original deed and requires no later conveyance.', 'No-interest-unless-later-deed false-source cut. Timothy''s interest was created in Daniel''s original deed; no subsequent deed from Barnabas is required. The dispositive instrument is the original conveyance.', 'Read the original deed. The cutting-shifting language is in Daniel''s original deed; the executory interest is created in the original deed and requires no later conveyance.', 'RC-REAL-PROPERTY-EXECUTORY-INTEREST-01'),
  ('6493c451-b6f1-524a-8440-e48c8282f495', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18111_camp_stipend_forgery'), 'A', 'Yes, because Daniel falsely made a check with apparent legal efficacy intending to defraud.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'Forgery is complete at the false making with intent to defraud; loss and successful uttering are not elements. A check payable to ''cash'' that purports to draw on a depositor''s account has apparent legal efficacy.', NULL, 'RC-CRIMINAL-FORGERY-FALSE-MAKING-01'),
  ('0f7d767a-a4fe-5863-b098-9a07ed069d88', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18111_camp_stipend_forgery'), 'B', 'No, because Stephen did not actually lose money yet.', 0, '["autonomy_appeal"]', '["forgery requires the victim to actually lose money","forgery requires the forger to successfully utter the document","checks are not writings with legal significance for forgery purposes"]', 'The student reaches for ''no harm, no foul.'' The lay intuition is that the victim must actually lose money for a forgery to occur. The breaker is the Gold Key: actual loss is not an element of forgery; the false making with intent is the actus reus.', 'The victim''s actual loss is not an element of forgery. The actus reus is the false making with intent to defraud. Model Penal Code § 241.1; common-law ''apparent legal efficacy'' test.', 'Apply the Gold Key: the actus reus of forgery is the false making with intent to defraud; the victim''s actual loss is not an element. Model Penal Code § 241.1; common-law ''apparent legal efficacy'' test.', 'RC-CRIMINAL-FORGERY-FALSE-MAKING-01'),
  ('febecde1-a72a-5caf-afda-6156cf744270', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18111_camp_stipend_forgery'), 'C', 'Yes, but only if Daniel successfully cashes the check.', 0, '["wrong_element"]', '["forgery requires the victim to actually lose money","forgery requires the forger to successfully utter the document","checks are not writings with legal significance for forgery purposes"]', 'The student reads ''plans to cash'' as a half-step and concludes the forgery is not yet complete. The breaker is the Silver Key: forgery is complete at the false making; successful uttering is a separate crime.', 'Successful uttering is not a required element of forgery. Forgery is complete at the false making; uttering a forged instrument is a separate crime.', 'Apply the Silver Key: forgery is complete at the false making. Uttering a forged instrument is a separate crime, but it is not a required element of forgery itself.', 'RC-CRIMINAL-FORGERY-FALSE-MAKING-01'),
  ('a4d06f58-eb54-563a-96e1-d07ecac51f40', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18111_camp_stipend_forgery'), 'D', 'No, because checks are not writings with legal significance.', 0, '["tiered_absolute"]', '["forgery requires the victim to actually lose money","forgery requires the forger to successfully utter the document","checks are not writings with legal significance for forgery purposes"]', 'The student is uncertain what counts as a ''writing'' in the forgery sense. The breaker is the Gold Key: a check is the textbook example of a writing with apparent legal efficacy because it purports to draw funds on a depositor''s account.', 'A check is a writing with apparent legal efficacy because it purports to draw funds on a depositor''s account. The ''checks are not writings with legal significance'' framing is a flat absolute and false.', 'Apply the Gold Key: a check payable to ''cash'' that purports to draw on a depositor''s account has apparent legal efficacy because, if presented and honored, it would operate to transfer funds.', 'RC-CRIMINAL-FORGERY-FALSE-MAKING-01'),
  ('3b794f1f-7cc0-5cb2-b0e8-07988d68b473', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18135_mountain_lodge_looseboard'), 'A', 'Strict liability for any injury on the premises.', 0, '["extreme_of_range"]', '["all landowners only have to warn (licensee rule applied to invitees)","strict liability for any injury on the premises","no duty if the possessor did not create the hazard"]', 'The student reaches for the strictest possible standard because a paying visitor was hurt. The breaker is that invitee status creates a duty of reasonable care, not automatic liability; strict liability is a separate doctrine (abnormally dangerous activities, products liability) and is not the default rule for ordinary premises.', 'Strict-liability overclaim. Invitees are owed a duty of reasonable care, not strict liability. Strict liability is a separate doctrine (abnormally dangerous activities, products liability) and is not the default rule for ordinary premises.', 'Classify the entrant. Mary paid admission to enter the lodge for a purpose that benefits the retreat center; that is the textbook definition of an invitee. Invitees are owed reasonable care, not strict liability. Strict liability is a separate doctrine (abnormally dangerous activities, products liability) and is not the default rule for ordinary premises.', 'RC-TORTS-INVITEE-DUTY-01'),
  ('b8639da9-b497-5e6d-b4b5-3212b59953e7', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18135_mountain_lodge_looseboard'), 'B', 'Reasonable care, including reasonable inspection for hazards it should discover.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'Mary is an invitee: she paid admission to enter the lodge for a purpose that benefits the retreat center. The duty owed to invitees is reasonable care, including reasonable inspection for discoverable hazards. The narrower ''warn only of known'' rule is the licensee standard, not the invitee standard. The hazard had been present for several days, no inspection schedule existed, and reasonable care would have discovered the condition.', NULL, 'RC-TORTS-INVITEE-DUTY-01'),
  ('c11dc898-a30f-593d-81e3-76929ee4058b', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18135_mountain_lodge_looseboard'), 'C', 'Only a duty to warn about hazards it actually knew about.', 0, '["bait_doctrine"]', '["all landowners only have to warn (licensee rule applied to invitees)","strict liability for any injury on the premises","no duty if the possessor did not create the hazard"]', 'The student half-remembers that ''landowners only have to warn'' and picks the narrower licensee rule. The breaker is the Gold Key: the warn-only-of-known rule is the licensee standard, not the invitee standard; Mary is a paying attendee (invitee), and the broader inspection duty applies.', 'Dominant invitee-vs-licensee conflation. The ''warn only of known hazards'' rule is the licensee standard, not the invitee standard. Mary is a paying attendee (invitee), so the broader inspection duty applies.', 'Classify the entrant. Mary paid admission to enter the lodge for a purpose that benefits the retreat center; that is the textbook definition of an invitee. The ''warn only of known'' rule is the licensee standard; the broader inspection duty is the invitee standard.', 'RC-TORTS-INVITEE-DUTY-01'),
  ('67026147-b5ab-5f8d-8e75-5dc009be2d4d', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18135_mountain_lodge_looseboard'), 'D', 'No duty because the retreat center did not create the loose board.', 0, '["misfit"]', '["all landowners only have to warn (licensee rule applied to invitees)","strict liability for any injury on the premises","no duty if the possessor did not create the hazard"]', 'The student assumes the duty runs only to hazards the possessor itself caused. The breaker is that the invitee duty is keyed to discoverable hazards regardless of who caused them; natural deterioration of a porch floorboard over several days, with no inspection schedule, is exactly the kind of hazard the invitee duty obliges the possessor to find and fix.', 'No-creation-no-duty misfit. The stem supplies a paying invitee and a discoverable hazard; the invitee duty is keyed to discoverable hazards regardless of who caused them.', 'Ask whether the hazard was discoverable. A porch floorboard that had been rocking for several days, with no inspection schedule, is a discoverable hazard. The invitee duty obliges the possessor to find and fix it regardless of who caused it.', 'RC-TORTS-INVITEE-DUTY-01'),
  ('655396e2-3ce4-56ad-ba38-4ef60d313ba6', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18179_campfire_rim_warning'), 'A', 'Yes, because the user suffered physical injury.', 0, '["wrong_element"]', '["every dangerous product needs a warning","injury alone creates a warning defect","outdoor firepits are not products"]', 'The student sees ''physical injury'' and assumes the warning defect is established. The breaker is the missing-element test: injury is the consequence, not the duty trigger. The call is the warning-defect question, not the burn claim.', 'Injury-claim misframing. The choice supplies the consequence (physical injury) but skips the antecedent duty question. The call is the warning-defect issue, not the burn claim.', 'Ask whether injury alone creates a warning defect. It does not; the duty must be grounded in a non-obvious product risk.', 'RC-TORTS-OBVIOUS-DANGER-01'),
  ('fbf688ee-f40f-5129-8911-7feac0ce665a', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18179_campfire_rim_warning'), 'B', 'No, because fire rings are never products for products-liability purposes.', 0, '["flat_misstatement"]', '["every dangerous product needs a warning","injury alone creates a warning defect","outdoor firepits are not products"]', 'The student reaches for a categorical exclusion to dismiss the claim. The breaker is the misstated-rule test: a steel campfire ring sold by a manufacturer is a product for § 402A and Restatement (Third) purposes; the ''never a product'' claim is a categorical exclusion the doctrine does not contain.', 'Categorical exclusion the doctrine does not contain. A steel campfire ring sold by a manufacturer is a product for § 402A and Restatement (Third) purposes; the ''never a product'' claim is a misstatement.', 'Ask whether a campfire ring sold by a manufacturer is a product. It is; the exclusion is a misstatement.', 'RC-TORTS-OBVIOUS-DANGER-01'),
  ('a6d8b5de-8da8-56d8-b80d-f4c1d998db57', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18179_campfire_rim_warning'), 'C', 'Yes, because every dangerous product needs a warning for every possible injury.', 0, '["tiered_absolute"]', '["every dangerous product needs a warning","injury alone creates a warning defect","outdoor firepits are not products"]', 'The student hears ''dangerous product'' and reaches for the all-purpose ''always warn'' intuition. The breaker is the Gold Key: the open-and-obvious non-duty rule carves out the obvious risks; the rim getting hot during a campfire burn is exactly such a risk.', 'Tiered-absolute dominant trap. The ''every dangerous product needs a warning'' claim asserts an always/only rule that has a doctrinal exception. The open-and-obvious non-duty rule carves out the obvious risks; the rim getting hot during a campfire burn is exactly such a risk.', 'Apply the Gold Key: the open-and-obvious non-duty rule carves out the obvious risks. The rim getting hot during a campfire burn is exactly such a risk.', 'RC-TORTS-OBVIOUS-DANGER-01'),
  ('4cdd542b-529d-5e34-b862-6c622efe7c9d', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18179_campfire_rim_warning'), 'D', 'No, because the burning risk of an ordinary steel fire ring rim is obvious.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'The rim getting hot during use is open, obvious, and commonly understood. The open-and-obvious non-duty rule eliminates the duty to warn for risks a reasonable ordinary user can plainly perceive. Stephen''s claim is weak.', NULL, 'RC-TORTS-OBVIOUS-DANGER-01'),
  ('2273d0b5-815d-5d04-b7ea-9a303fbbef62', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18182_bookstore_mandamus'), 'A', 'Yes, because litigation expense alone creates mandamus jurisdiction.', 0, '["wrong_element"]', '["mandamus is available whenever a party thinks an interlocutory ruling is wrong","litigation expense alone opens the writ","federal courts abolished mandamus in civil cases"]', 'The student sees ''litigation expense'' and assumes that cost alone opens the writ. The breaker is the missing-element test: the mandamus standard is two-prong; expense alone does not clear either prong.', 'Expense-alone misframing. The choice supplies the consequence (litigation expense) but skips the antecedent clear-and-indisputable-right question. The mandamus standard is two-prong; expense alone does not clear either prong.', 'Ask whether expense alone clears the standard. It does not; the writ is reserved for extraordinary causes.', 'RC-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-MANDAMUS-STANDARD-01'),
  ('55e832b5-c4ca-563b-a414-148ab847795e', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18182_bookstore_mandamus'), 'B', 'No, because federal courts abolished mandamus in civil cases.', 0, '["flat_misstatement"]', '["mandamus is available whenever a party thinks an interlocutory ruling is wrong","litigation expense alone opens the writ","federal courts abolished mandamus in civil cases"]', 'The student reaches for a categorical abolition to dismiss the question. The breaker is the misstated-rule test: the All Writs Act still authorizes the writ in civil cases; the ''abolished'' claim is a misstatement.', 'Categorical abolition the doctrine does not contain. The All Writs Act, 28 U.S.C. § 1651(a), still authorizes the writ in civil cases; the ''abolished'' claim is a misstatement.', 'Ask whether the All Writs Act still authorizes the writ in civil cases. It does (but only in extraordinary circumstances).', 'RC-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-MANDAMUS-STANDARD-01'),
  ('a75d0a8e-8aac-5f11-80d0-d968dc681ec1', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18182_bookstore_mandamus'), 'C', 'Yes, because mandamus is available whenever a party thinks an interlocutory ruling is wrong.', 0, '["tiered_absolute"]', '["mandamus is available whenever a party thinks an interlocutory ruling is wrong","litigation expense alone opens the writ","federal courts abolished mandamus in civil cases"]', 'The student hears ''party thinks an interlocutory ruling is wrong'' and reaches for the all-purpose ''mandamus is available whenever'' intuition. The breaker is the Gold Key: the clear-and-indisputable-right + no-adequate-alternative-means standard narrows the writ to extraordinary causes.', 'Tiered-absolute dominant trap. The ''mandamus is available whenever a party thinks an interlocutory ruling is wrong'' claim asserts an always/only rule that has a doctrinal exception. The clear-and-indisputable-right + no-adequate-alternative-means standard narrows the writ to extraordinary causes.', 'Apply the Gold Key: the clear-and-indisputable-right + no-adequate-alternative-means standard narrows the writ to extraordinary causes; ordinary legal error does not clear the bar.', 'RC-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-MANDAMUS-STANDARD-01'),
  ('6b275929-3889-55de-8289-ddc7ad41b6f9', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18182_bookstore_mandamus'), 'D', 'No, because mandamus requires a clear and indisputable right and no adequate alternative remedy.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'A denial of a Rule 12(b)(6) motion is an ordinary interlocutory ruling. The defendant can challenge the legal error in an appeal after final judgment; the no-adequate-alternative prong is not satisfied. The writ is not available.', NULL, 'RC-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-MANDAMUS-STANDARD-01'),
  ('ee032168-f90a-56bd-a25c-4472cd324a49', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18196_riverstone_citizenship'), 'A', 'No, because Mary and RiverStone are both Tennessee citizens through RiverStone''s principal place of business.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'A corporation is a citizen of both its state of incorporation and the state of its principal place of business. The plaintiff and the corporation share Tennessee citizenship; complete diversity is destroyed.', NULL, 'RC-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-CORPORATE-CITIZENSHIP-01'),
  ('e677e224-b27c-5564-b603-de308f4cd022', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18196_riverstone_citizenship'), 'B', 'Yes, because RiverStone is incorporated in Nevada.', 0, '["half_truth"]', '["incorporation is the only citizenship","corporations have only one citizenship","state-law claims are not proper in diversity"]', 'The student sees ''incorporated in Nevada'' and concludes the corporation is a Nevada citizen. The breaker is the Gold Key: corporate citizenship is dual; the principal place of business is also a citizenship.', 'Half-truth dominant trap. The ''incorporation is the citizenship'' claim correctly notes the incorporation state but skips the principal place of business. The Gold Key handles the dual-citizenship carve-out.', 'Ask whether corporate citizenship has a second criterion. Yes — the principal place of business is also a citizenship; the corporation is a Nevada citizen AND a Tennessee citizen.', 'RC-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-CORPORATE-CITIZENSHIP-01'),
  ('f12a61a5-9935-5ca2-aa94-972d7959b3e4', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18196_riverstone_citizenship'), 'C', 'No, but only because the claim is based on state law.', 0, '["misfit","wrong_frame"]', '["incorporation is the only citizenship","corporations have only one citizenship","state-law claims are not proper in diversity"]', 'The student treats the state-law nature of the claim as a no-diversity basis. The breaker is that a state-law claim is proper in diversity when § 1332 is otherwise satisfied; the only defect is the destroyed diversity.', 'State-law-claim framing. A state-law claim is proper in diversity when § 1332 is otherwise satisfied. The state-law nature of the claim is not a defect.', 'Ask whether state-law claims are proper in diversity. They are, when § 1332 is otherwise satisfied.', 'RC-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-CORPORATE-CITIZENSHIP-01'),
  ('5966d6b2-8684-555f-bf69-37b6a5bad2b8', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18196_riverstone_citizenship'), 'D', 'Yes, because a corporation can have only one citizenship.', 0, '["flat_misstatement"]', '["incorporation is the only citizenship","corporations have only one citizenship","state-law claims are not proper in diversity"]', 'The student reaches for a categorical exclusion to dismiss the no-diversity outcome. The breaker is the misstated-rule test: a corporation has DUAL citizenship under § 1332(c)(1); the ''only one citizenship'' claim is a categorical exclusion the doctrine does not contain.', 'Categorical exclusion the doctrine does not contain. 28 U.S.C. § 1332(c)(1) gives corporations DUAL citizenship: state of incorporation AND state of principal place of business. The ''only one citizenship'' claim is a misstatement.', 'Ask whether § 1332(c)(1) supplies a single-criterion rule. It does not; the dual-citizenship rule is the statute.', 'RC-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-CORPORATE-CITIZENSHIP-01'),
  ('23c1d6c1-2c4a-59f4-80ef-3cc3c76c2fc3', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18197_athletics_bus_collision'), 'A', '50 percent.', 0, '["bait_doctrine"]', '["multiple defendants means a proportional split for the plaintiff","plaintiff cannot collect from one joint tortfeasor","comparative negligence caps the plaintiff''s recovery at a percentage"]', 'The student confuses the plaintiff''s collection right (joint and several = full recovery from any one) with the defendants'' inter-se contribution problem. The breaker is the Gold Key: joint and several lets the plaintiff collect 100% from any one defendant; the proportional split is the defendants'' inter-se problem, not the plaintiff''s.', 'Dominant proportional-liability / contribution confusion. Joint and several liability lets the plaintiff collect 100% from any one defendant. The proportional split between defendants is the inter-se contribution problem, not the plaintiff''s collection problem.', 'Distinguish the two. Joint and several lets the plaintiff collect 100% from any one defendant; the proportional split is the defendants'' inter-se problem, not the plaintiff''s. Restatement (Third) of Torts: Apportionment of Liability §§ 1, 4; Uniform Contribution Among Tortfeasors Act (UCATA).', 'RC-TORTS-JOINT-AND-SEVERAL-01'),
  ('f5e55f0f-6478-5e88-98c2-ef40e83bba5d', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18197_athletics_bus_collision'), 'B', '100 percent.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'The court held the academy, Lydia, and Paul jointly and severally liable for Hannah''s damages, and the jury found Hannah free of fault. Under joint and several liability, the plaintiff may enforce the entire judgment against any one defendant. The proportional split between defendants is the inter-se contribution problem, not the plaintiff''s collection problem.', NULL, 'RC-TORTS-JOINT-AND-SEVERAL-01'),
  ('f1fcc465-8f66-51e4-8e26-b37d779a4665', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18197_athletics_bus_collision'), 'C', '0 percent.', 0, '["misfit"]', '["multiple defendants means a proportional split for the plaintiff","plaintiff cannot collect from one joint tortfeasor","comparative negligence caps the plaintiff''s recovery at a percentage"]', 'The student assumes the plaintiff must pursue the defendants together, or that the plaintiff can be blocked from collecting from any single defendant. The breaker is the very definition of joint and several liability: the plaintiff may enforce the full judgment against any one defendant.', 'Misfit cut. The very definition of joint and several liability is that the plaintiff may enforce the full judgment against any one defendant. The stem explicitly says the court held the defendants jointly and severally liable.', 'Read the very definition of joint and several liability: the plaintiff may enforce the full judgment against any one defendant. The stem explicitly says the court held the defendants jointly and severally liable.', 'RC-TORTS-JOINT-AND-SEVERAL-01'),
  ('616fb9ff-85fb-5590-a7e3-12393b0ffa60', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18197_athletics_bus_collision'), 'D', '40 percent.', 0, '["fabricated_rule"]', '["multiple defendants means a proportional split for the plaintiff","plaintiff cannot collect from one joint tortfeasor","comparative negligence caps the plaintiff''s recovery at a percentage"]', 'The student latches onto a number that does not correspond to any doctrinal principle and treats it as if it were a recognized share. The breaker is that 40% is not a recognized share in any contribution or apportionment doctrine, and the stem supplies no factual basis for 40%.', 'Non-doctrinal specific percentage. 40% is not a recognized share in any contribution or apportionment doctrine, and the stem supplies no factual basis for 40%.', 'Ask whether the stem supplies a factual basis for 40%. The stem supplies no factual basis. 40% is not a recognized share in any contribution or apportionment doctrine.', 'RC-TORTS-JOINT-AND-SEVERAL-01'),
  ('47cf9852-3101-5c5d-9667-6feea67588c4', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18240_family_table_refund'), 'A', 'No, because it is not legally certain on these facts that the plaintiff cannot recover more than $75,000.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'Naomi pleaded $90,000 in good faith; no statute or contract caps her recovery below $75,000. The legal-certainty test is not met; the court should not dismiss.', NULL, 'RC-CIVPRO-AMOUNT-CERTAINTY-01'),
  ('8f972b64-6b27-59a0-8f2b-26dd4003bcf3', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18240_family_table_refund'), 'B', 'No, but only if the claim arises under federal law.', 0, '["misfit"]', '["discovery will probably show lower damages defeats amount in controversy","a damages dispute itself defeats subject-matter jurisdiction","federal-question jurisdiction is required for a federal-court suit"]', 'The student imports federal-question jurisdiction into a pure amount-in-controversy problem. The breaker is the misfit test: federal-question jurisdiction is not in play when the suit is grounded in diversity of citizenship and the call is the amount question.', 'Wrong-doctrine. Federal-question jurisdiction is not in play when the suit is grounded in diversity of citizenship and the call is the amount question. The analysis is whether the pleaded amount clears the $75,000 threshold by legal certainty, not whether a federal claim is pleaded.', 'Ask whether the call is the amount question or the federal-question question. It is the amount question; the analysis is whether the pleaded amount clears the threshold by legal certainty, not whether a federal claim is pleaded.', 'RC-CIVPRO-AMOUNT-CERTAINTY-01'),
  ('d6f445e7-d242-5f7c-9e3a-860bd4723ad4', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18240_family_table_refund'), 'C', 'Yes, because likely recovery below $75,000 defeats jurisdiction.', 0, '["fabricated_rule"]', '["discovery will probably show lower damages defeats amount in controversy","a damages dispute itself defeats subject-matter jurisdiction","federal-question jurisdiction is required for a federal-court suit"]', 'The student confuses the legal-certainty test with a probability-of-recovery test. The breaker is the Gold Key: under St. Paul Mercury, a defendant''s prediction that discovery will reduce actual recovery is a factual probability, not a legal bar; the legal-certainty test requires a statute or contract cap, not a discovery prediction.', 'Probability-vs-certainty dominant trap. The student confuses the legal-certainty test with a probability-of-recovery test. Under St. Paul Mercury, a defendant''s prediction that discovery will reduce actual recovery is a factual probability, not a legal bar. No statute or contract caps recovery below the threshold; the legal ceiling is at least $90,000.', 'Ask whether any statute or contract makes it legally impossible to recover more than the threshold. If no, the good-faith pleading controls and the court will not dismiss.', 'RC-CIVPRO-AMOUNT-CERTAINTY-01'),
  ('0bf2a264-8a54-5e92-940f-7ade903bd699', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18240_family_table_refund'), 'D', 'Yes, because the defendant disputes damages.', 0, '["misfit"]', '["discovery will probably show lower damages defeats amount in controversy","a damages dispute itself defeats subject-matter jurisdiction","federal-question jurisdiction is required for a federal-court suit"]', 'The student treats the defendant''s factual contention that damages are lower than pleaded as itself defeating the amount in controversy. The breaker is the Silver Key: a damages dispute is a fact contest that goes to the merits, not a jurisdictional defect.', 'Damages-dispute misfit. A defendant''s contention that the plaintiff''s damages are lower than pleaded does not itself defeat the amount in controversy. Contested damages go to the merits, not to subject-matter jurisdiction.', 'Ask whether the dispute is a fact contest or a legal cap. A fact contest goes to the merits, not to subject-matter jurisdiction.', 'RC-CIVPRO-AMOUNT-CERTAINTY-01'),
  ('930cd6ae-16d1-5c05-9a79-e1e30beb8826', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18287_manger_cradle_collapse'), 'A', 'Res ipsa is likely unavailable because the accident may have resulted from Mary''s own alterations after rental.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'Mary''s own post-rental drilling of pilot holes in the base plate is a plausible competing cause wholly attributable to the plaintiff. The third prong of the res ipsa test (absence of voluntary action or contribution by the plaintiff) is not satisfied.', NULL, 'RC-TORTS-RES-IPSA-ALTERATION-01'),
  ('336994ab-ba34-5011-97d8-7d9b8923d3bf', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18287_manger_cradle_collapse'), 'B', 'Res ipsa makes Daniel strictly liable for all damage to rented hand-carved items.', 0, '["misfit","wrong_frame"]', '["res ipsa makes the defendant a strict insurer","res ipsa requires a statutory inspection regime","an undetermined cause always supports res ipsa"]', 'The student hears ''res ipsa'' and reaches for strict liability, treating the doctrine as a strict-liability backstop. The breaker is the call focus: the call asks for a res ipsa analysis, not a strict-liability question.', 'The choice answers a different liability question (strict liability for damage) rather than a res ipsa inference question. Res ipsa is an evidentiary inference of negligence, not a strict-liability backstop.', 'Ask whether the call asks for a strict-liability answer or a res ipsa analysis. Res ipsa is an evidentiary inference, not a strict-liability theory.', 'RC-TORTS-RES-IPSA-ALTERATION-01'),
  ('254c87a4-f294-549c-9548-d2ace17ee0d8', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18287_manger_cradle_collapse'), 'C', 'Res ipsa clearly applies because hand-carved manger cradles never collapse unless the maker was negligent.', 0, '["tiered_absolute"]', '["res ipsa makes the defendant a strict insurer","res ipsa requires a statutory inspection regime","an undetermined cause always supports res ipsa"]', 'The student sees an undetermined cause and reaches for ''res ipsa clearly applies,'' overclaiming that hand-carved cradles never collapse unless the maker was negligent. The breaker is the Gold Key: the plaintiff''s own post-rental drilling of pilot holes is a plausible alternative cause wholly attributable to the plaintiff, and the third prong of the res ipsa test is not satisfied.', 'The ''never unless the maker was negligent'' wording is an absolute-overclaim. The collapse can readily be explained by Mary''s own post-rental drilling of pilot holes in the base plate — a competing cause wholly attributable to the plaintiff. The third prong of the res ipsa test is not satisfied.', 'Ask whether the plaintiff''s own post-receipt conduct is a plausible alternative cause. If yes, the third prong of the res ipsa test is not satisfied, and the inference collapses.', 'RC-TORTS-RES-IPSA-ALTERATION-01'),
  ('2eecb6c7-e2b3-5d3e-8025-dc837d3a265b', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18287_manger_cradle_collapse'), 'D', 'Res ipsa applies only if a state statute required Daniel to inspect every cradle before each rental.', 0, '["fabricated_rule","wrong_frame"]', '["res ipsa makes the defendant a strict insurer","res ipsa requires a statutory inspection regime","an undetermined cause always supports res ipsa"]', 'The student hears ''applies'' and reaches for a statutory-violation frame, imagining that res ipsa requires a state inspection statute. The breaker is the call focus: res ipsa is a common-law evidentiary inference, not a statutory-violation theory, and the ''only if'' wording is a false premise.', 'Res ipsa is a common-law evidentiary inference, not a statutory-violation theory. The ''only if'' wording is a false premise; the presence or absence of a state inspection statute is irrelevant to whether res ipsa can be inferred on these facts.', 'Ask whether the call asks for a res ipsa analysis. Res ipsa does not require a statutory inspection regime; the ''only if'' wording is a false premise.', 'RC-TORTS-RES-IPSA-ALTERATION-01'),
  ('233e8a8d-aff4-5eca-af29-210c9fb3810c', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18298_fellowship_hall_grab'), 'A', 'the stranger, because Daniel had no reason to expect to be touched by the stranger.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'Assault requires the plaintiff to apprehend imminent harmful or offensive contact with herself. The stranger never made any threatening gesture toward Daniel, never approached him, and never looked at him; Daniel could not have apprehended contact with himself. The transferred-intent doctrine does not supply a missing plaintiff-side apprehension for an assault claim.', NULL, 'RC-TORTS-ASSAULT-THIRD-PARTY-01'),
  ('bda85ec9-74be-5441-b6e6-f8753778f4b6', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18298_fellowship_hall_grab'), 'B', 'the stranger, because Daniel did not perceive an injury being inflicted on Mary.', 0, '["bait_doctrine","wrong_frame"]', '["transferred intent covers assault","awareness of fright satisfies the assault elements","witnessing injury to a third party makes the bystander an assault plaintiff"]', 'The student picks the right party for the wrong reason. The ''perception of injury'' framing sounds plausible because Daniel did witness the injury to Mary, but the assault test is about plaintiff-side apprehension of contact with self, not plaintiff-side perception of injury to a third party. The breaker is the call focus.', 'The choice picks the right party for the wrong reason. The assault test is whether the plaintiff apprehended contact with HERSELF, not whether she perceived injury to a third party.', 'Ask whether the call asks about perception of injury to a third party or about apprehension of contact with self. The assault test is the latter.', 'RC-TORTS-ASSAULT-THIRD-PARTY-01'),
  ('8683660d-18f5-5771-89cc-6ef1fc7ef901', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18298_fellowship_hall_grab'), 'C', 'Daniel, because the court will transfer the stranger''s intent to grab Mary to Daniel''s assault claim.', 0, '["bait_doctrine"]', '["transferred intent covers assault","awareness of fright satisfies the assault elements","witnessing injury to a third party makes the bystander an assault plaintiff"]', 'The student reaches for the transferred-intent doctrine because the stranger did intend to harm a person (Mary), and the bystander (Daniel) was nearby. The trap is the doctrinal misapplication: transferred intent applies to battery, not to assault. The breaker is the Gold Key.', 'The transferred-intent doctrine is a real doctrine that applies to battery and certain other intentional torts against the person, but it does not apply to assault. The assault plaintiff must be the person put in apprehension of contact with herself. The stranger''s intent to grab Mary cannot be transferred to make Daniel an assault plaintiff.', 'Ask whether the transferred-intent doctrine applies to assault. It applies to battery, not to assault; the assault plaintiff must be the person put in apprehension of contact with herself.', 'RC-TORTS-ASSAULT-THIRD-PARTY-01'),
  ('ea64ed7c-e980-53e9-9cfc-6dda1fdecd22', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18298_fellowship_hall_grab'), 'D', 'Daniel, because the stranger was aware that his conduct would frighten Daniel.', 0, '["fabricated_rule","wrong_frame"]', '["transferred intent covers assault","awareness of fright satisfies the assault elements","witnessing injury to a third party makes the bystander an assault plaintiff"]', 'The student hears ''aware that his conduct would frighten'' and assumes that satisfies the assault elements. It does not — the elements are about plaintiff-side apprehension of contact with self, not defendant-side awareness of fright. The breaker is the call focus.', 'The ''aware that his conduct would frighten'' framing misstates the assault elements. The elements are about plaintiff-side apprehension of contact with the plaintiff, not defendant-side awareness that the conduct would frighten the plaintiff.', 'Ask whether the elements are about plaintiff-side apprehension or defendant-side awareness. Plaintiff-side apprehension is the test.', 'RC-TORTS-ASSAULT-THIRD-PARTY-01'),
  ('68ae136a-fead-5041-bab6-af866ab4994a', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18438_hannah_david_medicaid'), 'A', 'Rational basis applies because public benefits are always discretionary and the state may allocate them as it wishes.', 0, '["bait_doctrine","wrong_frame"]', '["public benefits are always discretionary","right to travel means international travel","states may always favor long-term residents under general police power"]', 'The student reaches for the default rational-basis framing for benefit-classification cases. The ''public benefits are always discretionary'' framing sounds plausible because welfare/Medicaid is a public benefit. The breaker is the Gold Key: public-benefit rules can still burden fundamental rights; the right to travel is a fundamental right.', 'The ''public benefits are always discretionary'' framing is the dominant trap. Public-benefit rules can still burden fundamental rights; the right to interstate travel is a fundamental right, and durational-residency rules that burden it trigger strict scrutiny.', 'Ask whether the classification burdens a fundamental right. The right to interstate travel is a fundamental right, and durational-residency rules that burden it trigger strict scrutiny.', 'RC-CONSTITUTIONAL-LAW-RIGHT-TO-TRAVEL-01'),
  ('09a910b3-fb26-5693-96e1-aeb1772932e3', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18438_hannah_david_medicaid'), 'B', 'The rule is valid because states may always favor long-term residents over newcomers.', 0, '["autonomy_appeal"]', '["public benefits are always discretionary","right to travel means international travel","states may always favor long-term residents under general police power"]', 'The student hears ''states may always favor long-term residents'' and assumes the state''s general police power controls. It does not — the constitutional right to travel limits the state''s general police power when the classification operates as a penalty on the right to move. The breaker is the constitutional-limit cut.', 'The ''states may always favor long-term residents'' framing is a general-police-power overclaim. The right to be treated equally to other residents once the traveler has established residence is a component of the constitutional right to travel, and a durational-residency classification is a textbook violation of that component.', 'Ask whether the constitutional right to travel limits the state''s general police power. It does, when the classification operates as a penalty on the right to move.', 'RC-CONSTITUTIONAL-LAW-RIGHT-TO-TRAVEL-01'),
  ('ffaa0399-86e7-566b-b38b-d472d7bfb333', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18438_hannah_david_medicaid'), 'C', 'Strict scrutiny applies only if the new resident crossed an international border.', 0, '["flat_misstatement"]', '["public benefits are always discretionary","right to travel means international travel","states may always favor long-term residents under general police power"]', 'The student reads ''right to travel'' and assumes it means international travel. It does not — the constitutional right at issue is INTERSTATE travel, and Hannah and David moved from State B to State A. The breaker is the constitutional-right cut.', 'The ''international border'' framing is wrong. The constitutional right at issue is INTERSTATE travel, not international travel; Hannah and David moved from State B to State A — an interstate move.', 'Identify the move — interstate (State B to State A) or international (foreign country). The right at issue is INTERSTATE travel.', 'RC-CONSTITUTIONAL-LAW-RIGHT-TO-TRAVEL-01'),
  ('c4c84f02-9cbc-50f6-97aa-28434bdcfb55', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18438_hannah_david_medicaid'), 'D', 'The rule may penalize the fundamental right to interstate travel and trigger strict scrutiny.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'The right to be treated equally to other residents once the traveler has established residence in the new state is a component of the constitutional right to travel. A durational-residency classification that disadvantages new residents because they recently moved operates as a penalty on the right to move and is therefore subject to strict scrutiny.', NULL, 'RC-CONSTITUTIONAL-LAW-RIGHT-TO-TRAVEL-01'),
  ('48364626-3944-5e54-b228-2d2f2b34fb8d', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18457_lydia_book_club'), 'A', 'Strict scrutiny applies because the book club uses race or ethnicity in its membership criteria.', 0, '["bait_doctrine","wrong_frame"]', '["strict scrutiny applies whenever race is involved (skips the state-action threshold)","the Constitution bans all private discrimination (overclaim)","private clubs receive deference under rational basis (wrong level of analysis)"]', 'The student skips the state-action threshold and goes straight to the scrutiny tier. Race is a suspect classification, and the student reasons that strict scrutiny applies — but the scrutiny tier is reached only after state action is established. The breaker is the Gold Key: the threshold problem is state action.', 'The student skips the state-action threshold and goes straight to the scrutiny tier. The scrutiny tier is reached only after state action is established; the book club is a private social group with no government funding and no public-function role.', 'Ask whether state action is present. The book club is a private social group with no government funding and no public-function role; the constitutional claim fails at the state-action threshold before any scrutiny tier is reached.', 'RC-CONSTITUTIONAL-LAW-STATE-ACTION-01'),
  ('c6ea6bc3-ed59-5023-9fd1-60e64430c64a', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18457_lydia_book_club'), 'B', 'The book club loses because the Constitution bans all private discrimination.', 0, '["tiered_absolute"]', '["strict scrutiny applies whenever race is involved (skips the state-action threshold)","the Constitution bans all private discrimination (overclaim)","private clubs receive deference under rational basis (wrong level of analysis)"]', 'The student overclaims that the Constitution bans all private discrimination. The overclaim is wrong; the Equal Protection Clause requires state action. The breaker is the threshold cut: the EP claim itself requires state action.', 'The ''Constitution bans all private discrimination'' overclaim is wrong. The Equal Protection Clause requires state action; other constitutional provisions and federal civil-rights statutes may reach some private discrimination, but the EP claim itself fails at state action.', 'Ask whether the Equal Protection Clause requires state action. It does; the overclaim is wrong; the constitutional claim itself fails at the state-action threshold.', 'RC-CONSTITUTIONAL-LAW-STATE-ACTION-01'),
  ('38ebc9bc-5a85-5709-954b-ee6d14b0f9c4', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18457_lydia_book_club'), 'C', 'The Equal Protection Clause does not reach purely private discrimination without state action.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'The book club is a private social group with no government funding and no public-function role. The Equal Protection claim fails at the state-action threshold before any scrutiny tier is reached. Other federal civil-rights statutes may reach the conduct, but the Equal Protection claim itself requires state action.', NULL, 'RC-CONSTITUTIONAL-LAW-STATE-ACTION-01'),
  ('b45e8025-9c45-570c-8d11-cf0592152445', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18457_lydia_book_club'), 'D', 'Rational basis applies because private clubs receive deference in membership decisions.', 0, '["bait_doctrine","wrong_frame"]', '["strict scrutiny applies whenever race is involved (skips the state-action threshold)","the Constitution bans all private discrimination (overclaim)","private clubs receive deference under rational basis (wrong level of analysis)"]', 'The student reaches for the ''private clubs receive deference'' framing. The constitutional claim fails at state action, not at the scrutiny tier. The breaker is the threshold cut: the constitutional claim fails at the state-action threshold, not at the scrutiny tier.', 'The ''rational-basis-deference'' framing is the wrong level of analysis. The constitutional claim fails at the state-action threshold, not at the scrutiny tier.', 'Ask whether the constitutional claim fails at state action or at the scrutiny tier. It fails at state action; the ''rational basis'' framing is the wrong level of analysis.', 'RC-CONSTITUTIONAL-LAW-STATE-ACTION-01'),
  ('39d11cc7-5913-5286-8d04-3b01b6a8dc79', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18477_church_craft_fair_quilt'), 'A', 'Yes, because the quilt was stolen before Naomi bought it.', 0, '["fabricated_rule"]', '["the fact that property was stolen makes anyone who received it guilty","later knowledge relates back to the purchase and supplies the missing mens rea","receiving stolen property applies only to cash"]', 'The student reasons that because the quilt was stolen, Naomi must be guilty. The error is that the offense also requires knowledge or belief at the time of receipt. The breaker is the Gold Key: receipt-time knowledge is the dispositive element; the offense is not made out by the fact that property was stolen before the defendant received it.', 'Stolen-before-purchase dominant trap. The student reasons that because the quilt was stolen, Naomi must be guilty. The error is that the common-law and modern-statutory offense also requires knowledge or belief at the time of receipt. Naomi had no such knowledge when she received the quilt at the fair; the next-day confession came too late. The Gold Key resolves: receipt-time knowledge is the dispositive element; the offense is not made out by the fact that property was stolen before the defendant received it.', 'Ask whether the defendant had knowledge or belief at the moment of receipt. If not, the offense fails on the mens rea element. The fact that the property was stolen before the defendant received it is necessary but not sufficient.', 'RC-CRIMINAL-RECEIPT-TIME-KNOWLEDGE-01'),
  ('f2f8535f-5f92-5a62-a23e-ec68ba6b3e25', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18477_church_craft_fair_quilt'), 'B', 'No, because she lacked knowledge when she received control of the quilt and promptly returned it after learning.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'Naomi had no knowledge or belief that the quilt was stolen when she received control of it at the fair; the next-day confession came too late. The element is missing; the offense is not made out.', NULL, 'RC-CRIMINAL-RECEIPT-TIME-KNOWLEDGE-01'),
  ('872538c6-4761-5ab9-bb53-0ac5c675843f', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18477_church_craft_fair_quilt'), 'C', 'No, because receiving stolen property applies only to cash.', 0, '["misfit"]', '["the fact that property was stolen makes anyone who received it guilty","later knowledge relates back to the purchase and supplies the missing mens rea","receiving stolen property applies only to cash"]', 'The student reads ''stolen property'' too narrowly and concludes that the offense covers only cash, not tangible personal property like a quilt. The breaker is the Silver Key: receiving stolen property covers all stolen personal property, not just cash; the dispositive question is the defendant''s knowledge or belief at receipt, not the type of property.', 'Cash-only misfit. The student reads ''stolen property'' too narrowly and concludes that the offense covers only cash, not tangible personal property like a quilt. The Silver Key resolves: receiving stolen property covers all stolen personal property, not just cash; the dispositive question is the defendant''s knowledge or belief at receipt, not the type of property.', 'Ask whether the offense is limited to currency. It is not; receiving stolen property covers all stolen personal property, including quilts, cameras, livestock, vehicles, and electronics.', 'RC-CRIMINAL-RECEIPT-TIME-KNOWLEDGE-01'),
  ('f4a43303-2651-54d9-b03b-33c994cc1a5e', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18477_church_craft_fair_quilt'), 'D', 'Yes, because later knowledge always relates back to the purchase.', 0, '["fabricated_rule"]', '["the fact that property was stolen makes anyone who received it guilty","later knowledge relates back to the purchase and supplies the missing mens rea","receiving stolen property applies only to cash"]', 'The student treats later knowledge as if it could supply the missing receipt-time mens rea. The offense is not made out by retroactive imputation. The breaker is the Gold Key: the mens rea must exist at the moment of receipt; later knowledge does not relate back.', 'Retroactive-mens-rea distractor. The student treats later knowledge as if it could supply the missing receipt-time mens rea. The offense is not made out by retroactive imputation. The Gold Key resolves: the mens rea must exist at the moment of receipt; later knowledge does not relate back.', 'Ask whether the mens rea must exist at the time of receipt. It does; later knowledge does not retroactively supply it.', 'RC-CRIMINAL-RECEIPT-TIME-KNOWLEDGE-01'),
  ('f743c030-78d4-514e-9644-10581c72a786', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18488_youth_retreat_trailer'), 'A', 'No, because the new driver lacked timely notice of the action and the omission was not shown to be a Rule 15(c) mistake concerning identity.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'It is the only answer that names both Rule 15(c)(1)(C)(ii) notice failure and Rule 15(c)(1)(C)(iii) mistake failure.', NULL, 'RC-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-RULE15C-NOTICE-01'),
  ('a5202a86-0ab6-50b7-b6dc-121dc0e73f5d', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18488_youth_retreat_trailer'), 'B', 'Yes, because notice to the new driver after limitations is enough.', 0, '["flat_misstatement"]', '["John Doe pleadings always satisfy Rule 15(c) mistake","post-limitations notice is enough","claims against drivers can never be amended"]', 'The student hears ''notice to the new driver'' and assumes notice is enough. The breaker is the timing rule: notice after limitations is the failure the rule is designed to police.', 'Notice after limitations is the failure the rule is designed to police; Rule 15(c)(1)(C)(ii) requires notice within the Rule 4(m) period.', 'Check whether the notice was within the Rule 4(m) period.', 'RC-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-RULE15C-NOTICE-01'),
  ('1a1b344f-dc79-58fc-a81b-82c653f97806', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18488_youth_retreat_trailer'), 'C', 'No, because claims against drivers can never be amended.', 0, '["extreme_of_range"]', '["John Doe pleadings always satisfy Rule 15(c) mistake","post-limitations notice is enough","claims against drivers can never be amended"]', 'The student hears ''never'' and assumes immutability. The breaker is Rule 15(a) and Rule 15(c): leave to amend is freely granted, and a proper party-change amendment does relate back.', 'Rule 15 freely grants leave to amend, and a proper party-change amendment does relate back; the immutability overclaim misstates the rule.', 'Read Rule 15(a) and Rule 15(c); the question is whether this specific amendment satisfies Rule 15(c), not whether all such amendments are foreclosed.', 'RC-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-RULE15C-NOTICE-01'),
  ('4b7d88f7-2c81-5fa6-aa8b-2c48d0cf6cac', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18488_youth_retreat_trailer'), 'D', 'Yes, because a John Doe pleading always satisfies the Rule 15(c) mistake requirement.', 0, '["tiered_absolute"]', '["John Doe pleadings always satisfy Rule 15(c) mistake","post-limitations notice is enough","claims against drivers can never be amended"]', 'The student hears ''John Doe'' and assumes the placeholder theory supplies the required identity mistake. The breaker is the Gold Key: a John Doe pleading is not, by itself, a Rule 15(c) identity mistake.', 'A John Doe pleading is not, by itself, a Rule 15(c) identity mistake; the plaintiff must show the new party knew or should have known it would have been named but for a mistake.', 'Ask whether the new party knew or should have known it would have been named but for a mistake; a mere lack of knowledge is not automatically that mistake.', 'RC-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-RULE15C-NOTICE-01'),
  ('86e16e7f-5cb5-5867-9168-16ca071801c8', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18586_hymnsong_records'), 'A', 'Hymnsong is not a citizen for Article IV Privileges and Immunities purposes.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'Article IV P&I protects only the natural-person citizens of other states; a corporation is not a citizen and cannot use the clause to challenge California''s higher fee.', NULL, 'RC-CONSTITUTIONAL_LAW-A4PI-PERSON-CLASS-01'),
  ('12c2943c-c7d1-55fe-b500-8a56e33d1151', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18586_hymnsong_records'), 'B', 'Article IV Privileges and Immunities protects corporations whenever they distribute across state lines.', 0, '["flat_misstatement"]', '["Article IV P&I protects corporations","interstate burden automatically triggers Article IV","Article IV P&I is a criminal-procedure clause"]', 'The student bridges from ''interstate burden'' to ''Article IV must apply.'' The breaker is the Gold Key: Article IV P&I protects only natural-person citizens of other states, and a corporation is not in the protected class.', 'Dominant trap. The student bridges from ''interstate burden'' to ''Article IV must apply.'' The Gold Key rejects the bridge: a corporation is outside the protected class; Article IV P&I does not protect corporations.', 'Ask whether the plaintiff is a natural-person citizen of another state. A corporation is not; the bridge is wrong.', 'RC-CONSTITUTIONAL_LAW-A4PI-PERSON-CLASS-01'),
  ('913f4d32-05b1-52b1-a50f-2fd778eb408a', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18586_hymnsong_records'), 'C', 'Hymnsong wins automatically because the higher fee is a fundamental burden on out-of-state commerce.', 0, '["tiered_absolute"]', '["Article IV P&I protects corporations","interstate burden automatically triggers Article IV","Article IV P&I is a criminal-procedure clause"]', 'The student asserts automatic victory on a ''fundamental burden'' merits theory. The breaker is the Gold Key: the case is resolved at the person-class threshold, not on the merits.', 'Tiered-absolute trap. The student asserts automatic victory on a ''fundamental burden'' theory. The Gold Key blocks the merits: the case is resolved at the person-class threshold, not on the merits.', 'The merits are never reached. The case is dismissed at the threshold because Hymnsong is not a citizen for Article IV P&I.', 'RC-CONSTITUTIONAL_LAW-A4PI-PERSON-CLASS-01'),
  ('62dc221a-36d2-5a6b-a314-d039e3b72e8b', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18586_hymnsong_records'), 'D', 'Article IV Privileges and Immunities applies only to criminal defendants.', 0, '["flat_misstatement"]', '["Article IV P&I protects corporations","interstate burden automatically triggers Article IV","Article IV P&I is a criminal-procedure clause"]', 'The student conflates Article IV P&I with the Sixth Amendment''s criminal-procedure rights. The breaker is a call to black-letter law: Article IV P&I protects civil economic rights of natural citizen-plaintiffs.', 'Criminal-only misstatement. Article IV P&I protects civil economic rights of natural citizen-plaintiffs, not criminal defendants. The Sixth Amendment is the criminal-procedure clause.', 'Article IV P&I protects civil economic rights of natural citizen-plaintiffs, not criminal defendants.', 'RC-CONSTITUTIONAL_LAW-A4PI-PERSON-CLASS-01'),
  ('8c6e49e6-af1d-5b26-adc7-d8b1450a9285', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18592_shiloh_campground'), 'A', 'It is a factor in the nuisance and remedy analysis, but it does not automatically defeat the claim.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'Coming to the nuisance is a reasonableness factor, not a complete defense. The camp''s expansion of its summer schedule after the Timothy family arrived means the ''was there first'' intuition is at its weakest.', NULL, 'RC-TORTS-COMING-TO-NUISANCE-01'),
  ('b771546c-9053-5f17-b36c-19cd15fc43c8', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18592_shiloh_campground'), 'B', 'It is a complete defense because the camp was there first.', 0, '["tiered_absolute"]', '["coming to the nuisance is a complete defense","the defendant''s pre-existing presence immunizes later expansions","coming to the nuisance changes the tort''s category"]', 'The student reaches for the ''was there first'' intuition. The breaker is the Gold Key: the doctrine is a reasonableness factor, not a complete defense, and the *expansion* after the move-in cuts against the intuition.', 'Complete-defense overclaim (dominant trap). The student reaches for the ''was there first'' intuition. The Gold Key rejects the overclaim: the doctrine is a reasonableness factor, not a complete defense, and the *expansion* after the move-in cuts against the intuition.', 'Ask whether the activity has substantially expanded after the move-in. If yes, the ''coming to the nuisance'' doctrine is at its weakest as a complete defense.', 'RC-TORTS-COMING-TO-NUISANCE-01'),
  ('52e04546-e67d-5667-98d0-7c21ef05f720', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18592_shiloh_campground'), 'C', 'It converts the claim from nuisance to strict liability only.', 0, '["misfit"]', '["coming to the nuisance is a complete defense","the defendant''s pre-existing presence immunizes later expansions","coming to the nuisance changes the tort''s category"]', 'The student reads the ''coming to the nuisance'' doctrine as changing the tort''s category. The breaker: timing does not convert a private nuisance claim into a strict-liability claim.', 'Tort-category misframe. The student reads the ''coming to the nuisance'' doctrine as changing the tort''s category. The breaker: timing does not convert a private nuisance claim into a strict-liability claim.', 'The doctrine affects reasonableness and remedy, not the tort''s category. The tort is fixed by the nature of the interference and the defendant''s intent or negligence.', 'RC-TORTS-COMING-TO-NUISANCE-01'),
  ('700320b6-1903-5b5e-8a1f-a12026e4ebbf', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18592_shiloh_campground'), 'D', 'It is irrelevant in every nuisance case.', 0, '["extreme_of_range"]', '["coming to the nuisance is a complete defense","the defendant''s pre-existing presence immunizes later expansions","coming to the nuisance changes the tort''s category"]', 'The student overclaims the irrelevance frame. The breaker: timing *can* matter to reasonableness and remedy; the doctrine is not always irrelevant.', 'Irrelevance overclaim. The student overclaims the irrelevance frame. The breaker: timing *can* matter to reasonableness and remedy; the doctrine is not always irrelevant.', 'Timing can matter to reasonableness and remedy (e.g., balancing the equities, shaping an injunction). The doctrine is not always irrelevant.', 'RC-TORTS-COMING-TO-NUISANCE-01'),
  ('2527b480-7a90-5a53-b984-c8b2c966e4b4', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18669_mary_negligence_survival'), 'A', 'Always continue the claim because federal courts prefer merits decisions.', 0, '["tiered_absolute"]', '["federal courts always prefer merits decisions","the defendant''s home-state rule applies automatically","the plaintiff''s death destroys diversity"]', 'The student reaches for the merits-preference intuition: federal courts like to decide cases on the merits. The breaker is the Gold Key: Erie requires application of state substantive law, and the forum state''s no-survival rule means the claim does not exist to be decided on the merits.', 'Merits-preference overclaim (dominant trap). The student reaches for the merits-preference intuition: federal courts like to decide cases on the merits. The Gold Key rejects the intuition: Erie requires application of state substantive law, and the forum state''s no-survival rule means the claim does not exist to be decided on the merits.', 'Erie requires application of state substantive law. The forum state''s no-survival rule means the claim does not exist to be continued.', 'RC-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-ERIE-SURVIVAL-01'),
  ('9b904954-15a9-544b-8151-712d1faad982', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18669_mary_negligence_survival'), 'B', 'Use the defendant''s home-state survival rule automatically.', 0, '["misfit"]', '["federal courts always prefer merits decisions","the defendant''s home-state rule applies automatically","the plaintiff''s death destroys diversity"]', 'The student imports a defendant-residence rule. The breaker is Klaxon: a federal court sitting in diversity applies the forum state''s choice-of-law rules, not the defendant''s home-state rule automatically.', 'Defendant-home-state misfit. The student imports a defendant-residence rule. The breaker: Klaxon requires the federal court to apply the forum state''s choice-of-law rules, not the defendant''s home-state rule automatically.', 'Klaxon requires the federal court to apply the forum state''s choice-of-law rules, not the defendant''s home-state rule automatically.', 'RC-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-ERIE-SURVIVAL-01'),
  ('3e761ebe-862f-5b37-9961-9b2201661a1d', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18669_mary_negligence_survival'), 'C', 'Apply state survival law because it determines whether the state-created claim continues to exist.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'Under Erie, the survival of a state-created claim is a substantive question; the forum state''s no-survival rule governs, and the federal court must apply it.', NULL, 'RC-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-ERIE-SURVIVAL-01'),
  ('e3b2b7e9-520e-5233-ab90-d1903c96166c', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18669_mary_negligence_survival'), 'D', 'Dismiss only if diversity is destroyed by the death.', 0, '["misfit"]', '["federal courts always prefer merits decisions","the defendant''s home-state rule applies automatically","the plaintiff''s death destroys diversity"]', 'The student confuses the survival question with a diversity-jurisdiction question. The breaker: the Erie analysis turns on the substantive survival of the claim, not on whether diversity is preserved.', 'Citizenship-mismatch misfit. The student confuses the survival question with a diversity-jurisdiction question. The breaker: the Erie analysis turns on the substantive survival of the claim, not on whether diversity is preserved.', 'The death of the plaintiff does not necessarily destroy diversity (the executor or administrator takes the plaintiff''s place for citizenship purposes). The Erie analysis turns on the substantive survival of the claim.', 'RC-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-ERIE-SURVIVAL-01'),
  ('ca96efdf-fa2a-548c-b9b4-c4563b53280e', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18676_mary_wellness_appropriation'), 'A', 'Yes, because any consent to use a photo is permanent and unlimited.', 0, '["tiered_absolute"]', '["once-consented-always-consented","a flattering photo authorizes any use","a privacy violation requires a false statement"]', 'The student reaches for the ''once-consented-always-consented'' intuition. The breaker is the Gold Key: a license to use a likeness is limited by its scope — time, medium, and purpose — and use outside the agreed scope can support an appropriation claim.', 'Permanent-and-unlimited overclaim (dominant trap). The student reaches for the ''once-consented-always-consented'' intuition. The Gold Key rejects the overclaim: a license to use a likeness is limited by its scope, and use outside the agreed scope can support an appropriation claim.', 'Compare the original license''s scope to the later use. If any dimension is exceeded, the use is unauthorized and the appropriation claim survives.', 'RC-TORTS-APPROPRIATION-SCOPE-01'),
  ('b252a671-29f9-5125-919a-64ba6d59bf54', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18676_mary_wellness_appropriation'), 'B', 'No, because the later online advertising exceeded the license''s time and medium scope.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'A license to use a likeness is limited by its scope — time, medium, and purpose. The March use (online ads, new product line) exceeded the January license (printed brochure) on all three scope dimensions.', NULL, 'RC-TORTS-APPROPRIATION-SCOPE-01'),
  ('32287e35-bc4a-5298-8971-20aa5067d797', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18676_mary_wellness_appropriation'), 'C', 'Yes, because the photo was flattering.', 0, '["misfit"]', '["once-consented-always-consented","a flattering photo authorizes any use","a privacy violation requires a false statement"]', 'The student imports a ''flattering photo'' analysis. The breaker: the photo''s aesthetic quality is irrelevant to the appropriation analysis; the right question is whether the licensee stayed within the scope of the consent.', 'Flattering-photo misfit. The student imports a ''flattering photo'' analysis. The breaker: the photo''s aesthetic quality is irrelevant to the appropriation analysis; the right question is whether the licensee stayed within the scope of the consent.', 'The photo''s aesthetic quality is irrelevant to the appropriation analysis. The right question is whether the licensee stayed within the scope of the consent.', 'RC-TORTS-APPROPRIATION-SCOPE-01'),
  ('3dc7abcd-9e63-59c1-b9fc-5131e49f967d', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18676_mary_wellness_appropriation'), 'D', 'No, but only because the company stated a false fact about the instructor.', 0, '["misfit"]', '["once-consented-always-consented","a flattering photo authorizes any use","a privacy violation requires a false statement"]', 'The student imports a ''false fact'' analysis from defamation or false light. The breaker: the ''false fact'' theory belongs to a different privacy tort; the right theory here is appropriation, and the facts do not require a false statement.', 'False-fact tort-category misfit. The student imports a ''false fact'' analysis from defamation or false light. The breaker: the ''false fact'' theory belongs to a different privacy tort; the right theory here is appropriation, and the facts do not require a false statement.', 'The ''false fact'' theory belongs to defamation or false light, not to appropriation. The wrong here is the unauthorized commercial use of the likeness, regardless of whether the use also makes a false statement.', 'RC-TORTS-APPROPRIATION-SCOPE-01'),
  ('61e9132a-b6ed-578f-a160-d44e594bfcd2', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18747_vintage_bible_embezzlement'), 'A', 'Larceny, because every wrongful taking of property is larceny.', 0, '["tiered_absolute"]', '["every wrongful taking is larceny","voluntary handover forecloses the offense","sale to a third party = false pretenses"]', 'Everyday English treats ''theft'' as a synonym for larceny, and a student who half-remembers the larceny elements reaches for the broad ''every wrongful taking'' formulation. The breaker is the Gold Key: larceny requires a trespassory taking, and here the possession was lawful.', 'Larceny requires a trespassory taking — a wrongful caption at the outset — and here Stephen''s possession was lawful. The colloquial ''every wrongful taking is larceny'' formulation misstates the rule.', 'Ask whether the defendant came into possession lawfully; if yes, the offense is embezzlement, not larceny.', 'RC-CRIMINAL-EMBEZZLEMENT-LAWFUL-POSSESSION-01'),
  ('c1aad61a-c413-57d9-a77b-c99a61ed63c1', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18747_vintage_bible_embezzlement'), 'B', 'No offense, because Timothy voluntarily handed over the Bible.', 0, '["flat_misstatement"]', '["every wrongful taking is larceny","voluntary handover forecloses the offense","sale to a third party = false pretenses"]', 'The student hears ''voluntarily handed over'' and assumes consent forecloses the offense. The breaker is that consent to entrust for one purpose does not authorize fraudulent conversion to a different purpose.', 'Consent to entrust property for one purpose does not authorize fraudulent conversion to a different purpose. The lawful possession comes from the entrustment, and the offense is the later conversion, not the original taking.', 'Ask whether the consent was for the specific purpose to which the defendant converted the property; if not, the consent does not authorize the conversion.', 'RC-CRIMINAL-EMBEZZLEMENT-LAWFUL-POSSESSION-01'),
  ('c3722b5e-ebb6-5863-88b3-06b41408ea1d', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18747_vintage_bible_embezzlement'), 'C', 'Embezzlement, because Stephen lawfully possessed the Bible before converting it.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'It is the only answer that matches the manner in which Stephen first came into possession (lawful entrustment) and the manner in which he disposed of the property (fraudulent conversion).', NULL, 'RC-CRIMINAL-EMBEZZLEMENT-LAWFUL-POSSESSION-01'),
  ('f743da18-a51f-59b4-a36e-1cd4b81abb78', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18747_vintage_bible_embezzlement'), 'D', 'False pretenses, because Stephen sold the Bible to someone else.', 0, '["bait_doctrine","wrong_frame"]', '["every wrongful taking is larceny","voluntary handover forecloses the offense","sale to a third party = false pretenses"]', 'The student hears ''sold the Bible to someone else'' and reaches for the false-pretenses label. The breaker is the false-pretenses element check: no misrepresentation, no title passage from Timothy.', 'False pretenses requires a false representation of a material fact that induces the victim to pass title; here Stephen never made a false representation to Timothy, and Timothy did not pass title.', 'Ask whether a false representation induced the victim to pass title; if not, false pretenses is a misfit and the offense is conversion of entrusted property.', 'RC-CRIMINAL-EMBEZZLEMENT-LAWFUL-POSSESSION-01'),
  ('d1856e03-537d-5987-9e3a-16e7910f3ad4', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18784_fellowship_lot_used_car'), 'A', 'Fraud never applies to private sales between acquaintances.', 0, '["tiered_absolute"]', '["fraud-never-applies","reliance-presumed","contract-integration-as-tort-defense"]', 'The student half-remembers that contract claims absorb oral assurances and reaches for the broadest negative framing. The ''fraud never applies'' phrasing sounds like a strong ''fraud elements are hard to meet'' defense. The ''between acquaintances'' hook looks like it tracks the parties'' relationship.', 'Broad-absolute overclaim. The word ''never'' is the visible overclaim marker. Fraud applies in every commercial context where the elements are met; acquaintance status is irrelevant. Dominant trap.', 'Lock choice C: ''Ruth may not have justifiably relied on a statement plainly contradicted by the obvious AS-IS term.''', 'RC-TORTS-FRAUD-AS-IS-01'),
  ('05304a76-e7be-5ed1-9aae-650327132379', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18784_fellowship_lot_used_car'), 'B', 'Ruth wins because reliance is presumed whenever a seller speaks.', 0, '["tiered_absolute"]', '["fraud-never-applies","reliance-presumed","contract-integration-as-tort-defense"]', 'The student who confuses the elements treats reliance as automatic once a seller speaks. The ''presumed'' phrasing sounds like a doctrinal shortcut, and the conclusion ''Ruth wins'' gives the student a quick exit from the analysis.', 'Presumed-reliance overclaim. The word ''presumed'' is the visible overclaim marker. Reliance is an affirmative element the plaintiff must prove; an obvious contradictory writing defeats reasonableness as a matter of law under Restatement (Second) of Torts § 541.', 'Lock choice C: the AS-IS term in bold above the signature defeats the reasonableness of reliance as a matter of law under Restatement (Second) of Torts § 541.', 'RC-TORTS-FRAUD-AS-IS-01'),
  ('c0e02a1d-34bf-53dc-9493-7ffc62c10525', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18784_fellowship_lot_used_car'), 'C', 'Ruth may not have justifiably relied on a statement plainly contradicted by the obvious AS-IS term.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'Breaks neither filter. Matches the credited outcome (the seller wins on the reliance element). Hard-structural from C3: visible from the stem (AS-IS clause in bold, directly above signature) and the choice text (the contradiction is the defense).', NULL, 'RC-TORTS-FRAUD-AS-IS-01'),
  ('ae8bac9c-5f85-5233-88d1-3e28b0819e30', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18784_fellowship_lot_used_car'), 'D', 'Daniel''s oral assurance was automatically incorporated into the bill of sale.', 0, '["misfit","wrong_frame"]', '["fraud-never-applies","reliance-presumed","contract-integration-as-tort-defense"]', 'The student who reflexively routes to the contracts lane treats the oral-vs-written tension as a parol evidence / integration question rather than a tort reliance question. The framing sounds lawyerly and feels like the ''real'' doctrinal move.', 'Contract-integration misframe. The call is in tort (fraud defense); the choice is in contract (parol evidence / integration). The choice answers a different question than the one asked. Mold: ISSUE_SENSE / misfit.', 'Lock choice C: justifiable reliance is a tort-reliance question, and the obvious writing defeats it as a matter of law.', 'RC-TORTS-FRAUD-AS-IS-01'),
  ('4facd963-1a35-5424-b72b-55731e8e30c7', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18811_elevator_access_class'), 'A', 'No class can be certified, because each student uses the elevator at different times of day.', 0, '["misfit"]', '["23(b)(3)-default-cliche","no-class-can-be-certified","23(b)(1)-misframe-for-injunctive-classes"]', 'The student who can''t reconcile the disability access with the class certification rules reaches for ''no class can be certified'' as a clean way out. The ''each student uses the elevator at different times'' framing sounds like a commonality problem.', 'Frame-shift. The call is ''which Rule 23(b) category is the most natural fit.'' The choice refuses the call — it answers ''no class can be certified,'' a different question. Mold: ISSUE_SENSE / misfit.', 'Lock choice C: Rule 23(a) commonality is satisfied because the dormitory-access claim raises common questions of law and fact (uniform conduct, uniform remedy). Rule 23(b)(2) is the right subsection.', 'RC-CIVPRO-RULE23-23B2-01'),
  ('a39656e8-2c95-557c-a727-cbc40b957bfc', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18811_elevator_access_class'), 'B', 'Rule 23(b)(1) only, because a judgment about one student could influence the university''s conduct toward the others.', 0, '["half_truth"]', '["23(b)(3)-default-cliche","no-class-can-be-certified","23(b)(1)-misframe-for-injunctive-classes"]', 'The student who knows that ''separate actions could affect others'' gestures at 23(b)(1) without checking the mandatory-class fit. The ''could influence the university''s conduct'' framing sounds lawyerly.', 'Wrong subsection. Rule 23(b)(1) covers mandatory classes where separate actions would risk inconsistent adjudications or impairment of absent members'' interests. The dormitory-access claim fits neither prong. The ''could influence the university''s conduct'' framing is a half-truth that gestures at the right area but picks the wrong subsection.', 'Lock choice C: Rule 23(b)(2) is the right subsection. The university''s refusal to repair the elevator is precisely the kind of ''refus[al] to act on grounds that apply generally to the class'' that Rule 23(b)(2) was written for.', 'RC-CIVPRO-RULE23-23B2-01'),
  ('f343aac8-6a7c-5f08-a5f0-988177cf5e14', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18811_elevator_access_class'), 'C', 'Rule 23(b)(2), because the requested injunction would provide classwide relief from conduct generally applicable to the class.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'Breaks neither filter. Matches the credited outcome. Hard-structural from C3: visible from the stem (single defendant, single uniform conduct, single injunctive remedy, no individualized damages) and the choice text (the rule fits).', NULL, 'RC-CIVPRO-RULE23-23B2-01'),
  ('acb0ee03-5779-5b13-af05-768c8bf4cf55', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18811_elevator_access_class'), 'D', 'Rule 23(b)(3), because every class action involving many people must satisfy predominance and superiority.', 0, '["tiered_absolute"]', '["23(b)(3)-default-cliche","no-class-can-be-certified","23(b)(1)-misframe-for-injunctive-classes"]', 'The student who half-remembers 23(b)(3) as the ''default'' class action applies it broadly. The ''every class action involving many people'' framing sounds like a strong ''class actions are governed by predominance'' shortcut. The dominant trap.', 'Broad-absolute overclaim. The word ''every'' is the visible overclaim marker. Rule 23(b)(2) is a separate category that does not require predominance or superiority. Dominant trap.', 'Lock choice C: the 1-1-1-0 pattern (one defendant + one uniform conduct + one injunctive remedy + no individualized damages) is the textbook Rule 23(b)(2) shape.', 'RC-CIVPRO-RULE23-23B2-01'),
  ('07674e07-899e-5a28-826f-1086252f12f1', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18843_patio_pressure_washer'), 'A', 'No, because she intended only a short, safe temporary use and return.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'Breaks neither filter. Matches the credited outcome. Anchor-assisted from C3: the temporary-use-and-return fact pattern is visible from the stem, and the Gold Key resolves the dominant-trap overclaim by naming the intent-to-permanently-deprive element as the doctrinal hinge.', NULL, 'RC-CRIMINAL-LARCENY-TEMPORARY-01'),
  ('0078df19-fa8b-5ea8-bbd4-14c7e7577af0', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18843_patio_pressure_washer'), 'B', 'No, because power tools are not personal property.', 0, '["half_truth"]', '["any-unauthorized-use-is-larceny","asportation-equals-larceny","personal-property-overclaim-negative"]', 'The student who can''t reconcile the temporary-use fact pattern with the larceny charge reaches for the personal-property element as a way to clear the call. The ''power tools are not personal property'' framing sounds like a clean doctrinal out, but it is a misstatement of the law and a NOT_RESPONSIVE move on the real issue.', 'Overclaim-negative on the personal-property element. A pressure washer is the textbook case of movable, tangible personal property; the ''not personal property'' framing is a `tiered_absolute` overclaim that loses to the obvious counterexample. The choice is also NOT_RESPONSIVE to the real issue, which is the intent-to-permanently-deprive element.', 'Lock choice A: the real issue is the intent-to-permanently-deprive element, and the asset is the textbook chattel. The temporary-use-and-return fact pattern kills the intent element.', 'RC-CRIMINAL-LARCENY-TEMPORARY-01'),
  ('33d562a7-6f14-5334-930b-72fd49edbf7f', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18843_patio_pressure_washer'), 'C', 'Yes, because any unauthorized use of property is larceny.', 0, '["tiered_absolute"]', '["any-unauthorized-use-is-larceny","asportation-equals-larceny","personal-property-overclaim-negative"]', 'The student who fuses the trespass element with the common-law definition picks the broadest ''Yes'' available. The word ''any'' is the visible overclaim marker, and the dominant-trap pull is high because the borrowing was unauthorized.', 'Any-unauthorized-use overclaim. The word ''any'' is the visible overclaim marker. Larceny requires the intent to permanently deprive, not merely unauthorized use. Safe, short, return-planned borrowing is the counterexample that the universal quantifier cannot capture. Dominant trap.', 'Lock choice A: the stem shows a defined short-term use and an actual return plan. The intent-to-permanently-deprive element is missing on its face. The ''any unauthorized use'' framing loses to the safe, short, return-planned borrowing counterexample.', 'RC-CRIMINAL-LARCENY-TEMPORARY-01'),
  ('65fee21b-789d-5043-bc11-4f724e285de5', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18843_patio_pressure_washer'), 'D', 'Yes, because the pressure washer moved from one place to another.', 0, '["wrong_element"]', '["any-unauthorized-use-is-larceny","asportation-equals-larceny","personal-property-overclaim-negative"]', 'The student who treats asportation (carrying away) as sufficient for larceny ignores the missing intent-to-permanently-deprive element. The ''the pressure washer moved'' framing sounds like a clean doctrinal argument because movement is one element of larceny.', 'Asportation-only reasoning. Asportation is one of the elements of larceny, not the whole thing. The intent-to-permanently-deprive element is independently required, and the stem supplies clear temporary-use and intent-to-return facts that defeat the intent element on their face.', 'Lock choice A: the stem shows clear temporary-use and intent-to-return facts that defeat the intent-to-permanently-deprive element on their face.', 'RC-CRIMINAL-LARCENY-TEMPORARY-01'),
  ('c11793cd-7eb9-5315-8db1-d9ddd2c994fb', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18890_hanna_erie_threshold'), 'A', 'It ignores the threshold question whether a valid federal directive directly controls the issue.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'Breaks neither filter. Matches the credited outcome. Anchor-assisted from C3: the federal-directive threshold is visible from the stem (FRE 801 directly on point), and the Gold Key resolves the dominant-trap overclaim by naming the Hanna first-step move.', NULL, 'RC-CIVPRO-ERIE-HANNA-01'),
  ('aec1e41d-38da-504c-9ce5-076a2ec75e29', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18890_hanna_erie_threshold'), 'B', 'Nothing; outcome effect is the complete Erie test in every case.', 0, '["tiered_absolute"]', '["Erie-is-the-outcome-test-cliche","state-law-always-applies-cliche","federal-law-always-applies-cliche"]', 'The student who half-remembers the Guaranty Trust outcome-determinative test picks the broadest ''Nothing'' available. The word ''every'' is the visible overclaim marker, and the dominant-trap pull is high because the outcome test is a common shorthand for Erie.', 'Outcome-test-only overclaim. The word ''every'' is the visible overclaim marker. Under Hanna, the outcome-determinative test runs only if no valid federal directive directly controls. The ''Erie is the outcome test in every case'' framing loses to the Hanna counterexample. Dominant trap.', 'Lock choice A: the stem names FRE 801 directly on point; the federal-directive threshold controls; the outcome-determinative test does not come into play. The ''Erie is the outcome test in every case'' framing loses to the Hanna counterexample.', 'RC-CIVPRO-ERIE-HANNA-01'),
  ('b7b76ce6-46ad-5e77-9a4e-9ba4c5b45193', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18890_hanna_erie_threshold'), 'C', 'It is wrong because state law always applies in diversity.', 0, '["tiered_absolute"]', '["Erie-is-the-outcome-test-cliche","state-law-always-applies-cliche","federal-law-always-applies-cliche"]', 'The student who fuses Erie substantive-law with an absolute ''state law wins'' framing picks the state-only cliche. The ''always'' marker is the visible overclaim.', 'State-only cliche. The word ''always'' is the visible overclaim marker. Erie holds that federal courts in diversity apply state substantive law, but federal procedural law — including valid Federal Rules — also applies when directly on point.', 'Lock choice A: the stem names FRE 801 directly on point; the state-only cliche cannot displace a valid federal directive. The ''state law always applies'' framing loses to the Hanna counterexample.', 'RC-CIVPRO-ERIE-HANNA-01'),
  ('eea7c88c-b80a-508e-947b-21c4255bbb19', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18890_hanna_erie_threshold'), 'D', 'It is wrong because federal law always applies in diversity.', 0, '["tiered_absolute"]', '["Erie-is-the-outcome-test-cliche","state-law-always-applies-cliche","federal-law-always-applies-cliche"]', 'The student who fuses Hanna federal-directive with an absolute ''federal law wins'' framing picks the federal-only cliche. The ''always'' marker is the visible overclaim.', 'Federal-only cliche. The word ''always'' is the visible overclaim marker. Erie holds that state substantive law applies in diversity; federal law applies only to the extent a valid federal directive directly controls the issue.', 'Lock choice A: the federal-only cliche cannot replace the Erie substantive-law rule. The ''federal law always applies'' framing loses to the Erie counterexample.', 'RC-CIVPRO-ERIE-HANNA-01'),
  ('2ea0e45b-6139-5f85-8ad3-0ca375752422', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18900_wedding_garden_spill'), 'A', 'Allow it if the missed bookings were caused by the ankle injury.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'Esther''s broken ankle is a tort-caused physical injury; the missed bookings flow from that injury and are recoverable parasitic economic damages. The standalone pure-economic-loss bar does not apply because the plaintiff has a physical injury.', NULL, 'RC-TORTS-LOST-WAGES-PARASITIC-01'),
  ('9afde043-26b1-52eb-969c-4fc8049fcd79', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18900_wedding_garden_spill'), 'B', 'Bar it, because missed-booking income in a negligence case is always pure economic loss.', 0, '["tiered_absolute","standalone-pure-economic-loss overclaim"]', '["pure economic loss is an absolute bar to financial recovery in negligence","lost future earnings are at most nominal damages","lost contract income requires a contract theory of recovery"]', 'The ''always pure economic loss'' framing sounds dispositive because the rule is a famous first-year rule. The trap is the tiered-absolute pattern: a true rule pushed too far into ''always'' language. The breaker is the Gold Key: the standalone pure economic loss bar applies only to plaintiffs with no physical injury; it does not bar financial harm that flows from a tort-caused physical injury.', 'The ''always pure economic loss'' framing is the dominant trap. The standalone pure economic loss bar applies only to plaintiffs with no physical injury; it does not bar financial harm that flows from a tort-caused physical injury.', 'Apply the Gold Key: economic damages that flow from a tort-caused physical injury are recoverable parasitic damages. The Silver Key call-focus move: when the defendant invokes ''pure economic loss,'' check for a physical injury.', 'RC-TORTS-LOST-WAGES-PARASITIC-01'),
  ('32f69dc5-9929-56c9-b58a-5014a382c23d', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18900_wedding_garden_spill'), 'C', 'Allow it only as nominal damages, because the receipts are contingent on future events.', 0, '["extreme_of_range","wrong_remedy"]', '["pure economic loss is an absolute bar to financial recovery in negligence","lost future earnings are at most nominal damages","lost contract income requires a contract theory of recovery"]', 'The trap banks on the contingency wrinkle: future-event income can be harder to prove, and the ''nominal only'' label sounds like a compromise. The breaker is the wrong-remedy cut: contingency is a damages-quantification issue (certainty, mitigation), not a remedy-category issue; nominal damages are reserved for cases with no compensable injury.', 'Contingent future bookings are a damages-quantification issue (certainty, mitigation), not a remedy-category issue. Nominal damages are reserved for cases with no compensable injury; here there is a broken ankle and provable lost bookings.', 'Once causation and a reasonable degree of certainty are established, lost future earnings are recoverable as compensatory damages; they are not capped at a nominal sum. Nominal damages are reserved for cases with no compensable injury.', 'RC-TORTS-LOST-WAGES-PARASITIC-01'),
  ('e11a00be-0cca-5802-a71b-1ef1ca64152a', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18900_wedding_garden_spill'), 'D', 'Bar it unless Esther also proves a separate contract with the venue for the lost income.', 0, '["misfit","wrong_frame"]', '["pure economic loss is an absolute bar to financial recovery in negligence","lost future earnings are at most nominal damages","lost contract income requires a contract theory of recovery"]', 'The trap is the contract-vs.-tort frame: ''lost contract income'' sounds contractual, so the student wants a contract theory of recovery. The breaker is the wrong-frame cut: the suit is in tort for premises-injury negligence, and the photography contracts measure the loss but are not the cause of action against the venue.', 'The suit is in tort for premises-injury negligence. The relevant contracts are the photography contracts Esther had with the bride-and-groom clients; those contracts measure the loss, but they are not the cause of action against the venue.', 'The lost income is recoverable as a parasitic economic consequence of the tort claim, not as a stand-alone contract claim against the venue. The relevant contracts measure the loss, but they are not the cause of action.', 'RC-TORTS-LOST-WAGES-PARASITIC-01'),
  ('d8148474-21c6-58f6-8c02-fa92d8953254', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18914_rare_bible_provenance'), 'A', 'The buyer may lack justifiable reliance because the provenance letter plainly contradicted the oral statement.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'The provenance letter was a plainly-worded, pre-sale written contradiction of the oral assurance. Ignoring an obvious contradiction can defeat justifiable reliance as a matter of law. This is Esther''s strongest defense.', NULL, 'RC-TORTS-FRAUD-JUSTIFIABLE-RELIANCE-01'),
  ('c43c7e5a-bd20-5216-8865-4f677836da11', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18914_rare_bible_provenance'), 'B', 'The buyer automatically wins because oral assurances always override written provenance in tort.', 0, '["misfit","wrong_frame"]', '["oral overrides written in tort","the originality of specific items is always opinion","fraud does not require reliance if the statement was intentional"]', 'The trap banks on a popular student myth: ''oral overrides written.'' The breaker is the wrong-frame cut: the suit is in tort for fraud, and the parol-evidence-style ''oral overrides written'' framing does not control the reliance analysis in tort. The relevant question is what a reasonable buyer would have noticed, not which version of a statement ''wins'' as a matter of form.', 'The suit is in tort for fraud, not for breach of contract. The parol-evidence-style ''oral overrides written'' framing is a private-law contract doctrine that does not control the reliance analysis in tort.', 'Apply the Silver Key: lock the call to the seller''s strongest defense — the lack-of-reliance defense, not a ''winning party'' rule. The parol evidence rule and the ''oral overrides written'' framing are private-law contract doctrines; they do not control the reliance analysis in tort.', 'RC-TORTS-FRAUD-JUSTIFIABLE-RELIANCE-01'),
  ('11c815cd-491c-5813-a6d7-9c71fc5e8409', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18914_rare_bible_provenance'), 'C', 'The seller made no factual statement because the originality of Bible pages is always opinion.', 0, '["flat_misstatement","fact/opinion confusion (verifiable historical fact, not opinion)"]', '["oral overrides written in tort","the originality of specific items is always opinion","fraud does not require reliance if the statement was intentional"]', 'The trap banks on the classic first-year fraud lever: the fact/opinion distinction. Page originality sounds like a connoisseur''s question rather than a fact. The breaker is the fact/opinion confusion cut: page originality in a specific copy of a specific book is a verifiable historical fact, not a value judgment. The ''always opinion'' framing is the tell.', 'Page originality in a specific copy of a specific book is a verifiable historical fact, not a value judgment or connoisseur''s opinion. The ''always opinion'' framing is the tell.', 'Apply the Gold Key: page originality in a specific copy of a specific book is a verifiable historical fact, not a value judgment or connoisseur''s opinion. The ''always opinion'' framing is the tell. The stronger defense is the lack of justifiable reliance from the plainly-worded written contradiction.', 'RC-TORTS-FRAUD-JUSTIFIABLE-RELIANCE-01'),
  ('0c0df62c-e505-58f9-b177-54bb241f87dd', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18914_rare_bible_provenance'), 'D', 'Fraud does not require reliance if the seller''s statement was intentional.', 0, '["flat_misstatement","no-reliance-element misstatement"]', '["oral overrides written in tort","the originality of specific items is always opinion","fraud does not require reliance if the statement was intentional"]', 'The trap is the worst-of-both trap: the student conflates scienter with reliance. The breaker is the flat-misstatement cut: reliance is an independent element of fraud, and proving the defendant''s intent does not waive the reliance requirement.', 'Reliance is an independent element of fraud; proving the defendant''s scienter does not waive the reliance requirement. Restatement (Second) of Torts § 525.', 'The plaintiff must prove all elements of fraud, including reliance, regardless of whether the defendant''s intent is also proved. Restatement (Second) of Torts § 525.', 'RC-TORTS-FRAUD-JUSTIFIABLE-RELIANCE-01'),
  ('cccb988c-25fc-5400-ac65-0d272069b649', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18974_subscription_meal_kit_23f'), 'A', 'Immediate appeal only if the case also contains a federal question.', 0, '["fabricated_rule","procedural_frame"]', '["importance of a non-final order creates appellate jurisdiction","federal question is the gate for class-certification appellate review","class-certification orders are never reviewable"]', 'The student treats ''federal question'' as the procedural gate. The Rule 23(f) route is the same whether or not the case has a federal question. The breaker is the rule-stated-is-not-the-law: the appellate route is not conditioned on the presence of a federal question.', 'False-rule cut. The appellate route for a class-certification order is Rule 23(f), which is not conditioned on the presence of a federal question. Federal-question jurisdiction is a subject-matter-jurisdiction concept, not a procedural-route trigger.', 'Ask whether the appellate route is conditioned on the source of subject-matter jurisdiction. Rule 23(f) is not; the route is the same whether or not the case has a federal question.', 'RC-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-RULE-23F-INTERLOCUTORY-01'),
  ('575ef426-fe16-564c-86dd-a1873dd8191d', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18974_subscription_meal_kit_23f'), 'B', 'Automatic appeal as of right because class certification is important.', 0, '["fabricated_rule","wrong_frame"]', '["importance of a non-final order creates appellate jurisdiction","federal question is the gate for class-certification appellate review","class-certification orders are never reviewable"]', 'The student accepts the importance argument and concludes that an as-of-right appeal must exist. The breaker is the final-judgment rule plus Rule 23(f)''s narrow, permission-based carve-out; importance is not a statutory hook.', 'Dominant trap. Importance does not create appellate jurisdiction. The default is the final-judgment rule of 28 U.S.C. § 1291; Rule 23(f) is a narrow, permission-based exception, not an as-of-right one.', 'Ask whether a statute or rule makes the class-certification order appealable as of right. None does; Rule 23(f) is a narrow, permission-based carve-out. Importance is not a statutory hook.', 'RC-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-RULE-23F-INTERLOCUTORY-01'),
  ('d16b8dd3-2024-5316-89e7-95cb40df3a87', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18974_subscription_meal_kit_23f'), 'C', 'A discretionary Rule 23(f) petition for permission to appeal.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'A class-certification order is interlocutory and not appealable as of right. Rule 23(f) supplies the narrow, permission-based carve-out for immediate federal appellate review. The court of appeals has discretion whether to grant the petition; importance of the question does not change the route.', NULL, 'RC-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-RULE-23F-INTERLOCUTORY-01'),
  ('4cb5cde9-104c-5234-916b-675b8fdda761', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18974_subscription_meal_kit_23f'), 'D', 'No possible review ever, even after final judgment.', 0, '["extreme_of_range","wrong_frame"]', '["importance of a non-final order creates appellate jurisdiction","federal question is the gate for class-certification appellate review","class-certification orders are never reviewable"]', 'The student overclaims a total bar on review. The breaker is that final-judgment review under 28 U.S.C. § 1291 is always available at the end of the case, and Rule 23(f) interlocutory review is available now.', 'Overclaim. Class-certification rulings are reviewable on appeal from final judgment under 28 U.S.C. § 1291 and, in many cases, immediately under Rule 23(f). The ''no review ever'' framing is a misstatement of the law.', 'Ask whether the order is absolutely unreviewable. A class-certification order is reviewable on appeal from final judgment and, in many cases, immediately under Rule 23(f).', 'RC-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-RULE-23F-INTERLOCUTORY-01'),
  ('b0fde0f6-ef28-5245-83b5-4c7e62333efd', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18982_homeschool_coop_month'), 'A', 'Absolute privilege, because all private newsletters are protected.', 0, '["fabricated_rule"]', '["any factual mistake defeats truth","private newsletters are absolutely privileged","license status is subjective opinion"]', 'The student treats ''private newsletter'' as automatically privileged. Absolute privilege is reserved for institutional speakers (legislators, judges, witnesses, executive officials); a private newsletter is not within that set. The breaker is the rule-stated-is-not-the-law: absolute privilege does not extend to private newsletters.', 'Invented-privilege cut. Absolute privilege is reserved for institutional speakers (legislators, judges, witnesses in judicial proceedings, executive officials speaking on matters of state); a private newsletter is not within that set.', 'Ask whether the speaker is an institutional actor speaking in the relevant capacity. A private newsletter is not.', 'RC-TORTS-DEFAMATION-SUBSTANTIAL-TRUTH-01'),
  ('05d829c9-bc4a-562c-be21-5b1e1a057943', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18982_homeschool_coop_month'), 'B', 'Opinion, because license status is subjective.', 0, '["fabricated_rule"]', '["any factual mistake defeats truth","private newsletters are absolutely privileged","license status is subjective opinion"]', 'The student treats ''license was revoked'' as a subjective statement. License status is a verifiable fact; ''license was revoked'' is a factual assertion, not an opinion. The breaker is the rule-stated-is-not-the-law: the opinion defense requires a non-verifiable statement.', 'False opinion recharacterization. The opinion defense requires a non-verifiable statement; license status is a verifiable fact. ''License was revoked'' is a factual assertion, not an opinion.', 'Ask whether the statement can be proven true or false. ''License was revoked'' is verifiable; it is a factual assertion, not an opinion.', 'RC-TORTS-DEFAMATION-SUBSTANTIAL-TRUTH-01'),
  ('596f27c9-b463-55d5-a19a-0781f65165ea', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18982_homeschool_coop_month'), 'C', 'Substantial truth, because the sting of the statement was true despite the wrong month.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'The defamatory sting is ''childcare license revoked for failing a state safety inspection.'' The license was revoked for failing a state safety inspection. The wrong month is a minor inaccuracy that does not change the sting. Substantial truth is the defense.', NULL, 'RC-TORTS-DEFAMATION-SUBSTANTIAL-TRUTH-01'),
  ('d983ed26-f565-5da6-af68-c68d99737168', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '18982_homeschool_coop_month'), 'D', 'No defense, because any factual mistake defeats truth.', 0, '["fabricated_rule"]', '["any factual mistake defeats truth","private newsletters are absolutely privileged","license status is subjective opinion"]', 'The student half-remembers ''defamation requires truth'' and reasons that any mistake (even a one-month error) makes the statement false. The breaker is the Gold Key: a statement is not false if the gist or sting is substantially true. The wrong-month inaccuracy does not change the sting.', 'Dominant trap. The any-inaccuracy myth is the classic MBE misconception. Gold Key: a statement is not false if the gist or sting is substantially true. The wrong-month inaccuracy does not change the sting here.', 'Identify the defamatory sting. If the sting is true — even if a minor detail (month, day, year) is wrong — substantial truth is the defense. The wrong-month inaccuracy does not change the sting here.', 'RC-TORTS-DEFAMATION-SUBSTANTIAL-TRUTH-01'),
  ('525dd7e4-2003-5e3e-bcc0-1b584762e957', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '19379_hymn_candle_invoice_van'), 'A', 'Overrule the objection because incriminating statements between spouses are never privileged in a criminal case.', 0, '["tiered_absolute"]', '["divorce_ends_all_spousal_privilege","criminal_confessions_never_privileged","correct_result_wrong_route"]', 'A student may think incriminating criminal statements should always come in. The breaker is that incriminating content alone does not erase a valid confidential marital communication privilege.', 'The word never overclaims; incriminating content does not itself erase communications privilege.', 'Ask whether any privilege route survives despite incriminating content.', 'REM-EVIDENCE-SPOUSAL-19379-01'),
  ('3b9c24b6-3d80-5bac-b2a5-25deda3c65fe', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '19379_hymn_candle_invoice_van'), 'B', 'Sustain the objection if Timothy’s statement was a confidential marital communication made during the marriage; divorce ends the adverse spousal testimonial privilege, but it does not by itself eliminate the marital communications privilege for prior confidential communications.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'B is correct. Gold Key: spousal testimonial privilege and confidential marital communications privilege are separate routes. The testimonial privilege depends on a current marriage; the communications privilege can protect confidential private communications made during the marriage even after divorce.

Silver Key: when the array splits “sustain/overrule” and “testimonial/communications,” match the privilege route to the timing facts. Timothy and Lydia are divorced now, but the statement was private and made while they were married. The communications route, not the testimonial route, is the responsive route.', NULL, 'REM-EVIDENCE-SPOUSAL-19379-01'),
  ('eff34b14-0efa-5135-b989-f5a747939925', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '19379_hymn_candle_invoice_van'), 'C', 'Overrule the objection because divorce ends every spousal privilege for statements made during the marriage.', 0, '["tiered_absolute"]', '["divorce_ends_all_spousal_privilege","criminal_confessions_never_privileged","correct_result_wrong_route"]', 'A student may know divorce matters and overapply that fact to every spousal privilege. The breaker is the Gold Key distinction between testimonial privilege and confidential communications privilege.', 'The word every collapses two spousal privileges into one divorce rule.', 'Separate testimonial privilege from confidential marital communications privilege.', 'REM-EVIDENCE-SPOUSAL-19379-01'),
  ('f3302a61-da43-5be8-9846-093187d3b944', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '19379_hymn_candle_invoice_van'), 'D', 'Sustain the objection under the adverse spousal testimonial privilege because Lydia is being asked to testify adversely against Timothy.', 0, '["bait_doctrine","wrong_frame"]', '["divorce_ends_all_spousal_privilege","criminal_confessions_never_privileged","correct_result_wrong_route"]', 'A student may stop at the correct surface result, sustain. The breaker is that the route is adverse testimonial privilege, and the divorce fact points away from that route.', 'It reaches sustain under the wrong privilege route.', 'Between two sustain answers, choose the privilege route that matches the timing facts.', 'REM-EVIDENCE-SPOUSAL-19379-01'),
  ('c3a97a30-2a04-50ff-8676-74bec0253126', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '19381_shepherd_olive_press_deed'), 'A', 'Naomi, because a deed that is not recorded is void.', 0, '["flat_misstatement"]', '["unrecorded means void","recorder stamp required","title passes only upon recording"]', 'This sells the harsh memory that missing a public filing kills a property transfer. The breaker is the Gold Key: recording protects priority, not validity between the original parties.', 'This choice states the recording myth in its harshest form: unrecorded equals void.', 'Apply the Gold Key and ask whether a protected third party appears.', 'RP-ANCHOR-RECORDING-VALIDITY-01'),
  ('fc2f686d-d147-588a-b74f-cd348bceec31', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '19381_shepherd_olive_press_deed'), 'B', 'Naomi, because the county recorder’s stamp is a deed-validity formality.', 0, '["fabricated_rule"]', '["unrecorded means void","recorder stamp required","title passes only upon recording"]', 'This sells a formalities reflex by turning the recorder’s stamp into another deed requirement. The breaker is that the stem already gives the deed-validity facts and the stamp belongs to recording.', 'This choice invents a recorder-stamp deed-validity requirement.', 'Use the stem: all required deed terms were already supplied.', 'RP-ANCHOR-RECORDING-VALIDITY-01'),
  ('563ff726-0427-59a6-8661-2fa93653dd0b', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '19381_shepherd_olive_press_deed'), 'C', 'Lydia, because recording is not required for a valid deed to pass title between grantor and grantee.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'C is correct. The stem gives the title-transfer facts: Naomi signed the deed, delivered it, Lydia accepted it, and the deed contained the required deed terms. No outsider claims the parcel. That makes the question a validity-between-the-parties question, not a recording-priority question.

Gold Key: A valid deed passes title between grantor and grantee when delivered and accepted; recording protects notice and priority against outsiders, not validity between the original parties.

Silver Key: Read the answer array as a 3-vs-1 recording trap. Three choices give the property back to Naomi because of recording; one choice answers the actual call: title between the original parties.', NULL, 'RP-ANCHOR-RECORDING-VALIDITY-01'),
  ('e10117cc-eae1-5e9b-9aa8-2e7c0b9c2627', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '19381_shepherd_olive_press_deed'), 'D', 'Naomi, until Lydia records the deed.', 0, '["backwards"]', '["unrecorded means void","recorder stamp required","title passes only upon recording"]', 'This sells a timing compromise: Lydia will own later, after recording. The breaker is that title passed when the valid deed was delivered and accepted; no later recording step is needed between these parties.', 'This choice reverses the timing by treating recording as the event that passes title.', 'Delivery and acceptance do the title-passing work; recording is not the timing gate here.', 'RP-ANCHOR-RECORDING-VALIDITY-01'),
  ('a8a885b5-8a96-5b46-aa06-2c14b15b04f5', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '19386_scripture_audio_cap'), 'A', 'Hannah may enforce the promise only up to the contract''s $12,000 liability cap.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'It is true and responsive after C is cut for overclaim, B for wrong route, and D under the same-contract cap axis.', NULL, 'REM-CONTRACTS-TPB-PACKAGE-01'),
  ('b0822cfd-c3c4-599f-a091-f022c4410dc8', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '19386_scripture_audio_cap'), 'B', 'Hannah may recover only if Peter first assigns her the audio-service contract.', 0, '["bait_doctrine","wrong_frame"]', '["nonparty_not_bound_by_limit","privity_blocks_all_beneficiaries","assignment_required_for_tpb","cap_does_not_travel"]', 'Assignment sounds like a legitimate way for a nonparty to receive contract rights. The breaker is that the stem already supplies third-party-beneficiary status, so assignment answers the wrong route.', 'Assignment is a real contract-rights route, but it answers a different route from the one in the stem.', 'Say: ''TPB route is already in the stem; assignment is not required.''', 'REM-CONTRACTS-TPB-PACKAGE-01'),
  ('976e7b07-1a8e-527c-b920-b2a7278da610', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '19386_scripture_audio_cap'), 'C', 'Hannah cannot recover anything because beneficiaries can never enforce audio-service contracts.', 0, '["tiered_absolute"]', '["nonparty_not_bound_by_limit","privity_blocks_all_beneficiaries","assignment_required_for_tpb","cap_does_not_travel"]', 'The privity reflex makes it feel safe to say an outsider gets nothing. The breaker is the categorical word ''never,'' because intended beneficiaries are not categorically barred from enforcing service contracts.', 'The word ''never'' overstates the rule by denying all intended-beneficiary enforcement of audio-service contracts.', 'Circle ''never'' and ask whether the stem directly names the beneficiary.', 'REM-CONTRACTS-TPB-PACKAGE-01'),
  ('bf0a2822-cc5e-556c-a767-54832d0c30c9', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '19386_scripture_audio_cap'), 'D', 'Hannah may recover the full $26,000 because she did not sign the liability cap.', 0, '["backwards","wrong_frame"]', '["nonparty_not_bound_by_limit","privity_blocks_all_beneficiaries","assignment_required_for_tpb","cap_does_not_travel"]', 'Nonparty status makes the liability cap feel like a private bargain Hannah never accepted. The breaker is the package rule: Hannah''s enforcement right and the cap come from the same contract.', 'The answer reverses the package rule by letting Hannah use the contract for the right while escaping the same contract''s cap.', 'Ask: ''What document creates the right, and what document creates the limit?''', 'REM-CONTRACTS-TPB-PACKAGE-01'),
  ('30653799-5f24-5ade-8036-fd5207a966ff', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '19430_harp_instructor_olive_grove'), 'A', 'Daniel, because the role description identified a specific person when the deed was delivered.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'A is correct. The deed uses a role description, not Daniel’s personal name. Gold Key: A deed can identify a grantee by an office or role if one person fitting that description is reasonably ascertainable when the deed is delivered. Silver Key: Read the recipient phrase before reading the formalities traps: “to the harp instructor” points first to the person who holds that role, not automatically to the school and not back to the grantor.

Daniel was the only harp instructor at the moment of delivery, and he accepted the deed. That makes Daniel the ascertainable grantee.', NULL, 'RP-DEED-ROLE-GRANTEE-01'),
  ('e63cc0af-03ad-5cc4-872b-df4b6ebc05b7', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '19430_harp_instructor_olive_grove'), 'B', 'Lydia, because Daniel did not sign the deed.', 0, '["fabricated_rule"]', '["personal_name_required","grantee_signature_required","organization_named_inside_phrase_owns","contract_formality_import"]', 'The student imports the contract habit that the recipient must sign. The breaker is the deed Gold Key: the grantee accepts, but generally need not sign.', 'Adds a required grantee signature that the Gold Key rejects.', 'Separate grantor execution from grantee acceptance.', 'RP-DEED-ROLE-GRANTEE-01'),
  ('d6a5f648-0cb0-549c-a079-06da28fbf2ef', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '19430_harp_instructor_olive_grove'), 'C', 'Lydia, because a deed must state the grantee’s personal name.', 0, '["fabricated_rule"]', '["personal_name_required","grantee_signature_required","organization_named_inside_phrase_owns","contract_formality_import"]', 'The student over-formalizes the deed and thinks only a personal name can work. The breaker is the ascertainability Gold Key: the role description can identify Daniel.', 'Adds a personal-name requirement that the Gold Key rejects.', 'Ask whether exactly one person fit the role at delivery.', 'RP-DEED-ROLE-GRANTEE-01'),
  ('bacb807e-86f1-50ef-b4e2-bc864a7f227a', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '19430_harp_instructor_olive_grove'), 'D', 'Capernaum Hymn School, because the role title automatically makes the school the grantee.', 0, '["flat_misstatement"]', '["personal_name_required","grantee_signature_required","organization_named_inside_phrase_owns","contract_formality_import"]', 'The student sees Capernaum Hymn School inside the phrase and jumps to the organization. The breaker is the recipient phrase: the deed says to the harp instructor, not to the school.', 'Changes the recipient from the role-holder to the school.', 'Read “to the harp instructor” as pointing to the role-holder.', 'RP-DEED-ROLE-GRANTEE-01'),
  ('6452f404-d83d-5fea-8c8e-c661fedd8a39', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '19434_hymn_hands_stimulator'), 'A', 'Yes, because Lydia was injured by the exact risk described in the warning.', 0, '["wrong_element"]', '["patient_must_always_be_warned_directly","injury_equals_defect","prescription_products_are_immune"]', 'The student sees that the harm matched the warning and treats injury as the missing puzzle piece. The breaker is that injury proves damage, while the call asks whether the warning duty was breached.', 'This choice proves injury by the warned-of risk, not failure to warn.', 'Name the missing element: warning defect or warning audience.', 'REM-TORTS-LI-01'),
  ('6271585b-5e69-5a2f-bca0-26d30c4bab79', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '19434_hymn_hands_stimulator'), 'B', 'No, because prescription medical devices are exempt from all products liability claims.', 0, '["tiered_absolute"]', '["patient_must_always_be_warned_directly","injury_equals_defect","prescription_products_are_immune"]', 'The student overcorrects for the special status of prescription products and converts a special warning rule into total immunity. The breaker is the word all, which overstates the result and dodges the actual warning-duty reason.', 'This choice overclaims by saying prescription medical devices are exempt from all products liability claims.', 'Mark the word all and ask whether the result is narrower.', 'REM-TORTS-LI-01'),
  ('8e33977a-0c24-57c0-9c0a-4f7705057c7d', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '19434_hymn_hands_stimulator'), 'C', 'Yes, because makers of prescription medical devices must always warn patients directly.', 0, '["tiered_absolute"]', '["patient_must_always_be_warned_directly","injury_equals_defect","prescription_products_are_immune"]', 'The student hears patient safety and assumes direct warning is always required. The breaker is the Gold Key: in this jurisdiction the prescribing physician can be the warning audience.', 'This choice overclaims by saying direct patient warning is always required despite the learned-intermediary rule.', 'Apply the learned-intermediary distinction.', 'REM-TORTS-LI-01'),
  ('b5a468d7-a46c-5f48-b206-cbe81f2969b5', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '19434_hymn_hands_stimulator'), 'D', 'No, because Barnabas Biotech adequately warned Stephen, the prescribing physician, who served as the learned intermediary.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'After A, B, and C each break one filter, D is the only true-and-responsive answer.', NULL, 'REM-TORTS-LI-01'),
  ('107ce665-631b-576f-a1fc-12ccd2995efd', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '19448_praise_night_tshirt_cannon'), 'A', 'Yes, because negligent entrustment makes Lydia strictly liable for any injury caused by the T-shirt cannon.', 0, '["tiered_absolute"]', '["title_equals_control","injury_equals_strict_liability","operator_alone_breaks_duty"]', 'The injury makes automatic liability feel satisfying because the chattel was dangerous. The breaker is that ''strictly liable'' and ''any injury'' overstate a negligence theory.', 'The answer converts a negligence-duty theory into strict liability for any injury.', 'Circle ''strictly'' and ''any'' and cut the overclaim.', 'RC-TORTS-NE-001'),
  ('2e4992d7-ef5e-536a-9c5e-3fff81cf5844', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '19448_praise_night_tshirt_cannon'), 'B', 'No, because only the legal owner of a chattel can be liable for negligent entrustment.', 0, '["fabricated_rule","wrong_frame"]', '["title_equals_control","injury_equals_strict_liability","operator_alone_breaks_duty"]', 'Formal title sounds like the clean legal gate for responsibility over property. The breaker is the Gold Key: negligent entrustment looks to control or supply, not just ownership.', 'The answer invents a legal-owner-only gate that the Gold Key defeats.', 'Ask whether the defendant supplied or controlled access to the chattel.', 'RC-TORTS-NE-001'),
  ('24ef69ba-c605-5135-ae2e-00e9a2a88ac0', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '19448_praise_night_tshirt_cannon'), 'C', 'No, because Barnabas alone fired the cannon and third-party conduct always cuts off Lydia''s duty.', 0, '["tiered_absolute"]', '["title_equals_control","injury_equals_strict_liability","operator_alone_breaks_duty"]', 'Barnabas physically fired the cannon, so the student may stop at the operator. The breaker is the word ''always'' and the call-focus move: Lydia''s duty can arise from foreseeable misuse by the person she supplied.', 'The answer overclaims that third-party conduct always cuts off the target defendant''s duty.', 'Reject ''always'' and ask whether the third-party misuse was the foreseeable risk.', 'RC-TORTS-NE-001'),
  ('6ced83c3-86a7-571b-90b2-a7917b5dfde8', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '19448_praise_night_tshirt_cannon'), 'D', 'Yes, because Lydia supplied the T-shirt cannon to a visibly intoxicated and inexperienced person.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'It is the only answer that is both true under the Gold Key and responsive to the duty call.', NULL, 'RC-TORTS-NE-001'),
  ('06eb07fa-94d9-5ccd-a02b-1cf6400c1acf', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '19499_jericho_chariot_night'), 'A', 'Wharton’s Rule always bars a conspiracy charge whenever the target offense involves any agreement.', 0, '["tiered_absolute"]', '["two-party offense always bars conspiracy","profit motive decides Wharton''s Rule","all agreement crimes merge with conspiracy"]', 'The student reaches for a clean universal rule because the answer sounds decisive. The breaker is that always/whenever language over-expands a narrow doctrine.', 'The answer expands a narrow rule with always/whenever language.', 'Mark always/whenever and ask whether a narrow exception exists.', 'REM-CRIMINAL-WHARTON-COUNT'),
  ('d73013e2-8934-5bfa-8648-f351108fa5d2', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '19499_jericho_chariot_night'), 'B', 'Wharton’s Rule bars the conspiracy charge only because the organizers hoped to make money from the event.', 0, '["wrong_element"]', '["two-party offense always bars conspiracy","profit motive decides Wharton''s Rule","all agreement crimes merge with conspiracy"]', 'The student treats profit as the reason the organizers matter. The breaker is that participant count, not payment, is the Wharton''s Rule axis.', 'The answer focuses on money rather than participant count.', 'Rename the axis as participant count, not compensation.', 'REM-CRIMINAL-WHARTON-COUNT'),
  ('6e0c87bc-e8e4-56ae-8d61-e229b2426a79', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '19499_jericho_chariot_night'), 'C', 'Wharton’s Rule does not bar the charge because the agreement includes extra participants beyond the two racers needed for the offense.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'After the overclaim, payment red herring, and base-rule trap are cut, C is the only true-and-responsive answer.', NULL, 'REM-CRIMINAL-WHARTON-COUNT'),
  ('c8bbbc0c-9ef1-51fd-a73c-875c3d014dcc', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '19499_jericho_chariot_night'), 'D', 'Wharton’s Rule bars the conspiracy charge because the illegal chariot race requires two racers.', 0, '["bait_doctrine","wrong_frame"]', '["two-party offense always bars conspiracy","profit motive decides Wharton''s Rule","all agreement crimes merge with conspiracy"]', 'The student remembers the base Wharton''s Rule idea that a two-party offense can absorb conspiracy. The breaker is the extra-party exception when unnecessary organizers join the agreement.', 'The answer states the base two-party idea but omits the extra-party exception.', 'Apply the extra-party exception after counting actual participants.', 'REM-CRIMINAL-WHARTON-COUNT'),
  ('2689acb9-79f4-50e2-b51f-2ed6944b923c', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '19502_manna_festival_finals_tax'), 'A', 'unconstitutional, because Congress violates the equal protection component of the Fifth Amendment by taxing final-round artisan food competitions while failing to tax other large paid food festivals.', 0, '["fabricated_rule"]', '["high_tax_is_unconstitutional","economic_harm_defeats_tax","all_similar_events_must_be_taxed","compelling_interest_always_helps"]', 'This sells the fairness instinct that if Congress taxes one large paid event category, it should tax neighboring paid event categories too. The breaker is the fabricated all-or-nothing tax rule; no suspect class or fundamental-right trigger appears.', 'The choice invents an all-or-nothing tax classification rule.', 'Require a protected trigger or a truly irrational classification before using equal protection to defeat a tax.', 'REM-CONLAW-TAX-REVENUE-01'),
  ('95624dd5-9994-5331-aaae-3db45e8d8a34', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '19502_manna_festival_finals_tax'), 'B', 'unconstitutional, because a 40% admission tax is likely to reduce attendance at these final-round competitions and, therefore, is not rationally related to Congress’s legitimate interest in retiring disaster-recovery debt.', 0, '["contradiction"]', '["high_tax_is_unconstitutional","economic_harm_defeats_tax","all_similar_events_must_be_taxed","compelling_interest_always_helps"]', 'This sells the economic-impact worry that a high tax might shrink attendance and therefore look self-defeating. The breaker is the non sequitur; a paid-admission excise tax can still be rationally connected to revenue even if attendance drops.', 'The attendance-impact reason does not prove the tax lacks a revenue relationship.', 'Say: burdening the taxed activity is not the same as lacking a revenue relation.', 'REM-CONLAW-TAX-REVENUE-01'),
  ('60c304ff-51fe-5d61-9f51-5c5a39442f22', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '19502_manna_festival_finals_tax'), 'C', 'constitutional, because an act of Congress that appears to be a revenue-raising measure on its face is not rendered invalid because it may have adverse economic consequences for the activity taxed.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'It answers the precise tax-power call and uses the correct revenue-tax frame.', NULL, 'REM-CONLAW-TAX-REVENUE-01'),
  ('f79bfa19-b20a-519d-be02-94cea4e43923', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '19502_manna_festival_finals_tax'), 'D', 'constitutional, because the compelling national interest in retiring federal disaster-recovery debt justifies this tax as a temporary emergency measure.', 0, '["bait_doctrine","wrong_frame"]', '["high_tax_is_unconstitutional","economic_harm_defeats_tax","all_similar_events_must_be_taxed","compelling_interest_always_helps"]', 'This sells the strongest-sounding constitutional vocabulary by pairing an emergency with a compelling national interest. The breaker is the wrong frame; the stem gives a revenue tax, not a strict-scrutiny trigger.', 'The choice imports strict-scrutiny language without a trigger.', 'Classify the claim home first: this is a tax-power item, not a heightened-scrutiny item.', 'REM-CONLAW-TAX-REVENUE-01'),
  ('f5b59a7a-0e86-5318-b42f-b137ab612e3f', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '19554_road_to_damascus_cashbox'), 'A', 'False pretenses, because the visitors handed the admission fees to Peter voluntarily.', 0, '["bait_doctrine","wrong_frame"]', '["employee_theft_equals_embezzlement","voluntary_payment_equals_false_pretenses","permission_to_handle_defeats_theft"]', 'The student sees that visitors voluntarily handed money to Peter and treats the handoff as the classification. The breaker is that voluntary payment to the business is not title-by-deception to Peter.', 'False pretenses is the neighboring property-crime frame; the voluntary payment fact does not show title transferred to Peter by deception.', 'Ask whether the payer meant to transfer title to the worker personally.', 'RC-CRIM-CUSTODY-POSSESSION-01'),
  ('51cca9e0-2ba9-522c-ad15-dc7f43d29d43', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '19554_road_to_damascus_cashbox'), 'B', 'No theft offense, because Peter was allowed to handle the admission money.', 0, '["wrong_element"]', '["employee_theft_equals_embezzlement","voluntary_payment_equals_false_pretenses","permission_to_handle_defeats_theft"]', 'The student focuses on Peter’s permission to touch and collect the money. The breaker is that permission to handle does not answer permission to keep.', 'Permission to handle the admission money does not answer permission to keep it.', 'Ask what the stem says the worker was allowed to do and what he actually did.', 'RC-CRIM-CUSTODY-POSSESSION-01'),
  ('91861b5b-99c4-5101-bbd0-a254c2d13fbb', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '19554_road_to_damascus_cashbox'), 'C', 'Embezzlement, because all theft by a worker from the business is embezzlement.', 0, '["tiered_absolute","wrong_frame"]', '["employee_theft_equals_embezzlement","voluntary_payment_equals_false_pretenses","permission_to_handle_defeats_theft"]', 'The student uses the familiar worker-theft-equals-embezzlement shortcut. The breaker is that all worker theft is too broad because custody-only workers can commit larceny.', 'All theft by a worker is too broad; worker status does not automatically produce embezzlement.', 'Circle all and classify by property path, not role.', 'RC-CRIM-CUSTODY-POSSESSION-01'),
  ('ba90cb80-5ac5-57bf-8bb6-69481f79e7cc', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '19554_road_to_damascus_cashbox'), 'D', 'Larceny, because Peter had custody of the admission money while Lydia’s business retained possession.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'D is the only choice that answers the classification call and uses the custody/possession distinction required by the Gold Key.', NULL, 'RC-CRIM-CUSTODY-POSSESSION-01'),
  ('0f8d2bba-58f6-5290-b241-686142432c39', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '19607_cana_hills_bethany_flats'), 'A', 'Strict scrutiny applies because the funding gap falls most heavily on low-income students, and low-income students are a suspect class.', 0, '["flat_misstatement","wrong_frame"]', '["education is fundamental","poverty is suspect","impact equals purpose","unfairness equals strict scrutiny"]', 'The choice makes the student’s inequality instinct do the work by treating low-income status as suspect. The breaker is that school-funding poverty or local wealth disparities do not trigger strict scrutiny under the Gold Key.', 'The choice treats low-income status as a suspect-class trigger for strict scrutiny.', 'Ask whether the claimed class is a recognized suspect class for this federal Equal Protection claim.', 'REM-CONLAW-TIER-TRIGGER-01'),
  ('263004ae-388e-5bc9-bc39-bc83e4b8519a', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '19607_cana_hills_bethany_flats'), 'B', 'Strict scrutiny applies because the disparity effectively denies students in the lower-tax-base district equal access to public education, which is a fundamental right.', 0, '["flat_misstatement","wrong_frame"]', '["education is fundamental","poverty is suspect","impact equals purpose","unfairness equals strict scrutiny"]', 'The choice feels right because education sounds too important to be reviewed deferentially. The breaker is the Gold Key that public education is not a federal fundamental right in this school-funding Equal Protection setting.', 'The choice treats public education as a federal fundamental right in a school-funding case.', 'Recall that Rodriguez places local-property-tax school funding in rational basis review.', 'REM-CONLAW-TIER-TRIGGER-01'),
  ('40c6f10e-0bad-51cd-baaa-3ccbd9c5816d', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '19607_cana_hills_bethany_flats'), 'C', 'Intermediate scrutiny applies because the facially neutral funding plan systematically disadvantages lower-income districts that substantially overlap with racial-minority communities.', 0, '["half_truth","wrong_frame"]', '["education is fundamental","poverty is suspect","impact equals purpose","unfairness equals strict scrutiny"]', 'The choice converts racial overlap and unequal effects into a heightened-scrutiny trigger. The breaker is that disparate impact alone is not enough without discriminatory purpose or a racial classification.', 'The choice gives impact and racial overlap, but not discriminatory purpose or a racial classification.', 'Ask whether the stem gives discriminatory purpose or a facial racial classification.', 'REM-CONLAW-TIER-TRIGGER-01'),
  ('571b16ef-9e89-5eb8-aeb5-36d5ffd2aea4', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '19607_cana_hills_bethany_flats'), 'D', 'Rational basis review applies, and the plan likely survives because local control of public schools and tying local tax revenue to local school spending are conceivable legitimate government interests.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'After the invalid heightened-review triggers are cut by Gold Keys, only the default-tier answer remains.', NULL, 'REM-CONLAW-TIER-TRIGGER-01'),
  ('54a6dc21-f58b-50c0-9146-9b4ed23f2f20', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '19721_jonah_whale_float'), 'A', 'Grant the motion, because a court would be unlikely to award more than $75,000 in damages on these facts.', 0, '["fabricated_rule","procedural_frame"]', '["notice_is_always_enough","unlikely_damages_defeat_jurisdiction","false_imprisonment_can_be_negligent","grant_result_is_enough"]', 'This choice gives the same grant result and makes the damages feel too speculative. The breaker is that likely recovery is not the amount-in-controversy standard and not the claim-sufficiency defect.', 'This invents a likely-recovery amount-in-controversy standard and does not resolve the claim-sufficiency defect.', 'Ask whether the reason is the actual motion defect.', 'REM-CIV-12B6-INTENT-01'),
  ('28d18d6e-a9e9-5508-a2fc-626917501c2b', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '19721_jonah_whale_float'), 'B', 'Deny the motion, because there is no indication that it is impossible for recovery to exceed $75,000.', 0, '["bait_doctrine","procedural_frame"]', '["notice_is_always_enough","unlikely_damages_defeat_jurisdiction","false_imprisonment_can_be_negligent","grant_result_is_enough"]', 'This choice states the amount-in-controversy frame students remember from diversity jurisdiction. The breaker is that the motion is granted because the complaint fails to state false imprisonment, not because federal jurisdiction is missing.', 'This uses the amount-in-controversy frame when the operative defect is failure to state false imprisonment.', 'Separate jurisdiction survival from claim survival.', 'REM-CIV-12B6-INTENT-01'),
  ('fdc75a02-5a16-59ad-9548-63c02316b2b4', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '19721_jonah_whale_float'), 'C', 'Grant the motion, because the complaint fails to state a claim.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'After the anchor-assisted cuts, C is the only answer true and responsive to the motion.', NULL, 'REM-CIV-12B6-INTENT-01'),
  ('07377504-81ea-553e-9418-3b907009e5b4', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '19721_jonah_whale_float'), 'D', 'Deny the motion, because the complaint gives MannaJoy notice of the claim being asserted.', 0, '["wrong_element","procedural_frame"]', '["notice_is_always_enough","unlikely_damages_defeat_jurisdiction","false_imprisonment_can_be_negligent","grant_result_is_enough"]', 'This choice leans on the familiar notice-pleading idea. The breaker is that notice does not cure a complaint that pleads facts defeating the selected claim.', 'This supplies notice but does not answer the complaint-negates-element problem.', 'Ask whether notice also shows entitlement to relief.', 'REM-CIV-12B6-INTENT-01'),
  ('6d89cb36-7f9d-528b-ab51-f26bcd1ee94d', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '19793_dove_candle_ledgers'), 'A', 'Yes, because any discovery order that deals with a side issue instead of the merits is immediately appealable.', 0, '["half_truth","procedural_frame"]', '["cost_creates_appeal","separate_prong_enough","no_immediate_appeal_means_never"]', 'The student recognizes that collateral-order doctrine deals with issues separate from the merits. The breaker is that one prong is not the whole appealability gate.', 'It treats separateness from the merits as enough and omits the narrowness/effective-unreviewability gate.', 'Say: one prong is not the test.', 'RC-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-COLLATERAL-ORDER-NARROWNESS-01'),
  ('1e30c264-0043-5dcc-abc2-1bd5da172b7a', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '19793_dove_candle_ledgers'), 'B', 'No, because an ordinary document-production order usually must wait for final-judgment review.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'B is the best answer. The order is an ordinary document-production order. The fact that retrieving and scanning the documents will cost money does not make the order immediately appealable.

Gold Key: Collateral-order review is narrow. A discovery order must fit the small class of orders that are effectively unreviewable after final judgment; ordinary discovery-cost complaints do not.

Silver Key: In an appealability array, read the fight as review now vs review later. Cut the extreme “any/every/never/automatically” answers first, then ask whether final-judgment review is enough.', NULL, 'RC-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-COLLATERAL-ORDER-NARROWNESS-01'),
  ('8146b47d-6116-5dd3-ab64-404663b6f086', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '19793_dove_candle_ledgers'), 'C', 'No, because a discovery order can never be challenged by any route.', 0, '["tiered_absolute","procedural_frame"]', '["cost_creates_appeal","separate_prong_enough","no_immediate_appeal_means_never"]', 'The student knows ordinary discovery orders usually are not appealable now and overcorrects. The breaker is that no immediate appeal does not mean no challenge by any route.', 'It says discovery orders can never be challenged, turning no immediate appeal into no review by any route.', 'Say: not now is not never.', 'RC-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-COLLATERAL-ORDER-NARROWNESS-01'),
  ('62208e9a-491a-5408-a6d4-e07e7673ae2d', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '19793_dove_candle_ledgers'), 'D', 'Yes, because an expensive production order automatically qualifies as a collateral order.', 0, '["tiered_absolute","procedural_frame"]', '["cost_creates_appeal","separate_prong_enough","no_immediate_appeal_means_never"]', 'The student treats expense as a fairness reason for immediate appellate protection. The breaker is that cost does not automatically satisfy collateral-order narrowness.', 'It says expense automatically creates a collateral order.', 'Say: cost is bait; gate first.', 'RC-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-COLLATERAL-ORDER-NARROWNESS-01'),
  ('7d345ffb-7f7d-5d30-9e78-147e94826664', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '19840_red_sea_bridge_bolts'), 'A', 'Yes, unless Lydia''s lawyer later states the name in open court.', 0, '["bait_doctrine","wrong_frame"]', '["everything_told_to_lawyer_is_secret","civil_cases_have_no_privilege","waiver_controls_all_privilege_questions"]', 'The choice sells a waiver story because lawyers repeating facts in court sounds like the moment a privilege would be lost. The breaker is that the asked-for item is an underlying fact, so waiver timing is not the question.', 'The choice answers a later waiver problem, not whether the underlying name was privileged when requested.', 'Circle the asserted ground in the stem and reject events not in the stem.', 'CIV-DISC-PRIV-01'),
  ('d2ca90dc-0b8c-5f8f-aee7-f8249d55edd0', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '19840_red_sea_bridge_bolts'), 'B', 'No, because attorney-client privilege never applies in civil cases.', 0, '["tiered_absolute"]', '["everything_told_to_lawyer_is_secret","civil_cases_have_no_privilege","waiver_controls_all_privilege_questions"]', 'The choice sells an easy no by using a broad civil-case shortcut. The breaker is the absolute never, which contradicts the privilege frame rather than answering the fact-versus-communication split.', 'The word never overclaims by erasing civil attorney-client privilege.', 'Test both halves: the outcome may be right while the reason is overbroad.', 'CIV-DISC-PRIV-01'),
  ('7983325e-6a2d-55c4-858f-e188caed49b1', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '19840_red_sea_bridge_bolts'), 'C', 'Yes, because every fact a client discusses with a lawyer while seeking legal advice becomes privileged.', 0, '["tiered_absolute"]', '["everything_told_to_lawyer_is_secret","civil_cases_have_no_privilege","waiver_controls_all_privilege_questions"]', 'The choice sells privacy because the fact was discussed with a lawyer during a legal-advice meeting. The breaker is the word every, which tries to convert all discussed facts into protected material.', 'The phrase every fact overextends privilege from communications to underlying facts.', 'Ask whether the request seeks the fact itself or the lawyer conversation.', 'CIV-DISC-PRIV-01'),
  ('d782465f-6a41-5eff-bca9-88033853dd18', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '19840_red_sea_bridge_bolts'), 'D', 'No, because an opposing party may discover relevant underlying facts even if those facts were communicated to counsel.', 1, '[]', '[]', NULL, 'It is the only choice that says no for the fact-versus-communication reason.', NULL, 'CIV-DISC-PRIV-01'),
  ('95dfda50-6008-5135-ad4e-fbe061185758', (SELECT question_id FROM questions WHERE external_id = '19904_psalm_orchard_foreclosure_notice'), 'A', 'order the sale of a remainder only.', 0, '["wrong_element","procedural_frame"]', '["visible_possessor_controls","future_interest_can_be_ignored","substantive_fee_answer_beats_process"]', 'The student sees Mary’s future interest and treats the remainder as the legally precise target. The breaker is that the court cannot choose a sale target before the affected interest holders are served.', 'The choice isolates the remainder but does not answer whether a sale may be ordered without notice/service to the interest holders.', 'Ask whether the remainderman was notified or served before any sale answer can be chosen.', 'REM-RP-FORECLOSURE-NOTICE-01'),
